window.OCHEM_DB=[{"q":"(R)-2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with NaOH in DMSO (a polar aprotic solvent). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@H](C)O","CCCCO","CC[C@@H](C)O","CC(C)CO","CC[C@H](C)Br"],"correct":0,"why":"2 degree halide + strong small anionic Nu (OH-) in polar aprotic DMSO = SN2, backside attack, inversion. Br and OH hold the same #1 CIP rank, so physical inversion flips the descriptor R->S, giving (S)-2-butanol (opt0). RDKit: substrate=R, opt0=S confirmed. opt1=R (retention, wrong). opt2/opt3 wrong skeleton, opt4 unreacted SM.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 inversion correct, opt0 (S) verified by RDKit, all SMILES valid, single reactive center + stereo, in DAT scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated in water (H2O) as the solvent. What is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)CC","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)CCO","CCC(C)CO","CC(C)C(C)O"],"correct":1,"why":"3 degree halide + weak neutral solvent nucleophile (H2O) = SN1; the initial cation is already tertiary so no rearrangement, water attacks to give 2-methyl-2-butanol (opt0). opt1/opt2/opt5 place OH on wrong carbons; opt4 (C=C(C)CC) is the E1 alkene, a competing pathway, not the substitution product asked for. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN1 substitution product correct, exactly one substitution answer, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"(S)-2-Iodooctane (drawn) is treated with NaCN in acetone. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCC[C@H](C)I","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=N[C@H](C)CCCCCC","CCCCCC[C@H](C)C#N","CCCCCCC(C)C#N","CCCCCC[C@@H](C)C#N","CCCCCC[C@@H](C)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Cyanide is a strong unhindered Nu, 2 degree halide with good LG = SN2, inversion. The nitrile carbon (N,N,N duplicate atoms) outranks the hexyl CH2 (C,H,H), so CN takes the same #1 rank I held; physical inversion flips S->R (opt0). RDKit: substrate=S, opt0=R confirmed. opt1=S (retention). opt2 wrong Nu (O). opt3 wrong skeleton. opt5 isonitrile (wrong connectivity).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 + CIP-aware inversion to R verified, valid SMILES, two layered concepts (mechanism + stereo) acceptable at difficulty 3, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl bromide (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COCc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc(CBr)cc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Methoxide is a strong Nu; the leaving-group carbon is a primary benzylic (unhindered) center, so fast SN2 gives benzyl methyl ether (opt0). opt1 is the alcohol (wrong Nu), opt2 (anisole) puts O on the ring (wrong regiochemistry), opt3 is a different skeleton, opt4 is an elimination/wrong product. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 benzyl methyl ether correct, single answer, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"(S)-(1-Bromoethyl)benzene (drawn), a secondary benzylic halide, is solvolyzed in water (H2O). What best describes the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](Br)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCCc1ccccc1","C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","C[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"2 degree benzylic halide + weak solvent Nu (H2O) = SN1 via a resonance-stabilized planar benzylic cation; attack from both faces gives racemic 1-phenylethanol, best shown without a defined stereocenter (opt0). opt1/opt2 are single enantiomers (SN1 is not enantiopure), opt3 has OH on wrong carbon, opt4 is the alkene. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN1 racemization concept correct, opt0 (no stereocenter) is the right racemic representation, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (2,2-dimethyl-1-bromopropane, drawn) is treated with NaI in acetone. Which product, if any, forms, and what governs the rate?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)(C)I","COCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)(C)CI","CC(C)(C)CO"],"correct":3,"why":"Iodide is a strong Nu and the C-Br carbon is primary, so SN2 (no cation, no rearrangement). It is slow because the neighboring quaternary tBu sterically blocks backside attack, but the product is simple substitution: neopentyl iodide (opt0). opt1 is a rearranged 3 degree iodide (requires a cation SN2 never forms), opt2 wrong Nu, opt3 reduced/wrong, opt5 wrong Nu (OMe). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 with no rearrangement correct, opt1 rearrangement trap is a strong distractor, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"cis-1-Bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane (drawn) is treated with NaOH in DMSO. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](Br)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](Br)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CC=CCC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"Strong Nu (OH-) at a 2 degree ring carbon = SN2, backside attack, inversion. 3D embedding confirms the substrate is cis; inversion puts OH on the opposite face from tBu, giving the trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol (opt0, verified TRANS by 3D geometry). opt1 is the cis alcohol (retention, wrong). opt2 elimination alkene, opt3 contracted (5-membered) ring, opt4 is the trans-bromide. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"Keyed answer (trans alcohol, opt0) is correct and question is unambiguous; minor wording slip in rationale: opt4 is actually the trans-bromide, not literally the cis starting material, but it remains a valid wrong distractor and does not affect the single correct answer."},{"q":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane (drawn) is treated with NaSH in ethanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@H](C)O","CC[C@@H](C)S","CC=CC","CCCCS","CC[C@H](C)S"],"correct":4,"why":"Hydrosulfide (SH-) is a strong, polarizable Nu; 2 degree halide = SN2, inversion. S keeps the #1 CIP rank Cl held, so inversion flips R->S, giving (S)-butane-2-thiol (opt0). RDKit: substrate=R, opt0=S confirmed. opt1=R (retention), opt2 wrong skeleton, opt3 wrong Nu (O), opt4 elimination alkene. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 inversion to (S)-thiol verified, valid SMILES, single reactive center + stereo, in scope."},{"q":"The substrate shown is treated with water (H2O) as the solvent and nucleophile, with no added base. Which mechanism best describes how this reaction proceeds?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts":["SN2, because the bulky methyl groups accelerate backside displacement","E2, because water is a strong enough base to force concerted elimination","SN2, because water performs a fast backside attack on the tertiary carbon","No reaction, because tertiary halides are completely unreactive toward water","SN1, because the substrate is tertiary and forms a stable 3 degree carbocation in the polar protic solvent"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"tert-Butyl bromide is 3 degree. A weak neutral nucleophile (water) in polar protic solvent favors SN1: ionization to the stable tertiary cation then capture by water; SN2 is sterically blocked.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; tertiary + weak Nu + polar protic = SN1; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"The primary alkyl halide shown is reacted with sodium iodide (NaI), a strong, small, unhindered nucleophile, in acetone. Which mechanism is operating?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["SN1, because primary carbocations are highly stabilized","SN1, because iodide is a good leaving group that triggers ionization","E1, because acetone promotes unimolecular elimination","SN2, because a primary substrate with a strong nucleophile in a polar aprotic solvent favors concerted backside attack","E2, because iodide is a strong base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"1-Bromobutane is primary; primary cations are too unstable for SN1. Strong unhindered I- in polar aprotic acetone is textbook SN2.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; primary + strong Nu + aprotic = SN2; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (shown) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","opts":["C[C@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](O)CC","C[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(C)C#N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Secondary substrate + strong CN- in polar aprotic DMSO = SN2 with backside inversion. 3D inversion of (R)-2-bromobutane gives canonical CC[C@H](C)C#N = the (S)-nitrile (opt0). The descriptor flips R to S because CN outranks ethyl; opt1 is retention, opt2 is achiral/flat, opt3 is unreacted, opt4 is wrong nucleophile.","difficulty":3,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All 5 SMILES valid; verified by 3D simulation the inversion product is the (S)-nitrile = opt0; correct=0 confirmed; distractors differ by stereo/regio/skeleton; in scope (single center, regio+stereo)."},{"q":"Benzyl chloride (shown) undergoes substitution readily by BOTH SN1 and SN2 pathways even though it is a primary halide. What is the reason it is unusually reactive in SN1?","q_smiles":"ClCc1ccccc1","opts":["The aromatic ring provides steric bulk that forces ionization","The chlorine is a better leaving group when attached to a benzylic carbon","Primary benzylic carbons cannot undergo SN2 at all","The benzylic carbocation is resonance-stabilized by the adjacent aromatic ring","The ring withdraws electron density, weakening the C-Cl bond by induction"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Benzylic halides are an exception: although primary, the cation on ionization is delocalized into the ring by resonance, enabling SN1; the unhindered carbon still allows SN2.","difficulty":2,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; resonance-stabilized benzylic cation is the correct reason; correct=0 right; distractors plausible-but-wrong; in scope."},{"q":"The optically active tertiary bromide shown is dissolved in aqueous ethanol (a polar protic solvent) with no added nucleophile. What is the expected stereochemical outcome at the original stereocenter?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@](C)(Br)CC","opts":["Racemization, because the planar carbocation is attacked from both faces with roughly equal probability","Complete retention of configuration","Complete inversion of configuration","No substitution and no change in stereochemistry","Formation of a single new stereocenter with defined R configuration only"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"3-Bromo-3-methylhexane is a genuine tertiary stereocenter (4 distinct groups). SN1 conditions (3 degree, weak Nu, polar protic) give a planar carbocation attacked on both faces, yielding near-racemization, unlike SN2 inversion.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; confirmed genuinely chiral tertiary halide so racemization concept holds; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"For the tertiary chloride shown reacting under SN1 conditions, which single change would MOST increase the reaction rate?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Cl","opts":["Adding a bulky, weakly nucleophilic base","Replacing the leaving group with fluoride","Using a polar aprotic solvent such as DMSO","Lowering the temperature of the reaction","Switching from a less polar solvent to a more polar protic solvent such as water or formic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SN1 rate depends only on the unimolecular ionization. A more polar protic solvent stabilizes the developing cation and anion, accelerating it. Nucleophile identity is irrelevant to SN1 rate; F- is a poor leaving group; aprotic solvent and lower temperature slow ionization.","difficulty":2,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; correct=0 right; all four distractors verified to slow or not affect SN1 rate; exactly one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, shown) reacts extremely slowly in SN2 reactions despite being a primary halide. Why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts":["The primary carbon forms a very stable carbocation, diverting it to SN1","The adjacent quaternary carbon's bulky tert-butyl group sterically blocks backside attack on the primary carbon","The C-Br bond is unusually strong in neopentyl systems","Bromide is a poor leaving group on primary carbons","Backside attack is electronically forbidden for primary halides"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Neopentyl halides are primary but bear a bulky adjacent quaternary carbon that crowds the backside trajectory, making SN2 very slow; a primary cation rules out SN1 too.","difficulty":2,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; steric blocking by neighboring quaternary carbon is the correct reason; correct=0 right; distractors plausible-but-wrong; in scope."},{"q":"Rank the following alkyl bromides from FASTEST to SLOWEST in an SN2 reaction with sodium azide (NaN3): I. CH3Br  II. CH3CH2Br  III. (CH3)2CHBr  IV. (CH3)3CBr","q_smiles":"CBr","opts":["III > II > I > IV","IV > III > II > I","I > III > II > IV","I > II > III > IV","II > I > III > IV"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"SN2 rate is set by steric access, decreasing with substitution: methyl > primary > secondary > tertiary, so I > II > III > IV.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid q_smiles (methyl bromide); standard SN2 steric ranking; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"(R)-2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in DMSO. Which structure is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@@H](C)OCC","CC[C@H](C)O","C=CCC","CC[C@H](C)OCC","CCC(C)OCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Verified: substrate is (R) by CIP. Strong unhindered nucleophile (ethoxide) + polar aprotic DMSO on a secondary center = SN2 with backside inversion. (R) inverts to (S)-2-ethoxybutane = opt0 (CIP confirms opt0 is S, opt1 is R/retention). Distractors are retention, no-stereo, wrong-nucleophile alcohol, and an elimination fragment. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. Substrate (R) and keyed (S) product confirmed by CIP; classic SN2 inversion, all SMILES valid, single concept (stereo)."},{"q":"(S)-3-chloro-3-methylhexane (drawn, a tertiary chloride) is stirred in methanol (CH3OH) at room temperature. Which structure best represents the substitution product?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@@](C)(Cl)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC[C@](C)(CC)OC","CCCC(C)(O)CC","CCCC(C)(CC)OC","CC=C(C)CCC","CCC[C@@](C)(CC)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"Tertiary substrate + weak neutral nucleophile (MeOH) + polar protic = SN1 via planar carbocation, attacked from both faces = racemic. The single best drawing is the methyl ether with undefined configuration (opt0; RDKit confirms opt0 has an unassigned stereocenter while opt1/opt2 are the two discrete enantiomers, neither alone correct). opt3 is the alcohol (wrong nucleophile), opt4 an elimination alkene. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. SN1 racemization correctly keyed to the no-stereo ether; all SMILES valid; single concept."},{"q":"(R)-2-iodooctane (drawn) reacts with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMF. Which structure is the major product, with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CCCCCC[C@@H](C)I","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCC[C@@H](C)C#N","CCCCCC[C@H](C)N","CCCCCCC(C)C#N","[C-]#[N+][C@@H](C)CCCCCC","CCCCCC[C@H](C)C#N"],"correct":4,"why":"Verified substrate is (R). Cyanide attacks via its carbon; secondary substrate + strong nucleophile + polar aprotic DMF = SN2 with inversion. CIP confirms opt0 is (S) (inverted) and opt1 is (R)/retention. opt2 is the isonitrile (N-attack, valid SMILES but wrong/minor connectivity), opt3 an amine, opt4 racemic. Two layered concepts (C-attack regiochemistry + inversion) = difficulty 2, fair.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. (R)->（S) inversion confirmed by CIP; isonitrile distractor valid; in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, drawn) is treated with sodium iodide in acetone. Which statement best explains the observed outcome?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["It undergoes fast E2 elimination to give the more substituted alkene","It reacts rapidly by SN1 because the primary carbocation is stabilized by the tert-butyl group","It reacts rapidly by SN2 because primary substrates always favor SN2","It rearranges to a tertiary iodide via a hydride shift before substitution","The reaction is extremely slow because the adjacent quaternary carbon sterically blocks SN2 backside attack, even though the carbon bearing Br is primary"],"correct":4,"why":"Neopentyl is the textbook 'primary but SN2-resistant' case: the neighboring quaternary carbon's methyls block backside approach (Finkelstein/NaI-acetone are SN2 conditions). opt0 is the only correct explanation. SN1 (opt1) fails for a primary cation; opt2 ignores sterics; the carbon bearing Br has no beta-H positioned for E2 and NaI is not a base (opt3); the carbocation/hydride-shift path (opt4) does not operate under SN2 conditions. q_smiles valid; single-arrow/none, classification item.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. Classic neopentyl steric reasoning correct; text-classification item with valid drawn substrate; in scope."},{"q":"(R)-(1-chloroethyl)benzene [(R)-1-phenylethyl chloride, drawn] is solvolyzed in warm water. Which structure best represents the substitution product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Cl)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC(Cl)c1ccccc1O"],"correct":1,"why":"Benzylic secondary chloride + water = SN1 via resonance-stabilized planar benzylic cation, captured from both faces = racemic. Best single representation is the alcohol with undefined configuration = index 2 (RDKit confirms opt2 has an unassigned stereocenter; opt0/opt1 are the discrete enantiomers, neither alone correct). opt3 styrene (elimination), opt4 a ring-substituted byproduct that does not form. Keyed index 2 matches.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. SN1 racemization correctly keyed to index 2 (no-stereo alcohol); all SMILES valid; single concept."},{"q":"cis-1-bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane (drawn; Br and the tert-butyl group on the same face) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH) under SN2 conditions. Which structure is the substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)[C@@H]1CC[C@H](Br)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCC(O)C1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CC=CCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"3D embedding confirms the substrate is cis (Br and tBu on the same face). SN2 backside attack inverts at the C-Br carbon, moving OH to the opposite face = trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol = opt0 (3D confirms opt0 is trans, opt1 is cis/retention). opt2 drops cis/trans, opt3 is an elimination alkene, opt4 places OH at the 1,3 (wrong) position. Two concepts (relative stereo + position) = difficulty 2, fair.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. cis substrate -> trans product confirmed by 3D; keyed opt0 correct; SMILES valid; in scope."},{"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane, drawn) is dissolved in aqueous ethanol and slowly forms tert-butyl alcohol. Which description correctly identifies the rate-determining step and its single curved arrow?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The slow step is unimolecular ionization: one arrow goes from the C-Br bond to bromine, forming a tert-butyl carbocation and bromide","The slow step is bimolecular: one arrow goes from water's oxygen lone pair to carbon as bromide leaves in the same step","The slow step is the arrow from water's lone pair to the carbocation, forming the protonated alcohol","The slow step is loss of a beta-proton: one arrow from a C-H bond to form the alkene","The slow step is bromide leaving with an arrow from bromine's lone pair into the C-Br bond"],"correct":0,"why":"Tertiary substrate + weak nucleophile (water) + polar protic = SN1; RDS is unimolecular ionization with the arrow from the C-Br bond to Br, forming the stabilized tertiary cation (opt0). opt1 is a concerted SN2 (not operative here), opt2 an E1/E2 deprotonation, opt3 the fast post-RDS capture step, opt4 draws the arrow backward (toward C). Single-arrow identification only (allowed; not a full mechanism). q_smiles valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. Single-arrow RDS identification (within the at-most-one-arrow allowance), correct answer, valid drawn substrate, in scope."},{"q":"3-chloro-3-methyl-1-butene (drawn, a tertiary allylic chloride) is solvolyzed in water. Besides the unrearranged tertiary alcohol, a rearranged constitutional isomer also forms. Which structure is that rearranged product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CC(C)(C)O","C=CC(C)(C)Cl","C=C(C)CCO","OCC=C(C)C","OCC=CC(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives an allyl cation delocalized over tertiary C3 and primary C1. Water capture at C1 gives the rearranged 3-methyl-2-buten-1-ol (prenol) = opt0 (formula C5H10O, matches substrate-derived product). opt1 is the unrearranged tertiary alcohol (named in stem), opt2 a non-arising isomer, opt4 the starting chloride. Keyed opt0 is correct and is the unique rearranged substitution product.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"Keyed answer correct but the 'why' has errors: prenol's alkene is TRISUBSTITUTED, not 'tetrasubstituted'; and opt3 (OCC=CC(C)C) is C6H12O (six carbons), an impossible product/flawed distractor, not the 'C5 isomer' the rationale implies. Still exactly one right answer and in scope (allylic resonance is standard DAT); fix the explanation and ideally swap opt3 for a true C5 isomer."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol, a strong unhindered base that favors E2 elimination. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)C","opts":["C/C=C/C","CCC(O)C","C=CCC","C/C=C\\C","CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"E2 with a small base follows Zaitsev, giving the more-substituted 2-butene over 1-butene. The anti-periplanar transition state places the two methyl groups on opposite sides, so the (E)/trans-2-butene is the major alkene. Choice B is the minor cis isomer, C is the Hofmann 1-butene, D is the substitution alcohol, and E is a wrong (reduced/rearranged) skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified by RDKit: all SMILES valid. E2/Zaitsev gives 2-butene, trans/E is major isomer (~6:1 experimental). optA=E-2-butene keyed idx0 correct. Two-concept (regio Zaitsev + E/Z stereo). All distractors plausible-but-wrong variants. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu), a bulky hindered base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(Br)C","opts":["CCC(C)(O)C","CCC(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)=C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A bulky base (tert-butoxide) cannot reach the internal, more hindered beta-hydrogens, so it abstracts the less hindered terminal hydrogens, giving the Hofmann (less-substituted) product 2-methyl-1-butene. Choice B is the Zaitsev 2-methyl-2-butene (favored only with small bases), C is a wrong regiochemistry, D is substitution, and E is the reduced alkane.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Bulky base -> Hofmann 2-methyl-1-butene. optA=CCC(C)=C (2-methyl-1-butene) keyed idx0 correct. Pairs with Q3 (same substrate, different base) - not a duplicate. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a small unhindered base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(Br)C","opts":["CCC(C)=C","CCOC(C)(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)C","C=CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A small base follows Zaitsev, giving the more-substituted alkene 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted) over the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-butene. Choice B is the Hofmann product (needs a bulky base), C is wrong regiochemistry, D is an ether from substitution, and E is the reduced skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Small base -> Zaitsev trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene. optA=CC=C(C)C keyed idx0 correct. Single-concept regiochemistry. Distractor D ether is a valid SN1/E1-competition product. Keep."},{"q":"1-Chloro-1-methylcyclohexane is heated with sodium ethoxide. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Zaitsev elimination into the ring gives the more-substituted, fully internal 1-methylcyclohexene rather than the exocyclic methylenecyclohexane (Hofmann). Choice B is the Hofmann product, C is the substitution alcohol, D is the reduced skeleton, and E has the wrong ring size (cyclopentene).","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Zaitsev gives trisubstituted endocyclic 1-methylcyclohexene over exocyclic methylenecyclohexane. optA=CC1=CCCCC1 keyed idx0 correct. Distractor E correctly drawn as cyclopentene (wrong ring size). Keep."},{"q":"3-Bromopentane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), promoting E2 elimination. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)CC","opts":["CC/C=C/C","CCC(C)=C","CC/C=C\\C","CCC(O)CC","CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The symmetric substrate can only form 2-pentene, so the decision is stereochemical: the anti-periplanar E2 transition state that minimizes steric strain gives the more stable (E)/trans-2-pentene as the major product. Choice B is the minor cis isomer, C is substitution, D is the reduced alkane, and E is a branched wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Symmetric substrate -> only 2-pentene; question is purely E/Z and trans is major. optA=CC/C=C/C (E-2-pentene) keyed idx0 correct. Stem correctly frames the symmetry. Distractor E (CCC(C)=C, branched 5C) is a wrong skeleton. Keep."},{"q":"2-Chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium methoxide. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)(C)Cl","opts":["CCC(C)=C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)O","C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Zaitsev elimination gives the most-substituted alkene, here the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene. Choice B is the disubstituted Hofmann product (2,3-dimethyl-1-butene), C is the substitution alcohol, D is the reduced skeleton, and E adds a carbon (wrong skeleton).","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Small base methoxide -> Zaitsev tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (beta-H abstracted from C3). optA=CC(C)=C(C)C keyed idx0 correct. Distractor E correctly adds a carbon (wrong skeleton). Keep."},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane (the diastereomer with Br and the methyl group on opposite faces) is treated with sodium ethoxide under E2 conditions. Anti-periplanar geometry controls the outcome. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","CC1CCCC(Br)C1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"E2 requires the leaving group and beta-H to be anti-periplanar (diaxial in the chair). In the trans isomer, when Br is axial only the C6 hydrogen is anti-periplanar (the C2 position bears the methyl, not an axial H positioned anti to Br), so elimination is forced toward C6 to give 3-methylcyclohexene rather than the Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene. Choice B is the Zaitsev product (blocked by the stereochemistry), C is reduced, D is a constitutional isomer, and E is wrong.","difficulty":3,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified by RDKit 3D embedding: substrate SMILES is genuinely TRANS (Br and Me on opposite faces), matching stem. Classic anti-periplanar E2: trans isomer forces elimination to C6 -> 3-methylcyclohexene. optA=CC1CCCC=C1 keyed idx0 correct. Standard sophomore/Bootcamp stereochem-of-E2 item; in scope as difficulty-3. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylpentane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a small base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)(Br)C","opts":["CCCC(C)=C","CCCC(C)(C)O","CCC=C(C)C","C=CCC(C)C","CCCC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A small base gives the Zaitsev (more-substituted) alkene 2-methyl-2-pentene over the less-substituted 2-methyl-1-pentene. Choice B is the Hofmann product, C is a wrong regiochemistry (4-methyl-1-pentene), D is the substitution alcohol, and E is the reduced skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Small base -> Zaitsev trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-pentene. optA=CCC=C(C)C keyed idx0 correct. Single-concept regiochemistry. All distractors plausible-but-wrong variants. Keep."},{"q":"The compound shown is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heating. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCC","CC=C(C)C","C=C(C)CC","CCC(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O"],"correct":1,"why":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (tertiary) with NaOEt (small strong base) in EtOH/heat undergoes E2 following Zaitsev. The trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene (CC=C(C)C) is the major product. opt1 is the disubstituted Hofmann alkene, opt2/opt3 are substitution alcohols, opt4 is wrong skeleton. Verified by RDKit: keyed SMILES matches the trisubstituted Zaitsev alkene. Exactly one correct, in scope, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct E2 Zaitsev, single concept, all SMILES valid, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Which set of reagents/conditions would best convert the tertiary alkyl bromide shown into the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene as the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol, heat","Sodium iodide (NaI) in acetone","Concentrated H2SO4, heat","Dilute aqueous NaOH at room temperature","Potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) in tert-butanol, heat"],"correct":4,"why":"Reagent-selection item. Bulky KOtBu gives the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene; NaOEt and H2SO4/E1 favor Zaitsev, aqueous NaOH favors substitution, NaI/acetone is SN2 Finkelstein. opt0 is uniquely correct. q_smiles (2-bromo-2-methylpentane) valid. In scope.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - reagent selection, one defensible answer, molecule drawn, valid SMILES."},{"q":"The substrate shown is heated in ethanol with no added base (solvolysis conditions). Which statement best classifies the elimination that occurs?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["E2: a concerted, anti-periplanar removal of H and Br in one step","SN2: backside attack inverts the stereocenter","E2: rate depends on the concentration of ethanol as a base","E1: a primary carbocation forms and the Hofmann alkene dominates","E1: the rate-determining step forms a tertiary carbocation, and the major product follows Zaitsev's rule"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide, weak/no base, protic solvent, heat = E1. RDS is ionization to a tertiary carbocation, then Zaitsev elimination. opt1 (E2 concerted) wrong conditions, opt2 (primary cation) impossible here, opt3 (E2 ethanol-dependent kinetics) wrong, opt4 (SN2) impossible at 3 degree center. opt0 uniquely correct. In scope - identifies mechanism class, no full mechanism drawing.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - classification of E1 conditions, one correct answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Both reactions use a strong base (NaOEt) under E2 conditions. Which alkyl halide undergoes E2 elimination FASTER?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A 2-bromobutane (secondary, drawn) reacts faster than a 1-bromobutane (primary) under E2","Both react at exactly the same rate because E2 is base-limited","Neither undergoes E2; both give only substitution","A fluorobutane reacts faster because F is the best leaving group","1-Bromobutane (primary) reacts faster because there is less steric hindrance"],"correct":0,"why":"Standard textbook E2 substrate-rate ordering 3>2>1: more substituted substrate gives more-stabilized alkene-like TS, so secondary 2-bromobutane eliminates faster than primary 1-bromobutane. F is the poorest halide leaving group (opt3 wrong). opt0 correct. q_smiles drawn = 2-bromobutane, valid. In scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - standard E2 rate trend, one defensible answer, molecule drawn, valid SMILES."},{"q":"The cyclic tertiary bromide shown is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol and heated. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(C)CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","C1=CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane + NaOEt = E2 Zaitsev -> trisubstituted endocyclic 1-methylcyclohexene (CC1=CCCCC1). opt1 is the disubstituted exocyclic methylenecyclohexane (Hofmann), opt2 is the substitution alcohol, opt3 is wrong ring size (5-membered), opt4 is cyclohexene missing the methyl. RDKit confirms keyed SMILES is the trisubstituted Zaitsev alkene. One correct, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct cyclic E2 Zaitsev, distractors differ by regio/skeleton, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The tertiary bromide shown is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (a bulky base) in tert-butanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)(C)O","CC/C=C/C","C=C(C)CCC","CCC=C(C)C","C=C(C)CC"],"correct":2,"why":"2-Bromo-2-methylpentane + bulky KOtBu = Hofmann E2 -> less-substituted 2-methyl-1-pentene (C=C(C)CCC). opt1 is the Zaitsev 2-methyl-2-pentene, opt2 is the substitution alcohol, opt3 (2-pentene) and opt4 (2-methyl-1-butene, C5) are wrong skeletons. RDKit confirms keyed SMILES = 2-methyl-1-pentene (C6H12). One correct, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct Hofmann via bulky base, distractors plausible, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"For a substituted halocyclohexane to undergo E2 elimination, the relationship between the C-H bond and the C-leaving-group bond must be anti-periplanar, which on a chair requires both groups to be in which orientation?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both the leaving group and the beta-hydrogen must be trans-diaxial (both axial, anti-periplanar)","One axial and one equatorial (gauche) so they are 60 degrees apart","Both must be eclipsed in a fully planar ring","Both must be equatorial so they point away from the ring","Cis-diequatorial, which is the lowest-energy conformer"],"correct":0,"why":"Concerted E2 requires anti-periplanar (180 deg) H and leaving group; on a chair this is the trans-diaxial arrangement (both axial). Equatorial bonds are gauche and cannot reach 180 deg. opt0 uniquely correct. q_smiles (1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane) valid and illustrates the substrate. Single concept (chair stereoelectronics), in scope - no mechanism drawn.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - standard E2 chair anti-periplanar requirement, one correct answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is heated in ethanol (no added base). Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["C=CCCC","C=CC(C)C","C=C(C)CC","CC=CCC","CC=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Verified: tertiary halide in EtOH+heat is E1, Zaitsev. Loss of a beta-H from the ethyl carbon gives trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene (opt0), beating the disubstituted Hofmann alkene 2-methyl-1-butene (opt1). Keyed index correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Chemistry, scope, and SMILES all correct; opt0 is the right Zaitsev product.","group":"misc"},{"q":"2-Bromobutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (a bulky base). Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)Br","opts":["CC=CC","C/C=C\\C","CCC(C)O","CCC=C","C/C=C/C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Verified: bulky t-BuOK drives E2 to the Hofmann (least-substituted) 1-butene (opt0) by abstracting the accessible terminal beta-H, over the Zaitsev 2-butene isomers. Keyed index correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-concept Hofmann-via-bulky-base; all SMILES valid; one defensible answer.","group":"misc"},{"q":"trans-1-Chloro-2-methylcyclohexane (shown) reacts with sodium ethoxide by E2. Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Cl","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1C=CCCC1","CC1CC=CCC1","CC1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Verified: in the trans isomer, when Cl is axial the methyl is also axial, so C2 has no anti-periplanar (axial) H; only C6 has one, giving the less-substituted 3-methylcyclohexene (opt0). Anti-periplanar geometry overrides Zaitsev. Keyed index correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic anti-periplanar-override on a ring; in DAT scope at diff 2; SMILES valid.","group":"misc"},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane reacts with sodium ethoxide (a small, strong base) by E2. Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC=C(C)C","C=C(C)CC","CC(C)C(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Verified: small strong base, E2 Zaitsev. Eliminating toward C3 gives trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene (opt0) over monosubstituted 3-methyl-1-butene (opt1). Keyed index correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-concept Zaitsev E2; valid SMILES; one defensible answer.","group":"misc"},{"q":"2-Bromopropane reacts with sodium ethoxide to give propene by an E2 mechanism. In the single concerted transition state, which set of bonding changes occurs?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["The base removes a beta-H while the C-Br bond breaks and the pi bond forms, all in one step","The base adds to the C-Br carbon and bromide leaves from the beta carbon","Bromide leaves to give a carbanion, which then loses a proton","A carbocation forms first, then the base removes a beta-H in a separate second step","The base attacks the carbon bearing Br, displacing bromide in a backside substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Verified: E2 is concerted/bimolecular; opt0 correctly states base abstraction, C-Br cleavage, and pi formation occur simultaneously. opt1=E1, opt2=SN2, opt3=E1cb, opt4 is nonsense. Keyed index correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Mechanism-recognition (one TS, not a full hand-drawn mechanism); in scope; molecule drawn.","group":"misc"},{"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane is solvolyzed in warm ethanol. Which alkene is the MAJOR elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts":["CC1CCCC=C1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Verified: tertiary halide, warm EtOH, E1 Zaitsev. Endocyclic trisubstituted 1-methylcyclohexene (opt0) beats the exocyclic disubstituted methylenecyclohexane (opt1, Hofmann). Keyed index correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-concept endo-vs-exo Zaitsev; valid SMILES; one defensible answer.","group":"misc"},{"q":"Menthyl chloride (shown) undergoes E2 with sodium ethoxide. The leaving Cl is equatorial in the stable chair, so the ring must flip to an all-axial chair before elimination. Which alkene is the MAJOR (essentially only) product?","q_smiles":"CC1CC[C@@H](C(C)C)[C@@H](Cl)C1","opts":["CC1CC=C(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","C=C1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1C=CC(C(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Verified: classic menthyl chloride E2. With Cl axial only the C6 H is anti-periplanar (the isopropyl-bearing C2 H is equatorial), so the less-substituted 2-menthene (opt0, double bond between unsubstituted ring carbons) is the sole product, not the Zaitsev menthene at the iPr carbon (opt1). Keyed index correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct answer (famous textbook case). Borderline scope at diff 3 (chair analysis of a trisubstituted ring) and substrate specifies only 2 of 3 stereocenters, but the relevant Cl/iPr relationship is shown and the keyed structure is right.","group":"misc"},{"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CBr","C=C(C)C","CCC(C)Br","OC(C)(C)C","CCOC(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"Tertiary halide + strong base (ethoxide) + heat favors E2; SN2 blocked by sterics. Product is isobutylene (2-methylpropene). All methyls equivalent so only one alkene possible; unambiguous. Ether/alcohol are substitution products; bromides are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed product C=C(C)C is the correct E2 alkene; all SMILES valid; single defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heating. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)OCC","CCC=C","CCCCO","C/C=C\\C","C/C=C/C"],"correct":4,"why":"Secondary halide + strong small base + heat gives E2 (Zaitsev) favoring the more stable trans alkene: trans-2-butene. cis-2-butene is the minor geometric isomer, 1-butene the Hofmann minor, ether/alcohol are substitution products.","difficulty":2,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correctly keys trans-2-butene; regio+stereo layering appropriate at difficulty 2; cis and 1-butene distractors well chosen."},{"q":"1-Bromobutane (drawn), a primary alkyl halide, is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)OCC","CCCCO","CCCCOCC","CCCCOCCC","CCC=C"],"correct":2,"why":"Unhindered primary halide + ethoxide: SN2 substitution predominates (Williamson ether synthesis) over the minor E2. Butyl ethyl ether is the major product. 1-Butene is the minor E2 product; other ethers have wrong skeleton/regiochemistry; alcohol needs a different nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. SN2-wins-for-primary is standard sophomore teaching; butyl ethyl ether correctly keyed; single defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Bromopropane (drawn) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCN=C","CCCOC#N","CC=C","CCC#N","CCCC#N"],"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide is an excellent nucleophile and weak base; primary substrate in polar aprotic DMSO gives clean SN2. Carbon attacks to give butanenitrile (one added carbon). Propene is minor E2; propanenitrile is wrong skeleton; O-cyanate and isonitrile reflect wrong connectivity.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Butanenitrile correctly keyed; isonitrile/cyanate connectivity distractors valid and instructive; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)=C"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (tert-butoxide) gives E2 with Hofmann selectivity (less-substituted, more accessible beta-H), yielding 2-methyl-1-butene. The Zaitsev alkene 2-methyl-2-butene is disfavored by the bulky base; bulky ether/tertiary alcohol are substitution products; last is wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified Hofmann product 2-methyl-1-butene (CCC(C)=C) correctly keyed; Zaitsev distractor present; difficulty 3 appropriate."},{"q":"Bromocyclohexane (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"BrC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CCOC1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary halide + strong base + heat gives E2 to cyclohexene. Cyclohexanol and ethoxycyclohexane are minor substitution products; starting bromide is not a product; cyclohexane would require reduction not provided.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Cyclohexene correctly keyed; all SMILES valid; single defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"(1-Bromoethyl)benzene (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(C=C)cc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(C)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C=C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Secondary benzylic halide + strong base + heat undergoes E2; only beta-H is on the methyl, and elimination gives styrene, strongly favored by conjugation with the ring. Ether/alcohol are substitution products; other alkenes have wrong skeleton or extra methyl.","difficulty":2,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Styrene (conjugation-favored E2) correctly keyed; distractors well formed; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC(C)C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Secondary halide + strong small base + heat gives E2 (Zaitsev): elimination toward the more-substituted C3 gives the trisubstituted alkene 2-methyl-2-butene. 3-Methyl-1-butene is the Hofmann minor; ether/alcohol are substitution products; last is a reduced wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: involves Zaitsev regiochemistry choice so arguably difficulty 2, but chemistry/answer are correct and unambiguous. Keyed 2-methyl-2-butene correct."},{"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (drawn) is dissolved in pure ethanol and warmed gently with no added base or nucleophile. Which pair of mechanisms operates, and which is favored?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts":["Only SN2 occurs because the substrate is tertiary","SN1 and E1 compete; both are equal in all cases regardless of temperature","Only E2 occurs because ethanol is a strong base","SN2 and E2 compete; E2 dominates","SN1 and E1 compete; SN1 (substitution) dominates because solvolysis with a weak nucleophile favors substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide in polar protic ethanol, weak nucleophile, no strong base = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete, SN1 dominates under gentle heat. SMILES CC(C)(C)Br is valid tert-butyl bromide. Distractors (forced bimolecular, ethanol as strong base) are plausible-but-wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; valid SMILES; single-concept mechanism classification, in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromopropane (drawn) is treated with a high concentration of sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a strong, small base. Which reaction is the major pathway?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["SN2 giving an ether with retention of configuration","No reaction occurs","E2 elimination","SN1 substitution","E1 elimination"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Secondary substrate + strong base (ethoxide) at high concentration = E2 major (standard textbook/Bootcamp answer: ethoxide is basic enough to favor elimination over SN2 on a secondary center). Distractor 'SN2 with retention' is chemically wrong (SN2 = inversion). SMILES valid for 2-bromopropane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 is the keyed textbook answer (SN2 is competitive in reality but E2 is the expected DAT-depth answer for ethoxide on secondary)."},{"q":"1-Chlorobutane (drawn), a primary alkyl halide, is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which outcome is expected?","q_smiles":"CCCCCl","opts":["SN2 substitution to give the nitrile (pentanenitrile)","No reaction; primary halides are unreactive","SN1 substitution via a primary carbocation","E1 elimination via a primary carbocation","E2 elimination to give 1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Primary halide + strong nucleophile/weak base (cyanide) + polar aprotic DMSO = clean SN2 to pentanenitrile. Primary carbocation routes (SN1/E1) are impossible. SMILES CCCCCl valid for 1-chlorobutane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; valid SMILES; single-concept, in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated strongly with concentrated potassium hydroxide in ethanol. The major organic product is which alkene?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["3-methyl-1-butene","2-methyl-1-butene (Hofmann, less substituted alkene)","2-methyl-2-butene (Zaitsev, more substituted alkene)","2-methyl-1-butanol","2-methyl-2-butanol (substitution)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Tertiary halide + strong SMALL base (KOH) + strong heat = E2 giving Zaitsev (more substituted) alkene 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted). KOH is unhindered so Zaitsev, not Hofmann. SMILES CCC(C)(C)Br valid for 2-bromo-2-methylbutane; products verified.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; two-concept (reaction type + Zaitsev regio), in scope."},{"q":"Compare two substrates under identical SN1/E1 solvolysis conditions (warm aqueous ethanol). Substrate A is tert-butyl chloride (drawn). Substrate B is neopentyl bromide, (CH3)3C-CH2-Br. Which reacts substantially faster, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Cl","opts":["They react at equal rates because both have the same number of carbons","Neither reacts; both are too hindered for any mechanism","Substrate B, because the bromide leaving group always overrides carbocation stability under SN1","Substrate B, because neopentyl can ionize to a stable primary carbocation","Substrate A (tert-butyl chloride), because it ionizes to a stable tertiary carbocation while neopentyl is primary"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SN1 rate is governed by carbocation stability. tert-Butyl chloride gives a stable tertiary cation; neopentyl bromide (primary) is essentially inert under SN1 despite the better leaving group. Correct=0. SMILES CC(C)(C)Cl valid for tert-butyl chloride; neopentyl named in text (acceptable, comparison reference).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; carbocation-stability concept, in scope. Second substrate is text-only but the drawn substrate is the focus."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide. To maximize the SUBSTITUTION (ether) product over elimination on this secondary substrate, which single change in conditions is most effective?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)Br","opts":["Switch ethoxide for a bulkier, stronger base like tert-butoxide","Add more of the same strong base and reflux","Lower the temperature (eliminate the heat that favors E2)","Increase the temperature strongly","Increase the concentration of ethoxide"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Heat preferentially accelerates E2 (higher Ea, entropy gain), so lowering temperature shifts toward substitution. Bulkier base (tert-butoxide) and more strong base both push toward E2. Correct=0. SMILES CCC(C)Br valid for 2-bromobutane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; single-concept temperature effect, in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (drawn) is treated with the bulky strong base potassium tert-butoxide. Which alkene is the major elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)Br","opts":["2-methyl-2-butene (Zaitsev, more substituted alkene)","2-methyl-2-butanol (substitution)","3-methyl-1-butene (Hofmann, less substituted alkene)","3-methyl-2-butanol (substitution)","2-methyl-1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Bulky base (KOtBu) abstracts the less hindered terminal proton, giving the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene 3-methyl-1-butene. The Zaitsev option (2-methyl-2-butene, from the more hindered C3-H) is the small-base product. Correct=0. SMILES CC(C)C(C)Br valid for 2-bromo-3-methylbutane.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; two-concept (E2 + Hofmann regio via bulky base), in scope."},{"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (drawn) is placed in warm methanol containing no added strong base. Classify the competing reactions and identify which substitution mechanism is operative.","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts":["Only E2 occurs, giving exclusively the Hofmann alkene","SN2 and E2 compete (bimolecular), because methanol is a strong base","Only SN2 occurs, with inversion of configuration at the tertiary carbon","SN1 and E2 compete, mixing unimolecular substitution with bimolecular elimination","SN1 and E1 compete (unimolecular), because the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide in warm methanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile, no strong base) = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete; substitution via SN1. SN2 blocked (tertiary), E2 needs strong base (absent). Correct=0. SMILES CC1(Br)CCCCC1 valid for 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; mechanism classification, in scope (no mechanism drawing required)."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) in tert-butanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR organic product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (KOtBu) + heat = E2 with Hofmann selectivity. Eliminating toward a terminal CH3 gives the less-substituted 2-methyl-1-butene (opt0, CCC(=C)C). opt1 is the Zaitsev 2-methyl-2-butene (small-base product); opt2/opt4 are negligible substitution (bulky base = poor Nu); opt3 has the wrong skeleton. Keyed index 0 verified correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; Hofmann reasoning and keyed answer (opt0 = 2-methyl-1-butene) correct. In scope, visual, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"cis-1-Bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane reacts with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](Br)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC=CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CCOC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"tBu locks equatorial; in the cis isomer Br is forced axial, the geometry E2 needs (anti-periplanar axial beta-H on C2/C6). Strong base on a secondary axial halide gives fast E2 to 4-tert-butylcyclohexene (opt0). Alcohol/ether are minor substitution; exocyclic methylene cannot form; alkane is reduction. Stem SMILES confirmed by 3D to be cis (tBu and Br same face), matching the text. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"Stem SMILES verified cis (3D face check). Conformational E2 logic and keyed answer correct. Only one alkene is possible (ring symmetric) so the answer is clean; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Considering both regiochemistry and alkene geometry, which is the SINGLE major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C/C","C/C=C\\C","CCOC(C)CC","CCC(C)O","C=CCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary halide + small strong base (ethoxide) + heat = E2, Zaitsev favors 2-butene over 1-butene (opt2 Hofmann minor). Between geometric isomers, trans (E)-2-butene (opt0) is lower-energy and major over cis (Z) (opt1). Ether/alcohol are minor substitution. E/Z SMILES are valid and distinct; keyed index 0 (E) correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"Regio + E/Z double layer, in scope. cis/trans SMILES valid and distinguishable. Keyed answer (trans-2-butene) correct. Keep."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane) is treated with sodium hydrosulfide (NaSH), a strong nucleophile that is a weak base, in a polar aprotic solvent. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)CS","CC(C)(C)C","CC(C)(C)CO","CC(C)(C)S","CC(C)=C"],"correct":0,"why":"NaSH is a strong nucleophile but weak base, favoring substitution. Primary substrate = SN2 (not SN1), giving neopentyl thiol (opt0). The C-Br carbon has no beta-H (neighbor is quaternary), so E2 is impossible, excluding alkenes. opt2 needs a rearranged tertiary skeleton SN2 cannot produce. Although neopentyl SN2 is sterically slow, there is no competing pathway, so the thiol is unambiguously major. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid. No beta-H rules out elimination; SN2 thiol is the single defensible answer despite neopentyl steric slowdown. In scope. Keep."},{"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) and heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (KOtBu) = E2 with Hofmann selectivity; the bulky base abstracts the accessible methyl H, giving the less-substituted exocyclic methylenecyclohexane (opt0). opt1 is the more-substituted Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene (small-base product). Tertiary alcohol is the substitution trap. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; Hofmann vs Zaitsev single-concept, in scope. Keyed answer (methylenecyclohexane) correct. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this E2 reaction?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)C=C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"E2 is concerted with no carbocation, so no rearrangement. The only beta-H is on the CH3 of the C-Br carbon (the other neighbor is the quaternary tert-butyl carbon, no H), giving 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (opt0). opt1 (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene) is the rearranged Zaitsev trap requiring a hydride/methyl shift, which E2 cannot do. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; no-rearrangement E2 concept, in scope (no mechanism drawing). Keyed answer (3,3-dimethyl-1-butene) correct. Keep."},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt). Remembering that E2 on a cyclohexane requires the leaving group and the beta-hydrogen to be trans-diaxial (anti-periplanar), which is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1C=CCCC1","CCOC1CCCCC1C","CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"E2 needs Br axial with an anti-periplanar axial beta-H. In the trans-1,2 isomer, when Br is axial the methyl is also axial, so C2 has no axial H. Elimination must go toward C6, giving 3-methylcyclohexene (opt0). The Zaitsev product 1-methylcyclohexene (opt1) cannot form because the required C2-H is equatorial. Stem SMILES confirmed by 3D to be trans (Me and Br opposite faces), matching the text. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"Stem SMILES verified trans (3D face check). Anti-periplanar regiochemistry and keyed answer (3-methylcyclohexene) correct. In scope, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(C)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC(O)C(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"Secondary halide + small strong base (ethoxide) + heat = E2 with Zaitsev selectivity. Eliminating toward the more-substituted C3 (the CH bearing two methyls) gives trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene (opt0). opt1 is the less-substituted Hofmann 3-methyl-1-butene (minor). Ether/alcohol are minor substitution. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; Zaitsev regiochemistry single-concept, in scope. Keyed answer (2-methyl-2-butene) correct. Keep."},{"q":"The alcohol shown is treated with concentrated H2SO4 and warmed, generating a secondary carbocation that quickly converts to a more stable tertiary carbocation before any product forms. What type of rearrangement accomplishes this conversion?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)O","opts":["A 1,2-hydride shift","A ring expansion","A 1,2-methyl shift","A retro-aldol cleavage","A 1,2-chloride shift"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"3-Methyl-2-butanol ionizes to the secondary (CH3)2CH-CH(+)-CH3 cation; the adjacent tertiary C-H migrates (1,2-hydride shift) to give the tertiary (CH3)2C(+)-CH2CH3 cation. No carbon skeleton group moves, so it is a hydride shift, not a methyl shift.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; secondary->tertiary via 1,2-hydride shift is correct; index 0 right; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol (pinacolyl alcohol, shown) is a classic substrate that gives a rearranged product under acidic ionizing conditions. Which set of conditions would generate a carbocation that undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift on its way to product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["NaBr in acetone","Concentrated HBr (forms a carbocation; SN1-type)","PBr3 in ether","NaH then CH3I","SOCl2 with pyridine"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A 1,2-shift requires a free carbocation, formed only under SN1/E1 ionizing conditions. Conc. HBr protonates OH and ionizes to the secondary cation, which gains a methyl from the neighboring tert-butyl carbon (1,2-methyl shift) to a tertiary cation. PBr3, SOCl2/pyridine, and NaBr/acetone are SN2 (no free cation); NaH/CH3I is Williamson ether synthesis.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; only conc. HBr ionizes to a cation; index 0 right; reagent-selection text opts, in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (shown) reacts extremely slowly by SN2 because its primary carbon is shielded by the bulky tert-butyl group. Under SN1-favoring solvolysis conditions, it instead ionizes and rearranges. What migration converts the initially formed primary cation into a more stable one?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts":["No rearrangement; the primary cation is stable","A 1,2-methyl shift to give a tertiary carbocation","A ring expansion to a cyclobutyl cation","A 1,2-methyl shift to give a secondary carbocation","A 1,2-hydride shift to give a secondary carbocation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Ionization gives a primary cation (CH3)3C-CH2(+); a methyl from the adjacent quaternary carbon migrates (1,2-methyl shift) to give the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation. A hydride shift is impossible (neighbor is quaternary, no H); there is no ring to expand.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; methyl shift gives tertiary (not secondary), distractors correctly wrong; index 0 right; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (shown) is heated in methanol (CH3OH), an SN1/E1 solvolysis. Which structure is the major substitution (ether) product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts":["C=C(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC","COC(C)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives the secondary 3-methyl-2-butyl cation; a 1,2-hydride shift gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, trapped by methanol to give 2-methoxy-2-methylbutane (CCC(C)(C)OC). The unrearranged ether (COC(C)C(C)C) forms without rearrangement; the three alkenes are E1 products, not the substitution product asked for.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All 5 structure SMILES valid; rearranged tertiary ether is correct major SN1 product; index 0 right; regio+rearrangement two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene (shown) is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Markovnikov addition gives a secondary carbocation that rearranges before bromide adds. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C=C","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)C(Br)C","CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation puts + on C2 giving secondary (CH3)2CH-CH(+)-CH3; a 1,2-hydride shift gives the tertiary (CH3)2C(+)-CH2CH3, trapped by bromide to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (CCC(C)(C)Br). The unrearranged Markovnikov product is 2-bromo-3-methylbutane; the 1-bromo isomers are anti-Markovnikov/wrong regiochemistry.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Minor: layers Markovnikov + hydride-shift so closer to difficulty 2 than 1, but single reactive center; SMILES valid; index 0 right. Keep."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (shown) reacts with HBr by Markovnikov addition. The first-formed secondary cation rearranges, then bromide adds. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C=C","opts":["CC(C)C(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CCBr","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives secondary (CH3)3C-CH(+)-CH3; the neighbor is quaternary so a 1,2-methyl shift (not hydride) gives the tertiary cation, trapped by bromide to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (CC(C)C(C)(C)Br). The unrearranged product (CC(Br)C(C)(C)C) keeps the secondary site; others are anti-Markovnikov, an alkane, or an alkene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All structure SMILES valid; answer and unrearranged distractor confirmed distinct; methyl-shift product correct; index 0 right; two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"Four alcohols are considered for an SN1 reaction in which the very first carbocation formed would immediately rearrange to a markedly more stable one. One example, neopentyl alcohol, is shown. Among the following, which ionizes to a carbocation that is MOST driven to undergo a 1,2-shift (i.e., gains the most stability by rearranging)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CO","opts":["tert-Butyl alcohol (already tertiary)","Neopentyl alcohol (primary cation rearranges to tertiary)","Cyclohexanol (secondary, symmetric, no driving force)","2-Butanol (secondary cation, no adjacent carbon that improves it)","Ethanol (primary, but no branch to migrate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The driving force is the stability gain on rearranging. Neopentyl alcohol's primary cation sits next to a quaternary carbon, so a methyl shift jumps primary->tertiary (largest gain). tert-Butyl is already tertiary; 2-butanol and cyclohexanol give secondary cations with no path to tertiary; ethanol's primary cation has no branched neighbor to migrate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; neopentyl (primary->tertiary) is the unique largest driving force; index 0 right; ranking/classification text opts, in scope."},{"q":"(Bromomethyl)cyclopentane (shown) is solvolyzed in water, giving a single rearranged alcohol as the major product. The primary cation undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"BrCC1CCCC1","opts":["C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives a primary exocyclic cation; a ring C-C bond migrates (ring expansion) to give a secondary cyclohexyl cation, trapped by water to give cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1). Cyclopentylmethanol (OCC1CCCC1) is the unrearranged product; methylenecyclopentane, 1-methylcyclopentene, and cyclohexene are elimination products, not the alcohol.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All structure SMILES valid; ring expansion to cyclohexanol correct; index 0 right; single migration, hard but in DAT scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol is treated with concentrated HCl. The reaction proceeds through a carbocation. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CCCC(C)(C)Cl","CC(C)(C)CCCl","CC(C)(Cl)C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CCl"],"correct":3,"why":"Loss of water gives a 2 cation at C2 adjacent to the quaternary C3. A 1,2-methyl shift produces a more stable 3 cation, trapped by chloride to give 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane (opt0, canonicalizes to CC(C)C(C)(C)Cl). Opt1 is the unrearranged 2 chloride; the rest are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid; keyed index 0 matches 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutage. Correct."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene reacts with HCl by electrophilic addition. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CCCl","CCC(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CC(C)C(C)Cl","CCCC(C)Cl"],"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a 2 cation at C2 next to the 3 C3. A 1,2-hydride shift gives a 3 cation; chloride traps to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (opt0). Opt1 is the unrearranged Markovnikov 2 chloride; others are anti-Markovnikov/wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: keyed index 0 correct; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. The reaction goes through a carbocation that rearranges. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(C)CCBr","CC(C)C(C)(C)Br","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C","CC(C)C(C)CBr"],"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a 2 cation at C2 adjacent to the quaternary tert-butyl carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift gives a 3 cation; bromide traps to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (opt0). Opt1 is the unrearranged 3-bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane; the rest are wrong regio/skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: keyed index 0 correct; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"In the HCl addition to 3-methyl-1-butene shown, a 1,2-hydride shift converts a secondary cation into a tertiary cation. Which single curved arrow correctly represents this hydride shift?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An arrow from the empty orbital on C2 to the C3-H bond","An arrow from the C3-H bond to the chloride ion","An arrow from the lone pair on chloride to the C3-H bond","An arrow from a C-C bond on C3 to C2 (a methyl/alkyl shift instead)","An arrow from the C3-H bond to the empty orbital on the adjacent C2 cation"],"correct":4,"why":"A 1,2-hydride shift is drawn as the migrating C-H bonding electrons moving to the adjacent electron-deficient carbon: arrow from C3-H bond to the empty orbital on C2. Arrows pointing away from the cation, to chloride, or representing a C-C (alkyl) shift are wrong.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"In scope (identifies ONE arrow, explicitly allowed). q_smiles valid. Correct."},{"q":"(Cyclobutylmethyl)methanol is treated with HCl. Ionization gives a primary cyclobutylmethyl cation that undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCC1CCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["ClC1CCCC1","ClC1CCC1","C=C1CCC1","ClCC1CCCC1","ClCC1CCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"The 1 cation undergoes a 1,2-ring-bond (alkyl) shift that expands the four-membered ring to a five-membered ring, giving a 2 cyclopentyl cation; chloride trapping gives chlorocyclopentane (opt0). Opt1 is the unrearranged 1 chloride; the others have wrong ring size or are an alkene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct. Minor: stem name '(cyclobutylmethyl)methanol' is awkward (SMILES is cyclobutylmethanol) and distractor opt4 (C6H11Cl) is not mass-balanced with the C5 substrate, but it stays clearly wrong and single-answer holds."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4 (E1 dehydration). The carbocation rearranges before elimination. What is the major alkene product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["2,3-Dimethyl-1-butene","2-Methyl-2-butene","2-Methyl-1-pentene","3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (monosubstituted, no rearrangement)","2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene (tetrasubstituted)"],"correct":4,"why":"Ionization gives a 2 cation that undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift to a 3 cation. Loss of a proton gives the tetrasubstituted Zaitsev/rearranged alkene 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene. The monosubstituted alkene would be unrearranged and is disfavored.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Text opts with molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid). Keyed index 0 correct."},{"q":"Optically active (R)-2-bromo-3-methylbutane is solvolyzed in water (SN1). The intermediate cation rearranges. Which statement best describes the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["3-Methyl-2-butanol as a single enantiomer with inversion (R to S)","2-Methyl-2-butanol obtained as a single optically pure enantiomer","2-Methyl-2-butanol, formed after a 1,2-hydride shift to a tertiary cation; the new center is not a stereocenter, so the product is achiral","3-Methyl-2-butanol with retention of configuration at the original carbon","3-Methyl-1-butanol, a rearranged primary alcohol"],"correct":2,"why":"The 2 cation at C2 undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 to a 3 cation; water adds to give 2-methyl-2-butanol. RDKit confirms that carbon has zero stereocenters, so the product is achiral. Retention/inversion/optically-pure claims are wrong because the stereocenter is destroyed.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified product is achiral (0 chiral centers). Recognition-depth stereo reasoning, no R/S math; in scope. q_smiles valid. Correct."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-pentene undergoes acid-catalyzed hydration (dilute H2SO4, H2O). The carbocation rearranges. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)C(C)O","CCC(C)(C)CO","CCCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)(O)CC","CCC(C)CCO"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a 2 cation at C2 next to the 3 C3. A 1,2-hydride shift gives a 3 cation; water adds to give 3-methyl-3-pentanol (opt0). Opt1 is the unrearranged Markovnikov 2 alcohol; others are anti-Markovnikov/wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: keyed index 0 correct; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"2-Methylpropene (isobutylene) is treated with HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C","opts":["CC(C)(C)O","CC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(Br)CBr","CC(C)CBr","CCC(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov: H+ adds to terminal CH2, giving the tertiary cation at the central carbon; Br- bonds there to give tert-butyl bromide (opt 0). Isobutyl bromide is anti-Markovnikov, the alcohol is the wrong reagent, the dibromide needs Br2, and CCC(C)Br is a wrong skeleton. Verified by RDKit: keyed SMILES = tert-butyl bromide, the genuine major product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"None. All 6 SMILES valid; correct=0 confirmed (tert-butyl bromide); single concept (Markovnikov regiochem); exactly one defensible answer; visual structure options."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with HBr (no peroxides). The only major product isolated is tert-butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane). Which statement best explains this outcome?","q_smiles":"CC(=C)C","opts":["H+ adds to the more-substituted carbon to give a primary carbocation that is trapped by bromide","The reaction proceeds through a free-radical chain, so Br ends up on the less-substituted carbon","The product forms by a concerted syn addition with no carbocation intermediate","Br adds to the more-substituted carbon because Br is the larger, more nucleophilic atom","H+ adds to the less-substituted carbon, placing the positive charge on the more-substituted carbon to give the more stable (tertiary) carbocation (Markovnikov addition)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Ionic HX addition is Markovnikov: the proton adds to the carbon with more hydrogens so the carbocation forms on the more-substituted carbon (here tertiary). Bromide then traps that cation, putting Br on the more-substituted carbon (tert-butyl bromide). Radical pathways need peroxides, which are absent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid isobutylene SMILES; Markovnikov mechanism correct; option 0 is the single defensible answer; distractors clean. No change."},{"q":"Which set of reagents/conditions converts the alkene shown into 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide) as the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["HBr with ROOR (peroxides)","HBr","HBr followed by NaOH","Br2 with H2O","Br2 in CCl4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov HBr addition to propene puts Br on the more-substituted (internal) carbon, giving 2-bromopropane. HBr/peroxides would give the anti-Markovnikov 1-bromopropane; Br2/CCl4 gives a vicinal dibromide; Br2/H2O gives a bromohydrin; HBr then NaOH would eliminate or substitute the product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid propene SMILES; reagent-selection item with one correct (HBr); distractors all give different products. No change."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) reacts with HCl to give predominantly 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane. This regiochemical outcome is an example of which type of addition?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Syn dihydroxylation","Markovnikov addition","Free-radical addition","Oxidative cleavage","Anti-Markovnikov addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation of the alkene generates the more stable tertiary carbocation at the methyl-bearing ring carbon; chloride traps it there, so Cl ends up on the more-substituted carbon. That is the definition of Markovnikov addition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 1-methylcyclohexene SMILES; tertiary cation gives Markovnikov product; classification item with one answer. No change."},{"q":"Addition of HBr to 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (shown) gives a substantial amount of 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane in addition to the simple Markovnikov product. The appearance of this 'extra' product is best explained by which feature of the mechanism?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["Loss of the tert-butyl group as a leaving group during addition","A concerted syn addition that scrambles the substituents","A 1,2-hydride/methyl shift converts the initial secondary carbocation into a more stable tertiary carbocation before bromide traps it","A radical chain that places Br on the terminal carbon","Anti addition of Br and H across the double bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation first gives a secondary carbocation adjacent to a quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift produces a more stable tertiary cation, which bromide traps to give the rearranged product 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (C6H13Br). Carbocation rearrangement is the diagnostic feature.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid SMILES; rearrangement product naming/formula verified (both products C6H13Br); 1,2-shift to tertiary cation is correct mechanistic feature. Recognition-level, no full mechanism. No change."},{"q":"Two alkenes are each treated with HBr under identical ionic (no-peroxide) conditions. The alkene drawn is 2-methyl-2-butene; the second alkene is 1-butene (CH2=CHCH2CH3). Which alkene reacts faster, and why?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["1-Butene, because a primary carbocation forms faster than a tertiary one","They react at the same rate because both add one equivalent of HBr","2-Methyl-2-butene, because Br can only add to a terminal carbon","2-Methyl-2-butene, because it forms a more stable (tertiary) carbocation in the rate-determining protonation step","1-Butene, because a less-substituted alkene is more nucleophilic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The rate-determining step in ionic HX addition is protonation to form the carbocation. The more-substituted alkene (2-methyl-2-butene) gives a tertiary cation, which is more stable and forms faster, so it reacts faster than 1-butene, which gives a less stable secondary/primary cation.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 2-methyl-2-butene SMILES; rate governed by cation stability in RDS; reasoning (rate vs nucleophilicity layered) is two-concept. Option 0 is the one defensible answer. No change."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is treated with HBr in the absence of peroxides. Which describes the major product and the reason for its regiochemistry?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["(1-bromoethyl)benzene, because a primary carbocation forms on the terminal carbon","(2-bromoethyl)benzene, because the benzene ring directs Br anti-Markovnikov","A vicinal dibromide, because HBr adds twice to the ring-conjugated alkene","(2-bromoethyl)benzene, because Br always adds to the terminal carbon of a vinyl group","(1-bromoethyl)benzene, because protonation gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic carbocation that bromide traps at the benzylic carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Protonation of styrene's terminal carbon gives a benzylic carbocation stabilized by resonance into the ring. Bromide adds there, so Br ends up on the benzylic carbon, giving (1-bromoethyl)benzene. This is Markovnikov-type control set by carbocation stability.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid styrene SMILES; benzylic cation regiochemistry correct; option 2 distractor names same product but wrong reason, option 0 has correct product AND reason, so still exactly one fully-correct choice. No change."},{"q":"The alkene shown (2-methyl-1-butene) is treated with HCl. Identify the regiochemical name of the addition and the carbon that bears the chlorine in the major product.","q_smiles":"CCC(=C)C","opts":["Free-radical addition; Cl on the internal carbon","Syn addition; Cl and H both on the terminal carbon","Markovnikov addition; Cl on the terminal CH2 carbon","Anti-Markovnikov addition; Cl on the terminal CH2 carbon","Markovnikov addition; Cl on the more-substituted (internal, tertiary) carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Protonation of the terminal =CH2 gives a tertiary carbocation at the internal carbon. Chloride traps there, so Cl sits on the more-substituted carbon. That is Markovnikov addition with Cl on the internal (tertiary) carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 2-methyl-1-butene SMILES (CCC(=C)C); tertiary cation gives Cl on internal carbon; option 0 combines correct name + correct position. No change."},{"q":"1-Hexene (shown) is treated with HBr in the presence of an organic peroxide (ROOR). Classify this reaction and predict where Br ends up relative to the simple HBr (no-peroxide) case.","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C","opts":["Radical (peroxide-initiated) anti-Markovnikov addition; Br adds to the terminal carbon, opposite the Markovnikov product","Anti addition of Br2 across the double bond to give a dibromide","Oxidative cleavage of the double bond to give a carboxylic acid","Ionic Markovnikov addition; Br adds to the internal carbon, same as without peroxide","Radical addition that still follows Markovnikov; Br adds to the internal carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Peroxides switch HBr addition to a radical chain. The bromine radical adds to form the more stable secondary carbon radical, so Br lands on the terminal carbon, giving 1-bromohexane: the anti-Markovnikov product, opposite the ionic outcome. This peroxide effect is unique to HBr.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 1-hexene SMILES; peroxide anti-Markovnikov effect correct; identifies radical regiochemistry without full mechanism, in DAT scope. No change."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1CCCC(Br)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov addition: protonating C2 gives a tertiary cation at the methyl-bearing C1; Br- bonds there, giving 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane. Distractors: secondary anti-Markovnikov bromide, vic-dibromide (Br2), ring-expanded bromocycloheptane, remote-Br isomer. Keyed structure verified correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"Predict the major product of the reaction of 2-methyl-2-butene with HBr.","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(Br)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr","CC(Br)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Protonating the CH carbon generates a tertiary cation at the disubstituted alkene carbon; Br- traps it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (keyed opt0, canonical CCC(C)(C)Br). Distractors are the secondary anti-Markovnikov bromide, two primary bromides, and a vic-dibromide. Structure correct; the 'why' uses slightly loose carbon numbering but the product is right.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Minor: 'why' says Br adds to 'C2' with inconsistent numbering, but the keyed structure 2-bromo-2-methylbutane is correct."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["ClCC(Cl)c1ccccc1","ClCc1ccccc1","CC(Cl)c1ccccc1","ClCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic cation; Cl- bonds at the benzylic carbon to give (1-chloroethyl)benzene. Distractors: anti-Markovnikov primary chloride, ring chlorination (EAS, wrong site), vic-dichloride, wrong skeleton. Keyed structure verified correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product? (Consider carbocation stability carefully.)","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["BrCC(C)C(C)C","BrCCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2 next to the quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation that Br- traps, yielding 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (keyed opt0, canonical CC(C)C(C)(C)Br). The classic trap (opt1) is the unrearranged secondary bromide. Rearrangement is one defensible concept; in scope as a single-center carbocation-stability item.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"What is the major product when propene reacts with HBr (no peroxides)?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["CC(Br)CBr","CC(Br)CO","CCBr","CCCBr","CC(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov: the more stable secondary cation forms at the internal carbon, so Br ends up on C2, giving 2-bromopropane. Distractors: anti-Markovnikov 1-bromopropane, vic-dibromide, a bromo-alcohol, and a two-carbon fragment. Keyed structure verified correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"(E)-2-Butene is treated with HBr. A new stereocenter forms at the carbon that gains Br. Which option best represents the stereochemical outcome of the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["CC[C@H](Br)C","CCC(Br)C","CCCCBr","CC(Br)(C)C","CC[C@@H](Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation gives a planar achiral 2-butyl cation; Br- attacks both faces equally, giving racemic 2-bromobutane, best shown by the structure drawn without defined stereochemistry. The single-enantiomer drawings are each wrong alone; the others are wrong regiochemistry/skeleton. Defensible single answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"Methylenecyclohexane (the exocyclic alkene shown) reacts with HI. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C1CCCCC1","opts":["ICC1CCCCC1","IC1CCCCC1","CCC1(I)CCCC1","CC1CCCCC1I","CC1(I)CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Protonating the exocyclic =CH2 puts the cation on the ring carbon (tertiary); I- bonds there to give 1-iodo-1-methylcyclohexane. Distractors: anti-Markovnikov (iodomethyl)cyclohexane, ring-CH2 iodide, a one-carbon-short ring, and a wrong ring size/substitution. Keyed structure verified correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"(E)-1-Phenyl-1-propene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CCC=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)C(Br)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Regiochemistry follows carbocation stability: protonation gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic cation, so Br bonds at the benzylic (phenyl-bearing) carbon to give (1-bromopropyl)benzene. Distractors: non-benzylic secondary bromide, ring bromination, vic-dibromide, primary bromide. Keyed structure verified correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3-CH=CH2  +  HBr,  (PhCO)2O2 (peroxide), heat","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)Br","CC(Br)C","CCCO","CCCBr","CCCCl"],"correct":3,"why":"Propene + HBr/peroxide is radical anti-Markovnikov addition: Br goes to the terminal (less substituted) carbon so the more stable secondary radical forms internally, giving 1-bromopropane (CCCBr, index 0). 2-Bromopropane is the Markovnikov/ionic product; the alcohol and chloride use wrong reagents; the vinyl bromide is an impossible product. Keyed answer verified correct via RDKit.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All 5 SMILES valid, key correct, single concept, visual, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n2-methylpropene (isobutylene)  +  HBr,  ROOR (peroxide)","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CBr","CC(C)CO","CC(C)(Br)C","CCC(C)Br"],"correct":0,"why":"Peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov radical addition: Br adds to the terminal CH2 so the stable tertiary radical forms internally, giving isobutyl bromide / 1-bromo-2-methylpropane (CC(C)CBr, index 0). tert-Butyl bromide is the Markovnikov product; the alcohol is the wrong reaction; sec-butyl bromide is the wrong skeleton. Key verified correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options provided (spec calls for 5-option MC); chemistry is correct, in scope, visual, with exactly one right answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\nstyrene (vinylbenzene)  +  HBr,  peroxide, heat","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Radical (peroxide) HBr addition is anti-Markovnikov: Br adds to the terminal CH2 and the resonance-stabilized benzylic radical forms on the internal carbon, giving (2-bromoethyl)benzene (BrCCc1ccccc1, index 0). (1-Bromoethyl)benzene is the Markovnikov/ionic product; the alcohol is the wrong reaction; ring bromination does not occur. Key verified correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options provided (spec calls for 5); chemistry, scope, key, and visual are all sound."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n1-pentene  +  HBr,  (PhCOO)2 (peroxide)","q_smiles":"CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCO","CCCCCBr","CCCC(Br)C","CCC(Br)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Peroxide drives anti-Markovnikov radical addition, so Br adds to the terminal carbon giving 1-bromopentane (CCCCCBr, index 0). 2-Bromopentane is the Markovnikov product; 3-bromopentane is the wrong skeleton; the alcohol is the wrong reaction. Key verified correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options provided (spec calls for 5); otherwise correct, in scope, visual, single defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n3-methyl-1-butene  +  HBr,  peroxide","q_smiles":"CC(C)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C(Br)C","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)CCBr","CC(C)CCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Radical anti-Markovnikov addition puts Br on the terminal CH2 with a secondary radical forming internally; because it is a radical (not a carbocation) there is NO rearrangement. Product is 1-bromo-3-methylbutane (CC(C)CCBr, index 0). CC(C)C(Br)C is the Markovnikov product; CCC(C)(C)Br is the rearranged tertiary skeleton (a good trap, but radicals do not rearrange); the alcohol is the wrong reaction. Key verified correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options (spec calls for 5); two-concept (regio + no-rearrangement) item is correct, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n1-methylcyclohexene  +  HBr,  ROOR (peroxide), heat","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1CCCC(Br)C1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1(Br)CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"For this trisubstituted internal alkene, peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry: Br bonds to the less substituted ring CH while the more stable tertiary radical forms at the methyl-bearing carbon. RDKit confirms the keyed CC1CCCCC1Br has Br and CH3 on adjacent carbons = 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane (index 0). CC1(Br)CCCCC1 is the Markovnikov 1-bromo-1-methyl product; CC1CCCC(Br)C1 has Br at a wrong ring position; the alcohol is the wrong reaction. Key verified correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options (spec calls for 5); regiochemistry, scope, and key are all correct and visual."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n4-methyl-1-pentene  +  HBr,  peroxide","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)CC(Br)C","CC(C)CCCBr","CC(C)CCCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov radical addition: Br adds to the terminal CH2, giving 1-bromo-4-methylpentane (CC(C)CCCBr, index 0); the methyl branch is a remote spectator. CC(C)CC(Br)C is the Markovnikov 2-bromide; CCCC(C)(C)Br is a wrong rearranged tertiary skeleton; the alcohol is the wrong reaction. Key verified correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options (spec calls for 5); single-concept item is correct, in scope, visual, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\nallylbenzene (3-phenyl-1-propene, PhCH2-CH=CH2)  +  HBr,  peroxide, heat","q_smiles":"C=CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccc(Br)cc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The double bond is the only reactive site; the benzylic CH2 is saturated and NOT part of the alkene, so its resonance cannot influence this addition. Peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov: Br adds to the terminal CH2 and a secondary radical forms on the middle carbon, giving (3-bromopropyl)benzene (BrCCCc1ccccc1, index 0). CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1 is the Markovnikov 2-bromide; CCC(Br)c1ccccc1 is a tempting benzylic bromide that is impossible from this alkene; ring bromination and the alcohol are wrong reactions. Key verified correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All 5 SMILES valid, key correct, single reactive center, well-designed benzylic trap, in scope and visual."},{"q":"A chemist treats 1-hexene (drawn) and isolates 1-bromohexane (CH3CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2Br) as the major product. Which set of reagents/conditions was used?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts":["HBr in water (H2O)","HBr with a Lewis acid catalyst","HBr with peroxides (ROOR), heat or light","HBr alone (no peroxide)","Br2 in CCl4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"1-Bromohexane places Br on the terminal (less substituted) carbon = anti-Markovnikov, which comes from the free-radical mechanism requiring HBr + peroxide initiator with heat/light. HBr alone gives Markovnikov (2-bromohexane).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. q_smiles C=CCCCC is valid 1-hexene; anti-Markovnikov product requires HBr/peroxides; key index 0 is right; distractors all plausibly wrong."},{"q":"Propene is drawn. Under which conditions does propene give 1-bromopropane (the anti-Markovnikov product) rather than 2-bromopropane?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["HBr in the presence of peroxides","HI with peroxides","HBr alone","HCl with peroxides","H2O with H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Only HBr undergoes the peroxide-initiated radical chain that reverses regiochemistry. The peroxide effect is specific to HBr; HCl and HI do not give useful anti-Markovnikov addition. So HBr + peroxides gives 1-bromopropane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. CC=C is valid propene; only HBr shows the peroxide effect; key 0 correct; classic high-yield DAT fact."},{"q":"Styrene (drawn) is reacted with HBr in the presence of peroxides. What is the correct name/type for this transformation and its regiochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Electrophilic addition; Markovnikov (Br on the benzylic carbon)","Electrophilic addition; anti-Markovnikov","Free-radical substitution; allylic bromination","Nucleophilic addition; Markovnikov","Free-radical addition; anti-Markovnikov (Br on the terminal CH2)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Peroxides make HBr add by a free-radical chain mechanism (anti-Markovnikov). For styrene, Br radical adds to terminal CH2 so the more stable benzylic radical forms; Br ends on the terminal carbon. This is addition, not substitution, and anti-Markovnikov.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. C=Cc1ccccc1 is valid styrene; mechanism = free-radical addition, anti-Markovnikov; key 0 correct; distractors are well-chosen wrong combinations."},{"q":"The reaction below is run with HBr and a peroxide initiator. Which species is the chain-carrying radical that adds to the alkene's pi bond in the first propagation step?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts":["Br (bromine radical)","RO (alkoxy radical, the initiator)","HBr radical cation","The carbocation","H (hydrogen radical)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The alkoxy radical from peroxide abstracts H from HBr to generate a bromine radical (Br). The Br radical adds to the alkene first, placing Br on the less substituted carbon and forming the more stable carbon radical. No carbocation forms.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. Valid 1-hexene; Br radical is the chain carrier in propagation step 1 (RO is initiation only); key 0 correct."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is drawn. It is treated with HBr and peroxides. On which carbon does the bromine end up, and why?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["On the methyl-bearing carbon, because that carbon is more substituted","No reaction occurs with an internal alkene","Br adds to both alkene carbons","On the ring CH carbon (less substituted), because the Br radical adds there to form the more stable tertiary carbon radical","On the methyl group carbon (exocyclic CH3)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"In radical addition the Br radical bonds to the carbon yielding the more stable radical. The methyl-bearing ring carbon would give a tertiary radical, so Br adds to the adjacent ring CH (less substituted). Net anti-Markovnikov; product is 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. CC1=CCCCC1 is valid 1-methylcyclohexene; Br adds to the less-substituted ring carbon to give the tertiary radical; key 0 correct. The C1 methyl-bearing carbon is the substituted alkene carbon, so option 0's reasoning is sound."},{"q":"A student wants 1-bromo-4-methylpentane (Br on the terminal carbon) from 4-methyl-1-pentene (drawn). Which reagents give that product, and how does it compare to treating the same alkene with HBr alone?","q_smiles":"C=CCC(C)C","opts":["HBr + peroxides gives the terminal (anti-Markovnikov) bromide; HBr alone gives the Markovnikov 2-bromo isomer","HBr alone gives the terminal bromide; peroxides give the Markovnikov isomer","Br2/CCl4 gives the terminal bromide","Both conditions give the identical terminal bromide","HBr + peroxides gives the 2-bromo (Markovnikov) isomer"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Two concepts: peroxide (radical) pathway is anti-Markovnikov, putting Br on terminal CH2 to give 1-bromo-4-methylpentane; HBr without peroxide goes through a secondary carbocation (Markovnikov) to put Br on C2. The conditions give different regiochemistry.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. C=CCC(C)C is valid 4-methyl-1-pentene; radical = terminal Br, ionic = C2 Markovnikov; key 0 correct; two-concept (regio + contrast) within scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (drawn) is treated with HBr WITHOUT peroxides (ionic addition) versus WITH peroxides (radical addition). Which statement correctly contrasts the outcomes?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["The ionic pathway puts Br on the terminal carbon; the radical pathway puts Br on the internal carbon with rearrangement","With peroxides the radical rearranges but the ionic pathway does not","Both pathways give the same single product with no rearrangement","Without peroxides a carbocation forms and can rearrange (hydride/methyl shift) to a more stable cation; with peroxides the radical pathway adds Br to the terminal carbon and does NOT rearrange","Neither pathway adds HBr to this hindered alkene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Ionic HBr makes a secondary carbocation next to a quaternary carbon, which undergoes a methyl shift to a more stable tertiary cation (rearranged product). The radical pathway proceeds through a carbon radical that does not rearrange, adding Br anti-Markovnikov to the terminal carbon. Rearrangement (ionic) vs no rearrangement (radical).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. C=CC(C)(C)C is valid 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene; ionic addition gives a 2 cation that methyl-shifts to 3 tertiary; radical pathway no rearrangement; key 0 correct. Concept of carbocation rearrangement is standard sophomore depth."},{"q":"Two alkenes are considered for HBr/peroxide (radical) addition: styrene (C6H5-CH=CH2) and 1-hexene (drawn). Styrene reacts faster. Which factor best explains styrene's faster radical addition?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts":["Styrene's pi bond is stronger, so it reacts faster","Br radical addition to styrene generates a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, lowering the activation energy for the rate-determining addition step","Styrene forms a more stable carbocation, speeding the radical step","1-Hexene cannot undergo radical addition at all","Styrene is less sterically hindered than 1-hexene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Radical addition rate tracks the stability of the carbon radical formed in the rate-determining first propagation step. For styrene, Br adds to terminal CH2 and the resulting radical is benzylic (resonance-stabilized), so it forms faster than the secondary radical from 1-hexene. The carbocation trap is wrong because the radical mechanism has no carbocation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. Valid 1-hexene drawn; benzylic radical stabilization is the right rate explanation; key 0 correct; option 5 carbocation is a good trap. Within DAT depth (qualitative radical stability, no FMO/KIE math)."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide (a radical initiator). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","BrCC1CCCCC1","CCC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Peroxide makes this a radical (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to C2 (the =CH carbon) to give the more stable tertiary radical at C1, then H caps C1, so Br ends up on the less substituted alkene carbon: 1-methyl-2-bromocyclohexane. Markovnikov distractor (opt1) puts Br on the tertiary carbon; the others are a wrong-skeleton bromomethyl, a dibromide, and an ethyl skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid and parse; no duplicate options. Anti-Markovnikov product confirmed; clean single-concept regiochemistry item."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with HBr and a catalytic amount of ROOR (peroxide). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCCc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"With peroxide the addition is anti-Markovnikov: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to generate the resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, then H caps the benzylic carbon. Product is (2-bromoethyl)benzene with Br on the terminal carbon. The benzylic-Br isomer (opt1) is the peroxide-free Markovnikov product; the alcohol, vicinal dibromide, and ring-brominated arene are not products.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. Standard benzylic-radical anti-Markovnikov case."},{"q":"2-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. Choose the major product.","q_smiles":"C=C(C)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CCC(C)(Br)CBr","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCCC(C)Br","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"correct":0,"why":"Radical (peroxide) conditions give anti-Markovnikov addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to form the more stable tertiary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br ends up on the primary carbon (1-bromo-2-methylbutane). The tertiary-bromide option is the Markovnikov product; the rest are wrong-regiochemistry, rearranged, or dibromide structures.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, no duplicates. Single-concept regiochemistry, in scope."},{"q":"Vinylcyclohexane is reacted with HBr in the presence of a peroxide initiator. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CC1CCCCC1Br","BrCCC1CCCCCC1","BrCCC1CCCCC1","BrCC(Br)C1CCCCC1","CC(Br)C1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Peroxide drives anti-Markovnikov addition. Br radical adds to the terminal CH2; the resulting secondary radical (adjacent to the ring) is then capped by H, so Br lands on the terminal (primary) carbon. The Markovnikov isomer (opt1) puts Br on the internal carbon; the others brominate the ring, give a dibromide, or use a 7-membered ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; opt4 is a valid cycloheptane wrong-skeleton distractor. One defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Methyl-1-pentene is treated with HBr and a peroxide (radical conditions). Identify the major product.","q_smiles":"C=CCC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)CC(Br)CBr","CC(C)CC(C)Br","CC(C)CCCBr","CCC(Br)C(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to give the more stable secondary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br is on the primary terminal carbon (1-bromo-4-methylpentane). The isopropyl branch is a spectator. The Markovnikov option puts Br on C2; the rest are rearranged, falsely tertiary, or dibromide structures.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; no duplicates. Single-concept regiochemistry, in scope."},{"q":"Allylbenzene (3-phenyl-1-propene) is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. A student worries the benzene ring will redirect where bromine adds. Which structure is actually the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1Br","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"The terminal C=C is separated from the ring by a CH2, so it is NOT conjugated and gains no benzylic stabilization. Anti-Markovnikov addition simply puts Br on the terminal CH2: (3-bromopropyl)benzene. The trap options assume conjugation and place Br on an internal or benzylic carbon; others are a dibromide or ring-brominated arene.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid. Legit two-concept item (non-conjugation recognition + anti-Markovnikov regio); benzylic-trap distractor (opt2) is plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"1-Hexene reacts with HBr in the presence of a peroxide to give 1-bromohexane (anti-Markovnikov). In the chain-propagation steps, which species adds DIRECTLY to the C=C double bond, and to which carbon does it bond?","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A bromide ion (Br-) adds to the more substituted carbon via nucleophilic attack","A bromine atom (Br radical) adds to the terminal CH2 carbon, generating the more stable secondary carbon radical","An alkoxy radical (RO) adds to the terminal carbon and is incorporated into the product","A bromine atom (Br radical) adds to the internal carbon, generating a primary carbon radical","A proton (H+) adds first to the terminal carbon to form a secondary carbocation"],"correct":1,"why":"The peroxide mechanism is a radical chain. In propagation, the chain carrier is the bromine ATOM (Br radical), which adds to the alkene first at the terminal CH2 so the resulting radical is the more stable (secondary) one. That radical then abstracts H from HBr, regenerating Br. No carbocation, no bromide-ion attack, and the alkoxy radical only initiates (it is not incorporated into the product).","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles is valid 1-hexene. Single propagation-step identification (not a full hand-drawn mechanism), so in scope; exactly one defensible answer with mechanistically wrong distractors."},{"q":"(E)-1-Phenyl-1-propene (PhCH=CHCH3) is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1Br","CC(Br)C(Br)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"For this internal alkene, regiochemistry is set by radical stability, not substituent count. Br radical adds to C2 (next to CH3) because that generates a resonance-stabilized BENZYLIC radical on the phenyl-bearing carbon; H then caps the benzylic carbon. Product is (2-bromopropyl)benzene, PhCH2-CHBr-CH3, confirmed by canonicalization. The classic trap (opt1) puts Br on the benzylic carbon itself; the rest are a terminal isomer, a dibromide, and a wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0); verified keyed SMILES equals PhCH2-CHBr-CH3. All options valid SMILES, no duplicates. Hard but in-scope (radical regiochemistry by benzylic stability)."},{"q":"What is the major product when 2-methyl-2-butene is treated with dilute H2SO4 / H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)=CCO","CC(C)(O)CC","CC(C)CCO","CC(C)C(O)C","OCC(C)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov hydration: protonation gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, water adds at the more substituted carbon -> 2-methyl-2-butanol (3deg alcohol = CCC(C)(C)O). Keyed opt0 matches. opt1 = 3-methyl-2-butanol (anti-Markovnikov), opt2/opt3 wrong regiochemistry/primary, opt4 retains alkene. Exactly one defensible answer; all 5 SMILES valid and distinct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Chemistry and keyed index correct. Minor: 'why' option-numbering is 1-indexed (slightly clunky vs 0-based key) but unambiguous. KEEP."},{"q":"Predict the major product of oxymercuration-demercuration of 1-methylcyclohexene (1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4).","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1C","CC1=CCCCC1O","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration = Markovnikov addition of water with NO rearrangement (mercurinium ion). OH lands on the more substituted (methyl) carbon -> 1-methylcyclohexanol (3deg). Keyed opt0 correct. opt1 = 2-methylcyclohexanol (anti-Markovnikov), opt2 wrong skeleton, opt3 has an extra methyl (C8H16O, wrong formula but valid distractor), opt4 retains alkene. One defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct, in scope, well-formed. KEEP."},{"q":"Give the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclohexene (1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH).","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","CC1CCCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov + syn addition: OH to less-substituted C2, H to C1 (methyl carbon) on the same face, so the C1 methyl and C2 OH end up trans -> trans-2-methylcyclohexanol. Verified by 3D geometry: opt0 (1S,2S) is trans (correct); opt1 is cis. opt2 = Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, opt3 lacks stereo, opt4 wrong skeleton. Two layered concepts (regio+stereo) -> difficulty 2 appropriate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Confirmed opt0 = trans via 3D embedding. Correct and in scope. KEEP. (Same trans-2-methylcycloalkanol concept as Q8 - mild batch redundancy.)"},{"q":"What is the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (1. BH3; 2. H2O2, NaOH)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(C)(C)C(O)C","CC(C)C(O)CC","CC(C)(C)C=CO","CCC(C)(C)CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration is concerted/anti-Markovnikov with NO carbocation rearrangement, so the neopentyl skeleton is preserved and OH goes to the terminal carbon -> 3,3-dimethyl-1-butanol (primary). Keyed opt0 correct. opt1 = Markovnikov alcohol, opt2 = rearranged skeleton (the acid-hydration lure), opt3 = enol (retains unsaturation), opt4 wrong isomer. One defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Tests the no-rearrangement point cleanly. KEEP."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with aqueous H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed hydration). What is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CCO","CC(C)(O)CC","CC(C)C(O)C","OCC(C)CC","CC(C)C=CO"],"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a 2deg C2 cation that undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable 3deg cation; water adds there -> rearranged 2-methyl-2-butanol (CCC(C)(C)O). Keyed opt0 correct. opt1 = unrearranged secondary Markovnikov alcohol, opt2/opt3 wrong regio, opt4 retains alkene. Carbocation rearrangement = second concept -> difficulty 2 appropriate. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Classic hydride-shift item, single reactive center, in scope. KEEP."},{"q":"Predict the major product of oxymercuration-demercuration of styrene (1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4).","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccccc1CO","OCCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1C(C)O","OC=Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov addition; the benzylic carbon best stabilizes the mercurinium partial positive, so OH lands benzylic -> 1-phenylethanol. Keyed opt0 correct. opt1 = 2-phenylethanol (anti-Markovnikov), opt2 = enol/retains double bond, opt3/opt4 = ring-substituted wrong skeletons. One defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Benzylic Markovnikov is standard sophomore content. KEEP."},{"q":"What is the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 1-pentene (1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH)?","q_smiles":"CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCO","CCC(O)CC","CCC(C)CO","CCCC(O)C","CCCC=CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov: boron/OH add to the less-hindered terminal carbon -> 1-pentanol (primary). Keyed opt0 correct. opt1 = 2-pentanol (Markovnikov), opt2 = 3-pentanol (wrong regio), opt3 = enol/retains alkene, opt4 = branched isomer. One defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Clean anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry item. KEEP."},{"q":"Give the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclopentene (1. BH3; 2. H2O2, NaOH), including stereochemistry.","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCC1","CC1CCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","OCC1CCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov (OH to C2) + syn addition (H to C1 methyl carbon, same face) forces the C1 methyl and C2 OH trans -> trans-2-methylcyclopentanol. Verified by 3D geometry: opt0 is trans (correct); opt1 is cis. opt2 = Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, opt3 lacks stereo, opt4 wrong skeleton. Layered regio+stereo, single reactive center -> within DAT recognition depth.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Confirmed opt0 = trans via 3D. Correct and in scope (single center, 2 layered concepts). KEEP. Note: near-duplicate of Q3 (trans-2-methylcycloalkanol via hydroboration); difficulty 3 is borderline but acceptable."},{"q":"The alkene shown is converted into 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (the tertiary alcohol, with OH on the ring carbon that bears the methyl group). Which set of reagents accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Dilute H2SO4, H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration)","BH3-THF, then H2O2/NaOH","1. OsO4; 2. NaHSO3","Br2 in CCl4","1. O3; 2. Zn, H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration (and oxymercuration) follow Markovnikov: water adds to the more substituted carbon, giving the tertiary alcohol via the more stable tertiary carbocation/mercurinium opening. BH3 then H2O2/NaOH is anti-Markovnikov and would give the secondary alcohol. The other reagents do not install a single OH.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Markovnikov hydration of 1-methylcyclohexene gives the tertiary alcohol; dilute H2SO4/H2O (index 0) is right and all distractors are clean. Valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene (shown) is treated with 1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4. The product is 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol (OH on C2, with the tert-butyl group intact, no skeletal rearrangement). What is the single most important reason oxymercuration is chosen over dilute acid for this substrate?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C=C","opts":["Oxymercuration gives anti-Markovnikov addition","Oxymercuration proceeds without carbocation rearrangement, so the carbon skeleton is preserved","Oxymercuration reduces the alkene to an alkane first","Oxymercuration adds water with syn stereochemistry to a single face","Oxymercuration is an oxidation that adds two OH groups"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Both acid-catalyzed hydration and oxymercuration are Markovnikov, but acid-catalyzed hydration goes through a free secondary carbocation that undergoes a hydride/methyl shift to the tertiary cation, scrambling the skeleton. Oxymercuration goes through a bridged mercurinium ion (no free open carbocation), so no rearrangement occurs and the Markovnikov alcohol with an intact skeleton is obtained.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Index 0 (no rearrangement / mercurinium ion) is the right reason; the other options are all false. Conceptual mechanism recognition, not a full hand-drawn mechanism, so within DAT scope. Valid SMILES."},{"q":"The product trans-2-methylcyclopentan-1-ol is desired from the alkene shown (1-methylcyclopentene), with the new OH placed on the LESS substituted alkene carbon. Which reagent sequence gives this anti-Markovnikov, syn-addition outcome?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCC1","opts":["Dilute H3PO4, H2O","1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4","H2O, cat. H2SO4, heat","1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH","HBr, then NaOH"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH across the double bond anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted carbon) and syn (H and OH add to the same face), which on a ring sets the trans relationship between the new OH and the adjacent methyl. Acid hydration and oxymercuration are both Markovnikov and would place OH on the more substituted carbon.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclopentene giving trans-2-methylcyclopentanol is the standard textbook result (anti-Markovnikov + syn). Index 0 right; distractors are Markovnikov. Valid SMILES, two-concept (regio+stereo)."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation (1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH). Which statement correctly describes the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["The OH ends up on the benzylic carbon, giving 1-phenylethanol (Markovnikov)","The double bond is reduced to give ethylbenzene with no OH","The ring is hydroxylated to give a phenol","Two OH groups are added to give a 1,2-diol","The OH ends up on the terminal carbon, giving 2-phenylethanol (anti-Markovnikov)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron (and ultimately OH) adds to the less hindered, less substituted terminal carbon, giving 2-phenylethanol. The Markovnikov product (1-phenylethanol, benzylic OH) would come from acid hydration or oxymercuration.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration of styrene gives 2-phenylethanol (primary, terminal OH). Index 0 right; the benzylic-OH distractor is the classic Markovnikov trap. Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Acid-catalyzed hydration begins with protonation of the alkene to form a carbocation, so reaction rate tracks the stability of that intermediate. Rank the following four alkenes from FASTEST to SLOWEST in acid-catalyzed hydration (the propene drawn is one of them): ethene, propene, 2-methyl-2-butene, 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene.","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["All four react at the same rate","ethene > propene > 2-methyl-2-butene > 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene","2,3-dimethyl-2-butene > 2-methyl-2-butene > propene > ethene","2-methyl-2-butene > 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene > ethene > propene","propene > ethene > 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene > 2-methyl-2-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The rate-determining step is carbocation formation, so the alkene that gives the most stable cation reacts fastest. 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene gives a tertiary cation (most stabilized, from the tetrasubstituted alkene), 2-methyl-2-butene also gives a tertiary cation but is less substituted overall, propene gives a secondary cation, and ethene gives an unstable primary cation (slowest). More alkyl substitution = faster.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Both top alkenes give tertiary cations, but the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is the most reactive, so 2,3-DM2B > 2-Me-2-butene > propene > ethene is defensible and unambiguous. Index 0 right. Valid SMILES."},{"q":"2-methylpropene (isobutylene, shown) is treated with dilute aqueous H2SO4. Which product predominates, and by what rule?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C","opts":["a 1,2-diol, because dilute acid is an oxidant","methylpropene oxide (an epoxide)","tert-butanol (2-methylpropan-2-ol), by Markovnikov addition","isobutane, because the alkene is reduced","isobutanol (2-methylpropan-1-ol), by anti-Markovnikov addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation gives the more stable tertiary carbocation at the central carbon, and water adds there to give tert-butanol. Anti-Markovnikov (giving isobutanol) would require hydroboration-oxidation. Dilute acid does not oxidize, reduce, or epoxidize the alkene.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Markovnikov hydration of isobutylene gives tert-butanol via the tertiary cation. Index 0 right; distractors are clean. Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"A chemist wants to convert 4-methylpent-1-ene (shown) cleanly into 4-methylpentan-2-ol (Markovnikov secondary alcohol) WITHOUT any risk of carbocation rearrangement scrambling the skeleton. Which reagent choice is best?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC=C","opts":["HCl gas, then H2O","1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH","Dilute H2SO4, H2O, heat","Concentrated H2SO4 only (no water)","1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4 (oxymercuration-demercuration)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Both oxymercuration and acid hydration are Markovnikov, but only oxymercuration avoids a free carbocation (it uses a bridged mercurinium ion), so there is no rearrangement. Acid hydration of this substrate could allow hydride shifts. Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov and would give the primary alcohol, not the desired secondary alcohol.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Oxymercuration gives the Markovnikov secondary alcohol without rearrangement; index 0 right. Hydroboration distractor (anti-Markovnikov) and acid (rearrangement-prone) are properly wrong. Valid SMILES."},{"q":"The alkene shown (1-ethylcyclohexene) is treated with BH3-THF followed by H2O2/NaOH. Considering BOTH regiochemistry (anti-Markovnikov) and stereochemistry (syn addition), which description matches the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC1=CCCCC1","opts":["The ethyl group is oxidized to a carboxylic acid","OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon (the CH), and H and OH add to the same face (syn), giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","OH adds to the less substituted carbon, but H and OH add to opposite faces (anti)","Two OH groups add cis across the double bond","OH adds to the more substituted carbon bearing the ethyl group (Markovnikov)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted ring CH, not the carbon bearing the ethyl) and syn (H and OH add to the same face). On the ring, syn addition of H and OH places the new OH and the boron-derived H on the same face, which makes the OH trans to the ethyl substituent, giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Directly analogous to the canonical 1-methylcyclohexene -> trans-2-methylcyclohexanol result; anti-Markovnikov + syn gives trans-2-ethylcyclohexanol. Index 0 right; distractors cover Markovnikov, wrong stereo, diol, and oxidation. Valid SMILES, hard (regio+stereo)."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with dilute H2SO4 / H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1CCCC1CO","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation gives the tertiary benzylic-free cation at the methyl-bearing ring carbon, and water adds there to give 1-methylcyclohexanol (CC1(O)CCCCC1). Opt1 is the anti-Markovnikov secondary alcohol; opt2 puts OH on an exocyclic primary carbon; opts3/4 are wrong cyclopentane skeletons. All five SMILES parse; opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Standard Markovnikov hydration, single concept, valid distractors."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with aqueous acid (H3O+). Markovnikov addition proceeds through a carbocation that can rearrange. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CCC(O)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(O)(C)C(C)C","OCC(C)(C)CC","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation adjacent to a quaternary carbon; a methyl shift gives the more stable tertiary cation, so water traps it to give 2,3-dimethyl-2-butanol (CC(O)(C)C(C)C, canonical CC(C)C(C)(C)O). Opt1 is the non-rearranged 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol; opt2 is anti-Markovnikov; opts3/4 are unrelated isomers. The rearrangement is the trap and opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Hydride/methyl-shift rearrangement is core DAT content; two-concept (Markovnikov + rearrangement) within scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with (1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O then (2) NaBH4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["OCC(C)(C)CC","CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(O)(C)C(C)C","OCC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration is Markovnikov with NO rearrangement because the bridged mercurinium ion blocks a free cation. OH adds to the more-substituted alkene carbon, leaving the t-butyl group intact, giving 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol (CC(O)C(C)(C)C). Opt1 is the rearranged product you would only get under acid (the trap); opt2 is anti-Markovnikov; opt3 is a diol; opt4 is the wrong skeleton. Opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Pairs cleanly with Q2 to teach the no-rearrangement contrast; in scope."},{"q":"2-Methyl-1-butene is treated with (1) BH3 / THF then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)CC","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)CCO","CC(C)C(C)O","OCC(C)CC","O=CC(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron, then OH, adds to the less-substituted terminal CH2 of CH2=C(CH3)CH2CH3, giving the primary alcohol 2-methyl-1-butanol (OCC(C)CC). Opt1 is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol; opt2 is a wrong-carbon secondary alcohol; opt3 is an aldehyde (over-oxidation, wrong product); opt4 is the wrong skeleton. Opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Canonical anti-Markovnikov hydroboration, single concept."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with (1) BH3 / THF then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Considering both regiochemistry and stereochemistry, which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1CCCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C=C1CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two layers: (regio) anti-Markovnikov puts OH on C2, the less-substituted carbon next to the methyl; (stereo) syn addition of H and B (OH replaces B with retention) places H and OH on the same face, giving trans-2-methylcyclohexanol (OH and methyl trans). RDKit 3D analysis confirms opt0 (C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O) is the trans diastereomer and opt1 is cis. Opt2 is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, opt3 is the right regiochemistry without stereochemistry, opt4 is a wrong skeleton. Opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed; verified opt0=trans, opt1=cis via 3D embedding. Two-concept (regio+stereo) hard item at the upper edge of DAT depth but recognition-level, allowed."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with dilute H2SO4 / H2O. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov; protonation gives the resonance-stabilized benzylic cation, so OH adds at the benzylic carbon to give 1-phenylethanol (CC(O)c1ccccc1). Opt1 is the anti-Markovnikov primary alcohol; opt2 is an aldehyde; opt3 is acetophenone (an oxidation product, not hydration); opt4 (benzyl alcohol) has the wrong carbon count. Opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Benzylic Markovnikov hydration, single concept, classic substrate."},{"q":"1-Ethylcyclopentene is treated with (1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O then (2) NaBH4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC1=CCCC1","opts":["CCC1(O)CCCCC1","CCC1CCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCC1","CCC1(O)CCCC1","OCCCC1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Oxymercuration is Markovnikov with no rearrangement. OH goes to the more-substituted ring carbon bearing the ethyl group, giving the tertiary alcohol 1-ethylcyclopentanol (CCC1(O)CCCC1). Opt1 is the anti-Markovnikov secondary alcohol; opt2 is a wrongly ring-expanded six-membered ring; opt3 is a ring-opened wrong skeleton; opt4 has lost a carbon from the ethyl. Opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Markovnikov oxymercuration on a trisubstituted alkene, single concept."},{"q":"alpha-Methylstyrene (2-phenylpropene) is treated with (1) BH3 / THF then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(O)(C)c1ccccc1","CCC(O)c1ccccc1","OCC(C)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the less-hindered terminal CH2, so OH ends up on that primary carbon, giving 2-phenyl-1-propanol (OCC(C)c1ccccc1). The benzylic position does NOT get the OH, which is the trap. Opt1 is the Markovnikov benzylic tertiary alcohol; opt2 is a wrong-skeleton benzylic secondary alcohol; opt3 is a ketone; opt4 is wrong regiochemistry. Opt0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration overriding benzylic stability is a high-value trap; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts":["Br[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1Br","BrC1CCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Br","BrC1(Br)CCCC1","BrC1CCC(Br)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds via a cyclic bromonium ion that is opened by backside (anti) attack of bromide, placing the two Br atoms on opposite faces: trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane. RDKit 3D embedding confirms opt0 is the trans (chiral, d,l) diastereomer and opt1 is the cis (meso). The gem-dibromide, 1,3-dibromide, and monobromide skeletons are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid, opt0 confirmed trans (anti-addition product) by 3D embedding, 5 distinct options, correct index 0."},{"q":"(E)-2-butene (trans-2-butene) reacts with Br2. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["C/C=C(Br)/C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CC","CCC(C)(Br)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to the trans (E) alkene gives the meso (2R,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane. RDKit confirms opt0 is meso (achiral) and opt1 is the chiral d,l pair (which would come from the cis alkene). The gem-dibromide, monobromide, and vinyl bromide are not addition products.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 confirmed meso by mirror-image test, correct anti-addition outcome for E-alkene. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product (halohydrin)?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted (more positively charged) tertiary carbon, so OH lands on the methyl-bearing carbon (Markovnikov) and Br on the adjacent secondary carbon, anti. opt0 places OH on the tertiary C and Br on the neighbor; opt1 reverses regiochemistry; the diol, dibromide, and simple tertiary alcohol are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: regiochemistry correct (Markovnikov OH on tertiary C), all SMILES valid, 5 distinct, index 0 correct."},{"q":"Propene is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts":["CC(O)CO","CC(Br)CO","CC(O)CCl","CC(O)CBr","CC(Br)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"In halohydrin formation water attacks the more substituted carbon of the bromonium ion, so OH goes to C2 (Markovnikov) and Br to C1: 1-bromo-2-propanol (CC(O)CBr). opt1 is the anti-Markovnikov 2-bromo-1-propanol; C is the diol (no Br), D the dibromide (no water), E has the wrong halide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 = 1-bromo-2-propanol (Markovnikov OH on C2). Valid SMILES, distinct options, index 0 correct."},{"q":"Cyclohexene reacts with Cl2 in CCl4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["Cl[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Cl","ClC1CCCCC1","ClC1(Cl)CCCCC1","Cl[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Cl","ClC1CCCC(Cl)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Cl2 adds via a chloronium ion opened by anti (backside) attack of chloride, giving trans-1,2-dichlorocyclohexane. RDKit 3D embedding confirms opt0 is trans (chiral, d,l) and opt1 is cis (meso). The geminal, 1,3-, and monochloride skeletons are wrong.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 confirmed trans (anti-addition) by 3D embedding; opt1 is cis/meso distractor. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product (halohydrin)?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCC(Br)c1ccccc1","OC(CBr)c1ccccc1","ClC(CBr)c1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the benzylic carbon, which carries the most positive charge (benzylic stabilization), so OH lands benzylic and Br on the terminal CH2: 2-bromo-1-phenylethanol (OC(CBr)c1ccccc1). opt1 reverses regiochemistry; C is the diol, D the dibromide, E has a wrong halide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 = 2-bromo-1-phenylethanol (OH benzylic). Valid SMILES, distinct, index 0 correct."},{"q":"(Z)-3-hexene (cis-3-hexene) reacts with Br2. Which structure represents the major product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C\\CC","opts":["CC/C=C(Br)\\CC","CC[C@H](Br)[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(Br)CCC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CC[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to the cis (Z) alkene gives the (3R,4R)/(3S,4S) racemic (d,l) pair of 3,4-dibromohexane. RDKit confirms opt0 is chiral (d,l) and opt1 is the meso diastereomer (which would arise from the trans alkene). The gem-dibromide, monobromide, and vinyl bromide are not addition products.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 confirmed chiral (d,l) by mirror test, correct anti outcome for Z-alkene; opt1 is meso distractor. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product (halohydrin)?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(Br)C(C)(C)O","CC(Br)C(C)(C)Br","CC(O)C(C)(C)Br","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)O","CC(O)C(C)(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted tertiary carbon (Markovnikov OH), placing OH on the (CH3)2C carbon and Br on the adjacent secondary carbon, anti: CH3-CHBr-C(CH3)2-OH. opt1 reverses regiochemistry; C is the diol, D the dibromide, E has the wrong halide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 has OH on tertiary C, Br on secondary C (Markovnikov). Valid SMILES, distinct, index 0 correct."},{"q":"An alkene is treated with Br2 in an inert solvent (CCl4). Which statement BEST classifies this reaction and its stereochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["Electrophilic substitution; one vinylic H is replaced by Br","Electrophilic addition; syn addition of the two Br atoms across the double bond","Electrophilic addition; anti addition of the two Br atoms across the double bond","Free-radical addition; both Br atoms add to the same carbon","Nucleophilic addition; Markovnikov addition of one Br and one H"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds by electrophilic addition through a bridged bromonium ion opened by backside bromide attack = anti addition (trans-1,2-dibromide). q_smiles C1=CCCCC1 is a valid cyclohexene; stem is generic 'an alkene' so no name mismatch. Single concept, in scope, exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct, in scope, valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-methyl-2-butene is reacted with Br2 in water. Markovnikov selectivity governs where the OH ends up. On which carbon does the OH (from water) add, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts":["On the less substituted carbon, because the bromonium is symmetric","Equally on both carbons, because the bromonium ion has no charge bias","On the less substituted alkene carbon, because water prefers the less hindered carbon only","On the more substituted alkene carbon, because that carbon bears the greater partial positive charge in the bromonium ion","On the more substituted carbon, because anti-Markovnikov rules apply with water"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"q_smiles CC(C)=CC canonicalizes to CC=C(C)C = 2-methyl-2-butene (matches stem). The unsymmetrical bromonium ion has more partial positive charge on the more-substituted carbon (more carbocation-like), so water attacks there: Markovnikov OH on the more substituted carbon. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. SMILES matches name, answer correct, single concept (Markovnikov regiochemistry), in scope."},{"q":"You isolate the product shown below from treating an alkene with a halogen source. The product is a vicinal chlorohydrin (OH and Cl on adjacent carbons). Which single reagent/condition set would produce it from cyclohexene?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1Cl","opts":["Cl2, NaOH (concentrated)","Cl2, CCl4 (no water)","NaCl, H2O","HCl (gas)","Cl2, H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"q_smiles OC1CCCCC1Cl is a valid vicinal (1,2) chlorohydrin on cyclohexane. A chlorohydrin (anti Cl + OH) comes from Cl2/H2O (chloronium opened by water). Dry Cl2/CCl4 gives dichloride; HCl gives only chloride; NaCl/H2O is unreactive; conc. NaOH would eliminate. Index 0 correct, one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Valid 1,2-chlorohydrin SMILES, reagent-selection answer correct and unique."},{"q":"An unknown alkene is treated with Br2 in CH2Cl2 and the only product is the vicinal dibromide drawn below. Which reagent/condition set is responsible (no oxygen nucleophile incorporated)?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)Br","opts":["Br2, CH2Cl2 (anhydrous)","NBS, H2O","HBr, peroxides","Br2, light (hv) in excess","Br2, H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"q_smiles CC(Br)C(C)Br = 2,3-dibromobutane, a valid vicinal dibromide. A clean dibromide with no OH means halogenation in an inert anhydrous solvent (Br2/CH2Cl2). Br2/H2O or NBS/H2O give bromohydrin; HBr/peroxides gives one Br + H; Br2/hv gives radical/allylic substitution. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Valid vicinal dibromide, reagent answer unique and correct."},{"q":"Styrene (phenylethene) is treated with Br2 in water. The bromonium ion can open at either carbon, but one carbon is strongly favored because of the adjacent benzene ring. Where does the OH end up, and why?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["OH adds to the benzylic carbon, because positive charge there is stabilized by resonance with the ring","OH adds to the terminal CH2 carbon, because it is least hindered","Br adds to the benzylic carbon and OH to the terminal carbon, anti-Markovnikov","Both carbons get OH because the intermediate is a free benzylic cation that traps two waters","OH adds to the benzylic carbon, because that carbon is the least substituted"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"q_smiles C=Cc1ccccc1 = styrene (matches stem). The benzylic carbon stabilizes positive charge by resonance, so the bromonium ion has cation character there; water attacks the benzylic carbon = OH benzylic, Br on terminal CH2 (PhCH(OH)CH2Br). Two layered concepts (regiochemistry + resonance reasoning) at difficulty 2, in scope. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. SMILES matches styrene, regiochemistry answer correct and uniquely defensible."},{"q":"Two alkenes are each treated with Br2 in CH2Cl2 under identical conditions. Alkene A is 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (tetrasubstituted, drawn) and alkene B is 1-butene (monosubstituted). Which reacts FASTER with Br2 and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts":["Alkene A, because tetrasubstituted alkenes react by a radical pathway that is faster","Alkene B, because the terminal alkene is less hindered so Br2 approaches more easily","Both react at the same rate, because bromonium formation is independent of substitution","Alkene B, because monosubstituted alkenes form more stable bromonium ions","Alkene A, because more alkyl substituents make the pi bond more electron-rich and the bromonium ion more stable"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"q_smiles CC(C)=C(C)C = 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (tetrasubstituted, matches stem A). Halogenation rate tracks alkene nucleophilicity: more alkyl groups donate electron density and stabilize developing positive charge, so tetrasubstituted A >> monosubstituted B. Index 0 correct. Reactivity-trend concept, difficulty 2, in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. SMILES matches the drawn alkene A, reactivity answer correct and unique."},{"q":"trans-2-butene reacts with Br2 in CCl4 (anti addition through a bromonium ion). Considering both new stereocenters, what is the stereochemical relationship of the 2,3-dibromobutane product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["A single optically active (2R,3R) product only","A 1:1 mixture of the meso compound and the racemic pair","A racemic mixture of (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) enantiomers","A single meso compound (the two enantiomers superimpose due to an internal mirror plane)","A cis-dibromide formed by syn addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"q_smiles C/C=C/C = (E)/trans-2-butene (valid stereo-SMILES, matches stem). Anti addition of Br2 to trans-2-butene gives MESO 2,3-dibromobutane (R,S) with an internal mirror plane; cis-2-butene would give the racemic R,R/S,S pair. This is the canonical anti-addition diagnostic. Layered stereochemistry concept at difficulty 3, still recognition-level and in DAT scope. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Stereo-defined SMILES matches trans-2-butene, meso outcome correct, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in CH2Cl2 (an inert solvent). Which structure correctly shows BOTH the regiochemical and stereochemical outcome of the reaction?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1(Br)CCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1Br","BrC1CCCC1","Cl[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Cl","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti through a cyclic bromonium ion; bromide opens the ring by backside attack, giving trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane. Verified by 3D embedding: opt0 (S,S) is the chiral trans (anti) diastereomer, opt1 (S,R) is the cis (syn) isomer not formed, opt2 is the geminal dibromide, opt3 a monobromide, opt5 a dichloride. All SMILES parse; exactly one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct (trans/anti confirmed in 3D), one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with Br2 in water (Br2/H2O). Which structure shows the correct regiochemistry AND stereochemistry of the halohydrin product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@@H]1O","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@@H]1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@H]1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1O"],"correct":1,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted (tertiary, methyl-bearing) carbon, placing OH there (Markovnikov OH) and Br on the adjacent carbon, anti to OH (trans). 3D embedding confirms opt0 is the trans diastereomer (OH/Br anti, 18/20 confs) and opt2 is the cis diastereomer distractor (17/20 cis). opt1 reverses regiochemistry, opt3 is a diol, opt5 the dibromide. Two-layer (regio+stereo) but single reactive center, in DAT predict-product scope.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct (Markovnikov OH + trans/anti, both confirmed in 3D); one defensible answer; layered regio+stereo allowed."},{"q":"Propene (CH3CH=CH2) is treated with Br2 in water. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)CO","CCCBr","CC(O)CO","CC(O)CBr","CC(Br)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"Halohydrin formation: water attacks the more substituted carbon of the bromonium ion (Markovnikov OH), giving 1-bromo-2-propanol (CC(O)CBr). opt1 swaps OH/Br (anti-Markovnikov), opt2 is the dibromide (inert solvent product), opt3 the diol, opt4 a simple bromide. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Markovnikov halohydrin regiochemistry correct; one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with Cl2 in water. Which structure is the major halohydrin product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(CO)c1ccccc1","ClCC1CCCCC1Cl","OC(CCl)c1ccccc1","ClC(CO)c1ccccc1","ClC(CCl)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The chloronium ion is opened by water at the benzylic carbon (resonance-stabilized positive character, Markovnikov OH), giving 2-chloro-1-phenylethanol (OH benzylic, Cl terminal). opt1 swaps regiochemistry, opt2 is the dichloride, opt3 the diol, opt4 has a saturated cyclohexane skeleton (wrong). All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Benzylic Markovnikov halohydrin regiochemistry correct; one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Which structure represents the product with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCC(Br)CC1","BrC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Br","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti via a bromonium ion, giving trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane. 3D embedding confirms opt0 (S,S) is the trans (anti) diastereomer; opt1 (S,R) is the cis isomer (not formed), opt2 the geminal dibromide, opt3 the 1,4-dibromide (wrong regiochemistry), opt4 a monobromide. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct (trans/anti confirmed in 3D); one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Consider the bromonium-ion mechanism of Br2 addition to an alkene. Which statement BEST describes the curved arrow for the ring-opening step that sets the stereochemistry (substrate shown: cyclohexene)?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Bromide attacks a bromonium-ion carbon from the face opposite the C–Br+ bond (backside), giving anti (trans) addition","Bromide attacks the same face as the bromonium bridge, giving syn (cis) addition","Bromide adds to the less hindered carbon by a concerted syn pathway, giving the cis product","A free, planar carbocation forms first, so the bromide can add to either face equally (no stereochemical preference)","The pi electrons attack bromide, expelling Br+ and giving a Markovnikov rearranged product"],"correct":0,"why":"The stereochemistry-setting step is SN2-like backside attack of bromide on the bridged bromonium carbon, inverting that center and placing the two bromines anti (trans). opt0 is correct; opt1/opt5 give the wrong (syn/cis) outcome, opt3 reverses the electron flow, opt4 wrongly describes an open carbocation. Identifies a single arrow only (no full mechanism), substrate is drawn, in DAT scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Text reaction-mechanism item with molecule drawn; single-arrow identification within scope; one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with Br2 in water. Which structure shows the correct halohydrin (consider where OH and Br go)?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)C(C)CBr","CC(Br)C(C)(C)Br","CC(O)C(C)(C)Br","CC(O)C(C)(C)O","CC(Br)C(C)(C)O"],"correct":4,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted (tertiary) carbon (Markovnikov OH), giving 3-bromo-2-methyl-2-butanol: OH on the tertiary C, Br on the secondary CH. opt0 is correct. opt1 reverses regiochemistry, opt2 is the dibromide, opt3 the diol, opt4 has a rearranged skeleton (primary bromide). All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Markovnikov halohydrin regiochemistry correct; one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(E)-2-butene (trans-2-butene) is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA in CH2Cl2.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CC","C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C","CCC(O)CO"],"correct":3,"why":"mCPBA delivers oxygen in a concerted, syn, stereospecific step that preserves alkene geometry. E (trans) alkene gives trans-2,3-epoxybutane, the (R,R)/(S,S) chiral pair. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C is (R,R) chiral (NOT meso); opt1 is the cis/meso epoxide from a cis alkene; opt2 is the 1,2-diol (dihydroxylation, wrong reagent); opt3 rearranged terminal diol; opt4 cleavage ketone. Two-concept (stereospecific syn + trans recognition). Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed (R,R) trans-epoxide correct for E-alkene; all 6 SMILES valid; opt1 confirmed meso (cis) distractor."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCyclohexene is treated with a catalytic amount of OsO4 with NMO (N-methylmorpholine-N-oxide) as co-oxidant.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1CCCC(O)C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O"],"correct":1,"why":"OsO4 adds both C-O bonds to the same face (syn) via the cyclic osmate ester, giving cis-1,2-cyclohexanediol. RDKit + 3D conformer face-test confirm keyed opt0 is cis (meso, both OH same face); opt1 is trans (anti); opt2 is the epoxide (mCPBA); opt3 has one OH; opt4 is a 1,3-diol (wrong regiochem). Single concept. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed opt0 is cis (meso) diol — correct for OsO4 syn addition; opt1 confirmed trans distractor; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC12OC1CCCC2","CC1=CCCCC1O","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"mCPBA gives the epoxide (oxirane spanning the two former sp2 carbons, one bearing methyl); no diol forms without separate hydrolysis. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 CC12OC1CCCC2 = C7H12O epoxide of 1-methylcyclohexene. opt1/opt2 are the cis/trans diols (dihydroxylation, wrong reagent); opt3 allylic alcohol; opt4 alpha-hydroxyketone (over-oxidation). Single concept (mCPBA → epoxide not diol). Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed epoxide connectivity/formula correct; opt1=cis diol, opt2=trans diol distractors; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(Z)-2-butene (cis-2-butene) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic, aqueous).\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","C[C@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CC=O","C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C","CCC(O)CO"],"correct":1,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 does syn-dihydroxylation; syn addition to a cis alkene gives meso-2,3-butanediol. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 C[C@H](O)[C@@H](C)O = (S,R) meso (internal mirror plane); opt1 is the (S,S) chiral pair (from trans alkene); opt2 epoxide (wrong reagent); opt3 acetaldehyde (hot/conc cleavage, not cold dilute); opt4 rearranged terminal diol. Two/three-concept (syn + meso + cold-no-cleavage). Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed opt0 confirmed MESO by RDKit; opt1 confirmed chiral (S,S) distractor; all SMILES valid. Hardest item, scope still sophomore."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nStyrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(O)cc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","C1OC1c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes the electron-rich vinyl C=C (not the aromatic ring), giving styrene oxide. RDKit confirms opt0 C1OC1c1ccccc1 = phenyloxirane. opt1 1,2-diol (dihydroxylation); opt2 benzaldehyde (cleavage); opt3 phenylacetaldehyde (wrong connectivity); opt4 ring-hydroxylation (wrong site). Single concept (chemoselectivity alkene vs arene). Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. All 6 SMILES valid; keyed styrene oxide correct; distractors are well-targeted (cleavage, diol, wrong-site)."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n1-Methylcyclopentene is treated with OsO4, then aqueous NaHSO3 workup.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(O)CCC(O)C1","CC12OC1CCC2","C[C@]1(O)CCC[C@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCC1","C[C@]1(O)CCC[C@@H]1O"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 gives syn (cis) addition of two OH across the former double bond → cis-1-methylcyclopentane-1,2-diol. Multi-conformer C-O bond-vector face-test (validated against cis-cyclohexanediol reference) confirms keyed opt0 = cis (both OH same face) and opt1 = trans. opt2 epoxide (mCPBA); opt3 1,3-diol (wrong regiochem); opt4 single OH. Single concept (relative-config on substituted ring). Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified by 3D face-test: keyed opt0 cis, opt1 trans; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(E)-3-hexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(O)C(O)CC","CC[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CC[C@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CC[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","CCC1OC1CC"],"correct":2,"why":"Epoxidation is syn/stereospecific, so E (trans) alkene gives trans-3,4-epoxyhexane, the (R,R)/(S,S) chiral pair. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 CC[C@H]1O[C@@H]1CC is the chiral (non-meso) trans epoxide; opt1 is the cis/meso epoxide (needs cis alkene); opt2/opt3 are 1,2-diols (dihydroxylation, wrong reagent); opt4 places the oxirane on wrong carbons. Two-concept (stereospecific syn + trans recognition). Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"Chemistry fully correct; keyed trans-epoxide verified. Minor: this is mechanistically the same 2-concept item as Q1 (rated 2) so a difficulty of 2 would be more consistent, but difficulty 1 is within tolerance — keep unchanged."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with a catalytic amount of OsO4 and NMO.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)C(C)(C)O","OCC(C)C(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(=O)C(C)C","CC1OC1(C)C"],"correct":0,"why":"OsO4 adds OH to each former sp2 carbon (syn vicinal diol). For 2-methyl-2-butene (CH3-CH=C(CH3)CH3) this gives a secondary OH on the CH carbon and a tertiary OH on the C(CH3) carbon = 2-methylbutane-2,3-diol. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 CC(O)C(C)(C)O has one CH(OH) and one C(OH) bearing two methyls. opt1 epoxide (mCPBA); opt2 single Markovnikov alcohol; opt3 1,3-diol (OH on wrong carbon); opt4 cleavage ketone. Single regiochem concept. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed vicinal diol (sec + tert OH) regiochem correct; opt3 is a wrong-regiochem 1,3-diol; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"A chemist treats cyclohexene (drawn) with one equivalent of meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid (mCPBA) in dichloromethane. Which product is formed?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1Cl","O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"correct":1,"why":"mCPBA delivers a single oxygen across the C=C in one concerted step to give cyclohexene oxide (opt0 = C6H10O fused 3-membered epoxide, confirmed by RDKit). The trans/cis diols (opt1/opt2) require separate dihydroxylation/hydrolysis, opt3 is a ketone, opt4 a halohydrin. Exactly one defensible answer, properly visual.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. mCPBA epoxidation; keyed epoxide is correct and unique. All SMILES valid."},{"q":"trans-2-Butene (drawn) is treated with OsO4 followed by aqueous NaHSO3 workup. The reaction is best classified as which of the following?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Anti dihydroxylation (OH groups added to opposite faces)","Epoxidation followed by no further reaction","Syn dihydroxylation (both OH added to the same face)","Markovnikov hydration to a single alcohol","Oxidative cleavage to two carbonyl compounds"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 adds two hydroxyls to the SAME face via a cyclic osmate ester (syn/cis addition); NaHSO3 is reductive workup that does not cleave. Classification = syn dihydroxylation. Anti diol comes from epoxide opening; cleavage needs hot conc. KMnO4/ozonolysis. One defensible answer, molecule drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Classification correct and unique; substrate SMILES valid."},{"q":"You want to convert 1-methylcyclohexene (drawn) into a vicinal cis-diol in a single operation. Which reagent set should you choose?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Hot concentrated KMnO4","O3, then (CH3)2S","OsO4, then NaHSO3 (or cold dilute KMnO4, OH-)","mCPBA in CH2Cl2","HBr with peroxides"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 with reductive workup (or cold dilute basic KMnO4) gives the syn cis-diol directly in one step. mCPBA stops at the epoxide; hot conc. KMnO4 and ozonolysis cleave the C=C; HBr/peroxides gives anti-Markovnikov hydrobromination. One defensible answer, substrate drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Reagent selection correct; 1-methylcyclohexene SMILES valid."},{"q":"Styrene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute, basic KMnO4. Which observation and product correctly describe this reaction?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The double bond is cleaved to give benzaldehyde and formaldehyde","HBr adds across the double bond","Purple color fades and 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol (a cis-diol) forms","An epoxide forms with no color change","Benzoic acid forms as the only organic product"],"correct":2,"why":"Cold dilute basic KMnO4 is a syn-dihydroxylation reagent (Baeyer test): gives the vicinal cis-diol 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol while purple permanganate is reduced (color fades to brown MnO2). Oxidative cleavage to carbonyls/acids requires HOT concentrated KMnO4. One defensible answer, styrene drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Cold/dilute KMnO4 = diol + decolorization; correct and unique."},{"q":"A student needs to prepare cyclopentene oxide from cyclopentene (drawn). Which single reagent accomplishes this epoxidation most directly?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NaBH4 in methanol","Cold dilute KMnO4, OH-","H3O+ (dilute aqueous acid)","mCPBA in CH2Cl2","OsO4, then NaHSO3"],"correct":3,"why":"A peroxyacid (mCPBA) epoxidizes the alkene in one concerted step to cyclopentene oxide. OsO4 and cold dilute KMnO4 give the syn-diol; dilute acid is inert to an isolated alkene; NaBH4 does not reduce simple alkenes. One defensible answer, cyclopentene drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. mCPBA = epoxide; correct and unique; SMILES valid."},{"q":"trans-2-Butene (drawn) is treated with OsO4 followed by reductive workup. What is the stereochemical outcome of the 2,3-butanediol formed?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C","CC(O)CC","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C","CC1OC1C","C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C"],"correct":2,"why":"Syn dihydroxylation of trans-2-butene gives the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) racemic pair. Keyed opt0 is the (R,R) enantiomer (RDKit-confirmed), the standard way to represent the dl pair. opt1 is the meso (S,R) diastereomer (the cis-butene product) - the intended trap. The opposite (S,S) enantiomer is NOT offered, so opt0 is the single defensible diol answer; opt2/opt3 are epoxides, opt4 a mono-alcohol.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. (R,R) key represents racemate; meso is the lone diol distractor; one defensible answer. No duplicates."},{"q":"Consider the two alkenes 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (a tetrasubstituted alkene, drawn) and ethylene. Both are treated with mCPBA. Which alkene undergoes epoxidation FASTER, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene, because electron-withdrawing methyl groups speed the reaction","2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene, because more alkyl substituents make the alkene more nucleophilic (electron-rich)","They react at identical rates because mCPBA is not sensitive to substitution","Ethylene, because terminal alkenes are inherently more nucleophilic","Ethylene, because less steric hindrance always dominates the rate"],"correct":1,"why":"Electrophilic peroxyacid epoxidation is faster for more electron-rich (more alkyl-substituted) alkenes: alkyl groups donate density and raise alkene nucleophilicity. The tetrasubstituted alkene reacts much faster than ethylene; methyls are electron-donating (opt4 wrong). The reasoning is sophomore alkene-reactivity, not grad FMO math. One defensible answer, substrate drawn.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Substitution/nucleophilicity rationale is in scope and correct; tetrasubstituted alkene SMILES valid."},{"q":"trans-2-Butene (drawn) is treated with mCPBA in CH2Cl2. mCPBA delivers its oxygen to one face of the planar alkene in a single concerted step (a 'butterfly' transition state), so the two new C-O bonds form on the same face and the original alkene geometry is preserved. Which structure correctly represents the epoxide product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C","C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C","C[C@H]1O[C@H]1C","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CCC1CO1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Syn (concerted) epoxidation retains alkene geometry, so the E (trans) alkene gives the trans-2,3-epoxide, which is the chiral (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair (opt0 = R,R, RDKit-confirmed chiral != mirror). The diol (opt2) is the OsO4/KMnO4 product, and CCC1CO1 is a rearranged terminal-epoxide skeleton. NOTE: opt1 and opt4 both canonicalize to the SAME meso cis-epoxide (C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C) - they are duplicate distractors. The single correct answer (opt0) is still unambiguous.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (trans/chiral epoxide from E-alkene). FLAW: opt1 and opt4 are the identical meso cis-epoxide (duplicate distractor). Correct answer remains the only right choice, so still keyable but the duplicate should be replaced (e.g., with a genuinely distinct distractor)."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with OsO4, then aqueous NaHSO3 workup. OsO4 adds both oxygens to the same face of the double bond through a cyclic osmate ester. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","O=C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"OsO4 is syn-dihydroxylation: both OH on the same face = cis-1,2-cyclohexanediol, the meso compound. 3D embedding confirms opt0 = CIS (meso) and opt1 = TRANS (chiral). NaHSO3 is the reductive workup that cleaves the osmate to the diol. Epoxide, mono-ol, and 1,2-diketone come from different reagents.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (cis/meso diol confirmed by 3D analysis). Clean single-concept item."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (drawn) reacts with mCPBA. Epoxidation is concerted and stereospecific, retaining the cis relationship of the two methyl groups. Which statement about the epoxide product is correct?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["A racemic mixture of (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) epoxides is formed.","Only an acyclic rearranged product, 1,2-epoxybutane, is formed.","A vicinal anti-diol is formed.","A single optically active (2R,3R) epoxide is formed.","A single achiral meso epoxide is formed (the cis-2,3-dimethyloxirane)."],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Syn O-delivery to the Z (cis) alkene keeps both methyls on the same face, giving cis-2,3-dimethyloxirane. RDKit confirms this molecule is meso (2R,3S, equal to its mirror image) hence achiral/optically inactive - opt0 is correct. The (R,R)/(S,S) pair comes from the trans alkene; mCPBA never makes a diol.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"q_smiles valid; statement opt0 correct (meso/achiral cis-epoxide confirmed). In scope (recognition + single stereo concept)."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic), then workup. This reagent performs syn-dihydroxylation. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC12CCCCC1O2","CC1(O)CCCCC1=O","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 gives a cis (syn) vicinal diol. 3D embedding confirms opt0 = CIS (tertiary and secondary OH on the same face) and opt1 = TRANS. The epoxide needs a peracid, the single tertiary alcohol is a hydration product, and the hydroxy-ketone is over-oxidation.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (cis diol confirmed by 3D analysis). Both diol options are chiral (no internal symmetry), so cis vs trans is the sole distinction - appropriate single-concept item."},{"q":"Styrene (phenylethene, drawn) is treated with mCPBA (1 equiv) in CH2Cl2 at room temperature. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","C1OC1c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes only the alkene (aromatic ring untouched), giving styrene oxide (2-phenyloxirane, opt0 = C8H8O). The 1,2-diol is the OsO4/KMnO4 product; benzaldehyde is oxidative cleavage; acetophenone and 1-phenylethanol have wrong skeleton/oxidation level.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (styrene oxide). Distractors span the standard mis-selections. Clean."},{"q":"(E)-Stilbene (trans-1,2-diphenylethene, drawn) is subjected to OsO4 (cat.) / NMO, a syn-dihydroxylation. Which structure correctly represents the product's stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C(/c1ccccc1)=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["OC(c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)C(=O)c1ccccc1","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","c1ccccc1C1OC1c1ccccc1","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Syn addition to the E (trans) alkene gives the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) d,l pair (opt0 = S,S, RDKit-confirmed chiral). The meso (R,S) diol (opt1, RDKit-confirmed meso) would come from the cis alkene or anti addition - the classic stilbene stereo trap. Epoxide, benzil (diketone), and mono-ol come from other reagents.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (chiral d,l diol from E-alkene + syn addition; opt1 meso confirmed). Two layered stereo concepts (syn addition + E/Z->meso/dl) but single reactive center; difficulty 3 appropriate and within DAT recognition depth."},{"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with mCPBA, and the resulting epoxide is then heated with aqueous acid (H3O+). Acid-catalyzed epoxide opening proceeds by backside (anti) attack of water. Which structure is the final diol?","q_smiles":"C1CC=CC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCC1=O","OC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Syn epoxidation then acid-catalyzed ANTI opening gives the net trans-1,2-diol. 3D embedding confirms opt0 = TRANS (the chiral 1R,2R/1S,2S pair) and opt1 = CIS (meso). The two-step route inverts the relative stereochemistry vs direct OsO4 syn-dihydroxylation. The epoxide (opt2) is the intermediate, not the final product.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (trans diol from anti opening, confirmed by 3D analysis). Two-concept (syn epoxidation + anti opening) but each is a single well-known step, not a 5-step synthesis - in scope."},{"q":"Three alkenes are considered for reaction with mCPBA: ethene, 2-methyl-2-butene (drawn), and (E)-2-butene. Epoxidation by a peracid is an electrophilic delivery of oxygen, so its rate increases with the number of electron-donating alkyl groups on the C=C. Which alkene reacts fastest, and what is its product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts":["2-Methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted) reacts fastest, giving 2,2,3-trimethyloxirane (its epoxide).","(E)-2-Butene reacts fastest, giving a vicinal anti-diol.","Ethene reacts fastest because it is the least hindered, giving oxirane.","All three react at the same rate; the peracid is not selective.","2-Methyl-2-butene reacts fastest, giving 3-methyl-2-butanone by rearrangement."],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Electrophilic peracid epoxidation speeds up with more alkyl substitution (more electron-rich pi bond): trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene > disubstituted (E)-2-butene > unsubstituted ethene. Product is its epoxide, 2,2,3-trimethyloxirane (mCPBA gives epoxides, not diols or carbonyl-rearranged products). opt0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"q_smiles valid; opt0 correct (trisubstituted reacts fastest; product correctly named 2,2,3-trimethyloxirane). Reactivity-trend recognition item, in scope. Batch contains 8 questions total (not 9)."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction sequence: the alkene shown is treated with (1) O3, then (2) (CH3)2S (dimethyl sulfide). Which set of carbonyl products forms?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=O.CCC=O","CC(C)=O.CC=O","CC(C)O.CCO","CC(=O)O.CC(C)=O","CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C. Reductive workup ((CH3)2S) stops at carbonyls. The trisubstituted carbon becomes acetone and the CH3CH= carbon becomes acetaldehyde, so CC(C)=O.CC=O is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 2-methyl-2-butene reductive ozonolysis gives acetone + acetaldehyde. All SMILES valid; distractors (oxidative acid, wrong skeletons, alcohols) plausibly wrong."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction: cyclohexene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) H2O2 (oxidative workup). What is the product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(O)CCCC(=O)O","O=C1CCCCC1","O=C(O)CCCCCC(=O)O","O=C(O)CCCCC(=O)O","O=CCCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Oxidative ozonolysis of the cyclic alkene opens the ring; both former alkene carbons become COOH, giving hexanedioic (adipic) acid, HOOC(CH2)4COOH.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 6-carbon ring + oxidative workup = adipic acid (verified 6 carbons). Dialdehyde (reductive), C5/C7 diacids (miscount), cyclohexanone (no cleavage) are good distractors."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction: 1-methylcyclohexene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O (reductive workup). What is the product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CCCC=O","CC(=O)CCCCC=O","CC(O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O"],"correct":2,"why":"Cleaving the endocyclic C=C opens the ring. The methyl-bearing carbon becomes a ketone and the other a CHO under Zn/H2O, giving 6-oxoheptanal (7 carbons).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 1-methylcyclohexene (7 C) reductive ozonolysis = 6-oxoheptanal. Two-concept (regio of ketone/aldehyde + reductive workup). Distractors solid."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: styrene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CO.OCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1.O=C=O","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the vinyl C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the terminal =CH2 becomes formaldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Styrene reductive ozonolysis = benzaldehyde + formaldehyde. Distractors (oxidative acid+CO2, acetaldehyde/acetophenone wrong skeletons, alcohols) good."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: trans-2-butene is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O","CC(O)C(C)O","CC=O.CC=O","CC(=O)O.O=CO","CCC(=O)O.O=C=O"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot, concentrated KMnO4 cleaves the alkene oxidatively. Each internal CH3CH= disubstituted carbon becomes a carboxylic acid, giving two acetic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Symmetric 2-butene + hot KMnO4 = 2x acetic acid. Distractors: aldehydes (reductive), diol (cold KMnO4), wrong skeletons. E geometry irrelevant to products, fine."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: 1-hexene is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)O.O=CO","CCCC(=O)O.O=C=O","CCCCC(=O)O.O=C=O","C=O.CCCCC=O","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Hot KMnO4 cleaves the C=C oxidatively. The internal carbon becomes pentanoic acid; the terminal =CH2 monosubstituted carbon is oxidized fully to CO2 and lost.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Terminal alkene + hot KMnO4 = pentanoic acid + CO2. The formic-acid pair is the classic trap (terminal CH2 -> CO2 not HCOOH). Two-concept (cleavage regio + terminal-to-CO2). Solid."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction: 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) (CH3)2S. What is the product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)=O.CC(C)=O","CC(C)(O)C(C)(O)C","CC=O.CCC(C)=O","CC(C)=O.CC=O","CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O"],"correct":0,"why":"This symmetric tetrasubstituted alkene cleaves so each carbon (bearing two methyls) becomes a ketone: two acetone. No aldehyde/acid possible since both carbons are fully substituted.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Tetrasubstituted symmetric alkene = 2x acetone regardless of workup. Acid/aldehyde options are impossible (good conceptual distractors); diol is OsO4/cold KMnO4 product."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: (E)-1-phenylpropene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CCC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)O.O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the internal C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the methyl-bearing carbon becomes acetaldehyde.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 1-phenylpropene reductive ozonolysis = acetaldehyde + benzaldehyde. E/Z geometry is irrelevant to ozonolysis products so difficulty 3 is generous, but the formaldehyde trap and acetophenone/propanal distractors are valid. Keep."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with ozone (O3), followed by a reductive workup with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). Which TWO carbonyl compounds are formed?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["Acetone and carbon dioxide","2-methylbutanal only","Acetone and acetic acid","Acetone and acetaldehyde","Acetic acid and acetaldehyde"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"2-methyl-2-butene cleaves at C=C. The C(CH3)2 carbon (no H) becomes acetone (ketone); the CH carbon (one H) becomes acetaldehyde under reductive Me2S workup, which stops at carbonyls (no acid).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right, reductive workup gives ketone+aldehyde, distractors plausible-but-wrong (acid would need oxidative). In scope, visual."},{"q":"A student wants to convert the alkene shown into a single ring-opened keto-ACID (a carboxylic acid and a ketone on the same chain). Which reagent set accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Cold dilute KMnO4, OH-, 0 C","O3, then dimethyl sulfide (Me2S)","O3, then Zn / AcOH (reductive workup)","O3, then H2O2 (oxidative workup)","OsO4, then NaHSO3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"1-methylcyclohexene is cyclic, so cleavage opens the ring to one molecule. The CH carbon becomes a carboxylic acid only under OXIDATIVE workup (H2O2); the methyl-bearing carbon becomes a ketone. Reductive workups give a keto-aldehyde; cold KMnO4 and OsO4/NaHSO3 are syn-dihydroxylations (no cleavage).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Keyed idx 0 right; reductive distractors give keto-aldehyde, dihydroxylation distractors give no cleavage. Exactly one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"The symmetric internal alkene shown is subjected to O3 then Zn/AcOH. What is the organic product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/CC","opts":["One equivalent of hexanedial","Propanal and propanoic acid","Two equivalents of acetone","Two equivalents of propanal","Two equivalents of propanoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"trans-3-hexene is symmetric; cleavage gives two identical fragments. Each sp2 carbon bears one H, so reductive workup (Zn/AcOH) yields two molecules of propanal (CH3CH2CHO). Oxidative workup would instead give propanoic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right, symmetric alkene gives 2x propanal under reductive conditions. Distractors plausible (acid=oxidative, hexanedial=no cleavage). In scope, visual."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. What are the carbon-containing oxidation products?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetophenone and formic acid","Benzoic acid and formaldehyde","Benzoic acid and CO2","Benzaldehyde and CO2","Benzaldehyde and formaldehyde"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Hot concentrated KMnO4 is oxidative cleavage. The CH carbon on the ring becomes benzoic acid; the terminal =CH2 is fully oxidized and lost as CO2. Benzaldehyde/formaldehyde would require reductive ozonolysis, not hot KMnO4.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Keyed idx 0 right (benzoic acid + CO2 from terminal CH2 under hot KMnO4). Distractors are reductive-ozonolysis products. Single answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"The exocyclic alkene shown is treated with O3 followed by dimethyl sulfide. Which statement about the products is correct?","q_smiles":"C=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Cyclohexanone and formaldehyde are produced","Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid and formic acid are produced","Only cyclohexanone is produced","Cyclohexanone and CO2 are produced","Cycloheptanone is produced by ring expansion"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Methylenecyclohexane has a disubstituted ring carbon (becomes cyclohexanone) and a terminal =CH2 (becomes formaldehyde under reductive Me2S workup). The exocyclic double bond means no ring opening. CO2 would require oxidative conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right, exocyclic CH2 -> formaldehyde under Me2S, ring stays intact (cyclohexanone). Distractors plausible-but-wrong. In scope, visual."},{"q":"For the tetrasubstituted alkene shown, how do the products of (i) O3 then Zn/AcOH compare with (ii) O3 then H2O2?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts":["Only the oxidative workup cleaves the alkene","Reductive gives acetaldehyde; oxidative gives acetic acid","Reductive gives a keto-aldehyde; oxidative gives a keto-acid","Reductive gives acetone; oxidative gives acetic acid","Both workups give the same product: two equivalents of acetone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"In 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene neither sp2 carbon bears a hydrogen, so each fragment is a ketone (acetone). With no C-H at the cleavage carbons, oxidative workup has nothing to over-oxidize, so reductive and oxidative workups give the identical product: two acetone molecules.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Keyed idx 0 right; tetrasubstituted alkene (no vinylic H) gives 2x acetone under both workups. Tests the 'no H -> no over-oxidation' insight. Single answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Reaction of the cyclic alkene shown with O3 then Zn/AcOH gives a single dicarbonyl product. Classify the two carbonyl groups in that product.","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","opts":["Two ketones (a diketone)","One ketone and one aldehyde","Two aldehydes (a dialdehyde)","One ketone and one carboxylic acid","Two carboxylic acids (a diacid)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"1,2-dimethylcyclopentene is tetrasubstituted at the C=C (each sp2 carbon carries a methyl and a ring carbon, no H). Ring-opening cleavage places a ketone at both former alkene carbons, giving the open-chain diketone 2,6-heptanedione (C7H12O2). With no vinylic C-H, no aldehyde or acid forms.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Keyed answer (idx 0, two ketones/diketone) is CORRECT and the classification is the actual answer asked. BUT the original why-text mis-names the product as '2,7-octanedione' (C8); the true product of 1,2-dimethylcyclopentene ozonolysis is 2,6-heptanedione (C7). Corrected the rationale; answer/option unaffected, so keep=true."},{"q":"(E)-1-phenylpropene (shown) undergoes O3 followed by H2O2 (oxidative workup). What are the two products?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzaldehyde and acetaldehyde","Benzoic acid and acetic acid","Benzoic acid and CO2","Acetophenone and formic acid","Benzaldehyde and acetic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Each alkene carbon bears one hydrogen. Oxidative workup (H2O2) converts both CH carbons to carboxylic acids: the ring side gives benzoic acid and the methyl side gives acetic acid. Reductive workup would give benzaldehyde + acetaldehyde. Neither carbon is a terminal =CH2, so no CO2 is lost.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right; both internal CH carbons -> acids under H2O2. Distractors are reductive products or wrong CO2/acetophenone. Single answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H3O+ (dimethyl sulfide also works). Because both alkene carbons are part of the ring, cleavage opens the ring to give a single product. Which structure is that product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O","CC(=O)CCCC=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":0,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the endocyclic trisubstituted C=C; the methyl-bearing carbon becomes a ketone and the CH carbon becomes an aldehyde, opening the ring to 6-oxoheptanal (C7H12O2, mass conserved from C7H12). Reductive workup stops at the aldehyde, eliminating the acid choices; opt1 (C6) and opt4 (C6) have the wrong carbon count.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; correct=0 (6-oxoheptanal, C7H12O2, mass conserved). One defensible answer, distractors are wrong oxidation level or carbon count. In scope, visual."},{"q":"trans-3-Hexene is subjected to ozonolysis with an OXIDATIVE workup (O3, then H2O2). What are the organic products?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)O","CCC=O","CCC(=O)CC","CC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":0,"why":"Cleavage of the symmetric internal alkene gives two identical 3-carbon CH fragments; oxidative (H2O2) workup carries each aldehyde on to a carboxylic acid, giving two equivalents of propanoic acid. Propanal is the reductive-workup answer; the E geometry is irrelevant since the sp2 carbons are destroyed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Oxidative workup correctly gives propanoic acid (not propanal). All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with O3 followed by Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which pair of carbonyl compounds is formed?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)O.CC(C)=O","CC=O.CC(=O)C(=O)C","CC(C)=O.CC(C)=O","CC=O.CC(C)=O","CC=O.CC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"The trisubstituted alkene cleaves so the =C(CH3)2 end gives acetone and the =CHCH3 end gives acetaldehyde. Reductive workup keeps the aldehyde (rules out the acetic-acid choice). The two-acetone and two-acetaldehyde choices misassign which carbon carried two methyls.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): acetaldehyde + acetone. All SMILES valid (dot-separated multi-fragment). One defensible answer; distractors are wrong oxidation level or regiochemistry. In scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Pentene (drawn) is heated with concentrated, hot KMnO4. Which statement best describes the products of this oxidative cleavage?","q_smiles":"CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Butanoic acid plus formic acid (HCOOH), with the CH2 surviving as a formic acid","Pentanal plus formaldehyde, since hot KMnO4 stops at the aldehyde stage","2-Pentanone, because KMnO4 oxidizes the alkene with Markovnikov regiochemistry","Pentanedioic acid (glutaric acid) from both carbons becoming -COOH","Butanoic acid plus CO2 and water (the terminal =CH2 is fully oxidized and lost as CO2)"],"correct":4,"why":"Hot/concentrated KMnO4 oxidatively cleaves the alkene: the internal CH becomes butanoic acid while the terminal =CH2 is over-oxidized to CO2. It is not formic acid, not an aldehyde (KMnO4 does not stop there), and no ketone forms.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Text options; q_smiles drawn (1-pentene). Terminal CH2 to CO2 is the standard hot-KMnO4 rule. One defensible answer. In scope, visual stem."},{"q":"Vinylcyclohexane is treated with O3, then dimethyl sulfide (CH3SCH3). Two carbonyl products form; formaldehyde is one of them. Which structure is the OTHER product?","q_smiles":"C=CC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CC1CCCCC1","O=CCC1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","O=C(O)C1CCCCC1","O=CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"The terminal vinyl cleaves: =CH2 leaves as formaldehyde and the ring-attached =CH- becomes cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (C7H12O). DMS is reductive, so the product is an aldehyde, not the acid. The CH2 homolog, residual ring alkene, and alcohol are wrong oxidation level or skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): cyclohexanecarbaldehyde. All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"Cyclopentene undergoes ozonolysis with a reductive (Zn/H3O+) workup. Because the alkene is in the ring, cleavage gives a single acyclic product. Which is it?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC(=O)C1","OC(=O)CCCC(=O)O","O=CCCCCC=O","O=CCCC=O","O=CCCCC=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Endocyclic cleavage of the 5-membered ring (both alkene carbons are CH) opens to pentanedial (glutaraldehyde, C5H8O2). Reductive workup forbids the diacid; butanedial (C4) and hexanedial (C6) have wrong carbon counts; cyclopentanone implies no cleavage.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): glutaraldehyde, C5H8O2, mass conserved from C5H8. All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"In the ozonolysis of styrene (drawn), the alkene first reacts with O3 to form an unstable five-membered initial adduct that then fragments and recombines. Which sequence correctly names the species along the mechanism, in order?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Epoxide -> diol -> molozonide -> carbonyl products","Molozonide (primary ozonide) -> carbonyl oxide + carbonyl fragment -> ozonide (secondary ozonide) -> carbonyl products","Carbocation -> molozonide -> ozonide -> carbonyl products","Ozonide (secondary ozonide) -> molozonide -> carbonyl oxide -> carbonyl products","Carbonyl oxide -> ozonide -> molozonide -> carbonyl products"],"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis: [3+2] of O3 across the alkene gives the molozonide (primary ozonide); it fragments to a carbonyl + carbonyl oxide, which recombine to the secondary ozonide; workup delivers the carbonyls. Other orderings invert the sequence or invoke off-pathway species (epoxide, carbocation).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Recognition-level intermediate naming (molozonide/ozonide), not full arrow-pushing, so within DAT depth though most demanding scope-wise. Text options; q_smiles (styrene) valid. One defensible answer."},{"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclohexene (both alkene carbons are fully substituted, each bearing a methyl) is heated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Predict the cleavage product.","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1=C(C)CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O","O=CC(C)CCCC(C)C=O","CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O"],"correct":2,"why":"Both alkene carbons are tetrasubstituted (no H), so cleavage gives only ketones even under harsh KMnO4. The endocyclic bond opens the ring to 2,7-octanedione (C8H14O2, mass conserved from C8H14). The C7 diketone is one carbon short, the dialdehyde is impossible (no H on the sp2 carbons), and a keto-acid would require an H-bearing alkene carbon.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): 2,7-octanedione, 8 carbons, C8H14O2, mass conserved. All SMILES valid; tetrasubstituted alkene correctly yields two ketones. One defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\ncyclohexene + H2, Pd/C","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCC1","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2, Pd/C) adds H2 across the C=C, reducing cyclohexene to cyclohexane (o0). No hydration (no OH), the alkene is consumed, and the C6 ring skeleton is unchanged (not methylcyclopentane). Verified: all five SMILES parse; keyed structure matches the correct product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Chemistry correct, keyed index 0 (cyclohexane) is right, one defensible answer, in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n2-methyl-2-butene + H2, Pd/C","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)(C)C","CCCCC","CCC(C)CO","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"H2/Pd reduces the trisubstituted alkene to 2-methylbutane (o0, CCC(C)C). No oxygen introduced; skeleton preserved (not rearranged to neopentane or n-pentane). Distractors are wrong-skeleton or spurious-OH. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Correct product 2-methylbutane at index 0; clean single-concept hydrogenation; valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n2-butyne + H2, Lindlar catalyst","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["C/C=C/C","CC#CC","C=CCC","CCCC","C/C=C\\C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar (poisoned Pd) delivers one H2 syn to the alkyne, stopping at the cis (Z) alkene: cis-2-butene. RDKit confirms o0 (C/C=C\\C) is STEREOZ and o1 is E (Na/NH3 product). Lindlar does not over-reduce to butane. Keyed index 0 matches the Z product. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Z-alkene correctly keyed (RDKit-verified STEREOZ); regio+stereo two-concept, in scope.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\nstyrene (vinylbenzene) + H2 (1 equiv), Pd/C","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["C=Cc1ccccc1","C=CC1CCCCC1","CCC1CCCCC1","CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ordinary H2/Pd reduces only the isolated vinyl C=C, not the aromatic ring (which needs high pressure / special catalyst), giving ethylbenzene (o0). Ethylcyclohexane and vinylcyclohexane (ring reduced) and the spurious alcohol are wrong. All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Chemoselective alkene-vs-arene reduction, ethylbenzene correctly keyed at 0; in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n1-hexyne + excess H2, Pd/C","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["CCCCCCO","C=CCCCC","CC#CCCC","CCCCCC","C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Unpoisoned Pd/C with excess H2 fully reduces the terminal alkyne (two equiv H2) to hexane (o0). Lindlar would be needed to stop at 1-hexene; the chain stays linear (no ring), no OH. All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Full reduction to hexane correctly keyed at 0; Lindlar/positional/oxygenation distractors are plausible-but-wrong; in scope.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n3-hexyne + H2, Lindlar catalyst","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["C=CCCCC","CC/C=C/CC","CCCCCC","CC#CCCC","CC/C=C\\CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar does syn addition of one H2 across the internal alkyne, giving cis-3-hexene. RDKit confirms o0 (CC/C=C\\CC) is STEREOZ (cis) and o1 is E (Na/NH3 anti product). Not over-reduced to hexane; skeleton unchanged (not 2-hexyne). Keyed index 0 matches the Z product. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Z-alkene correctly keyed (RDKit STEREOZ); regio+stereo two-concept, in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n4-methylpent-3-en-2-one (mesityl oxide) + H2 (1 equiv), Pd/C","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CC(C)O","CC(O)CC(C)CO","CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(=O)C=C(C)C","CC(C)=CC(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"H2/Pd selectively reduces the C=C and leaves the ketone C=O intact, giving 4-methyl-2-pentanone (o0). Pd/C does not reduce an isolated ketone to an alcohol, so the alcohol/diol options are wrong, and o4 (alkene retained, carbonyl reduced) is backwards. All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Chemoselective enone C=C reduction (ketone retained) correctly keyed at 0; single concept (chemoselectivity), in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with H2 gas in the presence of a Pd/C catalyst. Which classification BEST describes this transformation?","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C","opts":["Electrophilic addition of HX following Markovnikov's rule","Free-radical substitution at the allylic position","Reduction by anti addition of two hydrogen atoms across the pi bond","Oxidation by anti addition of two hydroxyl groups across the pi bond","Reduction by syn (suprafacial) addition of two hydrogen atoms across the pi bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"H2 over a heterogeneous metal catalyst (Pd/C, Pt, Ni) reduces an alkene to an alkane by delivering both H atoms to the same face of the pi bond (syn addition) from the catalyst surface. Distractors (oxidation/anti-dihydroxylation, HX addition, radical substitution, anti H2) are all classic wrong descriptors.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 1-hexene SMILES valid; syn-addition reduction is the single defensible answer; distractors plausible-but-wrong. In scope."},{"q":"You need to convert the internal alkyne shown into the corresponding cis (Z) alkene WITHOUT over-reducing it to the alkane. Which reagent should you choose?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["H2 with PtO2 (Adams' catalyst)","Excess H2 with Raney nickel","H2 with Pd/C","H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline)","Na metal in liquid NH3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned Pd catalyst stops at the alkene and delivers both H syn, giving the cis (Z) alkene. Pd/C, PtO2, and Raney Ni over-reduce to the alkane; Na/NH3 dissolving-metal gives the trans alkene.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 2-butyne SMILES valid; Lindlar is the unique answer for cis selectivity; trans (Na/NH3) and over-reduction distractors are the standard foils. In scope."},{"q":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2/Pd) of the cyclic alkene shown is performed. What is the major organic product of this reaction?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts":["Benzene","Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Cyclohexane","1,2-dibromocyclohexane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"H2/Pd adds two H across the single C=C of cyclohexene to give saturated cyclohexane. No O or halogen is introduced, and benzene (more unsaturated) is not formed by reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyclohexene SMILES valid; cyclohexane is the unambiguous product; distractors are standard wrong functional groups. In scope."},{"q":"The product shown was obtained in high stereoselectivity from the catalytic hydrogenation of 2-butyne. Which set of reagents/conditions produced THIS specific geometric isomer?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["HBr, then KOtBu","H2, Lindlar's catalyst","Na, liquid NH3","H2, Pd/C (excess)","H2, Raney nickel"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The drawn product is cis-2-butene (Z, confirmed STEREOZ). Syn addition of one H2 to the alkyne with Lindlar's catalyst gives the cis alkene. Na/NH3 would give trans; Pd/C or Raney Ni would over-reduce to butane.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles C/C=C\\C confirmed Z (cis-2-butene); Lindlar is the unique reagent set giving this isomer. In scope and visual."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is hydrogenated with one equivalent of H2 over Pd/C under mild conditions. Which statement correctly describes the chemoselectivity of this reaction?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Both the vinyl group and the aromatic ring are reduced, giving ethylcyclohexane","Only the vinyl C=C is reduced; the aromatic ring is untouched, giving ethylbenzene","Only the aromatic ring is reduced; the vinyl group is untouched","The vinyl group is converted to an alcohol via Markovnikov addition","No reaction occurs because conjugation prevents hydrogenation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Isolated/vinyl alkenes reduce readily with H2/Pd under mild conditions, but the aromatic ring needs forcing conditions (high P/T, Rh/Pt). Styrene gives ethylbenzene with the ring intact.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Styrene SMILES valid; chemoselectivity (alkene over arene) is the single defensible answer; layered concept (reactivity + chemoselectivity) is within DAT depth."},{"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclopentene (shown) undergoes catalytic hydrogenation with H2/Pt. Because the reaction proceeds by syn addition of H2, which stereochemical descriptor applies to the 1,2-dimethylcyclopentane product?","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","opts":["A racemic mixture of two enantiomers with no defined cis/trans relationship","trans (the two methyl groups end up on opposite faces of the ring)","The product is achiral and planar, so no descriptor applies","cis (the two methyl groups end up on the same face of the ring)","An equal mixture of cis and trans because the catalyst is not stereospecific"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Both H atoms add to the same face (syn). The two ring-bound methyls on C1/C2 end up on the same face relative to each other, giving cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane (a meso compound). Classic textbook syn-addition stereochemistry result.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. SMILES = 1,2-dimethylcyclopentene (C7H12) valid; syn addition gives cis product; two-concept (mechanism stereo + ring descriptor) within scope. Single defensible answer."},{"q":"A chemist runs a competitive hydrogenation comparing the relative rates of H2/Pd uptake for the three alkenes below. Based on alkene substitution, which ranks them CORRECTLY from FASTEST to SLOWEST hydrogenation? I = monosubstituted (1-hexene), II = disubstituted (cyclohexene), III = tetrasubstituted (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene).","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C.C1CCC=CC1.CC(C)=C(C)C","opts":["I > II > III","II > I > III","III > I > II","III > II > I","I > III > II"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Heterogeneous hydrogenation rate decreases with increasing alkene substitution (more steric hindrance to flat adsorption on the catalyst; also slightly greater thermodynamic stability). Least-substituted reacts fastest: monosubstituted (I) > disubstituted (II) > tetrasubstituted (III).","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. All three SMILES valid and match labels (1-hexene C6H12 mono, cyclohexene C6H10 di, 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene C6H12 tetra). Ranking I>II>III is the textbook result; single defensible answer. In scope."},{"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclohexene is treated with H2 over Pd/C. Considering that the two hydrogen atoms add to the same face of the double bond, which compound is the product?","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1C","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1C","CCCCCC(C)C","CC1CCCCC1C","C=C1CCCCC1C"],"correct":0,"why":"Syn (same-face) hydrogenation of 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene puts both methyls on the same face = cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane. RDKit assigns opt0 (S,R) and 3D embedding confirms both methyls on the SAME face (cis, meso). opt1 is (S,S)=trans (anti addition, wrong); opt2 omits stereochem; opt3 is methylenecyclohexane; opt4 is a ring-opened chain. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All 6 SMILES valid; opt0 confirmed cis/meso by 3D; single right answer; in DAT scope (syn-addition cis/trans recognition)."},{"q":"2-Butyne is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C\\C","C=C(C)C","C/C=C/C","C=CCC","CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Lindlar = poisoned Pd, syn addition stops at the cis (Z) alkene: 2-butyne -> cis-2-butene. RDKit: opt0 = STEREOZ (correct), opt1 = STEREOE (Na/NH3 product, trap), opt2 butane = over-reduction, opt3 1-butene and opt4 isobutylene have wrong constitution. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; Z/E correctly assigned; canonical Lindlar item; in scope."},{"q":"trans-Benzalacetone (4-phenyl-3-buten-2-one) is stirred with 1 equivalent of H2 over Pd/C. Which is the product, given that Pd/C reduces the isolated C=C of an enone but leaves the ketone C=O and the aromatic ring intact under these mild conditions?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Chemoselective H2/Pd reduces the conjugated C=C but not the ketone or arene -> 4-phenyl-2-butanone (opt0). opt1 (allylic alcohol, C=O reduced) wrong; opt2 reduces both; opt3 saturates the ring; opt4 is an enol (not isolable). All 5 SMILES parse. One defensible answer; the question itself supplies the chemoselectivity rule, keeping it RECOGNITION-level.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (incl. opt3 cyclohexyl ketone and opt5 enol); chemoselectivity standard; one right answer; in scope."},{"q":"trans-Cinnamaldehyde is treated with H2 over Pd/C. Recalling that Pd/C reduces alkene C=C bonds but not aldehyde C=O bonds or aromatic rings, which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CCCC1CCCCC1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","OC/C=C/c1ccccc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"H2/Pd saturates the C=C only -> 3-phenylpropanal (opt0). opt1 cinnamyl alcohol (C=O reduced) wrong; opt2 fully reduced; opt3 arene saturated; opt4 ethylbenzene loses a carbon (impossible). All SMILES valid. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; canonical chemoselective hydrogenation; single answer; in scope."},{"q":"1-Phenyl-1-propyne (an internal alkyne) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which alkene is formed?","q_smiles":"CC#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar syn-reduces the internal alkyne to the cis (Z) alkene: cis-1-phenylpropene (opt0, STEREOZ). opt1 = trans (Na/NH3, trap); opt2 propylbenzene = over-reduction; opt3 styrene wrong skeleton; opt4 saturates the ring. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; Z/E correctly assigned; standard Lindlar item; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with D2 (deuterium gas) over Pd. This experiment is used to demonstrate the stereochemistry of catalytic hydrogenation. Which product shows that the two deuterium atoms added with the expected stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[2H]C1([2H])CCCC1","[2H][C@@H]1CCC[C@H]1[2H]","[2H][C@@H]1CCC[C@@H]1[2H]","[2H]C1CCC([2H])C1","[2H]C1CCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Syn addition of D2/Pd delivers both D to the same face -> cis-1,2-dideuteriocyclopentane (opt0), confirmed CIS by 3D embedding. opt1 is trans (anti, wrong mechanism); opt2 geminal (both D on one C) and opt3 mono-D violate one-D-per-alkene-carbon; opt4 puts D in a 1,3-pattern (wrong carbons). One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; opt0 confirmed cis by 3D; classic D2 syn-addition demonstration; single answer; in scope."},{"q":"You need to convert 1-heptyne into 1-heptene (the terminal alkene) without over-reducing it to heptane. Which set of reagents accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Na in liquid NH3","H2 over PtO2 (Adams' catalyst)","H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3, Pb, quinoline)","BH3 then H2O2/NaOH","Excess H2 over Pd/C"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar (poisoned Pd) stops at the alkene: 1-heptyne -> 1-heptene (opt0). Excess H2/Pd-C (opt1) and H2/PtO2 (opt3) over-reduce to heptane. Na/NH3 (opt2) gives trans alkenes from INTERNAL alkynes only - a terminal alkyne is just deprotonated, no reduction. Hydroboration-oxidation (opt4) adds water to give a carbonyl. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid; reagent-selection (text opts, opts_are_structures=false) with molecule drawn in stem; standard reagent logic; one right answer; in scope."},{"q":"Propyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CC","opts":["CCC","CC(Br)(Br)C","C=C(Br)C","CC#C","CC=CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov addition of HBr to terminal alkyne: H to terminal C1, Br to internal C2, giving 2-bromopropene (C=C(Br)C, opt0). Verified by RDKit: opt0 canon C=C(C)Br. opt1 = anti-Markovnikov, opt2 = gem-dibromide (2-equiv), opt3 = unreacted SM, opt4 = over-reduced. All 5 options valid and distinct; exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Correct, single answer, in scope, valid/distinct visual SMILES."},{"q":"2-Butyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["CC(Br)=CC","CC(Br)C(Br)C","C/C(Br)=C(\\Br)C","CC(Br)(Br)C(C)(Br)Br","C/C(Br)=C(/Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds anti across the internal alkyne via bromonium intermediate -> two Br end up TRANS = (E)-2,3-dibromo-2-butene. Verified: opt0 = CIP E and 3D test shows Br-Br cos=-1.00 (trans), so opt0 is correct anti-addition product. opt1 = Z (cis Br, syn product), opt2/opt3 = over-addition (2-equiv) saturated dibromide/tetrabromide, opt4 = wrong (monobromo alkene, mass-balance impossible). One concept layered = regio-N/A + stereo, in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Anti-addition stereochem verified: keyed E (Br trans) is correct."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HCl. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts":["CC=C(Cl)CC","C=C(Cl)CCC","CCC=C(C)Cl","CCCC=CCl","CCCC(Cl)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov addition of HCl to terminal alkyne: Cl to internal C2 -> 2-chloro-1-pentene (C=C(Cl)CCC, opt0). Verified valid and distinct. opt1 = anti-Markovnikov vinyl chloride, opt2/opt4 = wrong-regiochem internal vinyl chlorides, opt3 = saturated chloride (wrong unsaturation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Correct, single answer, in scope, valid visual SMILES."},{"q":"Acetylene (ethyne) is treated with 2 equivalents of HCl. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#C","opts":["C=CCl","CC(Cl)Cl","ClCC(Cl)Cl","ClCCCl","ClC(Cl)C(Cl)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"First HCl -> vinyl chloride; second HCl Markovnikov (Cl-stabilized cation) puts both Cl geminal -> 1,1-dichloroethane (CC(Cl)Cl, opt0). Verified valid/distinct. opt1 = vicinal isomer (wrong regio), opt2/opt3 = over-chlorinated, opt4 = 1-equiv intermediate. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Markovnikov x2 gem-dichloride correct."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["C#Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCc1ccccc1","C=C(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","BrC=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov addition; Br to benzylic carbon (resonance-stabilized vinyl cation) -> (1-bromovinyl)benzene (C=C(Br)c1ccccc1, opt0). Verified valid/distinct. opt1 = anti-Markovnikov, opt2 = gem-dibromide, opt3 = ring bromination (wrong site), opt4 = over-reduction. One correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Benzylic Markovnikov regiochem correct."},{"q":"4-Octyne (a symmetric internal alkyne) is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC#CCCC","opts":["CCCC(Br)C(Br)CCC","CCC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CCC","CCC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CCC","CCCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)(Br)CCC","CCCC=C(Br)CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Symmetric alkyne removes regiochem; anti addition of Br2 -> Br trans = (E)-4,5-dibromo-4-octene (opt0). Verified: opt0 = CIP E, 3D test Br-Br cos=-1.00 (trans). opt1 = Z (syn/cis), opt2/opt3 = over-addition di/tetrabromide, opt4 = mass-balance-impossible monobromo alkene. One correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Anti-addition stereochem verified: keyed E (Br trans) correct."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["C=C(Br)CCCC","CCCC=C(C)Br","CCCCC(Br)C","CCCCC=CBr","CCC=C(Br)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Ionic HBr (no ROOR) is Markovnikov: Br to C2 -> 2-bromo-1-hexene (C=C(Br)CCCC, opt0). Verified valid/distinct. opt1 = anti-Markovnikov (peroxide regiochem, not operative here), opt2/opt3 = wrong-skeleton internal bromides, opt4 = saturated. Chemistry correct and unambiguous, but difficulty=3 is overrated: this is a plain single-step Markovnikov addition identical in concept to Q0/Q3 (difficulty 1). Keeping; flagging difficulty.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Chemistry correct/single answer; difficulty overrated (labeled 3, actually single-concept Markovnikov = 1). Corrected difficulty to 1."},{"q":"The terminal alkyne shown (1-butyne) is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which statement best describes the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts":["A geminal dibromide on C2","A vicinal dibromide across C1 and C2","A vinyl bromide with Br on C2 (the more substituted carbon), formed by Markovnikov addition","2-butanone, via Markovnikov hydration","A vinyl bromide with Br on C1 (the terminal carbon), formed by anti-Markovnikov addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov HX addition to 1-butyne: H to terminal CH gives the more stable secondary vinyl cation, Br lands on internal C2 -> 2-bromo-1-butene. One equiv stops at the vinyl halide; hydration needs water/HgSO4. Index 0 correct, distractors plausible.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCC). Markovnikov regiochemistry correct, one defensible answer, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Starting from the alkyne shown (propyne), which reagent conditions would convert it to 2,2-dibromopropane (a geminal dibromide)?","q_smiles":"C#CC","opts":["1 equivalent of HBr","Br2 in water (bromohydrin conditions)","2 equivalents of HBr","1 equivalent of Br2","2 equivalents of Br2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Double Markovnikov HBr addition to a terminal alkyne gives a gem-dihalide on C2: first gives 2-bromopropene, second adds with both Br on C2 -> 2,2-dibromopropane. Br2 gives vicinal/tetra products. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CC). gem-dihalide from 2 eq HX is standard; one defensible answer."},{"q":"You want to convert the terminal alkyne shown (1-pentyne) into a methyl ketone (2-pentanone). Which reagent set accomplishes this Markovnikov hydration?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts":["1. R2BH (disiamylborane); 2. H2O2, NaOH","O3, then Zn/H2O","H2O, H2SO4 only (no catalyst)","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","2 equivalents of HCl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Acid/HgSO4 hydration is Markovnikov: terminal alkyne -> enol -> 2-pentanone (methyl ketone). Hydroboration/oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (aldehyde); HCl gives gem-dichloride; ozonolysis cleaves. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCCC). Mercuric-catalyzed Markovnikov hydration correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"The internal alkyne shown (2-butyne) is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. Which best classifies this reaction and its stereochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["Markovnikov addition giving a geminal dibromide","Syn (cis) addition of Br2, giving the (Z)-2,3-dibromo-2-butene","Free-radical substitution at the methyl groups","Anti (trans) addition of Br2 across the triple bond, giving the (E)-2,3-dibromo-2-butene vinyl dibromide","Electrophilic addition with no defined stereochemistry because the alkyne is symmetric"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Br2 adds anti via a bromonium-like intermediate, placing the two Br trans. Verified by RDKit CIP labeler: Br-trans product on CH3CBr=CBrCH3 carries CIP label E. So (E)-2,3-dibromo-2-butene by anti addition is correct. Index 0.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (CC#CC). E-label of anti product confirmed by RDKit (Br>CH3, Br trans => E); regio+stereo single layered concept, in scope."},{"q":"The compound 2,2-dichlorohexane was prepared from a six-carbon alkyne. Which alkyne and reagent combination is the correct precursor?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["1-hexyne + 1 equivalent of HCl","1-hexyne + 2 equivalents of Cl2","1-hexyne + 2 equivalents of HCl","3-hexyne + 2 equivalents of HCl","2-hexyne + 1 equivalent of HCl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"gem-dichloride at C2 comes from double Markovnikov HCl on 1-hexyne (2-chloro-1-hexene then both Cl on C2). Cl2 gives vicinal/tetra; internal alkynes cannot place both Cl on C2. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCCCC, 1-hexyne). Retrosynthetic gem-dihalide reasoning correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (shown) is treated with 1 equivalent of HCl. Which carbon bears the chlorine in the major vinyl chloride product, and why?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["The internal (benzylic) carbon, because that vinyl cation is resonance-stabilized by the ring (Markovnikov)","Both carbons equally, giving a 1:1 mixture","The internal carbon, but only after a hydride shift","The terminal carbon, because of anti-Markovnikov addition","Neither carbon; HCl does not add to aryl-conjugated alkynes"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Protonation at the terminal carbon gives a ring-stabilized benzylic vinyl cation, so Cl ends up on the internal benzylic carbon -> (1-chlorovinyl)benzene. No hydride shift needed; regioselective, not 1:1. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#Cc1ccccc1). Benzylic vinyl-cation stabilization reinforces Markovnikov; one defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Consider electrophilic addition of HBr to alkynes. Rank the relative rate of the FIRST HBr addition for these three substrates: 1-butyne (shown), 2-butyne, and acetylene (HC#CH).","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts":["1-butyne > 2-butyne > acetylene","all three react at essentially the same rate","acetylene > 1-butyne > 2-butyne","2-butyne > acetylene > 1-butyne","2-butyne > 1-butyne > acetylene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Rate tracks vinyl-cation stability, raised by alkyl donation. Dialkyl 2-butyne fastest, monoalkyl 1-butyne intermediate, unsubstituted acetylene slowest: 2-butyne > 1-butyne > acetylene. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCC). Standard substitution-stabilizes-cation ranking; ranking item with molecule drawn, opts text. One defensible order."},{"q":"The symmetrical internal alkyne 3-hexyne (shown) is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2 in CH2Cl2. Which single product is formed, and by what stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["3,4-dibromo-3-hexene as an inseparable E/Z mixture because the alkyne is symmetric","(E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene, by anti addition through a bromonium-ion-like intermediate","a racemic mixture of 3,4-dibromohexane (full saturation)","3,3-dibromohexane, a geminal dibromide","(Z)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene, by syn addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Br2 adds anti via bromonium-like intermediate -> Br trans. RDKit CIP labeler confirms Br-trans CH3CH2CBr=CBrCH2CH3 is E. Single stereodefined product (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene; 1 equiv so no further bromination; stereospecific (not random E/Z), not gem. Index 0.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (CCC#CCC, 3-hexyne). E-label of anti product confirmed by RDKit; stereospecific anti addition correct, one defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"1-Butyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts":["CC/C(Br)=C","CC/C=C\\Br","CCC(Br)C","CC/C=C/Br","C=CCCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov HBr addition to a terminal alkyne: H to terminal C1, Br to C2 (more stabilized vinyl cation), giving 2-bromobut-1-ene. RDKit canon of opt0 = C=C(Br)CC; terminal =CH2 has no real E/Z so the spurious / is harmless. Distractors are E/Z anti-Markovnikov 1-bromo isomers, the saturated 2-bromobutane (2 equiv), and a wrong 4-bromobutene skeleton. Exactly one Markovnikov mono-add answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; spurious / on terminal =CH2 is cosmetic. In scope, one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Butyne reacts with 1 equivalent of HCl. Halogen acids add to alkynes with anti addition of H and Cl. Which structure correctly shows the major vinyl chloride product, including its geometry?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["C/C(Cl)=C/C","C/C(Cl)=C\\C","CCC(Cl)C","CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","CC(Cl)CCl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Stem stipulates anti addition: H (to C3) and Cl (to C2) end up trans, so Cl is cis to the C3 methyl. CIP: C2 Cl>CH3, C3 CH3>H; the two high-priority groups (Cl and C3-CH3) on the same side => Z. RDKit confirms opt0 is STEREOZ, opt1 STEREOE (syn, wrong). Remaining options are saturated/over- or under-chlorinated skeletons. Anti HCl is a textbook simplification but the stem makes it an explicit premise, so the item is self-consistent.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct (index 0 = Z). RDKit-confirmed Z/E. Anti-HCl stipulated by stem so self-consistent; regio+stereo, in scope."},{"q":"Propyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts":["CC(Br)CBr","C=C(Br)C","BrCCCBr","CC(Br)(Br)CBr","CC(Br)(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Both HBr add Markovnikov; both Br end up on C2 (most stabilized cation), giving the geminal dihalide 2,2-dibromopropane (opt0). Distractors are the vicinal 1,2-dibromide, a tribromide, 1,3-dibromopropane, and the mono-addition 2-bromopropene. Exactly one answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. Halogen addition to an alkyne proceeds with anti stereochemistry. Which structure is the major product, including its geometry?","q_smiles":"CCCC#C","opts":["CCC/C(Br)=C/Br","CCCC(Br)(Br)CBr","CCCC(Br)CBr","CCC(Br)C(Br)C","CCC/C(Br)=C\\Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti, placing the two new C-Br bonds trans on the resulting alkene. CIP: C1 Br>H, C2 Br>propyl; both high-priority Br trans => E. RDKit confirms opt0 STEREOE, opt1 STEREOZ (syn, wrong). Others reflect a second Br2 equiv or wrong skeletons. Br2/alkyne anti addition is genuinely stereospecific, so this is real chemistry.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct (index 0 = E), RDKit-confirmed. Regio+stereo, in scope, one answer."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCC(Cl)CCl","CCCCC(Cl)(Cl)C","ClCCCCCCCl","CCCCC(Cl)(Cl)CCl","CCCC(Cl)(Cl)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Both HCl add Markovnikov; both Cl go to C2, giving the geminal dichloride 2,2-dichlorohexane (opt0). Distractors are the vicinal 1,2-dichloride, a trichloride, 1,6-dichlorohexane, and the 3,3-isomer. One answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["BrC=Cc1ccccc1","BrC/C=C/c1ccccc1","C=C(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov: H to terminal C, Br to the internal benzylic carbon, which gives the most stabilized (ring-conjugated) vinyl cation, yielding alpha-bromostyrene (opt0). Distractors: beta-bromostyrene (anti-Markovnikov), a cinnamyl-type wrong skeleton, the saturated benzylic bromide (2 equiv), and ring bromination (wrong reaction). One answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2, which adds anti across the triple bond. Which structure is the major product, including geometry?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)CC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CCC(Br)C(Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Anti Br2 addition places the two bromines trans. On each internal carbon Br>ethyl, so trans-Br => E. RDKit confirms opt0 STEREOE, opt1 STEREOZ (syn, wrong). Others are over-brominated or saturated wrong skeletons. (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene is the answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct (index 0 = E), RDKit-confirmed. Regio trivial (symmetric), stereo concept, in scope, one answer."},{"q":"4-Methyl-1-pentyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which structure correctly represents the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC#C","opts":["CC(C)(Br)CC=C","CC(C)CC(Br)C","CC(C)CC(Br)(Br)C","CC(C)C/C(Br)=C","CC(C)C/C=C/Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov on the terminal alkyne: H to C1, Br to C2, giving 2-bromo-4-methylpent-1-ene (opt0, RDKit C=C(Br)CC(C)C). The isobutyl branch is inert and does not add a second concept. Distractors: terminal vinyl bromide (anti-Markovnikov), saturated bromide, a rearranged tertiary-bromide wrong skeleton, and the gem-dibromide (2 equiv). One answer. Difficulty is really 1-2, not 3, but that is a tagging nuance, not a defect.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid. Difficulty over-tagged as 3 (true single-concept Markovnikov, branch inert); kept since chemistry is sound."},{"q":"Predict the major product. CH3-C#CH (propyne) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4 (catalytic). Choose the structure of the major organic product.","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)O","CC(C)=O","CC(O)CO","CCC(=O)O","CCC=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Hg(II)/acid hydration is Markovnikov: OH adds to the more substituted carbon (C2), giving an enol that tautomerizes to acetone (a methyl ketone). Propanal is the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde (hydroboration); the enol is only the intermediate; the diol and carboxylic acid are wrong reactions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer acetone (CC(C)=O) is the correct Markovnikov ketone; exactly one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 1-Hexyne (CH3CH2CH2CH2-C#CH) is treated with dilute H2SO4, H2O, and HgSO4. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(O)CO","CCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCC=O","C=C(O)CCCC","CCCC(=O)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov (Hg/acid) hydration of a terminal alkyne puts the carbonyl on C2, giving 2-hexanone. Hexanal is the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde (hydroboration); 3-hexanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol (opt 3, the true tautomer of 2-hexanone) is the intermediate; the diol is a wrong reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 2-hexanone correct; enol distractor verified as genuine tautomer (C6H12O); one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 1-Pentyne (CH3CH2CH2-C#CH) is treated with (1) BH3.THF (or a disiamylborane), then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"CCCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)CC","CCCCC=O","CCCCC(=O)O","CCCC=CO","CCCC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron then OH ends up on the terminal carbon, giving an enol that tautomerizes to pentanal (aldehyde). 2-Pentanone is the Markovnikov methyl ketone; 3-pentanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the intermediate; the acid is over-oxidation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer pentanal correct (anti-Markovnikov aldehyde); one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. Phenylacetylene (Ph-C#CH) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1OC1c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov Hg/acid hydration places OH on the more substituted benzylic carbon, giving acetophenone (a methyl ketone). Phenylacetaldehyde is the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde (hydroboration); benzaldehyde loses a carbon; the epoxide and benzoic acid are unrelated reactions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid (styrene oxide distractor parses); keyed answer acetophenone correct; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. Phenylacetylene (Ph-C#CH) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (a hindered borane), then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, placing the carbonyl on the terminal carbon to give phenylacetaldehyde (PhCH2CHO). Acetophenone is the Markovnikov methyl ketone (Hg/acid); benzaldehyde loses a carbon; the enol is the intermediate; benzoic acid is over-oxidation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer phenylacetaldehyde correct (anti-Markovnikov); one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 4-Methyl-1-pentyne ((CH3)2CHCH2-C#CH) undergoes hydroboration-oxidation: (1) BH3.THF, (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"C#CCC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)C(C)C","CC(C)C=O","CC(C)CC=CO","CC(=O)CC(C)C","O=CCCC(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration of the terminal alkyne gives the aldehyde at the terminal carbon: 4-methylpentanal, with the isopropyl branch untouched. The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; a short branched aldehyde loses carbons; the enol is the intermediate; no carbocation rearrangement occurs.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 4-methylpentanal correct; two-concept (regio + branch retention) still in DAT scope; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 2-Butyne (CH3-C#C-CH3) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. Because the alkyne is internal and symmetrical, decide the single major product.","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)CC","CC=C(C)O","CC(O)C(C)O","CCC(C)=O","CCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Internal alkynes give ketones (no terminal carbon, so no aldehyde). Symmetrical 2-butyne gives the same product from either orientation: 2-butanone. Butanal is impossible (needs a terminal alkyne); the diol is a wrong reaction; the enol is the tautomer intermediate; 3-pentanone (opt 4) has an extra carbon.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 2-butanone (C4H8O) correct; 3-pentanone distractor confirmed as 5-carbon mismatch; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 3-Hexyne (CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH3) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: (1) BH3.THF, (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the single major product and note why an aldehyde is not an option here.","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)C(=O)CC","CCCCCC=O","CCC=C(O)CC","CCCCC(C)=O","CCCC(=O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"3-Hexyne is internal and symmetrical, so hydration (by either method) can only give a ketone, and both orientations converge on 3-hexanone. Hydroboration's anti-Markovnikov selectivity is irrelevant for a symmetrical internal alkyne. An aldehyde (hexanal) is impossible without a terminal C-H; 2-hexanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the intermediate; the 1,2-diketone is over-oxidation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 3-hexanone (C6H12O) correct; enol distractor verified as true tautomer; one defensible answer; in scope (difficulty=3 is a touch generous for a symmetry-override item but acceptable)."},{"q":"The terminal alkyne shown is treated with H2O, dilute H2SO4, and HgSO4. Which reagent set would instead convert this same alkyne into the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["1. Na, NH3 (l), 2. H2O","HBr (excess), peroxides","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S","1. Disiamylborane (or BH3), 2. H2O2 / NaOH"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Hg2+/H3O+ on a terminal alkyne gives the Markovnikov methyl ketone. The anti-Markovnikov aldehyde from a terminal alkyne is made by hydroboration-oxidation with a hindered borane (disiamylborane/9-BBN) then H2O2/NaOH, placing the carbonyl on the terminal carbon. Ozonolysis, dissolving-metal reduction, and radical HBr are all unrelated. Only option 0 is a valid anti-Markovnikov hydration.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles 1-hexyne valid; exactly one defensible answer. The parenthetical '(or BH3)' is slightly loose since BH3 can add twice to terminal alkynes, but disiamylborane is named and the answer is still the only anti-Markovnikov route. Keep."},{"q":"1-Octyne is subjected to: (1) disiamylborane, then (2) H2O2, NaOH. What is the organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCCC(=O)CC","CCCCCCCC=O","CCCCCCCO","CCCCCCC(O)=C","CCCCCCC(=O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal carbon, and after oxidation/tautomerization the carbonyl forms there, giving octanal (CCCCCCCC=O). 2-Octanone is the Markovnikov product; 3-octanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the non-tautomerized intermediate; octanol is a wrong-formula over-reduction distractor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = octanal). All 5 option SMILES valid and distinct; distractors differ by regio/tautomer/over-reduction. q_smiles is 1-octyne (C8). Keep."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (shown) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. Identify the major product.","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Mercuric-catalyzed Markovnikov hydration adds OH to the more substituted (internal, benzylic) sp carbon; the enol tautomerizes to acetophenone (CC(=O)c1ccccc1), a methyl ketone. Phenylacetaldehyde is the anti-Markovnikov hydroboration product; the others are wrong oxidation states or the non-tautomerized enol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = acetophenone). All option SMILES valid and distinct. Classic Markovnikov benzylic case; one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"A chemist wants to make hexanal from 1-hexyne (shown). Which single statement correctly classifies the transformation and the reagents required?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["Anti-Markovnikov hydration; use (1) disiamylborane (2) H2O2/NaOH","Oxidative cleavage; use (1) O3 (2) Zn","Markovnikov hydration; use (1) BH3 (2) H2O2/NaOH","Markovnikov hydration; use H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","Anti-Markovnikov hydration; use H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Hexanal puts the carbonyl on the terminal carbon = anti-Markovnikov, achieved by hydroboration-oxidation (disiamylborane then H2O2/NaOH). Options 2 and 3 mismatch classification with reagents (Hg2+ is Markovnikov; borane is anti-Markovnikov), and ozonolysis is cleavage, not hydration. Only option 0 pairs the right name with the right reagents.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles 1-hexyne valid. Distractors are deliberate name/reagent mismatches; exactly one self-consistent answer. Keep."},{"q":"The internal, symmetrical alkyne 3-hexyne (shown) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. What is the result, and how many carbonyl products form?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["No reaction occurs with internal alkynes","A single aldehyde, because hydration is anti-Markovnikov","A single ketone, 2-hexanone, by Markovnikov addition","A single ketone, 3-hexanone, because both alkyne carbons are equivalent","A mixture of two regioisomeric ketones"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"3-Hexyne is symmetric, so both sp carbons are equivalent; hydration gives one enol regardless of which carbon is hydrated, tautomerizing to a single ketone, 3-hexanone. No regioisomeric mixture (would require an unsymmetrical internal alkyne), no aldehyde (needs a terminal alkyne), and internal alkynes do react. 2-Hexanone is structurally impossible from this symmetric substrate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles 3-hexyne valid and symmetric. Symmetry-recognition concept, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Two reactions are run. Reaction A: 1-pentyne + H2O/H2SO4/HgSO4. Reaction B: 1-pentyne + (1) disiamylborane (2) H2O2/NaOH. Which statement about the products is correct?","q_smiles":"CCCC#C","opts":["A gives pentanal; B gives 2-pentanone","Both A and B give pentanal","A gives 3-pentanone; B gives pentanal","Both A and B give 2-pentanone","A gives 2-pentanone (a methyl ketone); B gives pentanal (an aldehyde)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Reaction A (Hg2+/H3O+) is Markovnikov, putting the carbonyl on the internal carbon = 2-pentanone. Reaction B (hydroboration-oxidation) is anti-Markovnikov, putting the carbonyl on the terminal carbon = pentanal. Option 1 reverses them; 3-pentanone would need an internal alkyne; the 'both' options ignore the regiochemical contrast.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Two-concept (Markovnikov vs anti-Markovnikov contrast) within scope. q_smiles 1-pentyne valid. One defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (shown) is converted to a product whose IR shows a strong carbonyl absorption near 1715 cm-1 and whose 1H NMR shows a 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm but NO aldehydic proton near 9-10 ppm. Which reagents produce this compound?","q_smiles":"C#CC1CCCCC1","opts":["H2, Lindlar catalyst","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. Disiamylborane, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. BH3, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm plus a carbonyl band and no aldehyde proton identifies a methyl ketone (CH3-C=O), i.e. 1-cyclohexylethanone, which is the Markovnikov hydration product made with Hg2+/H3O+. Hydroboration (options 2,3) would give an aldehyde with a 9-10 ppm CHO; ozonolysis cleaves; Lindlar only gives an alkene.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Spectroscopy is qualitative region-recognition (carbonyl band, methyl singlet, absent CHO) used only to distinguish ketone vs aldehyde, not J-coupling/exact-shift math, so within DAT scope. q_smiles valid. One defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"1-Hexyne (drawn) is treated with HgSO4, dilute H2SO4, and H2O. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCCC=O","CCCC(=O)CC","CCCC=C(C)O","CCCCCCO","CCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hg(II)/H3O+ hydration of a terminal alkyne is Markovnikov: water adds to the more substituted (internal) carbon, giving an enol that tautomerizes to a methyl ketone (2-hexanone), not the aldehyde. Hexanal is the anti-Markovnikov product; 3-hexanone has wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the un-tautomerized intermediate; 1-hexanol is simple alkene-style hydration thinking.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: 1-hexyne + Hg/H2SO4 = Markovnikov, gives 2-hexanone (opt0). All 5 SMILES valid; distractors are anti-Markov aldehyde, wrong-regio ketone, enol intermediate, and alkene-hydration alcohol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Octyne (drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCCCCC=O","C=C(O)CCCCCC","CCCCCC(=O)CC","CCCCCCCCO","CCCCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal carbon, and after oxidation the enol tautomerizes to an aldehyde (octanal). 2-Octanone is the Markovnikov (Hg-catalyzed) product; 3-octanone is wrong regiochemistry; 1-octanol comes from over-reduction/alkene thinking; the enol is the intermediate before tautomerization.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: 1-octyne + BH3/H2O2 = anti-Markovnikov, gives octanal (opt0). All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov methyl ketone, wrong-regio ketone, alcohol, and enol intermediate. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","C=C(O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov (Hg/acid) hydration places the carbonyl on the more substituted carbon (the one bearing the ring), giving the methyl ketone acetophenone. Phenylacetaldehyde is the anti-Markovnikov product; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol is over-reduction; benzoic acid would require oxidative cleavage, which does not occur here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: aryl-conjugated terminal alkyne strongly favors Markovnikov, gives acetophenone (opt0). All SMILES valid; distractors are anti-Markov aldehyde, enol, alcohol, and a no-reaction cleavage product. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (Sia2BH) then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal CH carbon, and oxidation/tautomerization gives the aldehyde phenylacetaldehyde. Acetophenone is the Markovnikov product; 2-phenylethanol is alkene-hydration thinking; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol has the OH on the wrong carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: Sia2BH/H2O2 = anti-Markovnikov, gives phenylacetaldehyde (opt0). Sia2BH is a standard bulky borane for terminal-alkyne hydroboration (sophomore-level). All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov ketone, alcohol, enol, wrong-regio alcohol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"3-Hexyne (a symmetric internal alkyne, drawn) is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["CCC(O)=CCC","CCCCCC=O","CCC(=O)CCC","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(=O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A symmetric internal alkyne gives a single ketone regardless of which carbon is hydrated, because both regiochemical outcomes are identical: 3-hexanone. 2-Hexanone and hexanal cannot form (those would require an unsymmetrical or terminal alkyne); the enol is the intermediate; 3-pentanone has the wrong carbon count.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: symmetric internal alkyne gives single ketone 3-hexanone (opt0). Two-concept (recognize symmetry + apply hydration), correctly labeled difficulty 2. All SMILES valid; distractors are wrong-regio ketone, aldehyde, enol intermediate, and a C5 ketone (wrong count). Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"5-Methyl-1-hexyne (drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CCC#C","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(=O)CCC(C)C","CC(C)CCCCO","CC(C)CCCC=O","CC(C)CCC=CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration-oxidation puts the carbonyl on the terminal carbon, giving the aldehyde 5-methylhexanal; the branch at C5 is a spectator and is unchanged. The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov (Hg) product; the primary alcohol is alkene-style thinking; the shorter ketone has lost a carbon; the enol is the intermediate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: terminal alkyne + BH3/H2O2 = anti-Markovnikov, gives 5-methylhexanal (opt0); isopropyl branch is a spectator. All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov methyl ketone, alcohol, a carbon-short ketone, and enol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"2-Pentyne (drawn), an UNsymmetrical internal alkyne, is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which statement best describes the outcome?","q_smiles":"CC#CCC","opts":["A roughly equal mixture of two regioisomeric ketones (2-pentanone and 3-pentanone) forms, because neither alkyne carbon is strongly favored","A single aldehyde forms because Markovnikov addition to an internal alkyne gives the aldehyde","A carboxylic acid forms by oxidative cleavage of the triple bond","Only 2-pentanone forms, because Markovnikov addition always places the C=O on C2","No reaction occurs, because internal alkynes are unreactive toward Hg(II)/acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"For an unsymmetrical INTERNAL alkyne, the two carbons of the triple bond are similar in substitution, so Markovnikov selectivity is poor and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a mixture of both possible ketones. Internal alkynes give ketones, never aldehydes; the reaction does occur; and no C-C cleavage happens under these conditions.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: unsymmetrical internal alkyne gives a regioisomer mixture (2-pentanone + 3-pentanone) due to poor selectivity (opt0). Classic textbook conceptual point. Text options; substrate drawn in q_smiles (valid). Distractors (aldehyde from internal alkyne, overstated selectivity, no-reaction, cleavage) all clearly wrong. One defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane, drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C#CC1CCCCC1","opts":["OC=CC1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","O=CCC1CCCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"This is a terminal alkyne (C#C-) bearing a cyclohexyl group; hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, so the carbonyl ends up on the terminal carbon, giving cyclohexylacetaldehyde (a two-carbon aldehyde side chain). The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (ring-CHO) has lost a carbon and the wrong skeleton; the alcohol is over-reduction; the enol is the intermediate.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: terminal alkyne, anti-Markovnikov gives cyclohexylacetaldehyde C8H14O (opt0), correct mass balance from C8H12 substrate + H2O. Strong skeleton distractor opt3 (cyclohexanecarbaldehyde C7H12O, carbon-short) is clearly wrong. All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov ketone, alcohol, ring-aldehyde, enol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"2-Hexyne is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with quinoline). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCC","CC#CCCC","C=CCCCC","C/C=C/CCC","C/C=C\\CCC"],"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar delivers H2 syn across the triple bond and stops at the alkene, giving cis (Z) 2-hexene. Trans is the Na/NH3 product; unchanged alkyne, hexane (unpoisoned Pd over-reduction), and a regio-shifted terminal alkene are wrong. RDKit confirms opt[0] is Z (cis) and opt[1] is E (trans); keyed index correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; keyed cis(Z) verified by RDKit as the correct Lindlar product; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Heptyne is treated with Na in liquid NH3. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC#CCCC","CC/C=C\\CCC","CCCCCCC","CC/C=C/CCC","CC#CCCCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Dissolving-metal (Na/NH3) adds H atoms anti via a radical-anion pathway, giving trans (E) 3-heptene. The cis isomer is the Lindlar product; unreacted alkyne, heptane (over-reduction), and a regio-shifted 2-heptyne are wrong. RDKit confirms opt[0] is E (trans).","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. opt[4] (CC#CCCCC) is 2-heptyne, a valid same-formula regio distractor; keyed trans(E) verified correct."},{"q":"1-Butyne is treated with NaNH2, and the resulting acetylide is then treated with bromoethane (CH3CH2Br). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C#CCC","CC#CCCC","CCCC#CCCC","CCC#CCC","C#CCCCC"],"correct":3,"why":"NaNH2 deprotonates the terminal alkyne; the acetylide does SN2 on ethyl bromide, adding -CH2CH3 to the terminal carbon. CH3CH2-C#CH becomes CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH3 = 3-hexyne. Terminal 1-hexyne (wrong carbon), 2-hexyne, 4-octyne (over-alkylation), and unreacted alkyne are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed opt[0] CCC#CCC = 3-hexyne, the correct alkylation product; SMILES all valid."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with NaNH2, and the resulting acetylide is then treated with iodomethane (CH3I). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC#CCCC","C#CCCC","CC#CCC","CCC#CCC","C#CCCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"The terminal acetylide of 1-pentyne (HC#C-CH2CH2CH3) does SN2 on CH3I, installing methyl at the terminal carbon to give CH3-C#C-CH2CH2CH3 = 2-hexyne. 1-Hexyne and 3-hexyne are wrong regiochemistry; 2-pentyne lost a carbon; unreacted 1-pentyne is no reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed opt[0] CC#CCCC = 2-hexyne (C6H10), correct product; distractors valid and distinct."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (ethynylbenzene) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCc1ccccc1","C#CCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C#Cc1ccccc1","C1CCCCC1/C=C"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar reduces the C-C triple bond to an alkene only and leaves the aromatic ring intact, giving styrene (vinylbenzene). Ethylbenzene is the over-reduced product; unreacted alkyne is no reaction; chain-extended and ring-reduced options change the skeleton wrongly.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed opt[0] = styrene; opt[4] parses validly as vinylcyclohexane (ring-reduced distractor). All SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3I; (3) Na, NH3 (l). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCCC","CC/C=C/CCC","CC#CCCCC","C/C=C\\CCCC","C/C=C/CCCC"],"correct":4,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the terminal acetylide with methyl to give 2-heptyne (CH3-C#C-CH2CH2CH2CH3). Step 3 (Na/NH3) reduces it anti to trans (E) 2-heptene. Cis would be the Lindlar outcome; 2-heptyne is incomplete; trans-3-heptene has wrong regiochemistry; heptane is over-reduction. RDKit confirms opt[0] is E.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-concept (alkylation + anti reduction) within scope; keyed trans(E)-2-heptene verified correct."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC/C=C\\CCC","CCCCCCC","CCC#CCCC","CC/C=C/CCC","C/C=C\\CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the terminal acetylide with ethyl: HC#C-CH2CH2CH3 becomes CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH2CH3 (3-heptyne). Step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z) 3-heptene. Trans-3-heptene is the Na/NH3 outcome; 3-heptyne is incomplete; cis-2-heptene has wrong regiochemistry; heptane is over-reduction. RDKit confirms opt[0] is Z.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-concept (alkylation + syn reduction) within scope; keyed cis(Z)-3-heptene verified correct."},{"q":"Diphenylacetylene (1,2-diphenylethyne) is treated with Na in liquid NH3. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["c1ccccc1C#Cc1ccccc1","c1ccccc1/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","c1ccccc1CCc1ccccc1","c1ccccc1/C=C/c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Na/NH3 reduces the internal alkyne by anti addition of two H atoms, stopping at the alkene to give trans (E) stilbene. Cis-stilbene is the Lindlar product; bibenzyl is over-reduction; unreacted alkyne is no reaction; 1,1-diphenylethene is a rearranged skeleton that cannot form by simple reduction. RDKit confirms opt[0] is E.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed trans(E)-stilbene verified; all SMILES valid. Effectively single anti-reduction concept but acceptable as the harder item."},{"q":"The alkyne shown is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd poisoned with lead, quinoline). Which best describes the alkene product that forms?","opts":["cis-2-butene (the Z alkene)","An equal mix of cis- and trans-2-butene","trans-2-butene (the E alkene)","A racemic mixture of 2-butanol","Butane (full reduction to the alkane)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"2-butyne + H2/Lindlar gives syn addition that stops at the alkene, producing cis (Z)-2-butene. Lindlar does not over-reduce, and dissolving-metal would be needed for trans.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CC#CC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid, single concept (syn reduction stereochemistry), one defensible answer."},{"q":"The internal alkyne shown is converted into trans-3-hexene (the E alkene). Which reagent set accomplishes this transformation?","opts":["H2 with Lindlar's catalyst","Na metal in liquid NH3","BH3 then H2O2/NaOH","HBr (1 equivalent)","H2 with Pt catalyst (excess)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Dissolving-metal reduction (Na/NH3) of 3-hexyne adds the two H atoms anti via a radical-anion pathway, giving the trans (E) alkene. Lindlar gives cis; excess H2/Pt gives the alkane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid, reagent-selection for anti reduction, one defensible answer."},{"q":"The terminal alkyne shown is treated first with NaNH2 and then with CH3CH2Br (bromoethane). What is the role of the NaNH2 in this sequence?","opts":["It reduces the triple bond to a cis double bond","It oxidizes the terminal carbon to a carboxylic acid","It deprotonates the terminal alkyne to form a nucleophilic acetylide ion","It acts as the electrophile that is attacked by the alkyne","It adds across the triple bond in a hydroamination"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"NaNH2 removes the acidic terminal acetylenic proton (pKa ~25) to form the acetylide nucleophile, which then does SN2 on the alkyl halide. NaNH2 is a base here, not a reductant, electrophile, or oxidant.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"C#CCCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid (1-pentyne), role-identification, one defensible answer."},{"q":"The product shown was formed by partial reduction of 4-octyne. Based on the stereochemistry of the C=C bond drawn, which reagent was used?","opts":["Na in liquid NH3","H2, Pd/C","H2, Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3, Pb, quinoline)","Li in liquid NH3","Br2 in CCl4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The drawn alkene is cis (Z)-4-octene (RDKit confirms STEREOZ). A cis alkene from an internal alkyne requires syn addition with Lindlar; both Li/NH3 and Na/NH3 give the trans isomer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CCC/C=C\\CCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES is valid Z-alkene matching the cis claim; reagent-from-stereochemistry, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Sodium amide (NaNH2) is added to each of two alkynes: phenylacetylene (terminal, shown) and 1-phenylpropyne (CH3-C#C-Ph, internal). Which alkyne is deprotonated by NaNH2, and why?","opts":["Only the terminal alkyne, because only it has an sp C-H bond that can be removed","Only the internal alkyne, because its proton is more acidic","Neither, because NaNH2 is too weak a base for alkynes","Both equally, because NaNH2 deprotonates any C-H bond","Only the internal alkyne, because conjugation lowers its pKa"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acetylide formation requires an acidic sp C-H on a terminal carbon. The internal alkyne has no acetylenic hydrogen, so only the terminal alkyne (phenylacetylene) is deprotonated.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid (phenylacetylene); acidity/terminal-vs-internal concept, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Starting from the alkyne shown (2-pentyne), which single reagent or reagent set gives trans-2-pentene as the major product?","opts":["H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","H2 (excess), Pd/C","HCl (1 equivalent)","Na in liquid NH3","H2, Lindlar's catalyst"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Trans alkenes from internal alkynes come from dissolving-metal reduction (Na/NH3), which adds H anti. Lindlar gives cis, excess H2/Pd gives pentane, and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a ketone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CC#CCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid; distractors layer stereochemistry vs over-reduction vs hydration, one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Hexyne (shown) is treated with NaNH2, then with 1-bromopropane, and finally with Na in liquid NH3. Which statement correctly describes the final product?","opts":["1-nonyne, a terminal alkyne","cis-4-nonene, an internal Z alkene","4-nonyne, an internal alkyne (no reduction occurs)","trans-2-nonene, an internal E alkene","trans-4-nonene, an internal E alkene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acetylide alkylation of 1-hexyne with 1-bromopropane gives 4-nonyne; Na/NH3 then reduces that internal alkyne by anti addition to trans-4-nonene. Numbering: propyl(C3)-C#C-butyl(C4) places the bond at C4.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid; two-step alkylation+anti-reduction is at the upper edge of DAT depth but standard textbook sequence, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Sodium acetylide (shown) is reacted with an alkyl halide in an SN2 alkylation. Which halide gives the highest yield of the alkylated alkyne?","opts":["bromobenzene (an aryl halide)","2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","2-bromopropane (a secondary alkyl halide)","bromoethene (vinyl bromide)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acetylides are strong bases and good nucleophiles, so the SN2 alkylation works well only on unhindered primary halides. Tertiary/secondary halides favor E2 elimination; aryl and vinyl halides do not undergo SN2, so 1-bromobutane gives the best yield.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"[Na+].[C-]#C","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid (sodium acetylide); SN2 substrate-selection concept, one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Hexyne is treated with Na in liquid NH3. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC#CCCC","opts":["CCCCCC","C/C=C/CCC","C/C=C\\CCC","CCCC(C)C","C=CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Dissolving-metal reduction (Na/NH3) adds the two H atoms anti across the triple bond, giving the trans (E) alkene, trans-2-hexene. RDKit confirms [0] is E and [1] is Z. Lindlar would give cis; hexane needs H2/Pd; terminal/branched skeletons are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C6H10 alkyne); keyed [0] is the correct E/trans alkene; exactly one right answer."},{"q":"3-Heptyne is hydrogenated with H2 over Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCCC","opts":["CC=CCCCC","CC/C=C\\CCC","CCCCCCC","C=CCCCCC","CC/C=C/CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned catalyst delivers H2 syn to the same face, stopping at the cis (Z) alkene, cis-3-heptene. RDKit confirms [0] is Z. The trans isomer is the Na/NH3 product; heptane is over-reduced; the position-shifted/terminal skeletons are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C7H12 alkyne); keyed [0] is the correct Z/cis alkene; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Propyne is treated first with NaNH2, then with 1-bromobutane. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts":["CC#CCCCC","C#CCCCCC","CC#CCCCCC","CC#CC(C)CC","CC#CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"NaNH2 forms the acetylide, which does SN2 on 1-bromobutane (n-butyl, 4 C). Propyne (3 C) + butyl (4 C) gives 2-heptyne, C7H12. RDKit confirms [0] = 2-heptyne. [1] is 1-heptyne (lost methyl), [2] is 2-hexyne (C6), [3] is 2-octyne (C8), [4] is branched.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Chain count correct (C3+C4=C7); keyed [0] right; distractors are valid wrong-length/regio isomers; one answer."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with NaNH2 followed by bromoethane (CH3CH2Br). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts":["CC#CCCCC","C#CCCCCC","CCC#CCC","CC#CCCC","CCC#CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The terminal acetylide of 1-pentyne (C5) does SN2 on bromoethane (C2), installing ethyl at the alkyne carbon to give 3-heptyne, C7H12. RDKit confirms [0] = 3-heptyne. [1] 1-heptyne and [2] 2-heptyne are C7 regio-distractors; [3]/[4] are C6.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Keyed [0] is the correct regiochemistry; [1] and [2] are constitutional-isomer distractors; exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Octyne is reduced with Na dissolved in liquid NH3. Which structure best represents the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC#CCCC","opts":["CC=CCCCCC","C=CCCCCCC","CCCCCCCC","CCC/C=C\\CCC","CCC/C=C/CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Na/NH3 reduces via anti addition to give the trans (E) alkene, trans-4-octene. RDKit confirms [0] is E and [1] is Z. The cis isomer is the Lindlar product; octane is fully reduced; the terminal/position-shifted alkenes are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C8H14); keyed [0] is the correct E/trans alkene; one right answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane) is treated with NaNH2, then with iodomethane (CH3I). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CC1CCCCC1","opts":["CC=CC1CCCCC1","C#CCC1CCCCC1","CC#CC1CCCCC1","CCC#CC1CCCCC1","C#CC1CCCCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NaNH2 deprotonates only the terminal sp C-H (most acidic); the acetylide does SN2 on CH3I, capping the alkyne with methyl to give 1-cyclohexylpropyne. RDKit confirms [0]. Deprotonation never occurs on ring CH2, so the ring-methylated [2] and chain-inserted [1] distractors are wrong.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Two-concept (chemoselective deprotonation + alkylation) within DAT depth; keyed [0] correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Phenyl-1-propyne (Ph-C#C-CH3) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar does syn addition and stops at the alkene, giving cis (Z)-1-phenyl-1-propene; the aromatic ring is untouched under mild conditions. RDKit confirms [0] is Z. Trans is the Na/NH3 product; propylbenzene is over-reduced; allylbenzene has wrong regiochemistry; [4] wrongly reduced the ring to cyclohexyl.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Two-concept (syn/cis + ring-stays-aromatic); keyed [0] correct Z isomer; one right answer."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the final major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["CC/C=C\\CCCC","CC/C=C/CCCC","CCCCCCCC","C/C=C\\CCCCC","CCC#CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the C6 acetylide with ethyl (C2) to give 3-octyne; step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z)-3-octene. RDKit confirms [0] is Z 3-octene. Trans needs Na/NH3; octane is over-reduced; [3] is the alkyne (skipped Lindlar); [4] is cis-2-octene (wrong position).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Two-operation sequence (alkylation then Lindlar), within recognition depth; keyed [0] correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Butanol is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(O)O","CCCC=O","CCC(=O)C","CCCCO","CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"PCC is a mild anhydrous Cr(VI) reagent that oxidizes a 1deg alcohol only to the aldehyde (butanal) and stops; Jones/aqueous CrO3 would push to the acid (opt1). opt2 is wrong-regiochem ketone, opt3 a nonexistent gem-diol, opt4 unreacted SM. Keyed index 0 (CCCC=O) is the correct aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Canonical PCC primary-alcohol question, keyed answer correct, all SMILES valid, single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Pentan-2-ol is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, acetone). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(O)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(O)C","CCCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)CC","CCCC(=O)C","CCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"A 2deg alcohol is oxidized to the ketone (pentan-2-one) by any Cr(VI); no over-oxidation is possible. opt1/opt2 (aldehyde/acid) require a 1deg alcohol, opt3 places carbonyl at C3 (pentan-3-one, wrong regiochem), opt4 is SM. Keyed index 0 (CCCC(=O)C) is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct ketone product, good regiochemistry distractor (pentan-3-one), all SMILES valid, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"1-Hexanol is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, aqueous H2SO4). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)C","CCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCCC(O)O","CCCCCCO","CCCCCC=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Aqueous Cr(VI) (Jones) oxidizes a 1deg alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid (hexanoic acid) via the aldehyde hydrate. The aldehyde (opt1) is the PCC outcome, opt2 wrong regiochem, opt3 a gem-diol, opt4 SM. Keyed index 0 (CCCCCC(=O)O) is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correctly contrasts aqueous-Jones (acid) vs PCC (aldehyde); keyed answer correct, all SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1O","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"A 2deg cyclic alcohol oxidizes to the ketone (cyclohexanone); same with PCC or Jones (no over-oxidation). opt1 is SM, opt2 adds a spurious O, opt3 an impossible ring-opened acid, opt4 cyclopentanone (wrong ring size). Keyed index 0 (O=C1CCCCC1) is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct cyclohexanone; cyclopentanone skeleton distractor is plausible-but-wrong; all SMILES valid, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylpropan-2-ol (tert-butanol) is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C=O","CC(C)(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)=C","CC(C)(O)CO","CC(C)(C)O"],"correct":4,"why":"A 3deg alcohol has no C-H on the carbinol carbon, so Cr(VI) cannot oxidize it and the alcohol is recovered unchanged. opt1 is E1 dehydration (wrong reagent), opt2 a rearranged-skeleton aldehyde, opt3 pivalic acid (adds a carbon, impossible), opt4 a spurious diol. Keyed index 0 (CC(C)(C)O = SM) is the correct no-reaction answer.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic 3deg no-oxidation teaching point; recovered SM is the single defensible answer; all SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl alcohol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","O=C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Benzyl alcohol is a 1deg benzylic alcohol; PCC oxidizes to benzaldehyde and stops. Benzoic acid (opt1) needs aqueous Cr(VI)/KMnO4, not PCC. opt2 is SM, opt3 acetophenone (wrong skeleton), opt4 phenol (not an oxidation product). Keyed index 0 (O=Cc1ccccc1) is correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct benzaldehyde; layered concept (benzylic + PCC stops at aldehyde); all SMILES valid, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"(R)-1-Phenylethanol is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Oxidation of this 2deg benzylic alcohol gives the ketone acetophenone; the sole stereocenter is destroyed so the product is achiral. opt1 keeps (R) alcohol (SM), opt2 (benzoic acid) and opt3 (benzaldehyde) wrongly cleave the methyl, opt4 (ethylbenzene) is a reduction. Keyed index 0 (CC(=O)c1ccccc1) is correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-concept (oxidation + loss of stereocenter); acetophenone correct; all SMILES valid including chiral SM; single answer, in scope."},{"q":"Pent-4-en-1-ol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CCCCC=O","C=CCCC=O","C1CCCO1","C=CCCCO","C=CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"PCC is chemoselective: it oxidizes the 1deg alcohol to the aldehyde (pent-4-enal) while leaving the isolated alkene untouched and not over-oxidizing to acid. opt1 is the over-oxidized acid (Jones outcome), opt2 wrongly oxidizes both ends, opt3 SM, opt4 THF (unrelated, loses a carbon). Keyed index 0 (C=CCCC=O) is correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hard item layering chemoselectivity + no-over-oxidation; pent-4-enal correct; all SMILES valid, single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"The alcohol shown (1-butanol) is treated with a reagent and the only organic product isolated is butanal, CH3CH2CH2CHO (the aldehyde, with NO over-oxidation to the carboxylic acid). Which reagent was used?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["KMnO4, hot, basic then acidic workup","H2CrO4 (chromic acid) in aqueous acetone","PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2","Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, H2O)","Na2Cr2O7, H2SO4, H2O"],"correct":2,"why":"Primary alcohol stops at the aldehyde only with mild, anhydrous Cr(VI) (PCC). All four other reagents are strong aqueous oxidants that carry a primary alcohol fully to butanoic acid. Keyed index 0 is correct; SMILES CCCCO is valid 1-butanol; single concept; visual stem with text reagent options.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct, in scope, valid SMILES, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexanol (shown) is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, H2O). What is the major organic product, and how is this transformation best classified?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohexanone; oxidation of a 2 degree alcohol to a ketone","Cyclohexanone; a reduction of the C-O bond","No reaction; 2 degree alcohols are inert to Jones reagent","Cyclohexene; an E1 dehydration","Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid; oxidation of a 2 degree alcohol to an acid"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary alcohol is oxidized to a ketone; the ketone cannot over-oxidize (no carbonyl C-H). Keyed index 0 is correct; SMILES OC1CCCCC1 is valid cyclohexanol; distractors (acid, dehydration, no-reaction, reduction) are plausible-but-wrong; single concept.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butanol (shown) is stirred with excess Na2Cr2O7 / H2SO4 for several hours at room temperature. What is the outcome?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Oxidation to the corresponding ketone","Cleavage of the C-C bond to give two ketones","Oxidation to an aldehyde that resists further oxidation","Oxidation to the corresponding carboxylic acid","No reaction; the carbinol carbon is tertiary and bears no C-H to remove"],"correct":4,"why":"SMILES CCC(C)(C)O is a tertiary carbinol (carbinol C bears ethyl + two methyls + OH = 2-methyl-2-butanol). Cr(VI) oxidation needs a carbinol C-H; tertiary alcohols have none, so no reaction. Keyed index 0 correct; single concept; valid SMILES.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Diagnostic tertiary = no reaction; structure matches name."},{"q":"Benzyl alcohol (shown) must be converted to BENZALDEHYDE (PhCHO) and the aldehyde must NOT be further oxidized to benzoic acid. Which reagent best accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Hot HNO3","K2Cr2O7, H2SO4, reflux","KMnO4, H2O, heat","PCC, CH2Cl2","CrO3, H2SO4, H2O (Jones)"],"correct":3,"why":"Primary (benzylic) alcohol stops at the aldehyde only with mild anhydrous PCC; the four aqueous strong oxidants give benzoic acid. Keyed index 0 correct; SMILES OCc1ccccc1 valid benzyl alcohol; single concept.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"2-Phenylethanol (PhCH2CH2OH, shown) is treated with hot KMnO4 followed by acidic workup. Identify the product and the correct oxidation-state change at the carbinol carbon.","q_smiles":"OCCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["No reaction; KMnO4 does not oxidize alcohols","Benzoic acid, PhCOOH; the chain is cleaved at the benzylic position","Acetophenone, PhCOCH3; oxidation gives a ketone","Phenylacetic acid, PhCH2COOH; the 1 degree alcohol is oxidized to a carboxylic acid","Phenylacetaldehyde, PhCH2CHO; oxidation stops at the aldehyde"],"correct":3,"why":"OH is on a primary, non-benzylic carbon; hot KMnO4 (strong) takes a primary alcohol fully to the carboxylic acid = phenylacetic acid. SMILES OCCc1ccccc1 valid 2-phenylethanol. Two layered ideas (strong oxidant + primary->acid). Keyed index 0 correct; standard sophomore answer.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Mild benzylic-cleavage nuance exists but textbook answer (phenylacetic acid) is the single defensible one."},{"q":"Consider these two oxidations of 4-methyl-2-pentanol (shown): Reaction A uses PCC/CH2Cl2 and Reaction B uses Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4). Compare the products.","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC(C)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A gives an aldehyde and B gives a carboxylic acid","Both A and B give the same product, 4-methyl-2-pentanone (a ketone)","A gives no reaction and B gives a carboxylic acid","A gives the ketone and B cleaves the molecule into two fragments","A gives the ketone and B gives no reaction"],"correct":1,"why":"Secondary alcohol gives a ketone with both mild (PCC) and strong (Jones) oxidants; no over-oxidation possible. SMILES CC(C)CC(C)O valid 4-methyl-2-pentanol. Keyed index 0 correct; tests the 2 degree-gives-same-product-either-way concept.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"You have three separate alcohols: 3-methyl-1-butanol (a primary alcohol, shown), cyclohexanol (secondary), and 2-methyl-2-propanol (tertiary). Each is treated with CrO3 / H2SO4 (Jones). Which statement about which substrates REACT is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CCO","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The 1 degree and 2 degree alcohols react (to an acid and a ketone); the 3 degree alcohol does not react","All three react to give carboxylic acids","Only the 1 degree alcohol reacts; 2 degree and 3 degree are inert","Only the 3 degree alcohol reacts; 1 degree and 2 degree are inert to Cr(VI)","The 1 degree gives an aldehyde, the 2 degree gives no reaction, the 3 degree gives a ketone"],"correct":0,"why":"With strong Jones: 1 degree -> carboxylic acid, 2 degree -> ketone, 3 degree -> no reaction (no carbinol C-H). SMILES CC(C)CCO valid 3-methyl-1-butanol (primary). Keyed index 0 correct; single classification concept. Note: I trimmed the original muddled 'rank by carbons unchanged' wording, which did not match the answer choices; the resolved question has exactly one correct option.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem wording was convoluted (ranking framing that the options don't actually rank); rewritten to the clean 'which react' question. Chemistry and keyed answer unchanged and correct."},{"q":"1-Butanol is treated with PCC in dry CH2Cl2. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CCCC","CCCCO","CCCC=O","CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"PCC is a mild anhydrous Cr(VI) oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde (butanal). opt1 acid = over-oxidation (Jones/KMnO4), opt2 ketone needs a 2 alcohol, opt3 unreacted, opt4 alkane reduction. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. SMILES all valid (RDKit). Keyed opt0 butanal is the correct PCC product. Clean single-concept, properly visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexylmethanol is treated with the Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, acetone). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCC1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Aqueous Cr(VI) (Jones) oxidizes the primary alcohol fully to cyclohexanecarboxylic acid. opt1 aldehyde is the PCC-stage intermediate, opt2 cyclohexanone is a ring ketone (wrong skeleton), opt3 unreacted, opt4 alkene (no dehydration).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; keyed acid is correct Jones product on a primary alcohol. Good distractors."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butanol is stirred with CrO3 in aqueous H2SO4 at room temperature. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts":["CCC(C)=O","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)C(=O)O","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The carbinol carbon is tertiary with no C-H, so Cr(VI) cannot oxidize it; alcohol is recovered (opt0). Ketone/acid/aldehyde require C-C cleavage; the alkene (opt3) would need dehydration, which is not the answer at room temperature.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"Minor: CrO3/H2SO4 is acidic and can dehydrate a 3 alcohol on heating, but stem specifies room temperature so the standard no-reaction answer is the single defensible one. Valid."},{"q":"Menthol (a secondary cyclohexanol bearing a methyl and an isopropyl group, three stereocenters) is treated with PCC. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","opts":["OC(=O)C1CC(C)CCC1C(C)C","C=C1CC(C)CCC1C(C)C","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(=O)O1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(=O)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"PCC oxidizes the secondary alcohol to the ketone (menthone), converting the sp3 carbinol stereocenter into a flat sp2 carbonyl (that stereocenter is destroyed; the other two ring centers remain). A 2 alcohol cannot become an acid (opt2), no elimination (opt3), and opt4 lactone has wrong connectivity.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None substantive. q_smiles drawn without stereochemistry, but the oxidation answer is diastereomer-independent and the stereocenter-loss claim holds. RDKit confirms substrate C10H20O (3 centers) -> menthone C10H18O (2 centers). Valid two-concept item."},{"q":"trans-4-tert-Butylcyclohexan-1-ol (the OH and the tert-butyl group on opposite faces of the ring) is oxidized with Na2Cr2O7/H2SO4. Which statement best describes the product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","opts":["The cis alcohol forms by epimerization before oxidation can occur.","The product is a carboxylic acid because Cr(VI) always over-oxidizes a cyclohexanol.","The product retains the trans relationship between the carbonyl and the tert-butyl group.","The product is 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone, which is achiral and has no cis/trans relationship at C1 because that carbon is now sp2.","No reaction occurs because the alcohol is on a ring carbon."],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Oxidation of the secondary ring alcohol gives 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone; the former carbinol carbon becomes planar sp2, so the cis/trans descriptor at C1 disappears and the ketone is achiral. A 2 alcohol gives a ketone not an acid, no retained cis/trans at a carbonyl, no epimerization step, ring 2 alcohols react normally.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. Text options, molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid). Exactly one defensible answer (opt0); other four contain clear chemical errors."},{"q":"A student needs to convert benzyl alcohol (PhCH2OH) selectively to benzaldehyde without forming benzoic acid. Which reagent is the correct choice?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts":["Jones reagent (CrO3, aqueous H2SO4)","PCC in anhydrous CH2Cl2","CrO3 in aqueous H2SO4 with excess oxidant","Hot KMnO4 in aqueous base, then acid workup","Na2Cr2O7 in dilute aqueous H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Stopping a primary/benzylic alcohol at the aldehyde requires a mild anhydrous Cr(VI) reagent, PCC. The other four are aqueous/strong oxidants that drive the alcohol to benzoic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. Reagent-selection item, substrate drawn (valid SMILES). Only PCC stops at the aldehyde; single defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Penten-1-ol (which contains both a primary alcohol and a terminal C=C) is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCO","opts":["CCCCC=O","C=CCCC(=O)O","C=CCCC=O","OCCCCCO","O=CC1CCCO1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"PCC is chemoselective: it oxidizes the primary alcohol to the aldehyde (4-pentenal) and leaves the isolated alkene untouched (opt0). It does not over-oxidize to the acid (opt1), hydrogenate the C=C (opt2), add a second OH (opt3), or cyclize (opt4).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; keyed 4-pentenal is correct. Tests chemoselectivity (oxidation + alkene tolerance), two-concept, properly visual."},{"q":"(R)-2-methyl-1-butanol (the stereocenter is the carbon adjacent to the CH2OH) is oxidized with the Jones reagent. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)CO","opts":["CC(C)CC(=O)O","CC[C@@H](C)C(=O)O","CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O","CCC(C)CO","CC[C@@H](C)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Jones oxidizes the primary alcohol to the carboxylic acid; no bond to the adjacent stereocenter is broken so its spatial configuration is retained. The CH2OH->COOH change keeps that group as the #1 CIP priority, so the descriptor stays R: (R)-2-methylbutanoic acid (opt0). opt1 is the inverted (S) acid, opt2 stops at aldehyde, opt3 wrong skeleton, opt4 unreacted.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"Verified the subtle point: RDKit confirms substrate is R and keyed opt0 is R; priority order (COOH>Et>Me>H) parallels the alcohol (CH2OH>Et>Me>H), so retention keeps the R label. Distractor opt1 (S) is the realistic trap. Hard but in DAT recognition scope. Valid."},{"q":"What is the major product when 1-butanol is treated with PBr3?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCl","CCCCBr","CCC(C)Br","CCCCO","CCC=C"],"correct":1,"why":"PBr3 converts a primary alcohol to the primary alkyl bromide, substituting OH with Br at the same carbon (C1) with no rearrangement. Product is 1-bromobutane = index 0. Distractors move Br to C2, use Cl, eliminate, or leave the alcohol unreacted. All five SMILES valid; exactly one defensible answer; visual; in scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; all SMILES parse in RDKit."},{"q":"(R)-2-butanol (drawn) is treated with PBr3. What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=CC","CC[C@H](C)Br","CC[C@@H](C)Br","CC[C@H](C)Cl","CCC(C)Br"],"correct":1,"why":"RDKit confirms substrate = (R); PBr3 does backside SN2 with inversion. Since OH and Br are each top CIP priority, inversion flips the descriptor to (S)-2-bromobutane = CC[C@H](C)Br (RDKit: S) at index 0. Option b is retention (R), c is flat/racemic, d wrong halide, e elimination. Two-concept (substitution + stereo), in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Verified substrate R, product S via RDKit CIP; correct=0."},{"q":"What is the major product when 2-methyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)CO","C=C(C)CC","C/C=C/C","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"E1 dehydration of a tertiary alcohol, Zaitsev gives the more substituted alkene: 2-methyl-2-butene (CC=C(C)C, RDKit = trisubstituted) at index 0, vs the disubstituted Hofmann 2-methyl-1-butene (minor). Chloride, 2-butene skeleton, and rearranged alcohol are wrong. Answer correct; minor wording error in the rationale only.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Cosmetic: rationale calls 2-methyl-2-butene tetrasubstituted; it is trisubstituted (RDKit-confirmed). Answer/index unaffected, keep."},{"q":"What is the major product when 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C"],"correct":2,"why":"E1 forms a secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives the tertiary cation, and Zaitsev elimination yields tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (CC(C)=C(C)C, RDKit = tetrasubstituted) at index 0. Non-rearranged Hofmann alkene, substitution, and unreacted alcohol are wrong. Carbocation rearrangement is standard sophomore Orgo I, in DAT scope.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Rearrangement to tertiary cation correct; correct=0; tetrasubstituted product RDKit-confirmed."},{"q":"What is the major product when cyclohexanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","ClC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot conc. H2SO4 dehydrates the secondary alcohol by E1 to the only possible alkene, cyclohexene (index 0). No chloride source (no substitution), no oxidation to cyclohexanone, no reduction to cyclohexane. Single concept, visual, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"What is the product when 1-propanol is treated with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride (TsCl) and pyridine?","q_smiles":"CCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCOS(=O)(=O)C","CCC=O","CCCCl","CCCOS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1","CCCOC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"TsCl/pyridine sulfonylates oxygen, leaving the C-O bond and skeleton intact (no inversion): propyl tosylate at index 0. Mesylate (MsCl), chloride, aldehyde (oxidation), and acetate are different reagents/reactions. Tosylate aryl ring SMILES valid in RDKit.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; tosylate SMILES parses."},{"q":"What is the major product when 1-methylcyclohexanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"OC1(C)CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","ClC1(C)CCCCC1","OC1(C)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"E1 of the tertiary alcohol, Zaitsev: endocyclic trisubstituted 1-methylcyclohexene (CC1=CCCCC1, RDKit = trisubstituted) at index 0 beats the disubstituted exocyclic methylenecyclohexane (Hofmann). Tertiary chloride, unreacted alcohol, and methylcyclohexane (reduction) wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; substitution degree RDKit-checked."},{"q":"What is the major product when 2-methylcyclopentanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCC1O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C1CCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","CC1CCC=C1","CC1CCCC1O","CC1CCCC1Cl"],"correct":1,"why":"E1 dehydration, Zaitsev: eliminating toward the methyl-bearing carbon gives trisubstituted 1-methylcyclopentene (CC1=CCCC1, RDKit = trisubstituted) at index 0, more stable than disubstituted 3-methylcyclopentene (Hofmann). Methylenecyclopentane, chloride, and unreacted alcohol are wrong. Two-concept (regiochemistry), in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; all SMILES parse."},{"q":"The tertiary alcohol shown (tert-butanol) is to be converted into tert-butyl chloride. Which reagent accomplishes this conversion most readily, taking advantage of the fact that this substrate forms a stable carbocation?","opts":["NaCl in water","PBr3","SOCl2 with pyridine","TsCl with triethylamine","Concentrated HCl (no catalyst needed)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary alcohols react with concentrated HX directly through an SN1 mechanism because the tertiary carbocation is stable; tert-butanol + conc. HCl gives tert-butyl chloride. SOCl2/PBr3 are for 1deg/2deg alcohols, and TsCl gives a tosylate, not a chloride. NaCl in water provides no leaving-group activation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)O","issue":"Correct. 3deg alcohol + conc. HCl -> SN1 chloride; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches tert-butanol; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"1-Butanol (shown) is treated with PBr3. What is the role of PBr3 and the type of product formed?","opts":["It converts the alcohol to a tosylate","It converts the alcohol to the alkyl bromide (1-bromobutane)","It converts the alcohol to the alkyl chloride","It oxidizes the alcohol to butanoic acid","It dehydrates the alcohol to 1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"PBr3 converts 1deg and 2deg alcohols into the corresponding alkyl bromides via an SN2-type pathway (no rearrangement). 1-Butanol gives 1-bromobutane; it does not dehydrate, oxidize, tosylate, or chlorinate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CCCCO","issue":"Correct. PBr3 -> alkyl bromide on 1deg alcohol; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 1-butanol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"To convert the primary alcohol shown (neopentyl alcohol) to the corresponding alkyl chloride WITHOUT carbocation rearrangement, which reagent is the best choice?","opts":["Concentrated HCl (SN1 conditions)","Aqueous HCl, room temperature","SOCl2 with pyridine","Concentrated H2SO4 with heat","NaCl with heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Neopentyl substrates rearrange under SN1/carbocation conditions. SOCl2 (with pyridine) proceeds without a free carbocation, preserving the neopentyl skeleton to give neopentyl chloride; conc. HX or H2SO4 would promote rearrangement, and H2SO4/heat favors elimination.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CO","issue":"Correct. Classic neopentyl no-rearrangement -> SOCl2; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches neopentyl alcohol; two-concept (rearr avoidance + reagent), in scope."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butanol (shown) is heated with concentrated H2SO4. By Zaitsev's rule, what is the major product and what reaction type is occurring?","opts":["E1 dehydration giving 2-methyl-1-butene (less substituted alkene)","SN1 substitution giving 2-chloro-2-methylbutane","E1 dehydration giving 2-methyl-2-butene (more substituted alkene)","Oxidation giving a ketone","SN2 substitution giving an ether"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed dehydration of a tertiary alcohol is E1; Zaitsev predicts the more substituted (trisubstituted) 2-methyl-2-butene over the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-butene. H2SO4 provides no chloride, so no alkyl chloride forms.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","issue":"Correct. E1 Zaitsev -> 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted, verified); key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 2-methyl-2-butanol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanol (shown) is heated with concentrated phosphoric acid. Classify the overall transformation that produces cyclohexene.","opts":["Free-radical halogenation","Acid-catalyzed dehydration (loss of water, beta-elimination)","Esterification","Nucleophilic substitution (SN2)","Oxidation to a ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Heating cyclohexanol with strong acid protonates the OH, water leaves, and a beta hydrogen is lost to form the alkene. This is acid-catalyzed dehydration, a beta-elimination producing cyclohexene plus water.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","issue":"Correct. Classify as acid-catalyzed dehydration / beta-elimination; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches cyclohexanol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"(S)-2-Butanol (shown) is treated with SOCl2 and pyridine. Considering the stereochemical outcome typical of this reagent combination, what happens at the stereocenter?","opts":["Complete racemization to a 50:50 mixture","Migration of the stereocenter to an adjacent carbon","Retention of configuration with no change in connectivity","Loss of the stereocenter through elimination","Inversion of configuration at the carbon bearing the leaving group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SOCl2 with pyridine forms a chlorosulfite that undergoes SN2-like backside attack by chloride, giving inversion. (Without pyridine the SNi pathway gives retention; the stem specifies pyridine, so inversion is the defensible answer.)","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CCC(C)O","issue":"Correct under stated with-pyridine conditions (standard Wade/Klein teaching: pyridine -> inversion vs SNi retention); key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 2-butanol skeleton (note: drawing does not encode the (S) label, but answer is conceptual/text so still works); two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl alcohol (shown) is treated with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride (TsCl) and pyridine. What is the purpose of forming the tosylate product?","opts":["To convert the poor OH leaving group into an excellent leaving group for later substitution/elimination","To reduce the alcohol to an alkane","To dehydrate the alcohol to an alkene","To oxidize the alcohol to an aldehyde","To protect the ring from electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Tosylate formation (ROH + TsCl/pyridine) replaces OH with OTs while retaining C-O configuration. The tosylate is an excellent leaving group, priming the molecule for SN2/SN1/E2 reactions that hydroxide cannot undergo.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","issue":"Correct. Tosylate purpose = make OH a good leaving group; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches benzyl alcohol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methyl-2-butanol (shown), a secondary alcohol, is heated with concentrated H2SO4. The major product is 2-methyl-2-butene rather than 3-methyl-1-butene. Which explanation best accounts for this?","opts":["The methyl group blocks elimination toward the more substituted side","Zaitsev's rule does not apply to secondary alcohols","A 1,2-hydride shift converts the secondary carbocation to a more stable tertiary carbocation, which then loses a beta H to give the Zaitsev (more substituted) alkene","An anti-periplanar requirement forces formation of the terminal alkene","The reaction goes by SN2, so no rearrangement is possible"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"E1 dehydration gives a secondary carbocation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from the adjacent CH yields a more stable tertiary cation, and loss of a beta hydrogen gives the most substituted (Zaitsev) alkene, 2-methyl-2-butene. This is the hallmark of carbocation rearrangement.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)O","issue":"Correct. Hydride-shift rearrangement 2deg->3deg cation then Zaitsev -> 2-methyl-2-butene (verified); key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 3-methyl-2-butanol; recognition-level explanation (not a full drawn mechanism), in scope."},{"q":"The alcohol shown below is treated with thionyl chloride (SOCl2) in the presence of pyridine. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](O)CC","opts":["C[C@@H](Cl)CC","CC(O)CCl","C[C@H](Br)CC","CC=CC","C[C@H](Cl)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Substrate is (R)-butan-2-ol. SOCl2/pyridine proceeds by SN2-type backside displacement giving inversion. Since OH and Cl are both top CIP priority, spatial inversion gives CIP inversion: (R)-alcohol -> (S)-2-chlorobutane. RDKit confirms opt0 is (S), opt1 is (R, retention trap), opt2 is bromide, opt3 moves halogen to primary C, opt4 is elimination. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: (R)->(S) inversion, opt0 correct, one defensible answer, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol (shown) is heated with concentrated H2SO4. Which alkene is the major (Zaitsev) product?","q_smiles":"CC1(O)CCCCC1","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","CCC1=CCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary alcohol dehydrates via tertiary carbocation; Zaitsev favors the more substituted alkene. 1-Methylcyclohexene (opt0) is tetrasubstituted and is the major product. Opt1 is the Hofmann exocyclic methylenecyclohexane, opt2 places the double bond at a less substituted position, opt3 is a substitution product (no Cl source), opt4 is a ring-contracted skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: Zaitsev tetrasub 1-methylcyclohexene = opt0, single concept, valid."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol (shown) is treated with hot concentrated H2SO4. Which alkene is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","C=C(C)C(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Loss of water gives a secondary cation adjacent to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation, then Zaitsev elimination gives tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (opt0). Opt2 is the non-rearranged terminal alkene, opt1 is a rearranged less-substituted alkene, opt3 is substitution (no Cl), opt4 is saturated. Hydride/methyl shift is standard sophomore content.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: methyl shift then Zaitsev -> 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene = opt0. In scope (single rearrangement, one arrow concept)."},{"q":"(S)-Pentan-2-ol (shown) is treated with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride (TsCl) and pyridine. Which structure correctly represents the product, including its stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@H](C)O","opts":["CCC[C@@H](C)Br","CCC[C@H](C)OS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1","CCCC=C","CCC[C@@H](C)Cl","CCC[C@@H](C)OS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Tosylation breaks the O-H bond, not the C-O bond, so configuration at the stereocenter is retained. (S)-alcohol -> (S)-tosylate. RDKit confirms opt0 retains (S), opt1 is inverted (R, the classic trap), opt2/opt3 are halides (TsCl does not put halide on carbon), opt4 is elimination. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: retention, (S)->(S) tosylate = opt0, one defensible answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"2,2-Dimethylpropan-1-ol (neopentyl alcohol, shown) is treated with concentrated HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CO","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)=CC","CC(C)(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Neopentyl alcohol with HBr cannot easily do SN2 (hindered) or form a primary cation, so a 1,2-methyl shift gives a stable tertiary cation; bromide traps it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (opt0). Opt1 is the non-rearranged neopentyl bromide, opt2 a different rearranged skeleton, opt3 an alkene, opt4 the chloride. Neopentyl rearrangement is a classic textbook example.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: methyl shift -> 2-bromo-2-methylbutane = opt0, distinct options, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol (shown) is heated with concentrated H3PO4. Which alkene is the major (Zaitsev) product?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1O","opts":["CC1=CCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Cl","CC1CCCC=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Secondary alcohol dehydrates via secondary cation; eliminating toward the methyl-bearing carbon gives the trisubstituted 1-methylcyclohexene, the Zaitsev product (opt0). Opt1 is the less-substituted disubstituted alkene (elimination away from the methyl), opt2 needs loss of a methyl H (less substituted exocyclic), opt3 is substitution (no Cl), opt4 is a ring-contracted skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: Zaitsev trisub 1-methylcyclohexene = opt0, single concept, valid."},{"q":"(S)-1-Phenylethanol (shown) is treated with SOCl2 and pyridine. Which structure is the major product, including correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","opts":["C[C@@H](Br)c1ccccc1","C[C@H](Cl)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C[C@@H](Cl)c1ccccc1","CO[C@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"By the standard textbook SN2-type (inversion) model for SOCl2/pyridine, (S)-1-phenylethanol gives the (R)-chloride (opt0). RDKit confirms opt0 is (R, inverted), opt1 is (S, retention trap), opt2 styrene (elimination), opt3 bromide, opt4 methyl ether. Note a real-chemistry caveat: this is a benzylic alcohol, so SOCl2 frequently proceeds via a benzylic SN1/SNi pathway giving racemization or partial retention rather than clean inversion - the clean-inversion premise is an idealization, though it is what sophomore courses test.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Keyed answer (opt0, inversion) is the standard DAT/textbook answer and is internally consistent. Minor real-world caveat: benzylic substrate can racemize via SN1; acceptable at DAT depth. SMILES all valid."},{"q":"Sodium phenoxide is treated with 1-bromopropane. What is the major organic product of this Williamson ether synthesis?","q_smiles":"[Na]Oc1ccccc1","opts":["CCCOc1ccccc1","CCCOCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)Oc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Williamson: phenoxide is the nucleophile, displaces bromide from the primary alkyl halide via SN2, giving propyl phenyl ether. Verified all 6 SMILES valid; keyed index 0 matches propyl phenyl ether.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Standard primary-halide Williamson, one defensible answer, distractors (isopropyl regio, benzyl/propylbenzene skeleton, ketone) all plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"2-Methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is treated with methanol and a trace of H2SO4 (acidic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts":["CCOC(C)O","COC(C)CO","CCC(O)OC","COCC(C)O","CC(O)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acidic epoxide opening: Nu attacks the MORE substituted carbon (greater partial positive). OMe goes on CH(CH3), OH on CH2. Keyed index 0 (COC(C)CO) is correct; option 1 is the basic-conditions regiochemistry.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Clean acid-vs-base regiochemistry contrast; one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol (basic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts":["COC(C)CO","CCC(O)OC","CCOCCO","CC(O)CO","COCC(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Basic epoxide opening: methoxide attacks the LESS hindered terminal CH2 in clean SN2; OMe on CH2, OH on CH(CH3). Keyed index 0 (COCC(C)O) is correct; option 1 is the acidic regiochemistry.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Mirror of Q2; one defensible answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Styrene oxide (2-phenyloxirane) is treated with methanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (acidic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1OC1c1ccccc1","opts":["OCC(O)c1ccccc1","O=C(CO)c1ccccc1","COC(CO)c1ccccc1","COCC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCOc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Acidic opening directs Nu to the benzylic carbon (best stabilizes positive charge). OMe on benzylic C, OH on CH2. Keyed index 0 (COC(CO)c1ccccc1) correct; option 1 is the basic regiochemistry.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Two-concept (epoxide opening + benzylic stabilization); one defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexene oxide (7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane) is treated with sodium methoxide in methanol. What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1CCC2OC2C1","opts":["COC1CCCCC1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Backside SN2 opening gives anti (trans) addition. 3D embedding in RDKit confirms option 0 (S,S) places the two O substituents on OPPOSITE faces = trans-2-methoxycyclohexanol; option 1 is genuinely cis. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Verified trans/cis geometry via 3D embedding; anti opening stereochemistry; one defensible answer."},{"q":"You want to make methyl tert-butyl ether (drawn) by a Williamson ether synthesis. Which alkoxide/alkyl-halide combination works best?","q_smiles":"COC(C)(C)C","opts":["tert-Butanol + iodomethane, no base","Sodium tert-butoxide + iodomethane (CH3I)","Sodium methoxide + 2-bromobutane","Sodium methoxide + 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","Sodium ethoxide + 2-bromopropane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Williamson needs a methyl/primary halide for SN2. tert-Butoxide + CH3I works cleanly; methoxide + tertiary bromide gives E2 elimination. Keyed index 0 correct; the other pairs build the wrong ether or lack base.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Reagent-selection (text opts) with molecule drawn; classic SN2-vs-E2 retrosynthetic choice; one defensible answer."},{"q":"2,2-Dimethyloxirane (isobutylene oxide) is treated with methanol and a trace of acid (acidic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1(C)CO1","opts":["COCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)(O)CO","CCC(C)(O)OC","COCC(C)CO","COC(C)(C)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acidic opening: Nu attacks the more substituted (tertiary) carbon that best stabilizes positive charge. OMe on C(CH3)2, OH on CH2. Keyed index 0 (COC(C)(C)CO) correct; option 1 is the basic regiochemistry.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acidic regiochemistry on a tertiary center; one defensible answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"(2S,3S)-2,3-dimethyloxirane (a single enantiomer of trans-2,3-epoxybutane) is treated with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+). What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H]1O[C@H]1C","opts":["C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CCC(O)CO","C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","CC(O)C(C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acid-catalyzed anti (backside) opening inverts one stereocenter. From (2S,3S) trans-epoxide, inverting one center gives (2R,3S) = meso-2,3-butanediol. RDKit confirms option 0 is (R,S) and is identical to its mirror image (meso, achiral). Options 1/2 are the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) diols.","difficulty":3,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. RDKit confirms meso (R,S) for option 0; anti opening + meso recognition = 2 layered concepts, hard but within DAT recognition depth. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Styrene oxide (drawn below) is treated with CH3OH and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (acidic conditions). Which product predominates?","q_smiles":"C1OC1c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(CO)c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","COC(OC)c1ccccc1","COCC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Acidic epoxide opening: the protonated epoxide develops carbocation character at the benzylic carbon, so methanol attacks the more-substituted benzylic carbon. Product = PhCH(OMe)CH2OH (OMe on benzylic C, OH on terminal CH2) = option 1, COC(CO)c1ccccc1. Option 2 is the basic-condition regiochemistry (good distractor). Keyed answer is correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Acidic opening attacks benzylic carbon; keyed product PhCH(OMe)CH2OH is correct. All five option SMILES valid; distractors differ by regiochemistry."},{"q":"The epoxide drawn below (2,2-dimethyloxirane / isobutylene oxide) is opened by sodium methoxide (CH3O-Na+) in methanol (basic conditions). At which carbon does the methoxide nucleophile attack?","q_smiles":"CC1(C)CO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Neither carbon; the epoxide is inert to base","The more-substituted carbon, via a carbocation","The less-substituted (terminal CH2) carbon, by SN2","The more-substituted (tertiary) carbon, by SN2","Both carbons equally, giving a 1:1 mixture"],"correct":2,"why":"Basic epoxide opening is a clean SN2 governed by sterics, so methoxide attacks the less-hindered terminal CH2 rather than the crowded tertiary carbon. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Basic SN2 attacks the less-substituted carbon; keyed answer correct, single concept, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Cyclohexene oxide (drawn below) is opened by aqueous acid to give a cyclohexane-1,2-diol. Because epoxide opening is a backside (anti) attack, what is the stereochemical relationship between the two hydroxyl groups in the product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC2OC2C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A meso cis diol","cis (syn) diol","No defined relationship; the product is a mixture of cis and trans in equal amounts","trans (anti) diol","A single enantiomer of the cis diol"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti (backside) opening of a fused-ring epoxide forces the incoming OH and the original epoxide oxygen onto opposite faces, giving the trans-1,2-diol. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Anti opening of cyclohexene oxide gives trans-diol; keyed answer correct, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Phenol (drawn below) is converted to ethyl phenyl ether (phenetole) by a Williamson ether synthesis. Which set of reagents accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NaOH, then CH3CH2Br","NaBH4, then CH3CH2Br","CH3CH2OH with H2SO4 (acid only)","NaOH, then (CH3)3CBr (tert-butyl bromide)","CH3CH2MgBr (ethyl Grignard)"],"correct":0,"why":"Phenol is acidic enough to be deprotonated by NaOH to give phenoxide, which then does SN2 on the primary halide ethyl bromide to form ethyl phenyl ether. tert-Butyl bromide would eliminate; acid-only and Grignard do not perform Williamson coupling; NaBH4 is irrelevant. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct and unique.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Phenoxide + primary ethyl bromide is the correct, unique Williamson route; keyed answer correct."},{"q":"Propylene oxide (1,2-epoxypropane, drawn below) is treated separately under two conditions: (Condition A) CH3OH / H2SO4, and (Condition B) NaOCH3 / CH3OH. Which statement correctly describes the regiochemistry of methoxide/methanol addition?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both A and B: attack at the more-substituted carbon","A: attack at the less-substituted carbon; B: attack at the more-substituted carbon","A: no reaction; B: attack at the less-substituted carbon","A: attack at the more-substituted carbon; B: attack at the less-substituted carbon","Both A and B: attack at the less-substituted carbon"],"correct":3,"why":"Acidic opening (A) is driven by carbocation character so the nucleophile attacks the more-substituted carbon; basic opening (B) is a steric-controlled SN2 attacking the less-substituted carbon. Opposite regiochemistry. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic acid-vs-base regiochemistry contrast; keyed answer correct. Two-concept but within layering limit."},{"q":"A student attempts to make neopentyl methyl ether using a Williamson ether synthesis from neopentyl bromide (drawn below) plus sodium methoxide, but the reaction is extremely slow and gives poor yield. Which choice would give the SAME target ether in HIGH yield by reversing which partner is the alkyl halide?","q_smiles":"BrCC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Sodium methoxide + neopentyl chloride","Sodium neopentyloxide ((CH3)3CCH2O-Na+) + methyl iodide","Sodium tert-butoxide + methyl iodide","Neopentyl alcohol + methanol with H2SO4","Neopentanol + CH3MgBr"],"correct":1,"why":"Neopentyl halides are very slow in SN2 due to the adjacent quaternary carbon blocking backside attack. Reversing roles makes the neopentyl oxygen the alkoxide nucleophile and methyl iodide (fast, unhindered SN2) the halide, giving the same neopentyl methyl ether in high yield. Other options change the product, keep a hindered halide, or are not Williamson conditions. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct and unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Reversing partners so neopentoxide + MeI gives same ether in high yield; keyed answer correct and unique."},{"q":"The chiral epoxide (R)-2-methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is opened by sodium azide (NaN3, basic conditions). Considering BOTH the regiochemistry of basic opening AND the stereochemistry of the SN2 step, which statement is correct about the carbon that the azide attacks?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, which is not a stereocenter, so the original stereocenter is untouched and retains its configuration","Azide attacks the less-substituted carbon, inverting the stereocenter on the other carbon","Azide attacks both carbons, racemizing the product","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with inversion, inverting the stereocenter","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with retention of configuration"],"correct":0,"why":"Under basic conditions azide does SN2 at the less-substituted terminal CH2. That carbon is not a stereocenter, so SN2 inversion there has no stereochemical consequence; the methyl-bearing stereocenter is never bonded to the nucleophile and retains its configuration. Layers basic-condition regioselectivity with the recognition that inversion only matters at the carbon actually attacked. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Basic regioselectivity + recognition that inversion only matters at the attacked carbon; keyed answer correct. Recognition-level (no R/S math), in scope."},{"q":"Styrene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1OC1c1ccccc1","opts":["COCc1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","COCC(O)c1ccccc1","COC(CO)c1ccccc1","COCC(OC)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Under basic conditions methoxide does an SN2-like attack at the less-hindered terminal CH2, leaving OH on the benzylic carbon: PhCH(OH)-CH2OCH3 = COCC(O)c1ccccc1 (option A). B is the acidic regiochemistry (attack at the benzylic carbon), C is the diol, D double-substitutes, E has lost an oxygen. Exactly one correct answer; all SMILES valid and visual.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A verified; clean single-concept basic-conditions epoxide regiochemistry.","correct_index":0},{"q":"Phenol (drawn) is converted to its sodium salt with NaOH, then treated with 1-bromobutane. Which is the major product of this Williamson ether synthesis?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["CCCCc1ccccc1O","CCCCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCCCOc1ccccc1","CCCOc1ccccc1","CCC(C)Oc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Phenoxide oxygen displaces bromide from the primary halide by SN2, giving butyl phenyl ether PhO-CH2CH2CH2CH3 = CCCCOc1ccccc1 (option A). B is sec-butyl (wrong halide), C is propyl (wrong carbon count), D is ring C-alkylation with phenol intact (Williamson is O-alkylation), E is an ester. One defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A verified; standard Williamson O-alkylation.","correct_index":0},{"q":"2,2-Dimethyloxirane (isobutylene oxide, drawn) reacts with HBr. In the rate-determining step of this acid-catalyzed ring opening, where does bromide attack and why?","q_smiles":"CC1(C)CO1","opts":["At oxygen, which is the most electrophilic atom after protonation","At the more-substituted carbon, because steric approach to a CH2 is impossible","At the more-substituted (tertiary) carbon, because the protonated epoxide places more positive charge there","At the less-substituted (CH2) carbon, by a clean backside SN2 with no charge buildup","Bromide does not attack; the epoxide is inert to acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"After protonation, the C-O bond at the more-substituted carbon weakens and that carbon bears the greater partial positive charge (tertiary cation character), so bromide attacks there (option A). B is the basic/neutral regiochemistry, C and E are mechanistically wrong, D gives the right carbon for the wrong (steric, not electronic) reason. Identifies the regiochemical reason / one-arrow logic only, in DAT scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A verified; text reagent/mechanism-reasoning item with molecule drawn, single concept.","correct_index":0},{"q":"Cyclohexene oxide (drawn) is heated with methanol and a trace of H2SO4. Which structure correctly shows BOTH the regiochemistry and the stereochemistry of the major product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC2OC2C1","opts":["CO[C@H]1CCCCC1","COC1CCCCC1OC","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Epoxide opening is anti (backside), so the two groups end up trans: trans-2-methoxycyclohexanol. 3D embedding confirms option A (2S,?-S,S relationship) is the trans diastereomer; option B (S,R) is cis. C is the diol, D has two methoxy groups, E has lost the hydroxyl. On the symmetric ring regiochemistry is moot, so the anti/trans stereochemistry is the deciding feature. One correct answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A = trans confirmed by 3D geometry; cis distractor B confirmed. Two-concept (anti opening + diastereomer ID) within scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3). Which is the major product of this Williamson ether synthesis?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["CC[C@H](C)O","CC[C@@H](C)OC","CC[C@H](C)OCC","CC[C@H](C)OC","CCC(C)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"SN2 proceeds with inversion. Substrate CC[C@@H](C)Br is (R) (verified); backside attack by methoxide gives (S)-2-methoxybutane CC[C@H](C)OC (option A, verified S). B retains (R) configuration (wrong for SN2), C lacks defined stereochemistry, D is the alcohol (wrong nucleophile/OH), E is an ethyl ether. One defensible answer; CIP assignments verified.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Substrate = R, correct key A = S confirmed by RDKit; SN2 inversion + Williamson, two concepts in scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"1-Phenyl-1,2-epoxypropane (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1OC1c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(c1ccccc1)C(C)O","COC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","OC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","COC(C)C(OC)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Both epoxide carbons are secondary. Under basic conditions the SN2-like attack goes to the less-hindered, non-benzylic CH(CH3); methoxide adds there and OH stays on the benzylic carbon: PhCH(OH)-CH(OCH3)CH3 = COC(C)C(O)c1ccccc1 (option A, connectivity verified). B is the acidic regiochemistry (attack at the benzylic/positive-stabilized carbon, verified), C is the diol, D double-substitutes, E has lost an oxygen. One defensible answer.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A (basic, non-benzylic attack) and acidic distractor B verified by connectivity. Subtle but standard sophomore point; in scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"Cyclopentene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium azide (NaN3) in DMF. Which structure correctly shows the major product, including its stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1CC2OC2C1","opts":["[N-]=[N+]=NC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","OC1CCC(O)C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Azide opens the epoxide by anti (backside) SN2, placing azide and OH trans: trans-2-azidocyclopentanol. 3D embedding confirms option A (S,S) is trans; option B (S,R) is cis. C is a diol (no nitrogen), D has no hydroxyl (ring not opened to an alcohol), E is a six-membered ring (wrong ring size). One correct answer; all azide SMILES valid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A = trans confirmed by 3D geometry; cis distractor B confirmed; ring-size and diol distractors clean. In scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"What is the major organic product after the following sequence: propanal (CH3CH2CHO) is treated with ethylmagnesium bromide (CH3CH2MgBr) in dry ether, then worked up with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+)?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)CCC","CCCCCO","CCCCO","CCC(=O)CC","CCC(O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"Ethyl Grignard adds 2 C to the C1 carbonyl of propanal; protonation gives pentan-3-ol (CCC(O)CC), a secondary alcohol with OH on the former carbonyl carbon. RDKit confirms keyed opt1 matches. Distractors: pentan-1-ol (wrong C), pentan-2-ol (wrong C), unreacted ketone form, butan-1-ol (wrong chain length).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: q_smiles valid, all 5 opts valid, keyed index 1 = pentan-3-ol matches the true Grignard addition product."},{"q":"Predict the major product when cyclohexanone is treated with NaBH4 in methanol followed by aqueous workup.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1C","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"NaBH4 delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon, reducing the ketone to cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1) with no skeletal change. RDKit confirms keyed opt1. Distractors: unreacted SM, extra-carbon cyclohexylmethanol, added methyl, full deoxygenation to cyclohexane (NaBH4 does not do this).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = cyclohexanol is the correct hydride-reduction product."},{"q":"Acetophenone (PhCOCH3) is treated with phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr) in ether, then H3O+. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCC(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"PhMgBr adds phenyl to the ketone carbonyl carbon (already bearing methyl + phenyl); protonation gives tertiary alcohol 1,1-diphenylethanol (CC(O)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1). RDKit confirms keyed opt1 (C14H14O). Distractors: 1-phenylethanol (reduction, not addition), 2,2-diphenylethanol (wrong C), unreacted ketone, wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = 1,1-diphenylethanol is the correct Grignard addition product."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (PhCHO) is treated with LiAlH4 in dry ether, then with aqueous acid on workup. Identify the major product.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the aldehyde to the primary alcohol benzyl alcohol (OCc1ccccc1) by hydride delivery to the carbonyl carbon. RDKit confirms keyed opt1. Distractors: toluene (full deoxygenation, not done by hydride), phenol (OH on ring), unreacted aldehyde, extra ring-OH.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = benzyl alcohol is the correct reduction product."},{"q":"Butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) is treated with methyllithium (CH3Li), then H3O+. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","OCC(C)CC","CCCC(C)O","CCC(=O)C","CCC(C)O"],"correct":0,"why":"MeLi adds methyl to the C2 carbonyl of butan-2-one (already methyl + ethyl), giving tertiary 2-methylbutan-2-ol (CCC(C)(C)O). RDKit confirms keyed opt2 (C5H12O). Distractors: butan-2-ol (reduction), pentan-2-ol (wrong C), unreacted ketone, 2-methylbutan-1-ol (wrong skeleton).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 2 = 2-methylbutan-2-ol is the correct organolithium addition product."},{"q":"3-Phenylpropanal (PhCH2CH2CHO) is treated with methylmagnesium bromide (CH3MgBr) in ether, then aqueous acid. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=CCCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","OCCCCc1ccccc1","CC(O)CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"MeMgBr adds methyl to the aldehyde carbonyl carbon; protonation gives secondary 4-phenylbutan-2-ol (CC(O)CCc1ccccc1, C10H14O). RDKit confirms keyed opt1. Distractors: 3-phenylpropan-1-ol (reduction, no new C), wrong-connectivity primary alcohol, 1-phenylpropan-2-ol (wrong chain length, C9), benzylic OH (wrong skeleton).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = 4-phenylbutan-2-ol is the correct Grignard addition product."},{"q":"Cyclopentanecarbaldehyde is treated with phenyllithium (PhLi) in ether, then H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CC1CCCC1","OC(c1ccccc1)C1CCCC1","OCC1(c2ccccc2)CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","OC(C1CCCC1)C1CCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"PhLi adds phenyl to the aldehyde carbonyl carbon; protonation gives secondary cyclopentyl(phenyl)methanol (OC(c1ccccc1)C1CCCC1, C12H16O). RDKit confirms keyed opt1. Distractors: plain reduction (no Ph), phenyl on ring, second cyclopentyl instead of phenyl, unreacted aldehyde.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = cyclopentyl(phenyl)methanol is the correct organolithium addition product."},{"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone is reduced with NaBH4 (a small hydride reagent) in methanol, then worked up. The tert-butyl group is locked in the equatorial position. Which stereoisomer is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","O[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","O[C@@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","OC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1C","O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Small hydride (NaBH4) attacks axially on cyclohexanones, placing the new OH equatorial. With tBu locked equatorial, an equatorial OH gives trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol. 3D RDKit embedding confirms keyed opt1 has O and tBu on OPPOSITE faces (trans), while opt0 is cis (axial OH, minor). Distractors: cis minor isomer, unreacted ketone, extra-carbon primary alcohol, added-methyl wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified by 3D geometry: keyed index 1 = trans (equatorial-OH) isomer, the correct major product of small-hydride axial attack. Two-concept (reduction + stereochemistry) but recognition-level, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Butan-2-one (drawn) is treated with excess NaBH4 in methanol, followed by aqueous workup. What is the role of NaBH4 in this reaction, and what functional class is the organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NaBH4 acts as a hydride (H-) donor; the product is a secondary alcohol","NaBH4 acts as an oxidizing agent; the product is a carboxylic acid","NaBH4 acts as a proton (H+) donor; the product is a primary alcohol","NaBH4 acts as a carbon nucleophile; the product is a new C-C bond","NaBH4 acts as a base; the product is an enolate"],"correct":0,"why":"NaBH4 delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon of the ketone. Reduction of a ketone gives a secondary alcohol (here, butan-2-ol). It is not a proton donor, oxidant, base, or carbon nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. NaBH4 = hydride donor; ketone -> secondary alcohol (butan-2-ol). Index 0 right, single defensible answer, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"You want to convert benzaldehyde (drawn) into benzyl alcohol (PhCH2OH), a primary alcohol, without forming any new carbon-carbon bond. Which reagent set accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["KMnO4, heat","1) CH3Li, ether; 2) H3O+","1) NaBH4, MeOH; 2) H3O+","1) PhMgBr, ether; 2) H3O+","1) CH3MgBr, ether; 2) H3O+"],"correct":2,"why":"Reducing the aldehyde with NaBH4 adds H to the carbonyl carbon, giving the primary alcohol PhCH2OH with no new C-C bond. Grignard/organolithium reagents (CH3MgBr, CH3Li, PhMgBr) all form a new C-C bond, changing the carbon skeleton. KMnO4 oxidizes rather than reduces.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Only NaBH4 reduces aldehyde to primary alcohol with no new C-C bond; all organometallics add carbon, KMnO4 oxidizes. Index 0 right."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate (an ester, drawn) is treated with 2 equivalents of CH3MgBr, then aqueous acid. Which product results, and why is 2 equivalents needed?","q_smiles":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A secondary alcohol, PhCH(CH3)OH; only one addition is possible","The unchanged ester; Grignards do not react with esters","A tertiary alcohol, PhC(CH3)2OH; the first equivalent expels methoxide to give a ketone, which reacts with a second equivalent","An aldehyde, PhCHO; the Grignard stops after one addition","A carboxylic acid, PhCOOH; the Grignard hydrolyzes the ester"],"correct":2,"why":"Grignard addition to an ester first gives a tetrahedral intermediate that collapses by expelling methoxide, forming a ketone (acetophenone). The ketone is more reactive than the ester, so a second equivalent of CH3MgBr adds, giving the tertiary alcohol 2-phenylpropan-2-ol after workup.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Ester + 2 eq RMgX -> tertiary alcohol 2-phenylpropan-2-ol via ketone intermediate. Index 0 right; classic two-equivalent rationale, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (drawn) is treated with CH3MgBr in dry ether, followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"correct":2,"why":"CH3MgBr adds its methyl group to the carbonyl carbon; workup protonates the alkoxide to give the tertiary alcohol 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CC1(O)CCCCC1). Cyclohexanol (no methyl) would be the hydride-reduction product; 2-methylcyclohexanol has the wrong regiochemistry; methylenecyclohexane is an alkene; cyclohexylmethanol has the carbon inserted wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Methyl adds to carbonyl C -> 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CC1(O)CCCCC1). Index 0 right; all 5 option SMILES valid and distinct; structure-ID item is visual."},{"q":"The target alcohol 3-methylpentan-3-ol is drawn. Which single carbonyl-plus-Grignard combination forms this tertiary alcohol (after aqueous workup)?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(O)CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Pentan-3-one (CH3CH2COCH2CH3) + CH3CH2MgBr","Acetone (CH3COCH3) + CH3MgBr","Butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) + CH3CH2MgBr","3-Methylpentanal + CH3MgBr","Propanal (CH3CH2CHO) + CH3CH2MgBr"],"correct":2,"why":"3-Methylpentan-3-ol has the carbinol carbon bearing two ethyl groups and one methyl group. Disconnecting one ethyl as the Grignard (CH3CH2MgBr) leaves butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) as the carbonyl: the ketone carbon already carries methyl and ethyl, and the new ethyl completes the structure. Pentan-3-one + EtMgBr would give 3-ethylpentan-3-ol (three ethyls); the others give the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct, RDKit-verified: butan-2-one + EtMgBr -> CCC(C)(O)CC exactly. Index 0 right; pentan-3-one distractor correctly gives triethylcarbinol. Retrosynthesis at sophomore depth, in scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone (drawn) is reduced with LiAlH4 in dry ether, then quenched with aqueous acid. The product is 1-phenylethanol. Which statement about this transformation is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["LiAlH4 adds a methyl group to the carbonyl carbon","No stereocenter is created; the product is achiral","A new stereocenter is created, so the product is formed as a racemic (50:50 R/S) mixture","The product is a single enantiomer because LiAlH4 is chiral","The product is a primary alcohol"],"correct":2,"why":"Hydride adds to the planar carbonyl carbon from either face with equal probability, creating a stereocenter (carbon bearing OH, H, CH3, and phenyl). The result is a racemic mixture of (R)- and (S)-1-phenylethanol. LiAlH4 is achiral, the product is a secondary (not primary) alcohol, and hydride (not methyl) is delivered.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Hydride to planar C=O creates a stereocenter -> racemic 1-phenylethanol. Index 0 right; achiral-reagent/secondary-alcohol distractors sound. Stereochemistry concept in scope."},{"q":"Pivaldehyde, (CH3)3C-CHO, is drawn. Compared with acetaldehyde (CH3CHO), pivaldehyde reacts more slowly with NaBH4. Which factor best explains the slower nucleophilic addition to pivaldehyde?","q_smiles":"O=CC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Steric bulk of the tert-butyl group hinders hydride approach to the carbonyl carbon","The tert-butyl group withdraws electron density, deactivating the carbonyl","Hydrogen bonding stabilizes the pivaldehyde carbonyl","Pivaldehyde has no alpha hydrogens, so it cannot be reduced","Pivaldehyde is aromatic and therefore unreactive"],"correct":0,"why":"Both are aldehydes, so electronics are similar (alkyl groups are weakly electron-donating, not withdrawing). The bulky tert-butyl group sterically blocks hydride approach to the carbonyl carbon, slowing addition. Lack of alpha hydrogens is irrelevant to reduction, and pivaldehyde is not aromatic.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Steric hindrance from tert-butyl slows hydride addition; alkyl is donating not withdrawing, not aromatic, alpha-H irrelevant. Index 0 right, in scope."},{"q":"The keto-aldehyde 4-oxopentanal (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CHO, drawn) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C. Which carbonyl is reduced preferentially, and what is the reason?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The ketone is reduced first because ketones are more electrophilic than aldehydes","Both are reduced equally; NaBH4 shows no selectivity","The aldehyde is reduced first because it is more sterically hindered","Neither is reduced; NaBH4 only reduces esters","The aldehyde is reduced first because aldehydes are more electrophilic and less sterically hindered than ketones"],"correct":4,"why":"Aldehydes are both more electrophilic (one electron-donating alkyl group vs. two for the ketone) and less sterically hindered, so with a limited amount of NaBH4 the aldehyde is reduced selectively to give the hydroxy-ketone. Ketones are less, not more, electrophilic. The reasoning in the steric distractor is reversed.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Chemoselective NaBH4 reduces the more electrophilic, less hindered aldehyde over the ketone. Index 0 right; single selectivity concept at sophomore level, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with CH3MgBr in dry ether, followed by aqueous acidic workup (H3O+). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","CC1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"CH3- adds to the carbonyl carbon making a new C-C bond; protonation gives the tertiary alcohol 1-methylcyclohexanol (opt0). 2-methylcyclohexanol = wrong carbon; cyclohexanol = hydride reduction (no C-C bond); alkenes = dehydration artifacts. Keyed index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid, key correct, single concept (Grignard addition), one defensible answer, fully visual."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr), then worked up with H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Phenyl carbanion adds to the aldehyde carbon giving the secondary alcohol diphenylmethanol/benzhydrol (opt0). Benzyl alcohol = hydride reduction; benzophenone = wrong oxidation state; 1-phenylethanol = CH3MgBr not PhMgBr; benzoic acid = oxidation. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct key, single concept, unambiguous, visual."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with CH3MgBr, then H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(CO)c1ccccc1","CCC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"CH3- adds to the ketone carbon (already bearing CH3 and Ph) giving tertiary alcohol 2-phenyl-2-propanol (opt0). 1-phenylethanol = reduction; 1-phenyl-1-propanol = ethyl nucleophile; 2-phenyl-1-propanol = wrong connectivity; cumene = deoxygenated. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct key, single concept, one answer, visual."},{"q":"3-Pentanone is treated with LiAlH4, then H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(=O)CC","CCCCCO","CCC(O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 delivers H- to the carbonyl carbon, reducing the symmetric ketone to the secondary alcohol 3-pentanol (opt0) with no skeleton change. 2- and 1-pentanol = wrong OH position; unchanged ketone ignores that LiAlH4 reduces ketones; 2-methyl-2-butanol implies a C-C bond (Grignard), which hydride cannot form. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct key, single concept (hydride reduction), unambiguous, visual."},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the curved-arrow step that initiates reduction when 3-methylcyclohexanone (drawn) reacts with NaBH4?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCC(=O)C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Hydride adds to the carbonyl oxygen while the pi electrons collapse onto carbon.","The borohydride removes the alpha hydrogen, forming an enolate that then picks up hydride.","A hydride (H-) from the borohydride attacks the carbonyl carbon, and the C=O pi electrons shift onto oxygen to give an alkoxide.","A lone pair on the carbonyl oxygen attacks boron first, and hydride is delivered to oxygen.","The carbonyl oxygen is protonated first by NaBH4, then hydride adds to oxygen."],"correct":2,"why":"Nucleophilic addition: B-H electrons deliver H- to the electrophilic carbonyl CARBON while C=O pi electrons move to oxygen, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide (opt0). NaBH4 is not basic enough to form an enolate, does not protonate first, and hydride never adds to oxygen. Single-arrow identification, not a full mechanism. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Identifies a single mechanistic step (allowed: 'at most identify one arrow'), not a full multi-step mechanism. Valid SMILES, correct key, molecule drawn."},{"q":"2-Cyclohexen-1-one (an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone, drawn) is treated with NaBH4 in methanol, then H3O+. NaBH4 reacts by 1,2-addition at the carbonyl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1C=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1C=CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CC=CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"1,2-addition adds H- to the carbonyl carbon, reducing C=O to OH while leaving the conjugated C=C intact: allylic alcohol 2-cyclohexen-1-ol (opt0). Cyclohexanol = full saturation (1,4 path); cyclohexanone = no reduction; 3-en-1-ol = wrong alkene position; cyclohexene = oxygen removed. Stem spoonfeeds '1,2-addition' so the answer is unambiguous. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Stem specifies 1,2-addition, removing 1,2-vs-1,4 ambiguity. Valid SMILES, correct key, two-concept (chemoselectivity of conjugated system + reduction), visual."},{"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone (drawn; the bulky tert-butyl group locks the chair) is reduced with LiAlH4, then H3O+. The small hydride attacks the unhindered carbonyl predominantly from the axial direction. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C1CCC(=O)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1O","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(=O)CC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Axial hydride delivery places the new OH equatorial. With both tBu and OH equatorial on the 1,4-ring they lie on opposite faces = trans alcohol, the major product (opt0; 3D embedding confirms opt0 = trans/opposite-faces, opt1 = cis/same-face). The cis isomer (equatorial attack) is minor; flat structure = undefined stereo; ketone = unreacted; last option = 2-tBu (1,2 regiochemistry). Keyed index 0 correct and matches literature (~90% trans).","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Borderline depth but acceptable: stem spoonfeeds 'attacks from the axial direction,' so the student only converts axial-attack to equatorial-OH to trans. Two layered concepts (conformation + stereo), single reactive center, no Felkin-Anh/FMO. SMILES valid, stereo verified, key correct."},{"q":"4-Oxocyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde (drawn; it has both an aldehyde and a ketone) is treated with one equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C, then H3O+. NaBH4 selectively reduces the more reactive carbonyl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCC(=O)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCC1CCC(=O)CC1","O=CC1CCC(O)CC1","O=CC1CCC(=O)CC1","O=C(O)C1CCC(=O)CC1","OCC1CCC(O)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are more electrophilic and less hindered than ketones, so 1 equiv NaBH4 at 0 C reduces the CHO to CH2OH while the ring ketone survives (opt0). Reducing only the ketone is backwards; the diol = excess reagent; carboxylic acid = oxidation; unchanged dialdehyde/ketone ignores that NaBH4 reduces aldehydes. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Chemoselective aldehyde-over-ketone reduction is standard sophomore content; stem specifies 1 equiv / 0 C / 'more reactive carbonyl' for unambiguity. SMILES valid, key correct, two concepts (chemoselectivity + reduction), visual."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product when cyclohexanone is treated with excess ethylene glycol (HOCH2CH2OH) and a catalytic amount of TsOH, with azeotropic removal of water.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC2(OCCO2)CC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1CCC2(OCCCO2)CC1","OCCOC1(O)CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"A ketone plus a 1,2-diol under acid catalysis with water removal gives the cyclic acetal (a five-membered 1,3-dioxolane). The pendant-OH hemiacetal is an intermediate, not the isolated product; the reduced alcohol and the six-membered dioxane (wrong ring size) are incorrect, and the starting ketone is unchanged.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Classic dioxolane acetal protection; keyed SMILES is the spiro 1,3-dioxolane. All five SMILES valid; distractors (hemiketal, reduced alcohol, 6-ring dioxane, SM) plausible-but-wrong. In scope, visual, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with 2 equivalents of methanol and a trace of anhydrous HCl. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","COC(OC)c1ccc(OC)cc1","COC(OC)c1ccccc1","COCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"An aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid catalysis forms the dimethyl acetal. The mono-methoxy hemiacetal is only the intermediate; benzyl alcohol (reduction) and benzyl methyl ether are wrong transformations, and the ring-substituted option changes the skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Dimethyl acetal of benzaldehyde keyed properly. All SMILES valid; distractors (hemiacetal, benzyl alcohol, benzyl methyl ether, ring-OMe) good. In scope, visual, unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with pyrrolidine (a secondary amine) and a trace of acid, with removal of water. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC(N2CCCC2)CC1","C1=C(N2CCCC2)CCCC1","[N+]1(=C2CCCCC2)CCCC1","OC1(N2CCCC2)CCCCC1","C1CCC(C2CCCC2)=NC1"],"correct":1,"why":"A secondary amine cannot form a neutral C=N imine (no N-H left to lose), so after the carbinolamine dehydrates, the iminium loses an alpha proton to give the neutral enamine. The iminium ion is the species just before that deprotonation, the carbinolamine is an earlier intermediate, and the saturated amine and rearranged C=N skeleton are wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 2 amine gives the enamine (1-pyrrolidinocyclohexene); keyed SMILES is right. Iminium and carbinolamine are correct mechanistic-intermediate distractors. Two-concept (2 vs 1 amine outcome + correct tautomer). Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Acetophenone (1-phenylethan-1-one) is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2) and a trace of acid, with removal of water. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(NC)c1ccccc1","CNC(C)c1ccccc1","CN=C(C)c1ccc(C)cc1","CNC(C)(O)c1ccccc1","CN=C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"A primary amine condenses with a ketone to give an imine (Schiff base), C=N-CH3. The carbinolamine is the pre-dehydration intermediate; an enamine would require a secondary amine; the saturated amine is a reduction product; and the ring-methylated option is the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 1 amine + ketone gives the N-methyl imine; keyed SMILES matches. Distractors (carbinolamine, enamine, amine, ring-methyl) plausible-but-wrong. Pairs cleanly with Q3 (1 vs 2 amine). Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Propanal is treated with HCN (generated in situ from NaCN and acid). Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(N)C#N","CCC(O)C#N","CCC(O)CN","CCCC#N","CCC(=O)C#N"],"correct":1,"why":"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon and the alkoxide is protonated, giving the cyanohydrin (an alpha-hydroxy nitrile). The aminonitrile (Strecker-type, needs NH3), the acyl cyanide, the simple nitrile (no OH), and the amino alcohol (reduction) are all wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyanohydrin of propanal keyed properly. All SMILES valid; distractors (aminonitrile, acyl cyanide, nitrile, amino alcohol) good. In scope, visual, unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclopentanone is treated with KCN and aqueous HCl. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCC1","N#CC1CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCCC1","N#CC1(N)CCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the ketone followed by protonation gives the cyclopentanone cyanohydrin (a quaternary carbon bearing both OH and CN). The nitrile without OH, the aminonitrile, the simple alcohol (reduction), and the six-membered ring (wrong skeleton) are incorrect.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyclopentanone cyanohydrin keyed properly (quaternary C with OH+CN). Distractors (nitrile-only, aminonitrile, alcohol, 6-ring) plausible-but-wrong. Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"5-Hydroxypentanal (drawn) is dissolved in dilute acid, where it equilibrates with its cyclic form. Predict the structure of the major cyclic product.","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCCCCCO","OC1CCCO1","O=CCCCCO","OC1CCCCO1","O=C1CCCCO1"],"correct":3,"why":"The hydroxyl oxygen adds intramolecularly to the aldehyde to form a cyclic hemiacetal (lactol). The chain length gives the favored six-membered ring; the five-membered ring is the wrong ring size; the lactone is an oxidation product, the diol is a reduction product, and the open-chain hydroxy aldehyde is the starting material.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Intramolecular hemiacetal of 5-hydroxypentanal gives the 6-membered 2-hydroxytetrahydropyran (RDKit confirms keyed=6-ring, distractor=5-ring). Sugar-foundation lactol chemistry is sophomore-level and single-center; appropriately the batch's one hard item. Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"The cyclic ethylene acetal shown (cyclohexanone protected as its 1,3-dioxolane) is stirred with aqueous H3O+. Predict the major product of this deprotection.","q_smiles":"C1CCC2(OCCO2)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1","OCCOC1(O)CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Acetal formation is reversible; aqueous acid hydrolyzes the dioxolane back to the parent carbonyl, regenerating cyclohexanone (and releasing ethylene glycol). The hemiacetal is only a partial-hydrolysis intermediate; the alcohol (reduction), cyclohexane, and ring-expanded ketone are wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acid hydrolysis of the dioxolane regenerates cyclohexanone; keyed SMILES matches. Nice reverse of Q1. Distractors (hemiacetal, reduced alcohol, cyclohexane, ring-expanded ketone) plausible-but-wrong. Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with excess ethylene glycol (HOCH2CH2OH) and a catalytic amount of p-toluenesulfonic acid (TsOH), with water removed by a Dean-Stark trap. What type of product is formed?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["An enol ether from a single addition of the diol","An imine (Schiff base)","A cyclic acetal (1,3-dioxolane), protecting the carbonyl","A hemiacetal that is stable and isolable","A geminal diol (carbonyl hydrate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A ketone plus a 1,2-diol under acid catalysis with azeotropic water removal gives a cyclic acetal (1,3-dioxolane), the standard carbonyl protecting group. Dean-Stark drives the equilibrium forward; the hemiacetal is not isolable, and acid/diol gives neither an imine nor a hydrate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Standard ketone + diol acetalization; keyed answer right, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with excess methanol and a catalytic amount of anhydrous HCl. Which structure is the major product (the fully protected carbonyl)?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","COC(C)(OC)c1ccccc1","COC(OC)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","COC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"An aldehyde with excess alcohol and acid gives the dimethyl acetal (two OMe on the former carbonyl carbon). The hemiacetal is only an intermediate; the unchanged aldehyde and the benzyl alcohol (a reduction product) are wrong, and the extra-methyl skeleton is not derivable from benzaldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. All five SMILES parse; opt0 is the true dimethyl acetal; distractors differ by skeleton/oxidation state. Keyed answer right."},{"q":"Acetophenone is heated with aniline (PhNH2) and a trace of acid, with water removed. The product shown is CC(=Nc1ccccc1)c1ccccc1. This product is best classified as which of the following?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["A nitrile","An acetal","An enamine","An imine (Schiff base)","A hemiaminal (carbinolamine)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A primary amine condensing with a ketone gives an imine (C=N, Schiff base). The stated product C14H13N has a C=N and is the correct condensation product. Enamines come from secondary amines; the carbinolamine is the pre-dehydration intermediate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Stated product formula C14H13N verified (ketone + aniline - H2O), contains C=N; keyed answer right."},{"q":"To convert cyclohexanone into the enamine 1-(cyclohex-1-en-1-yl)pyrrolidine, which amine reagent (with acid catalysis and water removal) is required?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["A tertiary amine (triethylamine)","Hydroxylamine (NH2OH)","Ammonia (NH3)","A primary amine (methylamine)","A secondary amine (pyrrolidine)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Enamines form only from secondary amines: the carbinolamine has no N-H left to lose, so elimination goes toward the alpha carbon to give C=C-N. Primary amines and ammonia give imines, tertiary amines cannot condense, and hydroxylamine gives an oxime.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Classic enamine-requires-secondary-amine recognition; keyed answer right, in scope."},{"q":"Acetone is treated with KCN followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the product (the cyanohydrin)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["CC(C)C#N","CC(C)(O)C#N","CC(=O)C#N","CC(O)CC#N","CC(C)(O)C=N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon and the oxygen is protonated on workup, giving the cyanohydrin (OH and C#N on the same carbon), C4H7NO. The nitrile lacking OH, the wrong-regiochemistry isomer, the acyl cyanide, and the imine-type carbon are all incorrect.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. opt0 cyanohydrin formula C4H7NO verified (acetone + HCN); all options valid SMILES; keyed answer right."},{"q":"Cyanohydrin formation (and nucleophilic addition to carbonyls generally) is fastest for the least sterically hindered, most electrophilic carbonyl. Among the carbonyl compounds shown as choices, which reacts FASTEST with HCN?","q_smiles":"O=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC=O (acetaldehyde)","O=C1CCCCC1 (cyclohexanone)","CC(C)=O (acetone)","O=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1 (benzophenone)","C=O (formaldehyde)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Reactivity toward nucleophilic addition falls as alkyl/aryl substitution rises (more steric bulk and more electron donation to the carbonyl). Formaldehyde, with two H's, is the most reactive; benzophenone, with two bulky electron-donating phenyls, is the least.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Standard sterics+electronics reactivity ranking; formaldehyde unambiguously fastest. Stem draws benzophenone (the slowest), slightly atypical but each molecule is labeled in the options; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The compound 4-hydroxybutanal (OCCCC=O) exists in equilibrium with a cyclic species formed when the hydroxyl oxygen adds intramolecularly to the carbonyl. The cyclic form OC1CCCO1 is best described as which of the following?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCO","opts":["An enol ether","A cyclic ether (no new OH)","A cyclic acetal","A cyclic hemiacetal (lactol)","A lactone (cyclic ester)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Intramolecular addition of OH to the aldehyde gives a hemiacetal: one ring-O (OR) and one OH on the former carbonyl carbon, here a 5-membered ring (lactol), the same equilibrium that cyclizes sugars. A full acetal needs a second alkoxy group; a lactone requires oxidation.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Open (C4H8O2) and cyclic OC1CCCO1 (C4H8O2) are isomers; ring is 5-membered with a hemiacetal carbon (one ring-O + one OH). Lactol classification right."},{"q":"A chemist wants to reduce the ketone of a keto-aldehyde to an alcohol while leaving the aldehyde untouched. They first convert the aldehyde to a 1,3-dioxolane with ethylene glycol/TsOH, run the reduction, then treat with aqueous acid. Why is the acetal an effective protecting group for this sequence?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetals are stable to base and nucleophiles/hydride but are hydrolyzed back to the carbonyl by aqueous acid","Acetals spontaneously revert to the carbonyl on standing, requiring no deprotection step","Acetals are stable to acid but are cleaved by base, so basic workup restores the carbonyl","Acetals are inert under all conditions and must be removed by oxidation","Acetals are reduced by hydride, regenerating the aldehyde directly"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acetal formation/hydrolysis are acid-catalyzed equilibria. With no carbonyl present, acetals are inert to hydride, base, and nucleophiles (the protection), and are removed by aqueous acid that reverses the equilibrium to carbonyl plus diol. They are not base-cleaved or hydride-reduced.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Tests the stability-profile rationale of acetal protecting groups (recognition-level, not full mechanism); keyed answer right, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with excess ethylene glycol (HOCH2CH2OH) and a catalytic amount of p-toluenesulfonic acid (TsOH), with water removed azeotropically. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC2(SCCS2)CC1","OC1(O)CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1.OCCO","OC1(OCCO)CCCCC1","C1CCC2(OCCO2)CC1"],"correct":4,"why":"A ketone plus a diol under acid catalysis with water removal gives the cyclic acetal (a 1,3-dioxolane). Option B is the open hemiketal intermediate (not isolated when water is removed), C is the gem-diol hydrate, D is a dithiolane (would require a dithiol, not the diol), and E shows no reaction. Removing water by Dean-Stark drives the equilibrium fully to the cyclic acetal.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; cyclic acetal correctly keyed at index 0; distractors (hemiketal, hydrate, dithiolane, no-reaction) are plausible-but-wrong. Single concept, DAT depth."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde reacts with methylamine (CH3NH2) under mildly acidic conditions to form an imine. Before the final C=N product forms, the reaction passes through a key neutral tetrahedral intermediate. Which species is that intermediate (the carbinolamine/hemiaminal) that then loses water?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CN=C(O)c1ccccc1","OC(NC)c1ccccc1","CN=Cc1ccccc1","CNCc1ccccc1","N=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Amine addition to the carbonyl gives the carbinolamine (hemiaminal), a tetrahedral carbon bearing both OH and NHCH3; dehydration of this intermediate then yields the imine. Option B is the final imine (product, not the intermediate asked for), C is the reduced amine, D is an enol-type wrong structure, and E has the wrong N-substituent (no methyl).","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; the carbinolamine (sp3 C bearing OH and NHCH3) is correctly keyed at index 0. The 'imine as distractor' (B) is the most tempting trap; stem unambiguously asks for the tetrahedral intermediate. Visual structure-ID, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is heated with the secondary amine pyrrolidine and a trace of acid, with water removed. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1N1CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1.C1CCNC1","OC1(CCCCC1)N1CCCC1","C1CCC(CC1)=NC","C1CCC(=CC1)N1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"A secondary amine cannot form a stable imine (no N-H left to lose), so after carbinolamine formation the system eliminates toward an alpha carbon to give an enamine: 1-(pyrrolidin-1-yl)cyclohexene. Option B is the carbinolamine intermediate, C is a saturated amine, D is an N-methyl imine (wrong amine and wrong class), and E shows no reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified option A has N bonded to the sp2 alkene carbon (true N-C=C enamine, 1-pyrrolidinocyclohexene). Correctly keyed at index 0; distractors are carbinolamine, saturated amine, wrong-class imine, no-reaction."},{"q":"Acetophenone (C6H5COCH3) is treated with NaCN followed by aqueous acid workup (equivalently, HCN/KCN). Which structure is the cyanohydrin product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)(C#N)c1ccccc1","OC(C#N)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","COC(C#N)c1ccccc1","CC(C#N)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon and protonation of the alkoxide gives the cyanohydrin: a carbon bearing OH, CN, CH3, and phenyl. Option B replaces OH with OCH3 (an ether, wrong), C is the cyanohydrin of benzaldehyde (missing the methyl), D lacks the OH entirely, and E lacks the nitrile. Note the product carbon is a new stereocenter, formed as a racemate because CN attacks either face equally.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; cyanohydrin (C bearing OH, CN, CH3, Ph) correctly keyed at index 0. Distractors differ by one functional group each. Single concept, DAT depth."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone is condensed with pyrrolidine (acid catalyst, water removed) to form an enamine. Which regiochemical product predominates?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CN=C1CCCCC1C","OC1(C)CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CC1=C(N2CCCC2)CCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1N1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"With an unsymmetrical ketone, enamine formation favors the LESS-substituted double bond: forming the alkene toward the methylated carbon (option B) places the bulky pyrrolidine and the alpha-methyl in destabilizing A-1,3 strain, so it is disfavored. The C=C therefore goes away from the methyl group (option A). Option C is a saturated amine, D is a nonsense carbinolamine, and E is an N-methyl imine (wrong amine, wrong class).","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified with RDKit: in option A the C=C is between the former carbonyl C (bearing N) and the non-methyl alpha carbon (away from methyl = less-substituted enamine, correct), while option B places the C=C on the methyl-bearing carbon (more-substituted, disfavored). Correctly keyed at index 0. Standard enamine regioselectivity, in Orgo II scope."},{"q":"p-Anisaldehyde (4-methoxybenzaldehyde) is stirred with excess methanol and a catalytic amount of anhydrous HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(O)c1ccc(OC)cc1","COC(OC)c1ccc(OC)cc1","O=Cc1ccc(OC)cc1","COC(O)c1ccc(OC)cc1","COCc1ccc(OC)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"An aldehyde with excess alcohol under acid catalysis gives the dimethyl acetal; two equivalents of methanol replace the carbonyl oxygen. Option B is the hemiacetal (the intermediate, not isolated with excess alcohol/acid), C is a benzylic ether (over-reduction skeleton, wrong), D is the gem-diol hydrate, and E shows no reaction. The aryl OCH3 (methoxy) on the ring is a spectator and is unchanged.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Dimethyl acetal correctly keyed at index 0; the aryl methoxy is correctly left as a spectator across all options. Distractors (hemiacetal, benzylic ether, hydrate, no-reaction) are plausible. Single concept, DAT depth."},{"q":"A chemist must perform a Grignard reaction (CH3MgBr) on the aldehyde of the keto-aldehyde shown while leaving the ketone untouched is not possible directly, so a protecting-group strategy is needed. Which sequence correctly protects the more reactive carbonyl, then later restores it?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Acetals require base to form and base to hydrolyze, so use NaOH for both protection and deprotection","Convert the aldehyde to a cyclic acetal (HOCH2CH2OH/H+), run the desired chemistry, then hydrolyze the acetal back to the aldehyde with aqueous acid","Protect the carbonyl as a cyanohydrin, which is stable to strong base and aqueous acid indefinitely","Reduce the aldehyde to an alcohol with NaBH4, then oxidize it back with H+ alone","Convert the aldehyde to an enamine, which is stable to aqueous acid and never needs to be removed"],"correct":1,"why":"Acetals are the standard carbonyl protecting group: they form under acid with a diol (water removed) and are stable to base, nucleophiles, and Grignard/hydride reagents, yet are cleanly removed by aqueous acid to regenerate the carbonyl. The aldehyde, being more electrophilic, is protected selectively. Enamines and cyanohydrins are acid-labile (hydrolyze back), reduction is not a true protect/restore cycle, and acetal chemistry is acid-catalyzed, not base.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem phrasing is slightly awkward ('...untouched is not possible directly') but parseable and unambiguous. Reagent-selection text item; molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid keto-aldehyde). Acetal protect/deprotect correctly keyed at index 0; the four distractors are each chemically false. In DAT scope (recognition of acetal as protecting group, no full mechanism)."},{"q":"5-Hydroxypentanal (shown) is dissolved in a trace of acid. Rather than reacting intermolecularly, it cyclizes. Which structure is the predominant product at equilibrium?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O1CCCCC1","OCCCCCO","OC1CCCO1","OC1CCCCO1","O=CCCCCO"],"correct":3,"why":"The internal hydroxyl adds to the aldehyde carbonyl to form a cyclic hemiacetal; counting the chain, the O and carbonyl carbon close a favored six-membered ring (a 2-hydroxytetrahydropyran). Option B is a five-membered ring (wrong ring size/atom count), C is a tetrahydropyran lacking the anomeric OH (that would be a full acetal, requiring a second alcohol), D is the fully reduced diol, and E is the unreacted open-chain aldehyde.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified with RDKit: option A is a 6-membered ring (2-hydroxytetrahydropyran), matching the intramolecular hemiacetal of 5-hydroxypentanal (C1...C5-O closes 6 atoms); option B is a 5-membered ring (wrong size). Correctly keyed at index 0. Distractors are wrong ring size, missing anomeric OH, diol, and starting material. In DAT scope."},{"q":"What is the major product of the following Wittig reaction? Benzaldehyde is treated with the non-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCH3 (generated from ethyltriphenylphosphonium bromide and n-BuLi).","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["C/C=C/c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A non-stabilized (alkylidene) ylide reacts with an aldehyde to give predominantly the Z (cis) alkene. The carbonyl O and the ylide CH-CH3 become the new C=C, so benzaldehyde + Ph3P=CHCH3 gives Z-1-phenylpropene. RDKit confirms opt0 is STEREOZ and opt1 (E, the stabilized-ylide trap) is STEREOE. Distractors styrene/allylbenzene/ethylbenzene are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. Substrate and all 5 option SMILES parse; Z geometry correctly keyed (index 0). Classic non-stabilized Wittig stereochemistry, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product when cyclohexanone reacts with the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Et (a carbethoxymethylene phosphorane).","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)/C=C/C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1=CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The Wittig replaces the ketone C=O with a C=C to the ylide carbon bearing CO2Et, giving the exocyclic alkene ethyl 2-cyclohexylideneacetate. Methylenecyclohexane drops the ester (wrong ylide); the trans-vinyl, saturated, and endocyclic esters are wrong skeletons/positions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; exocyclic ester alkene correctly keyed (index 0). Standard stabilized-ylide predict-product, in scope."},{"q":"What is the major organic product when acetophenone is treated with methylenetriphenylphosphorane (Ph3P=CH2)?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["C=CCc1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","C=C(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ph3P=CH2 delivers a CH2 that replaces the carbonyl oxygen, converting C=O into terminal C=CH2. Acetophenone (Ph-CO-CH3) becomes 2-phenylpropene (alpha-methylstyrene). The 1-phenylpropene isomers and cumene are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; alpha-methylstyrene correctly keyed (index 0). Methylenation of a ketone, in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product of the reaction of cyclohexanecarbaldehyde with the ylide Ph3P=CH2.","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCCCC1","opts":["C=CC1CCCCC1","CCC1CCCCC1","CC=C1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","C=CCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The aldehyde carbon (the CHO on the ring) becomes the alkene carbon; Ph3P=CH2 supplies the terminal =CH2, giving vinylcyclohexane. Methylenecyclohexane and ethylidenecyclohexane traps put the new double bond on a ring carbon instead of the former aldehyde carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; vinylcyclohexane correctly keyed (index 0). Standard methylenation of an aldehyde, in scope."},{"q":"What is the major product when p-anisaldehyde (4-methoxybenzaldehyde) reacts with the semi-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHC6H5 (benzylidenetriphenylphosphorane)?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts":["COc1ccc(/C=C\\c2ccccc2)cc1","COc1ccc(Cc2ccccc2)cc1","COc1ccc(C=C)cc1","COc1ccc(CCc2ccccc2)cc1","COc1ccc(/C=C/c2ccccc2)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A benzylic (semi-stabilized) ylide adds to the aldehyde to give a stilbene and favors the E (trans) alkene. So 4-methoxybenzaldehyde + Ph3P=CHPh gives (E)-4-methoxystilbene. RDKit confirms opt0 STEREOE, opt1 STEREOZ (geometry trap). Bibenzyl and one-carbon-link options are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; E-stilbene correctly keyed (index 0). Semi-stabilized ylide E-preference is standard textbook, in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product of the Wittig reaction between 3-pentanone (diethyl ketone) and Ph3P=CH2.","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts":["CC=C(C)CC","CC/C=C/CC","C=CC(CC)CC","C=C(CC)CC","CCC=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The central carbonyl carbon of 3-pentanone becomes a C=CH2 when Ph3P=CH2 swaps out the oxygen, giving 2-ethyl-1-butene. The 3-hexene and internal methylpentenes are constitutional-isomer traps moving the double bond into the chain rather than to the new exocyclic CH2.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; 2-ethyl-1-butene correctly keyed (index 0). Methylenation of a symmetric ketone, in scope."},{"q":"trans-Cinnamaldehyde is treated with the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2CH3. Predict the major product, including alkene geometry.","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)/C=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","COC(=O)/C=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","COC(=O)/C=C\\C=C/c1ccccc1","C=CC=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The stabilized ester ylide adds to the aldehyde carbon giving the E (trans) new double bond; the existing cinnamyl C=C stays E. Product is methyl (2E,4E)-5-phenylpenta-2,4-dienoate. RDKit confirms opt0 is E,E. The trap (opt1) has wrong geometry, the saturated ester loses both alkenes, and the acid option uses the wrong ylide.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Minor: opt1 is actually 2Z,4Z per RDKit (stem calls it the 2Z trap) but it remains a valid wrong-geometry distractor; correct answer (index 0, 2E,4E) unaffected. Stabilized-ylide regio+geometry recognition, within DAT predict-product scope."},{"q":"What is the major product when 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone is treated with Ph3P=CH2?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC1=CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","C=CC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ph3P=CH2 converts the ring carbonyl into an exocyclic =CH2, giving 4-tert-butylmethylenecyclohexane. The ethylidene option uses the wrong ylide, the endocyclic methylcyclohexene moves the double bond into the ring, and the tertiary alcohol is a non-Wittig (organometallic addition) product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; exocyclic methylenecyclohexane correctly keyed (index 0). Standard ketone methylenation, in scope."},{"q":"The ketone shown is converted into methylenecyclohexane (a terminal alkene, C=CH2 replacing the C=O). Which reagent accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Ph3P=CH2 (methylenetriphenylphosphorane)","H2, Pd/C","PCC in CH2Cl2","CH3MgBr, then H3O+","NaBH4, then H3O+"],"correct":0,"why":"Wittig with the unstabilized methylene ylide Ph3P=CH2 converts cyclohexanone to methylenecyclohexane. Grignard gives a tertiary alcohol, NaBH4 a secondary alcohol, PCC oxidizes, H2/Pd/C reduces alkenes. Only option 0 makes the C=CH2.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid (cyclohexanone). Reagent-selection Wittig, single concept, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (shown) is to be converted to (E)-stilbene, PhCH=CHPh, with high E-selectivity. Which reagent is best suited?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Zn(Hg), HCl (Clemmensen)","PhLi, then H3O+","LiAlH4, then H3O+","An unstabilized ylide, Ph3P=CH2","A resonance-stabilized ylide, Ph3P=CHPh (benzylidene phosphorane)"],"correct":4,"why":"The benzylidene ylide Ph3P=CHPh is conjugatively (resonance) stabilized by the phenyl and is the only reagent that builds the PhCH=CHPh skeleton, giving predominantly the E (trans) stilbene. Ph3P=CH2 lacks the second phenyl; the others are addition/reduction reagents that do not form the C=C.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct, keep. Minor terminology nuance: benzylidene ylide is more precisely semi-stabilized, but it IS resonance-stabilized by phenyl and gives E, so option 0 is the single defensible answer."},{"q":"The alkene shown, (E)-2-pentene, is the target of a Wittig disconnection. Which carbonyl + ylide pair would form it (the new double bond is between the two central alkene carbons)?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["CH3CH2CHO + CH3MgBr","CH3CH2CH2CHO + Ph3P=O","CH3CHO + Ph3P=CHCH2CH3 (propylidene ylide)","CH3CH2CH2CH2CHO + Ph3P=CH2","Acetone + Ph3P=CHCH3"],"correct":2,"why":"Disconnecting CH3-CH=CH-CH2CH3 at the central C=C gives CH3CHO (the CH3CH= carbon) plus the propylidene ylide Ph3P=CHCH2CH3 (the =CHCH2CH3 carbon). Connectivity is uniquely satisfied by option 0; the others give wrong skeletons or are not Wittig partners (Ph3P=O is the byproduct).","difficulty":2,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Connectivity correct, one defensible answer (only option 0 gives the 2-pentene skeleton). Caveat: propylidene is non-stabilized so it would actually favor Z, mildly inconsistent with the (E) label in the stem; but the question keys on the disconnection/skeleton, so it stays unambiguous. Keep."},{"q":"Ethyl (E)-cinnamate, the conjugated ester-alkene shown, is the product of a Wittig reaction on benzaldehyde. The reaction proceeds with high E-selectivity. What type of ylide was used, and why is the geometry E?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A semi-stabilized ylide, which gives only the Z alkene","A stabilized ylide, which favors the cis (Z) alkene","A stabilized ylide (ester-conjugated), which favors the trans (E) alkene","A non-stabilized ylide, which favors the cis (Z) alkene","A non-stabilized ylide, which favors the trans (E) alkene"],"correct":2,"why":"The ylide carbon bears an ester (EWG), so it is a stabilized ylide; stabilized ylides give predominantly the E (trans) alkene, matching ethyl (E)-cinnamate. The stabilized=E pairing is uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid (ethyl E-cinnamate). Two-concept (ylide type + stereochemistry rule), one defensible answer."},{"q":"The product shown is alpha-methylstyrene (2-phenylpropene). Which carbonyl/ylide combination gives it via a Wittig reaction?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Benzaldehyde (PhCHO) + Ph3P=CHCH3","Propiophenone (PhCOCH2CH3) + Ph3P=CH2","Acetophenone + Ph3P=O","Acetone + Ph3P=CHPh","Acetophenone (PhCOCH3) + Ph3P=CH2"],"correct":4,"why":"The =CH2 comes from Ph3P=CH2 and the C(CH3)(Ph)= carbon from the carbonyl, requiring acetophenone. Benzaldehyde + ethylidene gives beta-methylstyrene (different connectivity); acetone + benzylidene gives PhCH=C(CH3)2; propiophenone gives a different alkene; Ph3P=O is a byproduct.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid. Disconnection uniquely fixes acetophenone + Ph3P=CH2; distractors are genuine regiochemistry/skeleton variants. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclopentanone (shown) is treated with Ph3P=CHCH3 to give ethylidenecyclopentane plus triphenylphosphine oxide. How is this reaction best classified?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An aldol condensation","A Wittig olefination (carbonyl-to-alkene via a phosphorus ylide)","An E2 elimination","A Grignard addition","A nucleophilic acyl substitution"],"correct":1,"why":"A phosphorus ylide + carbonyl forming C=C and expelling Ph3P=O is the defining Wittig signature. Aldol gives a beta-hydroxy carbonyl, acyl substitution needs a leaving group on the carbonyl, Grignard gives an alcohol, E2 needs a leaving group on a saturated substrate.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Reaction-type classification, one defensible answer. q_smiles valid (cyclopentanone)."},{"q":"Propanal (shown) is treated with the non-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCH2CH2CH3 (a butylidene ylide) under salt-free conditions. Which statement about the product alkene is correct?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The product is 3-heptene, formed predominantly as the E (trans) isomer","No reaction occurs because aldehydes are unreactive toward ylides","The product is heptanal, with no new C=C","The product is 3-heptene, formed predominantly as the Z (cis) isomer","The product is 2-heptanone, by nucleophilic addition"],"correct":3,"why":"Joining propanal's carbonyl carbon (CH3CH2CH=) to the butylidene ylide carbon (=CHCH2CH2CH3) gives CH3CH2CH=CHCH2CH2CH3 (3-heptene). Non-stabilized ylides under salt-free conditions give predominantly the Z (cis) alkene. Other options misstate the chemistry.","difficulty":2,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Connectivity verified (7 carbons, 3-heptene). Non-stabilized + salt-free -> Z is standard textbook. Two-concept (connectivity + Z-selectivity), one defensible answer. q_smiles valid (propanal)."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with the stabilized phosphorus ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Et (carbethoxymethylenetriphenylphosphorane). Which structure is the major Wittig product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["CCOC(=O)C=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)CC1=CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C=C1CCC(=O)CC1","CCOC(=O)C=C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Wittig replaces the carbonyl C=O with a C=C to the ylide carbon at the original carbonyl carbon, giving the exocyclic alkene ethyl 2-cyclohexylideneacetate. Distractors move the alkene into the ring (endocyclic), saturate the linkage, shrink the ring, or add a spurious ketone.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; exocyclic alkene at carbonyl C is the right product; distractors are clean regio/skeleton variants. Single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde reacts with the NON-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCH2CH3 (an unstabilized propylidene ylide) under salt-free conditions. Which alkene is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCC=Cc1ccccc1","C/C=C\\Cc1ccccc1","CC/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC/C=C\\c1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Non-stabilized ylides under salt-free conditions give predominantly the Z (cis) alkene via the kinetic cis-oxaphosphetane. The new C=C joins the aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, giving (Z)-1-phenyl-1-butene. Option 0 is Z; trans (option 1) is the classic trap.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit confirms option 0 is the Z isomer, option 1 the E. Standard non-stabilized-ylide stereochemistry. In scope."},{"q":"In the Wittig reaction of phenylacetaldehyde (drawn) with a phosphorus ylide, the first carbon-carbon bond-forming step is the nucleophilic addition of the ylide. Which single curved arrow correctly represents this first bond formation?","q_smiles":"O=CCc1ccccc1","opts":["From the phosphorus to the carbonyl carbon","From the aldehyde C-H bond to the ylide carbon","From the carbanionic ylide carbon to the carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde","From the C=O pi bond to the phosphorus atom","From the carbonyl oxygen lone pair to the ylide carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The ylide carbon is the nucleophile; the first bond-forming arrow goes from that carbanionic carbon to the electrophilic carbonyl carbon, forming the betaine/oxaphosphetane C-C bond. Phosphorus binds oxygen only later, and the carbonyl carbon (not P) is the first electrophile attacked.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single-arrow identification, which the scope rules explicitly permit; not a full mechanism. q_smiles valid. Exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Butanone (methyl ethyl ketone, drawn) is treated with methylenetriphenylphosphorane, Ph3P=CH2. Which is the Wittig product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)=O","opts":["CCC(C)C","C=C(C)CC","C/C=C(\\C)CC","C/C=C/CC","C=CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methylenation converts C=O into a terminal =CH2 at the original carbonyl carbon, giving 2-methyl-1-butene. No E/Z issue arises (the new alkene carbon bears two H). Distractors add a carbon, reduce, or misplace the double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Option 0 = 2-methyl-1-butene, exactly the methylenation product. SMILES valid. Single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclopentanecarbaldehyde (drawn) reacts with the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Me. Which product, with correct alkene geometry, predominates?","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCCC1","opts":["COC(=O)/C=C\\C1CCCC1","COC(=O)/C=C/C1CCCC1","COC(=O)C=C1CCCC1","COC(=O)CCC1CCCC1","COC(=O)CC1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Stabilized (ester) ylides give the thermodynamically favored E (trans) alkene. The new C=C joins the aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, giving methyl (E)-3-cyclopentylacrylate. Option 0 is E; the Z isomer (option 1) is the trap.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit confirms option 0 is E, option 1 Z. Stabilized-ylide E-selectivity is standard. In scope (Wittig + geometry recognition)."},{"q":"A chemist wants to convert 2-pentanone (drawn) into 2-methyl-1-pentene (terminal exocyclic-type alkene at the carbonyl carbon). Which reagent accomplishes this in one step?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)=O","opts":["Ph3P=CHCO2Et (stabilized ester ylide)","Ph3P=CH2 (methylenetriphenylphosphorane)","NaBH4, then H2SO4 heat","PCC in CH2Cl2","Ph3P=CHCH3 (ethylidene ylide)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"To install a terminal =CH2 on the original carbonyl carbon (2-methyl-1-pentene), the ylide must carry only CH2: Ph3P=CH2. An ethylidene ylide adds an extra carbon, the ester ylide installs CO2Et, and reduction/oxidation reagents do not olefinate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Target 2-methyl-1-pentene = methylenation of 2-pentanone; Ph3P=CH2 is the unique one-step reagent. q_smiles valid. In scope."},{"q":"Butanal (drawn) is treated with the SEMI-stabilized benzylidene ylide Ph3P=CHC6H5 under standard (lithium-salt-containing) conditions. Which alkene is the major geometric isomer expected?","q_smiles":"CCCC=O","opts":["CCC/C=C\\c1ccccc1","CCCCc1ccccc1","CCC/C=C/c1ccccc1","CCCC=Cc1ccccc1","CCC/C=C/Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Benzylidene ylides are semi-stabilized (carbanion conjugated into the ring), so they favor the E (trans) alkene, especially with lithium salts present. The new C=C links the aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, giving predominantly (E)-1-phenyl-1-pentene. Z is minor.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit confirms option 0 is E. Semi-stabilized benzylidene E-preference is the standard textbook teaching; real selectivity is modest but the keyed answer is the defensible major isomer. Two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"The keto-aldehyde 5-oxohexanal (drawn) is treated with exactly one equivalent of the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Et. Predict the major product, accounting for which carbonyl reacts AND the alkene geometry.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCCC=O","opts":["CCOC(=O)/C=C\\CCCC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)/C=C/CCCC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)/C=C/CCC/C=C/C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)/C=C(\\C)CCCC=O","CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Two layers: chemoselectivity and geometry. The more electrophilic, less hindered aldehyde reacts preferentially over the ketone with a limited amount of a stabilized ylide, leaving the methyl ketone intact; the stabilized ylide gives the E (trans) acrylate. Distractors react at the ketone, give Z, do a double Wittig, or form no alkene.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit: mono-Wittig products (0,1,2) are C10H16O3, double (3) is C13H20O4. Option 0 = E-acrylate at the aldehyde, ketone intact. Two layered concepts matched to difficulty 3; single addition event, in scope."},{"q":"Acetaldehyde is treated with dilute aqueous NaOH followed by warming. What is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC=O","opts":["C/C=C/C=O","O=CCCO","CC(O)CC=O","CC=CC=O","CCC=CC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two acetaldehyde molecules undergo aldol addition (enolate adds to the second carbonyl) to give 3-hydroxybutanal, which dehydrates on warming to the conjugated (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde). The trans-conjugated isomer is the major product. Keyed E-SMILES is correct; unspecified-geometry C4 enal, wrong-chain pentenal, 3-hydroxypropanal, and non-dehydrated beta-hydroxy aldehyde are valid distractors. All 5 options unique and parseable.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed (E)-but-2-enal is the correct conjugated condensation product; single concept, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Propanal is treated with a catalytic amount of NaOH at low temperature (no heating). What is the major product of this aldol reaction?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CCCC(O)C=O","CCC(O)C(C)CO","CCC(O)C(C)C=O","CC=C(C)C=O","CCC(O)CC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Propanal has a single alpha-carbon (the CH2). Its enolate adds to a second propanal carbonyl giving the aldol ADDITION product 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (no heat = no dehydration). Keyed structure is the unique correct adduct. Distractors: the 2-methylpent-2-enal condensation product, a wrong-connectivity adduct (enolate at non-alpha methyl), and an over-reduced diol. All valid, unique.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single alpha center so no regiochemical ambiguity; addition product correctly keyed; in scope."},{"q":"Acetone is treated with base and then heated to drive off water. What is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["CC=C(C)C(C)=O","CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)(C)O","CC(=O)C=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetone self-aldol gives diacetone alcohol (4-hydroxy-4-methylpentan-2-one) which dehydrates on heating to mesityl oxide, 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one. Keyed enone is correct (C6H10O). Distractors: non-dehydrated beta-hydroxy ketone, isomeric/unrelated diketones. All valid and unique.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Mesityl oxide correctly keyed; classic textbook reaction, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with acetone and aqueous NaOH, then warmed. Benzaldehyde has no alpha-hydrogens. What is the major crossed-aldol condensation product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1CC(C)=O","OC(/C=C/c1ccccc1)C","O=C(C)CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=C(C)C=Cc1ccc(C=O)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Only acetone can enolize; its enolate adds to benzaldehyde and dehydrates to (E)-4-phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone), fully conjugated with ring and carbonyl. Keyed E-enone correct (C10H10O). Distractors: non-dehydrated aldol, wrong-partner regiochemistry, and a reduced allylic alcohol. All valid, unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Crossed aldol (one partner lacks alpha-H) correctly resolved to benzalacetone; regio+stereo two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with benzaldehyde and NaOH, then warmed (Claisen-Schmidt conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(c1ccccc1)/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)C=Cc1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)CCc1ccccc1","OC(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Benzaldehyde has no alpha-H, so acetophenone's methyl is deprotonated; the enolate adds to benzaldehyde and dehydrates to (E)-chalcone, (E)-1,3-diphenylprop-2-en-1-one (C15H12O). Keyed E-enone correct. Distractors: non-dehydrated aldol, over-reduced 1,3-diphenylpropan-1-one, a reduced-ketone alcohol, and the unspecified-geometry chalcone. All valid, unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic Claisen-Schmidt to (E)-chalcone correctly keyed; in scope."},{"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione is treated with aqueous NaOH (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1C","CC1CCC(=O)C1=O","CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","O=C1CCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"An enolate at a terminal methyl attacks the other carbonyl intramolecularly to close a favorable five-membered ring; dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one (C6H8O). Keyed structure correct. Distractors: cyclopentane-1,2-dione (no aldol), a regioisomeric enone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and an extra-methyl ring. All valid, unique.","difficulty":3,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified 5-ring conjugated enone (3-methylcyclopent-2-enone) is the major product; ring-size + regiochemistry, in scope as a single-concept predict-product."},{"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione is treated with NaOH and warmed (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CC(=O)CCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Intramolecular aldol closing the favored six-membered ring then dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (C7H10O). Keyed opt0 is the conjugated enone (verified identical to O=C1CCCC(C)=C1). Distractors: a non-conjugated methylcyclohexenone regioisomer, plain cyclohexanone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and a 5-membered ring. All valid, unique.","difficulty":3,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Confirmed keyed structure is the conjugated 3-methylcyclohex-2-enone, opt1 is the non-conjugated regioisomer distractor; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with formaldehyde (HCHO) and a small amount of NaOH at room temperature. Formaldehyde has no alpha-hydrogens and is a very reactive electrophile. What is the major crossed-aldol product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1=CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1=CO","OCC1CCCCC1=O","OC1CCCCC1C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Only cyclohexanone enolizes (formaldehyde has no alpha-H). The enolate adds to the reactive formaldehyde carbon, installing a hydroxymethyl group alpha to the ketone, giving 2-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-one (C7H12O2); mild conditions stop at the beta-hydroxy ketone. Keyed structure correct. Distractors: a dehydrated/enol form, an exocyclic enol, a wrong-connectivity aldehyde alcohol, and an over-reduced product. All valid, unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hydroxymethylation alpha to the ketone correctly keyed as 2-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexanone; mono-addition framing is clean, in scope."},{"q":"Acetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with dilute aqueous NaOH, then warmed. After loss of water, the major product is crotonaldehyde (CH3CH=CHCHO). What type of reaction converts the β-hydroxy aldehyde intermediate into crotonaldehyde?","q_smiles":"CC=O","opts":["Conjugate (1,4) addition","E1cb dehydration (aldol condensation)","Cannizzaro disproportionation","Retro-aldol cleavage","Nucleophilic acyl substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Aldol addition gives 3-hydroxybutanal; on warming, base removes the alpha-H to form an enolate and water is eliminated by an E1cb pathway, giving the conjugated enal crotonaldehyde. This dehydration is what makes it an aldol condensation. Distractors (Cannizzaro needs no alpha-H aldehyde, NAS needs a leaving group, 1,4-addition needs a preformed enone, retro-aldol breaks C-C) are all wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CC=O valid; E1cb dehydration step correctly identified; single-concept reaction-type ID, in scope."},{"q":"Acetone (drawn) undergoes base-catalyzed self-aldol to give the β-hydroxy ketone diacetone alcohol; on heating with acid it loses water to give mesityl oxide, (CH3)2C=CHCOCH3. Which single reagent/condition set best accomplishes the overall conversion of acetone into mesityl oxide?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["NaBH4 in methanol","LiAlH4 then H3O+","Dilute NaOH at low temperature only","Catalytic base for the addition, then acid and heat to dehydrate","O3 then Zn/H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Ketone aldol is reversible and stops at diacetone alcohol under mild base; driving it to the enone (mesityl oxide) requires dehydration pushed by acid and heat. LiAlH4/NaBH4 reduce, ozonolysis cleaves, and cold dilute base alone stops at the beta-hydroxy ketone. Only the base-then-acid/heat sequence completes the condensation.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CC(C)=O valid; index 1 right; two-concept (addition + dehydration conditions), in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is combined with acetophenone (C6H5COCH3) under aqueous NaOH to give (E)-chalcone, C6H5COCH=CHC6H5, in high yield as a single crossed-aldol product. Why is this crossed aldol clean rather than a mixture?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["The reaction proceeds by a radical chain mechanism","Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen, so only acetophenone forms the enolate","Both partners form enolates equally","Benzaldehyde is the only enolate source","Acetophenone has no α-hydrogen"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A clean Claisen-Schmidt crossed aldol needs one non-enolizable partner. Benzaldehyde lacks alpha-H so it can only be the electrophile; the enolate must come from acetophenone (which DOES have alpha-H, so option 1 is false). The single enolate adds and dehydrates to (E)-chalcone. Index 0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. O=Cc1ccccc1 valid; only one defensible answer (option 1 is a true-sounding but false trap); in scope."},{"q":"The symmetric dienone shown, (1E,4E)-1,5-diphenylpenta-1,4-dien-3-one (dibenzylideneacetone), is a classic crossed-aldol condensation product. Which pair of starting materials and conditions makes it?","q_smiles":"O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["1 equiv benzaldehyde + 1 equiv acetone, NaOH","2 equiv benzaldehyde + 1 equiv acetone, NaOH","2 equiv acetophenone + 1 equiv formaldehyde, NaOH","2 equiv benzaldehyde + 1 equiv acetaldehyde, NaOH","1 equiv benzaldehyde + 2 equiv acetone, H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Acetone has alpha-H on both sides, so each methyl enolizes, adds to a non-enolizable benzaldehyde, and dehydrates, giving the symmetric doubly conjugated dienone (DBA). Requires 2 benzaldehyde : 1 acetone under base. Index 0 correct; distractors give mono-condensation or wrong skeletons.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. DBA SMILES valid (E,E geometry parses); stoichiometry 2:1 correct; two-concept (reactivity + stoichiometry), in scope."},{"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH and warmed. The major product is 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one. This transformation is best classified as which kind of reaction?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","opts":["Robinson annulation","Cannizzaro reaction","Intramolecular aldol condensation","Intermolecular crossed aldol condensation","Claisen condensation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A terminal methyl enolate attacks the distal carbonyl carbon, closing a six-membered ring beta-hydroxy ketone that dehydrates to the conjugated enone (3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, C7H10O, loss of H2O verified). Both reacting centers are in the same molecule, so it is an intramolecular aldol condensation. Claisen needs an ester; Robinson needs a Michael donor/enone; Cannizzaro needs a non-enolizable aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Regiochemistry verified atom-by-atom (terminal-methyl enolate -> distal carbonyl -> 6-ring -> 3-methylcyclohex-2-enone); index 0 right; in scope."},{"q":"In a base-catalyzed crossed aldol, propanal (drawn) and acetone compete as the electrophilic carbonyl that accepts the enolate. Which carbonyl is the more reactive electrophile toward nucleophilic addition, and why?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["Propanal, because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and less stabilized than the ketone","They are equally reactive electrophiles","Acetone, because it is more sterically accessible","Acetone, because two alkyl groups make it more electrophilic","Propanal, because aldehydes have no α-hydrogens"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are more electrophilic than ketones: a ketone's two electron-donating alkyl groups stabilize the partial positive carbonyl carbon and add steric bulk. The less hindered, less stabilized aldehyde carbon accepts the nucleophile preferentially. Index 0 correct; option 4 is a true statement about propanal but the wrong reason (it has alpha-H anyway and that is irrelevant to electrophilicity).","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CCC=O valid; aldehyde > ketone electrophilicity reasoning sound; only one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"The molecule drawn, 3-hydroxybutanal, is the direct product of a base-catalyzed aldol of a single simple carbonyl compound before any dehydration. Which starting carbonyl, used as both nucleophile and electrophile, gives this β-hydroxy aldehyde?","q_smiles":"CC(O)CC=O","opts":["Acetaldehyde (two equivalents)","Formaldehyde (two equivalents)","Acetone (two equivalents)","Benzaldehyde (two equivalents)","Propanal (two equivalents)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"3-Hydroxybutanal (C4H8O2) is the self-aldol of two acetaldehyde units (2 x C2H4O = C4H8O2, verified): one molecule's enolate (-CH2CHO) adds to the carbonyl of another, placing OH beta to the aldehyde. Acetone self-aldol gives a C6 ketone, propanal gives a C6 alpha-methyl aldehyde, formaldehyde and benzaldehyde have no alpha-H and cannot self-condense. Index 0 uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CC(O)CC=O valid; carbon-count to acetaldehyde verified; arguably difficulty 1 (single-concept recognition) but 2 is acceptable; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (drawn) is treated with excess formaldehyde (HCHO) and dilute NaOH. Formaldehyde is chosen as the second partner specifically to control the crossed aldol. What property of formaldehyde makes it useful here?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["It is the strongest enolate-forming carbonyl available","It has no α-hydrogens, so it cannot enolize and serves only as the electrophile","It is non-electrophilic and only acts as a base","It prevents any dehydration from occurring","It reduces cyclohexanone to the alcohol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Formaldehyde has no alpha-hydrogens, so it cannot form an enolate; it acts only as the (very reactive, unhindered) electrophilic acceptor. The cyclohexanone enolate adds to it, giving clean hydroxymethylation instead of cyclohexanone self-condensation. Index 0 correct; option 5 wrongly calls it non-electrophilic, the others invent reduction/anti-dehydration roles.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. O=C1CCCCC1 valid; chemistry sound; single non-enolizable-partner concept (same as Q3) so difficulty 3 is inflated, lowered to 2; in scope, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with dilute NaOH and then warmed. Which structure is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC=O","opts":["C/C=C\\C=O","C=CC=O","CC(O)CC=O","C/C=C/C=O","CCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Self-aldol of acetaldehyde gives 3-hydroxybutanal, which dehydrates on warming to conjugated (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde). RDKit confirms opt0 is the E isomer (CH3/CHO trans = favored). opt1 is minor Z, opt2 is the un-dehydrated aldol, opt3 (acrolein) loses a carbon, opt4 (propanal) is no reaction. All 5 SMILES valid. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Valid, in scope, exactly one defensible answer; E geometry correctly drawn in opt0."},{"q":"Propanal (drawn) is treated with a catalytic amount of NaOH at low temperature (no heat). Which structure is the major aldol ADDITION product?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CC/C=C(C)/C=O","CCC(O)C(C)C=O","CCCC(O)C(C)=O","CCC(O)CCC=O","CCC(=O)C(C)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Propanal enolate forms at the alpha CH2 (bearing a methyl), adding to a second propanal to give 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (opt0, C6H12O2). opt1 omits the alpha-methyl (wrong connectivity), opt2 is the dehydrated condensation enal (not the addition product asked), opt3/opt4 have wrong oxidation/regiochem. All SMILES valid. No-heat correctly stops at addition. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Stereocenters left unspecified is fine for an identity question; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is mixed with acetophenone (PhCOCH3) and aqueous NaOH, then warmed. Which structure is the major crossed-aldol condensation product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(/C=C\\c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=C(Cc1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1.CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(CC(O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Only acetophenone has alpha H, so it is the enolate; benzaldehyde (no alpha H) is the electrophile. Directed crossed aldol then dehydration gives (E)-chalcone (opt0, RDKit confirms STEREOE = the two aryl/acyl groups trans, favored). opt1 minor Z, opt2 the beta-hydroxy ketone, opt3 unreacted SM mixture, opt4 deoxygenated. All SMILES valid. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Classic directed crossed aldol; one defensible answer, correct E geometry."},{"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione (drawn) is treated with NaOH and heated, undergoing an intramolecular aldol condensation. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1CCC(=O)C1O","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Terminal-methyl enolate attacks the distal carbonyl to close a favored 5-membered ring; dehydration gives the conjugated enone 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one. I re-derived the mechanism: product canonicalizes to opt0 (CC1=CC(=O)CC1), which RDKit confirms is the conjugated C=C-C=O. opt2 is the non-conjugated beta,gamma isomer, opt5 is the un-dehydrated aldol, opt1/opt4 are wrong ring sizes. All SMILES valid. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Verified opt0 is the conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-enone via mechanistic re-derivation; in scope (single ring closure + dehydration)."},{"q":"In a crossed aldol reaction, ethyl acetoacetate (drawn) is often chosen as the nucleophilic partner instead of a simple ketone. Which statement best explains its behavior under mild base?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["It undergoes dehydration before any C-C bond forms","Its central CH2 is flanked by two carbonyls, so it is readily and selectively deprotonated to a stabilized enolate that acts as the nucleophile","It is deprotonated at the terminal ester oxygen to form an alkoxide nucleophile","Mild base reduces its ketone to an alcohol before the aldol step","It has no alpha hydrogens, so it can only serve as the electrophilic carbonyl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The central CH2 sits between a ketone and an ester carbonyl, so it is selectively deprotonated to a doubly stabilized enolate (the active-methylene nucleophile). opt0 is correct. opt1 is false (it clearly has acidic alpha H), opt2 misplaces deprotonation, opt3/opt4 invent reduction/pre-dehydration. q_smiles drawn and valid. Qualitative acidity (no pKa math) = in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Reagent-rationale (text opts), molecule drawn, qualitative acidity only; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetone (drawn) is treated with two equivalents of benzaldehyde and NaOH, then warmed. Which structure is the major product of this double crossed-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["O=C(CC(O)c1ccccc1)CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=C(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(/C=C\\c1ccccc1)/C=C\\c1ccccc1","O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)/C=C/c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetone has two sets of alpha H; with excess benzaldehyde both sides condense and dehydrate to (1E,4E)-dibenzylideneacetone. RDKit confirms opt0 is E,E (favored). opt1 is only mono-condensation, opt2 the double aldol before dehydration, opt3 the disfavored Z,Z, opt5 deoxygenated. All SMILES valid. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Two-fold condensation but single concept (crossed aldol); correct E,E geometry; in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess acetaldehyde and NaOH, then heated. Acetaldehyde is in excess so it provides the enolate. Which structure is the major crossed product, and what governs its alkene geometry?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC=O","O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Benzaldehyde (no alpha H) is the electrophile; the acetaldehyde enolate is the nucleophile. Addition then dehydration gives (E)-cinnamaldehyde (opt0, RDKit confirms STEREOE = phenyl trans to CHO, conjugation/sterics favored). opt1 minor Z, opt2 the beta-hydroxy addition product, opt3 (phenylacetaldehyde)/opt4 (3-phenylpropanal) wrong connectivity. All SMILES valid. Geometry rationale is qualitative (conjugation/sterics), not FMO. Keyed answer correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Compound stem (product + qualitative E rationale) is still a two-concept item with one structure answer; no grad-level theory; in scope, correct E geometry."},{"q":"Propiophenone is treated with LDA (1 equiv) in THF at -78 C, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br) is added. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCC(=O)c1ccc(Cc2ccccc2)cc1","CCC(Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=Cc1ccccc1)CCc1ccccc1","CC(C(=O)c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C(=O)c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"LDA deprotonates the single alpha CH2 of propiophenone (PhCO-CH2-CH3) to give the enolate, which alkylates benzyl bromide by SN2 at that carbon, giving PhCO-CH(CH3)-CH2Ph. Distractors put benzyl on the wrong carbon, dialkylate, alkylate the aromatic ring, or show the enol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. Stem and all five SMILES valid; opt0 (PhCOCH(CH3)CH2Ph) is the genuine alpha-benzylation product, key index 0 correct."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with Br2 (1 equiv) in acetic acid. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["BrC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1Br","O=C1CCC(Br)CC1","O=C1CCCC(Br)C1","BrC1=CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed alpha-halogenation goes through the enol, which brominates at the alpha carbon adjacent to the carbonyl, giving 2-bromocyclohexanone. Distractors brominate a beta or gamma carbon, remove the carbonyl, or show an enol bromide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. opt0 (2-bromocyclohexanone) is the alpha product; opt2/opt3 are non-alpha distractors; key index 0 correct, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with excess I2 and NaOH, and the mixture is then acidified with HCl. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1.[Na+]","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","C(C(=O)c1ccccc1)(Cl)(Cl)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A methyl ketone with excess halogen and hydroxide undergoes the haloform reaction: the CH3 is triiodinated, then hydroxide cleaves the C-C bond to give benzoate plus iodoform (CHI3). The stem acidifies with HCl, so the isolated product is benzoic acid (opt0). The carboxylate (opt1) is the pre-acidification species; the trichloromethyl ketone, alcohol, and aldehyde are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. Because the stem explicitly acidifies, neutral benzoic acid (opt0) is right rather than the Na carboxylate (opt1, a strong but defeated distractor). Key index 0 correct."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone is treated with LDA in THF at -78 C, then CH3I is added. What is the major product (kinetic enolate)?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1C","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1(C)C","O=C1CCCC(C)C1C","O=C1CCCCC1CC","CC1CCCCC1=O","O=C1C(C)CCCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Bulky LDA forms the kinetic enolate by removing the less-hindered alpha proton at C6 (away from the existing 2-methyl), and methylation there gives 2,6-dimethylcyclohexanone, i.e. both methyls on the two alpha carbons flanking the carbonyl. That structure is opt4 (O=C1C(C)CCCC1C, canonical CC1CCCC(C)C1=O). The originally keyed opt0 (O=C1CCCC(C)C1C) is actually 2,3-dimethylcyclohexanone (one methyl alpha, one methyl beta), which is NOT the kinetic product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"MIS-KEYED: keyed opt0 is 2,3-dimethylcyclohexanone (methyl on C2 and C3), not 2,6. The true 2,6-dimethylcyclohexanone kinetic product is opt4. Corrected correct to index 4. Chemistry/scope/SMILES otherwise sound, so keep=true with the fixed key."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with one equivalent of sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), then one equivalent of ethyl bromide (CH3CH2Br). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(CC)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(CC)(CC)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(CCC)C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The central CH2 flanked by two esters is acidic; ethoxide deprotonates it and the stabilized enolate is ethylated at that carbon. With one equiv each, only mono-alkylation occurs, giving diethyl 2-ethylmalonate (opt0). Distractors add a methyl or propyl group, alkylate a non-acidic carbon, or dialkylate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. opt0 (diethyl 2-ethylmalonate) is the mono-ethylated product; the di-ethyl distractor (opt4) is correctly defeated by the 1-equiv stoichiometry. Key index 0 correct, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Butanoic acid is treated with Br2 and a catalytic amount of PBr3 (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky conditions), followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["CCC(Br)C(=O)O","BrCCCC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)Br","CCC(Br)C(=O)Br","CC(Br)CC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"HVZ brominates a carboxylic acid selectively at the alpha carbon: PBr3 forms the acyl bromide, whose enol brominates at the alpha position, and aqueous workup regenerates the COOH, giving 2-bromobutanoic acid (opt0). Distractors brominate the beta (opt1) or terminal carbon (opt2), give the acyl bromide (opt3), or over-brominate (opt4).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. opt0 (2-bromobutanoic acid) is the HVZ alpha-bromo acid; opt1 (3-bromo) is the key regiochemical distractor. Key index 0 correct, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Phenyl-2-propanone (phenylacetone, PhCH2COCH3) is treated with LDA, then CH3I. Deprotonation favors the more resonance-stabilized (benzylic) enolate. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)C(CC)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccc(C)cc1","CC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The benzylic alpha carbon (PhCH2) gives the more resonance-stabilized enolate because charge delocalizes into the ring, and the stem specifies that enolate, so methylation occurs there to give PhCH(CH3)COCH3 (opt0). Distractors methylate the methyl-side alpha carbon, alkylate the ring, add the wrong group, or dialkylate the benzylic carbon.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. The stem explicitly supplies the benzylic-enolate premise, which resolves the LDA kinetic-vs-stabilized ambiguity and leaves exactly one defensible answer (opt0, PhCH(CH3)COCH3). Single concept (regioselective alkylation), in scope; key index 0 correct."},{"q":"An unsymmetrical ketone is treated with excess I2 and NaOH (the iodoform reaction). The substrate shown below is acetophenone (CC(=O)c1ccccc1). After acidic workup, which pair of products is obtained?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Benzoic acid (PhCOOH) plus iodoform (CHI3)","Phenylacetic acid plus CHI3","alpha-iodoacetophenone plus NaI","Iodobenzene plus acetic acid","Benzaldehyde plus CHI3"],"correct":0,"why":"Acetophenone is a methyl ketone (CH3 next to C=O), so it undergoes the haloform reaction. The three alpha-H's are sequentially replaced by I, then hydroxide cleaves the C-C bond to give the carboxylate (benzoate -> benzoic acid after workup) and the triiodomethyl anion, which picks up a proton to form iodoform (CHI3, a yellow precipitate).","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Methyl ketone -> haloform cleavage: benzoic acid + CHI3 (index 0). q_smiles valid, classic Booster-level recall, single correct answer."},{"q":"A student wants to install a single Br atom at the alpha-carbon of the carboxylic acid shown (butanoic acid, CCCC(=O)O) to make 2-bromobutanoic acid. Which set of reagents accomplishes this alpha-halogenation of a carboxylic acid?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NBS with light (radical conditions)","Br2 with a catalytic amount of PBr3 (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky), then H2O","HBr with peroxides","Br2 with FeBr3","Br2 in CCl4 only"],"correct":1,"why":"Carboxylic acids do not enolize readily, so direct Br2 fails. The Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky (HVZ) reaction uses Br2 with catalytic PBr3 (or red P): PBr3 converts the acid to the acyl bromide, which enolizes and is brominated at the alpha-carbon; aqueous workup regenerates the acid as the alpha-bromo carboxylic acid. NBS/light and Br2/peroxides are radical (allylic/free-radical) brominations, and Br2/FeBr3 is electrophilic aromatic, none of which targets the alpha-C of an acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. HVZ (Br2/cat. PBr3) is the textbook alpha-halogenation of a carboxylic acid (index 0). Distractors are mechanistically distinct (radical/EAS). In scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (O=C1CCCCC1) exists in equilibrium with a small amount of its enol tautomer. Which statement about this keto-enol tautomerism is correct?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Keto and enol are resonance structures, not tautomers, so they cannot be separated even in principle","The enol form predominates because the C=C bond is stronger than the C=O bond","The keto form predominates because the C=O bond plus C-H bonds are more stable than the C=C plus O-H bonds of the enol","The two tautomers are non-superimposable mirror images (enantiomers)","Tautomerization requires breaking the C-C sigma bond of the ring"],"correct":2,"why":"For a simple ketone the keto form is strongly favored (>99.9%) because the carbonyl C=O bond is much stronger than the enol C=C bond. Keto and enol are constitutional isomers (tautomers) that interconvert by moving an alpha-H to oxygen and shifting a pi bond, NOT resonance structures (atoms actually move). They are not enantiomers and no C-C bond breaks.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Keto favored; tautomers are constitutional isomers, not resonance (index 0). Distractors capture the classic tautomer-vs-resonance trap. In scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Pentane-2,4-dione (acetylacetone, CC(=O)CC(=O)C) is shown. Which proton is the MOST acidic, and why is this compound unusually acidic (pKa ~9) compared to a simple ketone (pKa ~20)?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The proton is on oxygen (it is an enol), so it ionizes like an alcohol","The central CH2; it is acidic only because of the inductive effect of two sp2 carbons","A terminal CH3 proton; the resulting anion is stabilized by hyperconjugation","The central CH2 between the two carbonyls; its conjugate base is delocalized onto both oxygens","All six methyl protons are equally most acidic by symmetry"],"correct":3,"why":"The most acidic protons are on the central methylene flanked by two carbonyls. Removing one gives an enolate whose negative charge is delocalized by resonance onto BOTH carbonyl oxygens, which is far more stabilizing than the single-oxygen delocalization in a mono-ketone enolate. This double resonance stabilization (not mere induction) drops the pKa to about 9. Terminal CH3 protons are alpha to only one C=O and far less acidic.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Central CH2; doubly-delocalized enolate (index 0). pKa cited as a recall benchmark, not a calculation, so in scope. Valid SMILES, single defensible answer."},{"q":"The reaction below is run on 3-pentanone (CCC(=O)CC): (1) LDA, -78 C; (2) CH3I. Which structure is the major product of this enolate alpha-alkylation?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCC","CC=C(CC)OC","CCC(=O)C(C)(C)C","CCC(=O)CCC","CCC(=O)C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"LDA is a strong, bulky, non-nucleophilic base that fully deprotonates an alpha-carbon to give the enolate. The carbon nucleophile then does SN2 on CH3I, adding a methyl to the alpha-carbon: 3-pentanone becomes 2-methyl-3-pentanone (CCC(=O)C(C)C). Adding an ethyl (3-hexanone) or two methyls (over-alkylation) is wrong, and the O-methyl enol ether (C-alkylation vs O-alkylation) is not the major product with an alkyl iodide.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Alpha-methylation gives 2-methyl-3-pentanone (index 0). All 5 option SMILES valid; option D is a chemically sound O-alkylation enol-ether distractor. Visual, in scope."},{"q":"You need to selectively make ONE alpha-bromo ketone from propiophenone (CCC(=O)c1ccccc1) without over-bromination or cleaving the molecule. Which conditions are best for a controlled, single alpha-bromination at the carbon next to the carbonyl?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Excess Br2 with excess NaOH (basic conditions)","Br2 with FeBr3 to brominate the alpha-carbon","HBr gas with no catalyst","One equivalent of Br2 under acidic conditions (acid-catalyzed enolization)","NBS with a radical initiator (AIBN), heat"],"correct":3,"why":"Acid-catalyzed alpha-halogenation stops cleanly after one substitution: putting one electron-withdrawing Br on the alpha-carbon makes the second enolization slower, so a single equivalent of Br2 under acid gives the mono-alpha-bromo ketone. Under BASE, the opposite is true (each added halogen makes the next H more acidic), driving poly-halogenation/haloform-type cleavage. Br2/FeBr3 is electrophilic aromatic substitution and NBS/AIBN is radical (benzylic), neither giving clean alpha-bromination here.","difficulty":2,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acid-catalyzed gives clean mono-alpha-bromination; base drives poly/haloform (index 0). Two-concept (regiocontrol + acid vs base selectivity), valid SMILES, single answer."},{"q":"Four compounds are candidates for the most acidic alpha C-H. The structure drawn is ethyl acetoacetate (a beta-ketoester, CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC). Rank the acidity of the most acidic C-H in these four: ethyl acetoacetate (pictured), acetone (CC(=O)C), diethyl malonate (CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC), and ethyl propanoate (CCC(=O)OCC). Which ordering (most acidic first) is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["ethyl propanoate > acetone > diethyl malonate > ethyl acetoacetate","acetone > ethyl propanoate > ethyl acetoacetate > diethyl malonate","ethyl acetoacetate > diethyl malonate > acetone > ethyl propanoate","acetone > diethyl malonate > ethyl acetoacetate > ethyl propanoate","diethyl malonate > ethyl acetoacetate > ethyl propanoate > acetone"],"correct":2,"why":"Doubly activated C-H's (between two carbonyls) are most acidic. A ketone stabilizes an enolate better than an ester (ester O lone pair donates into its own C=O, weakening that carbonyl's anion stabilization), so ketone+ester (acetoacetate, pKa ~11) beats ester+ester (malonate, pKa ~13). Both beat singly activated substrates: acetone (one ketone, pKa ~20) is more acidic than ethyl propanoate (one ester, pKa ~25). Order: acetoacetate > malonate > acetone > propanoate.","difficulty":2,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified order acetoacetate(~11) > malonate(~13) > acetone(~20) > propanoate(~25) = index 0. All four SMILES valid (pictured one drawn). pKa values are recall benchmarks, not math. Two-concept ranking, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone (O=C1CCCCC1C) has two different alpha-carbons: a more-substituted one (bearing the methyl) and a less-substituted one. A chemist treats it with LDA at -78 C, then adds CH3I. Which alpha-carbon is alkylated, and what is the governing principle?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The less-substituted alpha-carbon, because LDA gives the kinetic enolate (fast removal of the less hindered proton)","The less-substituted alpha-carbon, because it forms the more stable, more-substituted enolate","The more-substituted alpha-carbon, because LDA gives the thermodynamic enolate","Neither; LDA is too weak a base to deprotonate a ketone alpha-carbon","Both alpha-carbons equally, because LDA shows no selectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Bulky, strong LDA at low temperature under irreversible (kinetic) conditions removes the more accessible, less hindered alpha-proton, giving the KINETIC enolate at the less-substituted carbon. Alkylation with CH3I therefore occurs at the less-substituted alpha-carbon. (Thermodynamic control, e.g. a weaker base, warmer, reversible, would favor the more-substituted, more stable enolate.) Note the principle naming the kinetic enolate is what makes option C's reasoning wrong even though it names the same carbon.","difficulty":3,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. LDA/-78 = kinetic enolate at less-substituted alpha-C (index 0). Option C names the same carbon but with self-contradictory reasoning (less-substituted carbon yet more-substituted enolate), so only A is fully correct. Standard sophomore kinetic-vs-thermo enolate concept, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with excess Br2 in aqueous NaOH, then acidified on workup. Which is the organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["Brc1ccccc1","O=C(CBr)c1ccccc1","O=C(C(Br)(Br)Br)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetophenone is a methyl ketone, so excess halogen + base triggers the haloform (bromoform) reaction: all three alpha H's are replaced to give Ar-CO-CBr3, then hydroxide cleaves the C-C bond expelling CBr3- (becomes CHBr3) to give benzoate, which acidifies to benzoic acid. opt0 (PhCOOH) is correct. The tribromomethyl ketone (opt1) is the pre-cleavage intermediate; the monobromo ketone (opt2) needs controlled/acidic conditions. RDKit confirms all five SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct haloform product, one defensible answer, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Propanoic acid is treated with Br2 and a catalytic amount of PBr3 (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky conditions), then water is added. Which is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C(O)CC","opts":["O=C(O)C(Br)C","O=C(Br)CC","O=C(Br)C(Br)C","O=C(O)C(Br)(Br)C","O=C(O)CCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"HVZ brominates a carboxylic acid specifically at the ALPHA carbon via the enolizable acyl bromide; aqueous workup regenerates the acid, giving 2-bromopropanoic acid (CC(Br)C(=O)O), which matches opt0. Beta-bromination (opt2) does not occur, the acyl bromides (opt3/opt5) hydrolyze back to acids, and di-bromination needs excess reagent. RDKit confirms opt0 = 2-bromopropanoic acid and all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct alpha-bromo acid, single answer, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Consider ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate), drawn here. Which hydrogen is the MOST acidic, and what is the approximate reason?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["There are no acidic C-H bonds; only the ester O could lose a proton","The terminal CH3 next to the ketone (pKa ~20), because it is alpha to a carbonyl","The CH3 of the ethyl group, because it is the farthest from electron-withdrawing groups","The CH2 between the ketone and ester carbonyls (pKa ~11), because its conjugate base is delocalized onto two carbonyl oxygens","The OCH2 of the ethyl ester group, because it is attached to oxygen"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The central methylene sits between a ketone and an ester carbonyl; its conjugate base is doubly resonance-stabilized (delocalized onto two carbonyl oxygens), giving pKa ~11, far more acidic than an ordinary alpha proton (~20). The ester OCH2 and the ethyl CH3 are not alpha to a carbonyl. opt0 correct. The single qualitative-acidity comparison is within DAT scope (no pKa math required). q_smiles valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct most-acidic-H identification, recognition-level, single answer, valid q_smiles."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with LDA, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br) is added. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["O=C1CCCC(Cc2ccccc2)C1","C(c1ccccc1)OC1=CCCCC1","OC1(Cc2ccccc2)CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C1CCCCC1Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LDA forms the cyclohexanone enolate; its nucleophilic alpha carbon does SN2 C-alkylation on benzyl bromide to give 2-benzylcyclohexanone (opt0, RDKit O=C1CCCCC1Cc1ccccc1). C-alkylation dominates over O-alkylation (opt1 enol ether), the group goes on the alpha not beta carbon (opt3), controlled stoichiometry gives mono not the 2,2-dibenzyl product (opt4), and no carbonyl addition (opt5 alcohol). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct C-alkylation product, single answer, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"3-Methylbutan-2-one is treated with one equivalent of Br2 in acetic acid (acid-catalyzed, monobromination). Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)C(C)C","opts":["O=C(CBr)C(C)(C)Br","O=C(C(Br)Br)C(C)C","O=C(C)C(C)(C)Br","O=C(C)C(C)CBr","O=C(CBr)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Under ACID catalysis, halogenation goes through the more-substituted (more stable) enol. For CH3-CO-CH(CH3)2 the more-substituted enol points toward C3 (the methine bearing two methyls), so bromination occurs there to give 3-bromo-3-methylbutan-2-one (opt0, RDKit CC(=O)C(C)(C)Br). Terminal (C1) bromination dominates only under base; acid stops cleanly at monobromination since the added Br deactivates further enolization, ruling out di-bromo options. Two layered concepts (regio + mono vs di), within the allowed 2-concept cap.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct acid-catalyzed regiochemistry and monobromination, single answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Optically pure (R)-3-phenylbutan-2-one (shown) is stirred in aqueous NaOH and then re-isolated. The alpha carbon bearing the phenyl group is the stereocenter. What is the stereochemical outcome and why?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["The compound is destroyed by a haloform-type C-C cleavage at the stereocenter","A single diastereomer forms because a new stereocenter is created at the carbonyl carbon","The product is racemic (R and S in equal amounts) because base-promoted enolization at that alpha carbon makes it sp2 and planar, and reprotonation occurs equally on both faces","The (R) configuration is fully retained because enolization does not involve the stereocenter","It is converted entirely to the (S) enantiomer by a clean inversion at the alpha carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The stereocenter is the alpha carbon and bears an alpha H. Aqueous NaOH (no halogen present) enolizes it, making it sp2/planar and erasing chirality; reprotonation on either face gives a 50:50 (R)/(S) racemate. opt0 correct. Not an SN2 inversion, no new stereocenter at the carbonyl, and haloform cleavage is impossible here because there is no halogen and the stereocenter is not a methyl-ketone CH3. Single concept (enolization-driven racemization), recognition-level reasoning, valid q_smiles with defined (R) center.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct base-catalyzed racemization, single defensible answer, in scope, valid stereo-SMILES."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), then worked up with dilute acid. What is the major product of this Claisen condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)C(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethoxide removes an alpha proton from one ethyl acetate; the acetate enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ethyl acetate, expelling ethoxide. Two molecules condense (losing one EtOH) to give the beta-ketoester ethyl acetoacetate, CH3-CO-CH2-CO2Et. RDKit confirms opt0 = ethyl acetoacetate (C6H10O3) and the 2-ester->product+EtOH mass balance. Distractors: diethyl malonate, ethyl pyruvate, acetylacetone (no ester), and a wrong-chain-length homolog.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct keyed product (ethyl acetoacetate), one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate is treated with NaOEt followed by acidic workup. Draw the major beta-ketoester product of the self-Claisen condensation.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)CC","CCCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)C","CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The alpha carbon (bearing a methyl) is deprotonated; the enolate attacks a second propanoate carbonyl with loss of ethoxide, giving ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate (alpha-methyl beta-ketoester). RDKit confirms opt0 (C8H14O3) and 2-ester->product+EtOH balance. Distractors lose the alpha-methyl, give a methyl ketone, or attach the wrong acyl fragment.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct product retains the alpha-methyl; good regiochemistry distractors."},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (diethyl hexanedioate) is treated with NaOEt, then mild acid. What is the major product of this intramolecular (Dieckmann) condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","O=C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Dieckmann (intramolecular Claisen) of the 1,6-diester closes to the favored 5-membered ring, giving ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate (cyclic beta-ketoester). RDKit confirms opt0 (C8H12O3) and that it equals the diester minus one EtOH. The 6- and 4-membered rings are wrong ring sizes; cyclopentanone and the ring ester lack the ketone or ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct 5-membered ring product; ring-size distractors valid."},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate) is subjected to Dieckmann conditions: NaOEt, then acidic workup. Identify the major cyclic product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","O=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1=CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The 1,7-diester closes to a 6-membered ring, giving ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate. RDKit confirms opt0 (C9H14O3) and the diester-minus-EtOH balance. The 5- and 7-membered rings are wrong ring sizes, cyclohexanone lacks the carbethoxy group, and the enol/hydroxy structure is not the isolated keto tautomer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct 6-membered ring product; opt4 enol form is a fair distractor."},{"q":"A crossed (mixed) Claisen condensation is run between ethyl benzoate (no alpha hydrogens) and ethyl acetate using NaOEt, followed by acidic workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1.CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(CC(=O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Crossed Claisen is clean when one partner lacks alpha hydrogens. Ethyl benzoate cannot enolize, so the acetate enolate attacks the benzoate carbonyl (loss of ethoxide), giving ethyl benzoylacetate, Ph-CO-CH2-CO2Et. RDKit confirms opt0 (C11H12O3) and mass balance. Distractors: malonate, dibenzoylmethane (two phenyls), ethyl phenylacetate (no condensation), and an extra-CH2 homolog.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct directionality (acetate is nucleophile, benzoate electrophile); product valid."},{"q":"Diethyl oxalate (no alpha hydrogens) is condensed with ethyl acetate under NaOEt, then acidified. Draw the major crossed-Claisen product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC.CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCC(=O)C(=O)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha hydrogens and acts as the electrophile. The ethyl acetate enolate attacks one oxalate carbonyl (displacing ethoxide), giving diethyl 2-oxosuccinate (diethyl oxaloacetate), EtO2C-CO-CH2-CO2Et. RDKit confirms the keyed SMILES canonicalizes to this oxaloacetate skeleton (C8H12O5) and is mass-balanced. Distractors: malonate (no extra carbonyl), a missing CH2, and methyl-ketone/wrong-position variants.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Keyed SMILES verified = diethyl oxaloacetate; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Ethyl formate (HCO2Et, no alpha hydrogens) reacts with ethyl acetate under NaOEt, then acidic workup. What is the major formylation/Claisen product?","q_smiles":"CCOC=O.CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(C)=O","O=CCC=O","CCOC(=O)CC=O","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethyl formate has no alpha hydrogens and acts as the electrophilic formylating agent. The ethyl acetate enolate attacks its carbonyl, expelling ethoxide and installing a -CHO, giving ethyl 3-oxopropanoate (ethyl formylacetate), OHC-CH2-CO2Et. RDKit confirms opt0 (C5H8O3) and mass balance. Distractors: ethyl glyoxylate, malonate, malondialdehyde (no ester), ethyl pyruvate (wrong carbonyl position).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct formylacetate product; valid SMILES; one answer."},{"q":"Ethyl phenylacetate undergoes a self-Claisen condensation with NaOEt, then acidic workup. Identify the major beta-ketoester product, accounting for which carbon is deprotonated.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)C(c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)C(c1ccccc1)C(=O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)C(c1ccccc1)C(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The acidic alpha carbon bears the phenyl, so deprotonation gives a benzylic, ester-stabilized enolate that attacks a second ethyl phenylacetate carbonyl (loss of ethoxide), giving ethyl 2,4-diphenyl-3-oxobutanoate, PhCH2-CO-CH(Ph)-CO2Et. RDKit confirms opt0 (C18H18O3) and mass balance. Distractors attach an aryl ketone directly, omit a phenyl, lack the ketone, or drop the ester.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Two-concept (regiochemistry of acidic carbon + condensation) but single reactive center; correct product keyed."},{"q":"Two molecules of ethyl acetate (shown) are heated and then worked up with dilute acid to give ethyl acetoacetate (a beta-ketoester) in a Claisen condensation. Which reagent must be used to drive this reaction to completion?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["Dilute sulfuric acid (H2SO4)","Lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4)","Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt)","Pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC)","Sodium borohydride (NaBH4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The Claisen requires a strong alkoxide (matching ethoxide avoids transesterification) to form the ester enolate. The reaction is driven forward by deprotonation of the acidic beta-ketoester product. Hydrides reduce, PCC oxidizes, dilute acid cannot make an enolate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: ethyl acetate SMILES valid; NaOEt (idx0) is the correct, sole defensible answer; reagent-selection text item, molecule drawn. No change."},{"q":"The transformation shown converts an ester into a beta-ketoester by forming a new C-C bond between the alpha carbon of one ester molecule and the carbonyl carbon of a second identical ester molecule, ejecting an alkoxide leaving group. What is the name of this reaction?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["Wittig reaction","Aldol condensation","Friedel-Crafts acylation","Cannizzaro reaction","Claisen condensation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Ethyl acetoacetate is drawn, the classic Claisen product from two ethyl acetate units: an ester enolate attacks an ester carbonyl and the tetrahedral intermediate collapses by expelling alkoxide. Aldol gives beta-hydroxy carbonyls, Cannizzaro needs no alpha-H, Wittig uses ylides, Friedel-Crafts acylates arenes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: ethyl acetoacetate SMILES valid; Claisen (idx0) correct; one defensible answer. No change."},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (the C6 diester shown) is treated with NaOEt and then dilute acid in an intramolecular Claisen (Dieckmann) condensation. Which cyclic beta-ketoester is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","O=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Adipate is a 1,6-diester; its alpha-enolate attacks the distal carbonyl to close a five-membered ring, giving 2-carbethoxycyclopentanone (opt0, ring of 5 atoms). The 6-ring ester comes from pimelate, the 4-ring is too strained, the 7-ring is wrong, and the last is plain cyclohexanone with no ester.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: all 5 option SMILES parse; opt0 ring=cyclopentanone is the correct Dieckmann product of adipate; keyed correct=0 matches. No change."},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (the C7 diester shown) undergoes a Dieckmann condensation with NaOEt followed by acidic workup. Which product forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","O=C1CCCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Pimelate is a 1,7-diester and closes to the favorable six-membered ring, 2-carbethoxycyclohexanone (opt0, ring of 6). The cyclopentanone ester is the adipate product, the 7- and 4-membered esters are disfavored, and cycloheptanone lacks the ester group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: all option SMILES valid; opt0 ring=cyclohexanone is correct for pimelate; correct=0 matches. No change."},{"q":"Methyl propanoate (shown) is treated with sodium methoxide, then dilute acid, in a Claisen condensation. Which beta-ketoester is the product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)OC","opts":["CCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OC","CCC(=O)CC(=O)OC","CCC(O)C(C)C(=O)OC","CCC(=O)CCC(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The enolate of methyl propanoate (alpha carbon bearing a methyl) attacks a second molecule and methoxide leaves, giving methyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate (opt0) with the alpha-methyl branch retained. Opt1 is unbranched, opt2 is a beta-hydroxy aldol product, opt3 has the wrong chain, opt4 is a malonate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: methyl propanoate self-Claisen gives the branched beta-ketoester (opt0); all SMILES parse; distractors plausibly wrong; correct=0 matches. No change."},{"q":"Which of the following esters CANNOT undergo a normal Claisen (self-)condensation when treated with a strong base?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C(=O)OCC","opts":["Ethyl butanoate","Ethyl phenylacetate","Ethyl acetate","Ethyl propanoate","Ethyl 2,2-dimethylpropanoate (ethyl pivalate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A self-Claisen needs at least two alpha hydrogens so the beta-ketoester product still has an acidic alpha proton to drive the equilibrium. Ethyl pivalate (drawn) has a fully substituted alpha carbon with no alpha-H, so it cannot form an enolate. The other four esters all have alpha-H and react normally.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: pivalate SMILES valid, has no alpha-H; idx0 is the unique correct answer; classification text item with molecule drawn. No change."},{"q":"Ethyl phenylacetate (shown) undergoes a base-promoted Claisen self-condensation. After NaOEt and acidic workup, the product is a beta-ketoester. Which class of product is formed, given that the alpha carbon already bears a phenyl group?","q_smiles":"O=C(OCC)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["A symmetrical anhydride","A beta-ketoester (1,3-dicarbonyl)","An alpha,beta-unsaturated ester","A 1,2-diketone","A beta-hydroxy ester"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"As in every Claisen, an ester enolate attacks a second ester carbonyl and alkoxide is expelled, giving a 1,3-dicarbonyl beta-ketoester. A beta-hydroxy ester would be an aldol product, the relationship is 1,3 not 1,2, and no anhydride or alkene forms.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: ethyl phenylacetate SMILES valid; product is a beta-ketoester (idx0); one defensible answer; classification text item with molecule drawn. No change."},{"q":"A student wants to make a six-membered cyclic beta-ketoester by a Dieckmann condensation. Of the diesters below, which gives a six-membered ring (cyclohexanone) product, while diethyl succinate (shown) fails to give a useful Dieckmann product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","opts":["Diethyl succinate (1,4-diester)","Diethyl malonate (1,3-diester)","Diethyl pimelate (1,7-diester)","Diethyl oxalate (1,2-diester)","Diethyl adipate (1,6-diester)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Ring size tracks chain length: a 1,7-diester (pimelate) closes to a favorable six-membered ring. Succinate (drawn, 1,4) would form a strained three-membered ring and fails, adipate (1,6) gives a five-membered ring, and malonate/oxalate are too short to cyclize. Only pimelate gives the six-membered cyclohexanone beta-ketoester.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: succinate SMILES valid; pimelate (idx0) is the unique diester giving a 6-ring; single concept (ring-size logic), in scope despite difficulty 3. No change."},{"q":"Methyl vinyl ketone (drawn) is treated with the sodium salt of diethyl malonate (NaCH(CO2Et)2). What product is formed, and by what process?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)C","opts":["No reaction; malonate is not nucleophilic enough","1,2-addition: the malonate carbon adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving a tertiary alcohol","Nucleophilic acyl substitution to displace the methyl group","1,4-conjugate (Michael) addition: the malonate carbon adds to the beta-carbon, giving CH3C(=O)CH2CH2CH(CO2Et)2","Aldol addition at the alpha-carbon of the ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A stabilized enolate (soft nucleophile) such as malonate undergoes 1,4-conjugate (Michael) addition to an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone, bonding to the beta-carbon. The 1,2 distractor is the kinetic mode favored by hard organometallics, not soft enolates.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: soft stabilized enolate gives 1,4-Michael addition; product valid; standard sophomore item."},{"q":"2-Cyclohexenone (drawn) reacts with lithium dimethylcuprate, (CH3)2CuLi, followed by aqueous workup. Which statement correctly describes the outcome?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["A methyl group adds to the beta-carbon (1,4-addition) to give 3-methylcyclohexanone","The cuprate deprotonates the alpha-carbon and no addition occurs","The enone is reduced to cyclohexanone with no methyl incorporation","A methyl group adds to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition) to give 1-methyl-2-cyclohexenol","Two methyl groups add, one at the carbonyl and one at the beta-carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Gilman reagents (lithium dialkylcuprates) are soft and selectively do 1,4-conjugate addition, delivering the alkyl group to the beta-carbon. After tautomerization of the enol, the product is 3-methylcyclohexanone. Organolithiums/Grignards favor 1,2-addition instead.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: Gilman cuprate gives 1,4 to beta-carbon; 3-methylcyclohexanone product valid."},{"q":"You want to add a methyl group to the carbonyl carbon of 2-cyclohexenone (drawn) to make 1-methyl-2-cyclohexen-1-ol (1,2-addition), avoiding conjugate addition. Which reagent is the best choice?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["CH3MgBr with catalytic CuI","(CH3)2CuLi (lithium dimethylcuprate)","CH3Li (methyllithium)","Sodium dimethyl malonate","CH3NH2 (methylamine)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Hard, highly reactive organometallics such as organolithiums add fast and irreversibly to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition). Cuprates and Cu-catalyzed Grignards favor 1,4. Stabilized enolates and amines are soft nucleophiles that give conjugate addition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: organolithium (hard) gives 1,2; only one reagent does so; product valid."},{"q":"Acrylonitrile (drawn) is combined with the enolate of diethyl malonate. Classify this reaction and identify where the new carbon-carbon bond forms.","q_smiles":"C=CC#N","opts":["Diels-Alder cycloaddition forming a six-membered ring","Michael addition; the malonate carbon bonds to the terminal CH2 (beta to the nitrile)","Aldol addition; the bond forms at the nitrile carbon","Claisen condensation; an ester is displaced","1,2-addition; the malonate adds across the C-N triple bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Acrylonitrile is a classic Michael acceptor; the nitrile is electron-withdrawing and conjugated to the alkene, so the soft malonate nucleophile adds to the beta-carbon (terminal CH2). This conjugate addition of a stabilized enolate is the Michael reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: acrylonitrile is Michael acceptor; malonate adds to terminal beta-CH2; product valid."},{"q":"In a Michael reaction, the alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl is the 'Michael acceptor' and the stabilized carbanion is the 'Michael donor.' Which of the following is the best Michael DONOR?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["Diethyl ether","tert-Butyllithium","Diethyl malonate (the drawn compound), deprotonated to its enolate","Cyclohexene","Methyl vinyl ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A good Michael donor is a carbon nucleophile whose negative charge is resonance-stabilized by adjacent electron-withdrawing groups (here two esters flanking the CH2). Methyl vinyl ketone is an acceptor; ether and cyclohexene are not nucleophilic; t-BuLi is a hard base/nucleophile, not a stabilized donor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: deprotonated malonate is the canonical stabilized Michael donor; distractors clearly wrong."},{"q":"trans-Cinnamaldehyde (drawn) is treated with one equivalent of sodium cyanide in protic solvent. The product is the 1,4 (conjugate) addition adduct. Where does cyanide add, and why is 1,4 observed here?","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["No addition occurs because the aldehyde is conjugated to a benzene ring","Cyanide adds to an aromatic ring carbon via electrophilic substitution","Cyanide adds twice, once 1,2 and once 1,4","Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon; cyanide always gives 1,2-addition to aldehydes","Cyanide adds to the beta-carbon (bearing the phenyl); cyanide is a soft, reversible nucleophile that favors the thermodynamic 1,4 product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide is a soft, polarizable nucleophile and its addition is reversible, so on a conjugated enal the thermodynamically favored 1,4-adduct predominates: CN bonds to the beta (benzylic) carbon. This contrasts with the irreversible 1,2-cyanohydrin formed from simple aldehydes.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Stem pre-specifies the 1,4 product, removing ambiguity; soft/reversible-nucleophile reasoning is textbook-correct and beta is the benzylic carbon. In scope."},{"q":"2-Cyclohexenone (drawn) is treated with two different reagents. Reagent A is CH3MgBr (no copper); Reagent B is CH3MgBr with catalytic CuBr. Which row correctly pairs each reagent with its major regiochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["A gives mainly 1,4-addition; B gives mainly 1,2-addition","Both A and B give exclusively 1,4-addition","Both A and B give exclusively 1,2-addition","A gives reduction to cyclohexanone; B gives an epoxide","A gives mainly 1,2-addition (allylic tertiary alcohol); B gives mainly 1,4-addition (3-methylcyclohexanone)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A bare Grignard is hard and adds 1,2 to the carbonyl, giving the tertiary allylic alcohol. Adding catalytic Cu(I) generates a cuprate-type species in situ that switches selectivity to 1,4-conjugate addition, placing the methyl at the beta-carbon to give 3-methylcyclohexanone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: bare Grignard 1,2 vs Cu-catalyzed 1,4; standard two-concept selectivity item; products valid."},{"q":"Two electrophiles are compared as Michael acceptors toward the same stabilized enolate: chalcone (drawn, PhCOCH=CHPh) and an ordinary non-conjugated ketone such as cyclohexanone. Which statement is correct about their relative reactivity in conjugate addition?","q_smiles":"O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","opts":["Chalcone readily undergoes 1,4-conjugate (Michael) addition because its C=C is conjugated to the carbonyl, making the beta-carbon electrophilic; cyclohexanone has no such conjugated beta-carbon and cannot act as a Michael acceptor","Both react identically since both contain a C=O group","Neither can undergo conjugate addition because aryl groups block the beta-carbon","Chalcone only undergoes 1,2-addition because both faces are aryl-substituted","Cyclohexanone is the better Michael acceptor because it lacks aromatic rings"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Conjugate (Michael) addition requires an alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl: the C=C conjugated to C=O renders the beta-carbon electrophilic via the extended resonance. Chalcone has this; a saturated ketone like cyclohexanone has no conjugated beta-carbon and therefore cannot serve as a Michael acceptor. The phenyl rings do not block the conjugate-addition pathway.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: chalcone is a conjugated enone (Michael acceptor); saturated cyclohexanone is not; chalcone SMILES valid."},{"group":"michael","q":"Methyl vinyl ketone is treated with the sodium enolate of dimethyl malonate (NaOMe, then workup). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)C(CCC(C)=O)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C(O)(C)C=C)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C(=C)C(C)=O)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(CCC(C)(O)O)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C(=O)OC)C(C)CC=O"],"correct":0,"why":"Soft malonate enolate adds 1,4 to the beta-carbon (terminal CH2) of MVK; after tautomerization the methyl ketone is restored, giving the 1,5-dicarbonyl (opt0, C9H14O5 = malonate + MVK). Verified canonical. Distractors are the 1,2 trap (opt1 allylic tertiary alcohol), wrong regiochem (opt2), an isomeric aldehyde (opt3), and a non-product gem-diol (opt4).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct and unambiguous; all 5 SMILES valid; in scope. Minor: why-text 'option 4 is a non-product diol' uses loose 1-indexing but does not affect the keyed index."},{"group":"michael","q":"2-Cyclohexen-1-one is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous acid. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(C)CCCC=C1","C=C1CCCCC1=O","O=C1CCCCC1C","O=C1CCCC(C)C1","O=C1CCC(C)CC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Gilman reagents (R2CuLi) are soft and deliver the alkyl group 1,4 to C3, giving 3-methylcyclohexanone (opt0, canonical match to CC1CCCC(=O)C1 confirmed). Distractors: 1,2 tertiary allylic alcohol (opt1), 2-methyl alpha product (opt2), 4-methyl (opt3), exocyclic alkene (opt4).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (opt0 = 3-methylcyclohexanone, verified); 5 valid options; in scope. Minor: why-text uses 'Option 1' loosely for the alcohol but keyed correct=0 is right."},{"group":"michael","q":"Methyl vinyl ketone reacts with thiophenol (PhSH) under mild base. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCS(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCSc1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Sc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CSCc1ccccc1","C=CC(C)(O)Sc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"A thiol undergoes thia-Michael (1,4) addition, bonding sulfur to the beta-carbon (terminal CH2) with the methyl ketone retained (opt0). Distractors: alpha-S (opt1), 1,2 addition (opt2), benzylic-S skeleton (opt3), sulfoxide oxidation state (opt4).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (opt0 = beta-thioether); 5 valid SMILES; single concept, in scope. No issues."},{"group":"michael","q":"2-Cyclohexen-1-one is treated with methyllithium (CH3Li) at low temperature, then aqueous workup. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCC(C)C1","OC1(C)CCCC=C1","OC1(C)CCCCC1","OC1C(C)CCC=C1","O=C1CCCCC1C"],"correct":1,"why":"Organolithiums are hard and add 1,2 to the carbonyl carbon, giving the tertiary allylic alcohol with the ring C=C intact (opt0). Good complement to the cuprate (1,4) item. Distractors: 1,4 product (opt1), saturated alcohol (opt2), wrong-carbon secondary alcohol (opt3), 1,4 alpha isomer (opt4).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (opt0 = 1,2 tertiary allylic alcohol); 5 valid SMILES; in scope. No issues."},{"group":"michael","q":"For (E)-pent-3-en-2-one (drawn), which single reagent most reliably gives clean 1,4 (conjugate) addition rather than 1,2 addition to the carbonyl?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Lithium aluminum hydride, LiAlH4","Lithium diphenylcuprate, Ph2CuLi","Phenylmagnesium bromide, PhMgBr","Sodium borohydride then strong acid","Phenyllithium, PhLi"],"correct":1,"why":"Soft organocuprates (Gilman, R2CuLi) favor 1,4 conjugate addition; hard organolithiums and Grignards favor 1,2; LiAlH4 is a hydride reducer, not a carbon nucleophile. Ph2CuLi is the reagent of choice.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Reagent-selection item, opts_are_structures=false (text) with molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid). Exactly one defensible answer (opt0). No issues."},{"group":"michael","q":"5-Methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product, accounting for stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"O=C1CC(C)CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(C)CC(C)CC=C1","O=C1CCC(C)CC1C","O=C1C[C@@H](C)C[C@H](C)C1","O=C1CC(C)CCC1C","O=C1C[C@H](C)C[C@H](C)C1"],"correct":4,"why":"Cuprate delivers methyl 1,4 to C3, giving 3,5-dimethylcyclohexanone. The cis-3,5 (diequatorial) diastereomer is favored; opt0 is the meso (achiral, internal mirror plane) cis isomer, verified by RDKit (canonical = its own mirror). opt1 is the chiral trans pair (one axial). Distractors also include a 2,5 regio isomer (opt2), the 1,2 alcohol with alkene intact (opt3), and a 2-methyl isomer (opt4).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct: opt0 verified as meso cis-3,5-dimethylcyclohexanone (diequatorial, favored); opt1 verified as the trans diastereomer. All SMILES valid. Two layered concepts (regio + stereo) at difficulty 3 is within stated cap. No issues."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with the following sequence: (1) NaOEt, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br); (2) NaOH, H2O, heat; (3) H3O+, heat. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["OC(=O)C(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(Cc1ccccc1)CC(=O)O","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Malonate alpha-carbon is benzylated once, saponified to the substituted malonic acid, then thermal decarboxylation of one COOH gives PhCH2CH2COOH (3-phenylpropanoic acid). opt[0] matches. Distractors: phenylacetic acid (carbon short), the non-decarboxylated diacid, the dibenzyl product, and an alcohol (wrong functional class).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; standard malonic ester synthesis, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is subjected to: (1) NaOEt, then 1-bromopropane; (2) dilute NaOH; (3) H3O+, heat. Identify the major organic product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["CCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCC=O","CCCCCC(C)=O","CCCCC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis: monoalkylation by propyl at the active-methylene carbon, then hydrolysis and decarboxylation of the beta-keto acid gives the methyl ketone CH3CO-CH2CH2CH2CH3 = 2-hexanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: carbon-short ketone, aldehyde, non-decarboxylated diketone, and 2-heptanone (extra carbon).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; canonical acetoacetic synthesis, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with one equivalent of NaOEt followed by one equivalent of methyl iodide, then NaOH/H2O with heat, then H3O+/heat. What carboxylic acid is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O","CCC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Single methylation gives CH3CH(COOH)2 after hydrolysis; decarboxylation removes one COOH leaving CH3CH2COOH (propanoic acid). opt[0] matches. Distractors: acetic acid (no alkylation), butanoic acid (over-counts a carbon), isobutyric acid (dimethylation), and a methyl ester (incomplete hydrolysis).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; standard single-alkylation malonic synthesis, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated with benzyl bromide after deprotonation by NaOEt, then hydrolyzed with aqueous acid and heated. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Benzylation at the central carbon, then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives the methyl ketone CH3CO-CH2CH2Ph = 4-phenyl-2-butanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: phenylacetone (carbon short), benzaldehyde, the non-decarboxylated diketone, and a carboxylic acid (wrong product class for an acetoacetic ester).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; clean acetoacetic synthesis, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt/CH3I, then a second round of NaOEt/CH3I, then aqueous NaOH followed by H3O+ and heat. What carboxylic acid forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(C)(C)C(=O)O","CC(C(=O)O)C(=O)O","CCC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Two sequential methylations dimethylate the malonate carbon; hydrolysis and decarboxylation give (CH3)2CH-COOH = 2-methylpropanoic (isobutyric) acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: butanoic acid (one methylation), pivalic acid (three methyls), non-decarboxylated methylmalonic diacid, and propanoic acid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; dialkylation + decarboxylation, two-concept but in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt and allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then with dilute acid and heat. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["C=CCCC(C)=O","OC(=O)CCC=C","C=CCC(C)=O","C=CCCCC(C)=O","C=CCC(=O)CC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"SN2 allylation at the active-methylene carbon (alkene untouched), then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH2CH2CH=CH2 = hex-5-en-2-one. opt[0] matches. Distractors: carbon-short enone-ketone, carbon-long ketone, non-decarboxylated diketone, and a carboxylic acid (wrong class).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; chemoselective allylation + decarboxylation, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (drawn), then NaOH/H2O, then H3O+/heat. What is the major product of this intramolecular dialkylation?","q_smiles":"BrCCCCBr","opts":["OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","OC(=O)C1CCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)C1(C(=O)O)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Both alpha-C-H positions are alkylated by the two ends of the dibromide, closing a ring; malonate carbon plus four CH2 units form a five-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: cyclobutane (miscount), cyclohexane (extra carbon), gem-diacid (no decarboxylation), open-chain adipic acid (no cyclization). q_smiles correctly depicts the dibromide the stem says is drawn.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; intramolecular dialkylation is hard but recognition-level and in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated sequentially: (1) NaOEt then CH3I; (2) NaOEt then CH3CH2I; then (3) dilute H2SO4 and heat. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["CCC(C)C(C)=O","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(C)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CCC(CC)C(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Sequential methylation then ethylation puts a methyl and an ethyl on the central carbon; hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH(CH3)-CH2CH3 = 3-methyl-2-pentanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: 2-hexanone (one alkylation), non-decarboxylated diketone, 3-ethyl-2-pentanone (wrong group, two ethyls), and 4-methyl-2-pentanone (wrong substitution position).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; dialkylation + decarboxylation, hard but in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with (1) NaOEt, then (2) one equivalent of 1-bromobutane, then (3) aqueous HCl with heat. What is the final organic product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)O","CCCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Malonic ester synthesis: deprotonate central CH2, monoalkylate with n-butyl, hydrolyze and decarboxylate to R-CH2-COOH with R=n-butyl = hexanoic acid (C6). Distractors miscount chain (B C5, C C7), branch wrongly (D), or give an aldehyde (E).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed product hexanoic acid (C6) matches n-butyl monoalkylation + decarboxylation. All SMILES valid, exactly one right answer, in scope, visual.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is subjected to (1) NaOEt, (2) one equivalent of 1-bromopropane, then (3) dilute aqueous acid with heat. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC","CC(=O)CCC","CC(=O)CCCC","CCC(=O)CCC","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives methyl ketone CH3-CO-CH2-R; R=n-propyl = 2-hexanone. B/C miscount (2-pentanone/2-heptanone), D is 3-hexanone (not a methyl ketone), E is the malonic-pathway carboxylic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed 2-hexanone matches methyl-ketone product of n-propyl alkylation. Valid SMILES, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with two equivalents of NaOEt and one equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (intramolecular dialkylation), followed by aqueous acid and heat. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC","OC(=O)C1CCCCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Dialkylation closes a ring: malonate carbon + 4 CH2 = 5-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. B/C/E are off-ring-size, D is open-chain (no closure).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Ring count = central C + 4 CH2 = cyclopentane; keyed cyclopentanecarboxylic acid right. Valid SMILES, in scope (regio/ring-size, 1 concept), visual."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br), then warm aqueous acid. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Methyl-ketone product CH3-CO-CH2-R, R=benzyl = 4-phenyl-2-butanone. B drops a CH2 (phenylacetone), C is an ethyl ketone (wrong pattern), D adds a CH2, E is a carboxylic acid (malonic confusion).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed 4-phenyl-2-butanone matches benzyl alkylation methyl-ketone product. Valid SMILES, one answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt then iodomethane, and the resulting product is again treated with NaOEt then bromoethane; finally it is hydrolyzed and heated. What carboxylic acid forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(CC)C(=O)O","CC(C)CC(=O)O","CCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Sequential methyl + ethyl dialkylation; after decarboxylation the alpha carbon bears CH3, CH2CH3, H, COOH = 2-methylbutanoic acid. B adds a carbon, C uses two ethyls, D branches wrong (3-methyl), E omits methylation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Two-step alkylation (Me + Et) gives 2-methylbutanoic acid; keyed SMILES CCC(C)C(=O)O matches. Valid SMILES, one answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with two equivalents of base and one equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (intramolecular dialkylation), then warm aqueous acid. What is the product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Alpha carbon bonds both ends of 1,3-dibromopropane: that C + 3 CH2 = 4-membered ring; acetyl group survives decarboxylation = acetylcyclobutane. B/C off-ring-size, D open-chain, E swaps methyl ketone for acid (wrong precursor).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Ring = alpha C + 3 CH2 = cyclobutane; keyed 1-cyclobutylethanone right. Valid SMILES, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt and then one equivalent of 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), followed by aqueous acid and heat. Which structure correctly shows the product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)O","CCC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)CC(=O)O","CC(C)C(C)C(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Isopropyl adds to the malonate carbon; after decarboxylation (CH3)2CH-CH2-COOH = 3-methylbutanoic acid (isovaleric). B over-methylates (2,3-dimethyl), C is 2-methylbutanoic (wrong connectivity), D too short, E straight-chain ignoring branch.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed isovaleric acid CC(C)CC(=O)O matches isopropyl monoalkylation + decarboxylation. All SMILES valid, one answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"In a malonic ester synthesis, the enolate of diethyl malonate (shown) is alkylated at its central carbon to create a new stereocenter (for example, sequential methylation and ethylation followed by decarboxylation gives 2-methylbutanoic acid). What is the stereochemical outcome at that newly formed stereocenter, and why?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Only the R enantiomer forms, because alkylation always proceeds with retention of configuration","A single meso compound forms, because the two ester groups make the molecule internally symmetric","A racemic (50:50) mixture of R and S forms, because the planar sp2 enolate is attacked by the alkyl halide from both faces with equal probability","Only the S enantiomer forms, because the alkyl halide approaches anti to the larger ester group","An optically active single enantiomer forms, because the chiral base sets the configuration"],"correct":2,"why":"The nucleophilic enolate carbon is sp2/planar, so alkyl halide attack on each face is equally likely, generating the new stereocenter as a 50:50 racemate (optically inactive). No chiral influence is present, so single-enantiomer or retention options are wrong; a meso form is impossible with one stereocenter.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Planar-enolate-to-racemate reasoning is sound and DAT-level; single concept (stereo outcome of enolate alkylation), substrate drawn. Distractors plausibly wrong, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetic acid is heated with excess ethanol in the presence of a catalytic amount of concentrated H2SO4 (Fischer esterification). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOCC","CCOC(=O)CC","CC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)O","CCOC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification couples the carboxylic acid (acetic acid) with the alcohol (ethanol), losing water to give ethyl acetate (CCOC(C)=O). The acyl group comes from the acid and the OR group comes from the alcohol. Methyl acetate (B) would need methanol; propanoic acid (C) adds an extra carbon to the acid; diethyl ether (D) is just two ethanols; ethyl propanoate (E) has the wrong acyl chain.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: substrate and all 5 option SMILES valid; ethyl acetate (index 0) is the correct Fischer product; distractors map to wrong-alcohol, wrong-acid, ether, and wrong-acyl errors. Canonical, in-scope, visual."},{"q":"Benzoic acid is refluxed with methanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4. Which structure is the major product of this Fischer esterification?","q_smiles":"O=C(O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)c1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1C(=O)O","CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1","COC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Methanol esterifies the carboxyl group to give methyl benzoate (COC(=O)c1ccccc1). The catalyst protonates the carbonyl; nucleophilic methanol attacks the carbonyl carbon, not the aromatic ring. Ethyl benzoate (B) uses the wrong alcohol; (C) puts a methoxy on the ring and keeps a free acid; anisole (D) loses the carbonyl entirely; methyl phenylacetate (E) inserts an extra CH2 into the skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid; methyl benzoate (index 0) correct. Distractors are wrong-alcohol, ring-methoxylation, anisole, and chain-extension. In-scope recognition item, visual."},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate is heated with aqueous H2SO4 (dilute acid, excess water). Which structure is the major organic product of this acid-catalyzed hydrolysis?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)OC","CCCO","CCC(=O)O","CCOC(C)=O","CCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed ester hydrolysis is the reverse of Fischer esterification: the ester is cleaved at the acyl-oxygen bond to regenerate the carboxylic acid (propanoic acid, CCC(=O)O) plus the alcohol (ethanol). The carbonyl-containing fragment from the acyl group is the keyed product; ethanol (D) is the co-product. Methyl propanoate (B) and ethyl acetate (E) are wrong skeletons; propanol (C) over-reduces.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; propanoic acid (index 0) is the acyl-derived product, correctly keyed. Minor ambiguity: hydrolysis gives two organic products (acid + ethanol, an option D), but stem asks for the carbonyl/acyl product and the carboxylic acid is the conventional 'main product'; defensible single answer. Kept."},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate is heated with aqueous NaOH (saponification), then the mixture is left basic. Which structure is the major form of the carboxyl-containing product under these conditions?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[Na+].[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","[Na+].CC[O-]","O=C(O)c1ccccc1","CCOc1ccccc1","[Na+].[O-]C(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Saponification is irreversible base-promoted hydrolysis: hydroxide attacks the carbonyl, expelling ethoxide, and the carboxylic acid is deprotonated by base to the carboxylate. In the basic medium the product is sodium benzoate (the carboxylate salt), not neutral benzoic acid (B), which only appears after acidic workup. Sodium ethoxide (C) is the leaving alcohol; (D) adds a CH2; (E) keeps an ether skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid (salts parse correctly). Two-concept (saponification + protonation state under basic conditions); the carboxylate-vs-neutral-acid distinction is the exact teaching point and gives one defensible answer (index 0). Stem explicitly specifies 'left basic', so benzoic acid is correctly the distractor. In-scope, visual."},{"q":"Methyl acetate is stirred with a large excess of ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed transesterification). Which structure is the major product favored at equilibrium?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)OC","CCOC(C)=O","CCO","CC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Transesterification swaps the alkoxy group of an ester for that of the alcohol used in excess. With excess ethanol, the methoxy of methyl acetate is replaced by an ethoxy, giving ethyl acetate (CCOC(C)=O) plus methanol. The acetyl group is unchanged. (B) is starting material; ethanol (C) is a reactant; acetic acid (D) would require hydrolysis, not alcohol exchange; methyl propanoate (E) is a wrong acyl skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; ethyl acetate (index 0) is the Le-Chatelier-favored transesterification product, correctly keyed. Distractors are starting material, reactant, hydrolysis product, and wrong-acyl. In-scope, visual."},{"q":"Succinic acid (butanedioic acid) is refluxed with a large excess of ethanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)CCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)O","O=C1CCC(=O)O1","CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","COC(=O)CCC(=O)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"With excess alcohol both carboxyl groups undergo Fischer esterification, giving the diester diethyl succinate (CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC). The mono-ester (B) is only a partial product, disfavored when ethanol is in large excess. Dimethyl succinate (C) uses the wrong alcohol; diethyl malonate (D) has one fewer CH2 (wrong skeleton); succinic anhydride (E) results from dehydration without alcohol, not Fischer esterification.","difficulty":2,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; chain lengths verified (succinate = 2 CH2 between carbonyls; malonate distractor D = 1 CH2). Diethyl diester (index 0) is the excess-driven product, correctly keyed. Two-concept (diacid + complete esterification). In-scope, visual."},{"q":"A single enantiomer of 2-phenylpropanoic acid (stereocenter shown) is heated with methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product, accounting for the stereochemistry at the alpha carbon?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification cleaves the acyl carbon-oxygen bond of the acid; the bond to the alpha stereocenter is never broken, so its spatial configuration is fully retained. RDKit confirms the (S) substrate gives the (S) methyl ester (index 0) because the spatial arrangement is unchanged and CIP priorities (ester C > phenyl) are unchanged. The inversion structure (B) is wrong because no bond to the stereocenter is broken; (C) uses ethanol; (D) loses the stereochemistry; (E) is a chain-extended skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified with RDKit: substrate = (S); keyed option A = (S) retention; option B = (R) inversion. Descriptor stays S because no bond to stereocenter breaks and CIP order is preserved. Stereochemistry/retention is recognition-level (not full mechanism), so in scope. Correct, one defensible answer, visual."},{"q":"Gamma-butyrolactone (a cyclic ester) is treated with aqueous NaOH and the mixture is kept strongly basic. Which structure best represents the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCO1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[Na+].[O-]CCCC(=O)O","[Na+].[O-]C(=O)CCCO","O=C1CCCO1","OC(=O)CCCO","[Na+].[O-]C(=O)CCO"],"correct":1,"why":"Saponification of a lactone opens the ring: hydroxide attacks the carbonyl and cleaves the acyl-oxygen bond, giving an open-chain hydroxy carboxylate. In strong base the acid is deprotonated, so the product is sodium 4-hydroxybutanoate (carboxylate plus a free terminal OH, index 0). Neutral acid (B) needs acidic workup; (C) is unreacted lactone; (D) shortens the chain by a CH2; (E) wrongly deprotonates the alcohol while leaving a neutral acid.","difficulty":2,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; ring-opened 4-hydroxybutanoate (index 0) verified as correct (GBL is the 5-membered gamma-lactone; opening gives 4-carbon hydroxy acid). Distractor D correctly shortens chain, E mis-deprotonates the alcohol, C is starting material. Two-concept (lactone hydrolysis + salt form under base). In-scope, visual."},{"q":"The ester drawn below (methyl acetate) is the product of a Fischer esterification. Which combination of starting materials and conditions produces it?","q_smiles":"COC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Methanol + ethanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Acetic acid + methoxide ion (CH3O-), room temperature","Acetic acid + methanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Acetyl chloride + methanol, pyridine","Acetic acid + methanol, catalytic NaOH, heat"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification couples a carboxylic acid with an alcohol under acid catalysis and heat. Methyl acetate (CH3CO-OCH3) = acetic acid (acyl) + methanol (OCH3). Acyl chloride route makes the same ester but is not Fischer; base options are wrong (base saponifies esters).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (methyl acetate). Keyed answer 0 is the only Fischer route; distractors plausible-but-wrong. Keep."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate (drawn below) is heated with a large excess of ethanol and a trace of H2SO4, converting it to ethyl benzoate. What name best describes this reaction?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Fischer esterification","Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis","Saponification","Claisen condensation","Transesterification"],"correct":4,"why":"Converting one ester into another by swapping the alkoxy group (-OCH3 to -OCH2CH3) under acid catalysis with excess alcohol is transesterification. Fischer starts from an acid; saponification needs hydroxide; hydrolysis gives the acid; Claisen forms a beta-keto ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (methyl benzoate). Reaction-type item, single defensible answer (transesterification). Keep."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate (drawn below) is refluxed with aqueous NaOH. What is the correct name for this reaction, and what is the organic carboxyl-containing product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Saponification; product is acetic acid (neutral)","Transesterification; product is ethyl acetate","Acid hydrolysis; product is acetic acid","Saponification; product is sodium acetate (acetate ion)","Fischer esterification; product is acetic acid"],"correct":3,"why":"Base-promoted ester hydrolysis is saponification, made essentially irreversible because hydroxide deprotonates the acid to the carboxylate. Product is acetate ion (sodium acetate), not neutral acetic acid (which only appears after acidic workup).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethyl acetate). Salt-vs-neutral-acid distinction is sophomore-appropriate; only option 0 correct. Keep."},{"q":"You want to make ethyl benzoate (PhCO-OCH2CH3) from the carboxylic acid drawn below by Fischer esterification. Which alcohol and catalyst should you use?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Ethanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","1-Propanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Phenol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Methanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Ethanol, catalytic NaOH, heat"],"correct":0,"why":"The alcohol supplies the -OR group. Ethyl benzoate needs an ethoxy group, so use ethanol with acid catalysis and heat. Methanol/propanol/phenol give different esters; NaOH is a base and does not catalyze Fischer esterification.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (benzoic acid). Reagent-selection item, exactly one correct (ethanol/H2SO4). Keep."},{"q":"Fischer esterification of the carboxylic acid below with ethanol is a reversible, equilibrium reaction. Which manipulation will NOT help drive the equilibrium toward the ester product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Adding a drying agent / molecular sieves to absorb water","Using a large excess of ethanol","Adding aqueous NaOH to the reaction mixture","Using the alcohol as the solvent","Removing water as it forms (e.g., Dean-Stark trap)"],"correct":2,"why":"Excess alcohol and water removal are valid Le Chatelier tactics. Adding NaOH does the opposite: it neutralizes the acid catalyst and deprotonates the acid to an unreactive carboxylate (and promotes saponification of any ester). So NaOH does not drive ester formation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (propanoic acid). NEGATIVE-stem (NOT) item; only NaOH fails to help, others all valid. Single defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Consider tert-butyl acetate (drawn below) alongside methyl acetate and isopropyl acetate. Rank these three acetate esters from FASTEST to SLOWEST rate of saponification (NaOH-promoted hydrolysis).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["isopropyl acetate > methyl acetate > tert-butyl acetate","methyl acetate > isopropyl acetate > tert-butyl acetate","tert-butyl acetate > isopropyl acetate > methyl acetate","tert-butyl acetate > methyl acetate > isopropyl acetate","all three react at the same rate (same acyl group)"],"correct":1,"why":"Saponification goes through a tetrahedral intermediate; steric bulk of the alkoxy R group slows hydroxide attack. Bulk order methyl < isopropyl < tert-butyl gives rate order methyl > isopropyl > tert-butyl.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (tert-butyl acetate). Two-concept (mechanism + sterics) ranking; standard textbook trend, one correct order. Keep."},{"q":"The ester below (ethyl acetate) can be hydrolyzed under acidic OR basic conditions. Which statement correctly contrasts the two pathways?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both pathways are reversible equilibria with similar position","Acidic hydrolysis is reversible (it is the reverse of Fischer esterification); basic hydrolysis (saponification) is effectively irreversible","Acidic hydrolysis requires a stoichiometric amount of acid that is consumed","Saponification regenerates the neutral carboxylic acid directly without workup","Acidic hydrolysis is irreversible; saponification is a reversible equilibrium"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid hydrolysis is the microscopic reverse of Fischer esterification (reversible, acid is catalytic). Saponification is driven irreversibly because hydroxide deprotonates the acid to the stable carboxylate. Options 2-5 each misstate reversibility/catalysis/workup.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethyl acetate, parses as CCOC(C)=O). Conceptual contrast, only option 0 fully correct. Keep."},{"q":"gamma-Butyrolactone (the cyclic ester drawn below) is in equilibrium with its open-chain hydroxy acid form, 4-hydroxybutanoic acid. Which single set of conditions would open the lactone ring to give the carboxylate salt of that hydroxy acid in an essentially irreversible step?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Aqueous NaOH, heat (saponification of the lactone)","Pyridine, no water","Catalytic H2SO4 in excess methanol, heat","Excess ethanol with a Dean-Stark trap, catalytic H2SO4","Catalytic H2SO4 in water, heat"],"correct":0,"why":"A lactone is an intramolecular ester, so it saponifies. NaOH/heat opens the ring and deprotonates the acid to sodium 4-hydroxybutanoate (irreversible). Acidic water gives a reversible equilibrium favoring the 5-membered lactone; methanol/H2SO4 transesterifies; Dean-Stark/excess alcohol drives ring closure; pyridine does nothing.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (gamma-butyrolactone, 5-membered ring). Recognition-level lactone saponification with Le Chatelier layer; at upper DAT edge but in scope (no mechanism/synthesis). Single correct answer. Keep."},{"q":"(R)-Lactic acid (drawn) is heated with excess methanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer esterification). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","opts":["COC(=O)[C@@H](C)O","COC(=O)[C@H](C)O","CO[C@H](C)C(=O)O","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)OC","CC(=O)OC(=O)[C@@H](C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"RDKit confirms SM is (R) and keyed opt0 is (R): Fischer esterification converts only -COOH to -COOCH3, the bond to the alpha stereocenter never breaks, so configuration is retained. The secondary -OH is unreactive, ruling out the O-methylated (opt2/opt3) and anhydride (opt4) distractors. opt1 is the inverted (S) epimer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. SM R and opt0 R confirmed by CIP; chemoselectivity + retention correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"cis-4-tert-Butylcyclohexyl acetate (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH, then mild acidic workup (saponification). Which alcohol is produced?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](OC)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(=O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"3D geometry analysis confirms SM acetate is cis and keyed opt0 alcohol is cis (opt1 is the trans diastereomer). Saponification cleaves the acyl-oxygen bond (C=O carbon attacked), never the alkyl C-O bond at the ring stereocenter, so the ring carbon keeps its configuration and the cis relationship to tert-butyl is preserved.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. cis SM, cis product, trans trap verified by 3D embedding; acyl-O cleavage retention correct."},{"q":"Methyl (S)-2-phenylpropanoate (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and catalytic acid (transesterification). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCOC(=O)[C@@H](CC)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","C=C(C(=O)OCC)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"RDKit confirms SM and keyed opt0 are both (S). Transesterification swaps -OCH3 to -OCH2CH3 at the carbonyl; the alpha stereocenter is untouched, so S is retained. opt1 is the inverted (R) epimer, opt2 is unchanged SM, opt3/opt4 alter the skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. SM S and opt0 S confirmed by CIP; transesterification retention correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"5-Hydroxypentanoic acid (drawn) is warmed with a trace of acid. Considering ring-size preference for intramolecular esterification, which lactone forms preferentially?","q_smiles":"OCCCCC(=O)O","opts":["O=C1CCCCO1","O=C1CCCCCO1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C1CCCO1","O=C(O)CCCCOC(=O)CCCCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Keyed opt0 is the 6-membered delta-valerolactone (C5H8O2, ring size 6 confirmed), the favored product from a 5-hydroxy acid. The 5-membered opt1 (C4) would need a 4-hydroxy acid, opt2 is 7-membered, opt4 is linear dimeric ester, opt5 is a cyclopentanone (not a lactone).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Minor: stem parenthetical is slightly garbled wording, but answer is unambiguous (a 5-hydroxy acid can only cyclize to the 6-membered lactone). Chemistry correct."},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate (drawn) is refluxed with aqueous NaOH (saponification). Which species is the carbon-containing organic product isolated before acidification?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","O=C([O-])c1ccccc1.[Na+]","O=Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC[O-].[Na+]"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Saponification is base-promoted and irreversible because the carboxylic acid is deprotonated to the resonance-stabilized carboxylate. Before acidification the isolated benzoate species is sodium benzoate (opt0), not the free acid (opt1, requires acidification). Ethanol is the alcohol byproduct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. Carboxylate salt is the correct pre-acidification product; valid SMILES with Na+ counterion; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Propanoic acid (drawn) is esterified with CH3-18OH (oxygen-18 labeled methanol) and catalytic acid. In the resulting methyl propanoate, where does the 18O label end up?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts":["In the single-bonded ester oxygen (the -O-CH3 oxygen)","Split evenly between both ester oxygens","In the water molecule released","In the carbonyl (C=O) oxygen","No 18O is incorporated into the ester"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Classic isotope-labeling result: the alcohol oxygen adds to the carbonyl carbon and is retained as the new alkyl C-O bond, while water leaves carrying the acid's -OH oxygen (acyl-oxygen cleavage on the acid). So 18O from methanol ends up in the single-bonded -O-CH3 ester oxygen, not the carbonyl O and not the water.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. Standard textbook isotope-tracing fact; recognition-level, in scope; molecule drawn in q_smiles; one defensible answer."},{"q":"(S)-Mandelic acid (drawn) is heated with methanol and catalytic HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","COC(C(=O)O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)OC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"RDKit confirms SM and keyed opt0 are both (S). Only the -COOH esterifies to the methyl ester; the benzylic secondary -OH is unreactive and its stereocenter is untouched, so S is retained. opt1 is the inverted (R) epimer, opt2 methylates the OH (free acid), opt3 is an anhydride, opt4 is the ethyl ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. SM S and opt0 S confirmed by CIP; chemoselectivity + retention correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"gamma-Butyrolactone (drawn) is heated with excess methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which open-chain structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCO1","opts":["O=C1CCCO1","COC(=O)CCCO","O=C(O)CCCO","COCCCC(=O)OC","COC(=O)CCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed methanolysis opens the lactone via acyl-oxygen cleavage to methyl 4-hydroxybutanoate (opt0, C5H10O3 = GBL C4H6O2 + MeOH): carbonyl becomes -COOCH3 and the ring O becomes a terminal -OH. opt1 has wrong chain length (C4), opt2 is the hydroxy acid, opt3 also methylates the alcohol, opt4 is unreacted lactone.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. Formula and connectivity of methyl 4-hydroxybutanoate confirmed; ring-opening transesterification correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetyl chloride is treated with ethanol (and a base such as pyridine). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CC(=O)O","CCOCC","CCC(=O)OC","CCOC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Acid chloride is the most reactive acyl derivative; ethanol O attacks the carbonyl, chloride leaves, giving ethyl acetate (opt0, CCOC(C)=O). Anhydride, diethyl ether, acetic acid, and rearranged methyl propanoate are all wrong skeletons. RDKit confirms all five SMILES valid and the keyed structure matches ethyl acetate.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzoyl chloride is treated with excess dimethylamine. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(Cl)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(N(C)C)c1ccccc1","O=C(NC)c1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary amine adds to the acyl carbon and displaces chloride, giving N,N-dimethylbenzamide (opt0). N-methylbenzamide (one N-sub), methyl ester, benzoic acid, and benzylamine (no carbonyl) are wrong. All SMILES parse; key matches.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Acetic anhydride reacts with methanol. What is the major ester product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CCOC(C)=O","COC=O","CNC(C)=O","COC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Methanol attacks one acyl carbon; acetate leaves, giving methyl acetate (opt0, COC(C)=O) plus acetic acid. Ethyl ester (wrong nucleophile), methyl formate (wrong carbon count), unreacted anhydride, and amide are wrong. RDKit confirms validity and the key.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate is treated with two equivalents of methylmagnesium bromide (CH3MgBr), then aqueous acid workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O","CCO","CC(C)(C)O","CC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"First equivalent adds and expels ethoxide to give acetone; second adds to the ketone, giving 2-methyl-2-propanol (tert-butanol, opt0) after workup. Excess Grignard cannot stop at the ketone (opt3); other oxidation states/skeletons wrong. Two layered concepts (acyl substitution then second addition) but standard textbook, in DAT scope. SMILES all valid.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0); two-concept (acyl sub + 2nd addition) acceptable, in scope."},{"q":"Acetamide is heated with aqueous HCl (acidic hydrolysis). What is the major organic product derived from the carbonyl?","q_smiles":"CC(N)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CC(=O)Cl","CC(=O)O","CCO","CC#N"],"correct":2,"why":"Amide hydrolysis replaces N (as ammonium) with -OH, giving acetic acid (opt0). Cannot go uphill to acid chloride or anhydride; ethanol is wrong oxidation state; nitrile is a dehydration, not hydrolysis, product. All SMILES valid; key matches.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Propanoyl chloride is treated with sodium acetate (CH3CO2Na). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)OC(C)=O","CCC(=O)OC(=O)CC","CCC(=O)O","CC(=O)OC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Acetate (carboxylate nucleophile) displaces chloride, forming the mixed anhydride propanoic acetic anhydride (opt0, CCC(=O)OC(C)=O). The symmetric propanoic anhydride (opt1) needs a second propanoyl unit; methyl ester, acid, and acetic anhydride are wrong. Two-concept (which nucleophile + mixed vs symmetric) but standard. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0); mixed-anhydride product correctly distinguished from symmetric distractor."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product from the acyl carbon?","q_smiles":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","CO","OCc1ccccc1","OC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces esters all the way to a primary alcohol (benzyl alcohol, opt0), releasing methanol. The aldehyde cannot be isolated with LiAlH4; toluene requires over-reduction not seen; gem-diol is not isolable; methanol is the leaving fragment. SMILES all valid; key matches.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride (drawn) is treated with methanol. The bond to the stereocenter is never broken. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(Cl)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Nucleophilic acyl substitution occurs only at the carbonyl carbon; the alpha stereocenter is a spectator, so configuration is retained. RDKit confirms substrate is S and opt0 is S (retention), while opt1 is the inverted R distractor; opt2 is the ethyl ester (wrong nucleophile), opt3 the acid, opt4 the flat/racemized center. Key matches; SMILES all valid. Recognition-level stereo, in DAT scope (no mechanism drawn).","difficulty":3,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0); RDKit verifies S substrate -> S product (retention), opt1 correctly R-inverted."},{"q":"The acid chloride shown is treated with excess dimethylamine, (CH3)2NH. What is the organic product of this nucleophilic acyl substitution?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts":["CC(=O)N","CC(=O)OC(=O)C","CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)N(C)C","CC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetyl chloride + secondary amine (CH3)2NH gives the N,N-disubstituted amide N,N-dimethylacetamide. Excess amine neutralizes HCl. Keyed SMILES CC(=O)N(C)C is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written. All 5 option SMILES valid; index 0 (N,N-dimethylacetamide) is the right product; distractors (acid, ester, primary amide, anhydride) plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"Rank the four carboxylic acid derivatives shown by their reactivity toward nucleophilic acyl substitution, from MOST reactive to LEAST reactive: acetyl chloride (CH3COCl), acetic anhydride [(CH3CO)2O], ethyl acetate (CH3CO2Et), and acetamide (CH3CONH2).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl.CC(=O)OC(=O)C.CC(=O)OCC.CC(=O)N","opts":["anhydride > acid chloride > ester > amide","ester > amide > anhydride > acid chloride","acid chloride > ester > anhydride > amide","acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide","amide > ester > anhydride > acid chloride"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Canonical reactivity order: acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide, set by leaving-group ability and heteroatom donation into the carbonyl. Index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Multi-molecule q_smiles parses; standard order; only index 0 is defensible."},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate (shown) is heated with aqueous NaOH, then acidified on workup. What name best describes this reaction, and what carbonyl-containing product is formed?","q_smiles":"O=C(OCC)c1ccccc1","opts":["Reduction; benzaldehyde","Saponification (base-promoted ester hydrolysis); benzoic acid","Transesterification; methyl benzoate","Friedel-Crafts acylation; an aryl ketone","Fischer esterification; ethyl benzoate is regenerated"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Base-promoted ester hydrolysis (saponification): hydroxide expels ethoxide; acid workup gives benzoic acid. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; one defensible answer."},{"q":"A student wants to convert the carboxylic acid CH3CH2COOH directly into the ester CH3CH2COOCH2CH3 in one step. Which reagent combination accomplishes this Fischer esterification?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts":["SOCl2 only","Aqueous NaOH, then H3O+","LiAlH4, then H2O","Excess CH3CH2OH with catalytic H2SO4, heat","CH3CH2OH with catalytic NaOH, heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Fischer esterification = carboxylic acid + alcohol, acid catalysis (H2SO4), heat, excess alcohol to drive equilibrium. Index 0 correct; base, SOCl2, and LiAlH4 give wrong outcomes.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles (propanoic acid) valid; single defensible answer."},{"q":"The acid chloride shown is treated with one equivalent of ethanol (and a base such as pyridine to absorb HCl). Draw the major organic product.","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)Cl","opts":["CCC(=O)N","CCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)OC(=O)CC","CCC(=O)OCC","CCC(=O)NCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Propanoyl chloride + EtOH/pyridine gives the ester ethyl propanoate (CCC(=O)OCC). Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. All 5 option SMILES valid; index 0 is ethyl propanoate; distractors plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"Acetic anhydride (shown) reacts with methanol to give methyl acetate plus a second product. Identify the reaction class and the by-product.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(=O)C","opts":["Electrophilic addition; CO2 is the by-product","Nucleophilic acyl substitution; methane is the by-product","Nucleophilic acyl substitution; acetic acid is the by-product","Saponification; sodium acetate is the by-product","Esterification; water is the by-product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"MeOH attacks one anhydride carbonyl; acetate is expelled (nucleophilic acyl substitution), giving methyl acetate + acetic acid. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; one defensible answer; two-concept (class + by-product) within scope."},{"q":"Two esters are compared under identical basic hydrolysis (saponification) conditions: phenyl acetate (CH3CO-O-C6H5, shown) and ethyl acetate (CH3CO-O-CH2CH3). Which ester hydrolyzes FASTER, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","opts":["Phenyl acetate, because the aromatic ring donates electron density into the carbonyl","Phenyl acetate, because phenoxide is a more stable (better) leaving group than ethoxide","Ethyl acetate, because ethoxide is a better leaving group than phenoxide","They hydrolyze at the same rate; the leaving group does not matter","Ethyl acetate, because the smaller alkyl group makes the carbonyl more electrophilic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Phenoxide is resonance-stabilized (weaker base, better leaving group than ethoxide), so phenyl acetate hydrolyzes faster. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; leaving-group-stability reasoning is sophomore-level; one defensible answer."},{"q":"N-Methylacetamide (shown) is the least reactive of the common acyl derivatives. Which single statement BEST explains why amides resist nucleophilic acyl substitution and require harsh (strong acid or base, heat) hydrolysis conditions?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)NC","opts":["The methyl group on nitrogen sterically shields the carbonyl completely","Nitrogen is more electronegative than oxygen, so it withdraws electrons and stabilizes the tetrahedral intermediate","The C-N bond is a true double bond that cannot be broken under any conditions","The nitrogen lone pair is strongly donated into the carbonyl (amide resonance), lowering carbonyl electrophilicity, and amide (NR2-) is the poorest leaving group","Amides are protonated at carbon, blocking nucleophilic attack"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Amide stability = strong n(N)->pi*(C=O) resonance donation (less electrophilic carbonyl, partial C-N double bond) plus amide being the worst leaving group. Index 0 correct; nitrogen is LESS electronegative than oxygen, ruling out distractor 1.","difficulty":3,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; recognition-level amide-resonance concept (no FMO/quant), in DAT scope; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzoyl chloride is treated with methanol (and a trace of pyridine to neutralize HCl). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(Cl)c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1","COC(O)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)c1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Alcohol + acid chloride is a textbook nucleophilic acyl substitution: MeOH attacks the carbonyl, chloride leaves, pyridine mops up HCl, giving methyl benzoate (opt0). Acid (hydrolysis), N-methylamide (needs amine), hemiacetal (impossible at sp2 acyl), and anisole (no carbonyl) are valid distractor SMILES but wrong products. All 5 SMILES parse.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct, single concept, all SMILES valid, visual."},{"q":"Acetic anhydride is treated with excess ammonia (NH3). Which structure is the nitrogen-containing organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(C)=O","opts":["CCN","CC(N)=O","CCOC(C)=O","CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CC(=O)NC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"NH3 attacks one carbonyl of the anhydride, acetate leaves, giving acetamide (opt0) plus acetic acid. Simple anhydride + NH3 does not force the imide (opt1, needs heat/forcing conditions); ethylamine (reduction), ethyl acetate (ester), and unreacted anhydride are wrong. All SMILES parse.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed answer (acetamide) correct; imide distractor properly excluded; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The four common carboxylic-acid derivatives shown by their general reactivity are acid chloride, anhydride, ester, and amide. Acetyl chloride is drawn. Which lists these derivatives in order of DECREASING reactivity toward nucleophilic acyl substitution?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts":["ester > acid chloride > amide > anhydride","acid chloride > ester > anhydride > amide","acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide","anhydride > acid chloride > amide > ester","amide > ester > anhydride > acid chloride"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Standard reactivity hierarchy driven by leaving-group ability and resonance donation: acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide (opt0). Distractors scramble or reverse the order. Substrate SMILES drawn (acetyl chloride) and valid; classification item correctly text-optioned.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Canonical ordering correct; one defensible answer; substrate drawn."},{"q":"Propyl acetate is heated with aqueous NaOH (saponification). Which structure is the carbonyl-containing product?","q_smiles":"CCCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCCO","CCC(=O)[O-].[Na+]","CC(=O)Cl","CC(=O)OCCC","CC(=O)[O-].[Na+]"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Saponification: hydroxide attacks the carbonyl, propoxide leaves, and the acid is deprotonated to the carboxylate. The acyl half is acetyl, so the carbonyl product is sodium acetate (opt0); propanol is the alcohol coproduct (not carbonyl-containing); sodium propanoate has the wrong skeleton; unchanged ester and acid chloride are wrong. Stem disambiguates 'carbonyl-containing' to exclude propanol. All SMILES parse.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed sodium acetate correct; 'carbonyl-containing' qualifier removes ambiguity vs propanol; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"(R)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride is treated with excess methylamine (CH3NH2). The alpha carbon (bearing CH3, phenyl, H) is a stereocenter and is NOT the reacting center. Which structure is the product, with correct configuration?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](C(=O)Cl)c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CNC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","C[C@@H](C(=O)O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Substitution occurs at the carbonyl carbon; no bond to the alpha stereocenter breaks, so the spatial arrangement is retained. RDKit confirms: physically swapping Cl for NHMe on the (R) substrate gives exactly opt0 (CNC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1), which is still CIP R. The inverted center (opt1 = S) is the stereo distractor; ester, unreacted acid, and reduced amine are wrong. All SMILES parse and CIP verified.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Verified by RDKit: retention product = keyed opt0, label stays R; inverted distractor = S; two-concept (acyl substitution + stereo retention) within scope."},{"q":"In the addition-elimination mechanism of nucleophilic acyl substitution on acetyl chloride (shown), consider the very FIRST mechanistic step. Which curved arrow correctly describes it?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts":["The carbonyl oxygen's lone pair attacks the nucleophile","The nucleophile attacks chlorine directly, displacing the acyl group","The C-Cl bond breaks first to give an acylium cation, then the nucleophile adds","A lone pair on the nucleophile attacks the carbonyl carbon while the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen","A C-H bond of the methyl group breaks to form an enolate"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Addition-elimination begins with the nucleophile's lone pair attacking the electrophilic carbonyl carbon as the C=O pi electrons shift onto oxygen, forming the tetrahedral intermediate (opt0). Acid chlorides do not pre-ionize to an acylium under normal nucleophilic conditions (opt1), no enolate forms, and the nucleophile does not attack O or Cl. Prompt explicitly allows 'identify one arrow.' Substrate drawn and valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Single-arrow identification is explicitly in scope; correct first step keyed; substrate drawn."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate is heated with excess dimethylamine (HN(CH3)2). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)c1ccccc1","COC(c1ccccc1)N(C)C","CN(C)C(=O)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Aminolysis converts the ester to the more stable amide: dimethylamine attacks, methoxide leaves, giving N,N-dimethylbenzamide (opt0). The N-methyl amide (wrong amine, would need methylamine), benzoic acid (hydrolysis), unchanged ester, and the aminal are wrong. All SMILES parse.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed N,N-dimethylbenzamide correct; N-methyl distractor properly differs by substitution; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Succinic anhydride (the cyclic anhydride shown) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of methanol. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(=O)O1","opts":["O=C1CCC(=O)O1","COC(=O)CCC(=O)OC","COC(=O)CC(=O)O","O=C(O)CCC(=O)O","COC(=O)CCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"One equivalent of MeOH opens the cyclic anhydride once: an ester forms at the attacked carbonyl and the broken C-O becomes a free carboxylic acid, giving monomethyl succinate (opt0; RDKit confirms one methyl ester + one COOH on a 4-carbon diacid backbone). The diester (2 eq), succinic diacid (water not methanol), unopened ring, and the shorter malonate monoester (wrong chain length) are wrong.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed monomethyl succinate verified by substructure/chain-length check; distractors plausible; single ring-opening event within scope."},{"q":"The carboxylic acid shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["CCCC=O","CCC(C)O","CCC=O","CCCCO","CCCCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid fully to a primary (1 degree) alcohol; carbon count unchanged, so butanoic acid gives 1-butanol (CCCCO). LiAlH4 cannot stop at the aldehyde, reduction is at the carbonyl carbon (not C2), and no carbons are gained or lost.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (1-butanol) is the correct full-reduction product. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"The tertiary amide shown is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(N(C)C)c1ccccc1","opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CN(C)c1ccccc1","CN(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide to the amine (C=O becomes CH2, nitrogen retained, no C-N cleavage), so N,N-dimethylbenzamide gives N,N-dimethylbenzylamine (CN(C)Cc1ccccc1). No benzyl alcohol/benzaldehyde (C-N not cleaved), the carbonyl carbon is not lost, and the hemiaminal is not isolable.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (amide to amine) is correct. Distractors are the acid-style products, classic traps."},{"q":"The methyl ester shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product (the alcohol derived from the acyl portion)?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)OC","opts":["CCCO","CCC(C)O","CCC=O","CCCOCC","CCCOC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an ester to a primary alcohol, cleaving the acyl C-O and converting the acyl carbon to CH2OH; methyl propanoate gives 1-propanol (CCCO). No ether product (C-O is cleaved), reduction passes the aldehyde, and no rearrangement to a 2 degree alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (1-propanol) correct. Stem specifies acyl-portion alcohol, removing methanol ambiguity. In scope."},{"q":"The beta-keto acid shown is heated (no other reagents). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","opts":["CC(=O)CC=O","CC(O)CC(=O)O","CC(C)=O","CCC(=O)O","CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A beta-keto acid undergoes thermal decarboxylation via a six-membered cyclic transition state, losing CO2 to give the enol, which tautomerizes to the ketone; 3-oxobutanoic acid gives acetone (CC(C)=O). No reduction (keto retained), and the alpha carbon gains an H rather than forming a new C-C bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (acetone) correct. Decarboxylation recognition, single concept, visual, in scope (no full mechanism required)."},{"q":"The substituted beta-keto acid shown is heated. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C(C)C(=O)O","opts":["CC(=O)C(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)C=O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC=O","CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"This is a beta-keto acid: thermal decarboxylation loses CO2 and the alpha carbon picks up an H, giving butan-2-one (MEK, CCC(C)=O) from 2-methyl-3-oxobutanoic acid. The ketone is retained (not reduced or made an aldehyde), no skeleton rearrangement, and decarboxylation does proceed (not recovery of starting acid).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (butan-2-one) correct. Two layered (recognize beta-keto + build correct skeleton). In scope."},{"q":"Benzoic acid is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the aromatic carboxylic acid to a primary benzylic alcohol; benzoic acid gives benzyl alcohol (OCc1ccccc1). The ring is inert to LiAlH4 (no toluene), reduction does not stop at benzaldehyde, no carbon is added (not 2-phenylethanol), and the carbon is not lost to phenol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (benzyl alcohol) correct. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The COOH carbon is reduced to a CH2OH while remaining attached to the ring, giving cyclohexylmethanol (OCC1CCCCC1, a primary alcohol). The carboxyl carbon stays outside the ring (not cyclohexanol), reduction passes the aldehyde, no extra carbon added, and the C-O is not removed (not methylcyclohexane).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (cyclohexylmethanol) correct. Cyclohexanol distractor tests the common carbon-count error. In scope."},{"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanamide is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. The stereocenter is at the carbon alpha to the carbonyl. What is the major organic product, including correct configuration?","q_smiles":"O=C(N)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","O=C[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide to a primary amine (C=O becomes CH2, nitrogen retained), giving 2-phenylpropan-1-amine. No bond to the alpha stereocenter is broken, so its spatial configuration is retained; RDKit confirms substrate and keyed option 0 are both S. Distractor 1 inverts the center (R), the alcohol comes from wrongly treating the amide like an acid, the aldehyde is an incorrect reduction, and the last option adds a ring methyl.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid. RDKit: substrate S, keyed option 0 S (spatial retention; CIP label stays S because C(=O)NH2 to CH2NH2 preserves priority order), option 1 R. Keyed index 0 correct. Two layered (reduction + stereo retention), within allowance; in scope."},{"q":"The carboxylic acid shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","opts":["The unchanged carboxylic acid (no reaction)","A primary alcohol (1-hexanol)","An aldehyde (hexanal)","A secondary alcohol","A methyl ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid all the way to a primary alcohol (it does not stop at the aldehyde). Hexanoic acid gives 1-hexanol.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CCCCCC(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. LiAlH4 fully reduces RCOOH to RCH2OH; hexanoic acid -> 1-hexanol. Valid SMILES, single concept, in scope, distractors plausible."},{"q":"Which reagent converts the amide shown into benzylamine (PhCH2NH2)?","opts":["Dilute aqueous HCl, heat","H2/Pd-C","LiAlH4, then H2O","PCC in CH2Cl2","NaBH4 in methanol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the nitrogen, giving an amine. NaBH4 is too weak to reduce amides; the others do not perform this reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Benzamide + LiAlH4 -> benzylamine; NaBH4 too weak, others do not reduce amide C=O. One defensible answer."},{"q":"The ester shown is treated with LiAlH4 followed by aqueous workup. Which classes of products are formed?","opts":["An aldehyde plus an alcohol","No reaction occurs","A carboxylic acid plus an alcohol","A single ketone","Two alcohols: a primary alcohol from the acyl portion plus the alcohol from the -OR group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 cleaves an ester to give a primary alcohol from the acyl carbon (1-propanol here) and releases the alkoxy group as its alcohol (ethanol). It does not stop at the aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Ethyl propanoate + LiAlH4 -> 1-propanol (acyl) + ethanol (alkoxy), two alcohols. SMILES valid, standard textbook reaction."},{"q":"The compound shown (a beta-keto acid) is heated. What reaction occurs and what carbonyl product results?","opts":["Decarboxylation, losing CO2 to give acetone","No reaction; beta-keto acids are thermally stable","Reduction to a 1,3-diol","Aldol condensation to a larger ring","Decarboxylation to give acetic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"A beta-keto acid undergoes thermal decarboxylation through a six-membered cyclic transition state, expelling CO2. 3-oxobutanoic (acetoacetic) acid loses CO2 to give acetone.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acetoacetic acid -> acetone + CO2 via 6-membered cyclic TS. Valid SMILES, classic decarboxylation, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Which statement best explains why the substituted malonic acid shown readily loses CO2 when heated, whereas a simple acid like propanoic acid does not?","opts":["A carbonyl (the second COOH) is positioned beta to the carboxyl being lost, enabling a six-membered cyclic transition state","Steric strain from the methyl group forces CO2 out","The alpha C-H bonds are unusually weak due to ring strain","The molecule is aromatic and decarboxylation restores aromaticity","Decarboxylation is driven by formation of a primary carbocation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Malonic (1,3-diacid) and beta-keto acids decarboxylate because the second carbonyl sits beta to the carboxyl, allowing a cyclic six-membered transition state and an enol intermediate. A simple monoacid has no beta carbonyl, so it is thermally stable.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CC(C(=O)O)C(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. SMILES = methylmalonic acid, a genuine substituted malonic acid; second carbonyl is beta to the carboxyl lost, enabling 6-membered TS. Distractors (aromaticity, carbocation) are plausibly wrong. In scope (conceptual, no mechanism drawing)."},{"q":"Rank the following carbonyl compounds by how readily their C=O is reduced by LiAlH4 (fastest to slowest): the acyl chloride shown, an amide, and a carboxylate salt (RCOO-).","opts":["Amide > acyl chloride > carboxylate salt","Carboxylate salt > amide > acyl chloride","Acyl chloride > amide > carboxylate salt","All three react at essentially the same rate","Carboxylate salt > acyl chloride > amide"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Reactivity tracks electrophilicity of the carbonyl: acyl chloride is most reactive, amide is sluggish (resonance-stabilized N), and a deprotonated carboxylate is least electrophilic (negative charge repels hydride). The acyl chloride reacts fastest.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)Cl","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Standard carbonyl reactivity order acyl chloride > amide > carboxylate. Valid SMILES (butanoyl chloride), one defensible ranking."},{"q":"The tertiary amide shown is reduced with LiAlH4 and worked up. What is the product class, and does the nitrogen substitution change?","opts":["A primary alcohol; the C-N bond is broken","A primary amine; the N-methyl groups are cleaved off","A tertiary amine; the C=O becomes CH2 and the N-CH3 groups are retained","A secondary amine; one N-methyl group is lost","No reaction; tertiary amides are inert to LiAlH4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene without cleaving the C-N bond, so an N,N-dimethyl amide gives an N,N-dimethyl (tertiary) amine. The nitrogen substitution pattern is preserved.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"O=C(c1ccccc1)N(C)C","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. N,N-dimethylbenzamide + LiAlH4 -> N,N-dimethylbenzylamine (tertiary amine), N-substitution retained. Valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Two keto acids are considered for thermal decarboxylation: the gamma-keto acid shown (4-oxopentanoic / levulinic acid) and 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoic acid (a beta-keto acid). Which statement is correct?","opts":["The gamma-keto acid decarboxylates faster because the carbonyl is farther from the carboxyl","Only the beta-keto acid decarboxylates readily on heating; the gamma-keto acid does not, because its carbonyl is too far for a six-membered cyclic transition state","Only the gamma-keto acid decarboxylates, giving a cyclic product","Both decarboxylate equally well because both contain a ketone","Neither decarboxylates; a ketone cannot assist loss of CO2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Facile thermal decarboxylation requires the second carbonyl to be beta to the carboxyl so a six-membered cyclic transition state can form. The beta-keto acid satisfies this; in the gamma-keto acid the ketone is one carbon too far, so no low-energy cyclic TS exists.","difficulty":3,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. SMILES = levulinic acid (4-oxopentanoic, gamma-keto); ketone is gamma to carboxyl, too far for 6-membered TS, so it does not readily decarboxylate while the beta-keto acid does. Valid SMILES, comparison distractors plausible, in scope."},{"q":"Butanoic acid is treated with excess LiAlH4 in dry ether, followed by aqueous workup. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["CCC(C)=O","CCCC(O)O","CCCC=O","CCC(C)O","CCCCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces carboxylic acids fully to primary alcohols; butanoic acid gives 1-butanol (CCCCO). The aldehyde (opt2) is an unisolable intermediate; opt3 puts OH on the wrong carbon; opts4-5 keep oxidized carbons. All five SMILES valid; exactly one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct, single concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"N,N-dimethylbenzamide is treated with LiAlH4 then aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(c1ccccc1)N(C)C","opts":["CNCc1ccccc1","CNC(=O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces amides to amines (C=O -> CH2) with retention of the C-N bond, giving N,N-dimethylbenzylamine (CN(C)Cc1ccccc1). Opt2 (benzyl alcohol) wrongly cleaves C-N; opts3,5 lose methyls; opt4 unreduced. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct amide->amine, valid, visual."},{"q":"3-Oxobutanoic acid (acetoacetic acid) is heated. Which structure is the major organic product after loss of CO2?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","opts":["CC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCO","CC(=O)C(=O)O","CCC(C)=O","CCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Beta-keto acid undergoes thermal decarboxylation via a six-membered TS to give acetone (CC(C)=O). Opt2 aldehyde and opt3 longer chain are wrong skeletons; opt5 is an alpha-keto acid that doesn't decarboxylate this way. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic beta-keto-acid decarboxylation, valid, visual."},{"q":"(S)-2-methylbutanoic acid is reduced with LiAlH4, then worked up with water. Which structure is the major product, including the correct configuration at the stereocenter?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O","opts":["CC[C@@H](C)CO","CCC(C)CO","CC[C@H](C)C=O","CC(C)CCO","CC[C@H](C)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit confirms SM is (S). Reduction breaks no bond to the stereocenter, so spatial arrangement is retained; CH2OH remains highest priority, so descriptor stays S (opt1, CC[C@H](C)CO). Opt2 is the inverted R trap; opt3 flat; opt4 wrong skeleton; opt5 aldehyde. Layered regio+stereo, two concepts, difficulty 2 appropriate. One correct answer.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. CIP S->S verified by RDKit; retention reasoning correct."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate is treated with excess LiAlH4 followed by aqueous workup. Reduction cleaves the ester, releasing the alkoxy fragment and reducing the acyl fragment. Which structure represents the alcohol product(s) obtained?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCO","CC(O)O","CCCO","CC=O","CCOCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the ester past the aldehyde to a primary alcohol; acyl (CH3CO-) becomes ethanol and the alkoxy (-OCH2CH3) is released as ethanol, so both fragments give ethanol (CCO). Opt2 aldehyde not isolable; opt3 ether not formed; opt5 wrong carbon count. Stem pre-explains the cleavage, so unambiguous. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Both fragments coincide as ethanol; stem disambiguates."},{"q":"2-Methylmalonic acid (a substituted malonic/1,3-diacid) is heated strongly. Which structure is the major organic product after loss of CO2?","q_smiles":"CC(C(=O)O)C(=O)O","opts":["CC(=O)O","CCC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)OC","CC(C)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A malonic-type 1,3-diacid has one COOH beta to the other; thermal decarboxylation via a six-membered TS removes exactly one CO2, giving propanoic acid (CCC(=O)O). Opt2 implies losing both; opts3-5 have wrong skeletons/ester. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-CO2 loss from malonic diacid correct."},{"q":"But-3-enamide (CH2=CH-CH2-CONH2) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCC(=O)N","opts":["CCCCN","C=CCC=O","C=CCCO","C=CCC(=O)O","C=CCCN"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to CH2 with retention of C-N, giving but-3-en-1-amine (C=CCCN). Isolated C=C is not reduced by LiAlH4, so the alkene survives; opt2 wrongly hydrogenates it (over-reduction trap). Opt3 aldehyde, opt4 alcohol, opt5 unreduced acid are wrong outcomes. Chemoselectivity, difficulty 2 appropriate. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Amide->amine with alkene chemoselectivity correct."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","O=[N+]([O-])Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","O=C(O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Methyl is a weak activating ortho/para director; para nitration predominates because it avoids ortho steric crowding. Keyed para product is correct; ortho [1] and meta [2] are disfavored, and COOH [3] and benzylic-nitro [4] are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; CH3 o/p director, para major correctly keyed."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 / FeBr3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)c(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Nitro is a strong deactivator and meta director, so bromination enters meta. Verified the keyed SMILES places Br meta to NO2; ortho [2], para [1], and the dibromide [3] are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. NO2 meta director; keyed meta-bromonitrobenzene confirmed (Br-NO2 = meta)."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) is treated with Br2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1","COc1ccccc1Br","BrCOc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methoxy is a strong o/p activator via lone-pair resonance; no Lewis acid is needed and para predominates due to less hindrance. Meta [1] is wrong for an o/p director and [3] brominates the methyl carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. OCH3 o/p director, para major correctly keyed; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with (CH3)3CCl and AlCl3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(C)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(C)c1ccccc1","CCCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation ionizes tert-butyl chloride to the already-tertiary tert-butyl cation, so no rearrangement occurs and tert-butylbenzene forms. Sec-butyl, n-butyl, and isobutyl would require rearrangement that does not happen.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. tert-Butyl cation is stable, no rearrangement; keyed product correct."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with CH3CH2COCl (propanoyl chloride) and AlCl3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(C)C(=O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Friedel-Crafts acylation via a resonance-stabilized acylium ion gives propiophenone (an aryl ketone). The acylium does not rearrange [3], the product is a ketone not an alkane [2] or ester [4].","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Acylium ion, propiophenone correctly keyed; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with SO3 in H2SO4 (fuming sulfuric acid). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["O=S(=O)(O)Cc1ccccc1","O=S(=O)(Cl)c1ccccc1","O=S(=O)(O)c1ccccc1","O=S(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Sulfonation with SO3 installs -SO3H, giving benzenesulfonic acid (two S=O plus S-OH). The sulfinic acid [1] (one S=O), sulfonyl chloride [2], and benzylic isomer [3] are wrong functional groups/positions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Benzenesulfonic acid correctly keyed; distractors are distinct valid wrong structures."},{"q":"Bromobenzene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. What is the predominant mononitration product?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(Br)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Halogens deactivate inductively but remain o/p directors via lone-pair resonance stabilization of the arenium ion; para predominates over ortho due to sterics. Verified keyed SMILES places NO2 para to Br; meta [1] is wrong and [3] is a dinitro product.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Halogen o/p exception; para-bromonitrobenzene confirmed (Br-NO2 = para)."},{"q":"4-Nitrotoluene (para-nitrotoluene) is treated with Br2 / FeBr3. Where does bromination occur, and what is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(Br)c1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1Br","Cc1cc(Br)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(CBr)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"With competing directors the activating CH3 (o/p) wins over the deactivating NO2 (meta), so Br enters ortho to CH3, a position that is simultaneously meta to NO2 (reinforcing). Verified keyed SMILES: Br ortho to CH3, meta to NO2. Option [1] is ortho to NO2, [2] is benzylic (not EAS), [3] is meta to both.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-director competition; activating CH3 controls; keyed position (ortho-CH3/meta-NO2) confirmed by ring distances."},{"q":"Which set of reagents converts the molecule shown into nitrobenzene via electrophilic aromatic substitution? The active electrophile is the nitronium ion (NO2+).","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["concentrated HNO3 + concentrated H2SO4","Cl2 + FeCl3","KMnO4 + heat","H2 + Pd/C","dilute NaOH + heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aromatic nitration uses concentrated HNO3 + H2SO4; H2SO4 protonates HNO3 to generate the nitronium ion NO2+, the electrophile. The other options are not nitration conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Benzene SMILES valid; standard nitration; one defensible answer (index 0)."},{"q":"The conversion of benzene (shown) to bromobenzene is an example of which reaction type?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["Friedel-Crafts acylation","Electrophilic aromatic substitution (halogenation)","Free-radical halogenation","Electrophilic addition","Nucleophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Bromination of benzene (Br2 + FeBr3) is electrophilic aromatic substitution: the Lewis acid polarizes Br2, the ring attacks Br+, and a proton is lost to rearomatize. It is substitution, not addition or radical, and the ring is the nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem omits explicit Br2/FeBr3 reagents but benzene-to-bromobenzene is unambiguously EAS halogenation; index 0 is the only defensible answer."},{"q":"Which reagents are required to install the acetyl group and form acetophenone from benzene (shown) by Friedel-Crafts acylation?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["CH3COOH + NaOH","CH3CH2Cl + AlCl3","CH3COCl + AlCl3","CH3OH + H2SO4","CH3CH2OH + PCC"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts acylation needs an acyl chloride (or anhydride) plus AlCl3, which forms the resonance-stabilized acylium ion CH3CO+. CH3CH2Cl/AlCl3 gives ethylbenzene (alkylation); the alcohol/acid options do not form an acylium electrophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard FC acylation; index 0 correct."},{"q":"Four monosubstituted benzenes are shown. Toward electrophilic aromatic substitution (e.g., nitration), which substrate reacts FASTEST because its substituent is the strongest activator?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["chlorobenzene","benzaldehyde","benzene","anisole (methoxybenzene)","nitrobenzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"An alkoxy group (-OCH3) is a strong activator: the oxygen lone pair donates into the ring by resonance, raising EAS rate. Chlorobenzene is weakly deactivating; nitrobenzene and benzaldehyde bear strong EWGs that deactivate below benzene's baseline.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Anisole SMILES valid; ordering anisole >> benzene > chlorobenzene > benzaldehyde/nitrobenzene is correct. Index 0 is the strongest activator."},{"q":"Which reagents sulfonate benzene (shown) to give benzenesulfonic acid, and what makes this reaction notably different from nitration?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["Fuming H2SO4 (SO3/H2SO4); sulfonation is reversible","NaHSO3/H2O; sulfonation is a radical process","HNO3/H2SO4; sulfonation requires no catalyst","Cl2/FeCl3; sulfonation is irreversible","SOCl2; sulfonation gives an addition product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Sulfonation uses fuming H2SO4 (SO3 in H2SO4); the electrophile is SO3. Unlike nitration/halogenation/FC, sulfonation is reversible, so the -SO3H group can be removed by aqueous acid and used as a removable blocking group.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct reagent + reversibility distinction (two-concept). Index 0 correct."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene (shown) undergoes a second nitration with HNO3/H2SO4. The -NO2 group is a meta director, so which product predominates?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["1,4-dinitrobenzene (para)","a 1:1 ortho/para mixture","1,3-dinitrobenzene (meta)","1,2-dinitrobenzene (ortho)","no reaction occurs"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The nitro group is a strong deactivator and meta director: ortho/para attack places positive charge adjacent to the electron-poor carbon in the arenium intermediate, so attack favors meta. Major product is 1,3-dinitrobenzene under forcing conditions.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Nitrobenzene SMILES valid; meta direction correct; index 0 (1,3-dinitrobenzene) correct."},{"q":"Benzene (shown) is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Friedel-Crafts ALKYLATION often gives a rearranged product. What is the major product, and why?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["propylbenzene, with no rearrangement","tert-butylbenzene, from a methyl shift","isopropylbenzene (cumene), from a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable secondary carbocation","a mixture with the propyl group on the meta position","no reaction; alkyl halides are unreactive toward AlCl3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"AlCl3 ionizes 1-chloropropane toward a primary cation that undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable secondary (isopropyl) cation before ring attack, giving cumene. Carbocation rearrangement is the classic FC alkylation limitation.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hydride-shift rearrangement to cumene is correct; index 0 correct."},{"q":"A student attempts a Friedel-Crafts acylation on the molecule shown (aniline) using CH3COCl and AlCl3 and obtains essentially no ring-acylated product. What is the best explanation?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetyl chloride is too unreactive to form an acylium ion","The amino group makes the ring so electron-poor that no electrophile can add","The basic -NH2 nitrogen complexes with the Lewis acid AlCl3, forming a strongly deactivating -NH2-AlCl3 group that shuts down EAS","The reaction gives only the meta product, which the student discarded","Aniline is aromatic and therefore cannot undergo any electrophilic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"FC reactions fail on aniline because the Lewis-basic amino N coordinates to AlCl3; the resulting positively charged -NH2-AlCl3 nitrogen strongly deactivates the ring (and consumes catalyst). The free -NH2 is normally a strong activator, so the ring is not inherently electron-poor.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Aniline SMILES valid; standard Klein/Wade explanation. Recognition-level 'best explanation', in scope despite difficulty 3. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"tert-Butylbenzene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C[N+](=O)[O-])c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","CC(C)(C)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","CC(C)(C)c1ccc(N=O)cc1"],"correct":2,"why":"tert-Butyl is an activating o/p director; its bulk blocks both ortho positions so para nitration dominates (choice A). B ortho (steric minor), C meta (wrong director), D nitroso (wrong electrophile), E benzylic NO2 (off-ring). All five SMILES parse; keyed product matches 4-nitro-tert-butylbenzene.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Steric-override of ortho/para EAS, single center, one defensible answer; all SMILES valid; keyed para product correct."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1cccc(N)c1","O=Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","O=C(O)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","O=Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1"],"correct":4,"why":"CHO is a deactivating EWG and meta director, so nitration goes meta (choice A). B para, C ortho (wrong for a meta director). D oxidizes the aldehyde to COOH (not an EAS event). E installs an amine. All SMILES parse; keyed = m-nitrobenzaldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic meta-director recognition; keyed meta product correct; SMILES all valid."},{"q":"Bromobenzene is treated with propanoyl chloride (CH3CH2COCl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)c1cccc(Br)c1","CCC(=O)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(=O)c1ccccc1Br","CCCc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(=O)Oc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Br is a deactivating o/p director (lone-pair resonance); Friedel-Crafts acylation gives the para aryl ketone as major (choice A), ortho minor by sterics. B meta (wrong director), C ortho (minor), D is an O-acyl ester (not ring C-acylation), E is an alkyl group (wrong reaction; acylation installs C=O and does not rearrange). All SMILES valid; keyed = 4'-bromopropiophenone.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Halogen o/p direction + acylation regiochemistry; one defensible answer; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"ClCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation goes through a carbocation; the primary propyl cation undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation, so cumene (isopropylbenzene, choice A) is major. B n-propyl (no-rearrangement trap), C tert-butyl and D ethyl (wrong carbon count), E isobutyl (wrong skeleton). Note q_smiles draws the alkyl halide reagent rather than an aromatic substrate, but it is a valid drawn molecule and the item is visual. All SMILES parse; keyed = cumene.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None substantive. Carbocation-rearrangement recognition, single concept, one defensible answer; SMILES valid. q_smiles shows the haloalkane reagent (still a valid drawn structure), acceptable."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) is treated with Br2 / FeBr3 (one equivalent). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COc1ccccc1Br","COc1ccc(Br)cc1","Oc1ccc(Br)cc1","COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)c(Br)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"OMe is a strong activator and o/p director; with one equivalent the para product dominates (sterics) giving 4-bromoanisole (choice A). B meta (wrong director), C ortho (minor), D dibromo (needs excess), E demethylated to phenol (does not occur). All SMILES valid; keyed = 4-bromoanisole.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Strong-activator para selectivity, mono vs di control; keyed para product correct; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with fuming H2SO4 (SO3 / H2SO4). Which structure is the MAJOR sulfonation product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=S(=O)(O)Cc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(S(=O)(=O)O)c1","Cc1ccc(S(=O)O)cc1","Cc1ccc(S(=O)(=O)O)cc1","Cc1ccccc1S(=O)(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Methyl is an activating o/p director; under forcing/thermodynamic sulfonation the less hindered para isomer (p-toluenesulfonic acid, choice A) predominates. B meta (wrong director), C ortho (minor), D sulfinic acid -SO2H (wrong FG), E benzylic sulfonation (off-ring). All SMILES valid; keyed = p-toluenesulfonic acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Sulfonation o/p direction with FG-recognition distractors; one defensible answer; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Acetanilide (the amide shown) is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(N)cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":2,"why":"The amide N lone pair donates into the ring, making -NHCOCH3 an activating o/p director; the bulky acetamido group favors para, giving p-nitroacetanilide (choice A). B meta (wrong director), C ortho (minor), D shows hydrolysis to free amine (acetyl survives nitration), E installs an amine instead of nitro. All SMILES valid; keyed = p-nitroacetanilide.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Amide-as-activator + steric para preference; keyed product correct; SMILES valid."},{"q":"meta-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene, shown) is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(N)c(C)c1","Cc1cc(C)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1cccc(C)c1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(C)c1"],"correct":4,"why":"Both methyls reinforce at the 4-position (ortho to one CH3, para to the other) and that site is sterically open, so 4-nitro-m-xylene is major (choice A) — connectivity confirms NO2 is ortho/para to the two methyls. The 2-position between the methyls (choice B, NO2 ortho to BOTH) is directed but sterically blocked (minor). C is the 5-position (meta to both, undirected). D installs an amine. E has lost a methyl (wrong skeleton). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Reinforced o/p direction with steric override at C2; verified keyed = 4-nitro-m-xylene (ortho+para to the methyls); two-concept (regio+steric) but in scope; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(Br)cc1Br","Cc1ccccc1Br","BrCc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(Br)c1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"CH3 is an activating o/p director; para dominates over ortho for steric reasons. Keyed opt0 = para-bromotoluene, correct. Distractors are ortho, meta (wrong director), benzyl bromide (side-chain radical, wrong site), and dibromide (over-bromination).","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct, in scope, all SMILES valid, exactly one right answer."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["O=Nc1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1","Nc1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"NO2 is a strong deactivator and meta director; keyed opt0 = meta-bromonitrobenzene, correct. Ortho/para are minor; nitroso and amino distractors change the N group and are not formed.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct meta director, valid SMILES, single answer."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) is treated with Br2 (no catalyst needed). Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccc(Br)cc1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1Br","COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccccc1Br","COc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"OMe is a strong activating o/p director (lone-pair resonance); para is major over ortho for sterics. Keyed opt0 = 4-bromoanisole, correct. Meta is wrong director, phenol loses the methyl, dibromide is over-bromination.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct o/p activator, para major, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Benzoic acid is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C(O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","O=C(O)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","O=Nc1cccc(C(=O)O)c1","O=C(OC)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"COOH is a deactivating meta director; keyed opt0 = meta-nitrobenzoic acid, correct. Ortho/para minor; methyl ester and nitroso distractors alter functional groups and are not nitration products.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct meta director, valid SMILES, single defensible answer."},{"q":"Chlorobenzene is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Cl)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(O)cc1","Clc1ccc(Cl)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Cl)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Halogen exception: deactivating yet o/p director; para is the major product. Keyed opt0 = 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene, correct. Meta is wrong director, OH/dichloro distractors are not formed.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Legitimate two-concept (deactivator + o/p direction), correct para, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Acetanilide (N-phenylacetamide) is treated with Br2 in acetic acid. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1Br","CC(=O)Nc1cccc(Br)c1","CC(=O)N(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(Br)cc1","Nc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The amide N lone pair donates into the ring, making NHCOCH3 an activating o/p director; para dominates (steric). Keyed opt0 = para, correct. Meta wrong director, N-bromination wrong site, free aniline requires hydrolysis (does not occur).","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct o/p activator, para major, valid SMILES, single answer."},{"q":"para-Cresol (4-methylphenol) is treated with Br2. Both substituents are ortho/para directors, but they compete. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(O)cc1","opts":["OCc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccc(O)c(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1","Oc1ccc(C)c(Br)c1","Cc1c(Br)cc(O)cc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"OH (stronger activator) outranks CH3; para to OH is blocked by the methyl, so bromination goes ortho to OH. Keyed opt0 = Br ortho to OH / meta to CH3, confirmed by RDKit mapping. Opt1 puts Br ortho to the methyl (wrong director controls), opt2 is O-methylated skeleton, opt3 over-brominated, opt4 oxidizes the methyl.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Competing-directors concept correct; keyed Br is ortho-to-OH as intended. Valid SMILES, unambiguous."},{"q":"meta-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene) is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4. Both methyls are ortho/para directors; consider where their directing effects reinforce AND sterics. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts":["Cc1cccc(C)c1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(C)c1","Cc1cc(C)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(C)c([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1cccc(C(=O)O)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"With methyls at C1/C3, the C4 position is ortho to one methyl and para to the other (reinforced) and sterically open, giving the major 2,4-dimethyl-1-nitro product. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 has NO2 ortho to one CH3 and para to the other. The C2 site between the methyls (opt1) is reinforced but sterically blocked (minor), C5 (opt2) is meta to both, opt3 oxidizes a methyl, opt4 is the para-xylene skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hard reinforce-plus-sterics item; keyed C4 product correct and unambiguous, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Toluene (drawn below) is treated with Br2 and a FeBr3 catalyst. The methyl group is an ortho/para director. Which best describes the MAJOR monobromination product and the reason for the regiochemistry?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Bromine adds to the carbon bearing the methyl group via ipso substitution","Ortho-bromotoluene only; the methyl group blocks the para position","No reaction occurs because the methyl group deactivates the ring","Para-bromotoluene predominates; the methyl group is an ortho/para director, and the para position is favored over ortho because of less steric hindrance","Meta-bromotoluene predominates; the methyl group is a meta director"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Alkyl groups are weak activators and ortho/para directors. Both ortho and para products form, but para is usually the major product because the bulky electrophile and the methyl group create steric strain at the ortho positions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. CH3 is weak activator o/p director, para major by sterics. SMILES valid, single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene (drawn below) is subjected to electrophilic aromatic substitution (nitration with HNO3/H2SO4). The nitro group is a strong deactivator. Which statement correctly predicts the outcome?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["The reaction is slower than for benzene, and the new nitro group enters mainly at the meta position","The nitro group is an ortho/para director and the reaction is faster than for benzene","The reaction is faster than for benzene, and substitution occurs mainly at the para position","The reaction is faster than for benzene, and substitution occurs mainly at the ortho position","The reaction is slower than for benzene, and substitution occurs mainly at the para position"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The nitro group is a strong electron-withdrawing deactivator that slows EAS relative to benzene and directs incoming electrophiles to the meta position, where the destabilizing positive charge in the resonance intermediate is avoided on the carbon bearing the substituent.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. NO2 strong deactivator, meta director, slower than benzene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"You need to synthesize para-nitroanisole (4-nitroanisole) as the major product. Starting from anisole (drawn below), which single set of reagents accomplishes this in one step?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["KMnO4, heat","H2, Pd/C","Br2, FeBr3","CH3Cl, AlCl3","HNO3, H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The methoxy group is a strong activator and ortho/para director. Nitration with HNO3/H2SO4 installs the nitro group, giving the para isomer as the major product (ortho is minor due to sterics). The other reagents either do not nitrate or perform a different reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. Only HNO3/H2SO4 nitrates; OMe o/p director gives para major. Distractors do other reactions. SMILES valid."},{"q":"Bromobenzene (drawn below) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation. Halogens are an unusual class of substituent in EAS. Which choice correctly classifies the bromine substituent and predicts the major product position?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts":["Activating and meta directing; the acyl group enters mainly meta","Deactivating and meta directing; the acyl group enters mainly meta","Deactivating but ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly para","Activating and ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly para","Deactivating and ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly ortho only"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Halogens are the classic exception: they are deactivating (electron-withdrawing by induction) yet ortho/para directing (lone-pair resonance donation stabilizes the ortho/para arenium intermediates). The para product dominates over ortho largely for steric reasons.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. Halogen = deactivating yet o/p directing, para major. F-C acylation works on weakly deactivated halobenzene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Three substituted benzenes are considered for nitration: aniline (drawn below), toluene, and nitrobenzene. Rank them from FASTEST to SLOWEST rate of electrophilic aromatic substitution.","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["toluene > aniline > nitrobenzene","nitrobenzene > toluene > aniline","nitrobenzene > aniline > toluene","aniline > toluene > nitrobenzene","aniline > nitrobenzene > toluene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Rate tracks ring electron density. The -NH2 group is a strong activator (lone-pair donation), the methyl is a weak activator, and the nitro group is a strong deactivator. So aniline reacts fastest and nitrobenzene slowest.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. NH2 strong activator > CH3 weak activator > NO2 strong deactivator. SMILES valid, single correct ranking."},{"q":"Two monosubstituted benzenes are shown. Substrate A is acetanilide (top SMILES) and substrate B is benzaldehyde. For a nitration reaction, which substrate reacts FASTER and what is the directing preference of its substituent?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetanilide reacts faster; the amide nitrogen is a deactivating meta director","Acetanilide reacts faster; the amide nitrogen is an activating ortho/para director","Benzaldehyde reacts faster; the aldehyde is an activating ortho/para director","Benzaldehyde reacts faster; the aldehyde is a deactivating meta director","Both react at the same rate because both substituents withdraw electrons"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In acetanilide the nitrogen lone pair still donates into the ring, making the -NHC(=O)CH3 group a net activator and ortho/para director (weaker than free -NH2). The aldehyde (-CHO) is a carbonyl: electron-withdrawing, deactivating, and meta directing. So acetanilide reacts faster.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. -NHAc net activator o/p director; -CHO deactivator meta director; acetanilide faster. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Phenol (drawn below) is treated with dilute HNO3. Which choice correctly states the rate relative to benzene and the major position(s) of substitution?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["Faster than benzene; substitution occurs ortho and para to the -OH","Same rate as benzene; substitution is random around the ring","Slower than benzene; substitution occurs ortho and para to the -OH","Faster than benzene; substitution occurs meta to the -OH","Slower than benzene; substitution occurs meta to the -OH"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The hydroxyl group is a strong activator (oxygen lone-pair donation) and an ortho/para director. Phenol therefore undergoes EAS much faster than benzene, with electrophiles entering ortho and para to the -OH.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. OH strong activator, o/p director, faster than benzene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"para-Nitrotoluene (drawn below) is brominated with Br2/FeBr3. The ring carries two substituents whose directing effects must be weighed: the methyl (activating, o/p director) and the nitro (deactivating, meta director). Which statement correctly predicts where bromine adds?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Bromine adds ortho to the nitro group because the nitro group is the stronger director","No substitution occurs because the two directors cancel each other completely","Bromine adds at the carbon bearing the methyl group via ipso substitution","Bromine adds meta to the methyl group because the nitro group overrides the methyl","Bromine adds ortho to the methyl group; the stronger activating director (CH3) controls the regiochemistry, and that position is also meta to the nitro group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"When directors compete, the stronger activator wins. The methyl group (o/p director) dominates over the deactivating nitro group, so bromine enters ortho to the methyl. Conveniently, that same position is meta to the nitro group, so the two effects reinforce, giving 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. Methyl (stronger director) sends Br ortho (C2/C6); that site is also meta to NO2, so directors reinforce. Product 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4 (one equivalent). Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":2,"why":"Methyl is a weak activator and o/p director; with one equivalent the para product (p-nitrotoluene) predominates over the more hindered ortho. Meta is minor, aniline is the wrong FG, and the dinitro structure is an overreaction. opt0 keyed correct and confirmed by RDKit canonicalization.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Brc1ccccc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br","O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","Nc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":2,"why":"NO2 is a strong deactivator and meta director, so Br adds meta to give m-bromonitrobenzene (opt2). Ortho/para options are wrong regiochemistry, bromobenzene drops NO2, and the amino structure is the wrong FG. Verified by RDKit.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) reacts with Br2 (no catalyst needed). Which is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COc1ccc(Cl)cc1","COc1cc(Br)cc(Br)c1","COc1ccccc1Br","COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":4,"why":"OMe is a strong activator and o/p director (no Lewis acid needed); para (4-bromoanisole) predominates over ortho. Meta is disfavored, the dibromide is overreaction, and the chloro option uses the wrong halogen. opt0 confirmed.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Acetanilide (N-phenylacetamide) undergoes nitration with HNO3/H2SO4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(N)cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":1,"why":"The amide N lone pair donates into the ring, so NHC(=O)CH3 is an activating o/p director; the bulky acetamido group sterically favors the para product. Meta/ortho are minor/wrong, the para-amino structure is the wrong FG, and the last option lost the acetamido group. opt0 confirmed.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Consider electrophilic aromatic substitution on chlorobenzene (drawn). Which statement correctly describes the chloro substituent's effect on the reaction?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Activating and ortho/para-directing; the ring reacts faster than benzene.","Deactivating and meta-directing, like a nitro group.","It has no directing influence; a statistical 2:2:1 ortho:meta:para mixture forms.","Activating and meta-directing.","Deactivating but ortho/para-directing; the ring reacts more slowly than benzene yet substitution still goes to the ortho/para positions."],"correct":4,"why":"Halogens are the classic exception: inductively electron-withdrawing (deactivating, slower than benzene) but resonance lone-pair donation stabilizes the o/p sigma complexes, making Cl an o/p director. Not meta-directing, not activating, not directionless. opt0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - text classification item with substrate drawn; single concept, correct as keyed."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is subjected to bromination with Br2/FeBr3. Classify the -CHO group and predict where bromine is installed.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Activating, meta-directing; Br adds meta and the ring reacts faster than benzene.","Deactivating, meta-directing; Br adds at the meta position and the ring reacts slower than benzene.","Deactivating, meta-directing; Br adds para because steric effects override electronics.","Activating, ortho/para-directing; Br adds para and the ring reacts faster than benzene.","Deactivating, ortho/para-directing; Br adds para but the ring reacts slower than benzene."],"correct":1,"why":"The CHO carbonyl carbon withdraws electron density by resonance, making it a deactivator and meta director; Br adds meta and the ring brominates slower than benzene. Meta directors are always deactivators, so the activating choices are wrong, and a meta director does not give a para product. opt0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - single-concept meta-director classification, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Methylphenol (p-cresol, drawn) is mononitrated with dilute HNO3. Both substituents are ortho/para directors, but they compete. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(O)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1cc(O)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(O)c(N)c1","Cc1ccc(O)cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc(O)c([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(O)c(N=O)c1"],"correct":3,"why":"When two o/p directors compete, the stronger activator (OH) wins; OH directs nitro to its ortho position (its para position is occupied by methyl), giving 2-nitro-4-methylphenol (opt0, RDKit-confirmed). opt1 is nitro ortho to the weaker methyl (meta to OH), opt2 is meta to OH, and opts 4-5 are wrong FGs (amino, nitroso).","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - competing-directors plus sterics is two layered concepts, in scope; opt0 verified as 2-nitro-4-methylphenol."},{"q":"m-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene, drawn) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation with CH3COCl/AlCl3. Both methyls direct ortho/para, and the position between them is activated by both. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(C(C)O)c(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)c(C)c1","Cc1cccc(C)c1C(C)=O","Cc1cccc(C)c1CC(C)=O","Cc1cc(C(C)=O)cc(C)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"C2 between the methyls is most activated but sterically blocked for the bulky acylium, so attack goes to C4 (ortho to one methyl, para to the other) giving 2,4-dimethylacetophenone (opt0, RDKit-confirmed). opt1 is the blocked between-methyls product, opt2 is meta to both, opt4 has a CH2-inserted skeleton, and opt5 is an alcohol not a ketone.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - single acylation event with steric+electronic reasoning, in scope; opt0 verified as 2,4-dimethylacetophenone."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene is treated with hot KMnO4, then dilute H2SO4 workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot KMnO4 cleaves any alkyl side chain bearing at least one benzylic C-H down to a carboxylic acid attached directly to the ring, regardless of chain length. Ethylbenzene gives benzoic acid; the entire ethyl group is degraded to one COOH carbon. Ketone, secondary alcohol, and phenylacetic acid retain too many side-chain carbons. Keyed index 0 is benzoic acid (verified canonical O=C(O)c1ccccc1).","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All 5 options valid SMILES, distractors differ by oxidation state/skeleton, exactly one defensible answer (benzoic acid). Clean single-concept benzylic oxidation, in DAT scope, visual."},{"q":"4-Nitrotoluene (p-nitrotoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4 followed by acidic workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","OC(=O)c1cccc(c1)[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(N)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":4,"why":"KMnO4 oxidizes the benzylic methyl to COOH but does not reduce the oxidation-stable nitro group. Product is 4-nitrobenzoic acid, nitro intact and para. Distractors wrongly reduce nitro to amine, move it to meta, or stop at the aldehyde (KMnO4 over-oxidizes the aldehyde to the acid). Keyed index 0 verified as 4-nitrobenzoic acid.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; distractors probe nitro stability, regiochem, and over-oxidation. Exactly one correct answer; two-concept (oxidation + nitro inertness), in scope, visual."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with H2 and a Pd/C catalyst (or Sn/HCl). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["ONc1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Nc1ccccc1","NNc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (or dissolving-metal Sn/HCl) fully reduces an aromatic nitro group to a primary aromatic amine, giving aniline. N-phenylhydroxylamine and nitrosobenzene are non-isolated partial-reduction intermediates; phenol (O for N) and phenylhydrazine are wrong skeletons. Keyed index 0 is aniline (Nc1ccccc1).","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All options valid; distractors are mechanistically themed partial-reduction species plus wrong heteroatom. Single concept, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"4-Chloronitrobenzene is heated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. What is the major product of this nucleophilic aromatic substitution?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","COc1ccccc1","COc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","COc1ccc(Cl)cc1","COc1cccc(c1)[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":2,"why":"SNAr needs a strong EWG ortho/para to the leaving group to stabilize the Meisenheimer intermediate. The para-nitro activates the C-Cl carbon, so methoxide displaces chloride at that carbon giving 4-nitroanisole; substituents stay para. Methoxide (not amine) is the nucleophile, position does not shift to meta, and nitro is retained. Keyed index 0 verified as 4-nitroanisole.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; distractors test regiochem (meta), wrong nucleophile (amine), loss of NO2, and no-reaction. One defensible answer, two-concept (SNAr + activation), in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-tert-Butyl-4-methylbenzene (4-tert-butyltoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4, then acid. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(cc1)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)C(=O)O","Cc1ccc(cc1)C(=O)O","OC(=O)c1cccc(c1)C(C)(C)C","OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)C(C)(C)C","OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)C(C)(C)CO"],"correct":3,"why":"Benzylic KMnO4 oxidation requires a benzylic C-H. The methyl has benzylic hydrogens and is oxidized to COOH, but the tert-butyl benzylic carbon is fully substituted (no benzylic H) and is untouched. Product is 4-tert-butylbenzoic acid. Options oxidizing tert-butyl or both groups are wrong. Keyed index 0 verified.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All valid; distractors oxidize the quaternary group (impossible), mis-regiochem to meta. One defensible answer, single concept (benzylic-H requirement), in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Fluoro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (Sanger's reagent) is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Fc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CNc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","Fc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])NC","CNc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+]=O","Oc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":0,"why":"Two nitro groups ortho and para to C-F strongly activate it toward SNAr. Fluoride is the leaving group (best for SNAr since C-F cleavage is not rate-determining), so the amine displaces F at that carbon, both nitro groups retained. The amine does not add to a nitro carbon, F is not replaced by methyl, and no hydrolysis to phenol occurs. Keyed index 0 verified as N-methyl-2,4-dinitroaniline; nitro positions preserved.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; regiochemistry verified (F-carbon bears new N-methylamino, both NO2 unchanged). Distractors test wrong site, no-reaction, wrong nucleophile/loss. One defensible answer, two-concept, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with Cl2 in the presence of UV light (hv). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1cccc(Cl)c1","Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1","ClCc1ccccc1","ClC(Cl)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1Cl"],"correct":2,"why":"Cl2 with light and no Lewis-acid catalyst proceeds by free-radical halogenation that selectively substitutes a resonance-stabilized benzylic C-H, giving benzyl chloride on monochlorination. Ring chlorination (ortho/para) requires Cl2/FeCl3 (EAS), not hv; with one equivalent the benzal dichloride is not the major product. Keyed index 0 is benzyl chloride.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All valid; distractors are EAS ring products (ortho/meta/para) and dichloride. Tests radical-vs-EAS selectivity; one defensible answer, single concept, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is heated with excess KMnO4, then treated with acid. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"The isopropyl group has one benzylic C-H, so KMnO4 degrades the whole side chain to a single COOH carbon on the ring, giving benzoic acid (the two methyls are lost as CO2/carbonate). Benzylic alcohol, acetophenone, and 2-phenylpropanoic acid wrongly retain side-chain carbons; KMnO4 does not stop at those stages. Keyed index 0 verified as benzoic acid.","difficulty":3,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry correct, keeps. Minor: difficulty is overstated as 3 (this is the same benzylic-oxidation principle as Q1, more like a 2), but it is a valid in-scope single-center question with one defensible answer."},{"q":"The molecule shown (p-nitrotoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4, then acidic workup. Which product results?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["4-nitrobenzyl alcohol","no reaction; the ring resists oxidation","4-nitrobenzoic acid","4-nitrobenzaldehyde","4-aminobenzoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes a methyl bearing benzylic H all the way to COOH; the ring and nitro are untouched, giving 4-nitrobenzoic acid. It does not stop at aldehyde/alcohol nor reduce the nitro.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; in scope and well-formed."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene (shown) is refluxed with excess hot KMnO4 followed by H3O+ workup. What is the organic product derived from the side chain?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts":["2-phenylacetaldehyde","Acetophenone (PhCOCH3)","1-phenylethanol","Benzoic acid (one carbon, COOH on the ring)","Phenylacetic acid (PhCH2COOH)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Benzylic oxidation degrades any alkyl chain with a benzylic C-H down to a single ring COOH; the terminal CH3 is lost as CO2, so ethylbenzene gives benzoic acid, not phenylacetic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; classic benzylic oxidation."},{"q":"tert-Butylbenzene (shown) is heated with KMnO4. Predict the outcome.","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["Oxidized to 2,2-dimethylpropanoic acid","Oxidized to benzoic acid","No oxidation of the side chain (no benzylic hydrogen present)","Oxidized to an aldehyde at the benzylic position","The ring is cleaved to a diacid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Benzylic oxidation needs at least one benzylic C-H. The benzylic carbon of tert-butylbenzene is quaternary, so KMnO4 cannot oxidize it; the side chain is inert. This is the classic non-oxidizable exception.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; standard exception."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene (shown) is treated with Sn (or Fe) and HCl, then made basic. What functional group is produced?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["A nitroso group (-N=O)","A carboxylic acid (-COOH)","An amide (-CONH2)","A hydroxyl group (-OH, phenol)","A primary aromatic amine (-NH2)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Sn/HCl (or Fe/HCl, H2/Pd, SnCl2) reduces an aromatic nitro group to a primary aromatic amine; nitrobenzene gives aniline. Standard nitro-to-amine reduction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; canonical reduction."},{"q":"1-Chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (shown) reacts readily with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) at the carbon bearing Cl. By what mechanism, and why is it fast here?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","opts":["Free-radical substitution","SN2 backside attack on the aromatic carbon","Nucleophilic aromatic substitution (addition-elimination), accelerated by the ortho/para nitro groups stabilizing the Meisenheimer intermediate","SN1 through an aryl cation","Electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"An aryl halide with strong EWGs ortho/para to the leaving group undergoes SNAr by addition-elimination via a Meisenheimer complex; the o-/p-NO2 groups delocalize the anionic charge, speeding the reaction. SN1/SN2 do not occur at sp2 aryl carbon.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles is genuine 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (Cl with ortho+para NO2); in scope."},{"q":"o-Xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene, shown) is treated with a large excess of hot KMnO4, then acidified. What is the product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1C","opts":["Benzoic acid (one carbon lost)","Phthalic acid (benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid)","Phthalaldehyde (both CH3 to CHO)","o-toluic acid (only one CH3 oxidized)","1,2-bis(hydroxymethyl)benzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Excess hot KMnO4 oxidizes every benzylic CH3 (each has C-H) to COOH; two adjacent methyls give the 1,2-diacid, phthalic acid. It does not stop at one acid or at the aldehyde/alcohol stage.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid o-xylene; standard double benzylic oxidation."},{"q":"Consider SNAr with methoxide. Which substrate reacts FASTEST with NaOCH3? (1-fluoro-4-nitrobenzene is shown for reference.)","q_smiles":"Fc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["4-fluorotoluene","1-Fluoro-4-nitrobenzene","1-Chloro-4-nitrobenzene","Fluorobenzene (no nitro group)","1-Iodo-4-nitrobenzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In SNAr the rate-determining step is nucleophilic addition to form the Meisenheimer complex, so the most electron-withdrawing C-X (C-F) reacts fastest among para-nitro aryl halides (opposite of SN1/SN2). Substrates lacking a para-nitro EWG barely react.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; classic SNAr leaving-group trend (F fastest)."},{"q":"You must convert 1-nitro-4-propylbenzene (shown) into 4-aminobenzoic acid. Which sequence of reagents accomplishes BOTH the side-chain and the nitro-group transformations in a workable order?","q_smiles":"CCCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["(1) H2/Pd to reduce NO2; then (2) HNO3/H2SO4","(1) hot KMnO4 only (it also reduces the nitro group)","(1) hot KMnO4, H3O+ to make the COOH; then (2) Sn/HCl, then base to reduce NO2 to NH2","(1) Sn/HCl only (it also oxidizes the propyl chain to COOH)","(1) Sn/HCl to make NH2 first; then (2) hot KMnO4, H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Two changes: propyl (has benzylic H) is oxidized to COOH by hot KMnO4, and nitro is reduced to NH2 by Sn/HCl. Oxidize first, since hot KMnO4 would destroy/oxidize an amine; then reduce the surviving nitro. No single reagent does both.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid. Two-transformation sequence-ordering item but each step is one canonical reaction; sophomore-level ordering logic, within scope (not a 5+ step synthesis)."},{"q":"p-Nitrotoluene is treated with hot KMnO4, then acidic workup. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(N)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","OC(=O)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","OC(=O)c1ccc(N)cc1","OCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes a benzylic CH3 (with at least one benzylic H) all the way to COOH; the methyl becomes carboxylic acid and the para-nitro group is untouched. It does not stop at the benzylic alcohol, does not reduce the nitro group, and does not move the substituent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed p-nitrobenzoic acid is correct; distractors (meta acid, benzyl alcohol, reduced nitro) all wrong. All SMILES valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Chloro-2-nitrobenzene is heated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which structure is the product of this nucleophilic aromatic substitution?","q_smiles":"[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","COc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","COc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Nc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","Clc1ccccc1OC"],"correct":0,"why":"The ortho-NO2 activates the adjacent C-Cl toward addition-elimination SNAr (Meisenheimer anion stabilized by the ortho nitro). Methoxide replaces Cl at the same carbon, so OCH3 ends up ortho to NO2 (o-nitroanisole). The nitro group is retained in its original position.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed o-nitroanisole (OMe ortho to NO2) is correct; para/meta isomers and undisplaced Cl are valid wrong distractors. SMILES valid, single SNAr center, in scope."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene is refluxed with excess KMnO4 followed by acidic workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)O","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)O","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Benzylic oxidation by hot KMnO4 cleaves the alkyl chain back to a single ring-attached COOH regardless of chain length, as long as one benzylic C-H is present. Ethyl becomes -COOH (benzoic acid), not phenylacetic acid, acetophenone, or a diacid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed benzoic acid correct; distractors (chain-retained acid, ketone, diacid, propanoic acid) all wrong. SMILES valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Sn and HCl, then neutralized with base. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","ON=Nc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Sn/HCl (like Fe/HCl or H2/Pd) reduces an aromatic nitro group to a primary aromatic amine. Nitrobenzene gives aniline. It does not give phenol (no C-O), benzylamine (no new CH2), an azo compound, and the nitro is not left unchanged.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed aniline correct; phenol/benzylamine/azo/unchanged-nitro are valid distractors. SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"4-tert-Butyltoluene is treated with hot KMnO4 and acidic workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C(C)(C)C)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(C(C)(C)C)cc1","Cc1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(C(C)(C)O)cc1","OCc1ccc(C(C)(C)C)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Benzylic oxidation requires a benzylic C-H. The methyl has benzylic hydrogens and is oxidized to COOH, but the tert-butyl quaternary benzylic carbon has no benzylic H and survives intact. Only the methyl is converted (4-tert-butylbenzoic acid).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed 4-tert-butylbenzoic acid correct; both-COOH, methyl-survives, and partial-oxidation distractors all wrong. Two-concept (oxidation + benzylic-H selectivity), in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Clc1ccc(NC)cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","CNc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1Cl","Clc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1NC","COc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","CNc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":4,"why":"Two nitro groups ortho and para to the C-Cl strongly activate it for SNAr; methylamine (Sanger reagent chemistry) displaces chloride at C1 while both nitro groups remain. The nucleophile is the amine, not methoxide, and a nitro group is not the leaving group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified substrate is correct 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (matches PubChem); keyed product correctly puts NHMe at C1 with both NO2 retained. Distractors all wrong. SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is to be converted to benzoic acid in one oxidative step. Which reagent/condition accomplishes the side-chain (benzylic) oxidation to -COOH?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["H2 / Pd-C","Hot KMnO4, then H3O+","Br2 / FeBr3","NaBH4 / CH3OH","Cold dilute KMnO4 (Baeyer test conditions)"],"correct":1,"why":"Hot (vigorous) KMnO4 performs benzylic oxidation: any alkyl group bearing a benzylic C-H is degraded to a ring-attached -COOH, so isopropyl becomes -COOH (benzoic acid). H2/Pd reduces, Br2/FeBr3 brominates the ring, NaBH4 reduces carbonyls only, and cold dilute KMnO4 only dihydroxylates alkenes (none present).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed hot KMnO4 is the only reagent giving benzylic oxidation to COOH; other four are valid wrong reagents. Reagent-selection text options with substrate drawn (correct format). SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"m-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene) is treated with a large excess of hot KMnO4, then acidified. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)O","OC(=O)c1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1","Cc1cccc(C(=O)O)c1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1cccc(C(=O)O)c1"],"correct":4,"why":"With excess hot KMnO4 each benzylic methyl is oxidized to -COOH. The 1,3 (meta) relationship is preserved, giving isophthalic acid (benzene-1,3-dicarboxylic acid). The product is not terephthalic (para) or phthalic (ortho) diacid, not a mono-acid, and not benzoic acid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed isophthalic acid (1,3) matches PubChem ref; para/ortho diacid, mono-acid, and benzoic acid are valid distractors differing by regiochemistry. SMILES valid, in scope (recognition depth)."},{"q":"Butanal is treated with ammonia (NH3) and sodium cyanoborohydride (NaBH3CN). What is the major product of this reductive amination?","q_smiles":"CCCC=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)N","CCCC#N","CCCCN","CCCCCN","CCCCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Reductive amination of butanal with NH3 forms an imine that NaBH3CN reduces to the primary amine butylamine (CCCCN), same carbon count. Distractors: alcohol (no over-reduction with cyanoborohydride at the carbonyl), branched 2-aminobutane (wrong regiochemistry), pentylamine (extra carbon), butanenitrile (wrong functional group). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 (CCCCN) correct; all 5 SMILES valid; single reactive center, in DAT scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with dimethylamine ((CH3)2NH) and NaBH3CN. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CN(C)C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CNC1CCCCC1","CN(C)C1=CCCCC1","NC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary amine + ketone gives an iminium ion (enamine in equilibrium) that NaBH3CN reduces to the tertiary amine N,N-dimethylcyclohexylamine. The enamine option is the intermediate, not the isolated product; the alcohol would need plain carbonyl reduction; the N-methyl and primary amines have the wrong N-substitution. Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid (enamine distractor well-formed); in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Benzyl bromide is subjected to a Gabriel synthesis: (1) potassium phthalimide, (2) hydrazine (NH2NH2). What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)N1Cc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","C(c1ccccc1)NCc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Phthalimide N displaces bromide by SN2 at the benzylic carbon; hydrazinolysis liberates the primary amine benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (no SN2 amine), aniline (N on ring), the N-benzyl phthalimide intermediate, and dibenzylamine (Gabriel avoids over-alkylation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid incl. phthalimide intermediate; Gabriel is standard DAT content.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Pentanenitrile (CH3CH2CH2CH2C≡N) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#N","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCNC","CCCCN","CCCCCN","CCCCC(N)=O","CCCCC=O"],"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a nitrile fully to a primary amine, adding two hydrides; the nitrile carbon becomes CH2NH2 with no loss of carbon, giving pentylamine (CCCCCN, 5 carbons). Distractors: butylamine (lost a carbon), pentanal (DIBAL-type partial stop), an N-methyl amine, and the amide (no C-N reduction). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: pentanenitrile = 5 C, product CCCCCN (index 0) correct; all SMILES valid; in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"N,N-Dimethylacetamide (CH3CON(CH3)2) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)N(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCNC","CCN(C)C","CN(C)C","CC(O)N(C)C","CCO"],"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene (C=O becomes CH2) while keeping the C-N bond, giving the tertiary amine N,N-dimethylethylamine (CCN(C)C). Distractors: ethanol (C-N cleavage, like ester reduction), trimethylamine (C-C cleavage), N-methylethylamine (wrong N-substitution), and a carbinolamine (not isolable). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid; amide-vs-ester contrast is sophomore depth, in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with H2 over Pd/C (equivalently Sn/HCl then base). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Nc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","c1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Reduction of an aromatic nitro group gives the aromatic amine aniline (Nc1ccccc1). Distractors: phenol (no O-retention here), nitrosobenzene (partial-reduction intermediate), benzylamine (N off-ring), and azobenzene (controlled basic conditions, not standard hydrogenation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 (aniline) correct; all SMILES valid incl. azobenzene distractor; in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Acetophenone (C6H5COCH3) is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2) and NaBH3CN. What is the major product of this reductive amination?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["NCCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(NC)c1ccccc1","CC(N)c1ccccc1","CNCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The ketone condenses with methylamine to give an iminium at the carbonyl carbon, reduced by NaBH3CN to the secondary amine N-methyl-1-phenylethylamine (CC(NC)c1ccccc1). Distractors: primary amine (ignores the N-methyl partner), benzylic alcohol (plain reduction), N-methylbenzylamine (wrong skeleton), and phenethylamine (N migrated). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct (secondary amine, N on benzylic C); all SMILES valid; layered regio+amine-class but in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Benzamide (C6H5CONH2) is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the C-N bond, converting C(=O)NH2 into CH2NH2 to give benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (C-N cleavage), aniline (wrong skeleton), benzaldehyde (incomplete), and toluene (full deoxygenation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid; difficulty 3 slightly generous but C-N-retention concept with multiple plausible distractors justifies it; in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"The secondary amine shown is the target product. Which combination of starting materials and reagent best prepares it through reductive amination?","q_smiles":"CCNC1CCCCC1","opts":["Cyclohexanol + ethylamine, then NaBH4","Cyclohexanone + ethylamine, then NaOH","Cyclohexanone + ethylamine, then LiAlH4 followed by H3O+","Cyclohexanone + ethylamine, then NaBH3CN (cat. acid)","Bromocyclohexane + ethylamine, then NaBH3CN"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Reductive amination condenses cyclohexanone with ethylamine to an iminium, and NaBH3CN at mild acid selectively reduces it to N-ethylcyclohexanamine without reducing the ketone. Alcohol cannot form the imine, NaOH does no reduction, and the alkyl-halide route is SN2 alkylation, not reductive amination.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept reductive amination, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl bromide is converted to benzylamine. Which reagent sequence accomplishes this transformation cleanly without over-alkylation to secondary/tertiary amines?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts":["NaNH2, then H2O","KCN, then LiAlH4","Potassium phthalimide; then hydrazine (or aqueous NaOH/H+) workup","NaN3, then no further treatment","Excess NH3, then heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Gabriel synthesis: the phthalimide anion (one nucleophilic N) does SN2 on the primary benzyl halide, and hydrazinolysis releases the pure primary amine, avoiding the over-alkylation of direct ammonia. KCN/LiAlH4 adds a carbon (phenethylamine) and NaN3 alone stops at the azide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; standard Gabriel, in scope."},{"q":"The nitrile shown is to be converted to butan-1-amine (a primary amine with the same number of carbons). Which reagent achieves this reduction?","q_smiles":"CCCC#N","opts":["LiAlH4, then H3O+","H2 with a poisoned Lindlar catalyst","NaBH4 in methanol","DIBAL-H (1 equiv) at low temperature, then H3O+","Hot aqueous H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 fully reduces R-C#N to R-CH2-NH2 with no change in carbon count, giving butan-1-amine. NaBH4 is too weak, Lindlar reduces alkynes, hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes to the carboxylic acid, and DIBAL-H (1 equiv) stops at the imine/aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept nitrile reduction, in scope."},{"q":"Aniline is to be made from the aromatic compound shown. Which reagent set reduces the nitro group to a primary amine?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["NaBH4 in ethanol","LiAlH4 only (no other change possible)","O3, then Zn/H2O","KMnO4, hot","Sn (or Fe) with HCl, then NaOH workup"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Aromatic nitro groups reduce to anilines under dissolving-metal conditions (Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl, then base). NaBH4 does not reduce nitroarenes, KMnO4 and O3 are oxidative/cleaving, and LiAlH4 on nitroarenes is unreliable (azo stage), so the metal/acid route is the standard answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept nitro reduction, in scope."},{"q":"The amide shown is to be reduced. What is the product, and which reagent is required?","q_smiles":"CCC(N)=O","opts":["Propan-1-amine, using NaBH4 in water","Propan-1-ol, using LiAlH4","Propanal, using NaBH4","Propanoic acid, using LiAlH4","Propan-1-amine (CH3CH2CH2NH2), using LiAlH4 then H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide C(=O)NH2 to CH2-NH2, removing the carbonyl oxygen and retaining nitrogen, so propanamide gives propan-1-amine. NaBH4 does not reduce amides, and LiAlH4 does not stop at the acid, alcohol, or aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept amide reduction, in scope."},{"q":"The intermediate shown forms when benzaldehyde reacts with methylamine. What kind of species is it, and what reagent converts it to N-methyl-1-phenylmethanamine (PhCH2NHCH3)?","q_smiles":"CN=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["An imine (Schiff base); reduce with NaBH4 (or NaBH3CN)","An amide; reduce with LiAlH4","A hemiaminal; oxidize with PCC","A nitrile; reduce with LiAlH4","An enamine; hydrolyze with H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aldehyde plus primary amine gives an imine (C=N, Schiff base); reducing the C=N with NaBH4/NaBH3CN gives the secondary amine PhCH2NHCH3. It has no N-C=C (not an enamine), the N is doubly bonded (not hemiaminal/amide), and it is not a nitrile.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); SMILES CN=Cc1ccccc1 is the correct imine; classification + reduction (two layered concepts), in scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone (shown) is treated with dimethylamine and NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions. What is the product, and why is dimethylamine able to undergo reductive amination here?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["No reaction; secondary amines cannot do reductive amination","1-phenylethanol; NaBH3CN reduces the ketone directly","A primary amine PhCH(CH3)NH2; one N-CH3 is lost during reduction","N,N-dimethyl-1-phenylethanamine; the secondary amine forms an iminium ion that NaBH3CN reduces","An amide; the amine attacks and water is lost"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A secondary amine plus a ketone forms an iminium ion (no neutral imine possible), and NaBH3CN reduces it to the tertiary amine N,N-dimethyl-1-phenylethanamine. NaBH3CN does not appreciably reduce the free ketone under mild acid, no amide forms, and no N-CH3 is lost.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; iminium-from-secondary-amine concept, two layered ideas, in scope."},{"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis using potassium phthalimide (shown) followed by hydrazinolysis, which alkyl halide will give the highest yield of the desired primary amine?","q_smiles":"O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)[N-]1.[K+]","opts":["bromobenzene (an aryl halide)","1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","2-chloro-2-methylbutane (a tertiary halide)","vinyl bromide (a vinyl halide)","2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The phthalimide anion alkylates via SN2, so it needs an unhindered substrate; 1-bromobutane (primary) gives the best yield. Tertiary halides undergo E2 elimination, and aryl/vinyl halides are unreactive toward SN2.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); potassium phthalimide SMILES valid (parses to phthalimide anion + K+); SN2 substrate selection, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Phenylacetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess dimethylamine and NaBH3CN (reductive amination). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=CCc1ccccc1","opts":["CN(C)CCc1ccccc1","CN(C)C(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CNCCc1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Reductive amination of PhCH2CHO with the secondary amine Me2NH forms an iminium that NaBH3CN reduces to the tertiary amine PhCH2CH2-NMe2, keeping all carbons. Distractors: mono-methyl secondary amine (wrong amine), dropped CH2 (wrong skeleton), over-reduction to alcohol, and an amide (not formed under these conditions). RDKit confirms keyed opt0 = PhCH2CH2NMe2 and all options parse.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, single concept, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (drawn) is reacted with NH3 and NaBH3CN. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["N=C1CCCCC1","CNC1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","NC1CCCCC1","NCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Reductive amination with ammonia gives the primary amine cyclohexylamine; the carbonyl carbon becomes a CH bearing -NH2. Distractors: alcohol (carbonyl reduction without amine), unreduced imine (NaBH3CN reduces it), N-methyl (not present in NH3), and an extra-carbon homolog (impossible). RDKit confirms keyed opt0 = cyclohexylamine and all parse.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, single concept, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis, potassium phthalimide is alkylated by benzyl bromide (drawn), and the resulting N-substituted phthalimide is then cleaved (hydrazine or aqueous base/acid). Which structure is the isolated amine product?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts":["Nc1ccccc1","c1ccc(CNCc2ccccc2)cc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","CNCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Gabriel delivers a pure PRIMARY amine: phthalimide N displaces bromide via SN2, then cleavage frees benzylamine PhCH2NH2; it cannot make secondary/tertiary or arylamines. Distractors: aniline (N on ring), a secondary amine and a dibenzyl secondary amine (both impossible by Gabriel), and the alcohol. The Gabriel sequence is one recognized named reaction, not a 5-step synthesis, so scope is fine. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 = benzylamine and all parse.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; Gabriel is a single named reaction within DAT scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"4-Methylbenzonitrile (drawn) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C#N)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(CO)cc1","Cc1ccc(CN)cc1","Cc1ccc(CCN)cc1","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","Cc1ccc(C=O)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a nitrile fully to a primary amine, the nitrile carbon becoming a CH2 bonded to NH2 (Ar-CH2-NH2): 4-methylbenzylamine. Distractors: N directly on ring (loss of a carbon), the alcohol, the aldehyde (partial reduction not given by LiAlH4), and an extra-CH2 homolog. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 = 4-methylbenzylamine and all parse.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, single concept, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"3-Nitrotoluene (drawn) is treated with H2/Pd-C (or Sn/HCl). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","opts":["Cc1cccc(CN)c1","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","Cc1ccccc1N","Cc1cccc(N)c1","Cc1cccc(C=O)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Reduction of an aromatic -NO2 gives -NH2 at the same ring position: m-toluidine. Regiochemistry must be preserved (meta). Distractors: para and ortho isomers (wrong positions), a benzylic amine (wrong inserted CH2), and an aldehyde. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 is the meta-substituted aminotoluene and the ortho/para distractors are distinct constitutional isomers.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, regiochem (meta) preserved, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"N-Methylbenzamide (drawn) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C(NC)c1ccccc1","opts":["CNCc1ccccc1","CN(C)c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide carbonyl to CH2 while RETAINING the C-N bond, so PhC(=O)NHMe gives the secondary amine PhCH2NHMe (N-methylbenzylamine). Distractors: benzylamine and aniline (both wrongly cleave/relocate N), the alcohol (loss of N), and N,N-dimethylaniline (wrong connectivity). RDKit confirms keyed opt0 = N-methylbenzylamine and all parse.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; two layered points (amide reduction + C-N retention) within DAT scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Butan-2-one (drawn) undergoes reductive amination with benzylamine and NaBH3CN. The product N-(butan-2-yl)benzylamine has a new stereocenter at the former carbonyl carbon. Which statement best describes the stereochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)=O","opts":["A single meso compound forms","A racemic mixture (equal R and S) is formed, because hydride adds to a planar iminium ion from either face with equal probability","No stereocenter is generated, so the product is achiral","Only the (S)-enantiomer forms, due to retention at the carbonyl carbon","Only the (R)-enantiomer forms, because hydride must add anti to the larger ethyl group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The prochiral carbonyl carbon goes through a planar sp2 iminium; with an achiral reductant and no chiral bias, hydride adds equally to both faces, giving a racemate. The product is chiral (four different groups on that C), so the achiral option is wrong; nothing biases one face; and only one stereocenter precludes a meso form. Recognition-level stereochemistry, not Felkin-Anh, so in scope. Substrate SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; conceptual stereochem at recognition depth, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"5-Aminopentanal (drawn) is treated with NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions, where the molecule undergoes INTRAMOLECULAR reductive amination. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCN","opts":["NCCCCCO","C1CCCNCC1","C1CCNC1","CCCCC=N","C1CCNCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The -NH2 and aldehyde condense to a cyclic iminium reduced to a saturated cyclic amine. Counting N + four CH2 + the former carbonyl carbon gives a six-membered ring: piperidine (C5H11N, RDKit-confirmed ring size 6). Distractors: pyrrolidine (5-membered) and azepane (7-membered) wrong ring sizes, the acyclic amino alcohol, and an acyclic imine that also lost a carbon. RDKit confirms keyed opt0 = piperidine.","difficulty":3,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; ring-size + intramolecular reductive amination at recognition depth, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Aniline is treated with acetic anhydride [(CH3CO)2O]. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["CCNc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1N","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Nc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetic anhydride N-acylates aniline's nucleophilic nitrogen to give acetanilide. Keyed SMILES CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1 is the amide and matches. Distractors (O-acyl phenyl acetate, N-ethyl reduction, ring C-acylation, carbamic acid) are all wrong, leaving exactly one defensible answer. SMILES valid, recognition-level, visual.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 verified, all SMILES parse."},{"q":"Cyclohexylamine reacts with benzoyl chloride (C6H5COCl) in the presence of base. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"NC1CCCCC1","opts":["O=C(c1ccccc1)NC1CCCCC1","ClC1CCCCC1","O=C(c1ccccc1)OC1CCCCC1","O=C(Cl)NC1CCCCC1","c1ccc(CNC2CCCCC2)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"An acid chloride acylates the primary amine to a secondary amide (N-cyclohexylbenzamide); keyed SMILES O=C(c1ccccc1)NC1CCCCC1 matches. Retained-Cl carbamoyl chloride, ester from an amine, alkyl chloride, and a reductive-amination amine are all incorrect. Exactly one right answer, valid SMILES, in scope, visual.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 verified."},{"q":"Butan-2-amine is exhaustively methylated with excess CH3I, then treated with Ag2O and heated (Hofmann elimination). What is the major alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)N","opts":["C=CCC","CCC(C)O","CCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C","CC=CC","CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Hofmann elimination of the bulky quaternary ammonium hydroxide gives the less-substituted (anti-Zaitsev) alkene by abstracting the most accessible terminal beta-H, yielding 1-butene (C=CCC). 2-butene (Zaitsev), the alcohol (substitution), the ammonium intermediate, and butane are wrong. One defensible answer, valid SMILES, single named reaction, in scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (1-butene) verified."},{"q":"Pentan-2-amine undergoes exhaustive methylation (excess CH3I) followed by Ag2O / heat. Predict the major (Hofmann) alkene.","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)N","opts":["CCCC(C)O","CCCCC","C=CCCC","C/C=C/CC","CC=CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Hofmann elimination favors the least-substituted alkene via abstraction of the terminal beta-H, giving 1-pentene (C=CCCC). Internal 2-pentene (Zaitsev) and its explicit trans isomer, the substitution alcohol, and pentane are wrong. Exactly one right answer; the cis/trans-2-pentene distractors are well-chosen. SMILES valid, in scope, visual.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (1-pentene) verified."},{"q":"Aniline is treated with NaNO2 / HCl at 0-5 C to form a diazonium salt, which is then treated with CuBr (Sandmeyer reaction). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Clc1ccccc1","Brc1ccccc1","BrCc1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Sandmeyer with CuBr replaces -N2+ by Br at the same ring carbon with loss of N2, giving bromobenzene (Brc1ccccc1). Chlorobenzene (wrong copper halide), phenol (aqueous hydrolysis path), benzyl bromide (wrong connectivity), and p-bromoaniline (retains N) are wrong. One defensible answer, valid SMILES, single named reaction, in scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 verified."},{"q":"p-Toluidine (4-methylaniline) is converted to its diazonium salt with NaNO2 / HCl (0-5 C), then warmed in H2O. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(N)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccc(N=O)cc1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","Oc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccccc1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Warm aqueous hydrolysis of an aryl diazonium salt replaces -N2+ by -OH at the same position, giving p-cresol (Cc1ccc(O)cc1) with methyl still para. The ortho phenol (position migration), aryl bromide (no Cu halide), nitroso compound, and hydroquinone are wrong. Two standard steps (diazotization + hydrolysis), recognition depth, in scope; one defensible answer; valid SMILES.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (p-cresol) verified."},{"q":"Consider the three nitrogen compounds: aniline (C6H5NH2), cyclohexylamine (an aliphatic primary amine), and acetanilide (C6H5NHCOCH3). Rank them from MOST to LEAST basic at nitrogen. The molecule drawn is aniline for reference.","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["acetanilide > aniline > cyclohexylamine","aniline > acetanilide > cyclohexylamine","cyclohexylamine > aniline > acetanilide","aniline > cyclohexylamine > acetanilide","cyclohexylamine > acetanilide > aniline"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Basicity follows lone-pair availability: localized alkyl-donated lone pair (cyclohexylamine) is most basic; ring delocalization lowers aniline; amide conjugation into the carbonyl makes acetanilide least basic. Correct order cyclohexylamine > aniline > acetanilide is option 0. Ranking item correctly uses text options with the substrate drawn; qualitative, in scope, one answer.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; ranking correct, opts_are_structures=false appropriate, q_smiles valid."},{"q":"A benzenediazonium salt (C6H5N2+, from aniline + NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C) is added to a cold basic solution of phenol (drawn). Predict the major azo-coupling product.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1O","Oc1ccc(Nc2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","O=C1C=CC(=Nc2ccccc2)C=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Diazonium salts are weak electrophiles that couple at the para position of the strongly activated phenol via EAS, giving 4-(phenylazo)phenol (Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1). The ortho isomer, the reduced N-H (diarylamine) bridge, an extra-OH catechol, and a quinone-imine are wrong. Distractors are distinct (verified), SMILES valid, recognition-level single EAS event, in scope; one defensible answer.","difficulty":3,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (para-azo) verified, all five options distinct and valid."},{"q":"Aniline (drawn) is treated with a reagent to convert the -NH2 group into an amide (acetanilide, CH3C(=O)NH-C6H5). Which reagent accomplishes this acylation most cleanly?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Methanol, CH3OH / H+","Methyl iodide, CH3I","NaBH4 then H2O","Acetic anhydride, (CH3CO)2O","Acetic acid, CH3COOH (no activator)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Acylation of a primary amine to an amide requires an activated carboxylic acid derivative. Acetic anhydride (or acetyl chloride) readily acylates the nucleophilic nitrogen to give acetanilide. A free carboxylic acid alone just forms an ammonium carboxylate salt; CH3OH/H+ makes esters, CH3I alkylates (not acylates), and NaBH4 is a reductant.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Aniline SMILES valid; acetic anhydride is the clean acylating agent for acetanilide; distractors all map to other amine reactions. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Rank the following nitrogen compounds from MOST basic to LEAST basic: ethylamine (CH3CH2NH2), ammonia (NH3), and aniline (drawn, C6H5NH2).","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["ethylamine > ammonia > aniline","aniline > ammonia > ethylamine","aniline > ethylamine > ammonia","ammonia > ethylamine > aniline","ethylamine > aniline > ammonia"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Alkyl groups donate electron density (inductive), making the lone pair more available, so ethylamine is the most basic. Ammonia is intermediate. In aniline the nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the benzene ring, greatly lowering availability and basicity, so aniline is the weakest base. Order: ethylamine > ammonia > aniline.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Standard textbook basicity order (alkylamine > NH3 > arylamine). Aniline drawn in stem. One defensible answer; in scope, no pKa math required."},{"q":"sec-Butyltrimethylammonium hydroxide (drawn) is heated, undergoing Hofmann elimination. Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C.[OH-]","opts":["C#CCC","CCCC","C/C=C\\C","C/C=C/C","C=CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hofmann elimination of a quaternary ammonium hydroxide follows the anti-Zaitsev (Hofmann) rule because of the bulky leaving group and acidic terminal protons: the LESS substituted alkene dominates. Removing a proton from the terminal CH3 gives 1-butene (C=CCC), not the more-substituted 2-butene.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Substrate is CH3CH2-CH(NMe3+)-CH3; Hofmann favors less-substituted terminal alkene = 1-butene (C=CCC, opt0). Distractors are 2-butene E/Z, the alkane, and an alkyne. All SMILES valid; visual structure options. One defensible answer."},{"q":"N-Ethylbenzamide (drawn) is the product of combining ethylamine with one reagent. What TYPE of reaction formed the C-N bond shown, and what is the best reagent partner?","q_smiles":"CCNC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Nucleophilic acyl substitution; benzoyl chloride (C6H5COCl)","Electrophilic aromatic substitution; benzene + Cl2","Reductive amination; benzaldehyde + NaBH4","Diazonium coupling; benzenediazonium chloride","SN2 alkylation; bromoethane (C2H5Br)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The amide C-N bond comes from an amine attacking an activated acyl carbon (nucleophilic acyl substitution). Benzoyl chloride + ethylamine gives N-ethylbenzamide. SN2 alkylation would give an amine, not an amide; reductive amination gives an amine; diazonium coupling forms azo (N=N) bonds, not amides.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Amide product drawn; nucleophilic acyl substitution with benzoyl chloride is the only route that gives an amide. Distractors give amines or azo products. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Aniline (drawn) is to be converted into a benzenediazonium salt for later substitution. Which reagent/condition set generates the diazonium ion?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Br2, FeBr3","(CH3CO)2O, pyridine","NaNO2, HCl, 0-5 degrees C","NaNO2, HCl, reflux at 80 degrees C","LiAlH4 then H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Diazotization of a primary aromatic amine uses nitrous acid generated in situ from NaNO2 + HCl, run COLD (0-5 degrees C) to keep the unstable aryldiazonium salt from decomposing. Warming (reflux) destroys it by hydrolysis to phenol. Acetic anhydride acylates, LiAlH4 reduces, Br2/FeBr3 brominates the ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Classic diazotization (NaNO2/HCl, 0-5 C). Cold temperature is the discriminator vs the reflux distractor. Aniline drawn. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride (drawn) is a versatile intermediate. Which reagent converts it into benzonitrile (C6H5-C#N)?","q_smiles":"[N+](#N)c1ccccc1","opts":["KI","H2O, heat","CuBr","CuCN","H3PO2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"In the Sandmeyer reaction, the diazonium group is displaced by the nucleophile delivered with copper(I). CuCN installs a nitrile (benzonitrile). CuBr gives bromobenzene, H2O/heat gives phenol, KI gives iodobenzene, and H3PO2 reduces the diazonium to benzene (loses N2 entirely).","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Sandmeyer with CuCN gives benzonitrile. Diazonium SMILES valid (canonicalizes to N#[N+]c1ccccc1). Each distractor is a real diazonium transformation giving a different product. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexylamine (drawn) is compared with aniline (C6H5NH2) for basicity. Which statement correctly identifies the stronger base and the reason?","q_smiles":"NC1CCCCC1","opts":["Aniline is more basic; the ring donates electron density to nitrogen","Aniline is more basic; aromatic resonance stabilizes the protonated form","Cyclohexylamine is more basic; its N lone pair is localized and not delocalized into any ring","They are equally basic; both nitrogens are sp3","Cyclohexylamine is less basic; the ring withdraws electrons inductively"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Cyclohexylamine is an aliphatic amine: its nitrogen lone pair stays localized and freely available to accept a proton. In aniline the lone pair conjugates into the aromatic ring, lowering availability and basicity. So cyclohexylamine is the stronger base because its lone pair is not delocalized.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyclohexylamine (aliphatic, localized lone pair) is more basic than aniline (lone pair delocalized into ring). Cyclohexylamine drawn. One defensible answer; reasoning is qualitative, in scope."},{"q":"Pentan-2-yltrimethylammonium hydroxide (drawn) is heated to give Hofmann elimination. Considering both regiochemistry (Hofmann orientation) and the favored geometry, which is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C.[OH-]","opts":["CC=C(C)C","C=CCCC","CC/C=C/C","CCCCC","CC/C=C\\C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hofmann elimination favors the LESS substituted (terminal) alkene because of the bulky NMe3+ leaving group and the more accessible/acidic terminal protons. Deprotonating the C1 methyl gives 1-pentene (C=CCCC), the major product, rather than the more substituted internal 2-pentene that Zaitsev would predict.","difficulty":3,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct keyed answer. Substrate CH3-CH(NMe3+)-CH2CH2CH3; Hofmann gives terminal 1-pentene (C=CCCC, opt0). All SMILES valid; distractors are 2-pentene E/Z, the alkane, and a wrong branched skeleton. Minor stem wording: '...favored geometry' is window-dressing since the major product (1-pentene) has no E/Z geometry, but it does not introduce ambiguity. One defensible answer."},{"q":"The amine shown (benzylamine) is treated with one equivalent of acetic anhydride, (CH3CO)2O. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"NCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)O","CCC(=O)NCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)NCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)N(Cc1ccccc1)C(C)=O","CC(=O)OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The nucleophilic primary amine N attacks the anhydride carbonyl; acetate leaves, giving N-benzylacetamide (N-acylation, mono-acylation with 1 eq, acetyl not propanoyl). Index 0 confirmed correct; all 5 SMILES valid; distractors (O-acyl, N,N-diacyl, propanamide, AcOH byproduct) plausible-but-wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; classic amine + anhydride acylation, in scope."},{"q":"sec-Butylamine is exhaustively methylated (excess CH3I), then treated with Ag2O and heated (Hofmann elimination). Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C/C","C=CCC","CC(C)=C","CCCC","C/C=C\\C"],"correct":1,"why":"Hofmann elimination of the bulky quaternary ammonium removes the least-hindered beta-H, giving the anti-Zaitsev (less-substituted) 1-butene. q_smiles is the correct quaternary ammonium of sec-butylamine. Index 0 confirmed. trans/cis-2-butene are Zaitsev distractors; 2-methylpropene is wrong skeleton; butane is a reduction. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, 1-butene). Hofmann regiochemistry standard DAT content; SMILES all valid."},{"q":"Rank the following four nitrogen compounds from MOST basic to LEAST basic (consider the basicity of the lone pair drawn): ethylamine, aniline, p-nitroaniline, and acetamide.","q_smiles":"CCN.Nc1ccccc1.Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1.CC(N)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["ethylamine > aniline > p-nitroaniline > acetamide","ethylamine > p-nitroaniline > aniline > acetamide","acetamide > ethylamine > aniline > p-nitroaniline","aniline > ethylamine > acetamide > p-nitroaniline","p-nitroaniline > aniline > ethylamine > acetamide"],"correct":0,"why":"Localized sp3 alkylamine (ethylamine) is most basic; aniline's lone pair delocalizes into the ring; p-nitro further withdraws/delocalizes making it weaker than aniline; amide N (acetamide) is least basic (lone pair in resonance with C=O). Order ethylamine > aniline > p-nitroaniline > acetamide is the textbook ranking. Index 0 confirmed; only one defensible order.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0). Resonance/induction basicity ranking is sophomore-level; q_smiles draws all four species and is valid."},{"q":"p-Toluidine (4-methylaniline) is treated with NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C to form the diazonium salt, then warmed in aqueous acid (H2O, heat). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(N)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1","Cc1ccc(O)cc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Aryldiazonium warmed in water hydrolyzes C-N2+ to C-OH at the same position, giving p-cresol (4-methylphenol). Index 0 confirmed. Aryl chloride needs CuCl (Sandmeyer); H-replacement needs H3PO2; nitro is not formed; benzoic acid is wrong skeleton. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, p-cresol). Standard diazonium hydrolysis; SMILES valid, properly visual."},{"q":"Aniline is treated with acetyl chloride (CH3COCl) in the presence of pyridine. Which structure is the major product, and what role does pyridine play?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","Clc1ccccc1","CC(=O)N(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Amine N attacks the acid chloride to give acetanilide; pyridine is a non-nucleophilic base scavenging HCl (not incorporated). Index 0 confirmed. O-acyl, N,N-diacyl, ring-chlorination, and Friedel-Crafts ketone are not formed. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, acetanilide). Classic amine acylation with base role; SMILES valid."},{"q":"2-Aminopentane is converted to its quaternary ammonium hydroxide (excess CH3I, then Ag2O/H2O) and heated. Identify the major alkene formed in this Hofmann elimination.","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC/C=C\\C","CCCCC","CC/C=C/C","C=CCCC","C=C(C)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"Hofmann removes the least-hindered beta-H, giving the terminal 1-pentene. q_smiles is the correct quaternary ammonium of 2-aminopentane (N on C2 of a pentyl chain). Index 0 confirmed. trans/cis-2-pentene are Zaitsev minors; 2-methyl-1-butene is wrong skeleton; pentane is not an elimination product. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, 1-pentene). Hofmann regiochemistry, single concept layered with stereo distractors; SMILES valid."},{"q":"An organic chemist wants to convert cyclohexanone plus methylamine (CH3NH2) into N-methylcyclohexanamine (a secondary amine, the C=O carbon now bearing N-H and CH3). Which single reagent set accomplishes this in one operation?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["CH3NH2 then LiAlH4 reduction of the amide","NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C (diazotization)","CH3COCl then pyridine (acylation)","CH3NH2 with NaBH3CN (reductive amination)","CH3NH2 with NaBH4 added before the amine, then acid workup"],"correct":3,"why":"Reductive amination: ketone + amine condense to iminium, selectively reduced by mild NaBH3CN to the secondary amine. Index 0 confirmed. Acylation gives an amide; diazotization needs a pre-existing 1 amine; LiAlH4/amide is the wrong substrate path; adding NaBH4 before imine forms reduces the ketone to alcohol. q_smiles (cyclohexanone) valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, reductive amination). Reagent-selection text item with valid drawn substrate; in scope."},{"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride (from aniline + NaNO2/HCl, 0-5 C) is added to a basic solution of phenol (azo coupling). Considering BOTH the position of attack on phenol AND the geometry of the azo (N=N) linkage, which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N\\c2ccccc2)cc1","Nc1ccc(O)cc1","Oc1cccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"Weak diazonium electrophile attacks the activated, less-hindered para position of phenol, and azo dyes adopt the more stable trans (E) N=N geometry: 4-(phenyldiazenyl)phenol, trans. Index 0 confirmed. ortho (steric), meta (not activated), cis-azo, and aminophenol (reduction) are wrong. RDKit confirms ortho/meta/para SMILES are distinct and correct; all valid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, para + trans). Two layered concepts (regio + stereo) within limit; azo coupling is standard sophomore Orgo II; SMILES valid."},{"q":"The diene shown (1,3-butadiene) is heated with maleic anhydride. Which structure is the major Diels-Alder product?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C.O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["C=CC=CC1CC(=O)OC1=O","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CC12","O=C1OC(=O)[C@@H]2CC=CC[C@@H]12","O=C1OC(=O)[C@@H]2CC=CC[C@H]12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Butadiene plus maleic anhydride gives cis-1,2,3,6-tetrahydrophthalic anhydride, a MESO cis-fused bicycle (the two anhydride C-H bonds stay cis, suprafacial-suprafacial). RDKit 3D analysis: opt1 has a H-C-C-H ring-junction dihedral of ~34 deg (CIS) and is meso/achiral = the real product. The keyed opt0 has a ~173 deg dihedral (TRANS) and is chiral, which is impossible from a cis dienophile. opt2 is the wrong (5-membered) ring, opt3 still has a diene, opt4 is aromatic phthalic anhydride.","difficulty":1,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"KEY INVERTED: keyed index 0 is the chiral TRANS-fused isomer (impossible from cis maleic anhydride); correct answer is index 1, the meso CIS adduct. Verified by 3D dihedral (34 vs 173 deg) and meso test across 5 conformers. Explanation already describes 'cis' correctly, so only the keyed index/SMILES choice is wrong. Corrected to 1."},{"q":"Isoprene (2-methyl-1,3-butadiene) reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) under thermal Diels-Alder conditions. Predict the major product (apply the 'para' regiochemistry rule).","q_smiles":"C=CC(=C)C.C=CC=O","opts":["C=CCC(=C)CC=O","Cc1ccc(C=O)cc1","CC1=CCC(C=O)CC1","CC1=CCCC(C=O)C1","CC1CCC(C=O)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A 2-substituted diene with a mono-substituted dienophile follows the 'para' rule: the ring methyl and the CHO end up 1,4 (para) on the cyclohexene. opt0 is the para product. opt1 is the 1,3 ('meta') isomer (wrong regiochemistry), opt2 is the wrong (cyclopentane) ring size, opt3 never cyclized, opt4 is fully aromatic.","difficulty":2,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; para regiochemistry sound; distractors are genuine meta/wrong-ring/open-chain/aromatic alternatives."},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene is mixed with maleic anhydride at room temperature. Which structure is the kinetically favored major product (endo rule)?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC=C1.O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1OC(=O)[C@@H]2[C@@H]1[C@H]1CC2C=C1","O=C(O)C1C=CC(C(=O)O)CC1","O=C1OC(=O)[C@H]2[C@@H]1[C@H]1CC2C=C1","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Cyclopentadiene + maleic anhydride gives a norbornene-fused anhydride; the endo rule (secondary orbital overlap of the carbonyls under the diene) makes the endo adduct the kinetic major product. opt1 is the exo diastereomer (distinct, minor), opt2 lacks the methylene bridge (wrong skeleton), opt3 is aromatic phthalic anhydride, opt4 is the open diacid lacking both bridge and anhydride.","difficulty":2,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Acceptable as keyed. opt0 and opt1 are confirmed distinct diastereomers; cis ring-fusion preserved; endo logic is standard. One bridgehead stereocenter parses as unassigned but it is ring-constrained, so no second valid answer is created. Endo-vs-exo is at the upper edge of DAT depth but is a recognized recognition-level concept."},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene reacts with acrylonitrile (prop-2-enenitrile) in a Diels-Alder reaction. Which structure is the major (endo) product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC=C1.C=CC#N","opts":["N#Cc1ccccc1","N#C[C@@H]1C[C@@H]2C=C[C@H]1C2","C=CC=CCCC#N","N#CC1CC=CCC1","N#C[C@H]1C[C@@H]2C=C[C@H]1C2"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Cyclopentadiene gives a bicyclo[2.2.1] (norbornene) ring; the nitrile is electron-withdrawing, so the endo adduct is favored kinetically. opt1 is the exo diastereomer (distinct, minor), opt2 is monocyclic with no methylene bridge (wrong skeleton), opt3 never reacted, opt4 is benzonitrile (aromatic).","difficulty":1,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. opt0/opt1 are confirmed distinct diastereomers (differ at one stereocenter); formula C8H9N matches the cycloadduct; distractors valid."},{"q":"1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) thermally. Predict the major product (apply the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule).","q_smiles":"COC=CC=C.C=CC=O","opts":["COC=CC=CCCC=O","COC1C=CC1C=O","COC1C=CCCC1C=O","COC1C=CC(C=O)CC1","COC1C=CCC(C=O)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A 1-substituted (terminal) diene with a mono-substituted dienophile follows the 'ortho' rule: the methoxy and the CHO end up on adjacent (1,2) carbons of the cyclohexene, with the new ring double bond between the former diene C2-C3. opt0 is the 1,2 product. opt1 is the 1,3 ('meta') and opt2 the 1,4 ('para') isomer (wrong regiochemistry), opt3 is a 4-membered ring (wrong skeleton), opt4 never cyclized.","difficulty":3,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed; ortho/para regiochemistry rules are standard DAT content, in scope. Difficulty 3 is slightly generous (ortho rule is a single-concept recognition item, ~2), but acceptable."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with one equivalent of p-benzoquinone in a Diels-Alder reaction. Which structure is the major mono-adduct?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C.O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1.C=CCC=C","O=C1CCC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1C=CC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","OC1=CC=C(O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1C=CC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"p-Benzoquinone is a strong dienophile: butadiene adds across ONE of its C=C bonds, leaving the other enedione double bond intact and forming a new fused cyclohexene ring (mono-adduct, formula C10H10O2). opt1 is over-reduced (the retained enedione C=C is gone), opt2 shows no reaction, opt3 is the hydroquinone (wrong tautomer/aromatization), opt4 is a wrong (naphthoquinone) skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. opt0 formula C10H10O2 = quinone + butadiene mono-adduct, retains one enedione C=C; distractors are valid over-reduced/no-reaction/tautomer/wrong-skeleton alternatives."},{"q":"Furan acts as the diene with maleic anhydride in a Diels-Alder reaction. Which structure is the cycloadduct?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1.O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1C=CC(=O)O1","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(C3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(O3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Furan reacts as a diene and its oxygen becomes a one-atom oxygen bridge, giving the 7-oxabicyclo[2.2.1]heptene fused to the anhydride (formula C8H6O4). opt1 has an all-carbon bridge (wrong bridging atom), opt2 is the no-bridge cyclohexene (wrong skeleton), opt3 is aromatic phthalic anhydride, opt4 is just unreacted maleic anhydride.","difficulty":3,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. opt0 formula C8H6O4 matches the oxabicyclic adduct with the correct oxygen bridge; distractors valid. In scope as a structure-recognition item."},{"q":"The cyclohexene product shown below is formed in a single step from two simpler unsaturated starting materials. Which combination of reactants, heated together, gives this product through a Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["1,3-Butadiene + ethane","1,3-Butadiene + ethylene","Two equivalents of ethylene","1-Butene + ethylene","Cyclohexane + ethylene"],"correct":1,"why":"Retro-Diels-Alder of cyclohexene (C6H10) cleanly gives 1,3-butadiene (4-pi diene) + ethylene (2-pi dienophile); the residual C2-C3 double bond is the diene's central bond. RDKit confirms cyclohexene C6H10 and butadiene+ethylene atom balance. Ethane, 1-butene, cyclohexane all lack the correct pi system. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - classic retro-DA, q_smiles valid (C6H10 cyclohexene), index 0 correct."},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn below) is a highly reactive diene. To run a fast Diels-Alder reaction at or near room temperature, you want to pair it with the most electron-poor dienophile available. Which reagent is the best dienophile partner?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohexane","Maleic anhydride","1-Hexene","Diethyl ether","Ethane"],"correct":1,"why":"Only maleic anhydride bears an activated C=C flanked by two carbonyls (electron-poor dienophile), the textbook fast partner. Cyclohexane, ethane, diethyl ether have no reactive C=C; 1-hexene is unactivated and sluggish. q_smiles canonicalizes to 1,3-cyclopentadiene (C5H6). Exactly one best answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - cyclopentadiene SMILES verified as C5H6 1,3-isomer; index 0 correct."},{"q":"2,3-Dimethyl-1,3-butadiene (shown) reacts cleanly with maleic anhydride on warming. What is the correct classification of this transformation?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C(=C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A free-radical chain halogenation","An E1 elimination","A [4+2] cycloaddition (Diels-Alder reaction)","An electrophilic aromatic substitution","An SN2 substitution"],"correct":2,"why":"Diene + alkene dienophile forming a six-membered ring is the definition of a [4+2] Diels-Alder cycloaddition. q_smiles = 2,3-dimethyl-1,3-butadiene (C6H10), correctly drawn. The other four mechanisms cannot build a ring from a diene plus dienophile. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - substrate SMILES valid (C6H10), reaction-type item, index 0 correct."},{"q":"The diene shown is (2E,4E)-2,4-hexadiene, which can rotate freely about its central single bond. For it to undergo a Diels-Alder reaction, which conformation must the diene adopt, and why?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An aromatic conformation, because aromaticity drives the reaction","The s-trans conformation, because it minimizes steric strain","A fully linear conformation, because it maximizes conjugation","The s-cis conformation, because it places both terminal carbons close enough to bond to the dienophile","Either conformation reacts equally well, since geometry does not matter"],"correct":3,"why":"Only the s-cis conformation brings C1 and C4 of the diene close enough to form the two new sigma bonds in the cyclic TS; s-trans holds them too far apart. Standard sophomore concept. q_smiles is valid (2E,4E)-2,4-hexadiene (C6H10) with correct E/E geometry. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - s-cis requirement is correct and in-scope; E/E SMILES valid."},{"q":"The bicyclic adduct drawn below is the kinetic Diels-Alder product of cyclopentadiene with maleic anhydride. The two carbonyl-bearing carbons sit tucked underneath the newly formed C=C bridge. Which descriptor names this stereochemical outcome, and what principle predicts it?","q_smiles":"O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(C3)C12","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The endo product, favored by the endo rule (secondary orbital overlap in the transition state)","The Markovnikov product, favored by carbocation stability","The exo product, favored because it is the more thermodynamically stable isomer","The trans product, favored by steric relief","The anti-Zaitsev product, favored by base-induced elimination"],"correct":0,"why":"The kinetic cyclopentadiene + maleic anhydride adduct is the endo isomer, explained by the endo rule (secondary orbital overlap). RDKit confirms q_smiles canonicalizes IDENTICALLY to the known CP+maleic-anhydride adduct skeleton (C9H8O3, 3 rings, norbornene-fused anhydride). Distractors (exo, trans, Markovnikov, anti-Zaitsev) are wrong. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Minor: q_smiles draws the correct adduct skeleton but carries no stereodescriptors, so it does not visually distinguish endo vs exo; acceptable since this is a text/concept item, not a structure-pick. Endo rule is standard Orgo II. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"Tetracyanoethylene (TCNE, shown) is one of the fastest known dienophiles in Diels-Alder reactions. Compared with simple ethylene, why does TCNE react so much faster with a given diene?","q_smiles":"N#CC(C#N)=C(C#N)C#N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["TCNE is aromatic, so it reacts by electrophilic substitution instead","The four cyano groups are electron-donating, raising the dienophile HOMO","The cyano groups make the alkene more nucleophilic toward the diene","TCNE is sterically smaller than ethylene, so it approaches the diene faster","The four cyano groups are strongly electron-withdrawing, lowering the dienophile LUMO so it overlaps better with the diene HOMO"],"correct":4,"why":"The four EWG nitriles make TCNE extremely electron-poor, accelerating cycloaddition; index 0 is the only chemically correct statement (nitriles withdraw, not donate; TCNE is not aromatic; it is not more nucleophilic). q_smiles = TCNE (C6N4), valid. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Borderline scope: correct answer is phrased in explicit HOMO/LUMO frontier-orbital terms, which leans toward the FMO content the rubric flags as grad-level. The underlying concept (electron-poor dienophile reacts faster) is in scope, and the distractors are only defeated by the EWG-vs-EDG distinction, so the LUMO wording is the natural correct choice; kept but flagged. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"You need to choose the diene that will react FASTEST in a Diels-Alder reaction with maleic anhydride. The structure of cyclopentadiene is drawn for reference. Which diene below would react fastest, and why?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["(2Z,4Z)-2,4-Hexadiene, because cis double bonds are more reactive","1,3-Butadiene, because it is the smallest diene","An isolated (non-conjugated) 1,4-pentadiene, because it has two double bonds","(2E,4E)-2,4-Hexadiene, because its methyl groups donate electron density","Cyclopentadiene, because its ring locks it permanently in the reactive s-cis geometry"],"correct":4,"why":"Cyclopentadiene is locked s-cis by its ring, so essentially every molecule is reaction-ready, making it the fastest classic diene - the standard textbook answer. Acyclic dienes pay a rotation penalty; 1,4-pentadiene is non-conjugated and cannot do a DA at all. q_smiles = cyclopentadiene (C5H6). Distractor B's EDG claim is a real minor factor but is not the dominant reason, so cyclopentadiene remains the single best answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Minor: distractor B (hexadiene methyls donate density) names a genuinely rate-enhancing effect, but the locked s-cis geometry of cyclopentadiene is the textbook dominant factor, preserving exactly one best answer. SMILES valid, index 0 correct."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with the dienophile shown below (acrolein, an alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde) under thermal conditions. Which statement best describes both the reaction type and the role of the CHO group?","q_smiles":"C=CC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An electrophilic addition of HBr; the CHO directs Markovnikov selectivity","An aldol addition; the CHO acts as the nucleophile","A Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition; the CHO is electron-donating and slows the reaction","A Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition; the CHO is an electron-withdrawing group that activates the dienophile and speeds the reaction","A Wittig olefination; the CHO is converted to a new alkene"],"correct":3,"why":"Butadiene (diene) + acrolein (dienophile) gives a cyclohexene-carbaldehyde via [4+2] Diels-Alder; the CHO is an EWG that lowers the dienophile reactivity barrier and accelerates the reaction. q_smiles = acrolein (C3H4O), valid. Options 2/3/4/5 are each chemically wrong (no aldol/HBr/Wittig; CHO is not donating). One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Difficulty mislabeled as 3 in source; this is a single/two-concept recognition item (reaction type + EWG role), effectively difficulty 2 - not a scope problem. CHO-activates phrasing is in scope (no frontier-orbital wording required). SMILES valid, index 0 correct."},{"q":"1,5-Hexadiene (shown) is heated to 200 degrees C, conditions for a [3,3]-sigmatropic Cope rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCCC=C","CC=CC=CC","C=CCC=CC","C=CCCC=CC","C=CC=CCC"],"correct":0,"why":"The Cope rearrangement is a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift: the C3-C4 sigma bond breaks and a new sigma bond forms between C1 and C6 while the two pi bonds shift. For the symmetric parent 1,5-hexadiene the rearrangement is degenerate, so the product is again 1,5-hexadiene (opt0 = substrate). SMILES all valid; distractors are conjugated 1,3- and 2,4-hexadiene, the isolated 1,4-diene, and a C7 skeleton. One defensible answer, but the 'product = starting material' framing is a degenerate-case gimmick and the named Cope rearrangement sits at the upper edge of DAT recognition depth.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, chemistry correct, one defensible answer (degenerate Cope returns the substrate). Cope is borderline DAT scope but appears in Booster at recognition level; the 'answer equals the drawn substrate' makes it slightly gimmicky. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"Allyl phenyl ether (shown) is heated to 200 degrees C, the classic aromatic Claisen ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCc1cccc(O)c1","C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1ccccc1O","CC=Cc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The aromatic Claisen delivers the allyl group to the ortho position; after tautomerization of the dienone and re-aromatization the product is 2-allylphenol (o-allylphenol), opt0. SMILES all valid and the correct option matches. Distractors place allyl para or meta, give bare phenol (loss of allyl, different formula), or show a conjugated 1-propenyl chain (wrong double-bond position). This is the single most canonical Claisen example and is the most defensibly in-scope item of the set.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, correct product (o-allylphenol), exactly one defensible answer, plausible regio/loss distractors. Canonical textbook Claisen at recognition depth. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"Allyl vinyl ether (shown) is heated, the conditions for an aliphatic Claisen ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCC1","C=CCCCO","CCC=CC=O","C=CCCC=O","CC=CCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"The aliphatic Claisen converts an allyl vinyl ether into a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl: the C-O sigma bond breaks, a new C-C sigma bond forms between the terminal allyl and vinyl carbons, and the enol becomes a carbonyl, giving 4-pentenal (pent-4-enal), opt0. SMILES all valid; correct option matches. Distractors move the alkene (3-pentenal, 2-pentenal), keep an alcohol (wrong formula), or invent cyclopentanone. Single regiochemical concept, canonical substrate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, correct product (pent-4-enal), one defensible answer, good distractors. Canonical aliphatic Claisen at recognition depth. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"3-Methyl-1,5-hexadiene (shown) is heated to undergo a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCCC=CC","C=CC(C)CC=C","CC=CCCC=CC","C=C(C)CCC=C","C=CCCCC=C"],"correct":0,"why":"In the Cope, the C3-C4 sigma bond breaks and a new C1-C6 sigma bond forms; the pi bonds shift and the C3 methyl ends up on a terminal sp2 carbon, giving hepta-1,5-diene (CH2=CH-CH2-CH2-CH=CH-CH3), opt0 (verified C7H12, unique). Distractors are the unchanged starting material, the 2-methyl isomer, 1,6-heptadiene, and a C8 octadiene (wrong carbon count). Correct and unambiguous, but tracking the methyl through a [3,3] shift is two-layer regiochemistry better tagged difficulty 2-3, and Cope regiochem prediction approaches the upper scope limit.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, correct product (hepta-1,5-diene) confirmed unique. One defensible answer. Substituted-Cope regiochemistry is on the hard edge of DAT scope but still recognition-level; difficulty is really 2-3, not 2 as the only concern. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"The compound shown (allyl vinyl ether) is heated. Which reaction type best describes its conversion to 4-pentenal (an unsaturated aldehyde)?","q_smiles":"C=CCOC=C","opts":["Nucleophilic acyl substitution","[1,2]-sigmatropic shift","[3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement (Claisen)","Diels-Alder cycloaddition","Electrocyclic ring closure"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Allyl vinyl ether (C5H8O) undergoes the Claisen rearrangement, a concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic shift, on heating to give 4-pentenal, a gamma,delta-unsaturated aldehyde. The C-O sigma bond migrates across the two allyl-type fragments. Verified: substrate and product isomeric (C5H8O).","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid, Claisen correctly keyed at index 0, distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope (named reaction recognition).","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The molecule drawn is allyl phenyl ether. When heated (no catalyst), it rearranges to o-allylphenol. This transformation is an example of which named reaction?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["Hofmann elimination","Aromatic Claisen rearrangement","Cannizzaro reaction","Cope rearrangement","Fries rearrangement"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Heating an allyl aryl ether triggers the aromatic Claisen rearrangement: a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift delivers the allyl group to the ortho carbon, then tautomerization restores aromaticity to give o-allylphenol. Fries is a Lewis-acid acyl migration of esters, not this thermal pericyclic process.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C9H10O allyl phenyl ether), aromatic Claisen correctly keyed, distractors (Fries/Cope/Hofmann/Cannizzaro) all plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"1,5-Hexadiene (shown) interconverts with an identical molecule when heated to roughly 200 C with no reagents. This degenerate rearrangement is classified as a:","q_smiles":"C=CCCC=C","opts":["Conrotatory electrocyclic ring opening","Cope rearrangement ([3,3]-sigmatropic)","1,3-dipolar cycloaddition","[1,5]-hydride shift","Claisen rearrangement"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A 1,5-diene undergoing a thermal [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement is the Cope rearrangement; for parent 1,5-hexadiene the product is identical (degenerate). A Claisen requires oxygen (allyl vinyl ether), which this all-carbon system lacks.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C6H10 1,5-hexadiene), Cope correctly keyed, distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"What single reagent/condition is required to convert allyl vinyl ether (shown) into 4-pentenal?","q_smiles":"C=CCOC=C","opts":["Simply heat (thermal, no added reagent)","Aqueous NaOH, then H3O+","O3, then Zn/H2O","H2 / Pd-C","mCPBA"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The Claisen rearrangement is a thermal, concerted pericyclic reaction needing only heat; no acid, base, oxidant, or catalyst. Ozonolysis, hydrogenation, and epoxidation would destroy the alkene rather than perform the [3,3] shift.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid, reagent-selection item correctly keyed (heat only), distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The conjugated triene shown is heated. It cyclizes to a cyclohexadiene by forming one new C-C sigma bond between the two terminal carbons. This bond-forming process is best classified as a:","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=C\\C=C/C","opts":["[3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement","Retro-Diels-Alder reaction","SN2 substitution","[1,5]-sigmatropic shift","Electrocyclic ring closure"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"This octa-2,4,6-triene is a fully conjugated 1,3,5-triene. When the ends of the conjugated pi system join to form a ring with one new sigma bond (and one fewer pi bond), the reaction is electrocyclic, closing to a dimethyl-1,3-cyclohexadiene. Sigmatropic shifts move an existing sigma bond rather than create a ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Minor: stem says 'two terminal carbons' but the reacting carbons are the conjugated termini (C2/C7); the methyls hang off the ends. Loose wording, not load-bearing; SMILES valid (C8H12 conjugated triene), electrocyclic correctly keyed.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclobutene (shown) is heated and ring-opens to 1,3-butadiene. Which statement correctly identifies this reaction and its product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC1","opts":["[1,3]-sigmatropic shift giving cyclopropene","Aldol condensation giving an enal","Cope rearrangement giving 1,3-butadiene","Diels-Alder retro giving two alkenes","Electrocyclic ring opening giving 1,3-butadiene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Breaking the cyclobutene C-C sigma bond to generate the conjugated diene is an electrocyclic ring opening (reverse of butadiene electrocyclization): one sigma bond becomes one pi bond. No oxygen or 1,5-diene array, so not Cope/Claisen.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Minor: difficulty marked 2 but really single-concept recognition. SMILES valid (C4H6 cyclobutene), electrocyclic ring opening correctly keyed, distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The diene shown is (Z)-1,3-pentadiene. On heating it undergoes a thermal [1,5] shift in which a hydrogen migrates along the pi framework. What migrates and what is the reaction class?","q_smiles":"CC=CC=C","opts":["A hydride adds; nucleophilic addition","A hydrogen atom migrates; [1,5]-sigmatropic shift","A proton is removed; E2 elimination","A hydrogen migrates; electrocyclic closure","A methyl group migrates; [3,3]-sigmatropic shift"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"1,3-Pentadiene undergoes a thermal [1,5]-hydrogen sigmatropic shift: a terminal/allylic H migrates across five atoms of the pi system to a new terminus, regenerating a 1,3-diene. It is concerted and suprafacial; no ring forms (not electrocyclic) and no 1,5-diene Cope array is present.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Minor: stem labels it '(Z)-1,3-pentadiene' but the SMILES CC=CC=C carries no stereochemistry. The Z/E descriptor is not load-bearing for the [1,5]-H answer, so the item still has exactly one defensible answer. SMILES valid (C5H8).","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The substrate shown is 1,5-hexadien-3-ol. Heating its alkoxide (anionic oxy-Cope) gives, after tautomerization, an unsaturated carbonyl compound. Which reaction class and product feature apply?","q_smiles":"C=CCC(O)C=C","opts":["[3,3]-sigmatropic (oxy-Cope) giving a delta,epsilon-unsaturated aldehyde/ketone (5-hexenal)","[1,5]-H shift giving a fully conjugated dienol","Retro-aldol giving two carbonyl fragments","Electrocyclic closure giving a cyclohexenol","Claisen rearrangement giving an allyl phenol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"This 1,5-diene with OH at C3 is the oxy-Cope substrate. The [3,3]-sigmatropic Cope shift forms a C1-C6 bond, breaks C3-C4, and generates an enol that tautomerizes to 5-hexenal (OHC-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2), confirmed isomeric to the substrate (C6H10O). The carbonyl is C1 and the alkene C5=C6 sits delta,epsilon to it, matching the keyed option. The OH-bearing terminus drives the irreversible enol-to-carbonyl step. No ring forms and no aromatic ether is present, ruling out electrocyclic and Claisen.","difficulty":3,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Keyed answer is correct: oxy-Cope to 5-hexenal, correctly named delta,epsilon-unsaturated in option 0. Minor internal inconsistency: the original 'why' text said 'gamma,delta' while the option (and the actual product) is delta,epsilon; corrected here. Borderline scope (anionic oxy-Cope, diff 3) but stays within named-reaction recognition depth. SMILES valid.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"When (Z)-1,3,5-hexatriene (drawn) is heated, it undergoes an electrocyclic ring closure. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=C/C=C/C=C","opts":["c1ccccc1","C=C1CCCC1","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCCC1","C1=CC=CCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A 6 pi-electron thermal electrocyclization forms one new sigma bond between the two terminal carbons of the triene, closing a six-membered ring and leaving two conjugated double bonds: 1,3-cyclohexadiene (opt 0, conjugated). The 1,4-cyclohexadiene isomer (opt 1) has non-conjugated double bonds; benzene needs an oxidation; cyclohexene has too few double bonds; methylenecyclopentane is the wrong ring size. Minor cosmetic flaw: the stem labels the triene (Z) but the SMILES central bond is drawn E; the recognition-level product answer is unaffected.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (1,3-cyclohexadiene, conjugated). Cosmetic mismatch: stem says (Z)-hexatriene but q_smiles draws central bond as E; product/answer unaffected at DAT recognition depth. Keep."},{"q":"Allyl vinyl ether (drawn) is heated, promoting a [3,3]-sigmatropic (Claisen) rearrangement. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=COCC=C","opts":["O=C1CCCC1","O=CCC=CC","O=CCCC=C","OCCCC=C","O=CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The aliphatic Claisen converts an allyl vinyl ether to a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl. The C-O sigma bond breaks and a new C-C sigma bond forms between the terminal carbons of the two pi systems, giving 4-pentenal (terminal aldehyde + terminal alkene). The alcohol, the conjugated 3-pentenal, the saturated pentanal, and cyclopentanone all have wrong functionality or skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: pent-4-enal (opt 0) is the correct Claisen product. In scope, valid, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Allyl phenyl ether (drawn) is heated to roughly 200 C, triggering an aromatic Claisen rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccccc1CC=C","Oc1ccc(CC=C)cc1","Oc1ccccc1C=CC","Oc1ccccc1","Oc1cccc(CC=C)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The aromatic Claisen is a [3,3] shift that delivers the allyl group to the ortho position; re-aromatization gives 2-allylphenol. The bond forms through the terminal allyl carbon, so the alkene stays terminal (opt 0). The para and meta isomers have wrong regiochemistry, the 1-propenyl phenol has a rearranged conjugated double bond, and plain phenol lacks the carbon chain.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: ortho 2-allylphenol with terminal alkene (opt 0) correct. In scope, valid. Keep."},{"q":"3-Phenyl-1,5-hexadiene (drawn) is heated, allowing a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement to reach the more stable isomer. Which structure is that product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(c1ccccc1)CC=C","opts":["C=Cc1ccccc1CCC=C","C1=CC=CC(c2ccccc2)C1","C=CCCC=Cc1ccccc1","C=CC(c1ccccc1)C=CC","C=CCC(C=C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The Cope [3,3] shift of the 1,5-diene breaks the C3-C4 sigma bond and forms a new C1-C6 bond, relocating the double bonds. The phenyl ends up conjugated with a newly formed alkene (styrene-type), giving 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene (opt 0), which is thermodynamically favored. Option 1 is the unchanged starting material; the others have incorrect connectivity or form a ring.","difficulty":3,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene (opt 0) is the correct, thermodynamically favored Cope product (styrene conjugation). Hard but recognition-level regiochemistry; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclobutene (drawn) is heated and opens by an electrocyclic ring-opening reaction. Which statement best describes this process and the curved arrows that represent it?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC1","opts":["The ring C-C sigma bond breaks and the electrons reorganize through a cyclic transition state to give 1,3-butadiene (a 4 pi-electron electrocyclic ring opening)","A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift relocates the double bond to give methylenecyclopropane","The pi bond is reduced and the ring is retained, giving cyclobutane","Two sigma bonds break and a triple bond forms, giving 2-butyne","A hydrogen migrates across the ring in a [1,3]-sigmatropic shift to give 1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Electrocyclic ring opening of cyclobutene is the microscopic reverse of butadiene ring closure: the ring sigma bond breaks and electrons redistribute through a single cyclic transition state (no discrete intermediate, no migrating atom) to give conjugated 1,3-butadiene; the open form involves 4 pi electrons. It is not a sigmatropic H-shift, a reduction, or an alkyne-forming process.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: opt 0 correct; distractors clearly wrong. Identifies one process/arrow set, no full mechanism. In scope, valid. Keep."},{"q":"1,5-Hexadien-3-ol (drawn) is heated, undergoing an oxy-Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement; the initial enol then tautomerizes. Which structure is the final carbonyl product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(O)CC=C","opts":["O=CCCC=CC","O=C1CCCCC1","OCCCCC=C","O=CCCCC=C","O=CCCC=C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The oxy-Cope is a Cope of a 1,5-diene bearing a hydroxyl at C3. After the [3,3] shift the hydroxyl-bearing carbon becomes an enol that tautomerizes to the more stable carbonyl, giving hex-5-enal (a 6-carbon aldehyde with a terminal alkene, opt 0). 4-Pentenal has too few carbons, the alcohol skipped tautomerization, cyclohexanone is a wrong ring product, and 4-hexenal has the double bond in the wrong place.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: hex-5-enal (opt 0) is the correct final carbonyl after oxy-Cope + tautomerization. In scope, valid, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) in CCl4 with a trace of a radical initiator (heat/light). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["BrC1CCC=CC1","BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCC=C1","BrC1CCCCC1Br","BrC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NBS at low Br2 concentration favors allylic radical substitution over addition. Br is installed at the sp3 carbon adjacent to C=C (allylic), giving 3-bromocyclohexene with the alkene retained. opt[0] (BrC1CCCC=C1 = 3-bromocyclohexene) is correct: opt[1] is vinylic, opt[2] is the 1,2-dibromide addition product, opt[3] is homoallylic (4-bromo), opt[4] is saturated bromocyclohexane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid (RDKit). Keyed allylic product is correct; distractors are vinylic/addition/homoallylic/saturated. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene is heated with Br2 in the presence of light (hv). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["BrC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","Cc1ccccc1Br","Cc1cccc(Br)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Br2/hv is free-radical halogenation, selective for the weak benzylic C-H (resonance-stabilized benzylic radical). One equivalent gives benzyl bromide (opt[0]). opt[1]/opt[2]/opt[5] are EAS ring-bromination products (wrong mechanism), opt[3] is the dibromide (over-bromination).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed benzylic-bromination product correct; one defensible answer. Mechanism-discrimination (radical vs EAS) is exactly DAT depth."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv). What is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1","BrCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. The benzylic C-H gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, so Br goes there: (1-bromoethyl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates the terminal primary non-benzylic carbon, opt[2]/opt[4] are ring (EAS) products, opt[3] is a geminal dibromide (over-bromination).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed benzylic product correct; single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Propene is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts":["CCCBr","C=CCBr","CCCO","CC(Br)C","CC(Br)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"HBr/peroxides is a radical chain (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to the less substituted carbon (forms the more stable secondary radical), so Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromopropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the Markovnikov product (ionic HBr), opt[2] is the Br2 dibromide, opt[3] is allyl bromide (allylic substitution), opt[4] is an alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Anti-Markovnikov keyed answer correct; distractors cover Markovnikov/dibromide/allyl/alcohol. One defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Methylpropane (isobutane) is treated with Br2 and light (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(Br)C","CC(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Radical bromination is highly selective for the most stable radical. The single tertiary C-H gives a 3 radical far more stable than the nine primary positions, so Br adds at the tertiary carbon: 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the primary product (minor, since Br2 is strongly selective), opt[2] is a dibromide, opt[3] has a rearranged/wrong C4 skeleton (n-butyl), opt[4] is a C5 dibromide (wrong formula).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Selectivity-of-Br2 keyed answer correct. Note opt[4] (CC(C)(Br)C(Br)C) is C5H10Br2 - a clearly impossible product (extra carbon), so it is an obviously-wrong distractor rather than a near-miss, but still parseable and not the keyed answer; question integrity intact."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv). Considering allylic selectivity, what is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["BrCC1=CCCCC1","CC1=C(Br)CCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1Br","CC1=CC(Br)CCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"NBS gives allylic bromination via a resonance-stabilized allylic radical; the secondary allylic CH2 is favored over the primary allylic methyl. The conventionally cited major product is 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene (opt[0]), retaining the trisubstituted alkene. opt[1] brominates the primary allylic methyl (minor), opt[2] is vinylic, opt[3] is the C6 allylic regiochemistry (a competing secondary-allylic site but the non-favored regiochemistry), opt[4] is the 1,2-dibromide addition product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Mild defensibility concern: opt[3] (6-bromo) is also a secondary allylic position, so opt[0] vs opt[3] is the only soft spot. The stem ('considering allylic selectivity') and the standard textbook answer (3-bromo, alkene retained as trisubstituted) make opt[0] the single defensible major product. Kept; flag the C3-vs-C6 nuance."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr and organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)C(Br)C","CC(C)CCBr","CC(C)C(Br)CBr","CCC(C)(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"HBr/peroxides is radical (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to terminal CH2 to give the more stable secondary radical; Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromo-3-methylbutane (opt[0]). Radical additions do NOT rearrange, so no hydride shift to tertiary occurs. opt[1] is Markovnikov, opt[2] is the rearranged (hydride-shift) tertiary product radicals don't give, opt[3] has wrong skeleton, opt[4] is the dibromide.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed anti-Markovnikov, no-rearrangement product correct. The no-rearrangement distractor (opt[2]) is a strong, instructive trap. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(C)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1Br","CC(C)(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1","BrCC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. Cumene's single benzylic C-H is on the central carbon; the resulting radical is both tertiary AND benzylic (doubly stabilized), far weaker than the primary methyl C-H. Br installs there: (2-bromopropan-2-yl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates a primary methyl (non-benzylic, minor), opt[2]/opt[3] are ring (EAS) products, opt[4] is a dibrominated over-reaction.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed doubly-stabilized benzylic/tertiary product correct; single concept (radical stability), visual, in scope. One defensible answer."},{"q":"The alkene below is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) and a trace of a radical initiator (AIBN, light, heat) in CCl4. Which statement BEST describes why these conditions are chosen instead of molecular Br2 in CCl4?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCC=C1","opts":["NBS reacts by an SN2 pathway that requires a polar protic solvent","NBS adds two bromine atoms across the double bond faster than Br2 does","NBS maintains a very low steady-state concentration of Br2, favoring radical allylic substitution over ionic addition across the C=C bond","NBS converts the alkene to a bromohydrin because succinimide releases hydroxide","NBS brominates only the sp2 (vinylic) carbons, never the allylic position"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"NBS slowly generates a low concentration of Br2 (and Br radicals); the low [Br2] disfavors electrophilic addition and favors radical allylic H-abstraction/substitution. With high [Br2] the alkene would simply add Br2 across the double bond. Verified: substrate SMILES valid (it is actually 3-methylcyclohexene, allylic CH3, but the stem never names it so no error); answer index 0 is the standard rationale; single concept, in scope.","difficulty":2,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None substantive. Substrate is 3-methylcyclohexene (methyl on sp3 allylic carbon), not 1-methyl, but stem does not name it so no factual error; answer correct."},{"q":"Toluene (shown) is treated with Cl2 and UV light (hv). At which type of position does chlorination occur, and what is the name of this reaction?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["No reaction occurs because the aromatic ring is unreactive toward Cl2","Benzylic position; nucleophilic substitution (SN2)","Benzylic position; radical (free-radical) halogenation of the methyl C-H","Ring carbon (meta); electrophilic aromatic substitution","Ring carbon (ortho/para); electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Cl2 with light (no Lewis-acid catalyst) means radical conditions. The benzylic C-H is abstracted because the resulting benzylic radical is resonance-stabilized by the ring, giving benzyl chloride. Ring substitution (EAS) requires Cl2 with FeCl3/AlCl3, not light. SMILES valid, index 0 correct, single concept, in scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Toluene SMILES valid; answer and reaction-type correct."},{"q":"2-Methylpropane (isobutane, shown) is subjected to free-radical halogenation. Which halogen gives the HIGHER selectivity for replacing the single tertiary (3°) C-H over the primary (1°) C-H?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C","opts":["Both are equally selective because selectivity depends only on the number of H's","Cl2, because the highly reactive Cl atom is more selective for the weaker tertiary bond","Br2, because bromination is faster than chlorination at every position","Br2, because the less reactive Br atom has a later, more product-like transition state and is more selective","Cl2, because chlorination always gives exclusively the tertiary product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Less reactive radicals are more selective (Hammond postulate / reactivity-selectivity). Br is much less reactive than Cl, so its transition state resembles the radical and it strongly prefers the weakest (3°) C-H. Cl is so reactive it is nearly indiscriminate. SMILES valid, index 0 correct, single concept, in scope (Hammond is a standard sophomore concept, not grad-level).","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Isobutane SMILES valid; reactivity-selectivity answer correct."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene (shown) is treated with one equivalent of Br2 under photochemical (hv) radical conditions. The most stable radical intermediate forms at one specific carbon. At which carbon is the H abstracted, and why?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts":["The benzylic CH2 carbon, because that radical is destabilized and therefore reacts fastest","The benzylic CH2 carbon, because the resulting radical is secondary AND resonance-stabilized by the aromatic ring","No single carbon is preferred; bromination is statistically random across all C-H bonds","The terminal CH3 carbon, because primary radicals are the most stable","An ortho ring carbon, because aromatic C-H bonds are the weakest"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Radical halogenation goes through the most stable radical. Abstracting an H from the benzylic CH2 gives a benzylic radical that is both secondary and resonance-delocalized into the ring, so it is the lowest-energy intermediate and the dominant product is (1-bromoethyl)benzene. SMILES valid, index 0 correct (options A and D both name the benzylic carbon but only A gives the right reason, so exactly one defensible answer), two-concept (radical stability + resonance), in scope.","difficulty":2,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Ethylbenzene SMILES valid; benzylic-resonance answer correct and uniquely right reason."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (shown) is treated with NBS, a radical initiator, and light in CCl4. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts":["BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1Br","BrC1C=CC(Br)CC1","BrC1CC=CCC1","BrC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"NBS/light = radical allylic bromination. The allylic C-H (sp3 carbon adjacent to C=C) is abstracted and replaced by Br, giving 3-bromocyclohexene with the double bond retained. RDKit confirms optA has Br on an sp3 allylic carbon with the C=C intact; optB is the ionic 1,2-dibromide, optC is a vinyl bromide, optD is bromocyclohexane (no double bond), optE is a dibromo distractor. All five SMILES valid; index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All option SMILES valid; keyed allylic-bromination product correct; distractors differ by regio/skeleton only."},{"q":"Propene (shown) is treated with HBr in the presence of peroxides (ROOR) and light. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["CCC","CC(C)Br","CCCBr","OC(C)C","BrCC(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Peroxides trigger a radical chain that adds HBr with anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry: Br adds to the LESS substituted (terminal) carbon because that places the radical on the more stable secondary carbon. The product is 1-bromopropane (optA, Br on terminal CH2). Without peroxides, ionic addition gives 2-bromopropane (optB). All five SMILES valid; index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All option SMILES valid; anti-Markovnikov peroxide-effect product correctly keyed; Markovnikov distractor present."},{"q":"Rank the following carbon radicals from MOST stable to LEAST stable: (I) tert-butyl radical (CH3)3C•, (II) allyl radical CH2=CH-CH2•, (III) methyl radical •CH3, (IV) ethyl radical CH3CH2•. (The alkene 1-methylcyclohexene is shown only as a reminder that allylic radicals are resonance-stabilized.)","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["II > I > IV > III","I > II > IV > III","II > IV > I > III","I > IV > II > III","III > IV > I > II"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Resonance beats hyperconjugation: the allyl radical (II) is delocalized over two carbons and is the most stable. Among the rest, stability follows 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl, so tert-butyl (I, 3°) > ethyl (IV, 1°) > methyl (III). Order: II > I > IV > III = index 0. q_smiles CC1=CCCCC1 is valid 1-methylcyclohexene, used as a decorative reminder of allylic resonance; molecule is drawn in stem as required. Single concept, in scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Ranking correct; q_smiles is a valid reminder molecule (1-methylcyclohexene), not the reacting species, but a molecule is drawn so the visual requirement is met."},{"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene, shown) is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (one equivalent). Considering that benzylic radicals are resonance-stabilized AND that this benzylic position is also tertiary, which single product predominates and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(C)c1ccc(Br)cc1, because radical bromination occurs para on the ring","CC(CBr)c1ccccc1, because primary methyl positions are brominated preferentially","CC(C)c1ccccc1Br, because the ring carbon ortho to the chain is most reactive","BrCC(C)c1ccccc1Br, because both a methyl and a ring carbon are brominated","BrC(C)(C)c1ccccc1, because abstracting the benzylic H gives a radical that is both tertiary and resonance-stabilized"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The single benzylic C-H sits on a carbon that is both tertiary and benzylic; abstracting it gives the most stabilized radical possible here (3° + resonance into the ring). Br caps that carbon, giving (2-bromopropan-2-yl)benzene. RDKit confirms optA has Br on the tertiary benzylic carbon (0 H). The methyl and ring positions give far less stable radicals or require EAS conditions. All embedded SMILES valid; index 0 correct. opts_are_structures=false is appropriate because each option pairs a SMILES with prose reasoning (hybrid, not a pure drawn structure). Two layered concepts (3° + benzylic resonance) at difficulty 3, still recognition-level and in scope (single reactive center).","difficulty":3,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None substantive. Options are SMILES+rationale hybrids so opts_are_structures=false is defensible; keyed tertiary-benzylic product correct."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) in CCl4 with a trace of a radical initiator (low [Br2], hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1=CCCCC1","BrC1CCCC(Br)C1","BrC1(Br)CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"NBS at low Br2 concentration favors allylic radical substitution over addition. The allylic C-H is abstracted, giving 3-bromocyclohexene (opt0) with the double bond retained. Distractors are addition (bromocyclohexane, gem-dibromide, anti-dibromide) or oxidation (cyclohexanone) products that arise from non-NBS pathways. Keyed index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = 3-bromocyclohexene). All five SMILES valid, distractors are sensible addition/oxidation alternatives, single allylic concept, in scope."},{"q":"Propylbenzene (drawn) reacts with Br2 and light (hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CCCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCc1ccccc1Br","BrCCCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Br2/hv is radical halogenation; the most stable radical is benzylic (resonance-stabilized into the ring), so Br goes to the benzylic carbon, giving (1-bromopropyl)benzene (opt0). The internal/terminal positions are non-benzylic, and ring substitution requires FeBr3 (ionic EAS), not radical conditions. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = benzylic bromide). All SMILES valid; distractors cover internal, terminal, para, and ortho positions. Single benzylic-selectivity concept, in scope."},{"q":"Propene (CH2=CH-CH3, drawn) loses an allylic hydrogen to form a carbon radical. Which statement best explains why the allylic radical is unusually stable compared with a typical primary radical?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The unpaired electron is delocalized by resonance over two carbons (the radical is shared between C1 and C3).","The radical carbon becomes sp hybridized, relieving angle strain.","Hyperconjugation alone makes it more stable than any tertiary radical.","An intramolecular hydrogen bond locks the radical in place.","The radical is stabilized by an inductive electron-withdrawing effect from the methyl group."],"correct":0,"why":"The allyl radical is adjacent to a C=C, so the unpaired electron is delocalized by resonance across both terminal carbons (two equivalent resonance structures). Resonance, not induction, hyperconjugation-alone, rehybridization, or H-bonding, is the dominant stabilization. Keyed index 0 correct; distractors are recognizable misconceptions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = resonance delocalization). q_smiles valid; text options with exactly one defensible answer. Conceptual recognition item, in scope."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (drawn) is treated with NBS (radical, allylic conditions). Among the allylic positions, the major product comes from abstraction at the secondary ring allylic carbon. Which structure is that major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1Br","BrCC1=CCCCC1","CC1=C(Br)CCCC1","CC1=CC(Br)CCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS abstracts an allylic H. The stem explicitly directs to the secondary ring allylic carbon, giving 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene (opt0) with the double bond retained. Distractors are the primary (methyl) allylic product, the gem position, a vinylic Br, and a non-allylic ring position. Keyed index 0 correct and the stem's restriction makes the answer unambiguous.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene). Stem pre-specifies the secondary ring allylic site, removing ambiguity between the two allylic radicals. All SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene (drawn) can react with Cl2/hv or with Br2/hv at the benzylic position. Compared with chlorination, why is radical benzylic BROMINATION far more selective (cleaner) for the benzylic C-H?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Bromination is ionic while chlorination is radical, so only bromination is selective.","Bromine abstracts hydrogen faster, leaving no time for side reactions.","The bromine atom is less reactive, so its transition state is later and more product-like, magnifying the preference for the most stable (benzylic) radical.","Bromine atoms are smaller than chlorine atoms and fit only at the benzylic site.","The benzylic C-Br bond is weaker, which speeds initiation."],"correct":2,"why":"Br is less reactive than Cl, so its H-abstraction transition state is later/more product-like (Hammond postulate), strongly reflecting radical-stability differences and favoring the most stable benzylic radical. Reactive Cl has an early TS and is much less selective. Keyed index 0 correct; this Br-vs-Cl selectivity contrast is standard sophomore Orgo I material.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Hammond/selectivity contrast (Cl unselective vs Br selective) is core textbook radical-halogenation content, not grad-level; in scope. q_smiles valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Hexene (drawn) is treated with NBS under radical allylic conditions. The allylic radical formed is delocalized, and the product that results from bromination at the terminal carbon (with double-bond transposition) predominates as the conjugated, more substituted alkene. Which structure is that product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC/C=C\\CCC","C=CC(Br)CCC","BrCCCCCC=C","BrC/C=C/CCC","C=CCC(Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS abstracts the C3 allylic H, giving an allyl radical delocalized between C1 and C3. Trapping at C1 transposes the double bond to C2=C3, giving the more substituted internal alkene (E)-1-bromo-2-hexene (opt0); the E isomer is favored over Z. The non-transposed 3-bromo-1-hexene keeps a terminal alkene; the Z isomer and homoallylic placements are wrong. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = (E)-1-bromo-2-hexene). Layers allylic transposition + E/Z (two concepts, appropriate for difficulty 3). All SMILES valid; E vs Z distractors differ only in geometry. In scope as the hard item."},{"q":"Isobutane (2-methylpropane, drawn) is treated with Br2 and light (hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)CBr","BrCC(C)CBr","CC(C)(C)Br","CCCCBr","CC(C)CBr"],"correct":2,"why":"Radical bromination is highly selective for the most stable radical. Isobutane has one tertiary C-H and nine primary C-H's; the tertiary radical is most stable, so Br2/hv gives almost exclusively tert-butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane, opt0). The primary isobutyl bromide is minor, and dibromides or chain-rearranged n-butyl bromide are wrong. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = tert-butyl bromide). All SMILES valid; distractors are primary, di-bromides, and a wrong skeleton. Single 3o>1o selectivity concept, in scope."},{"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone is treated with NaBH4 in methanol. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts":["OCCCC(C(C)(C)C)CCO","C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","OC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","OC(=O)C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NaBH4 selectively reduces the ketone to a secondary alcohol (4-tert-butylcyclohexanol). It does not reduce to alkane, oxidize to acid, or open the ring. opt0 is correct; distractors are over-reduction, oxidation, unchanged SM, and a ring-opened diol. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed, in scope, valid, single concept (carbonyl reduction)."},{"q":"Pentanoic acid is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)CCCC","opts":["O=CCCCC","CC(O)CCC","OCCCCC","CCCCC","OC(O)CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid fully to the primary alcohol (1-pentanol). It does not stop at the aldehyde or over-reduce to alkane. opt0 correct; distractors are aldehyde, alkane, wrong-position secondary alcohol, and gem-diol. Formula C5H12O confirmed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Butanol is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in dry CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCCCC","opts":["OCCCC","OC(=O)CCC","O=CCCC","CC(O)CC","O=C(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"PCC under anhydrous conditions stops oxidation of a primary alcohol at the aldehyde (butanal), not the carboxylic acid. opt0 correct; distractors are carboxylic acid, wrong-position ketone, unchanged SM, and secondary alcohol. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed, in scope, valid, single concept (mild oxidation)."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with O3, then dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["O=CCCCCCC=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1O","O=C(O)CCCCC(C)=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC12OC1CCCC2"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the ring C=C: the more-substituted carbon becomes a methyl ketone and the CH carbon becomes an aldehyde, giving the open-chain keto-aldehyde 6-oxoheptanal (C7H12O2, confirmed). opt0 correct; distractors are oxidative-workup acid, dihydroxylation diol, epoxide, and a wrong symmetric dialdehyde.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed; reductive vs oxidative workup is the single discriminating concept. In scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"4-Acetylstyrene (1-(4-vinylphenyl)ethan-1-one) is treated with H2 and a Pd/C catalyst (1 atm, room temperature). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1","opts":["C=Cc1ccc(C(O)C)cc1","CCc1ccc(C(O)C)cc1","CCC1CCC(C(C)=O)CC1","C=Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1","CCc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"H2/Pd at mild conditions chemoselectively reduces only the alkene (vinyl to ethyl); the aryl ketone and aromatic ring are untouched. opt0 (4-ethylacetophenone, C10H12O) correct; distractors reduce the ketone, hydrogenate the ring, leave SM unchanged, or reduce the wrong group.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed chemoselectivity question, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA (meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid) in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"mCPBA transfers one oxygen to the alkene in a concerted step giving the epoxide (cyclohexene oxide), not a diol; trans-diol requires a separate hydrolytic opening. opt0 (epoxide) correct. Distractors are cis-diol (meso, opt1) and trans-diol (opt2) confirmed by 3D analysis, plus ketone and alcohol. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed to the epoxide; the diol distractors are valid stereoisomers. In scope, single concept (epoxidation stops at epoxide)."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product derived from the acyl portion?","q_smiles":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the ester fully to a primary alcohol; the acyl carbon of methyl benzoate becomes benzyl alcohol (methanol is released). opt0 (benzyl alcohol, C7H8O) correct; distractors are aldehyde (does not stop there), acid, toluene over-reduction, and unchanged ester. Stem explicitly limits answer to the acyl portion, so exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Single reactive center, single concept (ester reduction). Stem disambiguates by specifying the acyl portion. In scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The transformation shown converts pentanal into 1-pentanol (CCCCCO). Which reagent best accomplishes this reduction WITHOUT also reducing an ester or carboxylic acid elsewhere in a molecule?","q_smiles":"CCCCC=O","opts":["OsO4, then NaHSO3","LiAlH4 in dry ether","PCC in CH2Cl2","NaBH4 in methanol","O3, then Zn/H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride that reduces aldehydes/ketones to alcohols but is too weak to touch esters or carboxylic acids, so it is the selective choice. LiAlH4 would also reduce those carbonyls. PCC oxidizes, and OsO4/O3 act on alkenes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. NaBH4 selective for aldehyde over ester/acid; valid pentanal SMILES; reagent-selection item, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is to be converted to benzyl alcohol. Which reagent is the strong hydride source that would accomplish this and could also reduce a carboxylic acid in the same molecule?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["H2/Pd","PCC","mCPBA","OsO4","LiAlH4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 is the powerful hydride reducing agent that reduces aldehydes, ketones, esters, and carboxylic acids to alcohols. PCC and mCPBA are oxidants, OsO4 dihydroxylates alkenes, and H2/Pd reduces alkenes/alkynes, not the aromatic aldehyde under standard conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. LiAlH4 strong hydride reducing acids; valid benzaldehyde SMILES; distractors are all oxidants/wrong-target. In scope."},{"q":"A chemist wants to oxidize the primary alcohol 1-pentanol (drawn) to the aldehyde pentanal and STOP there, avoiding over-oxidation to the carboxylic acid. Which reagent is correct?","q_smiles":"CCCCCO","opts":["NaBH4","LiAlH4","Hot KMnO4","PCC in CH2Cl2","Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"PCC is an anhydrous Cr(VI) oxidant that stops at the aldehyde for primary alcohols. Aqueous strong oxidants like KMnO4 or Jones reagent carry the primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid. NaBH4 and LiAlH4 are reducing agents.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. PCC stops 1deg alcohol at aldehyde; valid SMILES; canonical contrast vs KMnO4/Jones. In scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with a reagent and the product is cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol (a syn diol). Which reagent gives this syn-dihydroxylation?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["mCPBA, then H3O+","H2/Pd","O3, then (CH3)2S","OsO4, then NaHSO3","PCC"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"OsO4 adds two OH groups to the same face of the alkene (syn addition), giving the cis (syn) diol. mCPBA then aqueous workup gives the anti (trans) diol via epoxide opening. Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C, and H2/Pd just reduces it.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. OsO4 = syn diol; valid cyclohexene SMILES; mCPBA/H3O+ anti-diol distractor is the strong plausible foil. In scope."},{"q":"The alkene shown (1-phenylpropene) is converted to an epoxide in a single step. Which reagent performs this epoxidation?","q_smiles":"CC=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["LiAlH4","O3 then Zn","NaBH4","mCPBA","OsO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"mCPBA is a peroxyacid that delivers one oxygen across the C=C to form an epoxide in a concerted, stereospecific (syn) addition. OsO4 gives a diol, O3 cleaves the alkene, and NaBH4/LiAlH4 are reducing agents that do not react with isolated alkenes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Answer correct (mCPBA). STEM-LABEL BUG: original text 'the alkene shown (2-methyl-2-butene region drawn as cis-2-butene analog)' is internally contradictory and mislabels CC=Cc1ccccc1 (which is 1-phenylpropene / beta-methylstyrene). Reworded stem to match the actual drawn structure; reagent answer is unaffected, so keep=true."},{"q":"3-Heptene (drawn) is treated with O3 followed by reductive workup with (CH3)2S. What is the reaction type and the carbonyl product class?","q_smiles":"CCCC=CCC","opts":["Epoxidation giving one epoxide","Oxidative cleavage giving two aldehydes","Oxidative cleavage giving two carboxylic acids","Syn-dihydroxylation giving one diol","Catalytic hydrogenation giving an alkane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C double bond. With a reductive workup (Zn/H2O or dimethyl sulfide), each carbon of a disubstituted internal alkene becomes an aldehyde. Oxidative workup (H2O2) would instead give carboxylic acids.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. CCCC=CCC is 3-heptene; reductive ozonolysis of an internal disubstituted alkene gives two aldehydes (butanal + propanal). Two-concept (reaction type + workup-dependent product). In scope. The carboxylic-acid option is the right oxidative-workup foil."},{"q":"You need to reduce only the C=C double bond of 4-phenyl-3-buten-2-one to give the saturated ketone, leaving the C=O intact, in one step. Which reagent set is best?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["NaBH4 in methanol","PCC in CH2Cl2","OsO4, then NaHSO3","LiAlH4 in ether","H2 with Pd/C"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2, Pd/C) selectively reduces the carbon-carbon double bond (and is generally chemoselective for the alkene over an isolated ketone), preserving the C=O. NaBH4 and LiAlH4 reduce the carbonyl to an alcohol; OsO4 and PCC do not selectively give the saturated ketone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. H2/Pd-C reduces the alkene chemoselectively over the ketone (aromatic ring survives mild conditions); NaBH4/LiAlH4 hit the carbonyl. Valid benzalacetone SMILES. Two-concept (chemoselectivity). In scope."},{"q":"Two carbonyls are available for reduction by NaBH4 in methanol: acetophenone (an aryl methyl ketone, drawn) and benzaldehyde. Considering electronic and steric effects on hydride addition, which statement about relative rate is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzaldehyde is reduced faster than acetophenone because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and more electrophilic","Acetophenone is reduced faster because the extra methyl group donates electron density to the carbonyl","Acetophenone is reduced faster because ketones are inherently more electrophilic than aldehydes","Neither reacts because NaBH4 cannot reduce conjugated aryl carbonyls","Both are reduced at identical rates because NaBH4 reacts only with esters"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are reduced faster than ketones by hydride: the aldehyde carbonyl carbon is less sterically hindered and more electrophilic (only one alkyl/aryl group donating electron density), so benzaldehyde reacts faster than the more hindered, more electron-rich acetophenone.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. Aldehyde > ketone reactivity toward hydride (less steric hindrance, more electrophilic). Valid acetophenone SMILES. Conceptual reasoning item, sophomore-level (no FMO/quantitative); in scope. Distractor 4 (methyl donation) is the right common misconception foil."},{"q":"The molecule shown is 2-bromobutane. Which descriptor correctly assigns the configuration of its single stereocenter?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","opts":["Z","R","E","The center is not a stereocenter (achiral)","S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns R for the C2 stereocenter of C[C@@H](Br)CC. Priorities Br > CH2CH3 > CH3 > H, with H pointing back, trace clockwise = R. Keyed correct=0 (R) is right.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; keyed answer and rationale correct."},{"q":"Classify the alkene geometry drawn below using CIP (E/Z) nomenclature.","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["S","R","E","Z","Neither E nor Z (no geometric isomerism)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns Z for the C=C of cis-2-butene C/C=C\\C. Each sp2 carbon has methyl > H; the two methyls are cis (same side), so Z. Keyed correct=0 (Z) is right.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms Z."},{"q":"For the stereocenter in L-alanine shown below, rank the four attached groups from HIGHEST to LOWEST CIP priority.","q_smiles":"N[C@@H](C)C(=O)O","opts":["NH2 > CH3 > COOH > H","CH3 > NH2 > COOH > H","COOH > NH2 > CH3 > H","NH2 > COOH > CH3 > H","COOH > CH3 > NH2 > H"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"First atoms: N(7) beats both C, so NH2 highest. Between carbons, COOH (O,O,O) beats CH3 (H,H,H). H lowest. Order NH2 > COOH > CH3 > H. SMILES is valid canonical L-alanine (RDKit CIP = S, consistent with L). Question asks only priority order, so exactly one correct answer. Keyed correct=0 is right.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. SMILES is genuine L-alanine; ranking unambiguous."},{"q":"The alkene shown is (E)-2-pentene. Which single statement explains why this geometry is labeled E?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/C","opts":["There are two identical groups on one alkene carbon, so E/Z does not apply","The two higher-priority groups are on the SAME side of the double bond","The two higher-priority groups (ethyl and methyl) are on OPPOSITE sides of the double bond","Free rotation about the C=C averages the geometry, defaulting to E","The molecule has a stereocenter that is configured R"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns E for CC/C=C/C. Left carbon ethyl > H, right carbon methyl > H, higher groups trans = E. Keyed correct=0 is right.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E."},{"q":"D-glyceraldehyde is drawn below. Assign the CIP configuration of its stereocenter.","q_smiles":"OC[C@@H](O)C=O","opts":["S","E","R","Meso (internally compensated, no net configuration)","Z"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns R for the central carbon of OC[C@@H](O)C=O. OH > CHO(O,O,H) > CH2OH(O,H,H) > H, clockwise with H back = R. Canonical D-glyceraldehyde is (R). Keyed correct=0 is right.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; matches real D-glyceraldehyde."},{"q":"Identify the configuration of the trisubstituted alkene shown (3-methyl-2-pentene). Note that one alkene carbon bears both a methyl and an ethyl group.","q_smiles":"CC/C(C)=C\\C","opts":["S","R","Z","E","No E/Z designation is possible"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns Z for CC/C(C)=C\\C. Left carbon ethyl > methyl, right carbon methyl > H, higher groups same side = Z. Keyed correct=0 is right.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms Z."},{"q":"(E)-cinnamaldehyde is shown. Which statement about its stereochemistry is correct?","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["The C=C is Z, and the molecule has no R/S stereocenter","The molecule is achiral because E/Z isomerism does not apply to conjugated alkenes","The C=C is E, and the molecule also has one R stereocenter","The C=C is Z because the phenyl outranks the CHO group","The C=C is E, and the molecule has no R/S stereocenter"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns E for the C=C of O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1, and finds no chiral center. CHO > H and phenyl > H are trans = E. Keyed correct=0 is right.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E and no stereocenter."},{"q":"The molecule below is 2-bromo-3-chlorobutane drawn with both stereocenters defined. Assign the (C2, C3) configuration. (Number from the bromine-bearing carbon as C2.)","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Cl)C","opts":["(2S, 3S) — and the molecule is meso","(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3R) — and the molecule is meso","(2R, 3S)","(2R, 3R)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit CIP labeler returns R at both stereocenters of C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Cl)C, i.e. (2R,3R). It is not meso because the two ends carry different halogens (Br vs Cl), so no internal mirror plane. Keyed correct=0 is right; the meso distractors are good traps.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms (R,R) and not meso."},{"q":"Assign the R/S configuration of the stereocenter in the molecule shown (2-bromobutane).","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["The molecule is achiral (no stereocenter)","Configuration cannot be assigned without a 3-D model","It is a meso compound","R","S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"RDKit confirms the single stereocenter (C2) is R. Priorities Br > CH2CH3 > CH3 > H; with H back, 1->2->3 is clockwise = R. One stereocenter, chiral, not meso. Keyed index 0 (R) is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"The aldotetrose shown has two stereocenters. Reading from the aldehyde carbon (C1), what is the configuration at C2 and C3?","q_smiles":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)CO","opts":["It is a meso compound, so neither center has a fixed label","(2R, 3R)","(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3S)","(2S, 3S)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"RDKit assigns C2 = R and C3 = S for this SMILES, so the molecule is (2R,3S), which is option index 1. The original key (index 0, '2S,3R') and the rationale ('C2 = S, C3 = R') are both inverted. The ends differ (CHO vs CH2OH) so it is not meso.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"Keyed answer WRONG: RDKit gives C2=R, C3=S = (2R,3S) = index 1, not the keyed index 0 (2S,3R). Why-text inverted both labels. Corrected to 1."},{"q":"Assign the geometry (E or Z) of the carbon-carbon double bond in the alkene shown (a 2-bromo-3-chloropentene). Watch the priority comparisons carefully.","q_smiles":"CC/C(Cl)=C(/C)Br","opts":["The geometry is undefined as drawn","The alkene has no E/Z isomerism","E","Both an E and a Z descriptor apply equally","Z"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"RDKit assigns the double bond as E. Left carbon: Cl > CH2CH3; right carbon: Br > CH3. The two higher-priority groups (Cl, Br) are on opposite sides = E. Keyed index 0 (E) is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"Assign the geometry (E or Z) of the carbon-carbon double bond in 2-methylbut-2-enal, shown below.","q_smiles":"C/C=C(\\C)C=O","opts":["Z","No E/Z isomerism is possible here","The CHO group makes the descriptor undefined","Both descriptors are equally valid","E"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit assigns the double bond as E. On the substituted carbon CHO > CH3 (carbonyl carbon duplicates O); on the other carbon CH3 > H. Higher-priority groups (CHO and far CH3) are on opposite sides = E. Keyed index 0 (E) is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"Assign the R/S configuration of the stereocenter in but-3-en-2-ol, shown below. Be careful comparing the vinyl group to the methyl group.","q_smiles":"C=C[C@H](O)C","opts":["Cannot be assigned because of the double bond","R","It is achiral / meso","The center is not a stereocenter","S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"RDKit confirms the carbinol stereocenter is R. Priorities OH > CH=CH2 > CH3 > H (vinyl beats methyl via the duplicated alkene carbon); with H back, 1->2->3 is clockwise = R. Keyed index 0 (R) is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"cis-2-Butene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Anti addition occurs across the double bond. Which structure represents the product (one of the two enantiomers formed)?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C(Br)=C(Br)/C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CCC(Br)C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C","C[C@@H](Cl)[C@H](Cl)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Br2 adds anti across an alkene. Anti addition to the cis (Z) alkene gives the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) pair of 2,3-dibromobutane (option A, verified (2R,3R) chiral by RDKit). Option B is the meso (R,S) diastereomer (from the trans alkene). C uses Cl2 (wrong halogen), D is a monobromide, and E is a vinyl dibromide (no sp3 addition).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: q_smiles and all 5 options parse; option[0] is the chiral (R,R) diastereomer, option[1] is meso. Anti addition to Z-alkene correctly gives the chiral d,l pair. Keyed answer 0 correct, exactly one defensible answer, in DAT scope, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2. Anti addition across the ring double bond gives a racemic product. Which structure is the major product (one enantiomer shown)?","q_smiles":"C1CC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cl[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Cl","BrC1CCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1Br","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to a cyclic alkene places the two bromines on opposite faces: trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane. RDKit confirms option A is the (S,S) chiral trans isomer, while option B is the cis (meso) isomer requiring syn addition. C uses Cl2, D is a monobromide, and E is a diol (wrong reagent).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: option[0] is chiral trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane; option[1] is the cis meso. Anti addition gives trans. Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"(R)-2-Bromobutane is treated with NaSH in acetone. Which structure is the product of this SN2 reaction?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=CC","CC[C@@H](S)C","CCCC(C)S","CC[C@@H](O)C","CC[C@H](S)C"],"correct":1,"why":"SN2 proceeds with backside attack and inversion of spatial configuration. RDKit 3D modeling of backside displacement on (R)-2-bromobutane confirms the product is CC[C@@H](S)C ((S)-2-butanethiol, option A). Option B is the retention (R) product, C is the alcohol (wrong nucleophile), D is the elimination alkene, and E is a primary thiol on a rearranged skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified by 3D RDKit backside-attack model: spatial inversion of (R)-2-bromobutane gives the (S) thiol = option[0]. (CIP letter flips R->S because Br is replaced by lower-priority S.) Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"1-Methylcyclohexene reacts with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov addition of HBr places Br on the more-substituted (tertiary, methyl-bearing) carbon via the more stable tertiary carbocation, giving 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (option A). Option B is the anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, C is the alcohol, D adds two bromines, and E is a ring-expanded skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: option[0] is 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (Br on tertiary C). Markovnikov regiochemistry correct. Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual. Minor: this item tests regiochemistry, not stereochemistry, so 'stereo-relationships' group label is a slight mismatch, but it does not affect correctness/keep."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"Styrene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA in CH2Cl2. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","C1OC1c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes the alkene by syn delivery of one oxygen across the C=C, giving styrene oxide (option A). Option B is the 1,2-diol (requires subsequent epoxide hydrolysis), C is a benzylic alcohol on a shortened skeleton, D is a vicinal dibromide (wrong reagent), and E is an aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: option[0] is styrene oxide (epoxide). mCPBA gives the epoxide. Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"The molecule shown is meso-tartaric acid. A racemic mixture of (R,R)- and (S,S)-tartaric acid can be separated, but this isomer is a single achiral compound. Which statement best explains why the molecule shown is optically INACTIVE?","q_smiles":"O[C@@H]([C@@H](O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O","opts":["Its two stereocenters are both R, making it superimposable on its mirror image","It has no stereocenters at all, so it cannot rotate plane-polarized light","Free rotation about the central C-C bond averages the rotation to zero","It has an internal mirror plane, so the two stereocenters cancel each other's rotation (it is a meso compound)","It exists as a rapidly equilibrating 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers (a racemate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"RDKit confirms the drawn isomer has opposite configurations at the two stereocenters (R,S) and is superimposable on its mirror image = meso/achiral. It has an internal symmetry element (textbook: mirror plane), so it is optically inactive. It is NOT a racemate (that is a mixture of two compounds), and it DOES have two stereocenters, so distractors 1,2 are false; both R would be the chiral (R,R) form (distractor 3 false).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn molecule is meso (superimposable on mirror image). Answer index 0 correct. The internal symmetry element of meso-tartaric acid is strictly a center/plane depending on conformation, but textbook/DAT framing 'internal mirror plane' is standard and accepted."},{"q":"The single enantiomer of 2-bromobutane shown below is dissolved in aqueous ethanol and undergoes solvolysis by an SN1 mechanism. The major substitution product is 2-butanol obtained as a near-50:50 mixture of two forms. What is the stereochemical relationship between those two product molecules?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["Meso and its enantiomer (an internal mirror plane forms)","Identical molecules (SN1 proceeds with complete retention)","Enantiomers (the planar carbocation is attacked from both faces, giving a racemic pair)","Constitutional isomers (the carbon skeleton rearranges)","Diastereomers (one new stereocenter plus the old one give a syn/anti pair)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"SN1 proceeds through a planar, achiral secondary carbocation attacked by water on both faces with roughly equal probability. The 2-butanol product has a single stereocenter, so the two forms (R and S) are nonsuperimposable mirror images = enantiomers (racemic). With only one stereocenter there can be no diastereomers or meso forms.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: 2-bromobutane SMILES valid, single stereocenter. SN1 gives racemic enantiomers. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"Consider the compound drawn below, meso-2,3-dibromobutane. Which single statement correctly describes BOTH its optical activity and its relationship to its own mirror image?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C","opts":["Optically inactive, and identical to (superimposable on) its mirror image","Optically inactive, and a constitutional isomer of its mirror image","Optically active, and identical to its mirror image","Optically inactive, and a diastereomer of its mirror image","Optically active, and an enantiomer of its mirror image"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"RDKit confirms the drawn molecule is (R,S) and is superimposable on its mirror image = meso. A meso compound is achiral (optically inactive) and its mirror image is the same molecule (identical/superimposable). This pairs the correct optical activity with the correct mirror-image relationship.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn molecule is meso (superimposable on mirror image). Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"The molecule shown is 2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal (an aldotetrose) and contains stereocenters at C2 and C3. Using the 2^n rule and ignoring any meso coincidence, what is the MAXIMUM number of stereoisomers possible for a molecule with this number of stereocenters?","q_smiles":"OC[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C=O","opts":["16","4","3","2","8"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"RDKit confirms two stereocenters. The 2^n upper bound is 2^2 = 4. The stem explicitly says to ignore the meso coincidence and use the 2^n rule, so 4 is the maximum.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: aldotetrose SMILES valid, 2 stereocenters. 2^2=4. Answer index 0 correct. Note threose has no meso form so 4 is also the true count; the 'ignore meso' wording keeps it unambiguous."},{"q":"Examine the structure of 3-methylhexane drawn below. Which classification of the molecule itself is correct?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@@H](C)CC","opts":["It is chiral (it has one stereocenter) and is therefore optically active as a single enantiomer","It is achiral because all of its substituents are simple alkyl groups","It cannot be a stereocenter because carbon is bonded only to carbons and hydrogens","It is achiral because it has a plane of symmetry through C3","It is a meso compound with an internal mirror plane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"C3 bears four different groups: H, methyl, ethyl, and propyl, so it is a genuine stereocenter (RDKit confirms one assigned center). The molecule is chiral and optically active as a single enantiomer. There is no symmetry plane, and it is not meso. Distractor 5 (the common 'all-carbon means no stereocenter' trap) is the well-aimed wrong answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: 3-methylhexane SMILES valid, one stereocenter at C3 (different alkyl chains). Chiral. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"(R)-glyceraldehyde is shown. A chemist wants to convert this single enantiomer into its mirror-image partner, (S)-glyceraldehyde. Which description of (S)-glyceraldehyde relative to the (R)-form shown, and the change required, is correct?","q_smiles":"OC[C@@H](O)C=O","opts":["(S)-glyceraldehyde is identical; simply rotating the molecule in space converts one to the other","(S)-glyceraldehyde is its diastereomer; changing only one of two stereocenters is required","(S)-glyceraldehyde is a constitutional isomer; the OH and CHO must be relocated","(S)-glyceraldehyde is a conformer; rotation about the C-C bond converts one to the other","(S)-glyceraldehyde is its enantiomer; inverting the configuration at the single stereocenter is required"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit confirms the drawn molecule is the (R) configuration with a single stereocenter. Its mirror image, the (S)-form, is its enantiomer, and inverting configuration at that one stereocenter is the only way to interconvert nonsuperimposable mirror images. With one stereocenter there are no diastereomers, and enantiomers are distinct compounds not interconvertible by rotation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn molecule is (R)-glyceraldehyde, single stereocenter. Mirror image = enantiomer via inversion. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"The compound shown is trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane. Considering both its symmetry and its relationship to its mirror image, which classification is correct?","q_smiles":"Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts":["It is meso; it has an internal mirror plane and is achiral","It is chiral; its mirror image is a non-superimposable enantiomer (the trans isomer is NOT meso)","Its mirror image is a diastereomer because the ring restricts rotation","It is identical to the cis isomer once the ring flips","It is achiral; its mirror image is superimposable by simple rotation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"RDKit confirms the drawn trans molecule is (S,S) and is NOT superimposable on its mirror image = chiral, existing as a pair of enantiomers. The cis (R,S) isomer is the meso/achiral one (confirmed superimposable on its mirror image). Trans and cis are distinct diastereomeric compounds, so 'identical to cis' is false; the trans is not meso/achiral.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn trans (S,S) is chiral/non-superimposable; cis (R,S) is meso. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"2,3-dichlorobutane is drawn below. This molecule has two stereocenters, but one of its stereoisomers is a meso compound. Taking the meso coincidence into account, how many DISTINCT (different) stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","opts":["6 (2^n counted for both cis and trans)","1 (only the meso form exists)","4 (the full 2^n with no overlap)","2 (a single pair of enantiomers)","3 (a pair of enantiomers plus one meso compound)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Two stereocenters give a 2^n upper bound of 4, but because both stereocenters carry identical substituent sets the (R,S) and (S,R) forms are the same achiral meso compound. That overlap removes one isomer, leaving 3 distinct stereoisomers: the (R,R)/(S,S) enantiomeric pair plus the single meso (R,S). Classic 2^n meso exception.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: q_smiles is the flat constitution (no stereo tags), which is valid and appropriate for a count-all-stereoisomers question. Constitution has two equivalent stereocenters → 3 distinct stereoisomers. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is (2S,3S)-butane-2,3-diol. Which structure is its ENANTIOMER?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C","opts":["C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C","OCC(O)CC","CC(O)C(O)C","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C","C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"RDKit confirms stem = (2S,3S). Enantiomer inverts both centers to (2R,3R) = opt0 C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C (RDKit: 1R,3R) — distinct from stem, correct. opt1 (1S,3R) is the meso diastereomer (RDKit-verified meso, the key trap). opt2 is identical to the stem (1S,3S). opt3 = butane-1,2-diol (constitutional). opt4 = flat, no stereo. Exactly one correct enantiomer. In scope (single concept: enantiomer vs diastereomer), two layered (stereo + meso recognition) so difficulty 2 is apt.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written. RDKit confirms opt0 is (2R,3R) true enantiomer; opt1 is the meso trap; one defensible answer; all SMILES valid and in scope.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"Consider the molecule drawn below, which is meso-2,3-dibromobutane. What is the relationship between this molecule and its own mirror image?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","opts":["They are identical (superimposable) because the molecule has an internal mirror plane","They are diastereomers","They are conformational isomers that cannot interconvert","They are enantiomers because the molecule is chiral","They are constitutional isomers"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"RDKit confirms C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C is (2R,3S) and is IDENTICAL to its own mirror image (meso/achiral). So a meso compound, despite having stereocenters, is superimposable on its mirror image — opt0 is correct. opt1 falsely calls it chiral; opt2/opt3 wrong isomer relationships; opt4 false (it is one compound, not conformers). Single concept (meso recognition), one defensible answer, in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. RDKit verifies the SMILES is genuinely meso (identical to its mirror image). Clean single-answer text item, in scope.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"The molecule shown is (R)-1-phenylethanol. Which structure is its ENANTIOMER?","q_smiles":"O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C","opts":["O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C","OCc1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC","O[C@H](c1ccccc1)C","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"RDKit confirms stem = (R)-1-phenylethanol (single benzylic stereocenter). Enantiomer = (S) = opt0 O[C@H](c1ccccc1)C (RDKit: S) — distinct, correct. opt1 is identical to the stem (R). opt2 and opt3 are 1-phenyl-1-propanol (C9, extra CH2, constitutional). opt4 = 2-phenylethanol (no stereocenter). Exactly one correct enantiomer, single concept, in scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written. RDKit confirms opt0 is the (S) enantiomer; opt1 duplicates the stem as a trap; one defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane. Which single statement BEST classifies this molecule with respect to chirality and optical activity?","q_smiles":"C1C[C@H](Cl)C[C@H](Cl)C1","opts":["It is a meso compound: it has stereocenters but an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and optically inactive","It is chiral and optically active, existing as a pair of enantiomers","It is a constitutional isomer of 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane and is optically active","It exists only as a single non-superimposable mirror image","It has no stereocenters at all, so chirality is not possible"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"RDKit confirms C1C[C@H](Cl)C[C@H](Cl)C1 is achiral/meso (identical to its mirror image), with a mirror plane through C2/C5. So cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane is meso, achiral, optically inactive — opt0 correct. RDKit also confirms the rationale's claim that trans-1,3 is chiral (trans-1,3 is NOT identical to its mirror). opt2 wrongly denies stereocenters; opt3 confuses constitutional vs stereoisomerism. Single concept (meso recognition on a ring), one defensible answer, in scope (standard sophomore content; difficulty 3 is acceptable boundary).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. RDKit verifies the cis-1,3 SMILES is meso and that trans-1,3 (cited in the rationale) is chiral. Difficulty 3 is at the upper edge but the concept (ring meso recognition) is in DAT scope.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn) is treated with sodium hydride (NaH), a strong base, which removes the most acidic proton. Which structure is the major product, and why is this deprotonation so favorable?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1=CCCC1","[CH]1C=CC=C1","[CH-]1C=CC=C1","[CH+]1C=CC=C1","[CH2-]C=CC=C"],"correct":2,"why":"Removing the sp3 C-H gives the cyclopentadienyl ANION: planar, fully conjugated 5-ring with 6 pi e- (4n+2, n=1), aromatic, which is the driving force for the acidic CH2 (pKa ~16). The cation (4 pi e-, antiaromatic), open pentadienyl anion, radical, and cyclopentene are all wrong. All 5 SMILES valid and distinct; keyed answer correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Correct key, valid distinct SMILES, single concept, visual, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclopropene (drawn) is converted to a cation by treatment with a halogenating reagent followed by AgSbF6 to ionize the C-X bond at the sp3 carbon. Which structure is the major, unusually stable product?","q_smiles":"C1=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[cH+]1cc1","[CH2+]C=C","[CH-]1C=C1","C1=CC1","[CH+]1CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Ionizing the sp3 C-X gives the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation: 3-ring, 2 pi e- (4n+2, n=0), the smallest aromatic system, unusually stable. The cyclopropenyl anion (4 pi e-, antiaromatic), saturated cyclopropyl cation (C3H5+), ring-opened allyl cation, and neutral cyclopropene are all wrong. All 5 SMILES valid and distinct; keyed answer correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None substantive. Reagent route slightly contrived but defensible; chemistry and key correct.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclooctatetraene (COT, drawn) is treated with excess potassium metal (a strong two-electron reductant). Which structure is the major product, and why does the tub-shaped neutral molecule become planar?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH-]1C=CC=CC=C[CH]1","c1ccccc1","[CH-]1C=CC=CC=C[CH-]1","C1CCCCCCC1","[CH-]1C=CC=CC=CC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Two-electron reduction gives the COT DIANION (C8H8 2-): adding 2 e- takes the 8 pi-electron tub-shaped nonaromatic neutral to a 10 pi-electron (4n+2, n=2) system that flattens into a planar aromatic ring. The radical anion (9 pi, one unpaired e-) is an intermediate not the excess-K product; benzene and cyclooctane are wrong skeletons; the monoanion is wrong electron count. SMILES all valid/distinct; key correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Standard textbook aromaticity item; correct key, valid SMILES, in DAT recognition scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Pyrrole (drawn) is treated with a strong base that removes its most acidic proton (the N-H). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1cc[nH]c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[c-]1ccc[nH]1","[n-]1cccc1","C1=CC=NC1","c1ccncc1","Cn1cccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Deprotonating N-H gives the pyrrolide (pyrrolyl) ANION, which remains a planar 6 pi-electron aromatic ring (the p-orbital still holds the lone pair delocalized into the ring). Removing N-H (not a ring C-H) is correct. The C-anion (from a wrong deprotonation), N-methylpyrrole, pyridine, and non-aromatic 2H-pyrrole are wrong. SMILES all valid/distinct; key correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"Minor: 'why' lone-pair wording is loose but conclusion (aromatic 6-pi pyrrolide) and key are correct.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Indene (drawn) is treated with sodium amide (NaNH2), which removes the single acidic sp3 (CH2) proton on the five-membered ring. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1C=Cc2ccccc21","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH+]1C=Cc2ccccc21","[c-]1cccc2c1CC=C2","O=C1CCc2ccccc21","C1CCc2ccccc21","[CH-]1C=Cc2ccccc21"],"correct":4,"why":"Deprotonating the CH2 gives the indenyl anion: the 5-ring now has 6 pi e- (cyclopentadienyl-anion-like, fused to benzene), an aromatic stabilized carbanion, explaining the acidic CH2. The cation is destabilized, the aryl (ring-carbon) anion comes from an unfavorable deprotonation, and indane/indanone are wrong skeletons. SMILES all valid/distinct; key correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Correct key, valid distinct SMILES, single concept, visual, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Furan (drawn) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation with acetic anhydride and a Lewis acid catalyst (BF3). Furan is an aromatic heterocycle (O lone pair supplies 2 of the 6 pi electrons), so it reacts like benzene by electrophilic aromatic substitution. Which structure is the major monoacylation product?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CC=CC=O","CC(=O)c1ccoc1","CC(=O)c1ccc(C(C)=O)o1","CC(=O)C1CCCO1","CC(=O)c1ccco1"],"correct":4,"why":"Furan acylates preferentially at the 2-position (alpha to O) because that arenium intermediate is best stabilized; aromaticity is retained. The 3-acyl (beta) product is minor, the saturated tetrahydrofuranyl ketone and ring-opened diketone destroy the ring, and diacylation is not the major mono-substitution. SMILES all valid/distinct; key correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. EAS regioselectivity is standard sophomore content; correct key, valid SMILES, visual, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn) is deprotonated by potassium tert-butoxide to give its conjugate base. Among the candidate structures below, which one is the aromatic species that actually forms, where the negative charge is fully delocalized over a planar 6 pi-electron ring?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH]1C=CC=C1","[CH2-]C=CC=C","[CH-]1C=CC=C1","C1=CCCC1","[CH+]1C=CC=C1"],"correct":2,"why":"The cyclopentadienyl ANION (6 pi e-, 4n+2) is the aromatic conjugate base; charge delocalized over all five carbons. The cation (4 pi e-, antiaromatic), radical, acyclic pentadienyl anion, and cyclopentene are wrong. Key and SMILES correct. HOWEVER this item is essentially a duplicate of Q1: identical substrate (cyclopentadiene), identical five options, identical correct answer; only the stem wording differs.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"Chemistry fully correct but NEAR-DUPLICATE of Q1 (same substrate, same 5 options, same key). Drop one of Q1/Q8 to avoid redundancy.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cyclopentadiene (a neutral, non-aromatic diene with one sp3 CH2). Which single reagent best converts it into the aromatic 6-pi-electron cyclopentadienyl anion?","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=C1","opts":["BH3 then H2O2/NaOH (hydroboration-oxidation)","HCl gas (protonation to give a carbocation)","Br2 in CCl4 (electrophilic addition across a double bond)","NaH (a strong base that removes the sp3 C-H proton)","H2 with Pd/C (catalytic hydrogenation of the double bonds)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclopentadiene C5H6 (valid, non-aromatic as confirmed) is deprotonated at the sp3 CH2 (pKa ~16) by NaH/n-BuLi to give the planar, fully conjugated cyclopentadienyl anion with 6 pi electrons (4n+2, n=1), which is aromatic. Br2 addition, hydrogenation, hydroboration, and protonation all destroy conjugation rather than aromatize. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C5H6, non-aromatic as intended), correct=0 (NaH), single-concept deprotonation-to-aromatic-anion, in DAT scope, distractors all destroy conjugation."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cycloheptatriene (a neutral ring with one sp3 CH2 and three double bonds). Which reagent abstracts a hydride to generate the aromatic tropylium (cycloheptatrienyl) cation?","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=CC=C1","opts":["H2/Pt (catalytic hydrogenation)","LiAlH4 (a strong hydride donor)","NaOH (a Bronsted base)","Ph3C+ BF4- (trityl cation, a hydride abstractor)","NaBH4 (a hydride donor / reducing agent)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cycloheptatriene C7H8 (valid, non-aromatic as drawn). Removing a hydride from the sp3 carbon gives the 7-membered tropylium cation with 6 pi electrons (4n+2, n=1), which is aromatic and unusually stable. Trityl cation Ph3C+ is the classic hydride abstractor that effects this. NaBH4, LiAlH4, H2/Pt are reducers/hydride donors and NaOH is a base, none of which abstract hydride. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C7H8), correct=0 (trityl), classic tropylium fact, in scope; distractors are all reducers/base so unambiguous."},{"q":"Pyridine (drawn) and pyrrole (a 5-membered ring with N-H) are both aromatic nitrogen heterocycles, but their nitrogen lone pairs behave very differently. Which statement correctly classifies the lone pair on pyridine's nitrogen and its consequence?","q_smiles":"c1ccncc1","opts":["The lone pair is delocalized like pyrrole's, so pyridine is non-basic","Pyridine is antiaromatic because the nitrogen contributes its lone pair","The lone pair is in the p orbital and is part of the aromatic sextet, making pyridine a very weak base","Pyridine is non-aromatic because nitrogen disrupts the ring","The lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane, not part of the aromatic sextet, so pyridine is a good base/nucleophile"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"In pyridine the N contributes one electron via its p orbital (like a ring CH) and its lone pair sits in an in-plane sp2 orbital, NOT part of the aromatic sextet, so it is available as a base/nucleophile. Pyrrole's N lone pair IS in the p orbital and counts toward the 6 pi electrons, making it far less basic. Pyridine has 6 aromatic pi electrons (aromatic, not anti/non-aromatic). Option 0 is the only correct classification.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (pyridine, aromatic), correct=0, canonical pyridine-vs-pyrrole basicity concept, in scope, distractors are standard misconceptions."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cyclooctatetraene (COT). Although it has 8 pi electrons, it is NOT antiaromatic. Which statement best explains its behavior and reactivity?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC=CC=C1","opts":["It is aromatic with 8 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=1.5)","It is aromatic because 8 is an even number of pi electrons","It is antiaromatic and undergoes no addition reactions","It adopts a non-planar tub shape and reacts as an ordinary set of isolated alkenes (non-aromatic)","It is antiaromatic and so reacts faster than benzene in electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"COT C8H8 (valid, non-aromatic as drawn). With 8 pi electrons (4n, n=2) a planar form would be antiaromatic, so it puckers into a non-planar tub conformation that breaks cyclic conjugation, leaving four localized alkenes; it undergoes addition like a normal polyene. It is non-aromatic in its real geometry. Option 0 correct; n=1.5 and even-number rationales are false.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C8H8, RDKit confirms non-aromatic), correct=0 (tub/non-aromatic), standard COT teaching point, in scope, one defensible answer."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is furan, a 5-membered oxygen heterocycle. How many of oxygen's two lone pairs are counted toward the aromatic pi-electron sextet, and is furan aromatic?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1","opts":["Both lone pairs are in the pi system; furan is aromatic with 4 pi electrons","One lone pair counts, giving 4 pi electrons; furan is antiaromatic","One lone pair is in a p orbital (counts toward the 6 pi electrons); furan is aromatic","Neither lone pair is in the pi system; furan is non-aromatic","Both lone pairs are in the pi system, giving 8 pi electrons; furan is antiaromatic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Furan: two ring double bonds give 4 pi electrons; one oxygen lone pair (in a p orbital) adds 2, while the second lone pair stays in an in-plane sp2 orbital. 4+2=6 satisfies Huckel 4n+2, so furan is aromatic with exactly ONE O lone pair in the sextet. Counting both wrongly gives 8. Option 0 is the only self-consistent statement.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (furan, aromatic), correct=0, single concept (heteroatom lone-pair counting), in scope, distractors give wrong electron counts."},{"q":"Two structures are relevant: benzene (drawn) and cyclobutadiene. Both are fully conjugated carbocyclic rings, yet they could not be more different in stability. Which classification is correct?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzene (6 pi e-) is aromatic and stable; cyclobutadiene (4 pi e-) is antiaromatic and highly reactive","Both are antiaromatic because both have even pi-electron counts","Benzene is antiaromatic and cyclobutadiene is aromatic","Both are aromatic because both are cyclic and conjugated","Benzene is aromatic and cyclobutadiene is non-aromatic with no special instability"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Benzene: planar, cyclic, conjugated, 6 pi electrons (4n+2, n=1) -> aromatic, very stable. Cyclobutadiene: 4 pi electrons (4n, n=1) -> antiaromatic, strongly destabilized and so reactive it dimerizes on formation. Aromatic stabilization vs antiaromatic destabilization is the key contrast. Option 0 only correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (benzene, aromatic), correct=0, foundational aromatic/antiaromatic contrast, in scope, distractors are standard wrong classifications."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is imidazole, a 5-membered ring with two nitrogens (one =N- pyridine-type and one N-H pyrrole-type). Which statement correctly accounts for its 6 aromatic pi electrons?","q_smiles":"c1cnc[nH]1","opts":["Neither nitrogen contributes a lone pair; imidazole has only 4 pi electrons","Imidazole is non-aromatic because it has two different nitrogens","Both nitrogen lone pairs enter the pi system, giving 8 pi electrons (antiaromatic)","The two ring double bonds give 4 pi e- and the N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair (2 pi e-) for 6 total; the =N- lone pair stays in-plane and is basic","The =N- nitrogen donates its lone pair to the ring and the N-H lone pair is basic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Imidazole C3H4N2 (valid, aromatic). Two ring double bonds = 4 pi electrons; the pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair via a p orbital (+2) for 6 total (4n+2). The pyridine-type =N- keeps its lone pair in an in-plane sp2 orbital, which is basic/nucleophilic. Option 3 swaps which nitrogen donates (the common trap) and is wrong. Only option 0 is correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (imidazole as drawn), correct=0, two-nitrogen bookkeeping (regio of lone-pair donation) fits difficulty 3, in scope, distractor option 3 is plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation. Apply Huckel's rule: count the pi electrons and classify the ring.","q_smiles":"[CH+]1C=C1","opts":["6 pi electrons; aromatic","4 pi electrons (4n with n=1); antiaromatic","2 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=0); aromatic","0 pi electrons; non-aromatic","2 pi electrons; antiaromatic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Cyclopropenyl cation C3H3+ (valid; RDKit perceives it as aromatic). One C=C provides 2 pi electrons and the cationic carbon is an empty p orbital completing cyclic overlap. 2 pi electrons = 4n+2 with n=0 -> aromatic, the textbook reason this tiny cation is unusually stable. Option 0 is correct; the 4-, 0-, and 6-electron counts are wrong, and 2 pi electrons cannot be antiaromatic.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C3H3+ cation, RDKit confirms aromatic), correct=0, Huckel-count classification, in scope, electron-count distractors all internally wrong."},{"q":"Five C5 (or related) ring species are shown below. Cyclopentadiene readily loses a proton from its sp3 CH2 to give the species drawn. Which one of the five structures is AROMATIC?","q_smiles":"[CH-]1C=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH-]1CCCC1","[CH+]1C=CC=C1","[CH-]1C=CC=C1","C1C=CC=C1","[CH]1C=CC=C1"],"correct":2,"why":"Cyclopentadienyl ANION: two C=C (4 pi e-) + lone pair in p orbital (2 e-) = 6 pi e- = 4n+2 (n=1), aromatic. RDKit perceives only opt0 as aromatic (canon c1cc[cH-]c1). Neutral (sp3 CH2 breaks conjugation), cation (4 e-, antiaromatic), saturated anion (no pi system), and radical (5 e-) are all nonaromatic. All five SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. correct=0 confirmed by RDKit (only the anion is aromatic). All 5 SMILES valid and distinct by charge/saturation. Single Huckel-count concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Consider pyridine, drawn below. When counting pi electrons for the Huckel 4n+2 rule, how is the nitrogen lone pair treated, and is pyridine aromatic?","q_smiles":"c1ccncc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The N lone pair sits in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and is NOT counted; pyridine has 6 pi electrons and is aromatic","The N lone pair is in a p orbital but not counted; pyridine has 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic","Pyridine is nonaromatic because the nitrogen disrupts the ring conjugation","The N lone pair occupies the p orbital and IS counted; pyridine has 8 pi electrons and is antiaromatic","The N lone pair is counted, giving 6 pi electrons; pyridine is aromatic"],"correct":0,"why":"Pyridine-type N is part of a C=N pi bond; its remaining lone pair sits in an in-plane sp2 orbital and is NOT counted. The three ring pi bonds give 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic. opt2 reaches the right conclusion (aromatic, 6 e-) but via the wrong reason (lone pair counted), so the stem's explicit 'how is the lone pair treated' makes opt0 the single fully-correct choice.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"Minor: opt0 and opt2 share the 'aromatic, 6 pi e-' conclusion; discriminator is lone-pair treatment, which the stem explicitly asks about, so still exactly one correct. Pyridine valid+aromatic in RDKit. Keep."},{"q":"1,3,5,7-Cyclooctatetraene (COT), drawn below, has 8 pi electrons in the ring. Classify COT and give the correct reason.","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Aromatic: 8 pi electrons satisfy the 4n+2 rule","Nonaromatic: it adopts a nonplanar tub shape, so the p orbitals do not form a continuous cyclic overlap","Antiaromatic: it is planar with 8 pi electrons (4n, n=2)","Aromatic: it gains stability from the 8-membered ring size","Antiaromatic: all eight carbons are sp3 hybridized"],"correct":1,"why":"COT puckers into a nonplanar tub to escape antiaromaticity; the localized double bonds do not overlap continuously, so it is nonaromatic (behaves as an ordinary polyene). RDKit perceives 0 aromatic atoms. opt1 is the classic trap (treats it as planar). correct=0.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. C8H8 valid, nonaromatic in RDKit. Standard textbook nonplanarity concept, in scope. Exactly one correct."},{"q":"Furan is shown below. The oxygen bears two lone pairs. How many pi electrons does the aromatic system contain, and where do they come from?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["6 pi electrons, but they come entirely from three C=C bonds within the ring","6 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 2 from ONE oxygen lone pair in a p orbital; the other O lone pair stays in an sp2 in-plane orbital","4 pi electrons from the two C=C bonds only; the oxygen lone pairs are not involved","8 pi electrons: 4 from the C=C bonds plus 4 from both oxygen lone pairs; furan is antiaromatic","6 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 2 from BOTH oxygen lone pairs combined"],"correct":1,"why":"Furan O is sp2: one lone pair in a p orbital donates 2 e-, the other stays in-plane. With 4 e- from the two ring C=C bonds, total = 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic (RDKit: 5 aromatic atoms). opt4 is impossible (furan has only two ring C=C, not three). correct=0.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Furan SMILES valid+aromatic. Distractors plausible; opt4 factually impossible (good). Single heteroatom-donation concept, in scope."},{"q":"The cycloheptatrienyl (tropylium) cation is shown below. Counting pi electrons in the ring, classify it.","q_smiles":"[CH+]1C=CC=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Antiaromatic: the empty p orbital contributes zero electrons, giving 4n","Antiaromatic: 8 pi electrons in the seven-membered ring","Nonaromatic: the positive carbon is sp3 and breaks conjugation","Aromatic: 6 pi electrons (three C=C bonds) over a planar, fully conjugated 7-membered ring with an empty p orbital at the cationic carbon","Aromatic: 8 pi electrons satisfy the 4n+2 rule"],"correct":3,"why":"Tropylium: all 7 carbons sp2, cationic C has an empty p orbital (0 e-), three C=C give 6 pi e- = 4n+2 (n=1), aromatic. RDKit canon c1ccc[cH+]cc1, 7 aromatic atoms, C7H7+. correct=0.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Valid C7H7+, aromatic in RDKit. Single Huckel concept, visual, in scope. Exactly one correct."},{"q":"Pyrrole is shown below. Although the nitrogen carries an N-H and a lone pair, pyrrole is a very weak base (it does NOT protonate on nitrogen like an amine). Counting pi electrons, why is pyrrole aromatic?","q_smiles":"c1cc[nH]c1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The nitrogen lone pair sits in a p orbital and is donated to the ring: 4 e- from two C=C bonds + 2 e- from the N lone pair = 6 pi electrons","Pyrrole has 6 pi electrons from three C=C bonds; the nitrogen lone pair is not involved","The nitrogen lone pair stays in an in-plane sp2 orbital; the ring has only 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic","Pyrrole is nonaromatic because the N-H carbon is sp3","The nitrogen contributes 4 electrons from its lone pair, giving 8 pi electrons total"],"correct":0,"why":"Pyrrole-type N (not in a ring double bond) donates its lone pair from a p orbital (2 e-); with two ring C=C (4 e-) = 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic. The tied-up lone pair explains its weak basicity. RDKit: 5 aromatic atoms, C4H5N. correct=0.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Valid+aromatic. Pyrrole-vs-pyridine N contrast is core sophomore content. Single concept, exactly one correct, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Five three-membered ring species are shown. Cyclopropene can ionize to give either the structure shown (drawn) or related ions. Which ONE of the five is AROMATIC?","q_smiles":"[CH+]1C=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH+]1CC1","[CH-]1C=C1","[CH+]1C=C1","C1C=C1","[CH]1C=C1"],"correct":2,"why":"Cyclopropenyl CATION: one C=C (2 pi e-) + empty p orbital at cationic C (0 e-) = 2 pi e- = 4n+2 with n=0, aromatic. RDKit perceives only opt0 as aromatic (canon c1c[cH+]1, C3H3+). Anion (4 e-, antiaromatic), neutral (sp3 CH2), radical (3 e-), and cyclopropyl cation (no ring C=C) are nonaromatic. All 5 SMILES valid. n=0 is allowed.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. correct=0 confirmed by RDKit. All 5 SMILES valid, distinct by charge/saturation. 2-electron (n=0) aromaticity is standard. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Imidazole, drawn below, contains TWO nitrogen atoms in a five-membered ring: one is an N-H nitrogen and the other is a doubly-bonded (=N-) nitrogen. Determine the pi electron count and classify the ring.","q_smiles":"c1cnc[nH]1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["6 pi electrons, aromatic: the N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair (2 e-) to the ring, while the =N- nitrogen keeps its lone pair in an in-plane sp2 orbital; both nitrogens plus the ring double bonds give the sextet","8 pi electrons, antiaromatic: BOTH nitrogen lone pairs are donated into the pi system","6 pi electrons, aromatic: both nitrogen lone pairs are donated, and one C=C bond is left out","4 pi electrons, antiaromatic: neither nitrogen lone pair is counted","6 pi electrons, but nonaromatic because the two nitrogens cannot both be planar"],"correct":0,"why":"Imidazole: pyrrole-type N-H donates its lone pair (2 e-), pyridine-type =N- contributes via its C=N pi bond and keeps its remaining lone pair in-plane (uncounted, source of basicity). Two ring pi bonds (4 e-) + N-H lone pair (2 e-) = 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic. RDKit: 5 aromatic atoms, C3H4N2. correct=0.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Valid+aromatic. Layers two N types but remains a single-ring aromaticity classification - acceptable as the batch's hard item. Exactly one correct, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylpropene (isobutylene) is treated with HBr. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CBr","CC(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)Br","CCCCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"HBr adds by Markovnikov: the proton goes to the CH2 (less-substituted carbon) to generate the more stable tertiary carbocation at the central carbon, captured by bromide to give tert-butyl bromide. The isobutyl bromide is anti-Markovnikov (primary cation), and the sec-/n-butyl options have rearranged carbon skeletons not produced here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. q_smiles and all four C4H9Br options parse; key [0] = tert-butyl bromide is the correct Markovnikov product; single defensible answer; visual; in DAT scope."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene reacts with HCl. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Cl","ClCC1=CCCCC1","CC1(Cl)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov addition places H on the ring CH and the charge on the more substituted (tertiary) carbon bearing the methyl, giving 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane. The 2-chloro isomer is anti-Markovnikov, the allylic chloride retains the alkene, and the cyclopentane option is the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; key [0] = 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane correct (tertiary, Markovnikov); distractors plausibly wrong; single answer; visual."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with HBr. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The proton adds to the terminal CH2 so the positive charge develops on the resonance-stabilized benzylic carbon, which bromide captures to give (1-bromoethyl)benzene. The 2-bromo product needs a non-stabilized primary cation, the alcohol uses the wrong nucleophile, and the ring-dibrominated option comes from a different (EAS) reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = (1-bromoethyl)benzene correct via benzylic resonance stabilization; distractors plausible-but-wrong; single answer; visual."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HCl. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CCC(C)(C)Cl","CCCC(C)Cl","CC(C)CCCl","CC(C)C(C)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Protonation gives a secondary cation adjacent to a tertiary carbon; a 1,2-hydride shift forms the more stable tertiary cation, which captures chloride to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. The 2-chloro-3-methylbutane is the non-rearranged secondary product, and the remaining choices are anti-Markovnikov or wrong-skeleton structures.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All C5H11Cl options valid; key [0] = 2-chloro-2-methylbutane correct after hydride shift; two-concept (Markovnikov + rearrangement) in scope; single answer; visual."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene is treated with one equivalent of HBr under thermodynamic (higher-temperature) conditions. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C/CBr","CCCCBr","C=CCCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation. Under thermodynamic control bromide adds 1,4 to give the more stable internal alkene, (E)-1-bromo-2-butene (crotyl bromide). The 3-bromo-1-butene is the 1,2 (kinetic) product, while the 4-bromo-1-butene and saturated 1-bromobutane do not match the allylic cation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; stem specifies thermodynamic conditions, disambiguating the 1,4 product from the 1,2 kinetic distractor; key [0] (E)-crotyl bromide correct with proper E geometry; recognition-level diene chemistry, in DAT scope; visual."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene undergoes acid-catalyzed hydration (dilute H2SO4, H2O). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","OCC1=CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov hydration places OH on the more substituted (tertiary) carbon via the more stable tertiary carbocation, giving 1-methylcyclohexanol. The 2-methylcyclohexanol is anti-Markovnikov, the allylic alcohol keeps the double bond, and the cyclopentane choice is the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = 1-methylcyclohexanol correct (Markovnikov, tertiary); distractors plausible-but-wrong; single answer; visual."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC=C(C)Br","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The proton adds to the CH carbon so the charge forms on the more substituted (tertiary) carbon, giving the tertiary cation that bromide traps to form 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. The 2-bromo-3-methylbutane places Br on a secondary carbon (anti-Markovnikov), the vinyl bromide retains the double bond, and the last option is a rearranged skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (CCC(C)(C)Br) correct Markovnikov tertiary product; single answer; visual. Note option [2] vinyl bromide is C4H7Br (loses a carbon count vs substrate) but is clearly an implausible retained-alkene distractor, not a balance error in the keyed answer."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HCl. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(C)(Cl)C(C)(C)Cl","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","ClCCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Protonation gives a secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift creates the more stable tertiary cation, which captures chloride to give 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane. The non-rearranged Markovnikov secondary chloride (3-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane) is the classic trap; the primary chloride is anti-Markovnikov, and the dichloride adds an extra halogen not present.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane correct after methyl shift; two-concept (Markovnikov + rearrangement) in scope; non-rearranged secondary chloride is the strong distractor; single answer; visual."},{"q":"The cation drawn below is an intermediate in an SN1/E1 reaction. How many resonance structures (including the one shown) contribute significant character to this carbocation?","q_smiles":"[CH2+]c1ccccc1","opts":["4","1 (the cation is not resonance-stabilized)","5","3","2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Benzyl cation: charge delocalizes into the ring. Significant contributors = 1 drawn benzylic form + 2 ortho + 1 para = 4. Meta positions cannot bear the charge. Answer is 4, which is option index 3.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"MIS-KEYED: keyed index 4 selects '5', but benzyl cation has 4 resonance structures (1 benzylic + 2 ortho + 1 para). The 'why' even reasons to 4 yet selects '5'. Corrected correct to 3. The original 'why' was also garbled (stray '? Note:'); chemistry recoverable, so kept."},{"q":"Which alkyl halide ionizes fastest (forms the most stable carbocation) under SN1 conditions? Compare the carbocation each would generate.","q_smiles":"C[C+](C)C","opts":["A vinyl halide forming a vinyl cation","A methyl halide forming a methyl cation","A primary halide forming a primary, non-resonance-stabilized cation","A secondary halide forming an unconjugated secondary cation","A tertiary halide forming the tertiary cation drawn"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Carbocation stability: 3>2>1>methyl, and vinyl cations (sp carbon) are especially unstable. The drawn tert-butyl cation is tertiary, most stabilized by hyperconjugation and induction, so its precursor ionizes fastest in SN1.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 2). Valid SMILES, single concept, clean."},{"q":"The species below is a conjugate base produced by deprotonating a neutral acid. The negative charge is delocalized. What term best describes why this anion is unusually stable?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)[O-]","opts":["Hyperconjugation from adjacent C-H bonds only","Aromaticity of a 6-electron ring","sp3 hybridization of the charged atom","An intramolecular hydrogen bond","Resonance delocalization of the charge equally over two equivalent oxygen atoms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acetate, the conjugate base of acetic acid, delocalizes the negative charge over two equivalent electronegative oxygens via resonance (two identical major contributors), the dominant stabilizing factor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Two phenols are compared. The compound drawn is para-nitrophenol. Relative to plain phenol, is para-nitrophenol a stronger or weaker acid, and why?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Stronger; only because of the inductive effect, with no resonance involvement","Same acidity; the nitro group is too far away to matter","Weaker; the nitro group donates electron density by resonance","Stronger; the para-nitro group withdraws electron density and resonance-stabilizes the phenoxide","Weaker; nitro increases electron density on oxygen by induction"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A para-nitro group is a strong EWG. In the phenoxide conjugate base, the negative charge delocalizes onto the nitro oxygens, spreading charge and stabilizing the anion; a more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid, so p-nitrophenol is far more acidic than phenol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES (proper nitro group). Two-concept (resonance + acidity) within scope."},{"q":"The molecule shown is allyl chloride. Classify the type of carbocation formed when the C-Cl bond ionizes, and state the key consequence for product distribution.","q_smiles":"C=CCCl","opts":["A resonance-stabilized allylic cation; two possible sites of nucleophilic attack (allylic products)","An antiaromatic cation; the reaction does not proceed","An aromatic cation; no reaction at the terminal carbon","A tertiary cation; rearrangement to a secondary cation","A localized primary cation; one product only"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Ionization of allyl chloride gives the resonance-stabilized allyl cation (charge shared between C1 and C3). A nucleophile can attack either terminus, giving allylic/regioisomeric products. This delocalization is why a formally primary allylic halide ionizes readily.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Rank the acidity of the labeled O-H compounds: the carboxylic acid drawn (acetic acid), phenol, and ethanol. Which ordering of acid strength (strongest first) is correct, based on conjugate-base stability?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts":["carboxylic acid > phenol > ethanol","carboxylic acid > ethanol > phenol","phenol > carboxylic acid > ethanol","ethanol > phenol > carboxylic acid","ethanol > carboxylic acid > phenol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Conjugate-base stability sets acidity: acetate (charge over two equivalent O, pKa ~5) > phenoxide (delocalized onto ring carbons, pKa ~10) > ethoxide (no resonance, pKa ~16). So carboxylic acid > phenol > ethanol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 2). Valid SMILES. Standard pKa ranking, in scope."},{"q":"The radical shown is the allyl radical (CH2=CH-CH2 with the radical on the terminal carbon). Compared with a simple n-propyl (primary, non-allylic) radical, the allyl radical is:","q_smiles":"[CH2]C=C","opts":["Less stable, because radicals are destabilized by adjacent pi bonds","Equal in stability, because radicals are not affected by resonance","More stable, because the unpaired electron is delocalized over two carbons by resonance","Less stable, because it is antiaromatic","More stable, but only due to hyperconjugation, with no resonance contribution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The allyl radical is resonance-stabilized: the unpaired electron is delocalized between C1 and C3 (two equivalent contributors), making it substantially more stable than an isolated primary radical.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 2). Valid radical SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with one equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives an intermediate cation, and the products are a mix of 1,2- and 1,4-addition. What feature of the intermediate explains the formation of BOTH products, and which product dominates under thermodynamic control?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["An aromatic cation; the 1,4-product dominates because it is aromatic","Two separate non-resonance cations; products form in a fixed 1:1 ratio regardless of conditions","A primary cation; the 1,2-product dominates because it is more stable","A resonance-stabilized allylic cation with charge on two carbons; the 1,4-product (more substituted, conjugated-friendly internal alkene) dominates under thermodynamic control","A localized secondary cation; only the 1,2-product forms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of butadiene gives an allylic cation with charge delocalized over C2 and C4. Bromide attacks either carbon (1,2 vs 1,4). Under thermodynamic control the 1,4-product dominates because it has the more substituted, more stable internal double bond. Classic resonance-intermediate / conjugate-addition item.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES. Kinetic vs thermodynamic control of butadiene+HBr is standard Orgo I / high-frequency DAT; single resonance-intermediate concept, in scope as the batch hard item."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"3-Chloro-1-butene (drawn) is solvolyzed in water (SN1). The intermediate carbocation is resonance-delocalized over two carbons. Besides 3-buten-2-ol, which constitutional isomer is also formed because of this allylic resonance?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCCO","CCCC=O","CC=CCO","C=C(O)CC","C=CC(C)O"],"correct":2,"why":"3-Chloro-1-butene ionizes to the secondary allylic cation CH3-CH(+)...CH=CH2 (charge on C2 and C4). Water at C2 gives 3-buten-2-ol (C=CC(C)O); water at the resonance terminus C4 gives the constitutional isomer 2-buten-1-ol (CC=CCO), which is the resonance-derived extra product asked for. C=C(O)CC is an enol of a different skeleton, CCCC=O is butanal (no allylic relation), and C=CCCO (3-buten-1-ol) is not reachable from this cation. All five are C4H8O isomers, so distractors are plausible-but-wrong. Keyed index 0 is correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: stem and all 5 option SMILES valid and isomeric (C4H8O); allylic resonance gives exactly 2-buten-1-ol (opt0) as the extra product. Correct=0 confirmed. Two-concept (resonance+regio), in scope."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"The benzylic cation is drawn. Rank the following carbocations from MOST stable to LEAST stable: benzyl, allyl, tert-butyl (3 degree), ethyl (1 degree).","q_smiles":"[CH2+]c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["tert-butyl > benzyl > allyl > ethyl","benzyl > allyl > tert-butyl > ethyl","ethyl > allyl > benzyl > tert-butyl","benzyl > tert-butyl > allyl > ethyl","allyl > benzyl > tert-butyl > ethyl"],"correct":3,"why":"Benzyl is most stable (resonance over three ring carbons plus inductive stabilization). Tertiary tert-butyl (hyperconjugation + three alkyl groups) outranks the unsubstituted allyl cation (only two-carbon delocalization, no alkyl donors). Ethyl (1 degree) is least stable. Standard textbook ordering: benzyl > tert-butyl > allyl > ethyl. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem SMILES valid. Standard DAT/sophomore ranking; one defensible answer (benzyl>tBu>allyl>ethyl). Recognition-level ranking, in scope. Correct=0 confirmed."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Which of the following carbocations is the MOST stable?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)[CH+]C","CC(C)(C)[CH2+]","[CH2+]C=C","C[CH2+]","[CH2+]c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Benzyl cation [CH2+]c1ccccc1 is delocalized into the aromatic ring (multiple resonance forms), the most stable. Distractors: neopentyl 1 degree (no resonance), secondary, ethyl 1 degree, and allyl 1 degree (only two-carbon resonance, no alkyl substituents). Aromatic resonance beats all. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All 5 option SMILES valid carbocations. q_smiles is empty but the molecules are drawn AS the options (structure-ID), so visual requirement met. Correct=0 confirmed; one clear answer."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Ethylbenzene (drawn) is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) and a radical initiator (heat/peroxide). The reaction proceeds through the most stabilized radical intermediate. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":2,"why":"NBS/radical conditions abstract the benzylic H to give the resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, so Br ends up on the benzylic carbon: 1-bromo-1-phenylethane (CC(Br)c1ccccc1). BrCCc1ccccc1 is the unfavored primary position; ring-brominated products require EAS (wrong conditions); gem-dibromide is over-bromination. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem and all options valid. Benzylic radical bromination is canonical; correct=0 confirmed. Single concept, in scope, properly visual with structure distractors (regio + wrong-conditions)."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"para-Nitrophenol is drawn. Considering resonance stabilization of the conjugate base (phenoxide), rank these acids from STRONGEST to WEAKEST: para-nitrophenol, phenol, para-methylphenol (p-cresol), cyclohexanol.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["phenol > p-nitrophenol > p-cresol > cyclohexanol","cyclohexanol > phenol > p-cresol > p-nitrophenol","p-cresol > phenol > p-nitrophenol > cyclohexanol","p-nitrophenol > cyclohexanol > phenol > p-cresol","p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol > cyclohexanol"],"correct":4,"why":"Strongest acid = most stabilized conjugate base. p-Nitrophenoxide delocalizes charge onto the nitro oxygens (pKa ~7.1), strongest. Phenol (pKa ~10) ring-resonance only. p-Cresol slightly weaker (pKa ~10.3, methyl donates). Cyclohexanol (sp3 alkoxide, no resonance, pKa ~16) weakest. Order matches keyed index 0.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem p-nitrophenol SMILES valid. Acidity ranking is qualitative (resonance, not pKa math), in scope. Correct=0 confirmed; one defensible order."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Pentane-2,4-dione (acetylacetone) is drawn. Which proton in this molecule is the MOST acidic, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The central CH2 protons, because they are farthest from oxygen","The terminal CH3 protons, because methyl carbanions are most stable","A terminal CH3 proton, because its carbanion is aromatic","All C-H protons are equally acidic since the molecule is symmetric","The central CH2 protons, because the resulting carbanion is delocalized onto BOTH carbonyl oxygens"],"correct":4,"why":"Deprotonating the central CH2 gives an enolate flanked by two carbonyls; charge delocalizes onto both carbonyl oxygens, making beta-diketones unusually acidic (pKa ~9). Terminal CH3 enolates are stabilized by only one carbonyl, far less acidic. Keyed index 0 correct; other options give wrong reason or wrong site.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem acetylacetone SMILES valid (CalcMolFormula C5H8O2). Classic doubly-stabilized enolate; correct=0 confirmed. Single concept, in scope, molecule drawn in stem."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with 1 equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation, and under thermodynamic control the 1,4-addition product dominates. Which structure is that major 1,4-product (note its alkene geometry)?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C/CBr","CCC(Br)C","C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C\\CBr","C=CCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives allylic cation CH3-CH(+)...CH=CH2; bromide capture at the 1,4-terminus gives 1-bromo-2-butene, with the more stable (E) alkene under thermodynamic control: C/C=C/CBr. (Z) is wrong geometry; C=CC(C)Br is the kinetic 1,2-product; CCC(C)Br (2-bromobutane) is wrong skeleton; C=CCCBr (4-bromo-1-butene) is anti-Markovnikov. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem and all options valid; opts0/1 carry explicit E vs Z stereo. Thermodynamic 1,4 E-product = opt0 confirmed. Two-concept (resonance/1,4 regio + stereo), in scope, properly visual."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Acetamide is drawn. Which statement correctly describes the second major resonance contributor of an amide (the one explaining restricted C-N rotation)?","q_smiles":"CC(N)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A C-H bond breaks to place negative charge on carbon","Oxygen donates a lone pair to nitrogen across space, forming an O-N bond","The C=O pi bond shifts onto carbon, giving a carbanion at nitrogen","The nitrogen lone pair shifts into the C-N bond, giving C=N+ with a negative charge (lone pair) on oxygen","The nitrogen lone pair leaves the molecule entirely, giving a free carbene"],"correct":3,"why":"The key amide resonance form pushes the N lone pair into the C-N bond (C=N+) while C=O pi electrons move onto O (O-), giving partial C-N double-bond character that restricts rotation and makes amides planar. Other choices break sigma bonds, violate the octet, or invent through-space bonds, so they are not valid resonance contributors. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem acetamide SMILES valid. Amide resonance/restricted rotation is core sophomore content (no FMO/MO-level reasoning). Correct=0 confirmed; one valid contributor described."},{"q":"The molecule shown is one of four compounds. Using the anchor pKa ladder, which of the following is the MOST acidic? CH3COOH (acetic acid), C6H5OH (phenol), CH3CH2OH (ethanol), H2O (water).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts":["CH3CH2OH (ethanol)","All four are equally acidic","C6H5OH (phenol)","CH3COOH (acetic acid)","H2O (water)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Anchor ladder: carboxylic acid pKa ~4-5 < phenol pKa ~10 < water pKa ~15.7 < alcohol pKa ~16-18. Acetic acid is most acidic; its carboxylate conjugate base is resonance-stabilized over two equivalent oxygens.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (acetic acid); correct answer = acetic acid (index 0); standard anchor-ladder ranking, single concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"The compound shown is para-nitrophenol. Compared to plain phenol (pKa ~10), how does the para-NO2 group affect acidity, and why?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["More acidic; the electron-withdrawing NO2 stabilizes the phenoxide conjugate base by resonance/induction","Less acidic; NO2 increases the s-character of the O-H bond","Less acidic; NO2 destabilizes the phenoxide by adding electron density","Unchanged; substituents on the ring do not affect O-H acidity","More acidic; NO2 is an electron-donating group that delocalizes the O-H bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"A para-nitro group is strongly electron-withdrawing (resonance + induction), delocalizing and stabilizing the phenoxide conjugate base, so p-nitrophenol (pKa ~7) is more acidic than phenol (pKa ~10).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (p-nitrophenol); correct answer = more acidic via EWG stabilization (index 0); classic substituent-effect item, single concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Two carboxylic acids are compared: 2-chlorobutanoic acid (drawn) and unsubstituted butanoic acid (CH3CH2CH2COOH). Which is the stronger acid, and what is the controlling effect?","q_smiles":"CCC(Cl)C(=O)O","opts":["2-Chlorobutanoic acid; Cl donates electron density by resonance into the carboxylate","Butanoic acid; the alkyl chain stabilizes the carboxylate by hyperconjugation","Butanoic acid; Cl raises the pKa by withdrawing electrons from the O-H","They are equally acidic because both are carboxylic acids","2-Chlorobutanoic acid; the electronegative Cl inductively stabilizes the carboxylate conjugate base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The alpha-chloro substituent withdraws electron density inductively, stabilizing the carboxylate, so 2-chlorobutanoic acid (pKa ~2.8) is stronger than butanoic acid (pKa ~4.8).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (2-chlorobutanoic acid); correct answer = 2-Cl acid via inductive stabilization (index 0); standard inductive-effect item, single concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"For pentane-2,4-dione (shown), identify the MOST acidic proton and the reason it is unusually acidic (pKa ~9).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["A terminal CH3 proton; removing it gives a stable primary carbanion","A terminal CH3 proton; resonance with one carbonyl stabilizes the anion","There is no especially acidic proton; this compound is a hydrocarbon","The central CH2 (alpha) protons; the resulting carbanion is resonance-delocalized onto BOTH flanking carbonyl oxygens","The central CH2 protons; the carbanion is stabilized only by hybridization, not resonance"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The 1,3-dicarbonyl central CH2 (between two C=O) is most acidic (pKa ~9); its enolate is doubly resonance-stabilized over two carbonyl oxygens, far below a simple ketone alpha-proton (~20).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (acetylacetone); correct answer = central CH2 doubly resonance-stabilized (index 0); active-methylene concept, in scope. Note distractor opt2 names the right proton but wrong reason, still clearly inferior. No issue."},{"q":"Propyne (CH3-C#CH) is shown. Rank the acidity of its terminal/relevant C-H against propene (CH3-CH=CH2) and propane (CH3CH2CH3). Which is most acidic and why?","q_smiles":"C#CC","opts":["All three are equally acidic since all are C-H bonds","Propene; the sp2 C-H is most acidic due to resonance with the double bond","Propyne; the sp C-H is most acidic because of resonance into the triple bond","Propyne; the terminal sp C-H gives an sp carbanion that holds the lone pair in a high s-character (50%) orbital close to the nucleus","Propane; sp3 C-H is most acidic because of maximal hyperconjugation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Hybridization controls the ladder: sp C-H (~50% s, pKa ~25) < sp2 (~44) < sp3 (~50). Propyne's terminal sp C-H is most acidic; the effect is s-character/orbital, not resonance.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (propyne); correct answer = terminal sp C-H via s-character (index 0); hybridization-acidity ladder, in scope. Distractor opt4 (right compound, wrong 'resonance' reason) is a good trap. No issue."},{"q":"Ethanethiol (CH3CH2SH, shown) is compared with ethanol (CH3CH2OH). Which is the stronger acid, and which CARDIO factor explains the trend down a group?","q_smiles":"CCS","opts":["Ethanethiol; S is larger than O, so the conjugate base RS- spreads the charge over a bigger, more polarizable atom","Ethanol; O is more electronegative, so RO- is always the more stable conjugate base","They are equal because both are simple two-carbon compounds","Ethanol; the O-H bond is weaker than the S-H bond, making it more acidic","Ethanethiol; sulfur's lone pair is stabilized by resonance into the alkyl chain"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Down a column, atom size beats electronegativity: thiolate (larger, more polarizable S) is more stable than alkoxide, so ethanethiol (pKa ~10.6) is more acidic than ethanol (pKa ~16).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethanethiol); correct answer = thiol stronger via atom size/polarizability (index 0); size-down-a-group concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate (a beta-keto ester, shown). Which proton set is the MOST acidic, and what is the dominant stabilization of its conjugate base?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["The terminal CH3 of the acetyl group; it gives the most stable primary carbanion","The CH2 between the ketone and the ester carbonyls; its enolate is resonance-delocalized onto both carbonyl groups","The OCH2 protons of the ethyl ester group; oxygen induction makes them most acidic","The CH3 of the ethyl group; hyperconjugation stabilizes that carbanion best","The compound has no acidic proton because esters are not acidic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In a beta-keto ester the methylene flanked by ketone and ester C=O is most acidic (pKa ~11); the enolate charge delocalizes onto both carbonyl oxygens (acetoacetic ester logic).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethyl acetoacetate); correct answer = central CH2 between the two carbonyls (index 0); active-methylene concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Three protonated nitrogen acids are compared: methylammonium (CH3NH3+, shown), anilinium (C6H5NH3+), and pyridinium (C5H5NH+). Which is the strongest acid (lowest pKa), reflecting the WEAKEST/most-stabilized conjugate base?","q_smiles":"C[NH3+]","opts":["Methylammonium; the alkyl group makes methylamine the weakest base and the cation the strongest acid","Pyridinium; the aromatic ring makes pyridine a stronger base than methylamine","All three have identical pKa because each loses an N-H proton","Methylammonium; a primary amine cation is always the most acidic","Anilinium; its conjugate base aniline has the N lone pair delocalized into the benzene ring, making aniline a weak base and anilinium the strongest acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Aniline is the weakest base (N lone pair delocalized into the ring), so its conjugate acid anilinium (pKa ~4.6) is the strongest acid, below pyridinium (~5.2) and methylammonium (~10.6).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (methylammonium); correct answer = anilinium strongest acid via aniline being weakest base (index 0); conjugate-acid/base + resonance reasoning, layered but DAT-recognition depth, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Rank the following from MOST to LEAST acidic: trifluoroacetic acid (drawn), acetic acid, formic acid, and 2,2,2-trichloroacetic acid.","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C(F)(F)F","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["CF3COOH > CCl3COOH > HCOOH > CH3COOH","CH3COOH > HCOOH > CCl3COOH > CF3COOH","HCOOH > CH3COOH > CF3COOH > CCl3COOH","CCl3COOH > CF3COOH > CH3COOH > HCOOH","CF3COOH > HCOOH > CCl3COOH > CH3COOH"],"correct":0,"why":"Acidity tracks conjugate-base stabilization by inductive electron withdrawal. Three F (more electronegative) withdraw more strongly than three Cl, so CF3COOH (pKa ~0.2) > CCl3COOH (~0.66). With no EWG, formic acid (~3.75) beats acetic acid (~4.76) because the alkyl group weakly destabilizes the carboxylate. Order: CF3 > CCl3 > HCOOH > CH3COOH.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All standard pKa values: CF3COOH ~0.23, CCl3COOH ~0.66, HCOOH ~3.75, CH3COOH ~4.76. SMILES valid, single concept (inductive), one defensible answer."},{"q":"Each structure below is a substituted phenol. Which one is the MOST acidic (lowest pKa)? The parent phenol is drawn in the stem for reference.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(O)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(O)cc1","Nc1ccc(O)cc1","COc1ccc(O)cc1","Oc1ccc(F)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"A phenol is more acidic when its phenoxide is better stabilized. A para-nitro group is a strong resonance + inductive EWG: the negative charge delocalizes directly onto the nitro oxygens (p-nitrophenol pKa ~7.1). p-OMe, p-CH3, and p-NH2 are electron-donating (higher pKa); p-F is only a weak inductive withdrawer with no through-resonance acceptor. The nitro phenol wins.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). p-nitrophenol pKa ~7.15 clearly most acidic; donors raise pKa; p-F weak inductive only. All 5 option SMILES valid and distinct. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Phenol is drawn in the stem. Which compound has the LEAST acidic O-H or C-H bond among phenol, cyclohexanol, acetic acid, and water?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["All four have identical acidity","Acetic acid (pKa ~4.76)","Phenol (pKa ~10)","Cyclohexanol (pKa ~16-18)","Water (pKa ~15.7)"],"correct":3,"why":"Compare conjugate-base stability. Acetate is resonance-delocalized (most acidic), phenoxide spreads charge into the ring (next), hydroxide and cyclohexyl alkoxide both localize charge on one O, but the electron-donating cyclohexyl destabilizes the alkoxide relative to hydroxide. So cyclohexanol is least acidic (highest pKa).","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Cyclohexanol ~16-18 > water 15.7 > phenol ~10 > AcOH ~4.76, so cyclohexanol is least acidic. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Malononitrile is drawn in the stem. Which of the following has the MOST acidic C-H bond?","q_smiles":"N#CCC#N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Propane, CH3-CH2-CH3 (pKa ~50)","Ethene, CH2=CH2 (pKa ~44)","Toluene, C6H5-CH3 (pKa ~43)","Malononitrile, NC-CH2-CN (pKa ~11)","Acetonitrile, CH3-CN (pKa ~25)"],"correct":3,"why":"Malononitrile's central proton sits between two nitriles; its carbanion is resonance-delocalized onto both cyano nitrogens plus doubly inductively withdrawn (pKa ~11). A single nitrile (acetonitrile) gives only one stabilizing group (~25); propane, ethene, toluene give far less stabilized anions. Double cyano stabilization makes it by far the most acidic.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Malononitrile ~11 clearly lowest among ~25/~43/~44/~50. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"1,3-Cyclopentadiene is drawn in the stem. Rank these C-H bonds from MOST to LEAST acidic: 1,3-cyclopentadiene (sp3 CH2), cyclopentane, cyclopentene (allylic CH2), and propene (allylic CH3).","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["cyclopentene > 1,3-cyclopentadiene > propene > cyclopentane","cyclopentane > cyclopentene > propene > 1,3-cyclopentadiene","propene > 1,3-cyclopentadiene > cyclopentane > cyclopentene","1,3-cyclopentadiene = cyclopentene = propene > cyclopentane","1,3-cyclopentadiene > propene (allylic) > cyclopentene (allylic) > cyclopentane"],"correct":4,"why":"Deprotonating cyclopentadiene's sp3 CH2 gives the aromatic cyclopentadienyl anion (6 pi electrons), making it shockingly acidic (pKa ~16), far above ordinary allylic systems (~43). Cyclopentane gives an unstabilized sp3 carbanion (~50, least acidic). Aromaticity of the conjugate base is the deciding factor.","difficulty":3,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Borderline but keepable. Endpoints are unambiguous (cyclopentadiene most acidic via aromatic anion; cyclopentane least), and only option a places both endpoints correctly; option e is chemically wrong (cyclopentadiene NOT equal to allylic). The internal propene(~43) vs cyclopentene(~44) ranking is weak/essentially a tie, but it does not create a second selectable answer. One defensible answer by elimination."},{"q":"Propargyl alcohol (prop-2-yn-1-ol) is drawn in the stem. Which structure is the conjugate base formed by removing its MOST acidic proton?","q_smiles":"C#CCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C#CC[O-]","C#C[CH-]O","C#C[CH2-]","[C-]#CCO","[CH-]=C=CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Two candidates compete: the O-H and the terminal alkyne C-H. The O-H (pKa ~16) is more acidic than a terminal sp alkyne C-H (~25) because oxygen holds negative charge far better than carbon. So the most acidic proton is the O-H, giving the alkoxide. The acetylide, propargylic carbanion, allenyl anion, and the oxygen-less option are all higher-energy / wrong species.","difficulty":2,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Alcohol O-H pKa ~16 < terminal alkyne C-H ~25, so alkoxide is correct. All 5 option SMILES valid and distinct (e drops oxygen, a wrong skeleton). One defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 2-methyl-2-butene is treated with HBr.","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C(C)Br","CC=C(C)CBr","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"HBr adds by Markovnikov: proton to the less-substituted alkene carbon, generating the tertiary carbocation; Br bonds there to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (verified Br-bearing carbon has 3 carbon neighbors). Alkene carbons go sp2 to sp3. B = anti-Markov secondary, C = primary, D = allylic, E = wrong nucleophile. Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; Markovnikov tertiary product correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when propene is treated with HBr in the presence of a peroxide (ROOR).","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCO","C=CCBr","CC(C)Br","CC(Br)CBr","CCCBr"],"correct":4,"why":"Peroxides give radical anti-Markovnikov addition: Br radical adds to the terminal carbon (more stable secondary radical), placing Br on the less-substituted carbon to give 1-bromopropane. Alkene carbons go sp2 to sp3. B = Markovnikov ionic, C = dibromide, D = wrong nucleophile, E = allylic bromide. Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; anti-Markovnikov product correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when cyclohexene reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2.","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1(Br)CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1Br","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Br","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Bromonium ion opened by anti backside attack gives stereospecific anti addition: trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane. 3D embedding confirms option A places the two Br on opposite faces (trans, S,S of the d/l pair); option B is cis (meso). Ring carbons go sp2 to sp3. C = monobromide, D = gem-dibromide, E = bromohydrin (wrong reagent). Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"3D check confirms A=trans, B=cis; anti-addition stereochem correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 1-methylcyclohexene is treated with dilute H2SO4 in water (H3O+).","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1O","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","OCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration follows Markovnikov: protonation gives the tertiary carbocation at the methyl-bearing carbon, and water adds there to give 1-methylcyclohexanol. Reacting carbons go sp2 to sp3. B = anti-Markov secondary alcohol, C = wrong nucleophile, D = rearranged primary alcohol, E = allylic alcohol with alkene retained. Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; Markovnikov tertiary alcohol correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when trans-2-butene reacts with Br2.","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CC","CCC(Br)CBr","CC(Br)C(Br)C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Anti addition via bromonium ion: trans-2-butene gives the meso (2R,3S) 2,3-dibromobutane. RDKit CIP confirms option A is (R,S) = meso (achiral), while option B is (S,S) = the chiral d/l product expected from cis-2-butene. Alkene carbons go sp2 to sp3. C ignores stereochem, D wrong skeleton (1,2-dibromobutane), E monobromide. Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"CIP confirms A=meso(R,S), B=(S,S); stereospecific anti-addition correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 1-butyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HBr.","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(Br)Br","CCC(Br)=CBr","CC(Br)C(Br)CC","CCC(Br)CBr","CCC(C)(Br)Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Two Markovnikov additions place both Br on the same (more-substituted) carbon, giving the geminal dihalide 2,2-dibromobutane. The terminal carbon goes sp to sp2 (after first addition) to sp3 (after second). B = anti-Markov gem-dihalide, C = vicinal dihalide, D = wrong skeleton, E = single-addition vinyl dibromide. Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; double-Markovnikov gem-dibromide correct; sp/sp2/sp3 progression accurate; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with HBr.","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov is reinforced by resonance: protonating the terminal CH2 gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic cation, so Br bonds at the benzylic carbon to give (1-bromoethyl)benzene. Vinyl carbons go sp2 to sp3; the ring stays sp2 aromatic. B = anti-Markov isomer, C = ring EAS bromination, D = wrong nucleophile, E = dibromide. Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; benzylic Markovnikov product correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2.","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1(C)Br","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@]1(C)Br","C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@]1(C)Br","C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@@]1(C)Br","CC1(C)C(Br)(Br)CCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Bromonium ion opened by anti backside attack puts the two C-Br bonds trans across the ring: trans-1,2-dibromo-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane. 3D embedding confirms option A is trans (Br on opposite faces), option B is cis (syn, not observed). Each alkene carbon becomes a fully substituted sp3 stereocenter. C ignores stereochem, D = gem-dibromide rearranged skeleton, E = bromohydrin (wrong reagent). Keyed answer A confirmed correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"3D check confirms A=trans, B=cis; anti-addition stereochem correct; single alkene/single concept+stereo; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"In the molecule drawn (acetonitrile, CH3-C#N), what is the hybridization of the nitrogen atom and the approximate C-C#N bond angle around the nitrile carbon?","q_smiles":"CC#N","opts":["sp2, 180 degrees","sp, 120 degrees","sp2, 120 degrees","sp3, 109.5 degrees","sp, 180 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The nitrile carbon and nitrogen each have two electron domains (one sigma + the triple bond), so both are sp hybridized and the C-C#N unit is linear at 180 degrees. Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; sp/180 verified."},{"q":"For the allene drawn (penta-2,3-diene, CH3-CH=C=CH-CH3), what is the hybridization of the central carbon (the sp carbon bearing the two cumulated double bonds)?","q_smiles":"CC=C=CC","opts":["sp, and the whole molecule is planar","sp, and the two CH3-bearing ends lie in perpendicular planes","sp2, and the two ends lie in perpendicular planes","sp3, tetrahedral","sp2, and the whole molecule is planar"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The central allene carbon has two electron domains (two cumulated double bonds), so it is sp/linear, and its two orthogonal p orbitals force the terminal sp2 groups into perpendicular planes. SMILES CC=C=CC is penta-2,3-diene. Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; classic allene geometry verified."},{"q":"In acetamide (drawn: CH3-C(=O)-NH2), what is the hybridization of the nitrogen atom, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(N)=O","opts":["sp3, because nitrogen has three bonds and one lone pair like a typical amine","sp3, because the C-N bond is a single bond","sp2, because the lone pair is delocalized into the carbonyl (amide resonance), making N planar","sp2, because nitrogen forms a double bond to carbon in the major resonance form","sp, because nitrogen is adjacent to a carbonyl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Amide nitrogen is sp2/planar because its lone pair conjugates into the C=O pi system (amide resonance), giving restricted rotation. Distractor 4 reaches the same label but via the wrong reason (no formal N=C double bond in the major form). Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; only one fully right option (right answer + right reason)."},{"q":"For acetone (drawn: (CH3)2C=O), identify the hybridization of the carbonyl carbon and its approximate C-C-C / C-C=O bond angles.","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["sp, with bond angles of about 180 degrees","sp2, with bond angles of about 120 degrees","sp2, with bond angles of about 109.5 degrees","sp3, with bond angles of about 120 degrees","sp3, with bond angles of about 109.5 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Carbonyl carbon has three electron domains (two methyls + one C=O), so it is sp2/trigonal planar near 120 degrees. RDKit confirms carbonyl C is SP2. Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; sp2/120 verified."},{"q":"Consider propyne, CH3-C#CH (drawn). How many sp-hybridized carbons does this molecule contain, and what is the H-C#C bond angle at the terminal alkyne carbon?","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts":["Two sp carbons; 180 degrees","One sp carbon; 180 degrees","Two sp carbons; 120 degrees","One sp carbon; 120 degrees","Three sp carbons; 109.5 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Both triple-bond carbons are sp (RDKit: CSP3, CSP, CSP), so two sp carbons; the alkyne is linear at 180 degrees. SMILES CC#C is propyne with implicit terminal H. Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; two sp carbons / 180 verified."},{"q":"Carbon dioxide is drawn (O=C=O). What is the hybridization of the central carbon and the molecular geometry / O-C-O bond angle?","q_smiles":"O=C=O","opts":["sp, bent, 120 degrees","sp2, trigonal planar, 120 degrees","sp2, bent, 120 degrees","sp, linear, 180 degrees","sp3, bent, 109.5 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Central carbon has two electron domains, no lone pairs, so it is sp/linear with O-C-O at 180 degrees. Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; sp/linear/180 verified."},{"q":"Compare the nitrogen in the enamine drawn (ethenamine, CH2=CH-NH2) with the nitrogen of a simple amine such as ethylamine. Which statement correctly describes the enamine nitrogen?","q_smiles":"C=CN","opts":["The enamine N is closer to sp2 (more planar) because its lone pair conjugates with the C=C pi bond","The enamine N is sp hybridized and linear","The enamine N is sp3 but pyramidal inversion is impossible","The enamine N is sp2 only because it forms a double bond to carbon","The enamine N is sp3 and identical in geometry to an ordinary amine nitrogen"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Enamine N flattens toward sp2 because its lone pair conjugates into the adjacent C=C pi system, making the beta-carbon nucleophilic. Distractor 4 gives the wrong reason (no formal N=C double bond). Keyed answer index 0 is correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; conjugation rationale is the only fully right option."},{"q":"Methyl isocyanate is drawn (CH3-N=C=O). Going left to right, give the hybridization of the nitrogen, the central carbon, and the oxygen, and the geometry at the central carbon.","q_smiles":"CN=C=O","opts":["N sp, central C sp (linear), O sp","N sp3, central C sp (linear), O sp2","N sp2, central C sp2 (trigonal planar), O sp2","N sp3, central C sp2 (bent), O sp3","N sp2, central C sp (linear), O sp2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit confirms N SP2 (single bond + double bond + lone pair = 3 domains, bent), central C SP (two cumulated double bonds, linear), O SP2. Keyed answer index 0 is correct. Layered three-atom recognition but each is single-concept VSEPR with no mechanism/math, so it stays in DAT scope at difficulty 3.","difficulty":3,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; N sp2 / C sp / O sp2 verified by RDKit."},{"q":"Ketene (drawn below) has the connectivity H2C=C=O. What is the hybridization of the CENTRAL carbon atom, and what is the approximate H-C-C bond angle at the terminal (CH2) carbon?","q_smiles":"C=C=O","opts":["Central C is sp; terminal CH2 angle ~120 degrees","Central C is sp; terminal CH2 angle ~109.5 degrees","Central C is sp3; terminal CH2 angle ~109.5 degrees","Central C is sp2; terminal CH2 angle ~180 degrees","Central C is sp2; terminal CH2 angle ~120 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Central carbon of ketene bears two cumulated double bonds (C=CH2 and C=O) = two electron regions = sp, linear. The terminal CH2 has one C=C plus two C-H bonds = three regions = sp2, ~120 degree H-C-C angle. Distractors swap centers or impose tetrahedral angle.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, in scope, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"For acrolein (propenal, drawn below), how many carbon atoms are sp2 hybridized, and what is the approximate O=C-H bond angle at the aldehyde carbon?","q_smiles":"C=CC=O","opts":["All 3 carbons are sp2; O=C-H angle ~120 degrees","1 carbon is sp, 2 are sp2; O=C-H angle ~120 degrees","2 carbons are sp2, 1 is sp; O=C-H angle ~120 degrees","2 carbons are sp2, 1 is sp3; O=C-H angle ~109.5 degrees","All 3 carbons are sp2; O=C-H angle ~180 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acrolein is CH2=CH-CHO. Both vinyl carbons have a C=C (sp2) and the carbonyl carbon has a C=O plus two sigma bonds (sp2). All three carbons sp2, all ~120 degrees including O=C-H. No sp or sp3 carbons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, single-concept recognition, in scope."},{"q":"Allene (propa-1,2-diene, drawn below) is H2C=C=CH2. Which statement correctly describes the hybridization of its carbons AND the geometric relationship of its two CH2 groups?","q_smiles":"C=C=C","opts":["All three carbons are sp, and the molecule is fully linear and planar","All three carbons are sp2, and the two CH2 groups lie in the same plane","Central C is sp (linear), terminal carbons are sp2, and the two CH2 planes are perpendicular to each other","Central C is sp2, terminal carbons are sp3, and the two CH2 planes are perpendicular","Central C is sp (linear), terminal carbons are sp2, and the two CH2 groups lie in the same plane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Central carbon has two cumulated double bonds = sp, linear. Each terminal carbon has one C=C plus two C-H = sp2. The two perpendicular p orbitals on the sp central carbon force the terminal CH2 planes 90 degrees apart. Trap (option 4) keeps correct hybridization but wrongly makes ends coplanar.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Classic allene geometry, DAT-appropriate, well-constructed coplanar trap."},{"q":"In pyridine (drawn below), classify the ring nitrogen's hybridization and describe where its lone pair resides.","q_smiles":"c1ccncc1","opts":["sp2; the lone pair occupies the p orbital and IS part of the aromatic pi system","sp3; the lone pair is in an sp3 orbital pointing out of the ring","sp2; the lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and is NOT part of the aromatic pi system","sp; the lone pair lies along the molecular axis","sp2; the nitrogen contributes two electrons to the aromatic sextet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Pyridine nitrogen is sp2 (planar aromatic ring). It contributes ONE electron via its p orbital to the aromatic sextet; its lone pair sits in an in-plane sp2 orbital NOT delocalized into the ring, which is why pyridine is basic. Distractors 3/5 describe pyrrole.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Standard pyridine-vs-pyrrole distinction, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"A student claims the carbonyl carbon of methyl vinyl ketone (but-3-en-2-one, drawn below) is sp3 with ~109.5 degree angles. Which statement is correct?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=O","opts":["Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 but its angles are ~109.5 degrees","Correct; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 but its angles are ~180 degrees","Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 with ~120 degree bond angles","Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp with ~180 degree bond angles","Correct; the carbonyl carbon is sp3 with ~109.5 degree bond angles"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The carbonyl carbon has three sigma regions (vinyl, methyl, plus C=O) = three electron regions = sp2, trigonal planar, ~120 degrees. A double-bonded carbon is never sp3. Option 4 pairs right hybridization with wrong angle.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, single-concept, in scope."},{"q":"The molecule 2-methylbut-1-en-3-yne is drawn below. It contains carbons of three different hybridizations. Which of the following candidate molecules contains an sp-hybridized carbon (as in the drawn molecule's triple bond)?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C#C","opts":["CC=CC","c1ccccc1","C1CCCCC1","CC(C)=O","CC#CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"An sp carbon needs a triple bond or two cumulated double bonds. Only 2-butyne (CC#CC) has a triple bond, so its internal carbons are sp. Cyclohexane is all sp3; acetone carbonyl is sp2; benzene all sp2; 2-butene sp2.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Structure options all valid SMILES; exactly one contains an sp carbon. Visual stem + structure options."},{"q":"Acetamide is drawn below. Which statement best describes the hybridization and geometry of its NITROGEN atom?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)N","opts":["sp2 and roughly trigonal planar, because the N lone pair conjugates with the C=O (amide resonance)","sp2 but bent at ~109.5 degrees, with the lone pair localized on N","sp3 and pyramidal, with bond angles of ~109.5 degrees, like a normal amine","sp and linear, with bond angles of ~180 degrees","sp3 and trigonal planar, because nitrogen always has four electron regions"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"In an amide the N lone pair delocalizes into the carbonyl pi system, so nitrogen adopts sp2 character and a roughly planar (trigonal planar) geometry with ~120 degree angles, explaining restricted rotation and low basicity. A free amine (option 2) is sp3/pyramidal, but conjugation changes that here.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Standard amide-resonance concept, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Aniline (drawn below) has its nitrogen attached directly to a benzene ring. Compared with the nitrogen in a simple sp3 alkylamine, the aniline nitrogen is best described as:","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["sp2 with the nitrogen carbon of the ring becoming sp3","Closer to sp2/planar, because its lone pair partially conjugates with the aromatic ring","Purely sp and linear at nitrogen","sp3 with the lone pair fully delocalized into the ring like pyridine's","Purely sp3 and identical in geometry to a simple alkylamine"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The aniline N lone pair conjugates (donates) into the ring, flattening the nitrogen toward planarity and giving it sp2 character relative to an sp3 amine, which weakens its basicity. It is not sp/linear, and the ipso carbon stays sp2. Distractor 4 confuses aniline (exocyclic donor N) with pyridine (ring N).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Comparative framing ('closer to sp2/partially conjugates') keeps it defensible despite aniline's real slight pyramidalization; DAT-level, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Phenylethanol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. The product's IR spectrum shows a strong band near 1715 cm-1 and no broad absorption in the 3200-3500 cm-1 region. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"PCC oxidizes a secondary alcohol to a ketone (acetophenone) with no over-oxidation. IR: ~1715 ketone C=O, loss of broad O-H. Carboxylic acid (3) would show broad O-H and does not form from a secondary alcohol; aldehydes (1,2) have wrong connectivity; unreacted alcohol (4) keeps O-H. Keyed index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard PCC oxidation; qualitative IR (carbonyl + loss of O-H) in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct; distractors are over-oxidation/wrong skeleton/unreacted."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Butyne is treated with aqueous H2SO4 and HgSO4. The IR of the product shows a strong band near 1715 cm-1 and loss of the absorption near 2150 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC#C","CCCC=O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(C)O","CCC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov hydration of a terminal alkyne gives the methyl ketone (2-butanone) via enol tautomerization. IR: ~1715 C=O appears, ~2150 C#C disappears. Anti-Markovnikov aldehyde (1) is wrong regiochemistry; alcohol (2) shows O-H not C=O; unreacted alkyne (3) keeps 2150. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Canonical Markovnikov alkyne hydration; qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Propanenitrile is refluxed with aqueous H3O+ (excess water, heat). The IR of the product shows a very broad band over 2500-3300 cm-1 plus a strong band near 1710 cm-1, and the sharp absorption near 2250 cm-1 is gone. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#N","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(N)=O","CCC(=O)O","CCC#N","CCCO","CCC=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Complete acidic hydrolysis of a nitrile gives the carboxylic acid (propanoic acid). IR: broad 2500-3300 acid O-H, ~1710 C=O, loss of ~2250 C#N. Amide (partial hydrolysis) shows N-H and lower C=O; aldehyde/alcohol wrong group; unreacted nitrile keeps 2250. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard nitrile hydrolysis to acid; qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with NaBH4 in methanol. The IR of the product shows a strong broad band near 3300 cm-1 and loss of the strong band near 1715 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1O","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 reduces a ketone to a secondary alcohol (cyclohexanol). IR: broad ~3300 O-H, loss of ~1715 C=O. NaBH4 does not reduce to alkene, does not add a second O (no diol from one carbonyl), and unreacted ketone keeps 1715. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard NaBH4 ketone reduction; qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Hexyne is treated with NaNH2, then with CH3I. The IR of the product still shows a weak band near 2200 cm-1 (C#C) but the sharp band near 3300 cm-1 has disappeared. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC#CCCC","CC#CCCCC","C#CCCCC","C=CCCCCC","C#CCCCCC"],"correct":1,"why":"NaNH2 forms the acetylide, which alkylates CH3I (SN2) at the terminal carbon, giving internal 2-heptyne (verified 7 carbons). IR: sharp ~3300 terminal =C-H vanishes; weak C#C remains. Terminal-alkyne options keep 3300; 3-heptyne is wrong regio; alkene has no C#C. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard acetylide alkylation; carbon count correct (heptyne); qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Benzoic acid is treated with SOCl2. The IR of the product shows a strong carbonyl band that has shifted up to about 1790 cm-1, and the very broad 2500-3300 cm-1 absorption is gone. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","ClC(=O)c1ccccc1","ClCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"SOCl2 converts a carboxylic acid to the acyl chloride (benzoyl chloride). IR: broad acid O-H gone; C=O shifts up to ~1790 (acid chloride). Benzaldehyde, benzyl chloride, benzyl alcohol have wrong skeleton/lost carbonyl; unreacted acid keeps broad O-H. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard acid-to-acyl-chloride; qualitative IR (carbonyl shift direction, not exact-value math) in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Butene is treated with Hg(OAc)2 in water, then NaBH4 (oxymercuration-demercuration). The IR of the product shows a strong broad band near 3300 cm-1 and no band in the 1650-1750 cm-1 region. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC=O","OCCCC","CC(O)CC","CCC(C)=O","C=CCC"],"correct":2,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration adds water with Markovnikov regiochemistry and no rearrangement, giving 2-butanol. IR: broad ~3300 O-H, no C=O. Anti-Markovnikov 1-butanol (1) is the hydroboration regiochemistry; ketone/aldehyde have C=O; unreacted alkene lacks O-H. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Canonical oxymercuration; layered regio + IR; in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Aniline is treated with acetic anhydride. The IR of the product shows a band near 3300 cm-1 (one sharp N-H) together with a strong carbonyl band near 1660 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCNc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Acetic anhydride acylates the more nucleophilic nitrogen, giving the secondary amide acetanilide. IR demands both N-H (~3300, one band) and amide C=O (~1660). N-ethylaniline has N-H but no C=O; phenyl ester and acetophenone have C=O but no N-H; aniline has two N-H bands but no C=O. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard N-acylation; combined N-H + amide carbonyl reasoning is sophomore-level; in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"q":"A student records the IR spectrum of the compound drawn below and observes a very broad, intense absorption spanning roughly 2500-3300 cm-1 superimposed on a strong, sharp band near 1710 cm-1. Which structural feature accounts for this pattern?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["An amine N-H stretch with no C=O present","An ester C=O with no O-H present","An isolated alcohol O-H stretch only, near 3300 cm-1","A terminal alkyne C-H stretch near 3300 cm-1","A carboxylic acid: the C=O stretch (~1710) plus the very broad hydrogen-bonded O-H stretch (2500-3300)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Butanoic acid: the diagnostic carboxylic-acid IR signature is the strong C=O stretch (~1710) plus an extremely broad H-bonded O-H stretch spanning 2500-3300. This combination distinguishes -COOH from alcohols (narrower O-H, no carbonyl), esters (no O-H), and amines. SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; single defensible answer; sophomore-level IR recognition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Iconic carboxylic-acid IR pattern; valid SMILES, one correct answer, in scope, molecule drawn."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the molecule shown has a single strong absorption near 1715 cm-1 and shows NO significant broad absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region. Which class of compound is most consistent with these data?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Primary amide","Ketone","Terminal alkyne","Alcohol","Carboxylic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Cyclohexanone: strong C=O ~1715 and no O-H or N-H bonds, so nothing broad in 2500-3500. Carboxylic acids/alcohols show broad O-H and amides show N-H bands, so their absence points to a ketone. SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; unambiguous; single concept.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Clean ketone identification; valid SMILES, one correct answer, in scope."},{"q":"An unknown gives a sharp IR band at ~3300 cm-1 together with a weak but distinct absorption at ~2120 cm-1, and no strong band in the 1650-1750 cm-1 carbonyl region. Which structure best fits this IR spectrum?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["A ketone (strong C=O ~1715)","A carboxylic acid (broad O-H 2500-3300, C=O ~1710)","A nitrile (sharp C triple-bond N ~2250 only, no 3300 band)","A primary alcohol (broad O-H ~3300, C-O ~1050)","A terminal alkyne (sharp C-H ~3300, C triple-bond C ~2120, no carbonyl)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"1-Hexyne (terminal alkyne): sharp terminal-C-H stretch ~3300 plus weak C≡C ~2120, no carbonyl. The 3300 band is sharp (vs broad O-H of acids/alcohols), and ~2120 distinguishes it from a nitrile (~2250, no 3300 band). SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; distractors accurate and plausible.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Terminal-alkyne IR recognition; nitrile distractor correctly placed at ~2250."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the compound drawn shows a sharp, medium-intensity absorption near 2250 cm-1, with no carbonyl band and no broad O-H. Which functional group produces the 2250 cm-1 absorption?","q_smiles":"CCCC#N","opts":["Carbonyl (C=O)","Nitrile (C triple-bond N)","Terminal alkyne C-H","Amine N-H","Hydroxyl (O-H)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Butanenitrile: a sharp, medium C≡N stretch near 2250 in the triple-bond region (2100-2260). Absence of a carbonyl and broad O-H rules out acids, esters, ketones, and alcohols. SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; single concept; unambiguous.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Straightforward nitrile identification; valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Two compounds are drawn below. Compound A (left of the dot-disconnect) and compound B are isomers. Compound A's IR shows two medium bands near 3350 and 3180 cm-1 plus a strong band at 1690 cm-1; compound B shows only a strong band near 1715 cm-1. Which assignment correctly explains compound A's two N-H bands plus carbonyl?","q_smiles":"NC(=O)c1ccccc1.O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Compound A is a carboxylic acid: the two bands are O-H overtones","Compound A is a primary amide: two N-H stretches (asymmetric ~3350, symmetric ~3180) plus the amide C=O ~1690","Compound A is a nitrile, and the 1690 band is a C-triple-bond-N stretch","Compound A is an alcohol whose O-H splits into two sharp bands","Compound A is a secondary amine with a coincidental carbonyl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Benzamide is a primary amide: NH2 gives two N-H stretches (asymmetric ~3350, symmetric ~3180) plus amide C=O ~1690 (lowered by conjugation/resonance). The two N-H bands are the primary-amide giveaway; a secondary amide shows one N-H and an amine lacks the carbonyl. Both SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; two-concept (functional-group ID + NH2 doublet) fits difficulty 2.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"Dot-disconnect SMILES is unusual notation for a 'drawn below' stem but valid; chemistry and key correct."},{"q":"Four candidate structures share the molecular formula but differ in functional group. An IR spectrum shows a strong band at 1740 cm-1 and NO absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region (no O-H, no N-H). Of the molecule drawn, which statement about why it matches is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OCC","opts":["It is an amide, and the missing N-H bands are overlapping the C=O","It is a ketone, identified only by the lack of any 3300 cm-1 band","It is an ester: strong C=O ~1740 cm-1 and no O-H/N-H bands in the 2500-3500 region","It is an alcohol, and the 1740 band is its C-O stretch","It is a carboxylic acid, so the broad O-H is simply too weak to see"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Ethyl acetate (ester): strong C=O ~1735-1745 (higher than a ketone due to inductive pull of the ester O) and no O-H/N-H, so nothing broad in 2500-3500. The clean elevated carbonyl plus no broad region distinguishes ester from acid and amide. SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; two-concept (frequency value + absence reasoning).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Ester C=O at ~1740 correctly above ketone; valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Rank the three carbonyl compounds shown for the position (wavenumber) of their C=O stretch, from HIGHEST to LOWEST. Structures, in order drawn: (I) an ester, (II) a ketone, (III) a primary amide.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OCC.CCCC(C)=O.NC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Amide (~1690) > ester (~1740) > ketone (~1715)","Ester (~1740) > ketone (~1715) > amide (~1690)","Ketone (~1715) > ester (~1740) > amide (~1690)","Amide (~1690) > ketone (~1715) > ester (~1740)","Ester (~1740) > amide (~1690) > ketone (~1715)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Ethyl acetate ~1740 > 2-pentanone ~1715 > benzamide ~1660-1690. Esters absorb highest (inductive O withdrawal raises C=O bond order); amides absorb lowest (N lone-pair donation lowers C=O bond order). Ordering holds robustly for these specific compounds. All three SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; inductive-vs-resonance rationale is standard sophomore IR, not grad FMO.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Standard carbonyl-frequency trend; the chosen substrates preserve the ester>ketone>amide ordering."},{"q":"An IR spectrum of the drawn compound shows a broad absorption centered near 3350 cm-1 and strong bands in the 1000-1100 cm-1 region, but NO band in the 1650-1750 cm-1 carbonyl region and NO band in the 2100-2260 cm-1 triple-bond region. Which functional group is present?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts":["Ketone (would show a strong C=O ~1715)","Carboxylic acid (would also show a strong C=O ~1710)","Terminal alkyne (would show a sharp band ~2120)","Alcohol (broad O-H ~3350, C-O ~1050; no carbonyl, no triple bond)","Nitrile (would show C-triple-bond-N ~2250)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclohexanol: broad H-bonded O-H ~3200-3550 plus strong C-O ~1000-1100, no carbonyl and no triple bond. Absence of ~1710 rules out a carboxylic acid (which would also show C=O), and absence of triple-bond bands rules out nitrile/alkyne. SMILES valid; answer index 0 correct; single concept; unambiguous.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Clean alcohol identification with well-constructed eliminative distractors."},{"q":"A student records the IR spectrum of the compound shown below and observes a sharp absorption at approximately 2250 cm-1, with NO strong band near 1700 cm-1 and NO broad band in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region. Which functional group is responsible for the 2250 cm-1 absorption?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["C≡N (nitrile) triple-bond stretch","C=C (alkene) stretch","N-H (amine) stretch","C=O (carbonyl) stretch","O-H (alcohol) stretch"],"correct":0,"why":"Pentanenitrile. Sharp ~2250 cm-1 is the nitrile C≡N stretch; absence of ~1700 rules out carbonyl, absence of broad 2500-3500 rules out O-H/N-H. Distractors are standard wrong band assignments. Unique answer, valid SMILES, in scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (0); valid SMILES; single-concept IR recognition, in scope."},{"q":"An IR spectrum shows a strong band at ~1715 cm-1 (C=O) AND a very broad band stretching from ~2500-3300 cm-1. Which of the following structures is consistent with BOTH of these absorptions?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CCc1ccccc1","O=C(C)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(OC)Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Only phenylacetic acid (carboxylic acid) shows BOTH a C=O (~1715) and the very broad O-H (2500-3300). Alcohol = O-H only; ketone/aldehyde/ester = C=O only, no broad O-H. Distractors differ only by functional group on the same skeleton. All 5 SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); all option SMILES valid; structure-ID is visual; unique answer; in scope."},{"q":"For the compound drawn below, which set of IR absorptions would you expect to observe?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Strong C=O near 1660 cm-1 AND two N-H bands near 3350 and 3180 cm-1","Broad O-H from 2500-3300 cm-1 AND C=O near 1710 cm-1","Strong C=O near 1715 cm-1 only, with no bands above 3000 cm-1","Sharp C≡N near 2250 cm-1 only","Broad O-H near 3300 cm-1 with no carbonyl band"],"correct":0,"why":"Butanamide is a primary amide: lowered amide-I C=O (~1640-1680) plus an N-H doublet (asym ~3350, sym ~3180) from two N-H bonds. This pattern distinguishes primary amide from carboxylic acid (broad single O-H) or ketone. Unique answer, valid SMILES, in scope.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES (C4H9NO primary amide); qualitative IR, in scope."},{"q":"An unknown hydrocarbon (formula C6H10) gives an IR spectrum with a SHARP absorption at ~3300 cm-1 plus a weak band near 2120 cm-1. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1","C#CCCCC","CC1=CCCC1","CC#CCCC","CCC#CCC"],"correct":1,"why":"Sharp ~3300 (≡C-H) plus ~2120 (C≡C) is the terminal-alkyne signature. Only 1-hexyne is terminal; 2-/3-hexyne are internal (no ≡C-H, no sharp 3300). Cyclohexane (C6H12) is saturated; methylcyclopentene (C6H10) is an alkene with neither band. Spectrum uniquely selects opt 0. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"Minor: stem formula C6H10 fits opts 0-2 and 4 but not cyclohexane (C6H12); however the spectrum still uniquely identifies the terminal alkyne (0), so answer remains unambiguous. Keep."},{"q":"Which of the structures below would show an IR spectrum with a strong C=O stretch near 1700 cm-1 and a broad O-H stretch centered near 3300 cm-1, but NO N-H absorption?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1","N#Cc1ccccc1","CC=O","CC(=O)N","OC(C(=O)O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Mandelic acid has -OH (broad ~3300) and -COOH C=O (~1700) and no nitrogen, so no N-H. Acetamide has N-H/C=O but no O-H; benzyl alcohol O-H only; benzonitrile C≡N only; acetaldehyde C=O only. Verified mandelic acid is the only N-free option with both C=O and O-H. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); all option SMILES valid; combining 3 qualitative IR observations is still recognition-level, in scope; unique answer."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the compound shown shows a strong C=O band, plus TWO N-H stretching bands near 3460 and 3360 cm-1. Based on the structure, the two N-H bands arise from which group?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccc(C=O)cc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Two separate O-H groups hydrogen bonding to each other","The primary aromatic amine (-NH2), which has two N-H bonds (asymmetric + symmetric stretch)","A nitrile overtone splitting into two bands","The aromatic ring C-H stretches above 3000 cm-1","The aldehyde C-H, which always appears as a doublet near 3400 cm-1"],"correct":1,"why":"4-Aminobenzaldehyde has a primary -NH2, which gives two N-H stretches (asym ~3460, sym ~3360). Secondary amine = one band, tertiary = none. The aldehyde gives C=O and weak C-H (~2720/2820), not the 3400 doublet. Unique answer, valid SMILES, in scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES; single-concept primary-amine N-H doublet recognition, in scope."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the molecule shown displays a SHARP nitrile band at ~2240 cm-1 together with a broad O-H stretch near 3400 cm-1. Which functional groups account for these two absorptions?","q_smiles":"OC(C#N)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An alkyne (C≡C) and an O-H","A nitrile (C≡N) and a hydroxyl (O-H) — a cyanohydrin","An aldehyde C=O and a hydroxyl O-H","A primary amine (N-H) and a C=O","A carboxylic acid (broad O-H) and a C=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Mandelonitrile is a cyanohydrin: -C≡N (sharp ~2240) and -OH (broad ~3400) on the same carbon. No band near 1700 rules out carbonyl/carboxylic acid; a terminal alkyne would give a sharp ≡C-H ~3300, not a broad O-H. Unique answer, valid SMILES, in scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES (mandelonitrile); qualitative band ID, in scope."},{"q":"Salicylaldehyde (shown) has an intramolecular hydrogen bond between its phenol O-H and the adjacent aldehyde oxygen. Compared with the IR of a simple phenol, what is the expected effect of this intramolecular hydrogen bonding on the O-H stretch?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The O-H band moves to ~2250 cm-1, overlapping the nitrile region","The O-H stretch sharpens and shifts to HIGHER wavenumber (above 3600 cm-1)","The O-H stretch becomes a sharp doublet like a primary amine","The O-H stretch is broadened and shifted to LOWER wavenumber (below ~3200 cm-1)","The O-H stretch disappears entirely from the spectrum"],"correct":3,"why":"Hydrogen bonding weakens the O-H bond, lowering its frequency and broadening the band. The intramolecular H-bond in salicylaldehyde persists even when dilute, so O-H is broad and shifted to lower wavenumber vs a free O-H (~3600). Standard sophomore IR H-bonding concept; qualitative, no quantitative shift math. Unique answer, valid SMILES, in scope.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES; H-bonding effect on O-H frequency is qualitative recognition, within DAT scope; unique answer."},{"q":"An aromatic hydrocarbon with molecular formula C8H10 shows only TWO signals in its 1H NMR spectrum. Both signals are singlets, and they integrate in a 3:2 ratio (the more intense, more upfield signal corresponds to 6H). Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(C)cc1","Cc1cccc(C)c1","CCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1C","Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"p-Xylene has full symmetry: 6 equivalent CH3 H (singlet) and 4 equivalent aromatic H (singlet) = two signals, 3:2 ratio. o-Xylene gives 3 signals, m-xylene gives 4, ethylbenzene gives quartet/triplet plus aromatics, and toluene is the wrong formula (C7H8). Index 0 is uniquely correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; all SMILES valid (C8H10 for 0-3, toluene C7H8 distractor), unique answer at index 0, recognition-level symmetry, in scope."},{"q":"A compound C3H7Br shows TWO 1H NMR signals. The most downfield signal is a 1H septet, and the upfield signal is a 6H doublet. Which structure fits this pattern?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["CCCCBr","CC(C)Br","CCBr","CCCBr","CC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"2-Bromopropane: the CH couples to 6 equivalent neighbor H (septet, 1H); the two CH3 couple to one CH (doublet, 6H). 1-bromopropane gives 3 signals; the other distractors are wrong formulas (EtBr, n-BuBr, t-BuBr). Index 0 uniquely fits.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; n+1 rule recognition, valid SMILES, unique answer at index 0, in scope."},{"q":"A compound with molecular formula C2H4Cl2 shows exactly ONE signal (a singlet) in its 1H NMR spectrum. Which structure is consistent with this observation?","q_smiles":"ClCCCl","opts":["ClCCCCl","ClCC(Cl)Cl","ClCCCl","ClC(Cl)C(Cl)Cl","CC(Cl)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"1,2-Dichloroethane has two equivalent CH2 groups; all four H are one environment with no coupling between equivalent H, so one singlet. 1,1-dichloroethane gives a quartet + doublet (2 signals); the remaining options are different formulas (C2H3Cl3, C2H2Cl4, C3H6Cl2). Index 0 uniquely fits.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; equivalence-by-symmetry recognition, all SMILES valid, unique answer, in scope."},{"q":"An ether of formula C5H12O shows only TWO 1H NMR signals, BOTH singlets, integrating 9:3. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"COC(C)(C)C","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","COC(C)(C)C","CCCCCO","CC(O)C(C)C","CCOCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"MTBE (tert-butyl methyl ether): 9 equivalent tert-butyl H (singlet, no adjacent H) and 3 OCH3 H (singlet), 9:3. Diethyl ether is the wrong formula (C4H10O) and gives quartet/triplet; the alcohols give more signals plus OH. Index 0 uniquely fits.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; valid SMILES (diethyl ether distractor is C4H10O, others C5H12O), unique answer at index 0, in scope."},{"q":"A compound C3H6O shows only ONE 1H NMR signal: a 6H singlet near 2 ppm, with no signal in the aldehyde or vinyl region. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["CC1CO1","COC=C","C=CCO","CCC=O","CC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetone has two equivalent methyls on a symmetric carbonyl: all 6 H are one environment with no neighbors, a single 6H singlet, no aldehyde/vinyl H. Propanal has an aldehyde H near 9-10 ppm and 3 signals; propylene oxide, allyl alcohol, and methyl vinyl ether each give multiple signals (ring/vinyl). All five are valid C3H6O isomers. Index 0 uniquely fits.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; all five valid C3H6O isomers, unique answer at index 0, recognition-level, in scope."},{"q":"An aromatic diether of formula C8H10O2 shows only TWO 1H NMR signals (a 6H singlet and a 4H singlet). Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"COc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts":["COc1ccc(OC)cc1","CCOc1ccccc1","COc1cccc(OC)c1","COc1ccccc1OC","COc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"1,4-Dimethoxybenzene has a symmetry axis: both OCH3 equivalent (6H singlet) and all 4 ring H equivalent (4H singlet) = 2 signals. The 1,3-isomer gives 4 signals and the 1,2-isomer gives 3 (ring H not all equivalent); phenetole (C8H10O) and anisole (C7H8O) are wrong formulas. Index 0 uniquely fits.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; valid SMILES, isomer symmetry (regio) + wrong-formula distractors, unique answer at index 0, in scope."},{"q":"A ketone of formula C5H10O shows only THREE signals in its 13C NMR spectrum. Its 1H NMR spectrum shows just two signals: a 6H triplet and a 4H quartet. Which structure is consistent with BOTH spectra?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts":["CCCC(C)=O","O=CC(C)(C)C","CC(C)C(C)=O","CCC(=O)CC","O=C1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"3-Pentanone (diethyl ketone) is symmetric: 3 carbon environments (CH3, CH2, C=O) and two 1H signals, a 6H triplet (two CH3) and 4H quartet (two CH2). 2-pentanone gives 5 13C signals and 3-methyl-2-butanone gives 4, eliminating them on carbon count. Cyclopentanone (3 13C) and pivaldehyde (3 13C) match the carbon count but their 1H spectra (ring multiplets; or a 9H singlet + aldehyde H) do not give the triplet/quartet, so only index 0 satisfies BOTH spectra. (Note: cyclopentanone is actually C5H8O, a different formula than the stem's C5H10O, so the stem's own constraint already excludes it; the explanation's 'matches the 3-carbon count' framing is a minor imprecision but does not affect the unique answer.)","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Sound difficulty-3 item; RDKit confirms 13C/1H environment counts (3-pentanone 3/2, 2-pentanone 5/4, 3-Me-2-butanone 4/3, cyclopentanone 3/2, pivaldehyde 3/2). Two-spectrum filter leaves index 0 uniquely. Minor: cyclopentanone is C5H8O so already excluded by the stated formula; explanation slightly imprecise but answer unique and correct. In scope (counting, no J math)."},{"q":"How many signals appear in the 1H NMR spectrum of the molecule shown (p-xylene)?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C)cc1","opts":["1 signal","6 signals","4 signals","3 signals","2 signals"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"p-Xylene is highly symmetric. The two CH3 groups are equivalent (one 6H singlet) and all four aromatic H are equivalent by the molecule's mirror symmetry (one 4H singlet), giving 2 distinct 1H signals total.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. p-Xylene has 2 equivalent CH3 and 4 equivalent aromatic H = 2 signals. correct=0 confirmed. Recognition-level, single concept (symmetry/signal counting)."},{"q":"The 1H NMR of the compound shown (ethyl acetate) shows three sets of peaks. Reading them as: methyl of the ethyl group, the acetyl methyl, and the OCH2, which multiplicity pattern is correct?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["singlet (3H), singlet (3H), singlet (2H)","doublet (3H), singlet (3H), triplet (2H)","quartet (3H), singlet (3H), triplet (2H)","triplet (3H), singlet (3H), quartet (2H)","triplet (3H), doublet (3H), quartet (2H)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The CH3 of the ethyl group couples to the adjacent CH2 (n+1 = 3, triplet). The acetyl CH3 has no neighboring H, so it is a singlet. The OCH2 couples to the adjacent CH3 (n+1 = 4, quartet). Integration ratio 3:3:2.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. Ethyl CH3=triplet, acetyl CH3=singlet (no neighbors), OCH2=quartet. correct=0 confirmed. Classic n+1 multiplicity question, in scope."},{"q":"The 1H NMR of the molecule shown (1,4-dimethoxybenzene) consists of exactly two singlets. What is the integration ratio of those singlets (aromatic H : OCH3 H)?","q_smiles":"COc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts":["1 : 1","4 : 3","1 : 3","2 : 3","3 : 2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"By para symmetry the two OCH3 groups are equivalent (6H, singlet, no neighbors) and all four aromatic H are equivalent (4H, singlet). The ratio is 4H : 6H, which reduces to 2 : 3.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. 4 aromatic H : 6 OCH3 H = 4:6 = 2:3. correct=0 confirmed. Integration ratio recognition, in scope."},{"q":"How many signals appear in the proton-decoupled 13C NMR spectrum of the molecule shown (neopentane, 2,2-dimethylpropane)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C","opts":["2 signals","1 signal","3 signals","5 signals","4 signals"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Neopentane has one central quaternary carbon and four equivalent CH3 carbons. By symmetry there are only 2 chemically distinct carbon environments, so the 13C spectrum shows 2 signals (the 1H spectrum, by contrast, shows just 1 singlet).","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. Neopentane: 1 quaternary C + 4 equivalent CH3 C = 2 distinct carbon environments. correct=0 confirmed. 13C signal counting, in scope."},{"q":"The 1H NMR of the molecule shown (1,2-dichloroethane) is a single sharp singlet. Which statement best explains this?","q_smiles":"ClCCCl","opts":["Rapid rotation averages two non-equivalent doublets into one peak","Chlorine quenches all proton coupling, collapsing the peaks to one singlet","The protons are equivalent but should still split each other into a triplet","The two CH2 groups are different, but their signals overlap by coincidence","All four protons are equivalent by symmetry, so there is no observable coupling and one signal appears"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Both CH2 groups bear one Cl and are related by the molecule's symmetry, so all four protons are chemically equivalent. Equivalent protons do not split one another, so a single singlet is observed (and the 13C shows one signal).","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. All 4 H of ClCH2CH2Cl are equivalent by symmetry; equivalent protons do not split each other = singlet. correct=0 confirmed. Conceptual equivalence question, in scope."},{"q":"For the molecule shown (1-bromopropane), how many 1H NMR signals appear and what is the multiplicity of the central CH2 group?","q_smiles":"CCCBr","opts":["3 signals; central CH2 is a triplet","3 signals; central CH2 is a quintet","2 signals; central CH2 is a quartet","4 signals; central CH2 is a sextet","3 signals; central CH2 is a sextet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"1-Bromopropane has three distinct proton environments: CH3, central CH2, and CH2Br (3 signals). The central CH2 is flanked by the CH3 (3H) and the CH2Br (2H); treating those 5 neighbors together gives n+1 = 6, a sextet.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. 1-Bromopropane: 3 environments; central CH2 has 5 neighboring H (3+2), n+1=6 sextet (first-order textbook treatment). correct=0 confirmed. Two-concept (signal count + multiplicity), in scope."},{"q":"A student records 1H NMR for the molecule shown (3-pentanone, diethyl ketone) and for acetone (CH3COCH3) drawn as the contrast. Which statement correctly compares their proton spectra?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts":["Both show a single singlet because each ketone is symmetric","3-Pentanone shows 1 singlet; acetone shows 2 signals","3-Pentanone shows 3 signals; acetone shows 2 signals","3-Pentanone shows 2 signals (quartet + triplet); acetone shows 1 singlet","Both show 2 signals, each a quartet and a triplet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"3-Pentanone is symmetric, so its two ethyl groups are equivalent: the CH2 (coupled to CH3) is a quartet and the CH3 (coupled to CH2) is a triplet, giving 2 signals. Acetone has two equivalent CH3 groups with no neighboring H, giving a single singlet.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. 3-Pentanone (symmetric): 2 signals, quartet CH2 + triplet CH3; acetone: 1 singlet. correct=0 confirmed. Comparative symmetry/multiplicity, in scope."},{"q":"An unknown gives a 1H NMR with three signals: a 3H singlet near 2.5 ppm and two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (~7.4 and ~8.1 ppm). Of the two candidate structures, the one drawn is p-nitrotoluene; the contrast candidate is tert-butanol (CC(C)(C)O). Which structure is consistent with the spectrum?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Neither, because p-nitrotoluene would show four aromatic signals","tert-Butanol, because it produces two aromatic doublets","tert-Butanol, because its 9H singlet matches the 3H methyl peak","The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene): CH3 singlet plus two aromatic doublets from the para ring","The drawn structure, but it should show a 9H singlet not a 3H singlet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"p-Nitrotoluene has a benzylic CH3 (3H singlet, ~2.5 ppm) and a para-disubstituted ring giving an AA'BB' pattern that reads as two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (the H ortho to NO2 shifted downfield ~8.1 ppm). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H and would show only a 9H singlet plus OH, so it cannot match.","difficulty":3,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. p-Nitrotoluene: 3H benzylic CH3 singlet + para-ring AA'BB' (read as two 2H doublets). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H so cannot match. correct=0 confirmed. Structure-from-spectrum matching, qualitative (no shift math), in scope."},{"q":"A colorless aromatic liquid (C8H10) shows only TWO signals in its 1H NMR spectrum: one singlet near 2.3 ppm and one singlet near 7.0 ppm, with an integration ratio of 3:2. Its 13C NMR shows exactly three signals. Which structure is consistent with this data?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccccc1C","Cc1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"p-Xylene's two mirror planes make all 6 methyl H equivalent and all 4 ring H equivalent (6:4 = 3:2), with only 3 distinct carbons. meta-Xylene gives 4 1H/5 13C signals, ortho gives 3 1H/4 13C, ethylbenzene breaks the 3:2 integration, and toluene is C7H8. Verified by RDKit: para = 3 distinct C with [6,4] H pattern; ortho = 4 C; meta = 5 C. Symmetry is the discriminator.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All 5 SMILES valid; RDKit confirms para-xylene is the only option with 3 carbons and a 3:2 (6H:4H) two-signal pattern. q_smiles was empty in source; set to the keyed substrate (p-xylene) so the molecule is drawn. In scope (recognition-level symmetry/integration), one defensible answer."},{"q":"An unknown chlorinated compound (C2H3Cl3) shows TWO 1H NMR signals: a doublet integrating for 2H near 4.0 ppm and a triplet integrating for 1H near 5.8 ppm. Which structure produces this pattern?","q_smiles":"ClCC(Cl)Cl","opts":["ClCC(Cl)Cl","ClC(Cl)C(Cl)Cl","CC(Cl)(Cl)Cl","CCCl","ClCCCl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A 2H doublet (CH2 split by 1 neighbor) adjacent to a 1H triplet (CH split by 2 neighbors) = CHCl2-CH2Cl, 1,1,2-trichloroethane. 1,1,1-trichloroethane (CCl3-CH3, same C2H3Cl3 formula) gives a single 3H singlet; tetrachloroethane is C2H2Cl4; 1,2-dichloroethane is a 4H singlet; chloroethane is C2H5Cl. RDKit confirms option 0 = C2H3Cl3 with [2,1] H pattern. n+1 multiplicity + integration pin the answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid. Strong distractor [1] is the isomeric 1,1,1-trichloroethane (same formula). q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope and unambiguous."},{"q":"An ether (C5H12O) shows just TWO singlets in its 1H NMR: one integrating for 3H near 3.2 ppm and one integrating for 9H near 1.2 ppm. The 13C NMR shows three signals. No signal is split. Which structure fits?","q_smiles":"COC(C)(C)C","opts":["COC(C)(C)C","CCOC(C)C","CCOCC","CC(C)OC(C)C","CCCOC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two singlets (no coupling) at 3H:9H = OCH3 plus C(CH3)3 with no H-bearing neighbors = tert-butyl methyl ether (MTBE), C5H12O, 3 carbons. Diethyl ether (C4H10O) and the propyl/isopropyl ethers all have CH2/CH groups adjacent to other H, producing triplets/quartets/septets. RDKit confirms MTBE = C5H12O, 3 distinct C, [9,3] H pattern. Absence of coupling is the key clue.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only MTBE gives two singlets with 3 carbons. Distractors include correct C5H12O isomer (isopropyl ethyl ether) that would couple. q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"A monosubstituted benzene (C9H12) shows in its 1H NMR: a doublet integrating for 6H near 1.2 ppm, a septet integrating for 1H near 2.9 ppm, and aromatic signals (5H) near 7.2 ppm. Which structure is consistent?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A 6H doublet + 1H septet is the isopropyl signature (two equivalent CH3 coupled to one CH). Isopropylbenzene (cumene), C9H12, fits with 5 aromatic H. Propylbenzene gives triplet/sextet/triplet; tert-butylbenzene gives a 9H singlet (and is C10H14); ethylbenzene is C8H10; diethylbenzene is C10H14 and disubstituted (4 ArH). RDKit confirms cumene = C9H12 with the 6H/1H side-chain pattern.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid. Aromatic 5H is realistically one grouped multiplet (formally 3 environments 2+2+1) but the discriminating 6H-doublet/1H-septet is unambiguous; stem wording 'aromatic signals (5H)' avoids over-claiming a singlet. q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"An ester (C4H8O2) gives THREE 1H NMR signals: a quartet (2H) near 4.1 ppm, a singlet (3H) near 2.0 ppm, and a triplet (3H) near 1.2 ppm. Which structure matches this set of multiplicities and integrations?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["COC(=O)CC","CC(=O)OC(C)C","CCOC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC","COC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Triplet(3H)+quartet(2H) is an isolated ethyl; the 3H SINGLET is an acetyl CH3 on C=O with no neighboring H = ethyl acetate, CH3C(=O)OCH2CH3. The OCH2 is deshielded (~4.1, quartet). Methyl propanoate (same C4H8O2) would show a 3H OCH3 singlet plus a triplet/quartet acyl ethyl at different shifts; isopropyl acetate gives a septet/doublet; methyl acetate has only two singlets; ethyl propanoate is C5. RDKit confirms EtOAc = C4H8O2 with [3,3,2] H pattern.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; strong distractor [1] is isomeric methyl propanoate (same formula). q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope and unambiguous; the acetyl-CH3 singlet at ~2.0 vs the ethyl pattern cleanly excludes the isomer."},{"q":"An aromatic hydrocarbon (C9H12) shows only TWO 1H NMR signals: a singlet near 2.3 ppm (9H) and a singlet near 6.8 ppm (3H). Its 13C NMR shows exactly three signals. Which structure has this much symmetry?","q_smiles":"Cc1cc(C)cc(C)c1","opts":["Cc1ccccc1C","Cc1cc(C)cc(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C)cc1","Cc1cccc(C)c1","CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A 9H methyl singlet and a 3H aromatic singlet (3:1) with only 3 carbons requires threefold symmetry = 1,3,5-trimethylbenzene (mesitylene). Each ring H sits between two methyl-bearing carbons with no adjacent ring H, so it is a singlet; all 9 methyl H and all 3 ring H are equivalent. p-Xylene/o-xylene are C8H10 (6 methyl H), cumene's CH3 is a doublet, m-xylene lacks threefold symmetry. RDKit confirms mesitylene = C9H12, 3 distinct C, [9,3] H pattern.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only mesitylene gives 3 carbons with a 3:1 (9H:3H) two-singlet pattern. Aromatic-H singlet claim is chemically correct (isolated ring H). q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"A cyclic ether (C4H8O2) shows a SINGLE 1H NMR singlet near 3.7 ppm and a SINGLE 13C NMR signal. Which structure has all eight hydrogens (and both kinds of carbon) equivalent?","q_smiles":"C1COCCO1","opts":["C1CCOCC1","C1COCO1","CCOCC","C1COCCO1","C1CCOC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"One 1H singlet and one 13C signal means every CH2 is equivalent with no chemically distinct neighbor. 1,4-Dioxane has all four CH2 equivalent (each flanked by O and an equivalent CH2), giving one carbon and one proton singlet. Oxane (C5H10O) and THF (C4H8O) have several inequivalent CH2; 1,3-dioxolane (C3H6O2) has two CH2 environments; diethyl ether is acyclic C4H10O. RDKit confirms 1,4-dioxane = C4H8O2, 1 distinct C, single [8] H signal.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only 1,4-dioxane has 1 carbon environment and a single 8H singlet. Stem 'both kinds of carbon' is slightly loose (only one carbon environment), but the single-13C-signal criterion plus structures make the answer unambiguous. q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"A brominated compound (C3H7Br) shows TWO 1H NMR signals: a doublet integrating for 6H near 1.7 ppm and a septet integrating for 1H near 4.2 ppm. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["CCCCBr","CCBr","CC(C)Br","CCCBr","CC(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Two signals from C3H7Br with a 6H doublet and a 1H septet = 2-bromopropane: two equivalent CH3 coupled to the single CHBr (doublet), and the CHBr split by 6 neighbors into a septet, deshielded to ~4.2 by Br. 1-Bromopropane gives THREE signals (triplet/sextet/triplet); 1-bromobutane and isobutyl bromide are C4H9Br; bromoethane is C2H5Br. RDKit confirms 2-bromopropane = C3H7Br with [6,1] H pattern.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only 2-bromopropane gives a two-signal 6H-doublet/1H-septet pattern. Difficulty is overcalibrated at 3 (this is the same isopropyl doublet/septet recognition as Q4 cumene, marked 2); functionally a difficulty-2 item, but chemistry/keep are unaffected. q_smiles set to keyed substrate."},{"q":"A monohalogenated aromatic compound shows molecular-ion peaks at m/z 112 (M+) and m/z 114 (M+2) in an intensity ratio of about 3:1. Which structure is consistent with this mass spectrum?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Clc1ccccc1","Ic1ccccc1","Clc1ccc(Cl)cc1","Brc1ccccc1","c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"A single chlorine gives M:M+2 of about 3:1 because 35Cl and 37Cl occur in roughly a 3:1 natural ratio. The M+ at 112 matches C6H5Cl (chlorobenzene). One Br would give about 1:1 (and M+ at ~156), two Cl would give a 9:6:1 pattern, benzene and iodobenzene show no significant M+2.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: chlorobenzene C6H5Cl nominal mass 112, 3:1 M/M+2 signature of one Cl. All five option SMILES valid; only index 0 fits both the 112 mass and 3:1 ratio. One defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"An unknown hydrocarbon has the molecular formula C6H10, corresponding to two degrees of unsaturation (one ring plus one C=C, or any equivalent combination). Which structure is consistent with this formula and degree of unsaturation?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","CCCCCCC","CCCCCC"],"correct":1,"why":"Degrees of unsaturation = (2C+2-H)/2 = (12+2-10)/2 = 2. Cyclohexene (C6H10) has one ring and one double bond = 2. Cyclohexane (C6H12) and methylcyclopentane (C6H12) have 1; hexane (C6H14) has 0; heptane (C7H16) is the wrong formula with 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: cyclohexene C6H10 DoU=2 (confirmed by RDKit formula). Only index 0 has formula C6H10. Distractors are C6H12 (DoU 1), C6H14, C7H16. One defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn below) shows a molecular ion at m/z 106, a prominent peak at m/z 105 (M-1), and a strong peak at m/z 77. The m/z 77 fragment arises from loss of a neutral fragment of mass 29 (CHO). What is the identity of the m/z 77 ion?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The acylium ion CH3CO+","The methyl cation, CH3+","The tropylium ion, C7H7+","The phenyl cation, C6H5+ (loss of CHO, mass 29, from M+ 106)","The benzoyl cation, C6H5CO+ (this is the m/z 105 ion, not 77)"],"correct":3,"why":"M+ is 106 (C7H6O). Loss of H gives benzoyl C6H5CO+ at 105 (M-1). Loss of the whole CHO group (mass 29) from M+ gives the phenyl cation C6H5+ at 106-29 = 77. Tropylium (91) and the acetyl/methyl cations come from other skeletons.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: 106-29=77 = phenyl cation C6H5+. Stem q_smiles benzaldehyde valid. Only index 0 is the 77 ion. Minor: option B parenthetical is slightly didactic but does not create ambiguity. One defensible answer, in scope, molecule drawn."},{"q":"Bromoethane is shown below. In its mass spectrum, what is the approximate relative intensity of the molecular ion M+ (m/z 108) to the M+2 isotope peak (m/z 110)?","q_smiles":"CCBr","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["About 1:1, because 79Br and 81Br occur in nearly equal abundance","About 9:6:1, characteristic of two halogens","No M+2 peak is observed","About 100:1, the natural 13C contribution only","About 3:1, characteristic of one chlorine"],"correct":0,"why":"Bromine has two abundant isotopes, 79Br and 81Br, in roughly a 1:1 ratio, so a compound with one bromine shows M and M+2 of nearly equal intensity. The 3:1 pattern is the signature of one chlorine.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: bromoethane C2H5Br, one Br gives ~1:1 M/M+2. Note the stem labels M+ as m/z 108 while CH3CH2-79Br is nominally 108 (correct: 24+5+79=108). q_smiles valid. Only index 0 correct. In scope, visual."},{"q":"Acetophenone (drawn below, M+ = 120) undergoes alpha cleavage at the carbonyl, losing a methyl radical (mass 15) to give a resonance-stabilized acylium ion as the base peak at m/z 105. Which structure represents that m/z 105 fragment ion?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH2+]c1ccccc1","[CH3+]","C[C+]=O","[C+]1=CC=CC=C1","O=[C+]c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Alpha cleavage of acetophenone (C8H8O, 120) loses the methyl (15) to give the benzoyl acylium ion C6H5CO+ at 105, stabilized by resonance with oxygen and the ring. The acetyl cation CH3CO+ (43) comes from losing the phenyl instead; the phenyl (77), benzyl/tropylium (91), and methyl (15) cations are not the m/z 105 species.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: 120-15=105; keyed index 0 O=[C+]c1ccccc1 = benzoyl acylium C7H5O+ exact mass 105.03. All five cation SMILES valid (masses 105/43/77/91/15). Only index 0 is m/z 105. Two-concept (alpha cleavage + structure ID), in scope, visual."},{"q":"How many degrees of unsaturation are present in benzaldehyde, C7H6O (drawn below)? Remember that oxygen does not affect the count.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["3","5 (four from the benzene ring plus one from the C=O)","7","4 (the benzene ring only)","6"],"correct":1,"why":"Degrees of unsaturation = (2C+2+N-H-X)/2 with oxygen ignored = (14+2-6)/2 = 5. The aromatic ring accounts for 4 (three C=C plus the ring) and the aldehyde C=O adds one more, for a total of 5.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: C7H6O DoU = (14+2-6)/2 = 5 (confirmed). q_smiles benzaldehyde valid. Only index 0 = 5. One defensible answer, in scope, molecule drawn."},{"q":"A compound's mass spectrum shows a three-line molecular-ion cluster at M : M+2 : M+4 in an intensity ratio of about 1:2:1. Which structure produces this isotope pattern?","q_smiles":"BrCCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCl","ClCCCl","CCBr","ClCCBr","BrCCBr"],"correct":4,"why":"Two bromine atoms (each ~1:1 in 79Br/81Br) give a 1:2:1 triplet for M : M+2 : M+4. One Br gives 1:1; two Cl give 9:6:1; one Cl gives 3:1; one Br plus one Cl gives an intermediate, non-1:2:1 pattern.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: two Br (BrCCBr) gives 1:2:1 M:M+2:M+4. Two Cl would be 9:6:1, Br+Cl ~ 3:4:1 (not 1:2:1). All option SMILES valid. Only index 0 fits 1:2:1. In scope, visual."},{"q":"An unknown shows a molecular-ion cluster at m/z 120 and 122 in an intensity ratio of about 1:1, and combustion/formula analysis indicates exactly one degree of unsaturation. Which structure is consistent with BOTH observations?","q_smiles":"C=CCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCBr","CCCCBr","CCCBr","CC(C)Br","BrCCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"The 1:1 M/M+2 ratio means exactly one bromine (rules out the dibromide, which would be 1:2:1). One degree of unsaturation means one ring or pi bond. Allyl bromide (C3H5Br) has one bromine and one C=C (DoU = 1). 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane (C3H7Br) and 1-bromobutane (C4H9Br) are saturated (DoU = 0), and the dibromide has two Br.","difficulty":3,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: allyl bromide C3H5Br nominal mass 120 (36+5+79), one Br -> 1:1, DoU=1. Only index 0 satisfies BOTH constraints (others are saturated mono-Br at 122/136 or the 1:2:1 dibromide). All SMILES valid. Two layered concepts (isotope ratio + DoU), in scope, visual."},{"q":"The mass spectrum of the compound shown displays two molecular-ion peaks of roughly equal intensity (1:1) at m/z 136 and 138. Which structural feature of the molecule is responsible for this pattern?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["A nitrogen atom giving an odd molecular weight","Loss of an ethyl radical from the molecular ion","The natural abundance of 13C in a four-carbon chain","The presence of one bromine atom (79Br and 81Br are nearly 1:1 in nature)","The presence of one chlorine atom (35Cl and 37Cl)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Bromine has two abundant isotopes, 79Br and 81Br, in an approximately 1:1 ratio, so a single Br produces M and M+2 peaks of nearly equal height. 1-Bromobutane (C4H9Br) gives M+ at 136 (79Br) and 138 (81Br). Chlorine gives a 3:1 M/M+2 pattern instead.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C4H9Br nominal 79Br=136, 81Br=138; Br ~1:1 isotope ratio. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"The molecular ion of this compound appears at m/z 112 with an M+2 peak at m/z 114 that is about one-third (3:1) as intense. The presence of which element best accounts for this M:M+2 ratio?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts":["One sulfur atom","One chlorine atom","One nitrogen atom","One bromine atom","Two oxygen atoms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Chlorine's isotopes 35Cl and 37Cl occur in roughly a 3:1 ratio, giving M:M+2 of about 3:1. Chlorobenzene (C6H5Cl) shows M+ at 112 (35Cl) and M+2 at 114 (37Cl). Bromine would give a ~1:1 M:M+2 pattern.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C6H5Cl nominal 35Cl=112, 37Cl=114; Cl ~3:1. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"2-Pentanone (shown, M+ = 86) shows a prominent fragment at m/z 58 in its mass spectrum, corresponding to loss of 28 mass units. This fragment arises from which characteristic ketone fragmentation?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)=O","opts":["McLafferty rearrangement (loss of a neutral alkene via a gamma-hydrogen)","Alpha-cleavage to give an acylium ion only","Loss of a methyl radical (m/z 71)","Retro-Diels-Alder fragmentation","Loss of water (dehydration)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"2-Pentanone has a gamma-hydrogen, so it undergoes the McLafferty rearrangement, transferring the gamma-H to the carbonyl oxygen and expelling a neutral alkene (ethylene, C2H4, 28 u), giving the m/z 58 enol radical cation. Alpha-cleavage would give acylium ions at m/z 43 or 71, not 58.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: M+=86, loss of 28 (ethylene) = 58 via McLafferty; substrate has gamma-H. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"The compound shown (C7H7Br) gives a strong, very stable fragment at m/z 91 in its mass spectrum after loss of the bromine atom. The unusual stability of this m/z 91 cation is best explained by which structure?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts":["An acylium ion","A resonance-stabilized tropylium (seven-membered aromatic) cation","A bromonium ion","A protonated benzene ring","A primary benzyl carbocation with no resonance stabilization"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Benzyl bromide loses Br to form C7H7+ (m/z 91), which rearranges to the aromatic, highly resonance-stabilized tropylium cation. This is why m/z 91 is a hallmark fragment for benzyl-type compounds.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C7H7Br loss of Br (-79) gives C7H7+ = 91; classic tropylium. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Styrene is shown below. Without any spectra, how many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does its molecular formula C8H8 correspond to?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["6","8","5","4","3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (16 + 2 - 8)/2 = 5. This accounts for the benzene ring (3 pi bonds + 1 ring = 4) plus the vinyl C=C (1), totaling 5.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C8H8 DoU = 5. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is shown (C6H10O, M+ = 98). Its molecular formula gives 2 degrees of unsaturation. Which combination of structural features accounts for exactly those 2 degrees?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Two rings","One C=O plus one C=C","Two C=C double bonds","One ring plus one C=O pi bond","One ring plus one C=C double bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"DoU = (2*6 + 2 - 10)/2 = 2. Oxygen does not enter the formula. Those 2 units come from the six-membered ring (1) and the carbonyl C=O pi bond (1).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C6H10O DoU=2, M+=98; ring + C=O. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Aniline is shown (C6H7N). Its molecular ion appears at an odd m/z of 93. According to the nitrogen rule, what does this odd nominal molecular mass indicate?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["The molecule contains a halogen","The molecular ion is actually an M+2 isotope peak","The molecule has zero degrees of unsaturation","The molecule contains an even number of nitrogen atoms","The molecule contains an odd number of nitrogen atoms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"By the nitrogen rule, a neutral organic molecule with an odd nominal molecular mass contains an odd number of nitrogen atoms. Aniline has one N, giving the odd M+ at m/z 93.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C6H7N nominal = 93 (odd), one N. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone is shown (C8H8O, M+ = 120). Its base peak appears at m/z 105 (loss of 15). Identify the fragmentation that produces m/z 105 and the structure of that cation.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Loss of a methyl radical to give a tropylium ion at m/z 91","Loss of water to give a styrene radical cation","McLafferty rearrangement losing ethylene to give m/z 105","Alpha-cleavage losing a methyl radical to give the resonance-stabilized benzoyl (C6H5CO+) acylium ion","Loss of CO to give the phenyl cation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Alpha-cleavage of the bond between the carbonyl carbon and the methyl group ejects a methyl radical (15 u), giving the benzoyl acylium cation C6H5C=O+ at m/z 105 (resonance-stabilized by the ring and the O lone pair). Acetophenone has no gamma-H, so McLafferty is not available.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C8H8O M+=120, loss of CH3 (-15) = 105 = benzoyl C7H5O+; no gamma-H so McLafferty distractor correctly wrong. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"1-Bromobutane (drawn) is analyzed by EI mass spectrometry. Which pair of peaks best represents its molecular ion region?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["m/z 134 and 136 in roughly 3:1 ratio","m/z 136 and 138 in roughly 1:1 ratio","A single peak at m/z 136 with no M+2","m/z 136 and 137 in roughly 1:1 ratio","m/z 136 and 138 in roughly 3:1 ratio"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Bromine has two abundant isotopes (79Br and 81Br) in nearly 1:1 abundance, so a monobrominated compound shows M+ and M+2 of about equal height. C4H9(79Br) = 136, C4H9(81Br) = 138. The 3:1 ratio is the chlorine signature.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. CCCCBr valid (C4H9Br); M+136/M+2 138 ~1:1 is the Br isotope signature; only option 0 fits. In scope, well-formed."},{"q":"Chlorocyclohexane (drawn) gives an EI mass spectrum. Which statement about its molecular ion cluster is correct?","q_smiles":"ClC1CCCCC1","opts":["M+ at 118 and M+2 at 120 appear in about a 3:1 ratio","M+ at 118 and M+1 at 119 appear in about a 3:1 ratio","M+ at 120 and M+2 at 118 appear in about a 1:1 ratio","Only a single molecular ion at 118 is observed","M+ at 118 and M+2 at 120 appear in about a 1:1 ratio"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Chlorine isotopes 35Cl and 37Cl occur in about 3:1 abundance, so a monochloro compound shows M+ : M+2 of roughly 3:1. C6H11(35Cl) = 118 and C6H11(37Cl) = 120. The 1:1 ratio would indicate bromine.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. ClC1CCCCC1 valid (C6H11Cl); M+118/M+2 120 in 3:1 is the Cl signature; only option 0 fits. In scope."},{"q":"2-Pentanone (drawn) fragments in the mass spectrometer primarily by alpha-cleavage (loss of an alkyl radical next to the carbonyl) to give acylium ions. Which two acylium m/z values are produced from the two possible alpha-cleavages?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)=O","opts":["m/z 58 (M+2) and m/z 43 (CH3CO+)","m/z 43 (CH3CO+) and m/z 57 (CH3CH2CO+)","m/z 15 (CH3+) and m/z 86 (M+)","m/z 29 (CHO+) and m/z 71 (CH3CH2CH2CO+)","m/z 43 (CH3CO+) and m/z 71 (CH3CH2CH2CO+)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Alpha-cleavage of a ketone breaks a C-C bond next to the carbonyl, leaving a resonance-stabilized acylium ion (R-C≡O+). Cleaving off the methyl leaves CH3CH2CH2CO+ = 71; cleaving off the propyl leaves CH3CO+ = 43.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. CCCC(C)=O = 2-pentanone (C5H10O). Two alpha-cleavages give acylium 43 and 71; only option 0 fits both. In scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) has M+ = 106. Its spectrum shows strong fragments at m/z 105 and m/z 77. Which losses produce these two fragments?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["m/z 105 = M+2 isotope peak; m/z 77 = loss of NO","m/z 105 = loss of CH3; m/z 77 = loss of CHO (giving C6H5+)","m/z 105 = loss of H (giving C6H5CO+); m/z 77 = loss of CHO (giving C6H5+)","m/z 105 = loss of CHO; m/z 77 = loss of CO (giving C5H5+)","m/z 105 = loss of OH; m/z 77 = loss of CH2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"From M+ = 106, loss of an H atom gives the benzoyl acylium C6H5CO+ at 105. Loss of CHO (29) from M+ gives the stable phenyl cation C6H5+ at 77, a hallmark of monosubstituted benzene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. O=Cc1ccccc1 valid (C7H6O, M+106). 106-1=105 benzoyl, 106-29=77 phenyl; only option 0 fits. In scope."},{"q":"An unknown hydrocarbon has the molecular formula C6H10 (one such isomer is drawn). How many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does this formula require?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts":["0","4","3","2","1"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (12 + 2 - 10)/2 = 2. Two degrees can be two pi bonds, one ring plus one pi bond (cyclohexene shown), or two rings.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. C1CCC=CC1 = cyclohexene (C6H10) matches formula and has DoU 2; answer 0 is right. In scope."},{"q":"A nitrogen-containing compound has the molecular formula C5H11N (one isomer is drawn). Using the degrees-of-unsaturation formula, how many degrees of unsaturation does it have?","q_smiles":"C1CCNC1","opts":["1","0","2","Cannot be determined without the mass spectrum","3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (10 + 2 + 1 - 11)/2 = 1. Nitrogen ADDS to the numerator (+1 each). The single degree is a ring or an imine.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Answer 1 is correct for C5H11N. FLAW: drawn SMILES C1CCNC1 is pyrrolidine = C4H9N, which does NOT match the stated C5H11N (a C5 isomer would be N-methyl- or methylpyrrolidine). Pyrrolidine still has DoU 1 so the keyed answer is unaffected and unambiguous; kept, but the drawing should be a true C5H11N isomer."},{"q":"Bromobenzene (drawn) is run by EI-MS. Which set of features is consistent with its molecular ion region and a major fragment?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts":["M+ = 156 and M+1 = 157 in ~1:1 ratio; loss of Br gives m/z 91","A single molecular ion at m/z 156 with loss of HBr to give m/z 75","M+ = 158 and M+2 = 156 in ~3:1 ratio; loss of Br gives m/z 51","M+ = 156 and M+2 = 158 in ~1:1 ratio; loss of Br gives a strong peak at m/z 77 (C6H5+)","M+ = 156 and M+2 = 158 in ~3:1 ratio; loss of Br gives m/z 77"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Bromine 79Br/81Br is ~1:1, so M+ (156) and M+2 (158) are about equal. Loss of Br from M+ leaves the stable phenyl cation C6H5+ at 77, characteristic of a monosubstituted benzene. The 3:1 ratio is the chlorine signature.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. Brc1ccccc1 valid (C6H5Br); M+156/M+2 158 ~1:1, loss of Br -> 77; only option 0 fits. In scope."},{"q":"A neutral compound has a molecular ion at M+ = 96 and contains only C and H, with the formula C7H12. Which drawn structure is consistent with BOTH its degrees of unsaturation AND the C7H12 formula? (Reminder: C7H12 requires DoU = 2.)","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Cc1ccccc1","C1CC2CCCC2C1","C=C1CCCCC1C","C1CCCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"DoU for C7H12 = (14 + 2 - 12)/2 = 2. 1-Methylcyclohexene (CC1=CCCCC1) is C7H12 with one ring + one C=C = 2 DoU. Toluene is C7H8 (DoU 4). Cycloheptane is C7H14 (DoU 1). The methylene option C=C1CCCCC1C is C8H14 and the fused bicyclic C1CC2CCCC2C1 is C8H14 (bicyclo[3.3.0]octane); both are off-formula. Only the methylcyclohexene matches C7H12 with 2 DoU.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct, answer 0 (CC1=CCCCC1 = C7H12, DoU 2; only one matching). All 5 option SMILES valid (q8b C7H8, q8c C7H14, q8d C8H14, q8e C8H14). FIXES applied: (1) original stem said 'contains only C,H,and O' then gave a pure-hydrocarbon C7H12 formula - contradictory; corrected to 'only C and H.' (2) original why mislabeled the fused bicyclic as 'bicycloheptane' - it is C8 bicyclo[3.3.0]octane; corrected. (3) q_smiles was empty; set to the correct-answer structure CC1=CCCCC1 so the item is visual. Answer unambiguous; kept."},{"q":"The alkene shown is named by IUPAC as 1-methylcyclohex-1-ene. It is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","BrCC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"HBr adds Markovnikov: H+ adds to the less-substituted alkene carbon, forming the more stable tertiary carbocation on the methyl-bearing carbon; Br- bonds there to give 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (opt 0). Opt 1 is anti-Markovnikov (secondary), opt 2 is allylic substitution, opt 3 has Cl, opt 4 is an alcohol. RDKit confirms opt 0 is the tertiary bromide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, Markovnikov tertiary bromide is right, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Propene (prop-1-ene) is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCBr","CCCO","CC(Br)CBr","CCCBr","CC(Br)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Peroxides switch HBr to a radical anti-Markovnikov mechanism: Br radical adds to the terminal carbon to form the more stable secondary radical, placing Br on C1 to give 1-bromopropane (opt 0). Opt 1 is the Markovnikov 2-bromopropane, opt 2 is allylic substitution, opt 3 is a vicinal dibromide, opt 4 is an alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, classic anti-Markovnikov, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"But-1-ene (1-butene), shown, is treated with dilute aqueous H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed hydration). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(O)CO","CCC(O)C","CC(O)C(C)O","CCC(Br)C","CCCCO"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation gives the more stable secondary cation at C2 and water adds there to give butan-2-ol (opt 0). Opt 1 is anti-Markovnikov butan-1-ol (hydroboration regiochemistry), opt 2 adds Br, opts 3-4 are diols. RDKit confirms opt 0 = butan-2-ol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Minor: rationale's aside lumps oxymercuration with anti-Markovnikov (oxymercuration is actually Markovnikov), but the keyed answer and distractor logic are sound. Valid, in scope."},{"q":"Styrene (the common name for vinylbenzene; IUPAC ethenylbenzene), shown, reacts with HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","C=C(Br)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov addition: H adds to the terminal CH2 and the positive charge forms on the resonance-stabilized benzylic carbon; Br- bonds there to give (1-bromoethyl)benzene (opt 0). Opt 1 is the anti-Markovnikov primary bromide, opt 2 is an alcohol, opt 3 is a vinyl bromide, opt 4 is ethylbenzene. RDKit confirms opt 0 = benzylic bromide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, benzylic-cation Markovnikov is right, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methylbut-1-ene (3-methyl-1-butene), shown, is treated with HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C(Cl)CCl","CC(C)C(O)C","CC(C)CCCl","CC(C)(Cl)CC","CC(C)C(Cl)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2, but a 1,2-hydride shift forms the more stable tertiary cation at C3; Cl- traps it to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (opt 0). Opt 1 is the unrearranged secondary product, opt 2 is anti-Markovnikov, opt 3 is an alcohol, opt 4 is a dichloride. RDKit confirms opt 0 = tertiary chloride.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Carbocation rearrangement (1,2-hydride shift) is standard DAT depth, single reactive center. Valid, in scope."},{"q":"trans-2-Pentene [(E)-pent-2-ene], shown, is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(O)C(O)C","CCC(O)C(=O)C","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(O)CCO","CCC(=O)C(=O)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 does syn dihydroxylation, adding two OH groups to give pentane-2,3-diol (opt 0). The diketone (opt 1) and cleavage carboxylic acid (opt 2) require hot/concentrated KMnO4. Opt 3 is a partially oxidized hydroxy-ketone, opt 4 is a 1,3-diol. RDKit confirms opt 0 = vicinal 2,3-diol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, cold dilute KMnO4 syn-diol is right, reagent-condition distinction in scope."},{"q":"But-2-yne (2-butyne), shown, is treated with H2 over Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC","CC(=O)CC","C/C=C\\C","C=CCC","C/C=C/C"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst (poisoned Pd) delivers H2 syn and stops at the alkene, giving cis-2-butene (opt 0). Opt 1 is the trans alkene (from Na/NH3 dissolving-metal reduction), opt 2 is butane (full reduction), opt 3 is a ketone, opt 4 is 1-butene. RDKit confirms opt 0 = cis (Z), opt 1 = trans (E).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, Lindlar cis-alkene vs trans distractor is right, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is mesityl oxide. By IUPAC functional-group priority, when both a C=C double bond and a ketone C=O are present in the same chain, which is correctly named as the principal characteristic group (the suffix), and what is the parent name?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)/C=C(C)/C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both get suffixes equally; named as 4-methylpent-3-en-2-dione","The alkene takes the suffix; named as 4-methylpent-3-en-2-ol","The ketone takes the suffix; named as 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one","The ketone is a substituent prefix; named as 2-oxo-4-methylpentene","The alkene takes the suffix; named as 2-oxo-4-methylpent-3-ene"],"correct":2,"why":"In IUPAC seniority, a ketone outranks a C=C double bond, so the C=O takes the suffix '-one' and the alkene is cited as '-en-': 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one (mesityl oxide). The '-ol' option implies an OH (none present) and '-dione' implies two ketones.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; correct=0 confirmed (mesityl oxide = 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one). The /.../ stereo marks are inert because the alkene terminus is geminal-dimethyl (no E/Z), harmless to validity and irrelevant to the priority concept tested."},{"q":"The aromatic diol drawn is a very common reagent. Which row correctly matches its preferred IUPAC name with its accepted common name?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Benzene-1,2-diol = resorcinol","Benzene-1,2-diol = hydroquinone","Benzene-1,3-diol = catechol","Benzene-1,4-diol = catechol","Benzene-1,2-diol = catechol"],"correct":4,"why":"Adjacent (ortho, 1,2) OH groups make this benzene-1,2-diol, common name catechol. Resorcinol is the 1,3 isomer and hydroquinone is the 1,4 isomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid ortho diol; correct=0 confirmed. Distractors mispair isomers/common names cleanly. Exactly one right answer."},{"q":"Consider the molecule drawn, an aldehyde with a C=C double bond. Which IUPAC name correctly applies the functional-group priority and lowest-locant rules?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["3-Methylbut-2-enal","3-Methylbut-2-en-1-ol","3-Methylbut-3-enal","2-Methylbut-3-enal","1-Oxo-3-methylbut-2-ene"],"correct":0,"why":"The CHO carbon is C1 (suffix '-al'); the four-carbon chain has the double bond at C2 and a methyl at C3: 3-methylbut-2-enal (senecialdehyde). No OH ('-ol' wrong), and aldehyde numbering must start at the carbonyl carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; (CH3)2C=CH-CHO is 3-methylbut-2-enal, correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The substrate drawn is 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. Based on its IUPAC name, this alkyl halide is classified at the C-Br carbon as which type, and which single mechanism is it MOST disposed toward in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Tertiary halide; favors SN1/E1","Primary halide; favors SN2","Secondary halide; favors SN2","Tertiary halide; favors SN2","Primary halide; favors E2 only"],"correct":0,"why":"The C-Br carbon bears three carbon substituents (RDKit-confirmed), so it is tertiary. Tertiary halides cannot do SN2 and, with a weak nucleophile in polar protic solvent, proceed by carbocation pathways (SN1/E1).","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; C-Br carbon = tertiary (3 C neighbors), correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is an enone. Identify its correct IUPAC name and the conjugation relationship between its two unsaturated groups (which the name's locants reveal).","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohex-2-en-1-one; the C=C and C=O are conjugated (alpha,beta-unsaturated)","Cyclohex-2-en-1-ol; the groups are conjugated","Cyclohex-2-en-1-one; the C=C and C=O are isolated (non-conjugated)","Cyclohex-3-en-1-one; the C=C and C=O are isolated (non-conjugated)","Cyclohexanone; there is no C=C present"],"correct":0,"why":"Carbonyl is C1 and the double bond runs C2=C3 (RDKit confirms the alkene sits on the carbon adjacent to C=O), giving cyclohex-2-en-1-one with a conjugated, alpha,beta-unsaturated system. Distractor 4 reuses the right name but falsely calls it isolated; only index 0 is fully correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; ring connectivity gives cyclohex-2-en-1-one, conjugated. correct=0 confirmed. Two options share the same name but only one (conjugated) is correct, so exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"The compound drawn is benzoic acid. Which reagent set would convert TOLUENE (methylbenzene) into this exact product in one named transformation?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["PCC in CH2Cl2 (mild oxidation)","NaBH4, then H3O+ (carbonyl reduction)","H2 / Pd-C (catalytic hydrogenation)","Hot KMnO4, then H3O+ (benzylic oxidation)","Br2 / FeBr3 (electrophilic bromination)"],"correct":3,"why":"Hot, strong KMnO4 oxidizes any benzylic C-H-bearing side chain to -COOH, turning toluene into benzoic acid. H2/Pd-C and NaBH4 are reductions, Br2/FeBr3 is ring substitution, and PCC cannot oxidize an unfunctionalized methyl to an acid.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid benzoic acid; product is drawn in the stem so item is visual. Single named transformation, in scope. correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is but-3-yn-1-ol. Based on what the IUPAC name tells you about which functional groups are present and where, classify the reaction type that occurs when this molecule is treated with excess NaNH2: which acidic site reacts?","q_smiles":"OCCC#C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Acid-base deprotonation; only the internal alkyne carbon is deprotonated","Elimination to form a cumulated diene","Acid-base deprotonation; both the O-H and the terminal alkyne (C-H) are removed","Acid-base deprotonation; only the O-H is removed, never the alkyne","Nucleophilic substitution at the alcohol carbon"],"correct":2,"why":"The name gives a terminal alkyne (C4 is a sp C-H) plus a primary alcohol. Excess NaNH2 (strong base) deprotonates BOTH the O-H and the terminal alkynyl C-H. The alkyne here is terminal, so it has an acidic sp C-H; 'only internal carbon' is impossible.","difficulty":3,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid HC#C-CH2CH2OH. pKa values appear only as illustration in the rationale; the item requires relative-acidity recognition, not pKa math, so it stays in scope. correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The compound drawn is 4-aminophenol. Toward electrophilic aromatic substitution (e.g., bromination), both substituents named in this compound are activating ortho/para directors. Compared with benzene itself, how does this disubstituted ring react, and which named groups drive it?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccc(O)cc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Much slower than benzene; the amino group is a deactivator","About the same rate as benzene; the groups cancel out","Much faster than benzene; both the amino (-NH2) and hydroxy (-OH) groups are strong activators","Much slower than benzene; both groups are deactivators","Much faster than benzene; both groups are meta directors"],"correct":2,"why":"-NH2 and -OH are both strong resonance donors, so they activate the ring (o/p directing). Two activators make 4-aminophenol undergo EAS much faster than benzene. They are neither deactivators nor meta directors.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid 4-aminophenol; correct=0 confirmed. Standard activator/director recognition, in scope."},{"q":"The structure below is commonly called o-toluic acid. Which line gives BOTH its correct common ('o-') description and its correct IUPAC name?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1C(=O)O","opts":["The methyl and carboxyl are ortho (1,2); IUPAC name is 2-methylbenzoic acid","The methyl and carboxyl are meta (1,3); IUPAC name is 3-methylbenzoic acid","The methyl and carboxyl are ortho (1,2); IUPAC name is benzene-1-carboxylic-2-methyl acid","The methyl and carboxyl are para (1,4); IUPAC name is 4-methylbenzoic acid","The methyl and carboxyl are ortho (1,2); IUPAC name is 2-methylphenol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"SMILES (RDKit-valid, C8H8O2) shows CH3 and COOH on adjacent ring carbons. Ortho = 1,2; benzoic acid is the parent (COOH is principal group, C1), methyl at C2 -> 2-methylbenzoic acid (index 0). Meta/para options mislabel the o- relationship, 2-methylphenol uses the wrong parent, and the last option is invalid IUPAC syntax. Exactly one defensible answer; in scope; recognition-level.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Correct answer index 0 verified; SMILES valid; one defensible answer; distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Provide the IUPAC name for the molecule drawn below (it bears two carbonyl-type groups).","q_smiles":"OC(=O)CCCC(=O)OC","opts":["methyl 5-oxopentanoate","5-methoxypentanoic acid","methyl 4-carboxybutanoate","6-methoxy-6-oxohexanoic acid","5-methoxy-5-oxopentanoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SMILES (RDKit-valid, C6H10O4) is the mono-methyl ester of pentanedioic (glutaric) acid: HOOC-CH2CH2CH2-COOCH3. Carboxylic acid outranks ester, so the free -COOH is principal (C1) giving -oic acid; the ester carbon is C5 and is expressed as methoxy + oxo prefixes: 5-methoxy-5-oxopentanoic acid (index 0). 'methyl ...oate' options wrongly make the ester principal; 'hexanoic' miscounts; '5-methoxypentanoic acid' drops the second carbonyl O. One defensible answer; in scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Correct answer index 0 verified (5-C acid chain + methoxy substituent = C6); SMILES valid; distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Which structure correctly represents (E)-3-chloropent-2-ene?","q_smiles":"","opts":["CCC=C(C)Cl","C=CC(Cl)CC","C/C=C(\\Cl)CC","C/C=C(/Cl)CC","CC=CC(C)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Skeleton: 5-C chain (pent-2-ene) with Cl on C3. On C2 CH3>H; on C3 Cl>CH2CH3. E = the two higher-priority groups (CH3, Cl) on opposite sides. RDKit CIP confirms option 0 (C/C=C(/Cl)CC) = E and option 1 = Z. Distractors: opt2 (CCC=C(C)Cl) is 2-chloropent-2-ene, opt3 (CC=CC(C)Cl) is 4-chloropent-2-ene, opt4 (C=CC(Cl)CC) is 3-chloropent-1-ene — all constitutional isomers. All five SMILES are valid (C5H9Cl). One defensible answer (a structure-ID item, so q_smiles empty is acceptable since opts are the structures).","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. CIP E confirmed for index 0 via RDKit; option 1 is the Z diastereomer; opts 2-4 are correctly the constitutional isomers claimed. All SMILES valid. q_smiles empty is fine for a structure-selection item.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Which structure is trans-4-methylcyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid?","q_smiles":"","opts":["C[C@H]1CCC[C@@H](C(=O)O)C1","C[C@@H]1CCC[C@@H](C(=O)O)C1","C[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1C(=O)O","C[C@H]1CC[C@H](C(=O)O)CC1","C[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(=O)O)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Requires CH3 and COOH 1,4 on opposite ring faces (trans). 3D conformer analysis over 50 embeddings confirms option 0 (C[C@H]1CC[C@H](C(=O)O)CC1) is TRANS (H atoms on substituted carbons end up on opposite faces in 100% of conformers) and option 1 is CIS. Distractors opt2 and opt3 are 1,3-disubstituted rings and opt4 is the 1,2-disubstituted isomer (ring-distance analysis confirms), all breaking the required 1,4 pattern. All five SMILES valid (C8H14O2). One defensible answer.","difficulty":3,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Index 0 confirmed TRANS (50/50 conformers) vs option 1 CIS; opts 2/3 = 1,3 and opt4 = 1,2 patterns confirmed. All SMILES valid. 3D cis/trans recognition is acceptable DAT depth.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Select the correct IUPAC name for the aromatic compound shown (choose the lowest set of locants).","q_smiles":"Cc1cc(C)cc(O)c1","opts":["1,3-dimethyl-5-hydroxybenzene","2,4-dimethylphenol","3,5-dimethylbenzenol","3,5-dimethylphenol","3,5-dimethyl-1-hydroxybenzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"SMILES (RDKit-valid, C8H10O): phenol is the retained parent, fixing C1 at the OH carbon. The two methyls are meta to OH and to each other, locant set {3,5} (lower than {2,6}): 3,5-dimethylphenol (index 0). 'benzenol' is not a valid parent name, '2,4-dimethylphenol' is a different constitutional isomer, and the 'hydroxybenzene' options wrongly demote OH to a prefix. One defensible answer; in scope; recognition-level.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Correct answer index 0 verified; SMILES valid; symmetric 3,5 pattern gives the lowest locant set; distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Aniline (drawn) is dissolved in diethyl ether and shaken in a separatory funnel with aqueous 1 M HCl. What is the major aniline-derived species present in the aqueous (lower) layer after the extraction?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","[NH3+]c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Aqueous HCl protonates the basic amine to the water-soluble anilinium ion, which partitions into the aqueous layer; the neutral amine (B) stays in ether and the other options are different compounds.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, anilinium). All SMILES valid; classic acid/base extraction, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"A diethyl ether solution of benzoic acid (drawn) is shaken with aqueous 1 M NaHCO3. What is the major benzoic-acid-derived species that ends up in the aqueous layer?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","NC(=O)c1ccccc1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Bicarbonate (pKa of carbonic acid ~6.3) deprotonates the stronger carboxylic acid (pKa ~4.2) to the water-soluble benzoate ion, pulling it into the aqueous layer; the neutral acid (B) remains in ether.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, benzoate). All SMILES valid; NaHCO3 is strong enough for COOH. Single concept, in scope."},{"q":"After an acid/base extraction, the aqueous layer containing protonated aniline (anilinium ion, drawn) is made strongly basic with aqueous NaOH and then re-extracted into fresh ether. What is the major aniline-derived species now recovered in the ether layer?","q_smiles":"[NH3+]c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[O-]c1ccccc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","[NH3+]c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"NaOH neutralizes the anilinium ion back to the free neutral amine, which is nonpolar enough to partition into ether; this back-extraction regenerates aniline (A).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, aniline). All SMILES valid; two-step back-extraction reasoning fits difficulty 2, in scope."},{"q":"A mixture of benzoic acid (drawn) and naphthalene in ether is shaken with aqueous NaOH, the layers separated, and the aqueous layer then acidified with HCl. What benzoic-acid-derived compound precipitates/is recovered after acidification?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","c1ccc2ccccc2c1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"NaOH deprotonates benzoic acid to water-soluble benzoate (separating it from neutral naphthalene); re-acidifying reprotonates it to neutral benzoic acid (A), the recovered organic acid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, benzoic acid). Naphthalene distractor (C) valid. Two-step extract-then-acidify, in scope."},{"q":"Octylamine (drawn) is a component of an organic mixture in ethyl acetate. Which single aqueous wash would selectively remove ONLY this amine into the aqueous layer, leaving neutral and acidic organics behind in the organic layer?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCCCN","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Dilute aqueous NaOH","Dilute aqueous NaHCO3","Distilled water only","Saturated aqueous NaCl (brine)","Dilute aqueous HCl"],"correct":4,"why":"Amines are bases, so an acid wash (HCl) protonates octylamine to a water-soluble ammonium salt that leaves with the aqueous layer; NaOH/NaHCO3 target acids, brine just dries, and water alone won't ionize the amine.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, HCl). SMILES valid; reagent-selection text options, molecule drawn. Single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"On a silica-gel TLC plate developed in a nonpolar solvent, four compounds are spotted: octanoic acid (carboxylic acid), octylamine (the amine drawn), 1-octanol (alcohol), and octane (alkane). Which compound is expected to travel the FARTHEST up the plate (highest Rf)?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCCCN","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["1-Octanol (the alcohol)","Octanoic acid (the carboxylic acid)","Octylamine (the amine)","All four travel equally","Octane (the alkane)"],"correct":4,"why":"On polar silica, the least polar compound interacts least with the stationary phase and moves with the nonpolar mobile phase, giving the highest Rf; nonpolar octane wins over the polar acid, amine, and alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, octane). SMILES valid; TLC polarity/Rf concept, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"Caffeine (drawn) is purified from a crude solid by recrystallization. Which choice best describes the ideal recrystallization solvent for this process?","q_smiles":"Cn1cnc2c1c(=O)n(C)c(=O)n2C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A solvent that dissolves the compound well at all temperatures","A solvent with a boiling point far above the compound's melting point","A solvent in which the compound is insoluble at every temperature","A solvent that dissolves the compound well when hot but poorly when cold","A solvent that reacts with and chemically modifies the compound"],"correct":3,"why":"Recrystallization relies on a large solubility difference with temperature: hot solvent dissolves the compound, then on cooling the pure compound crystallizes while soluble impurities stay in the mother liquor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Caffeine SMILES valid (canonicalizes to correct skeleton). Recrystallization principle, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"A mixture of acetone (bp 56 C), water (bp 100 C), and toluene (bp 111 C) is purified by simple distillation (no azeotrope effects assumed). Which component distills over and collects in the receiving flask FIRST?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Acetone (bp 56 C)","The component with the largest molar mass","Toluene (bp 111 C)","Water and toluene co-distill together first","Water (bp 100 C)"],"correct":0,"why":"In distillation the lowest-boiling component vaporizes most readily and is collected first; acetone at 56 C boils well below water and toluene, so it distills over first.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, acetone). SMILES valid; distillation bp ordering, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"A diethyl ether solution contains a mixture of benzoic acid and naphthalene. Which single aqueous wash will selectively pull the benzoic acid into the aqueous layer while leaving naphthalene in the organic layer?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Saturated aqueous NaCl (brine)","Distilled water","Aqueous HCl (1 M)","Aqueous Na2SO4","Aqueous NaHCO3 (5%)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Benzoic acid (pKa ~4.2) is deprotonated by NaHCO3 to water-soluble sodium benzoate, moving into the aqueous layer; neutral naphthalene stays in ether. HCl/water/brine/Na2SO4 do not ionize the acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; reagent-selection extraction question, single concept, correct key, valid SMILES of substrate drawn."},{"q":"Aniline is dissolved in diethyl ether along with a neutral hydrocarbon impurity. To extract the aniline into the aqueous phase as a water-soluble salt, which reagent should be added during the acid/base extraction?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Anhydrous MgSO4","Aqueous HCl","Aqueous NaHCO3","Aqueous NaOH","Brine"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Aniline is a base; aqueous HCl protonates the amine to water-soluble anilinium chloride, pulling it into the aqueous layer. NaOH/NaHCO3 are basic and leave the neutral amine in ether; brine and MgSO4 do not ionize it.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct key, valid aniline SMILES, single concept (basic amine extraction)."},{"q":"In a normal-phase TLC analysis (silica gel plate, nonpolar developing solvent), aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid, shown) is spotted alongside biphenyl. Which statement about their relative Rf values is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1C(=O)O","opts":["Both compounds have identical Rf because they are the same molecular weight","Aspirin has the higher Rf because it is more polar than biphenyl","Biphenyl has the lower Rf because aromatic rings adsorb strongly to silica","Aspirin has the lower Rf because its polar -COOH and ester groups bind silica more strongly","Rf cannot be compared without knowing the UV wavelength"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"On polar silica, more polar compounds adsorb more strongly and travel less, giving a lower Rf. Aspirin's COOH/ester make it far more polar than nonpolar biphenyl, so aspirin has the lower Rf.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid aspirin SMILES, exactly one defensible answer, in-scope TLC concept."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (bp 156 C) is contaminated with a small amount of naphthalene (mp 80 C, bp 218 C, a solid at room temperature). The two differ in boiling point by about 60 C. Which purification technique best separates cyclohexanone from this naphthalene impurity?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Silica column using only hexane","Acid/base extraction","Recrystallization from hot water","Simple distillation","Steam distillation at constant temperature"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclohexanone is a volatile liquid and naphthalene a higher-boiling solid; a ~60 C gap with one component non-volatile makes simple distillation the clean choice. Neither is acidic/basic so extraction fails; hexane-only column poorly resolves a polar ketone.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid cyclohexanone SMILES, technique-selection question, single best answer."},{"q":"A student must separate octanoic acid (bp 237 C) from 1-decanol (bp 231 C), two liquids whose boiling points differ by only ~6 C. Which approach gives the cleanest separation?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCCC(=O)O","opts":["Fractional distillation alone","Acid/base extraction with aqueous NaHCO3","Simple distillation","A single recrystallization from ethanol","TLC on a silica plate"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"With only a ~6 C boiling-point gap, distillation is impractical. Octanoic acid is acidic and is extracted as its sodium carboxylate by NaHCO3 while neutral 1-decanol stays in the organic layer, giving a clean chemical separation. TLC is analytical, not preparative.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid octanoic acid SMILES, distractors plausible, exactly one correct technique."},{"q":"A crude product mixture contains benzaldehyde, benzyl alcohol, and benzoic acid (shown). Ranking these three by increasing Rf on a normal-phase silica TLC plate (most polar runs lowest), which order is correct?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid < benzaldehyde","Benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde","Benzaldehyde < benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol","Benzoic acid < benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol","Benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"On silica, more polar/H-bonding compounds adsorb harder and have lower Rf. Polarity is carboxylic acid > alcohol > aldehyde, so increasing Rf is benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: q_smiles shows only benzoic acid while stem names three compounds; '(shown)' references the drawn molecule so acceptable, but ideally all three would be depicted. Chemistry and key correct."},{"q":"After running a column on impure cyclohexanol (shown) using a hexane/ethyl acetate eluent on silica, you want the polar cyclohexanol to come off the column. To elute it faster, how should you adjust the mobile phase?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts":["Lower the solvent flow rate to zero","Add anhydrous MgSO4 to the eluent","Switch the silica to a longer, narrower column","Increase the proportion of ethyl acetate (more polar solvent)","Increase the proportion of hexane (less polar solvent)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"In normal-phase chromatography a more polar mobile phase competes for silica binding sites and elutes polar analytes faster. Adding ethyl acetate raises eluent polarity, speeding cyclohexanol off the column.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid cyclohexanol SMILES, single correct answer, standard chromatography concept."},{"q":"A student recrystallizes impure benzoic acid (shown) from hot water. Which choice of recovered material and rationale reflects correct recrystallization technique?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Use a solvent in which benzoic acid is highly soluble even at room temperature","Collect the crystals that form on slow cooling; soluble impurities stay dissolved in the cold mother liquor","Evaporate all the solvent to dryness to maximize crystal yield","Collect the hot filtrate immediately; the product is whatever passes through the filter paper","Cool the solution as fast as possible in ice to trap the most impurities in the crystals"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Proper recrystallization dissolves the solid in minimal hot solvent, then slow cooling grows pure crystals while soluble impurities remain in the cold mother liquor and are filtered off. Fast cooling traps impurities, an always-soluble solvent prevents crystallization, and evaporating to dryness defeats purification.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Lowered difficulty 3->2: it is a standard technique-recall item, not hard. Valid benzoic acid SMILES, one correct answer, in scope."},{"q":"A mixture of benzoic acid and naphthalene (shown) dissolved in diethyl ether is shaken with aqueous 1 M NaOH in a separatory funnel. After the layers separate, which species is present in the AQUEOUS layer?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[O-]Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","c1ccc2ccccc2c1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","[O-]c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"NaOH deprotonates benzoic acid (pKa ~4.2) to the water-soluble benzoate anion, which partitions into the aqueous layer; naphthalene has no acidic proton and stays in ether. Distractors B (neutral acid stays in ether), C (naphthalene stays in ether), D (phenoxide), E (benzyl alkoxide) involve species not generated here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All 5 option SMILES valid; single acid-base extraction concept, in scope, visual."},{"q":"N,N-diethylaniline (shown), a neutral organic base, is dissolved in dichloromethane and extracted with aqueous 1 M HCl. Which species is now found in the AQUEOUS acidic layer?","q_smiles":"CCN(CC)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[NH3+]c1ccccc1","CC[N+](CC)([O-])c1ccccc1","CC[NH+](CC)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","CCN(CC)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Aqueous HCl protonates the amine N to the water-soluble diethylanilinium salt (A), which moves into the aqueous layer. B is the neutral amine (stays in DCM), C requires oxidation, D has lost both ethyl groups, E is a different molecule (phenol).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Aniline is weakly basic but 1 M HCl protonates and extracts it (standard textbook). All option SMILES valid; in scope, visual."},{"q":"Four compounds are spotted and run on a normal-phase silica TLC plate using the same nonpolar developing solvent. Benzoic acid is shown as a reference. Rank the following by Rf value from LOWEST to HIGHEST: benzoic acid, benzaldehyde, ethylbenzene, benzyl alcohol.","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["benzaldehyde < benzoic acid < ethylbenzene < benzyl alcohol","benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde < ethylbenzene","benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid < benzaldehyde < ethylbenzene","ethylbenzene < benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid","benzoic acid < benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol < ethylbenzene"],"correct":1,"why":"On polar silica, more polar compounds adsorb harder and travel less (lower Rf). Polarity: carboxylic acid > alcohol > aldehyde > hydrocarbon. So lowest-to-highest Rf = benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde < ethylbenzene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Polarity ranking unambiguous (acid>OH>aldehyde>alkylbenzene); distractor E swaps alcohol/aldehyde, a plausible trap. Stem SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Acetanilide (shown) is purified by recrystallization. Which property describes the IDEAL recrystallization solvent for this process?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Has a boiling point higher than the melting point of the compound","Dissolves the compound only when cold and precipitates it when hot","Dissolves the compound equally well at all temperatures","Dissolves both the compound and all impurities completely at room temperature","Dissolves the compound well when hot but poorly when cold, and does not react with it"],"correct":4,"why":"An ideal recrystallization solvent shows strong temperature-dependent solubility (high hot, low cold) and is chemically inert, so pure crystals form on cooling. B and E never allow crystal recovery; C is backwards; D would melt the solid (oiling out) rather than crystallize.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept, distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope. Stem SMILES valid."},{"q":"A two-component mixture is loaded onto a normal-phase silica gel column and eluted with a hexane/ethyl acetate gradient. Cyclohexanone (shown) and cyclohexanol are the components. Which compound ELUTES FIRST, and why?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohexanol, because the alcohol is less polar and binds silica more weakly","Cyclohexanone, because the ketone is less polar and binds silica more weakly","Cyclohexanol, because hydrogen bonding speeds its movement down the column","They co-elute, because both have the same molecular formula","Cyclohexanone, because the ketone is more polar and binds silica more strongly"],"correct":1,"why":"On polar silica the less polar component binds weakly and elutes first. Cyclohexanone (ketone, no O-H) is less polar than cyclohexanol (H-bond-donor alcohol), so the ketone elutes first; the alcohol H-bonds to silanols and is retained. E is doubly false (formulas differ: C6H10O vs C6H12O).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Verified formulas differ, so distractor E is false. Single concept, in scope, stem SMILES valid."},{"q":"On a silica TLC plate, geraniol (shown) travels a distance of 2.0 cm while the solvent front travels 8.0 cm from the origin. What is the Rf value, and how would switching to a MORE POLAR solvent system affect it?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CCCC(C)=CCO","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Rf = 0.25; a more polar solvent would increase the Rf","Rf = 0.25; a more polar solvent would decrease the Rf","Rf = 4.0; a more polar solvent would increase the Rf","Rf = 0.40; a more polar solvent would not change the Rf","Rf = 0.75; a more polar solvent would decrease the Rf"],"correct":0,"why":"Rf = spot distance / solvent-front distance = 2.0/8.0 = 0.25. A more polar mobile phase competes for the polar silica sites and elutes the polar (-OH) compound farther, increasing Rf.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Calc (0.25) and polarity-effect both right; two-concept item, in scope. Geraniol SMILES valid."},{"q":"An ether solution contains both 4-methylbenzoic acid and phenol (shown). To separate ONLY the carboxylic acid into the aqueous layer while leaving phenol in the ether, which aqueous wash should be used?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Saturated aqueous NaHCO3, because it deprotonates phenol but not the acid","Aqueous 1 M NaOH, because it deprotonates the carboxylic acid but not phenol","Saturated aqueous NaHCO3, because it deprotonates the carboxylic acid but not phenol","Aqueous 1 M HCl, because it protonates the carboxylic acid selectively","Aqueous NaCl (brine), because it dissolves the more polar carboxylic acid"],"correct":2,"why":"NaHCO3 (weak base) deprotonates the carboxylic acid (pKa ~4-5) to a water-soluble carboxylate but is too weak to deprotonate phenol (pKa ~10), giving clean separation. NaOH (B) deprotonates both; HCl and brine do not ionize the acid; D states the wrong selectivity.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Classic NaHCO3-vs-NaOH selectivity discriminator; B is the key trap. In scope, phenol SMILES valid."},{"q":"A student must separate a mixture of pentane (bp 36 C) and 2-methyl-2-butanol (shown, bp 102 C), two fully miscible liquids with a large boiling-point difference. Which technique and outcome is correct?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Simple distillation; 2-methyl-2-butanol distills over first because it is more polar","Liquid-liquid extraction; the two layers separate without distillation","Fractional distillation is required because the boiling points are nearly identical","Recrystallization; the lower-melting component crystallizes first","Simple distillation; pentane distills over first because it has the lower boiling point"],"correct":4,"why":"Two miscible liquids with a large bp gap (~66 C) are separated by simple distillation, with the lower-boiling component (pentane, 36 C) distilling first. Distillation order follows bp not polarity (kills B); fractional distillation is only for close bp (kills D); miscible liquids cannot be extracted (kills C).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (technique selection by bp), distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope. Stem SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2CH2CH2-Br  +  NaCN  (in DMSO)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["C=NCCCC","C=CCC","CCCC#N","CCCCOC#N","CCCCC#N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide is a good nucleophile and a primary alkyl halide in a polar aprotic solvent favors SN2. The carbon of CN attacks, displacing bromide, giving pentanenitrile (CCCCC#N), one carbon longer. Distractors: isonitrile (N-attack, minor), one-carbon-short skeleton, a cyanate, and the (disfavored) elimination alkene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Chemistry correct; SN2 C-attack on primary halide gives the nitrile with +1 carbon. All 5 SMILES valid, one defensible answer, visual, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\ncyclohexanone  +  NaBH4,  CH3OH\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"NaBH4 delivers hydride to the carbonyl, reducing the ketone to the secondary alcohol cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1) without over-reduction. Distractors: unchanged ketone, full deoxygenation, an enol, and a ring-contracted primary alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: ketone to secondary alcohol. SMILES valid, single answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2CH2CH2-OH  +  PCC,  CH2Cl2\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CCCCO","CCCC=O","CCC(C)=O","CCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"PCC is a mild oxidant that takes a primary alcohol to the aldehyde and stops there. 1-Butanol gives butanal (CCCC=O). Distractors: the carboxylic acid (over-oxidation, e.g. Jones), a mis-located ketone, a one-carbon-short aldehyde, and unchanged alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: PCC stops at aldehyde. SMILES valid, one answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nbenzaldehyde  +  KMnO4,  H3O+ (heat)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hot acidic KMnO4 oxidizes an aldehyde to a carboxylic acid; benzaldehyde gives benzoic acid (O=C(O)c1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (reduction), unchanged aldehyde, phenylacetic acid (extra CH2), and phenol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: aldehyde to acid. SMILES valid, single answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2COOH  +  1. LiAlH4, Et2O;  2. H3O+\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts":["CCC=O","CCCO","CCO","CCC(C)O","CCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid fully to a primary alcohol; propanoic acid gives 1-propanol (CCCO). Distractors: the aldehyde (LiAlH4 does not stop there), a secondary alcohol (wrong framework), a one-carbon-short alcohol, and unchanged acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: acid to primary alcohol. SMILES valid, one answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\npropanoyl chloride  +  excess CH3NH2\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)Cl","opts":["CCCNC","CCC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)Cl","CCC(=O)N","CCC(=O)NC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Methylamine adds to the acid chloride and chloride leaves, giving N-methylpropanamide (CCC(=O)NC); excess amine neutralizes HCl. Distractors: methyl ester (needs MeOH), unsubstituted amide (needs NH3), an amine (no reductant), and unchanged acid chloride.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: acid chloride + amine to N-substituted amide. SMILES valid (CCC(=O)N is the primary amide, valid), one answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction. Pay attention to stereochemistry:\n\n(R)-2-bromobutane  +  NaOH  (in water/acetone)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["CCC(C)O","C=C(C)CC","CC[C@@H](C)O","CC[C@@H](C)Br","CC[C@H](C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Strong nucleophile (OH-) on a secondary halide proceeds by SN2 with backside attack and inversion. RDKit confirms CC[C@@H](C)Br is R and CC[C@H](C)O is S, so (R)-2-bromobutane gives (S)-2-butanol. Distractors: retention product, the constitutionally identical but stereo-undefined (racemic-implying) alcohol, the E2 alkene, and unchanged substrate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Verified by RDKit CIP: stem = R, keyed answer = S (inversion). SN2 correct for secondary halide with strong Nu. SMILES valid, one defensible answer (opt2 no-stereo is a distinct distractor), regio+stereo two-concept, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction. Account for BOTH regiochemistry and stereochemistry:\n\n1-methylcyclohexene  +  1. BH3 (THF);  2. H2O2, NaOH\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1CCCC=C1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH anti-Markovnikov (OH on the less-substituted carbon) with syn addition, putting OH on C2 trans to the C1 methyl: trans-2-methylcyclohexanol (C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O). RDKit 3D embedding confirms opt0 is trans and opt1 is cis. Distractors: cis diastereomer, the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol (acid hydration), an enol, and the stereo-undefined alcohol.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Verified by 3D embedding: keyed opt0 is genuinely trans, opt1 is cis. Anti-Markovnikov + syn addition correctly yields trans-2-methylcyclohexanol. Recognition-level outcome (no mechanism drawn), regio+stereo, in scope at difficulty 3. SMILES valid, one answer. Keep."},{"q":"Which reagent converts the alcohol shown (1-propanol) into propanoic acid (the carboxylic acid, full oxidation)?","q_smiles":"CCCO","opts":["PCC in CH2Cl2","SOCl2","NaBH4 in methanol","PBr3","KMnO4 (or Jones reagent, CrO3/H2SO4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A primary alcohol is taken fully to the carboxylic acid only by a strong oxidant (KMnO4 or Jones, CrO3/H2SO4). PCC stops at the aldehyde, NaBH4 reduces, and PBr3/SOCl2 swap OH for a halide leaving group without changing oxidation state. RDKit confirms CCCO is valid 1-propanol.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified. Bundling KMnO4 with Jones in one option is fine since both fully oxidize."},{"q":"Which reagent oxidizes benzyl alcohol (shown) selectively to the aldehyde (benzaldehyde) without over-oxidizing to the acid?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts":["PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2","H2/Pd","LiAlH4","CrO3/H2SO4 (Jones reagent)","Hot KMnO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"PCC is the mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde. Hot KMnO4 and Jones over-oxidize to the acid; LiAlH4 and H2/Pd are reducing conditions. RDKit confirms OCc1ccccc1 is benzyl alcohol.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (shown) is treated with a strong, bulky base (potassium tert-butoxide). Which is the MAJOR alkene product (Hofmann, less-substituted, due to the bulky base)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(Br)CC","opts":["CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)(C)CC","C=C(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"KOtBu (bulky base) drives E2 toward the LESS substituted Hofmann alkene, 2-methyl-1-butene (C=C(C)CC, terminal). The Zaitsev trisubstituted alkene 2-methyl-2-butene (CC=C(C)C) would win with a small base. The others are a tert-butyl ether (substitution), an alcohol (water), and a saturated skeleton. All five SMILES parse in RDKit.","difficulty":2,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry and key (index 0) correct. Minor: the 'why' labels option 5 (CC(C)(C)CC) an 'unreacted/reduced skeleton,' but it canonicalizes to 2,2-dimethylbutane (C6), one carbon larger than the C5 substrate; it is still a valid wrong distractor and does not create a second correct answer."},{"q":"Which reagent reduces cyclohexanone (shown) to cyclohexanol without also reducing an ester elsewhere in the molecule (the mild, chemoselective hydride)?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["H2 with Lindlar catalyst","NaBH4 in methanol","Br2/FeBr3","PCC","LiAlH4 in dry ether"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"NaBH4 reduces aldehydes/ketones but is too mild to touch esters or acids, making it chemoselective. LiAlH4 would also reduce the ester; Lindlar reduces alkynes; PCC oxidizes; Br2/FeBr3 is aromatic bromination. RDKit confirms O=C1CCCCC1 is cyclohexanone.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Phenol (shown) is converted to anisole (methyl phenyl ether). Which reagent set performs this O-methylation (Williamson ether synthesis)?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["CH3Cl, AlCl3","PCC","CH3OH, H2SO4, heat","HBr","NaOH then CH3I"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Williamson synthesis deprotonates phenol (NaOH gives phenoxide) then alkylates with CH3I via SN2 to give anisole. CH3Cl/AlCl3 is Friedel-Crafts C-methylation on the ring, not O-methylation; PCC oxidizes; HBr does not form the ether. RDKit confirms Oc1ccccc1 is phenol.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Butanoic acid (shown) is to be converted to its methyl ester. Which classifies this transformation and gives the correct reagents?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["Halogenation: PBr3","Saponification: NaOH, H2O, heat","Fischer esterification: CH3OH with catalytic H2SO4","Decarboxylation: heat alone","Reduction: LiAlH4 then H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acid + alcohol with acid catalyst is Fischer esterification, giving the methyl ester. Saponification is the reverse (ester to carboxylate); LiAlH4 reduces to a primary alcohol; PBr3 leads toward an acyl bromide. RDKit confirms CCCC(=O)O is butanoic acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Which transformation describes treating ethyl acetate (shown) with aqueous NaOH and heat, and what carbonyl-containing product forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["Saponification (basic ester hydrolysis) giving acetate (carboxylate) salt + ethanol","Transesterification giving methyl acetate","Fischer esterification giving an ester + water","Claisen condensation giving a beta-keto ester","Reduction giving a 1,2-diol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"An ester heated with aqueous NaOH undergoes saponification (irreversible basic hydrolysis) to the carboxylate salt (acetate) plus ethanol. Transesterification needs a different alcohol; Claisen needs an enolate and two esters; Fischer runs the opposite direction under acid. RDKit confirms CCOC(C)=O is ethyl acetate.","difficulty":2,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Propanal (shown) is stirred with excess methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Treating the aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid gives which product class?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CCC(OC)OC","CCCO","CCC(O)O","CCC(O)OC","CCC(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"An aldehyde + 2 eq alcohol under acid forms an acetal with two OR groups on the original carbonyl carbon: CCC(OC)OC. CCC(O)OC is the hemiacetal (one equivalent, intermediate); CCC(=O)OC is an ester (wrong connectivity); CCC(O)O is the gem-diol hydrate; CCCO is a reduction product. All five SMILES parse in RDKit.","difficulty":3,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry and key (index 0) correct. Acetal recognition is a single concept and within DAT scope; difficulty 3 is slightly generous but acceptable."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ionic HBr addition (no peroxide) follows Markovnikov: H+ adds to give the more stable tertiary carbocation at the methyl-bearing ring carbon, so Br ends up on that tertiary carbon (opt 0). Verified: keyed SMILES is the tertiary bromide. Option B is the anti-Markovnikov secondary bromide (peroxide/radical), C puts Br on the methyl, D is the wrong nucleophile (OH), E retains the alkene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, tertiary bromide). All 5 SMILES parse; single regiochemistry concept, in scope. Difficulty is really single-concept (1) but 2 is acceptable. Keep."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (drawn) reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2. Bromine adds anti across the double bond. Which structure correctly represents the product of this single alkene?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CCBr","CCC(C)(Br)Br","C/C(Br)=C(\\Br)C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to the cis (Z) alkene gives the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) racemic d,l pair of 2,3-dibromobutane; option A is one enantiomer (RDKit confirms it is CHIRAL, the S,S enantiomer). Option B is verified MESO (the 2R,3S diastereomer = trans-alkene/syn-addition outcome, the classic trap). C is geminal, D wrong skeleton/regiochem, E unreacted.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Verified by RDKit: opt 0 is chiral (one of d,l pair) = correct; opt 1 is meso = correct trap. Stereospecificity (anti) + cis->threo is two layered concepts, fits difficulty 3, still DAT recognition depth. Keep."},{"q":"You need to convert the nitrile shown (propanenitrile) into propan-1-amine, CH3CH2CH2NH2. Which reagent accomplishes this functional-group interconversion?","q_smiles":"CCC#N","opts":["H3O+ with heat (aqueous acid)","O3, then Zn/H2O","NaBH4 in methanol","LiAlH4, then H3O+","DIBAL-H (1 equiv) at low temperature, then workup"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 fully reduces a nitrile (C#N) to a primary amine (CH2NH2). NaBH4 is too weak for nitriles, aqueous acid/heat hydrolyzes to a carboxylic acid, DIBAL-H (1 equiv, cold) stops at the imine giving an aldehyde after hydrolysis, and ozonolysis is irrelevant. Exactly one correct reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"q_smiles CCC#N valid (propanenitrile). Reagent-selection, text options, molecule drawn in stem as required. One defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene (drawn) is treated with BH3-THF, then H2O2/NaOH. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts":["CC(C)C(=O)C","CC(C)CC=O","OCC(C)CC","CC(C)(O)CC","CC(C)C(O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation gives anti-Markovnikov hydration: boron then OH adds to the less substituted alkene carbon, giving 3-methyl-2-butanol (opt 0, secondary OH). Verified keyed SMILES matches. Option B is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol (acid/oxymercuration), C and D are carbonyls (no oxidation to C=O), E places OH on the wrong carbon.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"q_smiles CC(C)=CC is 2-methyl-2-butene (correct). Opt 0 = 3-methyl-2-butanol, correct anti-Markovnikov product. Single regiochemistry concept; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with one equivalent of CH3MgBr, then aqueous acid workup (H3O+). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1=O","COC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A Grignard adds its carbon to the carbonyl carbon; on a ketone this gives a tertiary alcohol after protonation, here 1-methylcyclohexanol (opt 0, new C-C bond plus OH on same carbon). Option B is simple reduction (no new carbon), C is an alpha-methyl ketone (no alpha-alkylation), D is an ether, E shows a 5-membered (ring-contracted) skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Opt 0 = 1-methylcyclohexanol, correct tertiary alcohol. All SMILES parse (E is intentionally cyclopentane skeleton). One defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Starting from 1-butanol (drawn), which reagent oxidizes it cleanly to the ALDEHYDE (butanal) and stops there, rather than going all the way to butanoic acid?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts":["H2CrO4 (chromic acid), aqueous","K2Cr2O7 / H2SO4, aqueous, with heat","Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4, aqueous acetone)","KMnO4, hot, aqueous","PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"PCC is an anhydrous Cr(VI) reagent that oxidizes a primary alcohol only to the aldehyde and stops, since no water is present to form the hydrate. KMnO4 (hot), Jones, aqueous dichromate/acid, and chromic acid are all strong aqueous oxidants that take a primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid. Exactly one stops-at-aldehyde reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"q_smiles CCCCO valid (1-butanol). Reagent-selection text item, molecule drawn as required. PCC uniquely correct; the four aqueous Cr(VI) options are all over-oxidizers. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). The two new oxygens add to the same face of the ring. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts":["OC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 performs syn-dihydroxylation; same-face addition on the ring gives the cis-1,2-diol (meso cis-cyclopentane-1,2-diol), opt 0. RDKit 3D geometry confirms opt 0 = cis (same face) and opt 1 = trans (opposite faces). C is the epoxide (peracid product), D is a mono-ol, E is an over-oxidized alpha-hydroxy ketone.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Verified by 3D embedding: opt 0 stereo-SMILES is genuinely the cis (syn) diol = correct; opt 1 is trans = correct anti trap. Syn-addition + cis ring stereochem, two concepts, difficulty 3 fits. Keep."},{"q":"Propanal (drawn) is treated with ONE equivalent of methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Which structure best represents the product of this functional-group interconversion?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CCCOC","CCC(OC)OC","CCC(O)O","CCC(O)OC","CCC(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"One equivalent of alcohol adds to an aldehyde under acid catalysis to give a hemiacetal: one OH and one OR on the former carbonyl carbon (opt 0). With excess alcohol you proceed to the acetal (opt 1, two OR groups - the classic trap). C is an ester, D is the hydrate (water not methanol), E is an ether with the framework collapsed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Opt 0 = hemiacetal CCC(O)OC, correct for one equiv MeOH. Opt 1 = acetal (excess), correct distractor. All SMILES parse; single concept, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Electrophilic HBr addition follows Markovnikov: H+ adds to give the tertiary carbocation, so Br ends up on the methyl-bearing carbon. opt1 is anti-Markovnikov (needs peroxides), opt2 is the wrong nucleophile, opt3 is a rearranged exocyclic skeleton, opt4 is Br2 dibromide chemistry.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Chemistry, key, SMILES all correct; one defensible answer; in scope.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"Styrene is treated with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide (ROOR). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Peroxides trigger the radical anti-Markovnikov pathway: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to give the stabilized benzylic radical, so Br lands on the terminal carbon. opt1 is the Markovnikov (no-peroxide) product, opt2 wrong nucleophile, opt3 Br2 chemistry, opt4 needs Br2/FeBr3.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct radical anti-Markovnikov product, valid SMILES, single answer.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"1-Butyne (CH3CH2C≡CH) is treated with 2 equivalents of HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)Br","CCC(Br)CBr","CCCC(Br)Br","CCC(Br)CC","CCC(Br)(Br)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Both HBr additions are Markovnikov, placing both Br on the same internal C2 to give the geminal dihalide 2,2-dibromobutane. opt1 is vicinal (wrong pattern), opt2 puts Br on the terminal C (wrong regiochem), opt3 is mono-addition, opt4 is over-addition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. q_smiles is 1-butyne; geminal dihalide correctly keyed.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"trans-2-Butene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. The product is best described as which stereochemical outcome (one enantiomer of the major product is drawn for each choice)?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)Br","BrCC(Br)CC","C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C","C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Br2 adds anti via a bromonium ion. Anti addition to trans-2-butene gives the chiral (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair (opt0 = one enantiomer, RDKit confirms non-meso R,R). opt1 is the (2R,3S) meso (from cis-alkene), opt2 is the diol (OsO4/KMnO4), opt3/opt4 are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. RDKit confirms opt0 = (R,R) chiral and opt1 = (R,S) meso; anti-addition stereochem correct.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic, then workup). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"correct":4,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 does syn dihydroxylation, adding both OH to the same face to give the cis (meso) 1,2-diol. RDKit confirms opt0 is the cis/meso diol and opt1 is the trans diol (anti, would need epoxide route). opt2 epoxide, opt3 ketone (oxidative cleavage), opt4 mono-ol are wrong.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. RDKit confirms opt0 = cis (meso) diol = correct syn product; opt1 = trans distractor.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCOC(C)(C)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"A 3deg halide with strong base ethoxide and heat gives E2; small base follows Zaitsev to the more substituted alkene 2-methyl-2-butene (opt0). opt1 is the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene, opt2 is SN1 substitution, opt3 SN2 ether (blocked at 3deg), opt4 a rearranged bromide not formed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Zaitsev E2 product correctly keyed; Hofmann distractor valid.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is subjected to the following two-step sequence: (1) NaBH4, MeOH; (2) PBr3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCCCC1Br","BrC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"NaBH4 reduces the ketone to cyclohexanol, then PBr3 converts the 2deg alcohol to bromocyclohexane (opt0). opt1 is the step-1 intermediate, opt2 dehydration, opt3 unchanged ketone, opt4 a dibromide. Two standard textbook steps, recognition depth.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard reduction-then-PBr3 sequence; correct product keyed; in scope (2 simple steps, single transformation each).","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane is treated with NaOH in water/acetone (conditions favoring SN2). What is the major product, including its configuration?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@@H](O)C","CCCCO","CCC(C)O","CC=CC","CC[C@H](O)C"],"correct":0,"why":"SN2 occurs with backside attack and inversion. RDKit confirms (R)-2-bromobutane gives opt0 = (S)-2-butanol. opt1 is the (R) retention product (not SN2), opt2 is the E2 alkene, opt3 is unspecified/racemic, opt4 is the wrong (1-butanol) skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. RDKit confirms inversion gives (S)-2-butanol = opt0; single concept (SN2 stereochem), in DAT scope.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene (drawn) is treated with H2O and a catalytic amount of H2SO4. Which statement best describes the product of this acid-catalyzed hydration?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["Anti-Markovnikov addition gives 3-methyl-2-butanol","Syn dihydroxylation gives a vicinal diol","No reaction occurs because the alkene is too substituted","Markovnikov addition gives 2-methyl-1-butanol (a primary alcohol)","Markovnikov addition gives 2-methyl-2-butanol (the tertiary alcohol)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration goes through the most stable carbocation: protonation of CC=C(C)C gives the tertiary cation at C2, so water adds Markovnikov to give 2-methyl-2-butanol (CC(C)(O)CC, C5H12O). No peroxide/borane (so not anti-Markovnikov) and no OsO4/cold KMnO4 (so not a diol). Keyed index 0 is correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: q_smiles parses as 2-methyl-2-butene; Markovnikov tertiary alcohol is correct; distractors are coherent isomers; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene (drawn) is heated with hot, concentrated KMnO4 followed by acidic workup (H3O+). What is the organic product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Benzoic acid (the benzylic carbon is oxidized to COOH)","Benzaldehyde (oxidation stops at the aldehyde)","No reaction; the aromatic ring is inert to KMnO4","Benzyl alcohol (the methyl is hydroxylated)","Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid (the ring is reduced and oxidized)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Hot concentrated KMnO4 oxidizes any alkyl side chain with a benzylic C-H fully to COOH, so toluene gives benzoic acid (O=C(O)c1ccccc1, C7H6O2). It does not stop at benzaldehyde and does not reduce the ring. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: toluene SMILES correct; benzylic oxidation to benzoic acid is the standard product; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"You want to convert cyclohexanone (drawn) into 1-methylcyclohexanol in one step. Which reagent sequence accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["1) PCC, CH2Cl2","1) CH3CH2OH, H+ (cat.)","1) CH3MgBr; 2) H3O+ workup","1) NaBH4; 2) H3O+ workup","1) CH3Cl, AlCl3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"CH3MgBr delivers a methyl nucleophile to the carbonyl carbon; aqueous workup protonates the alkoxide to give the tertiary alcohol 1-methylcyclohexanol (CC1(O)CCCCC1, C7H14O). NaBH4 only adds H (gives cyclohexanol), PCC oxidizes, and the others form no C-C bond at the carbonyl. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: cyclohexanone SMILES correct; Grignard methylation gives tertiary alcohol; reagent-selection item, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"1-Butyne (drawn) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: 1) BH3-THF (then disiamylborane equivalent for terminal alkyne), 2) H2O2, NaOH. What carbonyl product forms?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts":["Butanal (anti-Markovnikov; O ends up on the terminal carbon)","2-Butanol (Markovnikov alcohol)","Butanoic acid (over-oxidation to the acid)","2-Butanone (Markovnikov; O ends up on the internal carbon)","1-Butanol (simple alcohol, no carbonyl)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: B (then OH) goes to the terminal carbon, giving an enol that tautomerizes to the aldehyde, so 1-butyne gives butanal (CCC=O). Hg2+/H2SO4 hydration of the same alkyne would give the Markovnikov ketone 2-butanone. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: CCC#C is terminal 1-butyne; anti-Markovnikov aldehyde correct; two concepts (regiochem + tautomerization), in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with NaBH4 in methanol, then aqueous workup. Which best describes the outcome?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["No reaction; NaBH4 is too weak to reduce an aldehyde","Addition of methanol to give a stable hemiacetal as the isolated product","Oxidation of the aldehyde to benzoic acid","Selective reduction of the aldehyde to benzyl alcohol","Reduction all the way to toluene (loss of oxygen)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride that selectively reduces aldehydes/ketones to alcohols, so benzaldehyde gives benzyl alcohol (OCc1ccccc1, C7H8O). It does not oxidize, does not deoxygenate to the hydrocarbon, and the reduction outcompetes hemiacetal isolation. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: benzaldehyde SMILES correct; NaBH4 reduction to benzyl alcohol is standard; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"The target alcohol 2-butanol (drawn) is to be made from 1-butene. Which two-step sequence delivers 2-butanol as the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)O","opts":["1) Br2; 2) H2O — gives a bromohydrin","1) O3; 2) Zn, H2O — oxidative cleavage","1) H2O, H2SO4 (cat.) — Markovnikov hydration","1) OsO4; 2) NaHSO3 — gives a 1,2-diol","1) BH3-THF; 2) H2O2, NaOH — hydroboration-oxidation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"2-Butanol (CCC(C)O) is the Markovnikov secondary alcohol from 1-butene, OH on the more substituted internal carbon, delivered by acid-catalyzed hydration. Hydroboration-oxidation would give anti-Markovnikov 1-butanol; the others give a diol, halohydrin, or cleave the alkene. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: 2-butanol SMILES correct; Markovnikov hydration gives the secondary alcohol; reagent-selection, two concepts (regiochem + reagent), in scope."},{"q":"Three alkyl bromides are compared. Among the three drawn (tert-butyl bromide is shown), which substrate reacts FASTEST in an SN2 reaction with the strong nucleophile CN- in acetone?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts":["tert-Butyl bromide — the most substituted carbon","All three react at the same SN2 rate","Methyl bromide (CH3Br) — least steric hindrance at carbon","Neopentyl bromide — primary but with a bulky beta-tert-butyl group","tert-Butyl bromide, because it forms the most stable carbocation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"SN2 rate tracks steric access to the backside: methyl > primary > secondary > tertiary. CH3Br is fastest. tert-Butyl bromide is essentially unreactive in SN2 (favors SN1), and neopentyl bromide, though primary, is severely slowed by the adjacent quaternary carbon. Carbocation stability is an SN1 factor, not SN2. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: stem references three substrates but only tert-butyl bromide is drawn; the drawn molecule is valid and relevant context, and the SN2 trend answer (methyl fastest) is unambiguous. Acceptable as classification/ranking item, two concepts, in scope."},{"q":"Acetic acid (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer conditions). The product is then identified by IR. Which single answer correctly pairs the product with its diagnostic IR change relative to the starting acid?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts":["Product is ethyl acetate; a new broad O-H stretch appears and the C=O disappears","Product is ethanol only; all carbonyl absorption is lost","Product is acetamide; a sharp N-H stretch (~3300) replaces the carbonyl","Product is acetaldehyde; the C=O shifts to ~1715 and an aldehyde C-H (~2720) appears","Product is ethyl acetate; the broad O-H stretch (~2500-3300 cm-1) of the acid disappears while a strong C=O (~1740 cm-1) remains"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification of acetic acid with ethanol (H2SO4 cat.) gives ethyl acetate (CCOC(C)=O). Diagnostic IR: the very broad carboxylic acid O-H (2500-3300 cm-1) is lost while a strong ester C=O remains near 1740 cm-1. No new O-H, aldehyde C-H, or N-H forms. Keyed index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: acetic acid SMILES correct; Fischer ester product and qualitative IR regions are accurate. IR is qualitative region recognition (not J-coupling/exact-shift math), so within DAT scope; hard layered (reaction + IR) item appropriate as difficulty 3."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with HBr in the presence of peroxides (ROOR). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CCCBr","CC(C)CC(C)Br","CCC(Br)C(C)C","CCCCC(C)Br","CC(C)CCCO"],"correct":0,"why":"Peroxides give radical anti-Markovnikov HBr addition: Br adds to the less substituted terminal CH2, giving 1-bromo-4-methylpentane (CC(C)CCCBr). Markovnikov (Br on C2) is the trap; others scramble the carbon bearing Br, rearrange the skeleton, or give an alcohol. Verified: opt0 matches the anti-Markovnikov product; index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (radical regiochemistry), valid SMILES, in scope, plausible distractors. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (shown) is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Which structure correctly represents a product of this addition?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)C(Br)C","CCC(Br)C(Br)C","CCC(C)Br","C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds ANTI via a bromonium ion. Anti addition to cis (Z) 2-butene gives the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) d,l pair of 2,3-dibromobutane. RDKit confirms opt0 is (R,R) and chiral (not meso); opt1 is the (R,S) meso compound, which arises from the trans alkene, so it is the stereochemical trap. Remaining choices drop stereochemistry, use the wrong skeleton, or give a monobromide. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit: opt0=(R,R) chiral, opt1=(R,S) meso — matches the 'cis gives d,l, trans gives meso' logic. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclohexene is subjected to the two-step sequence: (1) mCPBA; (2) H3O+ (aqueous acid). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","O=C1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes the alkene; aqueous acid opens the epoxide by backside (SN2-like) water attack giving ANTI (trans) ring opening: trans-1,2-cyclohexanediol, which is chiral (R,R/S,S). RDKit + 3D geometry show opt1 (O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O) is the TRANS (chiral) diol and is the correct product, while opt0 (O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O) is the CIS meso diol (the syn trap). The keyed index was wrong; corrected to 1. Option 3 stops at the epoxide, and the mono-ol/ketone are wrong oxidation outcomes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"KEYING ERROR FIXED: options 0 and 1 were swapped. RDKit (meso test) and 3D embedding both show opt0 = cis/meso diol and opt1 = trans/chiral diol. Anti opening gives trans, so correct index is 1, not 0. Chemistry/scope fine; corrected index.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC1CCCCC1=O","CC(O)CCCCCO","CC(=O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the endocyclic C=C and OPENS the ring to an acyclic chain. Reductive (Zn/AcOH) workup makes the more-substituted (methyl-bearing) carbon a methyl ketone and the CH carbon an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (CC(=O)CCCCC=O, C7H12O2). Option 2 omits the methyl, option 3 is oxidative over-oxidation to an acid, option 4 is a diol, and option 5 wrongly keeps the ring intact (impossible for an endocyclic alkene). Index 0 correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Ring-opening ozonolysis is a recognition-level concept (one C=C cleaved), not a multi-step synthesis; stays in scope. Distractor 5 (ring intact) is a strong trap. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"2-Pentyne (shown) is hydrogenated with H2 over Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC=CC","CCCCC","CC/C=C/C","C=CC(C)C","CC/C=C\\C"],"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst (poisoned Pd) delivers H2 SYN and stops at the alkene, giving the cis (Z) alkene cis-2-pentene (CC/C=C\\C). The trans (E) isomer (opt1) is the Na/NH3 dissolving-metal product and is the stereochemical trap; pentane (opt2) is full reduction; opt3 lacks stereochemistry and opt4 has a rearranged skeleton. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (syn reduction stereochem), valid SMILES, in scope. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (shown) is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu), a bulky strong base. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=C(C)C","CCOC(C)(C)CC","CCC(C)(C)O","C=C(C)CC","C=CC(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"E2 on a tertiary halide with a bulky base (tert-butoxide) gives the LESS substituted Hofmann alkene, 2-methyl-1-butene (C=C(C)CC). The Zaitsev product 2-methyl-2-butene (opt1) is the trap (favored only by small bases). The tertiary alcohol (opt2) and ether (opt3) are substitution outcomes, and opt4 is a wrong-skeleton alkene. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Two layered concepts (E2 + Hofmann regiochemistry from steric base) is allowed. Valid SMILES, in scope. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"3-Pentanol (shown) is oxidized with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CCC(O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)CCO","CCCC(=O)O","CCCC(C)=O","CCC(O)C(C)O","CCC(=O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"A secondary alcohol oxidizes to a ketone, and mild/anhydrous PCC stops at the ketone: 3-pentanone (CCC(=O)CC). Secondary alcohols cannot over-oxidize to acids, so the carboxylic acid (opt4) is a deliberate trap; others move the carbonyl, add a spurious oxygen, or leave the molecule unchanged. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (PCC selectivity), valid SMILES, in scope. Note opt4 (C4) has a different carbon count, but it is clearly the wrong over-oxidation trap and not confusable. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with dilute H2SO4/H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1=CCCCC1O","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration goes through the more stable (tertiary) carbocation, so OH adds Markovnikov to the more substituted, methyl-bearing carbon: 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CC1(O)CCCCC1). The 2-methyl alcohol (opt1) is the anti-Markovnikov/hydroboration trap; opt2 is a primary CH2OH, opt3 an allylic alcohol, and opt4 the unchanged alkane. Index 0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (Markovnikov regiochemistry), valid SMILES, in scope. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with ethylene (heat). What is the major product of this [4+2] cycloaddition?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["C1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","c1ccccc1","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A Diels-Alder reaction joins a diene (4 pi) and a dienophile (2 pi) into a six-membered ring; butadiene's terminal carbons bond to ethylene and the central single bond becomes the new ring C=C, giving cyclohexene (C6H10, one alkene). Verified opt0=C1=CCCCC1=cyclohexene. Distractors: cyclohexane (saturated), 1,4-cyclohexadiene (extra DB), benzene (aromatic), cyclopentene (wrong ring size). All SMILES valid; single reactive center.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"2,3-Dimethyl-1,3-butadiene undergoes a Diels-Alder reaction with ethylene. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C(C)=C","opts":["Cc1ccccc1C","CC1(C)CCCC=C1","CC1CCCCC1C","CC1=CC(C)CCC1","CC1=C(C)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The two internal diene carbons (bearing the methyls) become the new ring double-bond carbons, giving 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene. Verified opt0=CC1=C(C)CCCC1 has both methyls on the C=C (C8H14). Distractors misplace methyls (1,3), make them geminal, fully saturate the ring (dimethylcyclohexane), or aromatize to o-xylene. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"1,3-Butadiene is heated with maleic anhydride (the dienophile shown). What is the major cycloaddition product?","q_smiles":"O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2CCCCC12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21","O=C1OC(=O)C2C=CCCC12"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The electron-poor C=C of maleic anhydride is the dienophile; cycloaddition with butadiene builds a cyclohexene ring fused to the anhydride (cis-4-cyclohexene-1,2-dicarboxylic anhydride, C8H8O3) with the new alkene across the ring from the fusion carbons. Verified opt0. Distractors: phthalic anhydride (aromatic), saturated (no alkene), alkene at wrong position (opt3), wrong ring size (opt4). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"Isoprene (2-methyl-1,3-butadiene) reacts with acrolein (CH2=CH-CHO). Applying the 'para' regiochemistry rule, which is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=C","opts":["O=CC1CCC(C)=CC1","O=CC1CC(C)=CCC1","OCC1CCC(C)=CC1","O=CC1CCC=CC1","O=CC1CCCC(C)=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A 2-substituted diene plus a monosubstituted dienophile gives the 'para' (1,4) product. Deriving the DA connectivity from first principles, the ring methyl and the aldehyde end up 1,4, giving 4-methylcyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde; verified opt0 matches the para connectivity. Distractors are the meta (opt1) and other (opt2) arrangements, methyl omitted (opt3), and aldehyde reduced to alcohol (opt4, DA does not reduce C=O). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"(E)-1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (CH2=CH-CHO). Applying the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule, which is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC=CC=C","opts":["O=CC1CCC=CC1","COC1CCC=CC1C=O","O=CC1CCC=CC1OC","O=CC1CC=CCC1OC","O=CC1CC=CC(OC)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A 1-substituted diene plus a monosubstituted dienophile gives the 'ortho' (1,2) product. Deriving the DA connectivity, the methoxy and aldehyde end up on adjacent ring carbons with the new alkene beta to OMe (2-methoxycyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde); verified opt0 matches. Distractors: non-adjacent substituents (opt2), methoxy dropped (opt3), adjacent but ring double bond moved (opt1, opt4). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"Cyclopentadiene reacts with maleic anhydride. According to the endo rule, which bicyclic product predominates?","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=C1","opts":["O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21","O=C1OC(=O)C2CCC=CC2C1","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3CCC(C3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(C3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C=CC1C2"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A cyclic diene gives a bicyclo[2.2.1] (norbornene) product fused to the anhydride, retaining one alkene in the two-carbon bridge; opt0 was verified as carbic anhydride (norbornene-2,3-dicarboxylic anhydride, C9H8O3, 3 rings, exactly one C=C). The endo isomer is kinetically favored. The other four options are constitutionally wrong skeletons (six-membered fused, aromatic, saturated norbornane, wrong framework), so opt0 is the unique correct connectivity. Endo/exo are stereoisomers sharing this SMILES, fine at DAT recognition depth.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"For the diene shown (1,3-butadiene) to undergo a Diels-Alder reaction, which conformational requirement must be met?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["The diene must be locked in the s-trans conformation","The diene must first isomerize to an alkyne","The diene must aromatize before reacting","The dienophile must be electron-rich for the reaction to proceed","The diene must adopt the s-cis conformation so both terminal carbons can reach the dienophile"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The concerted [4+2] requires both diene termini to point to the same side to bond the dienophile simultaneously, possible only in the s-cis conformation; the s-trans conformer cannot react. Dienes need not become alkynes or aromatize, and the dienophile is favored when electron-poor (opposite of the stated distractor). Text item with a valid drawn diene; opt0 correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"(E)-Dimethyl fumarate (the trans-diester dienophile shown) reacts with 1,3-butadiene. Because the reaction is stereospecific, which product forms?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)/C=C/C(=O)OC","opts":["COC(=O)C1C=CCCC1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)[C@H]1CC=CC[C@@H]1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)[C@H]1CC=CC[C@H]1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C1CCCCC1C(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The Diels-Alder is stereospecific and suprafacial on the dienophile, so a trans (E) dienophile gives the two esters trans on the new cyclohexene. Verified opt0 ([C@H]...[C@@H]) is the (S,S), chiral, trans-1,2-diester (confirmed by CIP and by 3D geometry: substituents on opposite ring faces); opt1 ([C@H]...[C@H]) is the (R,S) meso cis isomer. Other distractors: aromatic diester (wrong skeleton), saturated ring (no alkene), alkene at wrong position. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"q":"Methyl vinyl ketone is treated with KCN followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)C","opts":["CC(=O)CCC#N","CC(=O)C(C#N)C","C=CC(O)(C)C#N","CC(O)(C#N)C=C","CC(=O)CC#N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Cyanide is a soft nucleophile that undergoes 1,4 (conjugate/Michael) addition to the beta carbon of the alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone. After tautomerization the ketone is regenerated, placing the nitrile on the terminal (beta) carbon: 4-oxopentanenitrile (CC(=O)CCC#N). Option B adds at the alpha carbon (wrong regiochemistry). Options C and D are 1,2 cyanohydrin additions (disfavored for a soft stabilized nucleophile). Option E is missing a carbon. Verified by RDKit: all SMILES valid; index 0 matches NC-CH2-CH2-C(=O)-CH3.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclohex-2-en-1-one is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi, a Gilman reagent), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["OC1(C)CCCC=C1","CC1CCCCC1=O","CC1CCC(=O)CC1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1","OC1CCCCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Gilman (cuprate) reagents deliver the alkyl group via 1,4 (conjugate) addition to the beta carbon of an enone, giving 3-methylcyclohexanone (CC1CCCC(=O)C1). Option B is 2-methylcyclohexanone (alpha), option C is 4-methylcyclohexanone (wrong position), option D is the 1,2 tertiary allylic alcohol (organolithium/Grignard behavior, not cuprate), and option E shows carbonyl reduction, which a cuprate does not do. RDKit confirms the stem is cyclohex-2-en-1-one and all options are valid, distinct structures; index 0 = 3-methylcyclohexanone.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclohex-2-en-1-one is treated with methyllithium (CH3Li) at low temperature, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["CC1CCCCC1=O","OC1(C)CCCC=C1","OC1(C)CCCCC1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCC(=O)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Organolithium reagents are hard, strongly basic nucleophiles that add 1,2 directly to the carbonyl carbon, giving the tertiary allylic alcohol 1-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-ol (the conjugated C=C is retained): OC1(C)CCCC=C1. Option B is the 1,4 cuprate product, option C is alpha-methylation, option D is the 1,2 alcohol with the alkene wrongly reduced, and option E is a 1,4 product at the wrong position. RDKit verifies the stem enone and that the correct answer retains the C=C with a new tertiary C-OH at C1.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"trans-Chalcone (1,3-diphenylprop-2-en-1-one) is treated with dimethyl malonate and a catalytic amount of sodium methoxide. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this Michael reaction?","q_smiles":"O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(c1ccccc1)C(C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC)c1ccccc1","OC(/C=C/c1ccccc1)(c1ccccc1)C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(c1ccccc1)CC(=O)OC","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(c1ccccc1)C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC","O=C(c1ccccc1)C=C(c1ccccc1)C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The stabilized malonate enolate (a soft Michael donor) adds 1,4 to the beta carbon of the enone (the carbon bearing the second phenyl), forming the new C-C bond at the beta position with the ketone regenerated: PhC(=O)-CH2-CH(Ph)-CH(CO2Me)2. Option B attaches the malonate at the alpha carbon, option C is a 1,2 addition (disfavored), option D retains the alkene (incorrect vinylic substitution), and option E has lost one ester group. RDKit confirms all five SMILES are valid and distinct; index 0 is the canonical 1,4 adduct. The product has a stereocenter but the item asks only for constitution/regiochemistry, which is in DAT scope.","difficulty":2,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Acrolein (prop-2-enal) is treated with piperidine. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this aza-Michael (conjugate) addition?","q_smiles":"C=CC=O","opts":["O=CCCN1CCCCC1","O=CCN1CCCCC1","O=CC(C)N1CCCCC1","C=CC(O)N1CCCCC1","OC(C=C)N1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A secondary amine is a nucleophile in conjugate (1,4) addition, attaching nitrogen to the beta carbon of the alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde; the aldehyde is regenerated after tautomerization, giving 3-(piperidin-1-yl)propanal (O=CCCN1CCCCC1). Option B places N at the alpha carbon, options C and D are 1,2 hemiaminal-type additions (not the stable conjugate product), and option E is missing a carbon. RDKit verifies the stem is acrolein and all options are valid; index 0 is the beta amino aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Methyl acrylate is treated with thiophenol (PhSH) and a trace of base. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this thia-Michael addition?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)OC","opts":["COC(=O)C(C)Sc1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCSc1ccccc1","C=CC(OC)(O)Sc1ccccc1","COC(=O)CSc1ccccc1","COC(=O)C=CSc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Thiols are excellent soft nucleophiles for conjugate (1,4) addition; sulfur adds to the beta carbon of the alpha,beta-unsaturated ester, giving methyl 3-(phenylthio)propanoate (COC(=O)CCSc1ccccc1). Option B places sulfur at the alpha carbon, option C is a nonsensical 1,2 addition across the ester carbonyl, option D is missing a carbon, and option E retains the alkene (incorrect vinylic substitution). RDKit confirms all SMILES valid and distinct; index 0 is the beta thioether ester.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclopent-2-en-1-one (shown) can react with carbon nucleophiles to give either 1,2- or 1,4-addition products. Which reagent is MOST likely to give predominantly the 1,4 (conjugate) addition product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC=C1","opts":["Sodium borohydride is irrelevant; all four add 1,2","Lithium diphenylcuprate (Ph2CuLi)","Phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr) at low temperature","Phenyllithium (PhLi)","Lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Organocuprates (Gilman reagents) are the classic reagents for 1,4 (conjugate) addition to enones because the soft, less basic organocopper species adds to the beta carbon. Organolithiums (PhLi) and largely Grignards add 1,2 (hard nucleophiles), and LiAlH4 is a hydride that reduces the carbonyl 1,2 rather than acting as a carbon nucleophile. Option E is a false statement. Exactly one correct answer (index 0). RDKit confirms the stem O=C1CCC=C1 is cyclopent-2-en-1-one, a properly conjugated enone, so the reagent-selection item is correctly visualized.","difficulty":2,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone) is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C(C)/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The cuprate delivers the methyl group by 1,4 (conjugate) addition to the beta carbon (the one bearing the phenyl), and the ketone is regenerated, giving 4-phenylpentan-2-one (CC(=O)CC(C)c1ccccc1; formula C11H14O = SM C10H10O + CH4, confirmed by RDKit). Option B adds methyl at the alpha carbon, option C is the 1,2 allylic alcohol (organolithium behavior, alkene retained), option D shows extra carbonyl reduction, and option E retains the alkene (incorrect). Index 0 is correct.","difficulty":3,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"group":"michael","q":"(E)-Pent-3-en-2-one is treated with KCN (a soft nucleophile) and then aqueous acid. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C#N)CC=O","CCCC(C)=O","CC(C#N)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)C(C)CC#N","C/C=C/C(C)(O)C#N"],"correct":2,"why":"Cyanide is a soft nucleophile and adds 1,4. CN bonds to the beta carbon (C4, bearing the terminal CH3) giving CH3-CO-CH2-CH(CN)-CH3 = option 0 (verified: carbonyl->nitrile bond distance = 3, i.e. CN on beta; formula C6H9NO = stem + HCN). Option 1 is the 1,2-cyanohydrin (disfavored for soft CN, retains alkene). Option 2 is a wrong skeleton (CN at gamma/alpha-side). Option 3 is a rearranged aldehyde. Option 4 is simple reduction with no nitrile. Exactly one right answer, in scope, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct 1,4-addition regiochemistry verified; clean distractors; single right answer."},{"group":"michael","q":"For the conjugate (1,4) addition versus direct (1,2) addition to cyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn), which reagent is the BEST choice when you specifically want the 1,4-conjugate-addition product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Lithium dimethylcuprate, (CH3)2CuLi (a Gilman reagent)","Sodium borohydride then methylation","Methylmagnesium bromide, CH3MgBr","Methyllithium, CH3Li","Lithium aluminum hydride, LiAlH4"],"correct":0,"why":"Gilman (organocuprate) reagents are soft carbon nucleophiles that strongly favor 1,4-conjugate addition to enones (beta-alkylation). CH3MgBr and CH3Li are hard organometallics that add 1,2 to the carbonyl (allylic alcohol). LiAlH4 is a hydride reductant, not a carbon nucleophile. Option 5 is hydride chemistry and a nonsense route to a beta-alkyl ketone. Text item but stem molecule is drawn with a valid SMILES. Exactly one best answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Reagent-selection item, valid stem SMILES, single defensible answer (Gilman for 1,4)."},{"group":"michael","q":"4-Phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) reacts with ethanethiol and a catalytic base (a thia-Michael addition). Considering both regiochemistry and relative stereochemistry, which structure best represents the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(c2ccccc2)C=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(SCC)CCC(c2ccccc2)C=C1","O=C1CC[C@@H](c2ccccc2)[C@H](SCC)C1","O=C1C(SCC)CC(c2ccccc2)C=C1","O=C1CCC(c2ccccc2)C=C1SCC","O=C1CC[C@@H](c2ccccc2)[C@@H](SCC)C1"],"correct":1,"why":"Regiochemistry is sound: a thiol is a soft S nucleophile that adds 1,4, bonding S to the beta carbon (C3), which is adjacent to the C4-phenyl (connectivity of options 0/1 matches the saturated 3-SEt-4-Ph ketone, verified). Options 2 (1,2-adduct), 3 (S at alpha + retained alkene), and 5 (vinyl sulfide) are correctly wrong. Options 0 and 1 are the trans/cis diastereomers; the keyed answer is the trans (SEt anti to Ph) as the major from approach anti to the bulky phenyl. Caveat: true facial selectivity in this flexible 6-membered enone with a remote C4 substituent is only modest, so the absolute trans-major claim is somewhat over-stated, but it remains a defensible single answer at DAT depth with clean regio distractors. All SMILES valid; two layered concepts fit difficulty-2.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: the trans-major stereochemistry claim is an over-statement (facial selectivity here is modest), but regiochemistry is correct and exactly one answer is defensible. Keep; consider softening the stereochem framing."},{"group":"michael","q":"Methyl acrylate (drawn) is treated with dimethylamine (an aza-Michael / conjugate addition). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)OC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CNCCC(=O)OC","C=CC(OC)(OC)N(C)C","COC(=O)C(C)N(C)C","CN(C)CCC(=O)OC","C=CC(=O)N(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Dimethylamine adds 1,4 across the alpha,beta-unsaturated ester (aza-Michael), N bonding to the terminal beta carbon to give the beta-(dimethylamino) ester with the methyl ester intact = option 0 (verified COC(=O)CCN(C)C). Option 1 is the amide from carbonyl attack (acyl substitution), retaining the alkene. Option 2 puts N at the alpha carbon. Option 3 shows a secondary amine (N has 1 H = NHMe, verified) inconsistent with adding Me2NH. Option 4 is a nonsense orthoester. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Aza-Michael beta-amino ester correct; distractors clean (secondary-amine distractor confirmed as NHMe)."},{"group":"michael","q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone, drawn) can give either the 1,2- or the 1,4-addition product with methyl-metal reagents. Which statement correctly matches the reagent to its preferred mode of addition?","q_smiles":"C/C(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both reagents add 1,4 because conjugation always dominates","(CH3)2CuLi adds 1,2 (to the carbonyl); CH3MgBr adds 1,4 (to the beta-carbon)","(CH3)2CuLi adds 1,4 (to the beta-carbon); CH3MgBr adds 1,2 (to the carbonyl)","Neither reagent reacts; an enolate is required for any addition to an enone","Both reagents add 1,2 because the carbonyl is the only electrophilic site"],"correct":2,"why":"Soft organocuprate (Gilman) prefers 1,4-conjugate addition (Me to beta carbon); hard, more reactive Grignard adds 1,2 to the carbonyl (tertiary allylic alcohol). Hardness/softness, not conjugation alone, controls site selectivity, so options 3, 4, and 5 are wrong. Stem drawn with valid SMILES. Exactly one correct statement.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard hard/soft reagent-matching; valid stem SMILES; single right answer."},{"group":"michael","q":"In the base-catalyzed conjugate addition of a stabilized enolate to (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde, drawn), which description correctly identifies where the nucleophile's curved arrow points and the immediate intermediate formed?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The nucleophile attacks the beta-carbon; the arrows give an enolate intermediate that is protonated on oxygen/carbon during workup","The nucleophile attacks the alpha-carbon; the arrows give a carbocation intermediate","The nucleophile attacks the carbonyl carbon; the arrows give an alkoxide intermediate (1,2-addition)","The nucleophile attacks the carbonyl oxygen; the arrows give an oxocarbenium intermediate","A hydride shifts from the alpha-carbon to the beta-carbon before any nucleophile adds"],"correct":0,"why":"In 1,4-conjugate addition the nucleophile attacks the electrophilic beta carbon; pi electrons flow to oxygen forming a resonance-stabilized enolate that is protonated on workup. Option 2 is 1,2-addition (different mode). Options 3 (O-attack), 4 (alpha-attack/carbocation), and 5 (hydride shift) are mechanistically wrong. This is conceptual (single reactive center, no full mechanism drawn) and within scope. Stem SMILES valid. One correct answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Conceptual arrow/intermediate item stays at recognition depth (no full mechanism required); valid stem; single answer."},{"group":"michael","q":"5,5-Dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) is treated with the anion of nitromethane (-CH2NO2), a stabilized carbon nucleophile, then mild acid. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CC(C)(C)CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CC(C)(C)CCC1C[N+](=O)[O-]","OC1(C[N+](=O)[O-])CC(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(ON=O)C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(C[N+](=O)[O-])C1","O=C1C(C[N+](=O)[O-])C(C)(C)CC=C1"],"correct":3,"why":"The nitronate is a soft, stabilized carbon nucleophile and adds 1,4: its carbon bonds to the beta carbon (C3), and the enolate is protonated to restore the saturated ketone = option 0 (verified: nitromethyl carbon sits at the beta position, 2 bonds from the carbonyl carbon). Option 1 is the 1,2-adduct (allylic alcohol, alkene retained). Option 2 attaches nitromethyl at the alpha carbon and keeps the alkene. Option 3 shows nitrite O-N=O (O-attachment, no C-C bond). Option 4 places the new C-C bond at the alpha carbon. All SMILES valid; one correct answer.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Nitronate 1,4-addition at beta verified; gem-dimethyl is a spectator; clean regio/O-vs-C distractors; single answer."},{"q":"Two molecules of ethyl acetate (drawn) are treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), followed by acidic workup. What is the major organic product of this Claisen condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC(O)CC(=O)OCC","O=CCC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Ethyl acetate self-Claisen gives ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate), CH3-CO-CH2-CO-OEt. Enolate of one ester attacks the carbonyl of a second, ejecting ethoxide. Option 0 is correct. Distractors: aldehyde-ester, 2,4-pentanedione (would require ethoxy lost as carbon), beta-hydroxy ester (aldol, no condensation), diethyl malonate (no new C-C/ketone). All valid, exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then mild acid. Predict the major beta-ketoester product of the self-Claisen condensation.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts":["CCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCC(=O)C(C)C(C)=O","CCC(O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ethyl propanoate (CH3CH2-CO-OEt) has an alpha CH2 that bears a methyl after deprotonation (the alpha CH retains one methyl). Self-Claisen gives ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate, CH3CH2-CO-CH(CH3)-CO-OEt = option 0. RDKit confirms opt0 canonical CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC. Distractors: methyl-stripped, wrong skeleton, beta-hydroxy ester, diketone. Exactly one correct. (The 'why' prose has an awkward parenthetical but the structure and answer are unambiguous and correct.)","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl butanoate (drawn) undergoes a Claisen condensation with itself using NaOEt, then acidic workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCCC(=O)C(CC)C(=O)OCC","CCCC(=O)C(CC)C(C)=O","CCCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCCC(O)C(CC)C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Ethyl butanoate (CH3CH2CH2-CO-OEt) has an alpha carbon bearing an ethyl substituent. Self-Claisen gives ethyl 2-ethyl-3-oxohexanoate, CH3CH2CH2-CO-CH(CH2CH3)-CO-OEt = option 0. Distractors: ethyl-stripped, beta-hydroxy ester, diketone, methyl-branch-instead-of-ethyl. All valid SMILES, exactly one correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (diethyl hexanedioate, drawn) is treated with one equivalent of sodium ethoxide, then acid. What is the major product of this intramolecular (Dieckmann) condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)CCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCC1C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Dieckmann of diethyl adipate (1,6-diester, C6 diacid) closes to the favored five-membered ring beta-ketoester, ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate = option 0 (RDKit: CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O). Distractors: 6-ring, 4-ring, acyclic, cyclopentane-1,2-dione (no ester). All valid, exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate, drawn) is subjected to NaOEt and then acidic workup. Predict the major Dieckmann condensation product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["O=C1CCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","OC1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Diethyl pimelate (1,7-diester, C7 diacid) Dieckmann-closes to the favored six-membered ring, ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate = option 0 (RDKit: CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O). Distractors: 5-ring, 7-ring, acyclic, beta-hydroxy ester. All valid SMILES, exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate (drawn) is mixed with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, then worked up with acid. In this crossed Claisen condensation, what is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)C(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(N)=O","O=C(CC(=O)OCC)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ethyl benzoate has no alpha H, so it is the sole electrophile. Ethyl acetate enolate attacks the benzoate carbonyl, losing ethoxide, to give ethyl benzoylacetate Ph-CO-CH2-CO-OEt = option 0. Distractors: ethyl acetate self-product, wrong connectivity, an amide (no N reagent present), beta-hydroxy ester. All valid, exactly one correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Diethyl oxalate (drawn) is treated with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, followed by acidic workup. What is the major crossed-Claisen product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)OCC","O=CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha H and acts only as the electrophilic acceptor. Ethyl acetate enolate attacks one oxalate carbonyl, ethoxide leaves, giving diethyl oxaloacetate EtO2C-CO-CH2-CO-OEt = option 0 (RDKit: CCOC(=O)CC(=O)C(=O)OCC). Distractors: self-Claisen product, aldehyde variant, beta-hydroxy diester, double-addition over-condensation. All valid, exactly one correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"A student wants to convert ethyl acetate (drawn) into ethyl acetoacetate by a Claisen condensation. Which base/condition is the best choice to drive the reaction to product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["Dilute HCl in water","Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol","Sodium borohydride (NaBH4) in methanol","Pyridine, a weak amine base","Aqueous sodium hydroxide (NaOH) at room temperature"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Claisen needs an alkoxide base to form the enolate. NaOEt in ethanol matches the ethyl ester (no transesterification scrambling) and is the canonical choice = option 0. Aqueous NaOH saponifies and water quenches the enolate; HCl forms no enolate; NaBH4 reduces carbonyls; pyridine is too weak. Reagent-selection item is correctly text (opts_are_structures=false) while the molecule is still drawn in q_smiles. pKa values are cited descriptively, not as calculation, so in scope.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate is treated with a stoichiometric amount of sodium ethoxide, then worked up with dilute acid. Which structure is the major beta-ketoester product of this self-Claisen condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC","CCC(=O)OCC(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)CCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Self-Claisen: the alpha carbon of ethyl propanoate (which bears a methyl) forms the enolate, attacks a second ester carbonyl, and expels ethoxide, giving ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate, EtO2C-CH(CH3)-C(=O)-CH2CH3 (opt0). Formula C8H14O3 = 2 x C5H10O2 - EtOH, correct. Opt1 drops the alpha-methyl (acetate-like trap); opt2 spaces the carbonyls one carbon too far (gamma-keto ester); opt3 has an extra methyl; opt4 has no new C-C bond. Exactly one correct, all SMILES valid and distinct.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (diethyl hexanedioate) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acidified. Which is the major product of this intramolecular Dieckmann condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","O=C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A 1,6-diester (adipate) undergoes intramolecular Claisen to close the favored five-membered ring, giving ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate (opt0, C8H12O3). Opt1 is the six-membered ring (would require a 1,7-diester); opt2 is a strained four-membered ring; opt3 lacks the ketone; opt5 has lost the ester. Correct, in scope, single reactive system, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate) is subjected to Dieckmann conditions (NaOEt, then H3O+). What is the major cyclic product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Pimelate is a 1,7-diester, so Dieckmann closure forms the favored six-membered ring, ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate (opt0, C9H14O3). Opt1 is the five-membered ring from adipate; opt2 is a seven-membered ring; opt4 lacks the ketone; opt5 has lost the ester. One correct answer, valid distinct SMILES, in scope.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate and ethyl acetate are mixed with sodium ethoxide (a crossed Claisen), then worked up with acid. Ethyl benzoate is drawn below. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(=O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(CC(=O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethyl benzoate has no alpha hydrogens, so it is the electrophile; the ethyl acetate enolate attacks it to give ethyl benzoylacetate, PhC(=O)-CH2-CO2Et (opt0, C11H12O3). Opt1 lacks the CH2 (alpha-keto ester); opt2 is a double-acylation product; opt3 is phenylacetate-derived (extra CH2); opt4 places acylation on the ring (impossible). One correct answer, valid distinct SMILES.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"In the base-promoted Claisen/Dieckmann condensation (ethyl phenylacetate shown), what is the first mechanistic event, and why is a full equivalent of strong base required to reach completion?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Alkoxide adds directly to the ester carbonyl first; base is consumed building the tetrahedral intermediate","Alkoxide removes an alpha proton to form a resonance-stabilized ester enolate; a full equivalent is needed because the acidic beta-ketoester product is deprotonated, pulling the equilibrium forward","Loss of ethoxide occurs before any deprotonation, generating an acylium ion that is then attacked","The ester is protonated to activate the carbonyl, so acid rather than base drives the reaction","An alpha proton is removed only after the new C-C bond forms; the base is purely catalytic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The Claisen begins with alkoxide removing an acidic alpha proton to give a resonance-stabilized enolate. The C-C bond-forming step is otherwise unfavorable and is driven forward by the essentially irreversible deprotonation of the very acidic alpha proton of the beta-ketoester product (pKa ~11), which consumes a full equivalent of base. Opt0 correct; the others misstate the order or the driving force. Conceptual single-fact item, in scope; substrate is drawn in q_smiles.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl oxalate and ethyl propanoate are combined with sodium ethoxide (a crossed Claisen), then acidified. Diethyl oxalate is shown below. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)C(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CCC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha hydrogens and serves as the electrophile. The ethyl propanoate enolate (retaining its alpha-methyl) attacks one oxalate carbonyl, giving diethyl 2-methyl-3-oxosuccinate, EtO2C-C(=O)-CH(CH3)-CO2Et (opt0, C9H14O5 = C6H10O4 + C5H10O2 - EtOH). Opt1 drops the methyl (acetate-like); opt2 spaces carbonyls too far; opt4 is the propanoate self-Claisen product ignoring oxalate; opt5 is over-incorporated. One correct, valid distinct SMILES.","difficulty":2,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl 2-methyladipate (shown) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acid. Of the two possible Dieckmann ring closures, which beta-ketoester is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(C)CCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC(C)C1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CC(C)CC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1(C)CCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Dieckmann is reversible and is driven by deprotonation of the product's carbon flanked by both carbonyls. If the methyl-bearing alpha carbon attacks the far carbonyl, that carbon becomes quaternary (opt1) with no acidic proton, so that path reverses. The favored product comes from the unsubstituted CH2 enolate attacking, leaving an acidic alpha proton on the ester-bearing ring carbon: opt0 (C9H14O3), verified to equal the path-B closure. One correct answer, all SMILES valid and distinct. Note: the structure opt0 is correct, but the explanation's name 4-methyl-2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate is a minor naming nit (strict IUPAC locants give 3-methyl); structure is unambiguous and right.","difficulty":3,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Rationale names the product 4-methyl-2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate; strict IUPAC locants give 3-methyl-2-oxo. Naming nit only; the drawn structure (opt0) is the correct, unique product."},{"q":"Ethyl cyclopropanecarboxylate is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acidified, undergoing a self-Claisen condensation. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C1CC1","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1(CC1)C(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)C1CC1","CCOC(=O)C1(CC1)C(=O)C1CC1","O=C(C1CC1)C1CC1","CCOC(=O)C1CC1C(=O)C1CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The only alpha carbon is the ring carbon bearing the ester (it has one H). Its enolate attacks a second molecule's carbonyl, making that ring carbon quaternary (ester + acyl + two ring bonds): ethyl 1-(cyclopropanecarbonyl)cyclopropanecarboxylate (opt0, C10H14O3 = 2 x C6H10O2 - EtOH). Opt1 acylates a ring CH2 (a beta carbon, not the alpha); opt2 has an open chain; opt4 has the wrong acyl group; opt5 has lost the ester. One correct answer, all SMILES valid and distinct.","difficulty":2,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br), then (3) aqueous HCl with heat. What is the major final organic product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)C(Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)O","O=C(O)CCCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)CCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Malonic ester synthesis: the central carbon is alkylated by benzyl bromide, then acidic hydrolysis of both esters with heat drives decarboxylation, giving PhCH2CH2COOH (3-phenylpropanoic acid). Phenylacetic acid (opt1) is one carbon short; the undecarboxylated diacid (opt2) results if you forget decarboxylation; 4-phenylbutanoic acid (opt3) is one carbon too long; phenylacetone (opt4) is a methyl ketone (acetoacetic route, wrong start). Verified correct=0.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) ethyl iodide (CH3CH2I), then (3) dilute aqueous H2SO4 with heat. What is the major final organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)CC","CCCC(C)=O","CCC(C)=O","CCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis yields a methyl ketone. Ethylation of the active methylene, then hydrolysis and decarboxylation, gives CH3-CO-CH2-CH2CH3 (2-pentanone). 2-Hexanone (opt1) is one carbon too long; 2-butanone (opt2) corresponds to no alkylation; butanoic acid (opt3) is the carboxylic-acid class (malonic route); 3-pentanone (opt4) has the carbonyl in the wrong position. Verified correct=0.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is subjected to (1) NaOEt, (2) 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), then (3) H3O+ and heat. Which structure is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(C)CC(=O)O","CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)(C)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Single alkylation with isopropyl bromide installs (CH3)2CH- on the malonate carbon; hydrolysis and decarboxylation give (CH3)2CH-CH2-COOH (3-methylbutanoic/isovaleric acid). Isobutyric acid (opt1) is one carbon short (no new CH2); pentanoic acid (opt2) implies a straight wrong skeleton; pivalic acid (opt3) needs a quaternary center; 4-methyl-2-pentanone (opt4) is a methyl ketone from the acetoacetic route. Verified correct=0.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then (3) aqueous acid and heat. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["C=CCC(C(C)=O)C(C)=O","C=CCCCC(C)=O","C=CCC(C)=O","C=CCCC(C)=O","C=CCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives a methyl ketone. Allylation of the active methylene, then hydrolysis and decarboxylation, gives CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2 (5-hexen-2-one). 4-Penten-2-one (opt1) is one carbon short; 6-hepten-2-one (opt2) is one carbon too long; 4-pentenoic acid (opt3) is the wrong (acid) product class; the diketone (opt4) retains an undecarboxylated acetyl group. Verified correct=0.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt then CH3I, and the product is treated again with NaOEt then CH3CH2I; finally it is heated in aqueous HCl. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O","CCC(C)(C)C(=O)O","CCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two sequential alkylations (methyl, then ethyl) place both groups on the malonate carbon; hydrolysis and decarboxylation give the 2,2-disubstituted acetic acid bearing one residual H: CH3CH2-CH(CH3)-COOH (2-methylbutanoic acid). 2-Methylpentanoic acid (opt1) has an extra carbon; 2-methylpropanoic acid (opt2) is missing the ethyl; 2,2-dimethylbutanoic acid (opt3) would require three alkylations (impossible, only two acidic H); pentanoic acid (opt4) has no branch. Verified correct=0.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) benzyl bromide, then (3) hot aqueous acid. Which structure is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["O=C(O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives a methyl ketone: benzylation of the active methylene followed by hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-Ph (4-phenyl-2-butanone). Phenylacetone (opt1) is one carbon short; 5-phenyl-2-pentanone (opt2) is one carbon too long; 3-phenylpropanoic acid (opt3) is the acid class (malonic route); 1-phenyl-2-butanone (opt4) places the carbonyl on the wrong side of the benzyl group. Verified correct=0.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (BrCH2CH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous HCl. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"BrCCCCBr","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCCC1","O=C(O)C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C(O)CCCCCC(=O)O","O=C(O)C1CCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"With two equivalents of base, the malonate carbon undergoes two intramolecular alkylations with the 1,4-dibromide: the central carbon plus the four-carbon chain closes a five-membered (cyclopentane) ring bearing the geminal diester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation give cyclopentanecarboxylic acid (ring size 5 confirmed). Cyclobutane (opt1, 3-C tether) and cyclohexane (opt2, 5-C tether) rings have the wrong size; the open diacid (opt3) skips cyclization/decarboxylation; cyclopentanone (opt4) lacks the carboxyl. Verified correct=0. Note the q_smiles draws the dibromide alkylating agent (not the malonate), which the stem states explicitly.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (BrCH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous acid. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. Which structure is the major final product?","q_smiles":"BrCCCBr","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Double intramolecular alkylation of the acetoacetate carbon by the 1,3-dibromide closes a ring: the central carbon plus the three-carbon tether forms a four-membered (cyclobutane) ring still bearing the acetyl and ester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation leave the methyl ketone, giving 1-cyclobutylethanone/acetylcyclobutane (ring size 4 confirmed). The cyclopropyl ketone (opt1, 2-C tether) and cyclopentyl ketone (opt2, 4-C tether) are the wrong ring size; the open diketone (opt3) skips cyclization; cyclobutanecarboxylic acid (opt4) is the acid class (malonic route). Verified correct=0. Note the q_smiles draws the dibromide alkylating agent, which the stem states explicitly.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with sodium ethoxide and then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br). The product is then heated with aqueous acid (hydrolysis) and decarboxylated. What is the final carboxylic acid product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Malonic ester synthesis: alpha-C benzylated, then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives PhCH2-CH2-COOH (3-phenylpropanoic acid), option A. The malonate contributes the CH2-COOH unit so one extra carbon remains between ring and acid; phenylacetic acid (C) is the classic trap. Verified: A = O=C(O)CCc1ccccc1. All 5 options valid and unique.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is deprotonated with NaOEt, alkylated with allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then subjected to hydrolysis and decarboxylation. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CCC=C","OC(=O)C(C)CC=C","CC(O)CCC=C","CC(=O)CC=C","CC(=O)CCC=C"],"correct":4,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives a methyl ketone: alpha-C allylated, then hydrolysis/decarboxylation leaves CH3CO-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2, option A. The acid options (C/E) are wrong synthesis type; B drops a CH2. Structure verified C=CCCC(C)=O. NOTE: the actual product is hex-5-en-2-one (6 carbons), not the 'hept-6-en-2-one' loosely stated in the rationale; the drawn correct STRUCTURE is right, only the offhand name is off.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Rationale mis-names the product as 'hept-6-en-2-one'; correct name is hex-5-en-2-one. The structure/answer (idx 0) is correct and unaffected."},{"q":"The diester shown (a malonate dialkylated with one methyl and one ethyl group) is hydrolyzed in hot aqueous acid and decarboxylated. What carboxylic acid is formed?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(C)(CC)C(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CC(C)C","OC(=O)CCCC","OC(=O)C(C)CC","OC(=O)C(C)(C)C","OC(=O)C(CC)CC"],"correct":2,"why":"Geminal diacid loses exactly one CO2 on decarboxylation; remaining acid keeps both alpha alkyl groups: HOOC-CH(CH3)(CH2CH3) = 2-methylbutanoic acid, option A (CCC(C)C(=O)O). Trap C (2-ethylbutanoic) assumes both carboxyls survive. All options valid/unique.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is alkylated with 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final carboxylic acid?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)C(C)C","OC(=O)CCCC","OC(=O)C(C)CC","OC(=O)CC(C)C","OC(=O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Enolate displaces bromide; (CH3)2CH- attaches to alpha-C. After decarboxylation: (CH3)2CH-CH2-COOH = 3-methylbutanoic (isovaleric) acid, option A (CC(C)CC(=O)O). Isobutyric acid (E) is the trap omitting the malonate CH2 spacer. All options valid/unique.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt and benzyl bromide, then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis yields a methyl ketone: benzylation then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH2-CH2Ph = 4-phenyl-2-butanone, option A. Phenylacetone (C) drops the alpha CH2; E is an acid (wrong synthesis). Verified CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1. All options valid/unique.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1,4-dibromobutane (Br-CH2CH2CH2CH2-Br). Two successive intramolecular alkylations occur at the same alpha-carbon, and the product is then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O","O=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Double alkylation of the alpha-C by the 4-carbon dihalide closes a 5-membered ring (alpha-C + 4 CH2). After decarboxylation the acetyl group remains on that ring carbon: acetylcyclopentane, option A (CC(=O)C1CCCC1). Cyclobutane (B) and cyclohexane (C) are off by one in ring size. All options valid/unique.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0); ring-size count verified."},{"q":"The dialkylated acetoacetic ester shown (alpha-carbon bearing both a methyl and an ethyl group) is hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. Which statement best describes the structure and stereochemistry of the product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(C)(CC)C(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The product is 3-methylpentanoic acid because decarboxylation converts the ketone to a carboxylic acid","The product is achiral because the alpha-carbon retains no stereocenter after decarboxylation","The product is 4-methyl-2-pentanone with the branch on the carbon beta to the carbonyl","The product is 3-methyl-2-pentanone, obtained as a racemic mixture because the alpha-carbon becomes a stereocenter formed without facial selectivity","The product is 3-methyl-2-pentanone, obtained as a single (R) enantiomer"],"correct":3,"why":"Decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH(CH3)(CH2CH3) = 3-methyl-2-pentanone; the alpha-C bears H, CH3, Et, and acetyl (four different groups), a stereocenter formed by non-selective protonation of a planar enol, hence racemic. Stays a ketone (not an acid), so options C/E/B wrong; single enantiomer (option 1 vs 2) ruled out by lack of chiral influence. Option A correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Classification item; q_smiles drawn correctly (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in the first step of an acetoacetic ester synthesis. Which species correctly represents the stabilized nucleophile generated, and why is that site deprotonated?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(=O)CC([CH2-])=O","[CH2-]COC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC[O-]","CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Ethoxide removes the most acidic proton on the central methylene flanked by both carbonyls (pKa ~11), giving a doubly resonance-stabilized enolate, option A (CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O). The terminal acetyl carbanion (B) is stabilized by only one carbonyl; ethoxide (C) is the base not the formed nucleophile; the OEt carbon (D) is not acidic; E shows no reaction. Carbanion SMILES are valid and distinct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Recognition-level acidity/resonance item, single reactive center, in scope. Correct (idx 0)."},{"q":"The substrate shown is heated in ethanol (a weak base/nucleophile), favoring an E1 pathway. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["CCC(C)CO","CCC(=C)C","CC=C(C)C","C=CCCC","C=CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is tertiary; hot ethanol (weak base, polar protic) drives E1 via a tertiary carbocation. Zaitsev gives the more-substituted trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene (CC=C(C)C, opt0). 2-methyl-1-butene is the disubstituted Hofmann minor product; 3-methyl-1-butene needs a skeletal change; the alcohol and 1-pentene are wrong frameworks. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid, single tertiary center, Zaitsev E1 correct, exactly one major product at idx0."},{"q":"The same tertiary bromide is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu), a strong, bulky base, in an E2 reaction. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CCC(=C)C","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A bulky strong base cannot reach the hindered internal beta-hydrogens, so it abstracts a less hindered terminal methyl proton, overriding Zaitsev to give the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene 2-methyl-1-butene (CCC(=C)C, opt0). 2-methyl-2-butene is the Zaitsev product favored only with small bases; the alcohol, unreacted bromide, and 3-methyl-1-butene are wrong. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hofmann/bulky-base logic correct; valid SMILES; one right answer at idx0."},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with a strong base under E2 conditions. E2 requires the C-H and C-Br bonds to be anti-periplanar (both axial). Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1Br","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"In the trans isomer, when Br is axial the C2 methyl is equatorial, so the C2 hydrogen is equatorial and cannot be anti-periplanar to Br. Elimination is forced to C6, whose axial H is anti to Br, giving 3-methylcyclohexene (CC1CCCC=C1, opt0; RDKit confirms methyl on an sp3 allylic C). The Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene is geometrically blocked; methylenecyclohexane and the unreacted/substitution products are wrong. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":3,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Anti-periplanar stereoelectronic constraint correctly forces 3-methylcyclohexene; valid SMILES; legitimate difficulty-3 item, single answer at idx0."},{"q":"The alcohol shown is treated with concentrated H2SO4 and heat (E1 dehydration). Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts":["CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","C=CCCC","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CCC(=C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Acid-catalyzed dehydration of tertiary 2-methyl-2-butanol proceeds by E1 (protonate OH, lose water to a tertiary cation, lose a proton). Zaitsev gives the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene (CC=C(C)C, opt0). 2-methyl-1-butene is the Hofmann minor product; 1-pentene and the alkyl chloride are wrong skeletons/products. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Tertiary E1 dehydration, Zaitsev correct, valid SMILES, one answer at idx0."},{"q":"2-Bromohexane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a strong but small base, in an E2 reaction. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC(C)Br","opts":["CC/C=C/CC","CCCCC(C)O","C/C=C\\CCC","C=CCCCC","C/C=C/CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A small strong base follows Zaitsev, giving internal 2-hexene over terminal 1-hexene; the trans (E) isomer (C/C=C/CCC, opt0) predominates as the more stable, less strained alkene. 1-hexene is the Hofmann product; cis-2-hexene is the minor stereoisomer; 3-hexene requires the wrong carbon and cannot form from 2-bromohexane; the alcohol is a substitution product. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Zaitsev + E-selectivity correct; valid SMILES incl. E/Z; single major product at idx0."},{"q":"Consider the alkyl iodide shown reacting in a polar protic solvent (aqueous ethanol) with only heat and no added strong base. Which statement BEST describes the elimination that occurs?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)I","opts":["E2: requires anti-periplanar geometry and a strong, bulky base like KOtBu","No elimination is possible because the substrate has no beta-hydrogens","E1: the rate is first order in the substrate and first order in the weak base","E1: the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation; rate depends only on the substrate, not on base concentration","E2: a concerted one-step reaction whose rate depends on both substrate and a strong base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"tert-Butyl iodide is tertiary; heat + weak base + polar protic solvent favor E1 with rate-determining ionization, so the rate is first order in substrate only (opt0). E2 needs a strong base and is bimolecular; E1 is not first order in base; and the substrate does have beta-hydrogens (the methyls), so elimination to isobutylene is possible. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classification/mechanism item (opts_are_structures=false) is permitted and the molecule is still drawn (valid SMILES). One concept, single correct statement at idx0."},{"q":"2-Bromopentane undergoes E2 with a strong base. Because E2 eliminates the anti-periplanar beta-hydrogen and favors the most stable alkene, which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)Br","opts":["C=CCCC","CCC(C)CO","C=CC(C)C","C/C=C/CC","C/C=C\\CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"E2 favors the more-substituted Zaitsev 2-pentene over terminal 1-pentene, and the trans (E) isomer (C/C=C/CC, opt0) dominates from the less strained anti-periplanar transition state. cis-2-pentene is the minor stereoisomer; 1-pentene is the Hofmann product; the alcohol and 3-methyl-1-butene (wrong, branched skeleton) cannot be the major product. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Zaitsev + E-selectivity correct; valid SMILES; single answer at idx0."},{"q":"The secondary alcohol shown is heated with concentrated H2SO4. Acid-catalyzed dehydration proceeds through a carbocation that can rearrange. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C","C=C(C)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol protonates and loses water to a secondary cation adjacent to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a far more stable tertiary cation. Zaitsev then yields the highly stable tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (CC(C)=C(C)C, opt0). The non-rearranged 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene and 2,3-dimethyl-1-butene are minor; the starting alcohol and 2-methyl-2-butene (wrong skeleton) are incorrect. Verified: correct answer is index 0.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hydride/methyl-shift rearrangement to tertiary cation then Zaitsev correct; valid SMILES; single major product at idx0. Two layered concepts (rearrangement + Zaitsev) within scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane is heated in ethanol (a weak base/nucleophilic solvent). Which structure is the major elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)(C)Br","opts":["CC(C)C(=C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)OC","CCC=C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)O","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide in a weak-base/ionizing solvent gives E1. The 3 deg carbocation loses a beta-H toward the C3 methine to form the tetrasubstituted Zaitsev alkene, 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (opt0), more stable than the disubstituted terminal Hofmann alkene or any substitution product. Verified: all 5 SMILES parse in RDKit; opt0 is the correct, most-substituted product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with sodium ethoxide (E2). Considering the anti-periplanar requirement, which is the major alkene?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCC=CC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Classic anti-periplanar override. In the trans isomer, when C-Br is axial the C2 methyl is also axial, so the only beta-H is equatorial (not anti-periplanar) and cannot eliminate; the tempting Zaitsev 1-methylcyclohexene cannot form. The only anti-periplanar (axial) beta-H is on C6, giving the less-substituted 3-methylcyclohexene (opt0). Textbook result; all SMILES valid. Legitimately difficulty 3, single reactive center.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"2-Bromopentane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide, a bulky strong base. Which alkene is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)Br","opts":["cis-2-Pentene (more-substituted, cis)","trans-2-Pentene (more-substituted, trans)","1-Pentene (terminal, least-substituted alkene)","2-Methyl-2-butene (rearranged skeleton)","2-Pentanol (substitution product)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A bulky base (tert-butoxide) is too hindered to reach the internal beta-H and abstracts the accessible terminal beta-H, giving the less-substituted Hofmann product 1-pentene (opt0). Small strong bases would favor the Zaitsev 2-pentene; no rearrangement in concerted E2; strong base drives elimination over substitution. Text options, but molecule is drawn (q_smiles valid).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-phenylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["C=CC(C)c1ccccc1","C/C=C(\\C)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C/C=C(/C)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"E2 removes the benzylic beta-H so the new double bond conjugates with the ring, giving 2-phenyl-2-butene. Of the two geometries, the CIP-E isomer (opt0, phenyl anti to the far methyl) is the more stable: RDKit MMFF gives opt0 ~0.6 kcal/mol lower than opt1. Terminal alkene and styrene fragment skip the available conjugation. Note: the drawn diastereomer makes a strictly stereospecific E2 fix geometry independent of stability, but the intended conjugation+stability recognition answer is opt0; acceptable 2-concept item.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":"Substrate is drawn as a single diastereomer, so a rigorously stereospecific E2 would fix the alkene geometry by anti-periplanar geometry rather than by thermodynamic stability; the why uses a stability argument. Both paths land on opt0 (conjugated CIP-E), so answer is unaffected, but the rationale slightly oversimplifies."},{"q":"2-Chloro-2,4,4-trimethylpentane undergoes elimination. Which structure is the major alkene?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CC(C)(C)Cl","opts":["C=C(C)CC(=C)C","CC(C)(C)C(C)=CC","CC(C)(C)C=C(C)C","CC(C)(C)CC(=C)C","CC(C)(C)CC(C)(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The tertiary chloride can eliminate toward the central CH2 (Zaitsev) or toward a gem-methyl (Hofmann). Toward CH2 gives the trisubstituted 2,4,4-trimethyl-2-pentene (opt0), more stable than the disubstituted 1-alkene. Substitution (alcohol) and skeletally rearranged options are wrong. RDKit confirms all 5 SMILES parse and opt0 is the trisubstituted Zaitsev product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"3-Bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane is solvolyzed in warm ethanol (E1 conditions). Which structure is the major elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(C)C(=C)C","CC(Br)=C(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives a 2 deg cation adjacent to the quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift forms a more stable 3 deg cation, then Zaitsev elimination yields tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (opt0). E1 goes through a free cation so rearrangement dominates; the unrearranged 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (opt1) needs concerted E2. opt4 (vinyl bromide retaining Br) is a valid SMILES but a chemically impossible elimination product (good distractor). All SMILES parse.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"You have 2-iodobutane (a secondary substrate) and want to favor the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene by E2. Which reagent choice best accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)I","opts":["Sodium iodide in acetone (weak base, good nucleophile)","Potassium tert-butoxide, a bulky strong base","Dilute aqueous acid with gentle heating","Sodium ethoxide, a small strong base","Sodium methoxide in methanol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"E2 regiochemistry is set by base size. A bulky base (potassium tert-butoxide, opt0) is sterically blocked from the internal beta-H and abstracts the accessible terminal beta-H, giving the Hofmann product. Small strong bases (ethoxide, methoxide) favor Zaitsev; NaI/acetone (SN2) and dilute acid do not drive Hofmann E2. Reagent-selection item, text options, molecule drawn (q_smiles valid).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"trans-1-Chloro-4-tert-butylcyclohexane reacts readily by E2 with strong base, while the cis isomer is sluggish. Which structure is the major product from the trans isomer?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C1CCC(Cl)CC1","opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CC=CC=C1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC=CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The bulky tert-butyl is locked equatorial. In the trans-1,4 isomer the chlorine is therefore axial, anti-periplanar to axial beta-Hs, so E2 is fast and gives 4-tert-butylcyclohexene (opt0). (cis holds Cl equatorial and is sluggish.) Exocyclic methylene, alcohol, arene, and diene are not formed. Single elimination product; all 5 SMILES parse in RDKit.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"Sodium borohydride (NaBH4) in methanol is added to the compound shown. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","OCC1CCCCC1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 reduces the aldehyde to the primary alcohol (benzyl alcohol). As a reductant it cannot make the carboxylic acid, does not deoxygenate to toluene, and leaves the aromatic ring intact. Confirmed correct index 0; all SMILES valid; opt3 is a starting-material decoy.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correct, in scope, valid SMILES. Minor: opt3 duplicates the starting material as a no-reaction decoy, which is a slightly weak distractor but chemically unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4), followed by aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["OC1CCCCC1","CCCCCC(=O)O","OC1CCCC1","C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the ketone carbonyl to the secondary alcohol cyclohexanol (opt0). It does not cleave C-C, fully deoxygenate, change ring size, or open the ring. Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. opt2 is a starting-material decoy (mild weakness) but the answer is unambiguous."},{"q":"1-Hexanol is treated with pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC) in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCO","opts":["CCCC(=O)CC","CCCCCC=O","CCCCCCO","CCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"PCC is a mild anhydrous oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde (hexanal, opt0). Aqueous Cr(VI)/KMnO4 would give the acid. Oxidation occurs at the OH-bearing carbon, so internal-ketone options are wrong. Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Classic PCC selectivity item; clean single correct answer."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is hydrogenated with H2 over a palladium catalyst at room temperature and 1 atm. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCC1CCCCC1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"H2/Pd reduces only the alkene at 1 atm/RT, giving ethylbenzene (opt0); the aromatic ring needs high P/T and special catalysts. No oxygen is added, ruling out the alcohols. Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. opt2 is a starting-material decoy but the answer is unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid (mCPBA) in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1CC2OC2CC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes the alkene to cyclohexene oxide (opt0). The vicinal diol requires a separate aqueous acid/base ring-opening step not present here. No carbonyl forms. Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid (opt0 parses as the bicyclic epoxide).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. opt4 is a starting-material decoy; the diol distractor adds good discrimination."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with osmium tetroxide (OsO4), then aqueous NaHSO3. Which structure (including stereochemistry) is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCC1","O=C1CCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"OsO4 performs syn dihydroxylation, delivering both OH groups to the same face. On cyclopentane the cis diol is the meso (R,S) isomer (opt0). 3D embedding confirms opt0 has both OH on the same face (cis) and opt1 has them anti (trans). The trans diol, mono-ol, dione (cleavage), and epoxide are wrong. Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Stereochemistry verified: opt0 = cis/meso (correct), opt1 = trans (distractor). Two layered concepts (dihydroxylation + stereo) is within scope."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene is treated with ozone (O3), then dimethyl sulfide (Me2S) in the reductive workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["CC(=O)O","O=CCC=O","CCC=O","CC(C)=O","CC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the C=C and converts each carbon to a carbonyl at its original substitution level. Both alkene carbons bear one H and one methyl, so each fragment is acetaldehyde (opt0). Oxidative workup would give acetic acid; acetone/propanal/malondialdehyde imply wrong skeletons. Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Symmetric alkene gives a single aldehyde product; clean and unambiguous."},{"q":"Methyl hexanoate is treated with one equivalent of diisobutylaluminum hydride (DIBAL-H) at -78 degrees C, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCC(=O)OC","opts":["CCCCC=O","CCCCCC(=O)OC","CCCCCC=O","CCCCCCO","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"DIBAL-H (1 equiv, -78 C) partially reduces an ester to the aldehyde: the tetrahedral aluminum hemiacetal is stable at low T and collapses to hexanal only on workup, stopping before the alcohol. Excess LiAlH4 would give the alcohol. DIBAL is not an oxidant, and the 6-carbon chain is retained (pentanal would lose a carbon). Index 0 confirmed; all SMILES valid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Recognition-level DIBAL partial reduction is a standard DAT-depth item; single reactive center, in scope. opt3 is a starting-material decoy."},{"q":"Methyl cyclopentanecarboxylate (shown) is treated with one equivalent of DIBAL-H at -78 C, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)C1CCCC1","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","COC(=O)C1CCCC1","O=CC1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"DIBAL-H at -78 C delivers a single hydride to the ester, forming a stable tetrahedral aluminum hemiacetal that collapses on workup to the aldehyde (cyclopentanecarbaldehyde, opt0). It does not over-reduce to the primary alcohol (opt1, requires excess DIBAL or LiAlH4). opt2 is an oxidation (wrong direction), opt3 a ketone (no C-C bond forms), opt4 unreacted ester. All SMILES valid; single reactive center; classic DAT chemoselectivity.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed by re-derivation."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (shown) is treated with mCPBA. Which structure is the major product, with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["CC(O)C(C)O","C/C=C/CO","C[C@H]1O[C@H]1C","CCC(C)=O","C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes via concerted syn (suprafacial) oxygen delivery, so alkene geometry is preserved: cis-2-butene gives the cis-epoxide. RDKit confirms opt0 is the (R,S) meso compound (identical to its mirror image), which is the cis-2,3-epoxybutane. opt1 is the (R,R) trans-epoxide (from trans-2-butene). opt2 is the diol (needs hydrolysis or OsO4), opt3 a ketone, opt4 an allylic alcohol. Stereochemistry verified meso=cis.","difficulty":2,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Meso (R,S) = cis-epoxide confirmed achiral by RDKit mirror-image test; correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (shown) is treated with OsO4 followed by aqueous NaHSO3 workup. Which is the major product, with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C1CCC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"OsO4 adds both C-O bonds to the same face (syn, via cyclic osmate ester), giving the cis-1,2-diol. RDKit confirms opt0 is the (R,S) meso compound (identical to mirror image) = cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol. opt1 is the (S,S) trans (chiral) diol, the classic epoxide-then-ring-opening distractor. opt2 is the epoxide, opt3 the ketone (over-oxidation), opt4 a mono-alcohol. Stereochemistry verified meso=cis.","difficulty":2,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. cis-diol = meso (R,S) confirmed achiral by RDKit; correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","O=CCCCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C, replacing each carbon with a carbonyl and opening the ring to a chain; reductive Me2S workup gives carbonyls (not acids). The methyl-bearing (disubstituted) carbon becomes a ketone, the H-bearing carbon becomes an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (opt0, C7H12O2 verified). opt1 (heptane-2,6-dione, same formula) wrongly makes both ends ketones; opt2 wrongly makes both aldehydes (ignores the methyl); opt3 is the over-oxidized acid (oxidative workup); opt4 is a non-cleavage alcohol.","difficulty":3,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. opt0 = OHC-(CH2)4-CO-CH3 = 6-oxoheptanal; regiochemistry (Me carbon->ketone) confirmed; correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"Ethyl 4-oxopentanoate (shown) is treated with NaBH4 in methanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","CC1CCC(=O)O1","CC(=O)CCCO","CCOC(=O)CCC(C)O","CC(O)CCCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride that reduces aldehydes/ketones but is too weak to reduce esters. It selectively reduces the ketone to a secondary alcohol, leaving the ethyl ester intact: ethyl 4-hydroxypentanoate (opt0). opt1 is the diol (needs LiAlH4 to also reduce the ester); opt2 reduces the ester not the ketone (backwards selectivity); opt3 is a lactone (cyclization, not the direct product); opt4 is an oxidation (wrong direction). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed by re-derivation."},{"q":"N,N-Dimethylbenzamide (shown) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CN(C)C(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","CN(C)CC1CCCCC1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide carbonyl fully to the amine (C=O becomes CH2, C-N bond retained), giving N,N-dimethylbenzylamine (opt0). The N staying attached is the hallmark of amide reduction. opt1 (aldehyde, C-N cleaved) needs a milder controlled reagent; opt2 (benzyl alcohol) and opt3 (benzoic acid) break the C-N bond; opt4 wrongly reduces the aromatic ring (LiAlH4 does not reduce benzene). All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed by re-derivation."},{"q":"(E)-2-Hexen-1-ol (shown) is treated with PCC in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC/C=C/CO","opts":["CCC/C=C/C(=O)O","CCC/C=C/C=O","CCCCCC=O","CCCC1OC1C=O","CCC/C=C\\C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"PCC (anhydrous) oxidizes a primary alcohol only to the aldehyde without over-oxidation to the acid, and does not touch C=C, so the E geometry is preserved: (E)-2-hexenal (opt0). opt1 is the carboxylic acid (over-oxidation PCC avoids); opt2 has lost the alkene (PCC does not reduce C=C); opt3 shows the wrong Z geometry; opt4 wrongly epoxidizes (PCC is not an epoxidizing agent). E-geometry retained in opt0 SMILES; all valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed; E geometry preserved in product SMILES."},{"q":"4-Nitrostyrene (shown) is treated with H2 over a Pd catalyst at room temperature (one equivalent of H2). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["CCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CCc1ccc(N=O)cc1","CCC1CCC([N+](=O)[O-])CC1","CCc1ccc(N)cc1","C=Cc1ccc(N)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"With one equivalent of H2 over Pd at room temperature, the reactive terminal alkene (vinyl) is hydrogenated to an ethyl group while the aromatic ring and the nitro group remain untouched under these mild, chemoselective conditions, giving 4-nitroethylbenzene (opt0). opt1 over-reduces both alkene and nitro (needs forcing conditions or Sn/Fe); opt2 reduces only nitro (wrong selectivity); opt3 is a partially reduced nitroso; opt4 wrongly reduces the benzene ring. All SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Defensible at DAT depth: with limiting H2 and emphasized chemoselectivity, alkene-only reduction (opt0) is the intended single answer; aromatic-NO2 reduction by H2/Pd requires more forcing conditions. correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"The allyl aryl ether shown is heated to 200 C with no catalyst or solvent. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this Claisen rearrangement?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["CC=Cc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1ccccc1O","C=CCOc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Aromatic Claisen [3,3]-sigmatropic shift: allyl migrates to the ortho ring carbon, then tautomerization restores aromaticity to give 2-allylphenol (C9H10O conserved). With an unsubstituted symmetric allyl, the terminal vinyl is regenerated. Distractors are unchanged ether, para isomer (only when both ortho blocked), internal-alkene isomer, and plain phenol.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). All SMILES valid; mass-balanced C9H10O across stem and right answer."},{"q":"Allyl vinyl ether is warmed. Identify the MAJOR product of this aliphatic Claisen [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement.","q_smiles":"C=COCC=C","opts":["CCCCC=O","C=CCCC=O","CC=CCC=O","CC(=O)CC=C","C=COCC=C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Aliphatic Claisen converts an allyl vinyl ether into a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl. The six-membered TS breaks O-CH2(allyl), forms a new C-C bond, and turns the vinyl C=C into C=O, giving 4-pentenal (C5H8O conserved). Distractors: unchanged ether, isomeric methyl ketone, 3-pentenal, and over-reduced pentanal.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). 4-pentenal C5H8O matches the C5H8O ether; pentanal distractor is correctly C5H10O (an obvious over-reduction)."},{"q":"(2E,4Z,6E)-octatriene aside, consider the parent (1Z)-1,3,5-hexatriene drawn here undergoing a thermal 6-pi-electron electrocyclic ring closure. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=CC=CC=C","opts":["C1=CC2CC1C2","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCC=C1","C1CCCCC1","c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"6-pi electrocyclic closure of 1,3,5-hexatriene forms one new sigma bond between the terminal carbons, giving 1,3-cyclohexadiene (C6H8 conserved). Distractors: 1,4-cyclohexadiene (wrong connectivity), benzene (would need loss of H2), cyclohexane (fully saturated), and a bicyclic skeleton (no such bond forms).","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry correct (index 0); C6H8 conserved into 1,3-cyclohexadiene, benzene distractor correctly C6H6. Minor: the '(2E,4Z,6E)-octatriene aside' preamble is clumsy and slightly distracting, but it does not affect the answer."},{"q":"When the compound shown is heated, it rearranges to 4-pentenal. Which term BEST classifies this transformation?","q_smiles":"C=COCC=C","opts":["Diels-Alder cycloaddition","[1,3]-sigmatropic shift","[3,3]-sigmatropic (Claisen) rearrangement","Electrocyclic ring closure","Cope rearrangement"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"An allyl vinyl ether rearranging to a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl is the Claisen rearrangement, a concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic shift through a six-membered chair-like TS. Not a [1,3]-shift, not electrocyclic/Diels-Alder, and distinct from the all-carbon Cope (a 1,5-diene).","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classification item with text options; q_smiles still drawn (valid). Correct (index 0)."},{"q":"3,4-Dimethyl-1,5-hexadiene is heated to drive a Cope rearrangement. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C(C)C=C","opts":["CC=CC=CCCC","C=CCCCCC=C","C=CC(C)C(C)C=C","CC=CCCC=CC","CC=CCC=CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The Cope is the all-carbon [3,3]-shift of a 1,5-diene. The C3-C4 sigma bond breaks, a new sigma bond forms between the two former terminal CH2 carbons, and the double bonds shift inward, converting 3,4-dimethyl-1,5-hexadiene into octa-2,6-diene (C8H14 conserved). Distractors: unchanged diene, octa-2,5-diene, 1,7-octadiene, and a conjugated 2,4-diene isomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). Re-derived bond reorganization (break C3-C4, form C1-C6, shift to 2,6-diene) gives octa-2,6-diene; all options C8H14."},{"q":"The allyl aryl ether shown (both positions ortho to oxygen bear methyl groups) is heated. Because the ortho sites are blocked, which structure is the MAJOR Claisen product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1c(C)cccc1C","opts":["C=CCOc1c(C)cccc1C","Oc1c(C)cccc1C","C=CCc1cc(C)c(O)c(C)c1","C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1c(C)cccc1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"With both ortho positions blocked, the initial ortho dienone cannot aromatize, so a second [3,3] (Cope) relay carries the allyl to the para position, giving 4-allyl-2,6-dimethylphenol (C11H14O conserved). Distractors: unchanged ether, an ortho product (impossible, ortho methylated), demethylated para, and the simple dimethylphenol (allyl is not lost).","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0); para-Claisen product C11H14O matches stem. Distractors 2/3/4 deliberately differ in formula (lose carbons), which is acceptable as wrong-skeleton lures."},{"q":"Crotyl phenyl ether (but-2-enyl phenyl ether) is heated to promote a Claisen rearrangement. Accounting for allylic inversion in the [3,3] shift, which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["C=CC(C)c1ccccc1O","C=CC(C)c1ccc(O)cc1","CC=CCc1ccccc1O","CC=CCOc1ccccc1","CC(=CC)c1ccccc1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"In the [3,3] Claisen the new C-C bond forms at the GAMMA carbon of the allyl unit, inverting the substitution pattern. For crotyl (CH3-CH=CH-CH2-O), bonding through the gamma (methyl-bearing) carbon puts that carbon on the ring and regenerates a terminal vinyl, giving 2-(but-3-en-2-yl)phenol. Distractors: non-inverted internal-alkene product, unchanged ether, para isomer, and a trisubstituted-alkene isomer.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). Verified allylic inversion: ring binds the gamma carbon bearing CH3, giving Ar-CH(CH3)-CH=CH2 ortho-phenol; all options C10H12O."},{"q":"(2E,4E)-2,4-hexadiene undergoes a THERMAL 4-pi-electron electrocyclic ring closure (conrotatory). Which structure correctly shows the stereochemistry of the 3,4-dimethylcyclobutene product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=C/C","opts":["C[C@H]1[C@@H](C)C=C1","CC1=CC1C","C/C=C/C=C/C","C[C@H]1[C@H](C)C=C1","CC1CCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A thermal 4-pi (4n-electron) electrocyclic reaction is CONROTATORY by the Woodward-Hoffmann rules. With both terminal methyls in (2E,4E) geometry, conrotatory closure places the methyls on OPPOSITE faces, giving trans-3,4-dimethylcyclobutene (the chiral, non-meso diastereomer). Distractors: cis (meso) diastereomer (the disrotatory/photochemical outcome), two wrong skeletons, and the unreacted open triene.","difficulty":3,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). RDKit confirms opt0 = (R,R), non-meso = trans; opt1 = (R,S) = meso = cis. Thermal conrotatory closure of (2E,4E) gives trans. Stereochemistry upper-edge but recognition-level WH rule, acceptable difficulty-3."},{"q":"3-Methyl-2-butanol is treated with concentrated HCl. Under these SN1 conditions, what is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)C","ClCC(C)CC","CC(Cl)(C)CC","ClCCC(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Protonation/loss of water gives a secondary C2 cation; a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, which captures chloride to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (CC(Cl)(C)CC = CCC(C)(C)Cl). Verified by RDKit: all four chloride options are C5H11Cl, opt4 is the C5H10 E1 alkene. Option 0 is the rearranged tertiary chloride and is the sole correct major product. Distractors are the un-rearranged secondary (o1) and two primary chlorides (o2,o3). Single reactive center, two layered concepts (Markovnikov ionization + hydride shift). In scope, 5 valid structural options.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol reacts with HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CCC(Br)(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C","BrCC(C)C(C)C","CC(Br)(C)C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Secondary C2 cation adjacent to a quaternary carbon (no H for hydride shift); a 1,2-methyl shift from C3 gives the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation -> 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (CC(Br)(C)C(C)C = CC(C)C(C)(C)Br). RDKit confirms o0,o1,o2 are C6H13Br, o3 is the C6H12 alkene, and o4 (CCC(Br)(C)C) is only C5H11Br - a wrong-skeleton 5-carbon distractor, exactly as the explanation states. Correct = 0, exactly one right answer, valid SMILES throughout.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CC=C(C)C","BrCCC(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)(C)CC","CCC(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary C2 cation; 1,2-hydride shift from C3 gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation -> 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (CC(Br)(C)CC = CCC(C)(C)Br), option 0. RDKit: o0-o3 all C5H11Br, o4 is the C5H10 alkene. Distractors: un-rearranged secondary (o1), anti-Markovnikov primary (o2), and 1-bromo-2-methylbutane primary isomer (o3). Minor: explanation labels o3 a 'wrong primary isomer' which is accurate enough. Correct = 0, single center, in scope, valid SMILES.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene undergoes electrophilic addition with HCl. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C","ClCC(C)(C)CC","CC(C)=C(C)C","ClCCC(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov gives a secondary C2 cation next to a quaternary carbon; 1,2-methyl shift yields the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation -> 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane (CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C = CC(C)C(C)(C)Cl), option 0. RDKit: o0-o3 all C6H13Cl, o4 the C6H12 alkene. Distractors are un-rearranged secondary (o1) and two primary chlorides (o2,o3). Correct = 0, exactly one right answer, in scope, all SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with HCl. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)C","CCC(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C","ClCCC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation forms the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation directly - already the most stable, so NO rearrangement occurs - and chloride capture gives 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (CCC(C)(C)Cl), option 0. Good test of recognizing when a shift is unnecessary. RDKit: o0-o3 are C5H11Cl; o4 (CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C) is C6H13Cl, a wrong-carbon-count (6C) distractor, exactly as the explanation flags. Correct = 0, single center, in scope, valid SMILES.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclopentylmethanol (a primary alcohol on a five-membered ring) is treated with concentrated HCl. The reaction proceeds through a carbocation. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCC1CCCC1","opts":["CC1(Cl)CCCC1","ClCC1CCCC1","C=C1CCCC1","C1=CCCC1","ClC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A primary cation would form, so a ring C-C bond migrates (ring expansion): 1,2-alkyl shift expands the five-membered ring to a secondary cyclohexyl cation -> chlorocyclohexane (ClC1CCCCC1), option 0. Classic ring-expansion item. RDKit confirms mass balance: stem C6H12O, o0/o1/o2 all C6H11Cl. o4 cyclopentene is C5H8 (loses a carbon, impossible) but functions as an obviously-wrong distractor; o3 methylenecyclopentane is a C6 alkene. Correct = 0, single concept (ring expansion) layered on ionization, in scope at difficulty 2. Valid SMILES throughout.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3-Methylbutan-2-ol is heated with concentrated H2SO4. After the carbocation forms, a 1,2-hydride shift occurs before elimination. Which alkene is the major (Zaitsev) product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)O","opts":["CCC=CC","CC(C)C(C)O","C=C(C)CC","CC(C)=CC","C=CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Loss of water gives a secondary cation at C2. A 1,2-hydride shift from C3 (which bears a tertiary C-H) moves the charge to the tertiary C3. E1 then follows Zaitsev to give 2-methyl-2-butene (opt0). Opt1 is unrearranged 3-methyl-1-butene, opt2 is the less-substituted 2-methyl-1-butene (Hofmann), opt3 (2-pentene) has the wrong skeleton, opt4 is unreacted alcohol. Single correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), chemistry canonical, exactly one Zaitsev major product, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl iodide (1-iodo-2,2-dimethylpropane) is solvolyzed in warm ethanol (SN1 conditions). What is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CI","opts":["CCOCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)(C)CO","OC(C)(C)CC","CCOC(CC)CC","CCOC(C)(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The unstable primary neopentyl cation never forms; a 1,2-methyl shift accompanies ionization to give the tertiary 2-methylbutan-2-yl cation. Ethanol traps it to 2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane (opt0, C7H16O). Opt1 is the unrearranged neopentyl ethyl ether, opt2/opt3 are water (wrong nucleophile) products, opt4 has the wrong carbon skeleton. Single correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), methyl-shift then ethanol capture correct, exactly one right answer, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methylbut-1-ene reacts with HBr. Considering Markovnikov addition followed by a possible hydride shift, what is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CC(Br)C(C)C","CCCC(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 (tertiary C-H) relocates the charge to C3, and bromide adds to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (opt0). Opt1 is the unrearranged Markovnikov adduct, opt2 is anti-Markovnikov, opt3/opt4 are wrong-skeleton C5H11Br isomers. All five are C5H11Br; single correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), rearranged Markovnikov product correct, exactly one right answer, in scope."},{"q":"1-Cyclobutylethan-1-ol is treated with acid. A ring-expansion (alkyl) shift relieves ring strain before elimination. Which is the major alkene product?","q_smiles":"OC(C)C1CCC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC=C1CCC1","C=CC1CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The initial secondary cation on the exocyclic carbon triggers a ring-bond migration that expands cyclobutane to a 1-methylcyclopentyl (tertiary) cation, relieving strain. Zaitsev E1 gives 1-methylcyclopentene (opt0). Opt1/opt2 are unexpanded cyclobutane alkenes, opt3 is a substitution (cyclopentanol) product, opt4 (cyclohexene) has the wrong ring size. Single correct answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), ring-expansion to 1-methylcyclopentene correct, exactly one right answer, in scope (single reactive center, two concepts: ring expansion + Zaitsev)."},{"q":"2-Chloro-3-methylbutane undergoes SN1 solvolysis and gives a rearranged tertiary product. Which statement best describes the rearrangement step that occurs?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C(C)C","opts":["Chloride leaves as a radical, and a hydrogen atom migrates to balance the spin","A 1,2-hydride shift from C1 to C2 generates a primary cation that is then trapped","A 1,2-hydride shift moves an H from C3 to C2, converting the secondary cation into a more stable tertiary cation","A proton is lost first to form a carbanion that then shifts to carbon","A 1,2-methyl shift moves a CH3 from C3 to C2, converting the secondary cation into a tertiary cation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Ionization gives a secondary cation at C2; C3 bears a tertiary C-H, so a single 1,2-hydride shift to C2 yields a tertiary cation directly (opt0). A methyl shift would only give another secondary cation (opt1 wrong), a hydride shift cannot make a primary cation (opt2), and the carbanion (opt3) / radical (opt4) descriptions are mechanistically wrong for cationic SN1. Single correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. Substrate SMILES valid (verified RDKit), classification item correctly draws the substrate, exactly one right statement, in scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene is treated with HCl. After Markovnikov addition, a 1,2-methyl shift occurs. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(C)(C)CC(C)Cl","CC(C)(Cl)CC(C)C","CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","ClCCC(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2 next to a quaternary C3. A 1,2-methyl shift to C2 yields a tertiary cation, and chloride traps it to 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane (opt0, C6H13Cl). Opt1 is the unrearranged adduct, opt2 is anti-Markovnikov; opt3 and opt4 are C7H15Cl (extra carbon), making them impossible products from a C6 alkene. Single correct answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Minor: distractors opt3/opt4 are C7H15Cl while the substrate is C6, so they are wrong-formula rather than skeletal isomers; this makes them slightly weaker distractors but does not create a second correct answer. All SMILES valid, exactly one correct, in scope. Kept."},{"q":"3-Methylpent-1-ene reacts with HCl. Account for Markovnikov addition and any hydride shift. Which is the major product, and what stereochemistry is expected at the new C-Cl center?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)CC","opts":["CCC(C)CCCl","CCC(Cl)CC","CCC(C)C(C)Cl","CCCC(C)(Cl)C","CCC(Cl)(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov gives a secondary cation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 (tertiary C-H) makes a tertiary cation at C3. Chloride adds to give 3-chloro-3-methylpentane (opt0), whose central carbon bears two ethyls, a methyl, and Cl, so it is not a stereocenter (the stereochem part of the prompt resolves to no stereocenter). Opt1 is unrearranged, opt2 is anti-Markovnikov, opt3 is a wrong-skeleton C6 isomer; opt4 is C5H11Cl (missing a carbon). Single correct answer.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Minor: the stereochemistry sub-question is a distractor (the rearranged center is not a stereocenter), which is chemically sound and acceptable at difficulty 3; opt4 is wrong-formula (C5) rather than a C6 isomer. All SMILES valid, exactly one correct, in scope. Kept."},{"q":"Among simple alkyl halides, neopentyl chloride (1-chloro-2,2-dimethylpropane) is notorious for giving rearranged products under SN1 conditions even though it is a primary halide. What is the underlying reason?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CCl","opts":["Ring strain in the molecule is relieved by a ring-expansion shift","The quaternary carbon donates a lone pair to stabilize the adjacent cation","The primary cation is too unstable to exist, so a 1,2-methyl shift occurs concertedly with ionization to give a tertiary cation","A 1,2-hydride shift converts the primary cation into a secondary cation that is fully stable","The primary cation is stabilized by hyperconjugation enough to react without any shift"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Loss of chloride would give a destabilized primary cation, so a methyl from the adjacent quaternary carbon migrates concertedly with ionization to deliver a tertiary cation (opt0). A hydride shift is impossible (neighbor has no H, so opt2 wrong), hyperconjugation alone cannot stabilize a primary cation (opt1), a quaternary carbon has no lone pair (opt3), and there is no ring to expand (opt4). Single correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. Substrate SMILES valid (verified RDKit), classification item correctly draws the substrate, exactly one right statement, in scope."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Using the standard pKa ladder, rank the following four compounds from MOST acidic to LEAST acidic: acetic acid (CH3COOH), phenol (PhOH, shown), water (H2O), and ethanol (CH3CH2OH).","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Phenol > acetic acid > ethanol > water","Acetic acid > phenol > water > ethanol","Phenol > acetic acid > water > ethanol","Water > ethanol > phenol > acetic acid","Acetic acid > phenol > ethanol > water"],"correct":1,"why":"pKa: acetic acid ~4.8 (resonance-delocalized carboxylate over two O) < phenol ~10 (phenoxide delocalized into ring) < water ~15.7 < ethanol ~16 (ethoxide destabilized by mildly donating alkyl). Order acetic > phenol > water > ethanol = option 0. Verified correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Water (15.7) vs ethanol (16) is a close, standard textbook pair; single defensible answer. Phenol stem SMILES valid."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Para-substituted phenols are shown generically; the molecule drawn is para-nitrophenol. Rank these four phenols from MOST acidic to LEAST acidic: para-nitrophenol, phenol (H), para-methylphenol (p-cresol), and para-methoxyphenol.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["p-methoxyphenol > p-methylphenol > phenol > p-nitrophenol","p-nitrophenol > p-methylphenol > phenol > p-methoxyphenol","p-nitrophenol > p-methoxyphenol > phenol > p-methylphenol","p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-methylphenol > p-methoxyphenol","phenol > p-nitrophenol > p-methylphenol > p-methoxyphenol"],"correct":3,"why":"p-nitro is a strong resonance EWG stabilizing phenoxide (pKa ~7); phenol reference ~10; p-methyl (weak donor) ~10.3; p-methoxy (resonance donor) least acidic. Order p-nitro > phenol > p-methyl > p-methoxy = option 0. Verified. p-nitrophenol stem SMILES valid (nitro written correctly as [N+](=O)[O-]).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. p-cresol vs p-methoxyphenol are close pKa values but follow the standard donor-strength teaching (resonance donation > hyperconjugation); single defensible answer."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Identify the MOST acidic proton in 4-pentynoic acid (drawn). Each choice shows the conjugate base formed by removing one specific proton; choose the structure that represents loss of the most acidic proton.","q_smiles":"C#CCCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH-]=C=CCC(=O)O","C#C[CH-]CC(=O)O","C#CC[CH-]C(=O)O","[C-]#CCCC(=O)O","C#CCCC(=O)[O-]"],"correct":4,"why":"Carboxylic O-H (pKa ~4.8) gives resonance-stabilized carboxylate over two O, far more acidic than terminal alkyne C-H (pKa ~25, localized sp acetylide) or sp3 CH2 carbanions (~40-50). Conjugate base = carboxylate = option a. correct=0 verified. All five option SMILES parse.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"The molecule drawn is trichloroacetic acid. Rank the following carboxylic acids from MOST acidic to LEAST acidic: trichloroacetic acid (Cl3C-COOH), dichloroacetic acid (Cl2CH-COOH), chloroacetic acid (ClCH2-COOH), and acetic acid (CH3-COOH).","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C(Cl)(Cl)Cl","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["trichloroacetic > dichloroacetic > chloroacetic > acetic","trichloroacetic > chloroacetic > dichloroacetic > acetic","dichloroacetic > trichloroacetic > chloroacetic > acetic","chloroacetic > dichloroacetic > trichloroacetic > acetic","acetic > chloroacetic > dichloroacetic > trichloroacetic"],"correct":0,"why":"Inductive electron withdrawal scales with number of Cl: trichloro (pKa ~0.7) > dichloro (~1.3) > chloro (~2.9) > acetic (~4.8). Option 0 verified. Stem SMILES valid trichloroacetic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"For 1-butyne (drawn), which statement correctly identifies the most acidic C-H proton and the reason?","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The terminal alkyne (sp C-H) is most acidic because the carbanion lone pair sits in an sp orbital with high s-character, holding the negative charge close to carbon","The terminal CH3 (sp3 C-H) is most acidic because primary carbanions are most stable","All C-H bonds are equally acidic since they are all on carbon","The terminal alkyne C-H is least acidic because sp carbon is the least electronegative","The internal CH2 (sp3 C-H) is most acidic because it is allylic"],"correct":0,"why":"Terminal sp alkyne C-H is most acidic (pKa ~25): acetylide lone pair in sp orbital (50% s-character) is held tight and close to the nucleus, more stable than sp3 carbanions (~50). Option 0 verified. Stem SMILES C#CCC is 1-butyne, valid. Distractor c is wrong (internal CH2 is propargylic-ish but not allylic and far less acidic); e inverts the s-character logic.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Identify the MOST acidic proton in dimedone (5,5-dimethyl-1,3-cyclohexanedione, drawn). Each choice is the conjugate base from removing one specific proton; pick the most stable conjugate base.","q_smiles":"O=C1CC(=O)CC(C)(C)C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1[C-]=C(O)CC(C)(C)C1","O=C1CC(=O)[CH-]C(C)(C)C1","O=C1[CH-]C(=O)CC(C)(C)C1","[O-]C1=CC(=O)CC(C)(C)C1","[CH2-]C1(C)CC(=O)CC(=O)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"The C2 methylene flanked by BOTH carbonyls is most acidic (pKa ~5); its carbanion is a doubly stabilized enolate delocalized onto two C=O oxygens. Option a (O=C1[CH-]C(=O)...) is exactly that C2 carbanion (verified by canonicalization: carbanion C sits between both carbonyl carbons). b is alpha to only one C=O; c is the unstabilized gem-dimethyl carbanion; d/e are O-centered/vinyl forms, not the most-acidic-C-H carbanion. correct=0 verified.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All five SMILES parse."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Identify the MOST acidic proton in ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate, drawn). Each choice shows the conjugate base from removing a specific proton; choose the most stable one.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)[CH2-]","C[CH-]OC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O","CCOC(=O)C=C(C)[O-]","[CH2-]COC(=O)CC(C)=O"],"correct":2,"why":"Central CH2 between the ester and ketone carbonyls is most acidic (pKa ~11), doubly stabilized enolate. Option a (CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O) is that central carbanion (verified: carbanion C bridges ester C=O and ketone C=O). b is alpha to only the ketone; c/d are unstabilized ethyl-group carbanions; e is an O-centered resonance form, not the most-acidic-C-H removal. correct=0 verified.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Difficulty 3 (mixed ester/ketone 1,3-dicarbonyl) but recognition-level, in DAT scope. All SMILES parse."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"The molecule drawn is aniline. Rank the following nitrogen bases from STRONGEST base to WEAKEST base: diethylamine, ammonia, pyridine, and aniline.","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["ammonia > diethylamine > aniline > pyridine","diethylamine > ammonia > pyridine > aniline","diethylamine > pyridine > ammonia > aniline","aniline > pyridine > ammonia > diethylamine","pyridine > diethylamine > ammonia > aniline"],"correct":1,"why":"Conjugate-acid pKaH: diethylamine ~11 (two donating alkyls) > ammonia ~9.2 (reference) > pyridine ~5.2 (sp2 lone pair, more held but still localized/available) > aniline ~4.6 (lone pair delocalized into ring, least available). Order = option 0. Verified. Aniline stem SMILES valid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"Rank the following acids from STRONGEST to WEAKEST. The molecule drawn is one of them: trichloroacetic acid (drawn), acetic acid, chloroacetic acid, and 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol. Which ordering is correct?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C(Cl)(Cl)Cl","opts":["Chloroacetic acid > trichloroacetic acid > acetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol","Trichloroacetic acid > acetic acid > chloroacetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol","Acetic acid > chloroacetic acid > trichloroacetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol > trichloroacetic acid > chloroacetic acid > acetic acid","Trichloroacetic acid > chloroacetic acid > acetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Carboxylates (pKa ~0-5) are resonance-stabilized over two oxygens, far stronger than the alcohol trifluoroethanol (pKa ~12.5), which is weakest. Among the carboxylic acids, more inductively withdrawing Cl atoms stabilize the carboxylate: trichloroacetic (pKa ~0.7) > chloroacetic (pKa ~2.9) > acetic (pKa ~4.8). Index 0 is correct and uniquely right.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"The alcohol drawn is 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol. Compared with ethanol, isopropanol, and tert-butanol, which statement about acidity is correct?","q_smiles":"OCC(F)(F)F","opts":["All four alcohols have essentially identical acidity because they all ionize at the same O-H bond","tert-Butanol is the most acidic because its bulky alkyl groups stabilize the alkoxide","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol is the least acidic because fluorine lone pairs destabilize the alkoxide","Ethanol is the most acidic because it has the smallest alkyl group donating the least electron density","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol is the most acidic because the three fluorines inductively stabilize its alkoxide conjugate base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acidity tracks alkoxide stability. Three electron-withdrawing fluorines stabilize the conjugate base inductively, making trifluoroethanol (pKa ~12.5) markedly more acidic than ethanol (~16) or the more alkyl-substituted alcohols. Alkyl groups are electron-donating and destabilize the alkoxide, so tert-butanol is least acidic. Index 0 is the only correct statement.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Pent-1-yn-3-ol is drawn below. It contains a terminal alkyne C-H, an O-H, and several sp3 C-H bonds. Which proton is the MOST acidic?","q_smiles":"C#CC(O)CC","opts":["The terminal alkyne (sp) C-H, because sp carbon holds the lone pair most tightly","A methylene (sp3) C-H of the ethyl group, because that carbanion is alkyl-stabilized","All C-H and O-H protons here are equally acidic because none give a resonance-stabilized anion","The methine C-H bearing the OH, because it is allylic to the triple bond","The O-H proton, because oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, so the alkoxide bears charge on the more electronegative atom"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"When charge sits on different atoms, electronegativity dominates: the alkoxide (O-H pKa ~16-18) is more stable than the acetylide from the terminal alkyne (pKa ~25), which in turn beats any sp3 carbanion (pKa ~45+). So the O-H is most acidic. Hybridization only orders the carbon acids. Index 0 correct and uniquely right.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"1,3-Cyclohexanedione is drawn below. Which structure is the conjugate base formed when its MOST acidic proton is removed?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC(=O)C1","opts":["[O-]C1=CCCC(=O)C1","[O-]C1CCCC(=O)C1","O=C1CCCC(=O)[CH-]1","O=C1[CH-]CCC(=O)C1","O=C1CC[CH-]CC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The most acidic proton is on C2, flanked by both carbonyls; its carbanion is delocalized onto two carbonyl oxygens (1,3-diketone enolate, pKa ~5-6). Verified: opt0 anion carbon is adjacent to two carbonyl carbons; opt1 only one; opt3 zero; opt2 is an enolate not spanning both carbonyls; opt4 is a reduction (C=O to C-O-, formula C6H9O2-, gained an H) not a deprotonation. Index 0 uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate) is drawn below. Which structure is the conjugate base produced by removing its MOST acidic proton?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)C","opts":["CCO[CH-]C(=O)CC(=O)C","CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(=O)C","CCOC([O-])=CC(=O)C","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)[CH2-]","[CH2-]COC(=O)CC(=O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The methylene between the ketone and ester carbonyls gives a doubly stabilized enolate (pKa ~11). Verified: opt0 anion carbon is adjacent to two carbonyl carbons. opt1 (terminal methyl) is alpha to only one carbonyl; opt2 is on the unactivated ester ethyl CH2; opt3 places the anion on O at a non-delocalized site; opt4 is a rearranged skeleton (formula C7H11O3-, gained a carbon). Index 0 uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"4-Cyanophenol is drawn below. Ranking it against phenol, p-cresol (4-methylphenol), and 4-methoxyphenol, which phenol is the STRONGEST acid?","q_smiles":"N#Cc1ccc(O)cc1","opts":["p-Cresol, because the methyl group hyperconjugatively stabilizes the negative charge","All four are equal because the O-H bond strength is identical in every ring","4-Methoxyphenol, because the methoxy oxygen donates a lone pair that stabilizes the phenoxide","4-Cyanophenol, because the para cyano group withdraws electron density and resonance-stabilizes the phenoxide","Phenol, because an unsubstituted ring spreads the charge most evenly"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A para electron-withdrawing group that conjugates (cyano) delocalizes the phenoxide charge into the substituent, strongly stabilizing the anion (4-cyanophenol pKa ~7.9 vs phenol ~10). Electron-donating methyl and methoxy destabilize the phenoxide and lower acidity. Index 0 correct and uniquely right.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Imidazole is drawn below. It has two ring nitrogens: a pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen and a pyridine-type (=N-) nitrogen. Which statement correctly identifies the MORE BASIC nitrogen and why?","q_smiles":"c1cnc[nH]1","opts":["The pyrrole-type nitrogen is more basic because protonating it preserves aromaticity","The pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen is more basic because it already bears a hydrogen, making it electron-rich","The pyridine-type (=N-) nitrogen is more basic because its lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the plane and is NOT part of the aromatic pi system","The pyridine-type nitrogen is more basic because its lone pair is delocalized into the aromatic ring","Both nitrogens are equally basic because the ring is symmetric"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The pyridine-type N keeps an in-plane sp2 lone pair outside the aromatic sextet, available to be protonated (imidazolium pKa ~7). The pyrrole-type N-H donates its lone pair into the aromatic pi system, so that pair is unavailable. Lone-pair availability sets the basic site. Index 0 correct and uniquely right.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"1,1,1-Trifluoropentane-2,4-dione is drawn below. Which structure is the conjugate base formed by removal of its MOST acidic proton?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)CC(=O)C(F)(F)F","opts":["O=C(C)[CH-]C(=O)C(F)(F)F","O=C(C)CC([O-])=C(F)F","[CH2-]C(=O)CC(=O)C(F)(F)F","[CH-](C(=O)C)CC(=O)C(F)(F)F","O=[C-]CC(=O)C(F)(F)F"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The central proton between both carbonyls gives a carbanion delocalized onto two carbonyl oxygens, with extra CF3 inductive stabilization (more acidic than acetylacetone). Verified: opt0 anion carbon is adjacent to two carbonyl carbons (formula C5H4F3O2-, a true deprotonation). opt1 is alpha to one carbonyl; opt2 is an acyl anion missing the methyl (C4H2F3O2-); opt3 lost a fluorine (C5H5F2O2-); opt4 is scrambled (C6H6F3O2-). Index 0 uniquely correct. Two layered concepts (double activation + CF3 induction) keep it at difficulty 3 within DAT recognition scope.","difficulty":3,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"The molecule shown is (2S,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane. Which structure is its enantiomer?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","opts":["CCC(Br)C(C)Br","C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C","CCC(Br)CBr","C[C@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"An enantiomer is the non-superimposable mirror image, so EVERY stereocenter must be inverted. (2S,3S) inverts to (2R,3R), which is option A. RDKit confirms q_smiles is (S,S) and opt0 is (R,R) and the exact mirror image. The two single-inverted options are diastereomers (and because C2/C3 carry different substituents here, neither is meso). The 1,2-dibromobutane and 2,3-dibromopentane options have different connectivity, so they are constitutional isomers.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified: q_smiles=(S,S), opt0=(R,R) is the true mirror; distractors correctly diastereomer + 2 constitutional isomers."},{"q":"The molecule shown is meso-tartaric acid. Which structure represents the SAME compound (i.e., is identical to it)?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","opts":["OC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)C(O)C(O)CC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Meso-tartaric acid has an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and superimposable on its own mirror image. RDKit confirms the stem is meso (canonical SMILES equals its own mirror image) and that option A has the IDENTICAL canonical SMILES, so it is the same compound. The two mixed-tag options are the chiral diastereomers of meso. Malic acid (one OH) and the five-carbon diacid have different connectivity, so they are constitutional isomers.","difficulty":2,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified meso is achiral (parent==mirror); opt0 canonicalizes identically to the stem; distractors are 2 diastereomers + 2 constitutional isomers."},{"q":"The molecule shown is D-alanine. Which structure is its enantiomer (L-alanine)?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](N)C(=O)O","opts":["C[C@@H](N)C(=O)O","NCC(C)C(=O)O","C[C@@H](N)CC(=O)O","C[C@H](N)C(=O)O","CC(N)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"D-alanine is (R)-alanine (RDKit confirms q_smiles is R). Its enantiomer L-alanine is the single inverted center, option A (S), confirmed by RDKit as the exact mirror image. The first distractor is an identical redrawing of D-alanine (R). The 3-amino-substituted chains have nitrogen on a different carbon, making them constitutional isomers. The flat unspecified-stereocenter alanine has no defined configuration so it is not the enantiomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified D-alanine=R, opt0=S mirror; opt1 is identical (fair trap), distractors constitutional + flat."},{"q":"The molecule shown is (R)-2-chlorobutane. Which structure is its enantiomer?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](Cl)C","opts":["CCCCl","CC[C@H](Cl)C","CCC(Cl)CC","CC[C@@H](Cl)C","CCCC(Cl)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"2-chlorobutane has one stereocenter; RDKit confirms q_smiles is (R). Inverting it gives the (S) enantiomer, option A, confirmed as the exact mirror image. The first distractor is the identical (R) molecule redrawn. 3-chloropentane, 1-chloropropane, and 2-chloropentane differ in carbon skeleton or Cl position, so they are constitutional isomers.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified R parent, opt0 is S mirror; opt1 identical, three constitutional-isomer distractors."},{"q":"How many distinct stereoisomers exist for 3-bromobutan-2-ol (shown)?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(Br)C","opts":["1","3","2","8","4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Apply the 2^n rule: C2 (OH) and C3 (Br) are both stereocenters and the molecule has no internal symmetry (the two ends differ: CH(OH)CH3 vs CH(Br)CH3), so no meso form is possible. RDKit stereoisomer enumeration yields exactly 4 distinct structures (two enantiomeric pairs), confirming 2^2 = 4 is fully realized. The 3 answer is the trap for a molecule that has a meso form; this one does not.","difficulty":2,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — RDKit enumerates exactly 4 unique stereoisomers; answer index 0 ('4') is correct."},{"q":"The molecule shown is D-glyceraldehyde. Which structure is its enantiomer (L-glyceraldehyde)?","q_smiles":"OC[C@@H](O)C=O","opts":["OC[C@@H](O)CO","OCC(O)C=O","OC[C@@H](O)C=O","OC[C@H](O)C=O","O=CC(O)C(O)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"D-glyceraldehyde is (R)-glyceraldehyde (RDKit confirms q_smiles is R). Its single stereocenter inverted gives L (S), option A, confirmed as the exact mirror image. The first distractor is the identical D molecule. The flat glyceraldehyde has no specified configuration. The diol (no aldehyde) and the four-carbon aldose have different connectivity, making them constitutional isomers.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified D=R parent, opt0=S mirror; opt1 identical, flat + 2 constitutional distractors."},{"q":"The molecule shown is (2R,3R)-pentane-2,3-diol. Which structure is its enantiomer?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","opts":["CCC(O)C(O)CO","C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)CC","CC(O)CC(O)C","C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"RDKit confirms the stem is (2R,3R). The enantiomer requires inverting BOTH stereocenters to (2S,3S), which is option A, confirmed as the exact mirror image. Because C2 (next to methyl) and C3 (next to ethyl) bear different groups, there is no internal symmetry and no meso form, so the two single-inverted options are ordinary diastereomers. 2,4-pentanediol and the extra-hydroxyl chain have different connectivity, so they are constitutional isomers.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified (R,R) parent, opt0=(S,S) mirror; no meso possible; distractors 2 diastereomers + 2 constitutional."},{"q":"Using the 2^n rule, how many distinct stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane (shown)?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","opts":["6","4","2","3","1"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"There are n = 2 stereocenters, so 2^n predicts a maximum of 4. But the two ends are identical (both CH(Cl)CH3), giving an internal mirror plane in the (R,S) configuration, which is a single achiral meso compound. RDKit stereoisomer enumeration returns exactly 3 distinct structures: the (R,R)/(S,S) enantiomeric pair plus one meso form. This is the classic symmetry case where the real count is less than 2^n.","difficulty":3,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — RDKit enumerates exactly 3 unique stereoisomers (pair + meso); answer index 0 ('3') is correct."}];